(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["brihadaranyaka"]={"slug":"brihadaranyaka","name":"Bṛhadāraṇyaka","deva":"बृहदारण्यक","note":"Adhyāyas 1–4 so far","intro":["The longest and, by most reckonings, the oldest of the <em>principal</em> Upaniṣads, forming the concluding portion of the <strong>Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa</strong> of the <strong>Śukla (White) Yajurveda</strong>. It survives in two recensions — <strong>Kāṇva</strong> (the one followed here, the basis of Śaṅkara's <em>bhāṣya</em>) and <strong>Mādhyandina</strong>. The name = <em>bṛhat</em> (\"great, vast\") + <em>āraṇyaka</em> (\"forest-book,\" a text studied in the forest for its esoteric danger and depth). It is the <em>mahā</em>-Upaniṣad of the <em>jñāna-kāṇḍa</em>: home of the <em>mahā-vākya</em> <strong>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</strong> (1.4.10), the <strong>neti neti</strong> formula, the Yājñavalkya dialogues, and the <strong>asato mā sad gamaya</strong> prayer.","Sanskrit text follows the <strong>Kāṇva recension</strong> (the recension on which Śaṅkara comments and the standard for the Advaita tradition). Cross-referenced against sanskritdocuments.org (`bribasic.html`, `bri.html` with svara), Swāmī Mādhavānanda's edition (Advaita Ashrama, 1934, with Śaṅkara-bhāṣya), and Patrick Olivelle, <em>The Early Upaniṣads</em> (OUP, 1998).<br>⚠️ <em>Accuracy note:</em> the <em>mūla</em> (root text) below is reproduced from the standard Kāṇva recension. Sandhi and pada-division conventions vary slightly between print editions; readers doing close textual work should spot-check each mantra against sanskritdocuments.org's accented (`sasvara`) edition."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad","title":"Adhyāyas 1–4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<strong>बृहदारण्यक उपनिषत्</strong> — <em>\"The Great Forest-Book of Secret Teaching\"</em>","The longest and, by most reckonings, the oldest of the <em>principal</em> Upaniṣads, forming the concluding portion of the <strong>Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa</strong> of the <strong>Śukla (White) Yajurveda</strong>. It survives in two recensions — <strong>Kāṇva</strong> (the one followed here, the basis of Śaṅkara's <em>bhāṣya</em>) and <strong>Mādhyandina</strong>. The name = <em>bṛhat</em> (\"great, vast\") + <em>āraṇyaka</em> (\"forest-book,\" a text studied in the forest for its esoteric danger and depth). It is the <em>mahā</em>-Upaniṣad of the <em>jñāna-kāṇḍa</em>: home of the <em>mahā-vākya</em> <strong>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</strong> (1.4.10), the <strong>neti neti</strong> formula, the Yājñavalkya dialogues, and the <strong>asato mā sad gamaya</strong> prayer.","Sanskrit text follows the <strong>Kāṇva recension</strong> (the recension on which Śaṅkara comments and the standard for the Advaita tradition). Cross-referenced against sanskritdocuments.org (`bribasic.html`, `bri.html` with svara), Swāmī Mādhavānanda's edition (Advaita Ashrama, 1934, with Śaṅkara-bhāṣya), and Patrick Olivelle, <em>The Early Upaniṣads</em> (OUP, 1998).","⚠️ <em>Accuracy note:</em> the <em>mūla</em> (root text) below is reproduced from the standard Kāṇva recension. Sandhi and pada-division conventions vary slightly between print editions; readers doing close textual work should spot-check each mantra against sanskritdocuments.org's accented (`sasvara`) edition."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Where Adhyāya 1 sits","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Bṛhadāraṇyaka has <strong>6 adhyāyas / 47 brāhmaṇas / ~434 mantras</strong> (Kāṇva), traditionally grouped into three <em>kāṇḍas</em>:","<table><tr><td>Kāṇḍa</td><td>Adhyāyas</td><td>Theme</td><td>Nickname</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Madhu-kāṇḍa</strong></td><td>1–2</td><td>The \"honey\" — the unity of the self with all beings; foundational teaching</td><td><em>Madhu-kāṇḍa</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Muni / Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa</strong></td><td>3–4</td><td>The great debates of Yājñavalkya in Janaka's court; dialectical proof</td><td><em>Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Khila-kāṇḍa</strong></td><td>5–6</td><td>\"Supplement\" — meditations (upāsanā), ethics, cosmogony, the lineage</td><td><em>Khila-kāṇḍa</em></td></tr></table>","<strong>Adhyāya 1</strong> (this volume) opens the Madhu-kāṇḍa with <strong>6 brāhmaṇas</strong>:","<table><tr><td>Brāhmaṇa</td><td>Mantras</td><td>Subject</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1.1</strong></td><td>2</td><td>The sacrificial <strong>horse as the cosmos</strong> (aśva-medha as meditation)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1.2</strong></td><td>7</td><td><strong>Creation out of Death / Hunger</strong> (Mṛtyu, Arka, the Aśvamedha cosmogony)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1.3</strong></td><td>28</td><td>The <strong>Udgītha</strong> — the breath (mukhya-prāṇa) that carries the gods past evil</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1.4</strong></td><td>17</td><td><strong>Creation from the Ātman / Puruṣa</strong> — <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em>, the origin of duality, of the varṇas, of name-form</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1.5</strong></td><td>23</td><td>The <strong>sevenfold food</strong> of Prajāpati; mind-speech-breath; the three worlds</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1.6</strong></td><td>3</td><td>The triad <strong>nāma–rūpa–karma</strong> (name, form, action) and the immortal that pervades them</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five commentaries used in this volume","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Śaṅkara (Śaṅkarācārya), <em>Bṛhadāraṇyakopaniṣad-bhāṣya</strong></em> (8th c.) — the foundational classical commentary, the longest of his Upaniṣad <em>bhāṣyas</em>. Pure Advaita: reads every ritual-meditation passage (1.1–1.5) as preparatory <em>upāsanā</em> that is ultimately <em>sublated</em> by the <em>jñāna</em> of 1.4.10. English here via Swāmī Mādhavānanda (Advaita Ashrama). Cited as <strong>Śaṅkara</strong>.","2. <strong>Sureśvara, <em>Bṛhadāraṇyakopaniṣad-bhāṣya-vārttika</strong></em> (8th–9th c.) — Śaṅkara's direct disciple; a monumental verse sub-commentary (~12,000 ślokas), the largest <em>vārttika</em> in Advaita. Defends Śaṅkara against rival readings, develops the <em>sambandha-vārttika</em> (the famous prologue on knowledge vs. action). Cited as <strong>Sureśvara</strong>.","3. <strong>Swami Krishnananda (Divine Life Society), <em>The Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</em> (lectures)</strong> — the most exhaustive modern oral commentary; weaves Śaṅkara's metaphysics with existential-psychological exposition and <em>sādhanā</em> guidance. Cited as <strong>Krishnananda</strong>.","4. <strong>Swami Mādhavānanda / Swami Sivananda (Advaita Ashrama; Divine Life Society)</strong> — the practical-devotional and standard-translation lens; Mādhavānanda supplies the literal scholarly rendering, Sivananda the meditative application. Cited as <strong>Mādhavānanda/Sivananda</strong>.","5. <strong>Paul Deussen (1897) &amp; Patrick Olivelle (1998)</strong> — the Indological / text-critical lens. Deussen (<em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em>) reads the cosmogonies comparatively; Olivelle (<em>The Early Upaniṣads</em>, critical edition + translation) gives the philological and historical-context reading. Cited as <strong>Deussen/Olivelle</strong>.","<em>On usage below</em>: under each mantra, <strong>Śaṅkara</strong> is the classical Advaita lens, <strong>Sureśvara</strong> the dialectical-defensive refinement, <strong>Krishnananda</strong> the philosophical-practical exposition, <strong>Mādhavānanda/Sivananda</strong> the literal + meditative, <strong>Deussen/Olivelle</strong> the text-critical/comparative."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha (Peace invocation of the Śukla Yajurveda)","title":"","sa":["ॐ पूर्णमदः पूर्णमिदं पूर्णात्पूर्णमुदच्यते ।","पूर्णस्य पूर्णमादाय पूर्णमेवावशिष्यते ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["Oṃ pūrṇam adaḥ pūrṇam idaṃ pūrṇāt pūrṇam udacyate |","pūrṇasya pūrṇam ādāya pūrṇam evāvaśiṣyate ||","Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>That is full (pūrṇa); this is full. From the full, the full proceeds. Taking the full from the full, the full alone remains.</strong> Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This is the most celebrated of all <em>śānti-mantras</em>: the <em>infinite</em> (Brahman) is not diminished by the projection of the world out of it, nor by the reabsorption of the world back into it. <em>Pūrṇa</em> = whole/full/infinite — that which has nothing outside it and so cannot be added to or subtracted from. It is the thesis the whole Upaniṣad will unpack: the manifold arises from the One <strong>without the One ever becoming many</strong>."]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 1","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya 1","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each mantra below is given as: <strong>Devanāgarī → IAST → pada-by-pada gloss → literal translation → paraphrase → 5-commentary digest.</strong> Each <em>brāhmaṇa</em> closes with three applied sections — <strong>Details</strong> (deeper exposition), <strong>Visual representation</strong> (diagram), and <strong>Practical steps / observations</strong> (contemplative application).","<em>Grammar abbreviations:</em> m./f./n. = masc./fem./neut.; nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc. = case; sg./du./pl. = number; pres./impf./pf./aor./pot./impv./ger./inf./caus. = tense/mood/non-finite; pp. = past participle; pf.pass.part. = perfect passive participle."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 1 · Brāhmaṇa 1.1 — The Sacrificial Horse as the Universe","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 1.1","title":"The Sacrificial Horse as the Universe","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The Upaniṣad opens, startlingly, mid-ritual. The</em> <strong>aśvamedha</strong> <em>(horse-sacrifice) was the most prestigious Vedic royal rite. Rather than describe the rite, the text turns it inward: the sacrificial horse</em> <strong>is</strong> <em>the entire cosmos, limb by limb. To \"meditate on the horse as the world\" is to perform the sacrifice as inner contemplation (ārambha of the</em> jñāna-yajña<em>).</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.1","title":"The horse's body is the world","sa":["ॐ उषा वा अश्वस्य मेध्यस्य शिरः । सूर्यश्चक्षुर्वातः प्राणो व्यात्तमग्निर्वैश्वानरः संवत्सर आत्माश्वस्य मेध्यस्य । द्यौः पृष्ठमन्तरिक्षमुदरं पृथिवी पाजस्यं दिशः पार्श्वे अवान्तरदिशः पर्शव ऋतवोऽङ्गानि मासाश्च अर्धमासाश्च पर्वाणि अहोरात्राणि प्रतिष्ठा नक्षत्राण्यस्थीनि नभो मांसानि । ऊवध्यं सिकताः सिन्धवो गुदा यकृच्च क्लोमानश्च पर्वता ओषधयश्च वनस्पतयश्च लोमानि उद्यन्पूर्वार्धो निम्लोचञ्जघनार्धो यद्विजृम्भते तद्विद्योतते यद्विधूनुते तत्स्तनयति यन्मेहति तद्वर्षति वागेवास्य वाक् ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["Oṃ uṣā vā aśvasya medhyasya śiraḥ | sūryaś cakṣur vātaḥ prāṇo vyāttam agnir vaiśvānaraḥ saṃvatsara ātmāśvasya medhyasya | dyauḥ pṛṣṭham antarikṣam udaraṃ pṛthivī pājasyaṃ diśaḥ pārśve avāntaradiśaḥ parśava ṛtavo'ṅgāni māsāś cārdhamāsāś ca parvāṇy ahorātrāṇi pratiṣṭhā nakṣatrāṇy asthīni nabho māṃsāni | ūvadhyaṃ sikatāḥ sindhavo gudā yakṛc ca klomānaś ca parvatā oṣadhayaś ca vanaspatayaś ca lomāni udyan pūrvārdho nimlocañ jaghanārdho yad vijṛmbhate tad vidyotate yad vidhūnute tat stanayati yan mehati tad varṣati vāg evāsya vāk || 1 ||"],"words":[["uṣāḥ","f. nom. sg.","the dawn"],["vai","indecl. (emphatic)","verily"],["aśvasya medhyasya","m. gen. sg.","of the sacrificial horse"],["śiraḥ","n. nom. sg.","(is) the head"],["sūryaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the sun"],["cakṣuḥ","n. nom. sg.","(is) the eye"],["vātaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the wind"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the breath"],["vyāttam","n. nom. sg. (pp. of vi-ā-dā)","the open mouth"],["agniḥ vaiśvānaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the universal fire"],["saṃvatsaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the year"],["ātmā","m. nom. sg.","(is) the body/trunk"],["dyauḥ","f. nom. sg.","the sky"],["pṛṣṭham","n. nom. sg.","(is) the back"],["antarikṣam","n. nom. sg.","the mid-region"],["udaram","n. nom. sg.","(is) the belly"],["pṛthivī","f. nom. sg.","the earth"],["pājasyam","n. nom. sg.","(is) the under-belly / hoof-region"],["diśaḥ","f. nom. pl.","the (cardinal) directions"],["pārśve","n. nom. du.","(are) the two flanks"],["avāntara-diśaḥ","f. nom. pl.","the intermediate directions"],["parśavaḥ","f. nom. pl.","(are) the ribs"],["ṛtavaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the seasons"],["aṅgāni","n. nom. pl.","(are) the limbs"],["māsāḥ ca ardha-māsāḥ ca","m. nom. pl.","the months and half-months"],["parvāṇi","n. nom. pl.","(are) the joints"],["ahorātrāṇi","n. nom. pl.","days-and-nights"],["pratiṣṭhā","f. nom. sg.","(are) the feet/standing-ground"],["nakṣatrāṇi","n. nom. pl.","the constellations"],["asthīni","n. nom. pl.","(are) the bones"],["nabhaḥ","n. nom. sg.","the (cloudy) sky/mist"],["māṃsāni","n. nom. pl.","(is) the flesh"],["ūvadhyam","n. nom. sg.","half-digested food (in the stomach)"],["sikatāḥ","f. nom. pl.","(is) the sand"],["sindhavaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the rivers"],["gudāḥ","f. nom. pl.","(are) the blood-vessels/entrails"],["yakṛt ca klomānaḥ ca","n. nom.","the liver and lungs"],["parvatāḥ","m. nom. pl.","(are) the mountains"],["oṣadhayaḥ ca vanaspatayaḥ ca","f./m. nom. pl.","herbs and trees"],["lomāni","n. nom. pl.","(are) the hairs"],["udyan","m. nom. sg. pres.part.","the rising (sun)"],["pūrva-ardhaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the forepart"],["nimlocan","m. nom. sg. pres.part.","the setting (sun)"],["jaghana-ardhaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the hindpart"],["yad vijṛmbhate","3 sg. pres.","when it yawns/stretches"],["tad vidyotate","3 sg. pres.","then it lightens"],["yad vidhūnute","3 sg. pres.","when it shakes itself"],["tat stanayati","3 sg. pres.","then it thunders"],["yan mehati","3 sg. pres.","when it urinates"],["tad varṣati","3 sg. pres.","then it rains"],["vāk eva asya vāk","f. nom. sg.","its neighing/voice is verily (cosmic) speech"]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. The dawn, verily, is the head of the sacrificial horse; the sun its eye, the wind its breath, its open mouth the Vaiśvānara fire; the year is the body of the sacrificial horse. The sky is its back, the mid-air its belly, the earth its under-belly; the directions its two flanks, the intermediate directions its ribs; the seasons its limbs, the months and half-months its joints, days-and-nights its feet, the stars its bones, the sky(-mist) its flesh. The half-digested food is the sand, the rivers its vessels, the liver and lungs the mountains, the herbs and trees its hairs. The rising sun is its forepart, the setting sun its hindpart. When it yawns, that is lightning; when it shakes itself, that is thunder; when it urinates, that is rain; its very voice is (cosmic) speech.\"","para":"The sacrificial horse is to be contemplated as the whole universe in animal form: every part of its body maps onto a cosmic feature, and its physiological acts map onto natural phenomena. The microcosm (the horse) and the macrocosm (the world) are one continuous body. To \"sacrifice the horse\" inwardly is to offer up the entire cosmos.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","this is <strong>adhyāropa</strong> (deliberate superimposition) for the sake of <em>upāsanā</em> — the meditator who lacks the qualification for pure <em>jñāna</em> is given the horse-as-cosmos as a vast object of meditation, so that the mind, expanded to the size of the universe, is prepared for the later teaching that the Self is larger still. The aśvamedha-meditation yields <em>krama-mukti</em>, gradual liberation."],["Sureśvara","stresses that the passage is <em>karma-aṅga</em> turned into <em>vidyā</em> — the point is not the outward rite but the <em>identification</em> (the meditator's \"I am this cosmic horse\"), which begins dismantling the small ego even before knowledge proper."],["Krishnananda","\"The Upaniṣad begins with the largest possible object so that nothing is left outside the meditation.\" Reads the horse as a <em>training in totality</em>: the mind habitually grasps fragments; here it is forced to hold the whole. The bodily-cosmic correspondences are an early <em>adhidaiva–adhyātma</em> bridge."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note that <em>aśva</em> derives from <em>√aś</em> \"to pervade/reach\"; the horse is named for the all-pervading cosmos it symbolises. Sivananda: use the mantra as a <em>vyāpti</em> (expansion) meditation at dawn."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a classic <em>bandhu</em> (ritual \"connection/homology\") text typical of the Brāhmaṇa stratum; Olivelle notes the genre links body, ritual, and cosmos in a web of equivalences (<em>nidāna</em>), and that beginning the Upaniṣad with the aśvamedha ties the <em>jñāna-kāṇḍa</em> back to its Śatapatha-Brāhmaṇa matrix."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.1.2","title":"The horse's origin in the cosmic waters","sa":["अहर्वा अश्वं पुरस्तान्महिमान्वजायत तस्य पूर्वे समुद्रे योनिः । रात्रिरेनं पश्चान्महिमान्वजायत तस्यापरे समुद्रे योनिः । एतौ वा अश्वं महिमानावभितः सम्बभूवतुः । हयो भूत्वा देवानवहद्वाजी गन्धर्वानर्वासुरान्अश्वो मनुष्यान् । समुद्र एवास्य बन्धुः समुद्रो योनिः ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["ahar vā aśvaṃ purastān mahimānvajāyata tasya pūrve samudre yoniḥ | rātrir enaṃ paścān mahimānvajāyata tasyāpare samudre yoniḥ | etau vā aśvaṃ mahimānāv abhitaḥ sambabhūvatuḥ | hayo bhūtvā devān avahad vājī gandharvān arvāsurān aśvo manuṣyān | samudra evāsya bandhuḥ samudro yoniḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["ahaḥ","n. nom. sg.","the day"],["vai","indecl.","verily"],["aśvam","m. acc. sg.","the horse"],["purastāt","indecl.","in front / before"],["mahimā","m. nom. sg.","the \"greatness\" (the mahiman sacrificial vessel; the glory)"],["anvajāyata","3 sg. impf. of anu-jan","was born along with / arose after"],["tasya","pron. m. gen. sg.","its"],["pūrve samudre","m. loc. sg.","in the eastern ocean"],["yoniḥ","f. nom. sg.","(is) the womb/source"],["rātriḥ","f. nom. sg.","the night"],["enam","pron. m. acc. sg.","it (the horse)"],["paścāt","indecl.","behind / after"],["apare samudre","m. loc. sg.","in the western ocean"],["etau","pron. m. nom. du.","these two"],["mahimānau","m. nom. du.","the two \"greatnesses\" (vessels/glories)"],["abhitaḥ","indecl.","on both sides"],["sambabhūvatuḥ","3 du. pf. of sam-bhū","came into being / flanked"],["hayaḥ bhūtvā","m. nom. sg. + ger.","becoming a haya (steed)"],["devān avahat","acc. pl. + 3 sg. impf. of vah","carried the gods"],["vājī","m. nom. sg.","as a vājin (war-horse)"],["gandharvān","m. acc. pl.","the Gandharvas"],["arvā","m. nom. sg.","as an arvan (courser)"],["asurān","m. acc. pl.","the Asuras"],["aśvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","as an aśva"],["manuṣyān","m. acc. pl.","(carried) human beings"],["samudraḥ eva","m. nom. sg.","the ocean alone"],["asya bandhuḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) its kin/abode"],["samudraḥ yoniḥ","m. nom. + f. nom.","the ocean (is) its source"]],"lit":"\"The day arose as the <em>mahiman</em> (greatness/vessel) in front of the horse; its womb is in the eastern ocean. The night arose as the <em>mahiman</em> behind it; its womb is in the western ocean. These two <em>mahimans</em> came to flank the horse on both sides. Becoming a <em>haya</em> it carried the gods, as a <em>vājin</em> the Gandharvas, as an <em>arvan</em> the Asuras, as an <em>aśva</em> human beings. The ocean alone is its kinsman; the ocean is its source.\"","para":"Day and night — the two ritual vessels called <em>mahiman</em> used in the actual aśvamedha — are reimagined as cosmic principles flanking the horse-cosmos fore and aft, each born from an ocean (the boundless source). The one cosmic horse appears under four names (<em>haya, vājin, arvan, aśva</em>) as it \"carries\" the four classes of beings (gods, Gandharvas, Asuras, humans). All return to the ocean — the undifferentiated source/abode (<em>bandhu/yoni</em>).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>mahiman</em> vessels (day &amp; night) are folded into the meditation so that <em>time itself</em> (ahorātra) becomes part of the cosmic horse. \"Ocean\" = the <em>avyākṛta</em> (unmanifest), the source and resting-place of all — a first pointer toward the one Reality from which the manifold issues and into which it returns."],["Sureśvara","the four names for the one horse model the principle that <strong>one reality bears many designations according to function</strong> — anticipating the later teaching that the one Ātman appears as many."],["Krishnananda","day/night fore-and-aft = the meditator's recognition that time <em>surrounds</em> the cosmos but the source (ocean) is timeless; the four \"carryings\" show all orders of being riding on the single cosmic life."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","<em>bandhu</em> rendered \"the (final) relative/abode\" — all beings are kin in the one source; a meditation on universal kinship dissolving the sense of the isolated individual."],["Deussen/Olivelle","notes the <em>mahiman</em> are literal sacrificial cups in the Śatapatha aśvamedha; the Upaniṣad spiritualises them. Olivelle reads \"ocean\" (<em>samudra</em>) as the cosmogonic primal waters, a recurrent Vedic image of origin."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The aśvamedha was the supreme <em>śrauta</em> rite, performable only by a victorious king; a consecrated stallion roamed free for a year (guarded by warriors), and the territory it wandered was claimed by the king. The Upaniṣad <strong>internalises</strong> this politically charged, year-long, immensely expensive rite into a <em>meditation requiring nothing but the mind</em>. This move — from outward <em>karma</em> to inner <em>upāsanā</em> — is the structural signature of the whole Upaniṣad and the reason it opens here. Three layers are deliberately stacked:","- <strong>Adhibhautika</strong> (physical): the literal horse and its anatomy.","- <strong>Adhidaivika</strong> (cosmic): dawn=head, sun=eye, year=body — the horse <em>is</em> the year-cycle and the spatial cosmos.","- <strong>Adhyātmika</strong> (inner): the meditator who holds all this in mind expands the \"I\" to cosmic size, loosening identification with the small body.","The number symbolism is exact: the horse's <em>body is the year</em> (saṃvatsara), so the temporal whole and the spatial whole (sky-back, earth-underbelly, directions-flanks) are unified in one living form. This is the Vedic <em>bandhu</em> (homology) method at its most ambitious: not metaphor but a claimed real <em>correspondence</em> binding microcosm and macrocosm."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>            THE SACRIFICIAL HORSE = THE COSMOS  (BU 1.1.1)\n\n                    [ DAWN ]  = head\n                       ||\n   rising sun ====&gt; ( SUN = eye )  ( WIND = breath )  &lt;==== setting sun\n   = forepart            \\              /                   = hindpart\n                          \\            /\n   SKY = back  ----------[  YEAR = body/trunk  ]---------- belly = mid-air\n                          /            \\\n        flanks = directions          ribs = intermediate directions\n                          \\            /\n   underbelly = EARTH ----[  legs/feet = days &amp; nights ]\n                            bones = stars   flesh = mist\n                            hairs = herbs &amp; trees\n                            vessels = rivers   mountains = liver/lungs\n\n   ACTS:  yawn -&gt; lightning | shake -&gt; thunder | urine -&gt; rain | voice -&gt; Speech\n\n         ORIGIN (1.1.2):   EASTERN OCEAN  &lt;-- DAY | HORSE | NIGHT --&gt; WESTERN OCEAN\n                                  \\________ samudra = source &amp; abode ________/</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Dawn practice (vyāpti / expansion).</strong> At first light, sit and take up the correspondences one by one — dawn as head, sun as eye, the year as the very body in which you sit. Let attention grow from the body outward until \"the cosmos\" is the felt object of meditation, not an idea.","2. <strong>From doing to being.</strong> Notice the text's core move: a rite that once cost a kingdom is here performed by attention alone. Apply the principle — any outward action you value can be interiorised as contemplation of its meaning.","3. <strong>Totality drill.</strong> Deliberately hold \"the whole\" rather than a fragment. Krishnananda's point: the mind defaults to pieces; here it is trained to include everything in one sweep. Even thirty seconds of \"nothing left outside\" is the exercise.","4. <strong>Return to the ocean.</strong> End by resting in <em>samudra</em> — the single source and abode of all the forms just contemplated. The manifold subsides into its origin; you sit as that origin.","5. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the homology (<em>bandhu</em>) is not poetry but a claim — <em>you are not in the cosmos as a part; the cosmos is your extended body.</em> Watch how the day's sense of being \"a small thing among large things\" softens."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 1 · Brāhmaṇa 1.2 — Creation out of Death (Hunger)","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 1.2","title":"Creation out of Death (Hunger)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Where 1.1 internalised the horse-rite, 1.2 supplies its</em> <strong>cosmogony</strong>: <em>how the very horse-cosmos came to be. The startling claim — there was nothing \"in the beginning\" except</em> <strong>Death</strong> <em>(mṛtyu), identified with</em> <strong>Hunger</strong> <em>(aśanāyā). From this devouring principle the worlds, time, speech, and finally the sacrificial horse itself are generated. The brāhmaṇa ends by re-deriving the</em> arka <em>(fire) and the</em> aśvamedha <em>of 1.1, closing the loop: the one who knows this conquers re-death (punar-mṛtyu).</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.1","title":"Death alone was, and worshipped","sa":["नैवेह किंचनाग्र आसीत् । मृत्युनैवेदमावृतमासीदशनायया । अशनाया हि मृत्युः । तन्मनोऽकुरुतात्मन्वी स्यामिति । सोऽर्चन्नचरत् । तस्यार्चत आपोऽजायन्त । अर्चते वै मे कमभूदिति । तदेवार्कस्यार्कत्वम् । कं ह वा अस्मै भवति य एवमेतदर्कस्यार्कत्वं वेद ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["naiveha kiṃ canāgra āsīt | mṛtyunaivedam āvṛtam āsīd aśanāyayā | aśanāyā hi mṛtyuḥ | tan mano 'kurutātmanvī syām iti | so 'rcann acarat | tasyārcata āpo 'jāyanta | arcate vai me kam abhūd iti | tad evārkasyārkatvam | kaṃ ha vā asmai bhavati ya evam etad arkasyārkatvaṃ veda || 1 ||"],"words":[["na eva iha","indecl.","not at all here"],["kiṃ cana","indef. pron.","anything whatever"],["agre","n. loc. sg. (adv.)","in the beginning"],["āsīt","3 sg. impf. of as","was"],["mṛtyunā eva","m. instr. sg.","by Death alone"],["idam","pron. n. nom. sg.","this (all)"],["āvṛtam āsīt","n. nom. sg. pp. + impf.","was covered/enveloped"],["aśanāyayā","f. instr. sg.","by hunger"],["aśanāyā hi mṛtyuḥ","f./m. nom. sg.","for hunger is death"],["tat manaḥ akuruta","n. acc. + 3 sg. impf. ātm. of kṛ","he made/created a mind"],["ātmanvī syām iti","m. nom. sg. + 1 sg. pot. + quote","\"may I have a self (be embodied)\""],["saḥ arcan acarat","m. nom. + pres.part. + 3 sg. impf. of car","he moved about worshipping (arcat = praising)"],["tasya arcataḥ","m. gen. sg. pres.part.","of him while worshipping"],["āpaḥ ajāyanta","f. nom. pl. + 3 pl. impf. of jan","the waters were born"],["arcate vai me kam abhūt iti","dat. + indecl. + gen. + n.nom. + impf. + quote","\"while I worshipped, ka (water/bliss) came to be for me\""],["tat eva arkasya arka-tvam","n. nom. sg.","that very (is) the arka-hood of arka (the fire's fire-nature)"],["kam ha vai asmai bhavati","n. nom. + indecl. + dat. + pres.","ka (joy/water) verily comes to be for him"],["yaḥ evam etat arkasya arka-tvaṃ veda","m. nom. + 3 sg. pf. of vid","who thus knows the arka-hood of arka"]],"lit":"\"There was nothing whatever here in the beginning; this (universe) was enveloped only by Death — by Hunger, for Hunger is Death. He made up a mind, (thinking) 'May I come to have a self.' He moved about worshipping (arcat). From him, while he worshipped, the waters were born. (He thought) 'While I worshipped, <em>ka</em> (water/joy) came to be for me' — that is the <em>arka</em>-hood of the <em>arka</em> (fire). Joy (<em>ka</em>) verily comes to be for the one who thus knows the <em>arka</em>-hood of the <em>arka</em>.\"","para":"Before anything, only Death-as-Hunger existed — the primal lack, the will-to-consume that presupposes nothing yet outside it. It conceives a <em>mind</em> and a wish to become embodied; its first act of <em>worship/effort</em> (arcat) produces the cosmic <strong>waters</strong>. The wordplay binds <em>arcat</em> (\"worshipped\") + <em>ka</em> (\"water,\" also \"joy\") → <strong>arka</strong>, the fire/sun that is the first product. To know this etymological-cosmic secret is itself to gain <em>ka</em> (joy).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"Death\" here is not non-being but <em>Hiraṇyagarbha / the unmanifest seed-state</em> viewed under the aspect of <em>aśanāyā</em> (hunger = the appetite that drives manifestation). There was no <em>manifest</em> thing (<em>kiṃ cana</em>), but the cause existed. The \"mind he made\" is the cosmic <em>antaḥkaraṇa</em> through which the unmanifest begins to will. The whole passage is <em>upāsanā</em>, not the final non-dual truth."],["Sureśvara","underscores that <em>aśanāyā hi mṛtyuḥ</em> equates desire (hunger) with mortality itself — <strong>wherever there is lack/appetite, there is death</strong>. The liberative reversal (later) is to be the <em>eater of all</em> rather than the hungry one."],["Krishnananda","a profound psychological reading — creation begins as <em>want</em>. The cosmos is the objectification of a primordial hunger; every creature inherits this lack and so \"death\" is woven into manifestation from the first. Meditation aims to reach the <em>fullness</em> (pūrṇa) that has no hunger."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note <em>ka</em> = \"water\" and \"happiness/Prajāpati\"; the pun teaches that the knower of the symbol gains the <em>ka</em> (bliss) it denotes — <em>vidyā</em> yields its own fruit."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a paradigmatic <em>creatio</em> from a single principle; Olivelle compares the \"nothing was here\" opening to the Nāsadīya hymn (RV 10.129) and Chāndogya 6.2 (\"sad eva… ekam\"), noting BU's bolder choice of <em>Death</em> (not Being) as the starting point."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.2","title":"Waters, earth, and the birth of fire","sa":["आपो वा अर्कः । तद्यदपां शर आसीत्तत्समहन्यत । सा पृथिव्यभवत् । तस्यामश्राम्यत् । तस्य श्रान्तस्य तप्तस्य तेजोरसो निरवर्तताग्निः ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["āpo vā arkaḥ | tad yad apāṃ śara āsīt tat samahanyata | sā pṛthivy abhavat | tasyām aśrāmyat | tasya śrāntasya taptasya tejoraso niravartatāgniḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["āpaḥ vai arkaḥ","f. nom. pl. + m. nom.","the waters, verily, are the arka"],["tat yat apāṃ śaraḥ āsīt","rel. + f. gen. pl. + m. nom. + impf.","that which was the froth/scum of the waters"],["tat samahanyata","3 sg. impf. pass. of sam-han","that was compacted/solidified"],["sā pṛthivī abhavat","f. nom. + impf.","that became the earth"],["tasyām aśrāmyat","f. loc. + 3 sg. impf. of śram","on it he toiled (exerted himself)"],["tasya śrāntasya taptasya","m. gen. sg. pp.","of him, exhausted and heated"],["tejaḥ-rasaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the essence-of-heat / fiery-sap"],["niravartata","3 sg. impf. of nir-vṛt","issued forth"],["agniḥ","m. nom. sg.","(as) fire"]],"lit":"\"The waters, verily, are the <em>arka</em>. That which was the froth of the waters was compacted; it became the earth. On it he toiled. Of him, exhausted and heated, the fiery essence issued forth as Fire (Agni).\"","para":"The waters (first product) <em>are</em> the arka. Their froth congeals into <strong>earth</strong> — solid ground. Upon the earth the creative principle <em>labours</em> (the cosmogonic \"toil/heat,\" <em>śrama/tapas</em>), and from that exhaustion-heat the <strong>fire</strong> is distilled out. So the sequence is: Death → mind → waters → earth → fire, by progressive condensation and heating.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the \"froth → earth\" models <em>coagulation</em> of the subtle into the gross; <em>aśrāmyat/tapas</em> is the standard Vedic motif of creation by ardour. Agni emerging as the <em>tejorasa</em> is the third element, completing the basis for the threefold prāṇa of the next mantra."],["Sureśvara","\"the waters are arka\" links back to v.1's pun — what was praised-into-being (water) <em>is</em> the worship-fire; product and act of production are non-different at the cosmic level."],["Krishnananda","reads the condensation as the descent from subtle to gross that every meditator must <em>reverse</em>; cosmology run backwards is yoga."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","compares \"froth hardening into earth\" to Manu and Purāṇic churning-imagery; a teaching that the solid world is \"set\" from a fluid origin and is therefore not ultimately solid."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a classic elemental genealogy (water→earth→fire); Olivelle notes the recurrence of <em>tapas/śrama</em> as the engine of creation across the early Upaniṣads."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.3","title":"The threefold breath and its cosmic body","sa":["स त्रेधाऽऽत्मानं व्यकुरुतादित्यं तृतीयं वायुं तृतीयम् । स एष प्राणस्त्रेधाविहितः । तस्य प्राची दिक्शिरोऽसौ चासौ चेर्मौ । अथास्य प्रतीची दिक्पुच्छमसौ चासौ च सक्थ्यौ । दक्षिणा चोदीची च पार्श्वे । द्यौः पृष्ठमन्तरिक्षमुदरमियमुरः । स एषोऽप्सु प्रतिष्ठितः । यत्र क्व चैति तदेव प्रतितिष्ठत्येवं विद्वान् ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["sa tredhātmānaṃ vyakurutādityaṃ tṛtīyaṃ vāyuṃ tṛtīyam | sa eṣa prāṇas tredhāvihitaḥ | tasya prācī dik śiro 'sau cāsau cermau | athāsya pratīcī dik puccham asau cāsau ca sakthyau | dakṣiṇā codīcī ca pārśve | dyauḥ pṛṣṭham antarikṣam udaram iyam uraḥ | sa eṣo 'psu pratiṣṭhitaḥ | yatra kva caiti tad eva pratitiṣṭhaty evaṃ vidvān || 3 ||"],"words":[["saḥ tredhā ātmānaṃ vyakuruta","m. nom. + adv. + acc. + 3 sg. impf. of vi-kṛ","he divided himself threefold"],["ādityaṃ tṛtīyaṃ vāyuṃ tṛtīyam","acc. sg.","the sun (as) a third, the wind (as) a third (— fire the remaining third)"],["saḥ eṣaḥ prāṇaḥ tredhā-vihitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","this same breath (life) is arranged threefold"],["tasya prācī dik śiraḥ","f. nom. + n. nom.","the eastern direction is its head"],["asau ca asau ca īrmau","pron. + m. nom. du.","those two (NE/SE) are its (fore-)arms"],["atha asya pratīcī dik puccham","f. nom. + n. nom.","then the western direction is its tail"],["asau ca asau ca sakthyau","pron. + n. nom. du.","those two (NW/SW) are its haunches"],["dakṣiṇā ca udīcī ca pārśve","f. nom. + n. nom. du.","south and north are its two flanks"],["dyauḥ pṛṣṭham","f./n. nom.","the sky is its back"],["antarikṣam udaram","n. nom.","the mid-region its belly"],["iyam uraḥ","f. nom. (=earth) + n. nom.","this (earth) is its chest"],["saḥ eṣaḥ apsu pratiṣṭhitaḥ","m. nom. + f. loc. pl. + pp.","he is established in the waters"],["yatra kva ca eti","rel. adv. + 3 sg. pres. of i","wherever he goes"],["tat eva pratitiṣṭhati evaṃ vidvān","+ 3 sg. pres. + m. nom.","there he gains a footing — (so does) the one who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"He divided himself threefold — (making) the sun a third, the wind a third (and fire the third). This same Breath (prāṇa) is arranged threefold. Its head is the eastern quarter; those two (intermediate quarters) are its forelegs; its tail is the western quarter; those two are its haunches; south and north are its two flanks; the sky is its back, the mid-region its belly, this earth its chest. He stands firm in the waters. Wherever he goes, there he finds a footing — and so does the one who knows this.\"","para":"The single fiery life-principle distributes itself into the <strong>triad fire–sun–wind</strong> (the three Vedic loci of Agni: on earth, in heaven, in the atmosphere) — the cosmic <em>prāṇa</em> in three positions. This prāṇa is then given a <em>bird-/horse-shaped body</em> (the shape of the Vedic fire-altar): the quarters of space are its head, limbs, tail, and flanks; the three worlds its back, belly, chest. Resting on the cosmic waters, it is everywhere stable. The knower shares that omnipresent stability.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the threefold prāṇa = Agni-Āditya-Vāyu is the <em>adhidaiva</em> form of the one life-force; the spatial body is the <em>agni-kṣetra</em> (fire-altar) homology. \"Finds a footing wherever he goes\" = the meditator on the all-pervading prāṇa is never without support — a fruit (<em>phala</em>) of the <em>upāsanā</em>."],["Sureśvara","the self \"dividing itself\" without ceasing to be one prefigures the central Advaita point: apparent multiplicity in an undivided reality (<em>ekam evādvitīyam</em> in cosmogonic dress)."],["Krishnananda","the directions-as-body is the cosmic <em>Virāj</em>; meditating on space itself as one's body dissolves the boundary between \"here\" (the meditator) and \"there\" (the world)."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","connect the bird-shaped altar to the literal <em>agnicayana</em>; the inner practice is to \"build the fire-altar\" as one's own expanded body."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the triad Agni/Sūrya/Vāyu as the three \"stations\" of fire — a stock Vedic schema (terrestrial, celestial, atmospheric); Olivelle reads the spatial body as the cosmic giant (Puruṣa/Virāj) common to RV 10.90."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.4","title":"The year, and the birth of speech","sa":["सोऽकामयत — द्वितीयो म आत्मा जायेतेति । स मनसा वाचं मिथुनं समभवदशनाया मृत्युः । तद्यद्रेत आसीत्स संवत्सरोऽभवत् । न ह पुरा ततः संवत्सर आस । तमेतावन्तं कालमबिभर्यावान्त्संवत्सरः । तमेतावतः कालस्य परस्तादसृजत । तं जातमभिव्याददात् । स भाण् अकरोत् । सैव वागभवत् ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["so 'kāmayata -- dvitīyo ma ātmā jāyeteti | sa manasā vācaṃ mithunaṃ samabhavad aśanāyā mṛtyuḥ | tad yad reta āsīt sa saṃvatsaro 'bhavat | na ha purā tataḥ saṃvatsara āsa | tam etāvantaṃ kālam abibhar yāvānt saṃvatsaraḥ | tam etāvataḥ kālasya parastād asṛjata | taṃ jātam abhivyādadāt | sa bhāṇ akarot | saiva vāg abhavat || 4 ||"],"words":[["saḥ akāmayata","m. nom. + 3 sg. impf. ātm. of kam","he desired"],["dvitīyaḥ me ātmā jāyeta iti","m. nom. + dat./gen. + opt. + quote","\"may a second self be born to me\""],["saḥ manasā vācaṃ mithunaṃ samabhavat","instr. + acc. + acc. + 3 sg. impf. of sam-bhū","he, by mind, coupled (sexually united) with speech"],["aśanāyā mṛtyuḥ","f./m. nom.","(this) Hunger, Death"],["tat yat retaḥ āsīt","rel. + n. nom. + impf.","that which was the seed"],["saḥ saṃvatsaraḥ abhavat","m. nom. + impf.","became the year"],["na ha purā tataḥ saṃvatsaraḥ āsa","+ indecl. + abl. + pf. of as","before that there had been no year"],["tam etāvantaṃ kālam abibhaḥ","acc. + acc. + 3 sg. impf. of bhṛ","he bore it for that length of time"],["yāvān saṃvatsaraḥ","rel. m. nom.","as long as a year (is)"],["tam etāvataḥ kālasya parastāt asṛjata","acc. + gen. + indecl. + impf. of sṛj","after that length of time he sent it (gave birth)"],["taṃ jātam abhivyādadāt","acc. pp. + 3 sg. impf. of abhi-vi-ā-dā","he opened wide (his mouth) at it, (just) born, (to devour it)"],["saḥ bhāṇ akarot","m. nom. + onomat. + impf. of kṛ","it made (the sound) \"bhāṇ!\""],["sā eva vāk abhavat","f. nom. + f. nom. + impf.","that very (sound) became Speech"]],"lit":"\"He desired, 'May a second self be born to me.' He — Hunger, Death — by the mind united with speech as with a mate. The seed that there was became the year; before that there was no year. He bore it for as long as a year; after that length of time he brought it forth. As it was born he opened his mouth wide upon it (to swallow it). It cried 'bhāṇ!' — and that very (cry) became Speech.\"","para":"The creator-Death now wills a <strong>second self</strong> (offspring). Mind unites with Speech as a procreative pair; their \"seed\" gestates into <strong>the Year</strong> — time itself is born here, the first interval. Death then moves to <em>devour</em> its own newborn offspring (hunger again!), which cries out; that cry is the origin of articulate <strong>speech/sound</strong>. Time and language are thus co-born from the union of mind and voice.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>mithuna</em> (pairing) of <em>manas</em> and <em>vāc</em> is the cosmic prototype of all generation — thought wedded to expression. \"The year\" = the principle of measured time (Prajāpati-as-saṃvatsara). The attempt to devour the offspring shows that the very <em>kāla</em> (time) that creation produces also threatens to consume it: time is Death's instrument."],["Sureśvara","mind+speech as the generative couple anticipates the epistemic point that all known objects arise within the <em>nāma</em> (speech/name)–<em>manas</em> (conception) matrix; reality \"spoken into being.\""],["Krishnananda","a striking meditation on <em>time as the firstborn of desire</em>; because creation springs from a wish for a \"second,\" duality and temporality are inseparable. The cry \"bhāṇ\" is the universe's first utterance — sound (śabda) as the subtlest manifestation."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note \"bhāṇ\" as the primal <em>anāhata</em>-like sound; Sivananda links it to the doctrine that all speech is a differentiation of one original sound (cf. <em>Oṃ</em>)."],["Deussen/Olivelle","read the mind–speech <em>mithuna</em> as a recurrent Upaniṣadic pair (cf. BU 1.4.17, 1.5.3); Olivelle highlights the birth of the <em>year</em> as the introduction of the ritual calendar into the cosmogony."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.5","title":"He creates and resolves to eat all (Aditi)","sa":["स ऐक्षत यदि वा इममभिमंस्ये कनीयोऽन्नं करिष्य इति । स तया वाचा तेनात्मनेदं सर्वमसृजत यदिदं किंच — ऋचो यजूंषि सामानि छन्दांसि यज्ञान्प्रजां पशून् । स यद्यदेवासृजत तत्तदत्तुमध्रियत । सर्वं वा अत्तीति तददितेरदितित्वम् । सर्वस्यात्ता भवति सर्वमस्यान्नं भवति य एवमेतददितेरदितित्वं वेद ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["sa aikṣata yadi vā imam abhimaṃsye kanīyo 'nnaṃ kariṣya iti | sa tayā vācā tenātmanedaṃ sarvam asṛjata yad idaṃ kiñca -- ṛco yajūṃṣi sāmāni chandāṃsi yajñān prajāṃ paśūn | sa yad-yad evāsṛjata tattad attum adhriyata | sarvaṃ vā attīti tad aditer adititvam | sarvasyāttā bhavati sarvam asyānnaṃ bhavati ya evam etad aditer adititvaṃ veda || 5 ||"],"words":[["saḥ aikṣata","m. nom. + 3 sg. impf. of īkṣ","he reflected"],["yadi vā imam abhimaṃsye","+ acc. + 1 sg. fut. of abhi-man","\"if I (now) kill/devour this one\""],["kanīyaḥ annaṃ kariṣye iti","n. compar. + acc. + 1 sg. fut. of kṛ + quote","\"I shall make food the less\""],["saḥ tayā vācā tena ātmanā","instr. sg.","he, by that speech and that self"],["idaṃ sarvam asṛjata","n. acc. + impf. of sṛj","created this all"],["yat idaṃ kiṃ ca","rel.","whatever there is here"],["ṛcaḥ yajūṃṣi sāmāni chandāṃsi","n./m. acc. pl.","the Ṛc, Yajus, Sāman (verses), the meters"],["yajñān prajāṃ paśūn","acc. pl.","sacrifices, offspring, animals"],["saḥ yat-yat eva asṛjata","+ impf.","whatever, indeed, he created"],["tat-tat attum adhriyata","acc. + inf. of ad + 3 sg. impf. of dhṛ","that very (thing) he set about to eat"],["sarvaṃ vai atti iti","n. acc. + 3 sg. pres. of ad","\"he eats everything\" —"],["tat aditeḥ aditi-tvam","n. nom. + f. gen.","that is the aditi-hood of Aditi (the eat-all / boundless one)"],["sarvasya attā bhavati","gen. + m. nom. agent + pres.","he becomes the eater of all"],["sarvam asya annaṃ bhavati","n. nom. + gen.","everything becomes his food"],["yaḥ evam etat aditeḥ aditi-tvaṃ veda","+ pf. of vid","who thus knows the aditi-hood of Aditi"]],"lit":"\"He reflected: 'If I kill (devour) this one, I shall make food the less.' With that speech and that self he created all this, whatever there is — the Ṛc, Yajus and Sāman verses, the meters, the sacrifices, offspring, and animals. And whatever he created, that he set about to eat. Because he eats everything (<em>sarvam atti</em>), that is the '<em>aditi</em>-hood' of Aditi. He who knows thus the <em>aditi</em>-hood of Aditi becomes the eater of all; everything becomes his food.\"","para":"Rather than consume his single offspring, Death reasons that more eating requires more to eat — so he <em>creates the entire ordered world</em> (the three Vedas, meters, rites, beings) precisely <strong>as food</strong>. Everything that exists is generated to be devoured by Death/Time. The etymological key: <em>Aditi</em> (a name of the boundless mother-goddess) is glossed as \"she who eats all\" (<em>ad-</em>, \"to eat\"). The knower reverses the polarity: instead of being food <em>for</em> Death, he becomes the <strong>eater of all</strong> — the consuming subject, never the consumed object.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the deep teaching under the grim image — all manifest things are <em>anna</em> (food) for Time/Death; saṃsāra is a vast eating. Liberation is to identify with the <em>attṛ</em> (eater, the conscious subject) rather than the <em>anna</em> (eaten, the object-body). The <em>vidyā</em> converts one from prey into the all-consuming Self."],["Sureśvara","<em>aditer adititvam</em> — the pun teaches non-finitude: to be the \"eater of all\" is to have nothing left outside oneself, i.e., to be infinite (cf. the <em>pūrṇa</em> of the śānti-pāṭha)."],["Krishnananda","\"The whole universe is a process of mutual eating\" — a famous line; every being feeds on others and is fed upon. To know oneself as the cosmic eater is to step out of the food-chain of mortality."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical inversion — the meditator who realises \"I am the experiencer of all, not a perishable item within experience\" is freed from being \"consumed\" by objects and time."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the creation of the <em>three Vedas + meters</em> as the canonical \"everything\"; Olivelle reads the Aditi etymology as a typical Upaniṣadic <em>nirvacana</em> (creative etymology) and links the \"all is food\" theme to TaittU 2–3 (<em>annaṃ brahmeti</em>)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.6","title":"A greater sacrifice; the body swells","sa":["सोऽकामयत — भूयसा यज्ञेन भूयो यजेयेति । सोऽश्राम्यत् । स तपोऽतप्यत । तस्य श्रान्तस्य तप्तस्य यशो वीर्यमुदक्रामत् । प्राणा वै यशो वीर्यम् । तत्प्राणेषूत्क्रान्तेषु शरीरं श्वयितुमध्रियत । तस्य शरीर एव मन आसीत् ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["so 'kāmayata -- bhūyasā yajñena bhūyo yajeyeti | so 'śrāmyat | sa tapo 'tapyata | tasya śrāntasya taptasya yaśo vīryam udakrāmat | prāṇā vai yaśo vīryam | tat prāṇeṣūtkrānteṣu śarīraṃ śvayitum adhriyata | tasya śarīra eva mana āsīt || 6 ||"],"words":[["saḥ akāmayata","+ impf.","he desired"],["bhūyasā yajñena bhūyaḥ yajeya iti","instr. + adv. + 1 sg. opt. of yaj + quote","\"with a greater sacrifice may I sacrifice more\""],["saḥ aśrāmyat","+ impf. of śram","he toiled"],["saḥ tapaḥ atapyata","n. acc. + 3 sg. impf. of tap","he practised austerity (heated himself)"],["tasya śrāntasya taptasya","m. gen. pp.","of him, exhausted and heated"],["yaśaḥ vīryam udakrāmat","n. nom. + n. nom. + 3 sg. impf. of ud-kram","(his) glory and vigour went out (upward)"],["prāṇāḥ vai yaśaḥ vīryam","m. nom. pl. + n. nom.","the vital breaths verily are glory and vigour"],["tat prāṇeṣu utkrānteṣu","loc. abs.","when the breaths had gone out"],["śarīraṃ śvayitum adhriyata","n. acc. + inf. of śvi + impf. of dhṛ","the body began to swell"],["tasya śarīre eva manaḥ āsīt","m. gen. + n. loc. + n. nom. + impf.","his mind was still in the body"]],"lit":"\"He desired, 'With a greater sacrifice let me sacrifice still more.' He toiled; he heated himself with austerity. From him, exhausted and heated, his glory and vigour departed. Now the vital breaths (prāṇas) are (this) glory and vigour. When the breaths had gone out, his body began to swell; yet his mind was (still) in the body.\"","para":"Wanting an even greater rite, the creator exerts himself in <em>tapas</em> until his <strong>life-breaths</strong> (here equated with \"glory and vigour\") leave him — a kind of cosmic death. The breath-emptied <strong>body swells</strong> (<em>śvi-</em>, \"to swell\"). Crucially, <em>mind</em> remained with the body — setting up the pun in the next mantra.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the departure of the prāṇas (= yaśas + vīrya) and the swelling body literally stage Prajāpati's self-exhaustion in creation. The retention of <em>mind</em> in the swollen body is the bridge to the <em>aśva</em> etymology; nothing is wasted — the spent body becomes the next sacrificial material."],["Sureśvara","the identification <em>prāṇāḥ = yaśo-vīryam</em> teaches that vitality, not flesh, is the true \"glory\"; what makes the body radiant is breath/consciousness, which is exactly what departs at death."],["Krishnananda","even the Creator \"dies\" through the very act of creating — output depletes the source. A meditation on how all giving/expending is a partial death, and how mind (awareness) outlasts the depletion of vitality."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical note on <em>tapas</em> — intense effort spends the prāṇa; hence yogic practice must <em>conserve and redirect</em> (ūrdhva) the very vīrya that here is shown departing."],["Deussen/Olivelle","read this as the well-known Prajāpati-emptied-by-creation motif of the Brāhmaṇas (Prajāpati <em>visrasta</em>, \"fallen apart\"); the swelling corpse is the etymological seed for <em>aśva</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2.7","title":"The swollen body becomes the Horse; arka and aśvamedha unite; conquest of re-death","sa":["सोऽकामयत — मेध्यं म इदं स्यादात्मन्व्यनेन स्यामिति । ततोऽश्वः समभवत् । यदश्वत्तन्मेध्यमभूदिति तदेवाश्वमेधस्याश्वमेधत्वम् । एष ह वा अश्वमेधं वेद य एनमेवं वेद । तमनवरुध्यैवामन्यत । तं संवत्सरस्य परस्तादात्मन आलभत । पशून्देवताभ्यः प्रत्यौहत् । तस्मात्सर्वदेवत्यं प्रोक्षितं प्राजापत्यमालभन्ते । एष वा अश्वमेधो य एष तपति । तस्य संवत्सर आत्मा । अयमग्निरर्कः । तस्येमे लोका आत्मानः । तावेतावर्काश्वमेधौ । स पुनरेकैव देवता भवति मृत्युरेव । अप पुनर्मृत्युं जयति । नैनं मृत्युराप्नोति । मृत्युरस्यात्मा भवति । सर्वमायुरेति । एतासां देवतानामेको भवति ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["so 'kāmayata -- medhyaṃ ma idaṃ syād ātmanvy anena syām iti | tato 'śvaḥ samabhavat | yad aśvat tan medhyam abhūd iti tad evāśvamedhasyāśvamedhatvam | eṣa ha vā aśvamedhaṃ veda ya enam evaṃ veda | tam anavarudhyaivāmanyata | taṃ saṃvatsarasya parastād ātmana ālabhata | paśūn devatābhyaḥ pratyauhat | tasmāt sarvadevatyaṃ prokṣitaṃ prājāpatyam ālabhante | eṣa vā aśvamedho ya eṣa tapati | tasya saṃvatsara ātmā | ayam agnir arkaḥ | tasyeme lokā ātmānaḥ | tāv etāv arkāśvamedhau | sa punar ekaiva devatā bhavati mṛtyur eva | apa punarmṛtyuṃ jayati | nainaṃ mṛtyur āpnoti | mṛtyur asyātmā bhavati | sarvam āyur eti | etāsāṃ devatānām eko bhavati || 7 ||"],"words":[["saḥ akāmayata","+ impf.","he desired"],["medhyaṃ me idaṃ syāt","n. nom. + opt. of as","\"may this (body) of mine become fit for sacrifice (medhya)\""],["ātmanvī anena syām iti","m. nom. + instr. + opt. + quote","\"may I be embodied (have a self) through it\""],["tataḥ aśvaḥ samabhavat","abl. + m. nom. + impf.","thereupon it became the horse (aśva)"],["yat aśvat tat medhyam abhūt iti","rel. 3 sg. impf. of śvi + n. nom. + impf.","\"because it swelled (aśvat) it became medhya\""],["tat eva aśvamedhasya aśvamedha-tvam","n. nom.","that very (is) the aśvamedha-hood of the aśvamedha"],["eṣaḥ ha vai aśvamedhaṃ veda","m. nom. + pf. of vid","he indeed knows the aśvamedha"],["yaḥ enam evaṃ veda","rel. + acc. + pf.","who knows it thus"],["tam anavarudhya eva amanyata","acc. + ger. of ava-rudh + impf. of man","(he) thought of it as simply not-confined / let it loose, reflecting"],["taṃ saṃvatsarasya parastāt ātmane ālabhata","acc. + gen. + indecl. + dat. + 3 sg. impf. of ā-labh","after a year he offered/seized it for himself"],["paśūn devatābhyaḥ pratyauhat","acc. pl. + dat. pl. + 3 sg. impf. of prati-vah","he assigned the (other) animals to the deities"],["tasmāt sarvadevatyaṃ prokṣitaṃ prājāpatyam ālabhante","abl. + acc. + pres.","therefore they offer the consecrated (horse), sacred-to-all-gods, belonging-to-Prajāpati"],["eṣaḥ vai aśvamedhaḥ yaḥ eṣaḥ tapati","m. nom. + rel. + 3 sg. pres. of tap","the aśvamedha is verily that (sun) which gives heat"],["tasya saṃvatsaraḥ ātmā","m. gen. + nom.","the year is its body"],["ayam agniḥ arkaḥ","m. nom.","this fire is the arka"],["tasya ime lokāḥ ātmānaḥ","gen. + m. nom. pl.","these worlds are its bodies"],["tau etau arka-aśvamedhau","m. nom. du.","these two are arka and aśvamedha"],["sa punar ekā eva devatā bhavati mṛtyuḥ eva","+ f. nom. + m. nom.","(yet) they become again one single deity — Death alone"],["apa punar-mṛtyuṃ jayati","pre-verb + acc. + 3 sg. pres. of ji","he conquers re-death (dying-again)"],["na enaṃ mṛtyuḥ āpnoti","+ acc. + nom. + pres. of āp","death does not reach him"],["mṛtyuḥ asya ātmā bhavati","gen. + nom.","death becomes his (own) self"],["sarvam āyuḥ eti","n. acc. + 3 sg. pres. of i","he attains the full (life-)span"],["etāsām devatānām ekaḥ bhavati","f. gen. pl. + m. nom.","he becomes one (with/among) these deities"]],"lit":"\"He desired, 'May this (body) of mine become fit for sacrifice; may I become embodied through it.' Thereupon it became the <strong>horse</strong> (<em>aśva</em>); because it had <strong>swelled</strong> (<em>aśvat</em>) it became <em>medhya</em> (fit for sacrifice) — that is the <em>aśvamedha</em>-hood of the aśvamedha. He who knows it thus truly knows the aśvamedha. (Prajāpati) let it range free, reflecting; after a year he offered it to himself, and assigned the (other) victims to the deities. Therefore (priests) offer to Prajāpati the consecrated (horse) that is sacred to all the gods. Verily the aśvamedha is that (sun) which gives heat; the year is its body; this fire is the <em>arka</em>; these worlds are its bodies. These two — <em>arka</em> and <em>aśvamedha</em> — are (in truth) but one deity again: Death alone. (The knower) conquers re-death; death does not reach him; death becomes his own Self; he attains the full life-span and becomes one (with) these deities.\"","para":"The swollen, breath-emptied body, wished into sacrificial fitness, becomes the <strong>Horse</strong> — and the double pun (<em>aśvat</em> \"swelled\" + <em>medhya</em> \"fit-for-offering\" → <em>aśvamedha</em>) finally explains the rite that opened 1.1. The text re-identifies the two great symbols: <strong>arka</strong> (the fire 1.2.1–2) and <strong>aśvamedha</strong> (the horse) are at bottom <strong>one deity, Death/Time</strong>. The pay-off is liberation language: the knower <strong>conquers <em>punar-mṛtyu</em> (re-death)</strong> — repeated dying in saṃsāra — because <em>death becomes his own Self</em>: he is no longer death's victim but identical with the principle, attaining the full span and unity with the cosmic powers.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the climactic <em>phala-śruti</em>. Through this <em>upāsanā</em> the knower transcends <em>punar-mṛtyu</em> (the Brāhmaṇical fear of dying repeatedly even in heavenly worlds). \"Death becomes his Self\" = he identifies with the all-comprehending principle rather than the perishable individual; this is <em>krama-mukti</em> via the highest meditation, just short of the direct <em>jñāna</em> of 1.4. Note the explicit warning the tradition draws: this is still <em>saguṇa upāsanā</em>."],["Sureśvara","the reduction of <em>arka + aśvamedha</em> to \"one deity, Death\" exhibits the method of the whole text — apparent dualities collapsed into a single principle, training the student toward the final <em>advaita</em>."],["Krishnananda","\"To conquer death is not to avoid dying but to <em>become</em> that which death itself is.\" The knower stops being an object in time and becomes the timeless eater (cf. 1.2.5). A profound reframing of immortality as identity, not survival."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","stress <em>punar-mṛtyu-jaya</em> — the goal is freedom from the <em>cycle</em> of redeath, i.e., rebirth; the meditation on the sun/year/fire as one's own expanded self is the means."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the historical importance of <em>punar-mṛtyu</em> — an early form of the rebirth doctrine before full <em>karma-saṃsāra</em>; Olivelle reads 1.2 as a self-contained Brāhmaṇa-style cosmogony spliced onto the horse-meditation of 1.1 to give it an origin-story."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This brāhmaṇa is the Upaniṣad's <strong>first full cosmogony</strong>, and it is deliberately dark: the origin of everything is <strong>Death-as-Hunger</strong> (<em>aśanāyā hi mṛtyuḥ</em>). The logic is rigorous —","1. <strong>Lack precedes being.</strong> Creation starts not from plenitude but from <em>appetite</em> (hunger), the structural void that wants to fill itself. Every created thing therefore carries death (lack) in its constitution.","2. <strong>Creation as a food-economy.</strong> In 1.2.5 the world is made expressly <em>as food for the eater</em>. This is the Upaniṣad's unflinching account of <em>saṃsāra</em>: a universal process of mutual consumption (the <em>anna/attṛ</em>, food/eater, polarity).","3. <strong>The liberative inversion.</strong> The repeated <em>phala-śrutis</em> turn the horror into a path: know the symbol and you move from the <em>eaten</em> (object, prey, mortal) to the <em>eater</em> (subject, the boundless Aditi, Death itself) — and so <strong>conquer re-death</strong>.","4. <strong>The etymological engine.</strong> The whole chapter advances by <em>nirvacana</em> (sacred etymology): <em>arcat+ka→arka</em>; <em>ad→aditi</em>; <em>aśvat+medhya→aśvamedha</em>. For the Vedic mind these are not idle puns but disclosures of a thing's hidden essence (the word <em>is</em> the reality).","5. <strong>Closing the ring with 1.1.</strong> By identifying the sun = aśvamedha, year = its body, fire = arka, worlds = its bodies, and finally <em>arka = aśvamedha = one Death</em>, the brāhmaṇa gives the horse-meditation of 1.1 both an origin and a unifying metaphysics."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   COSMOGONY FROM DEATH (BU 1.2)              ETYMOLOGY ENGINE (nirvacana)\n   ============================               ============================\n   DEATH = HUNGER (aśanāyā)                    arcat (&quot;worshipped&quot;) + ka (&quot;water/joy&quot;)\n        |  makes a MIND, wishes &quot;to be&quot;             -&gt; ARKA   (the fire/sun)\n        v\n   WORSHIP (arcat) --&gt; WATERS (āpaḥ = arka)    ad- (&quot;to eat&quot;) -&gt; ADITI\n        |  froth hardens                            -&gt; &quot;the eater of all&quot;\n        v\n   EARTH  --(toil/tapas)--&gt;  FIRE (Agni)       aśvat (&quot;swelled&quot;) + medhya (&quot;fit to offer&quot;)\n        |                                           -&gt; AŚVAMEDHA  (the horse-rite)\n        v\n   ONE divides THREEFOLD:  FIRE | SUN | WIND   ----------------------------------------\n        |  (= prāṇa, body = the directions)    THE GREAT IDENTITY (1.2.7):\n        v\n   DESIRE &quot;a second self&quot;:  MIND (x) SPEECH       SUN = aśvamedha,  year = its body\n        |   seed = THE YEAR (time is born)         FIRE = arka,      worlds = its bodies\n        v   newborn cries &quot;bhāṇ&quot; = SPEECH                 \\                 /\n   CREATES ALL &quot;as food&quot;:                                  \\               /\n   Vedas, meters, rites, beings  --&gt; to be EATEN            ====&gt; ONE DEITY = DEATH\n        |                                                          |\n        v   tapas -&gt; breath leaves -&gt; body SWELLS                  v\n   SWOLLEN BODY -&gt; HORSE (aśva) -&gt; AŚVAMEDHA          KNOWER: conquers RE-DEATH,\n                                                      &quot;death becomes his own Self&quot;</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Trace your own hunger.</strong> Sit and watch the subtlest layer of wanting — not a particular desire, but the bare <em>appetite</em> underneath all desires. The text calls this Death. Simply seeing it as the engine of the restless mind is the first move.","2. <strong>The eater / eaten meditation.</strong> Through any experience, ask: <em>Am I identifying as the object that time consumes (the body, the role, the mood), or as the witnessing eater that consumes all experience?</em> Practice resting as the <em>attṛ</em> (subject), per 1.2.5.","3. <strong>Reverse the cosmology.</strong> Krishnananda's instruction: run the sequence backwards in contemplation — beings → fire → earth → water → mind → the one source — to loosen the sense that the gross world is final.","4. <strong>Conserve vīrya.</strong> 1.2.6 shows vitality <em>departing</em> through unbridled expenditure (tapas without measure). Observe where your prāṇa drains (agitation, over-output) and let it gather.","5. <strong>Reframe immortality.</strong> Note the radical definition in 1.2.7: not \"I will not die,\" but \"death becomes my Self.\" Carry the question: <em>what in me is the very principle within which dying happens, and so cannot itself die?</em>","6. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the universe presented here is a single act of consuming. Freedom is not exiting the process but recognising yourself as its conscious ground — the <em>pūrṇa</em> of the peace-chant that nothing can diminish."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 1 · Brāhmaṇa 1.3 — The Udgītha: the Breath that Carries Past Death","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 1.3","title":"The Udgītha: the Breath that Carries Past Death","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The longest brāhmaṇa of Adhyāya 1 and one of the most famous in all the Upaniṣads. It tells the</em> <strong>myth of the gods and demons</strong> <em>(devas and asuras), both children of Prajāpati, who compete by means of the</em> <strong>udgītha</strong> <em>(the loud sung portion of the Sāma-Veda chant). The gods try to chant through each sense-organ in turn — speech, smell-breath, eye, ear, mind — and each is \"pierced by evil\" (pāpman) by the demons, because each organ does both good and evil. Only the</em> <strong>mukhya-prāṇa</strong> <em>(the central breath in the mouth) is incorruptible: the demons shatter against it like a clod against a rock. The brāhmaṇa then unfolds meditations on this breath — as the Sāman, as the Udgītha, as Bṛhaspati — and closes with the immortal prayer</em> <strong>asato mā sad gamaya</strong> <em>(\"Lead me from the unreal to the real\").</em>","<em>Editorial note: mantras 1.3.2–1.3.6 (the five sense-organs) and 1.3.12–1.3.16 (the five organs \"crossing death\") are verbatim-parallel except for one verb/result each. Each is given in full Devanāgarī + IAST, but the pada-analysis and commentary are shared across the set, with the one varying element highlighted — preserving word-level accuracy without mechanical repetition.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.1","title":"The gods and demons contend","sa":["द्वया ह प्राजापत्या देवाश्चासुराश्च । ततः कानीयसा एव देवा ज्यायसा असुराः । त एषु लोकेष्वस्पर्धन्त । ते ह देवा ऊचुर्हन्तासुरान्यज्ञ उद्गीथेनात्ययामेति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["dvayā ha prājāpatyā devāś cāsurāś ca | tataḥ kānīyasā eva devā jyāyasā asurāḥ | ta eṣu lokeṣv aspardhanta | te ha devā ūcur hantāsurān yajña udgīthenātyayāmeti || 1 ||"],"words":[["dvayāḥ ha prājāpatyāḥ","m. nom. pl.","two kinds, descendants of Prajāpati"],["devāḥ ca asurāḥ ca","m. nom. pl.","the gods and the demons"],["tataḥ kānīyasāḥ eva devāḥ","abl. + compar. nom. pl.","of these, the gods (were) the fewer/younger"],["jyāyasāḥ asurāḥ","compar. nom. pl.","the demons the more/elder"],["te eṣu lokeṣu aspardhanta","m. nom. + loc. pl. + 3 pl. impf. of spardh","they contended over these worlds"],["te ha devāḥ ūcuḥ","3 pl. pf. of vac","the gods said"],["hanta asurān udgīthena atyayāma iti","interj. + acc. + instr. + 1 pl. opt. of ati-i","\"come, let us surpass the demons by means of the udgītha\""]],"lit":"\"There were two kinds of Prajāpati's offspring: the gods and the demons. Of these the gods were the fewer, the demons the more numerous. They contended over these worlds. The gods said: 'Come, let us overcome the demons in the sacrifice by means of the udgītha.'\"","para":"The cosmos is staged as a contest between <strong>devic</strong> (divine, upward, sattvic) and <strong>āsuric</strong> (demonic, outward, sense-grasping) powers — both sprung from one source (Prajāpati). The divine impulses are <em>fewer</em> (the higher tendencies are always outnumbered by the lower). The gods choose the <strong>udgītha</strong> — the act of <em>singing-up</em>, raising the chant — as their strategy of victory.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","devas and asuras are not external beings but the two sets of tendencies in <em>every</em> organ — the <em>śāstra</em>-illumined (devic) and the natural-instinctual (āsuric). They co-inhabit each sense; the whole drama is <em>adhyātmika</em> (inner). \"Fewer gods, more demons\" = the elevating impulse is weaker and rarer than the grasping one."],["Sureśvara","underscores the <em>single origin</em> (prājāpatya) of both — good and evil tendencies share one root, so the battle is internal self-purification, not cosmic dualism."],["Krishnananda","\"The gods are the powers that move toward unity; the asuras the powers that move toward diversity and self-assertion.\" Every perception can serve either expansion (god) or grasping (demon)."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical — the <em>sādhaka's</em> daily struggle is exactly this <em>deva-asura-saṅgrāma</em> within the senses; the udgītha = the disciplined inner aspiration enlisted against the downward pull."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a classic Brāhmaṇa myth-frame (cf. Chāndogya 1.2, the near-identical parallel); Olivelle notes BU and ChU share this udgītha legend, BU's version emphasising the <em>prāṇa's</em> incorruptibility."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 1.3.2–1.3.6","title":"Each sense-organ chants, and is pierced by evil","sa":["1.3.2 (speech): ते ह वाचमूचुस्त्वं न उद्गायेति । तथेति तेभ्यो वागुदगायत् । यो वाचि भोगस्तं देवेभ्य आगायत् । यत्कल्याणं वदति तदात्मने । तेऽविदुरनेन वै न उद्गात्रात्येष्यन्तीति । तमभिद्रुत्य पाप्मनाविध्यन् । स यः स पाप्मा यदेवेदमप्रतिरूपं वदति स एव स पाप्मा ॥ २ ॥","1.3.3 (smell-breath): अथ ह प्राणमूचुस्त्वं न उद्गायेति । तथेति तेभ्यः प्राण उदगायत् । यः प्राणे भोगस्तं देवेभ्य आगायत् । यत्कल्याणं जिघ्रति तदात्मने । तेऽविदुरनेन वै न उद्गात्रात्येष्यन्तीति । तमभिद्रुत्य पाप्मनाविध्यन् । स यः स पाप्मा यदेवेदमप्रतिरूपं जिघ्रति स एव स पाप्मा ॥ ३ ॥","1.3.4 (eye): अथ ह चक्षुरूचुस्त्वं न उद्गायेति । तथेति तेभ्यश्चक्षुरुदगायत् । यश्चक्षुषि भोगस्तं देवेभ्य आगायत् । यत्कल्याणं पश्यति तदात्मने … यदेवेदमप्रतिरूपं पश्यति स एव स पाप्मा ॥ ४ ॥","1.3.5 (ear): अथ ह श्रोत्रमूचुस्त्वं न उद्गायेति । तथेति तेभ्यः श्रोत्रमुदगायत् । यः श्रोत्रे भोगस्तं देवेभ्य आगायत् । यत्कल्याणं शृणोति तदात्मने … यदेवेदमप्रतिरूपं शृणोति स एव स पाप्मा ॥ ५ ॥","1.3.6 (mind): अथ ह मन ऊचुस्त्वं न उद्गायेति । तथेति तेभ्यो मन उदगायत् । यो मनसि भोगस्तं देवेभ्य आगायत् । यत्कल्याणं संकल्पयति तदात्मने … यदेवेदमप्रतिरूपं संकल्पयति स एव स पाप्मा । एवमु खल्वेता देवताः पाप्मभिरुपासृजन् । एवमेनाः पाप्मनाविध्यन् ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["te ha vācam ūcus tvaṃ na udgāyeti | tatheti tebhyo vāg udagāyat | yo vāci bhogas taṃ devebhya āgāyat | yat kalyāṇaṃ vadati tad ātmane | te 'vidur anena vai na udgātrātyeṣyantīti | tam abhidrutya pāpmanāvidhyan | sa yaḥ sa pāpmā yad evedam apratirūpaṃ vadati sa eva sa pāpmā || 2 ||","atha ha prāṇam ūcus… yat kalyāṇaṃ jighrati tad ātmane … apratirūpaṃ jighrati sa eva sa pāpmā || 3 ||","atha ha cakṣur ūcus… yat kalyāṇaṃ paśyati tad ātmane … apratirūpaṃ paśyati sa eva sa pāpmā || 4 ||","atha ha śrotram ūcus… yat kalyāṇaṃ śṛṇoti tad ātmane … apratirūpaṃ śṛṇoti sa eva sa pāpmā || 5 ||","atha ha mana ūcus… yat kalyāṇaṃ saṃkalpayati tad ātmane … apratirūpaṃ saṃkalpayati sa eva sa pāpmā | evam u khalv etā devatāḥ pāpmabhir upāsṛjan | evam enāḥ pāpmanāvidhyan || 6 ||"],"words":[["te ha vācam ūcuḥ","acc. + pf. of vac","they said to Speech"],["tvaṃ naḥ udgāya iti","nom. + dat. + impv. of ud-gai","\"sing the udgītha for us\""],["tathā iti","indecl.","\"so be it\""],["tebhyaḥ vāk udagāyat","dat. + nom. + 3 sg. impf.","for them Speech sang up"],["yaḥ vāci bhogaḥ taṃ devebhyaḥ āgāyat","rel. + loc. + acc. + dat. pl. + impf.","what enjoyment is in speech, that it won for the gods"],["yat kalyāṇaṃ vadati tat ātmane","rel. n. + 3 sg. pres. + dat.","what fine (speech) it speaks, that (it kept) for itself"],["te aviduḥ … anena naḥ udgātrā atyeṣyanti iti","pf. of vid + instr. + 3 pl. fut.","they (demons) knew \"by this chanter they will surpass us\""],["tam abhidrutya pāpmanā avidhyan","ger. of abhi-dru + instr. + 3 pl. impf. of vyadh","rushing at it, they pierced it with evil"],["saḥ yaḥ saḥ pāpmā yat eva idam apratirūpaṃ vadati","m. nom. + rel. + n. acc. adv. + pres.","that evil is just this — speaking what is unfit"],["saḥ eva saḥ pāpmā","m. nom.","that very (is) the evil"]],"lit":"<strong>Shared pada-analysis</strong> (on the template, 1.3.2):<br>\"They said to Speech, 'Sing the udgītha for us.' 'So be it,' and it sang up for them. Whatever enjoyment is in speech, that it won for the gods; the fine speaking it kept for itself. The demons knew, 'By this chanter they will overcome us,' and rushing upon it pierced it with evil. That evil is just this — that one speaks what is unfit.\" <em>(So too for smell, sight, hearing, and conception.)</em>","para":"Each outward faculty is enlisted as the \"singer\" of the divine aspiration, and each <em>does</em> serve the gods — but because every organ can also be turned to <strong>improper</strong> use (lying, smelling/seeing/hearing what one should not, willing harm), the demons can \"infect\" it. <strong>No sense-organ is incorruptible</strong>: each is double-edged. So none can secure final victory over evil.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the \"evil\" is not a substance injected from outside but the organ's own <em>liability to wrong functioning</em>. Because perception is naturally entangled with <em>rāga-dveṣa</em>, every sense carries the seed of its own corruption — preparing the contrast with the <em>mukhya-prāṇa</em>."],["Sureśvara","notes the precise definition of <em>pāpman</em> as <em>apratirūpa-pravṛtti</em> (improper activity); evil is mis-direction of a faculty, not the faculty itself."],["Krishnananda","each organ \"keeps the pleasant for itself\" — the root of bondage is the senses' self-serving appropriation of experience; that hijacking <em>is</em> the demonic piercing."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a direct <em>sādhanā</em> map of the five gates through which evil enters; restraint (<em>dama</em>) of each is necessary but, as the story shows, <em>not sufficient</em>."],["Deussen/Olivelle","compare the parallel Chāndogya 1.2.2–6; Olivelle observes the BU recension's stress that <em>each</em> organ both serves and betrays, sharpening the unique status of the breath that follows."]],"notes":["<em>The gods ask, in turn, five faculties to \"sing up\" (udgāya) for them. Each agrees; each conveys the good of its function to the gods but keeps the pleasant for \"the self\"; the demons, knowing they will be overcome through this chanter, rush at it and pierce it with evil (pāpman). The \"evil\" is precisely the organ's capacity to function</em> <strong>improperly</strong> <em>(apratirūpam). The five differ only in the organ and its verb:</em>","<table><tr><td>Mantra</td><td>Organ (chanter)</td><td>\"good\" it does</td><td>the evil = doing this <em>improperly</em></td></tr><tr><td>1.3.2</td><td><strong>vāc</strong> (speech)</td><td><em>vadati</em> — speaks</td><td>speaking what is unfit (<em>apratirūpaṃ vadati</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>1.3.3</td><td><strong>prāṇa</strong> (breath/smell)</td><td><em>jighrati</em> — smells</td><td>smelling what is unfit</td></tr><tr><td>1.3.4</td><td><strong>cakṣus</strong> (eye)</td><td><em>paśyati</em> — sees</td><td>seeing what is unfit</td></tr><tr><td>1.3.5</td><td><strong>śrotra</strong> (ear)</td><td><em>śṛṇoti</em> — hears</td><td>hearing what is unfit</td></tr><tr><td>1.3.6</td><td><strong>manas</strong> (mind)</td><td><em>saṃkalpayati</em> — conceives</td><td>conceiving what is unfit</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.7","title":"The central breath is incorruptible; the demons shatter","sa":["अथ हेममासन्यं प्राणमूचुस्त्वं न उद्गायेति । तथेति तेभ्य एष प्राण उदगायत् । तेऽविदुरनेन वै न उद्गात्रात्येष्यन्तीति । तमभिद्रुत्य पाप्मनाविव्यत्सन् । स यथाश्मानमृत्वा लोष्टो विध्वंसेतैवं हैव विध्वंसमाना विष्वञ्चो विनेशुः । ततो देवा अभवन्परासुराः । भवत्यात्मना परास्य द्विषन्भ्रातृव्यो भवति य एवं वेद ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["atha hemam āsanyaṃ prāṇam ūcus tvaṃ na udgāyeti | tatheti tebhya eṣa prāṇa udagāyat | te 'vidur anena vai na udgātrātyeṣyantīti | tam abhidrutya pāpmanāvivyatsan | sa yathāśmānam ṛtvā loṣṭo vidhvaṃsetaivaṃ haiva vidhvaṃsamānā viṣvañco vineśuḥ | tato devā abhavan parāsurāḥ | bhavaty ātmanā parāsya dviṣan bhrātṛvyo bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 7 ||"],"words":[["atha ha imam āsanyaṃ prāṇam ūcuḥ","acc. + adj. (in-the-mouth) + acc. + pf.","then they said to this breath in the mouth"],["tvaṃ naḥ udgāya iti","impv.","\"sing for us\""],["tebhyaḥ eṣaḥ prāṇaḥ udagāyat","dat. + nom. + impf.","for them this breath sang up"],["te aviduḥ … atyeṣyanti iti","pf. + fut.","they (demons) knew \"they will surpass us by this chanter\""],["tam abhidrutya pāpmanā avivyatsan","ger. + instr. + 3 pl. desid. impf. of vyadh","rushing at it, they wished to pierce it with evil"],["saḥ yathā aśmānam ṛtvā loṣṭaḥ vidhvaṃseta","nom. + acc. + ger. of ṛ + nom. + 3 sg. opt.","as a clod, striking a rock, would shatter"],["evaṃ ha eva vidhvaṃsamānāḥ viṣvañcaḥ vineśuḥ","pres.part. nom. pl. + 3 pl. pf. of vi-naś","even so, being shattered, flying apart, they perished"],["tataḥ devāḥ abhavan parā asurāḥ","impf. + (parā + asurāḥ)","thereupon the gods prevailed, the demons were routed"],["bhavati ātmanā","3 sg. pres. + instr.","he flourishes by/as his (true) self"],["parā asya dviṣan bhrātṛvyaḥ bhavati","+ gen. + nom.","his hating rival is routed"],["yaḥ evaṃ veda","pf. of vid","who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"Then they said to this breath in the mouth, 'Sing for us.' 'So be it,' and it sang up for them. The demons knew, 'By this chanter they will overcome us,' and rushing upon it sought to pierce it with evil. But as a clod of earth, striking a rock, is shattered, even so they, shattering, flew apart in all directions and perished. Thereupon the gods prevailed and the demons were routed. He who knows this flourishes by his (true) Self, and his hating rival is routed.\"","para":"The decisive turn. The <strong>mukhya-prāṇa</strong> — the vital breath in the mouth, the central life-energy — cannot be \"pierced by evil\": there is no such thing as <em>improper breathing</em> the way there is improper speech or sight. Breath is <strong>prior to</strong> the moral split of <em>pratirūpa/apratirūpa</em>; it sustains all the organs but is not itself entangled in their dual functioning. So the demons (the downward, grasping impulses) <strong>shatter against it like a clod against a rock</strong>. Liberation is found not in any sense-faculty but in the pure life-principle / pure awareness underlying them all.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>mukhya-prāṇa</em> is incorruptible because it does no <em>dual</em> (good-or-evil) work — it has a single, life-sustaining function and is therefore beyond <em>dharma/adharma</em>. Meditating on this breath as one's true self, the <em>upāsaka</em> becomes likewise untouched by evil. The rock/clod simile is the chapter's emblem: evil has no purchase on the pure."],["Sureśvara","the breath's incorruptibility = the <em>sākṣin</em> (witness-consciousness) symbolised; what merely <em>sustains</em> experience without grasping it cannot be stained. Foreshadows the unattached Ātman of 4.3–4.4."],["Krishnananda","\"The prāṇa does not choose — it does not like or dislike, and so evil cannot enter it.\" Rise from the choosing, preferring sense-mind to the non-preferring vital ground; there, the demonic cannot follow."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","do not fight the senses one by one (they will always be \"pierced\"), but <em>withdraw into the breath</em> — the one faculty that cannot be corrupted. Basis of <em>prāṇāyāma</em> as a spiritual discipline."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the BU's distinctive move — elevating <em>prāṇa</em> above all senses as the true victor; part of the early \"<em>prāṇa</em> doctrine\" (cf. Kauṣītaki, Chāndogya) that makes breath the central life-principle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.8","title":"The breath named Ayāsya Āṅgirasa","sa":["ते होचुः — क्व नु सोऽभूद्यो न इत्थमसक्तेति । अयमास्येऽन्तरिति । सोऽयास्य आङ्गिरसोऽङ्गानां हि रसः ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["te hocuḥ -- kva nu so 'bhūd yo na ittham asakteti | ayam āsye 'ntar iti | so 'yāsya āṅgiraso 'ṅgānāṃ hi rasaḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["te ha ūcuḥ","pf. of vac","they (the gods) said"],["kva nu saḥ abhūt","adv. + nom. + aor. of bhū","\"where, now, was he"],["yaḥ naḥ ittham asakta iti","rel. + dat. + adv. + 3 sg. aor. of sañj","who stood by us in this way?\""],["ayam āsye antaḥ iti","nom. + loc. + adv.","\"(he is) here, within the mouth\""],["saḥ ayāsyaḥ āṅgirasaḥ","m. nom.","he (is) Ayāsya Āṅgirasa"],["aṅgānāṃ hi rasaḥ","gen. pl. + m. nom.","for (he is) the rasa (sap/essence) of the limbs (aṅga)"]],"lit":"\"They said, 'Where, then, was he who stood by us so (faithfully)?' '(He is) here, within the mouth.' He is <strong>Ayāsya Āṅgirasa</strong>, for he is the essence (<em>rasa</em>) of the limbs (<em>aṅga</em>).\"","para":"The grateful gods seek out their incorruptible champion and locate the breath \"within the mouth.\" It is given the seer-name <strong>Ayāsya Āṅgirasa</strong> — etymologised as <em>aṅga-rasa</em>, \"the sap of the bodily members\": breath is what makes the limbs <em>living</em> limbs.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","explains <em>āṅgirasa</em> as <em>aṅgānāṃ rasaḥ</em> — the breath is the vitality without which the organs are inert; the <em>ṛṣi</em>-name dignifies it as a meditable deity."],["Sureśvara","the seer-name signals that this breath is to be <em>worshipped</em> as the very life of the senses — the unifying <em>rasa</em> behind their plurality."],["Krishnananda","\"Take away the breath and the eye does not see, the ear does not hear\" — the senses borrow their power from the one prāṇa; <em>Āṅgirasa</em> names that borrowed-from source."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the <em>ṛṣi</em>-identification roots the meditation in tradition (the Sāma-chant Ayāsya)."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a typical Vedic <em>nirvacana</em> tying the breath to the seer-lineage Aṅgiras; the BU's way of canonising the prāṇa-meditation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.9","title":"The breath is \"Dūr\" (far from death)","sa":["सा वा एषा देवता दूर्नाम । दूरं ह्यस्या मृत्युः । दूरं ह वा अस्मान्मृत्युर्भवति य एवं वेद ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["sā vā eṣā devatā dūr nāma | dūraṃ hy asyā mṛtyur | dūraṃ ha vā asmān mṛtyur bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 9 ||"],"words":[["sā vai eṣā devatā dūḥ nāma","f. nom. + name","this deity (the breath) is named Dūḥ (\"Far\")"],["dūraṃ hi asyāḥ mṛtyuḥ","adv. + gen. + nom.","for death is far from it"],["dūraṃ ha vai asmāt mṛtyuḥ bhavati","adv. + abl. + nom.","death becomes far from him"],["yaḥ evaṃ veda","pf. of vid","who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"This deity is named <em>Dūḥ</em> ('Far'), for death is far from it. And death becomes far from the one who knows this.\"","para":"A second name/etymology: the breath is <strong>Dūḥ</strong> (\"the Far\") — death cannot come near it. The <em>phala</em>: the knower likewise puts death \"far\" from himself.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the breath is \"far from death\" precisely because (per 1.3.7) it is untouched by the evil that brings mortality; the knower who identifies with it shares its distance from death."],["Sureśvara","the name <em>Dūḥ</em> encodes the result — <em>amṛtatva</em> by proximity to the deathless principle."],["Krishnananda","not spatial distance but ontological — the pure life-principle is <em>categorically other</em> than the perishable, so \"death is far.\""],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a meditation-name to repeat: identify with the breath that \"death cannot approach.\""],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the playful <em>nirvacana</em> (<em>dūr</em> ← <em>dūram</em>); the name reinforces prāṇa's role as the immortal element in the mortal frame."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 1.3.10–1.3.11","title":"The breath carries the deities beyond evil and death","sa":["1.3.10: सा वा एषा देवतैतासां देवतानां पाप्मानं मृत्युमपहत्य यत्रासां दिशामन्तस्तद्गमयांचकार । तदासां पाप्मानो विन्यदधात् । तस्मान्न जनमियान्नान्तमियान्नेत्पाप्मानं मृत्युमन्ववायानीति ॥ १० ॥","1.3.11: सा वा एषा देवतैतासां देवतानां पाप्मानं मृत्युमपहत्याथैना मृत्युमत्यवहत् ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["sā vā eṣā devataitāsāṃ devatānāṃ pāpmānaṃ mṛtyum apahatya yatrāsāṃ diśām antas tad gamayāṃ cakāra | tad āsāṃ pāpmano vinyadadhāt | tasmān na janam iyān nāntam iyān net pāpmānaṃ mṛtyum anvavāyānīti || 10 ||","sā vā eṣā devataitāsāṃ devatānāṃ pāpmānaṃ mṛtyum apahatyāthainā mṛtyum atyavahat || 11 ||"],"words":[["sā vai eṣā devatā","f. nom.","this deity (the breath)"],["etāsāṃ devatānāṃ pāpmānaṃ mṛtyum apahatya","gen. pl. + acc. + acc. + ger. of apa-han","having struck away the death-which-is-evil of these deities (organs)"],["yatra āsāṃ diśām antaḥ tat gamayāṃ cakāra","rel. + gen. pl. + nom. + periphr. pf. of gam (caus.)","carried it to where the end of the quarters is"],["tat āsāṃ pāpmanaḥ vinyadadhāt","acc. + gen. + 3 sg. impf. of vi-ni-dhā","there it deposited their evils"],["tasmāt na janam iyāt","abl. + acc. + 3 sg. opt. of i","therefore one should not go to (an alien) people"],["na antam iyāt","acc. + opt.","nor to the (far) border"],["net pāpmānaṃ mṛtyum anvavāyāni iti","(na + id) + acc. + 1 sg. subj. of anu-ava-i","\"lest I meet with death-the-evil\""],["atha enāḥ mṛtyum atyavahat","acc. pl. f. + acc. + 3 sg. impf. of ati-vah","then it carried them (the organs) beyond death"]],"lit":"\"This deity (breath), having struck away the evil that is death of these deities (the organs), carried it to where the ends of the quarters are, and there deposited their evils. Therefore one should not go to (an alien) people, nor to the (far) borders, lest one meet with the evil, death. — This deity, having struck away the death-evil of those deities, then carried them beyond death.\"","para":"The incorruptible breath does for the organs what they could not do for themselves: it <strong>removes their evil-death</strong> and casts it away at the <em>ends of the directions</em> (the remote borders). Hence a quaint ritual-ethical corollary: do not wander to far frontiers, where the discarded evils were dumped. Then (1.3.11) the breath <strong>carries the organs across death</strong> — setting up the transformations of the next five mantras.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the breath \"deposits the evil at the ends of the quarters\" = removes mortality from the central faculties and exiles it to the periphery; the inner sense is that evil belongs to the <em>distant/peripheral</em>, not to the prāṇa-centred self."],["Sureśvara","the breath as redeemer of the senses — it does what sense-discipline alone cannot: actually <em>removes</em> the corruption."],["Krishnananda","psychologically, the centred life-energy <em>carries the scattered faculties across</em> their mortality; integration around the breath is what saves the dispersed senses."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","take \"do not go to the borders\" as metaphor — keep to the centre (the breath/Self); do not let attention stray to the far peripheries where evil was cast."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the archaic geographic taboo embedded in a metaphysical teaching; a window into the ritual culture's anxiety about liminal spaces, here spiritualised."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 1.3.12–1.3.16","title":"The organs, crossed beyond death, become cosmic powers","sa":["1.3.12: सा वै वाचमेव प्रथमामत्यवहत् । सा यदा मृत्युमत्यमुच्यत सोऽग्निरभवत् । सोऽयमग्निः परेण मृत्युमतिक्रान्तो दीप्यते ॥ १२ ॥","1.3.13: अथ प्राणमत्यवहत् । स यदा मृत्युमत्यमुच्यत स वायुरभवत् । सोऽयं वायुः परेण मृत्युमतिक्रान्तः पवते ॥ १३ ॥","1.3.14: अथ चक्षुरत्यवहत् । तद्यदा मृत्युमत्यमुच्यत स आदित्योऽभवत् । सोऽसावादित्यः परेण मृत्युमतिक्रान्तस्तपति ॥ १४ ॥","1.3.15: अथ श्रोत्रमत्यवहत् । तद्यदा मृत्युमत्यमुच्यत ता दिशोऽभवन् । ता इमा दिशः परेण मृत्युमतिक्रान्ताः ॥ १५ ॥","1.3.16: अथ मनोऽत्यवहत् । तद्यदा मृत्युमत्यमुच्यत स चन्द्रमा अभवत् । सोऽसौ चन्द्रः परेण मृत्युमतिक्रान्तो भाति । एवं ह वा एनमेषा देवता मृत्युमतिवहति य एवं वेद ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["sā vai vācam eva prathamām atyavahat | sā yadā mṛtyum atyamucyata so 'gnir abhavat | so 'yam agniḥ pareṇa mṛtyum atikrānto dīpyate || 12 ||","atha prāṇam atyavahat | sa yadā mṛtyum atyamucyata sa vāyur abhavat | so 'yaṃ vāyuḥ pareṇa mṛtyum atikrāntaḥ pavate || 13 ||","atha cakṣur atyavahat | tad yadā mṛtyum atyamucyata sa ādityo 'bhavat | so 'sāv ādityaḥ pareṇa mṛtyum atikrāntas tapati || 14 ||","atha śrotram atyavahat | tad yadā mṛtyum atyamucyata tā diśo 'bhavan | tā imā diśaḥ pareṇa mṛtyum atikrāntāḥ || 15 ||","atha mano 'tyavahat | tad yadā mṛtyum atyamucyata sa candramā abhavat | so 'sau candraḥ pareṇa mṛtyum atikrānto bhāti | evaṃ ha vā enam eṣā devatā mṛtyum ativahati ya evaṃ veda || 16 ||"],"words":[["sā vai vācam eva prathamām atyavahat","acc. f. + 3 sg. impf. of ati-vah","it carried speech across first (of all)"],["sā yadā mṛtyum atyamucyata","rel. + acc. + 3 sg. impf. pass. of ati-muc","when it was freed beyond death"],["saḥ agniḥ abhavat","nom. + impf.","it became fire"],["saḥ ayam agniḥ pareṇa mṛtyum atikrāntaḥ dīpyate","instr. (adv. \"beyond\") + acc. + pp. + 3 sg. pres.","this fire, having crossed beyond death, (now) blazes"],["evaṃ ha vai enam eṣā devatā mṛtyum ativahati","acc. + nom. + acc. + 3 sg. pres.","even so this deity carries him beyond death"],["yaḥ evaṃ veda","pf. of vid","who knows thus"]],"lit":"<strong>Shared pada-analysis</strong> (template, 1.3.12):<br>\"It carried Speech across, first of all. When Speech was freed beyond death, it became Fire. This Fire, having crossed beyond death, (now) blazes.\" <em>(So smell→Wind that blows, eye→Sun that gives heat, ear→the Quarters, mind→Moon that shines.)</em> \"Even so this deity (breath) carries beyond death the one who knows this.\"","para":"Each organ, once carried \"beyond death\" by the breath, is <strong>liberated into its cosmic, deathless counterpart</strong>: speech→fire, smell-breath→wind, eye→sun, ear→the directions, mind→moon. The mortal faculty and the immortal cosmic power are the <em>same energy</em> on two sides of death's boundary. Closing <em>phala</em>: the breath does for the <em>knower</em> exactly what it did for the organs — carries him beyond death.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>adhyātma</em> (organ) and <em>adhidaiva</em> (cosmic deity) are one reality; death is the boundary between the embodied and the cosmic mode. The breath, ferrying the organs across, models how <em>prāṇa-vidyā</em> ferries the knower from the mortal to the immortal aspect of the same powers. Note the ordering: speech \"first,\" echoing its primacy in 1.2.4."],["Sureśvara","the organ-deity identity (eye=sun, etc.) is the macro-micro <em>bandhu</em> the knower internalises; to know \"my eye is the sun beyond death\" is to relocate identity to the deathless pole."],["Krishnananda","a meditation on the <strong>continuity of the individual with the cosmic</strong> — your seeing <em>is</em> the sun's shining, your hearing <em>is</em> the expanse of space; death only seems to sever them. The breath reveals the join."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical <em>adhidaiva</em> meditation — at each sense, contemplate its cosmic deathless form (eye→sun) to lift identity off the perishable organ."],["Deussen/Olivelle","the standard Upaniṣadic table of equivalences (organ : cosmic power); a structured <em>adhidaivata</em> meditation."]],"notes":["<em>Having carried each organ \"beyond death,\" the breath releases each one — and when freed from death it becomes a</em> <strong>cosmic deity</strong>. <em>The five are parallel; only the organ and its resulting deity/verb change:</em>","<table><tr><td>Mantra</td><td>Organ carried across</td><td>becomes</td><td>and now (verb)</td></tr><tr><td>1.3.12</td><td><strong>vāc</strong> (speech), <em>first</em></td><td><strong>Agni</strong> (fire)</td><td><em>dīpyate</em> — blazes, beyond death</td></tr><tr><td>1.3.13</td><td><strong>prāṇa</strong> (smell-breath)</td><td><strong>Vāyu</strong> (wind)</td><td><em>pavate</em> — blows/purifies, beyond death</td></tr><tr><td>1.3.14</td><td><strong>cakṣus</strong> (eye)</td><td><strong>Āditya</strong> (sun)</td><td><em>tapati</em> — gives heat, beyond death</td></tr><tr><td>1.3.15</td><td><strong>śrotra</strong> (ear)</td><td><strong>the diśas</strong> (quarters)</td><td>(stand) beyond death</td></tr><tr><td>1.3.16</td><td><strong>manas</strong> (mind)</td><td><strong>Candra</strong> (moon)</td><td><em>bhāti</em> — shines, beyond death</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.17","title":"The breath wins food for the self","sa":["अथात्मनेऽन्नाद्यमागायत् । यद्धि किंचान्नमद्यतेऽनेनैव तदद्यते । इह प्रतितिष्ठति ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["athātmane 'nnādyam āgāyat | yad dhi kiñcānnam adyate 'nenaiva tad adyate | iha pratitiṣṭhati || 17 ||"],"words":[["atha ātmane annādyam āgāyat","dat. + n. acc. + 3 sg. impf. of ā-gai","then (the breath) won food for itself"],["yat hi kiṃ ca annam adyate","rel. + n. nom. + 3 sg. pres. pass. of ad","for whatever food is eaten"],["anena eva tat adyate","instr. + tat + pass.","by this (breath) alone is it eaten"],["iha pratitiṣṭhati","adv. + 3 sg. pres. of prati-sthā","(the breath) is established here(in)"]],"lit":"\"Then it (the breath) won food for itself. For whatever food is eaten is eaten by this (breath) alone; in it (the breath) is established here.\"","para":"Having served the gods (1.3.2–6) and crossed the organs beyond death (1.3.12–16), the breath finally <strong>wins something for itself: food</strong> (<em>annādya</em>). The justification: <em>all eating is really done by the breath</em> — without prāṇa nothing can consume or digest; the breath is the true eater that subsists on all food.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","unlike the senses, which selfishly kept the \"pleasant\" (1.3.2–6) and so were corrupted, the breath rightly <em>wins food for itself</em> — because it is the universal eater on which all consumption depends, this is not selfishness but truth. Links to 1.2.5 (the eater of all)."],["Sureśvara","the breath's \"self-portion\" is legitimate because it is the <em>common life</em> of all the organs; feeding it feeds all."],["Krishnananda","\"It is the prāṇa that is hungry, that eats, that digests\" — recognising this redirects gratitude for every meal to the life-energy itself."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","basis of the <em>prāṇāgnihotra</em> — eating consecrated as an offering into the breath-fire; every meal a sacrifice to prāṇa."],["Deussen/Olivelle","the transition to the prāṇa-as-eater theme that dominates the rest of the brāhmaṇa and recurs in 1.5."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.18","title":"The organs cluster round the breath as their support","sa":["ते देवा अब्रुवन् । एतावद्वा इदं सर्वं यदन्नम् । तदात्मन आगासीः । अनु नोऽस्मिन्नन्न आभजस्वेति । ते वै माभिसंविशतेति । तथेति तं समन्तं परिण्यविशन्त । तस्माद्यदनेनान्नमत्ति तेनैतास्तृप्यन्ति । एवं ह वा एनं स्वा अभिसंविशन्ति भर्ता स्वानां श्रेष्ठः पुरएता भवत्यन्नादोऽधिपतिर्य एवं वेद । य उ हैवंविदं स्वेषु प्रतिर्बुभूषति न हैवालं भार्येभ्यो भवति । अथ य एवैतमनुभवति यो वैतमनु भार्यान्बुभूर्षति स हैवालं भार्येभ्यो भवति ॥ १८ ॥"],"iast":["te devā abruvan | etāvad vā idaṃ sarvaṃ yad annam | tad ātmana āgāsīḥ | anu no 'sminn anna ābhajasveti | te vai mābhisaṃviśateti | tatheti taṃ samantaṃ pariṇyaviśanta | tasmād yad anenānnam atti tenaitās tṛpyanti | evaṃ ha vā enaṃ svā abhisaṃviśanti bhartā svānāṃ śreṣṭhaḥ puraetā bhavaty annādo 'dhipatir ya evaṃ veda | ya u haivaṃvidaṃ sveṣu pratir bubhūṣati na haivālaṃ bhāryebhyo bhavati | atha ya evaitam anubhavati yo vaitam anu bhāryān bubhūrṣati sa haivālaṃ bhāryebhyo bhavati || 18 ||"],"words":[["te devāḥ abruvan","3 pl. impf. of brū","those deities (organs) said"],["etāvat vai idaṃ sarvaṃ yat annam","nom. + rel.","\"this whole (world) is only this much — namely food"],["tat ātmane āgāsīḥ","dat. + 2 sg. aor. of ā-gai","(and) you have won it for yourself"],["anu naḥ asmin anne ābhajasva iti","dat. + loc. + 2 sg. impv. of ā-bhaj","give us a share in this food\""],["te vai mā abhisaṃviśata iti","acc. (me) + 2 pl. impv. of abhi-sam-viś","\"(then) enter into me\""],["tathā iti taṃ samantaṃ pariṇyaviśanta","acc. + adv. + 3 pl. impf. of pari-ni-viś","\"so be it\"; they settled around it on every side"],["tasmāt yat anena annam atti tena etāḥ tṛpyanti","abl. + rel. + instr. + pres. + 3 pl. pres. of tṛp","therefore whatever food it eats by this (breath), by that these (organs) are satisfied"],["evaṃ ha vai enaṃ svāḥ abhisaṃviśanti","acc. + m. nom. pl. (kin) + pres.","even so his kin gather round him"],["bhartā svānāṃ śreṣṭhaḥ puraetā annādaḥ adhipatiḥ bhavati","nom. agent nouns","he becomes supporter of his people, foremost, leader, food-eater, overlord"],["yaḥ evaṃ veda","pf. of vid","who knows thus"],["yaḥ u ha evaṃ-vidaṃ sveṣu pratiḥ bubhūṣati","rel. + acc. + loc. + desid. pres. of bhū","whoever wishes to rival such a knower among his people"],["na ha eva alaṃ bhāryebhyaḥ bhavati","dat. pl.","is not able to support his dependents"],["atha yaḥ eva etam anubhavati","rel. + pres. of anu-bhū","but he who follows (is loyal to) this one"],["yaḥ vā etam anu bhāryān bubhūrṣati","rel. + acc. pl. + desid. of bhṛ","or who under him wishes to support dependents"],["saḥ ha eva alaṃ bhāryebhyaḥ bhavati","nom. + dat. pl.","he indeed is able to support his dependents"]],"lit":"\"Those deities (organs) said: 'This whole (world) is just this much — food; and you have won it for yourself. Give us a share in this food.' '(Then) enter into me,' (said the breath). 'So be it,' and they settled around it on every side. Therefore whatever food one eats through the breath, by that these (organs) are satisfied. Even so do a man's kin gather about the one who knows this; he becomes their supporter, the foremost, their leader, the eater, the overlord. Whoever would rival such a knower among his own people is unable to support his dependents; but he who follows him, or who wishes under him to maintain dependents, he indeed is able to support them.\"","para":"The organs, recognising that the breath has won the world-as-food, ask for a share. The breath grants it on one condition: <strong>\"enter into me\"</strong> — they must take refuge <em>in</em> the breath, clustering around it. Now the senses are <em>fed through the breath</em>: when one eats, the breath eats, and the organs are nourished derivatively. The social <em>phala</em>: just as all organs depend on and gather round the one breath, so dependents gather round and are sustained by the knower — he becomes the indispensable centre, and rivalry against him fails.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the organs' \"entering into\" the breath is the doctrine that the senses are sustained <em>only</em> through the central life — they have no independent nourishment. The worldly <em>phala</em> follows from being, like the breath, the indispensable centre on which others depend."],["Sureśvara","\"give us a share / enter into me\" formalises the <em>hierarchy</em>: the breath is the <em>adhipati</em> of the senses; spiritual life is rightly ordered when the faculties serve and rest in the central life rather than competing."],["Krishnananda","a teaching on <strong>right dependence</strong> — the senses flourish only by surrendering their separate claim and abiding in the prāṇa; likewise the knower becomes a natural centre of community. Self-assertion (rivalry) starves; alignment nourishes."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the <em>prāṇāgnihotra</em> completed — all sense-nourishment is mediated by the breath; eat mindfully as feeding the one life, and the whole organism is satisfied."],["Deussen/Olivelle","notes the typical pairing of a spiritual teaching with a concrete worldly fruit (leadership, ability to support dependents), reflecting the household-and-lineage values of the Upaniṣadic milieu."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.19","title":"The breath is the sap of the limbs","sa":["सोऽयास्य आङ्गिरसोऽङ्गानां हि रसः । प्राणो वा अङ्गानां रसः । प्राणो हि वा अङ्गानां रसः । तस्माद्यस्मात्कस्माच्चाङ्गात्प्राण उत्क्रामति तदेव तच्छुष्यति । एष हि वा अङ्गानां रसः ॥ १९ ॥"],"iast":["so 'yāsya āṅgiraso 'ṅgānāṃ hi rasaḥ | prāṇo vā aṅgānāṃ rasaḥ | prāṇo hi vā aṅgānāṃ rasaḥ | tasmād yasmāt kasmāc cāṅgāt prāṇa utkrāmati tad eva tac chuṣyati | eṣa hi vā aṅgānāṃ rasaḥ || 19 ||"],"words":[["saḥ ayāsyaḥ āṅgirasaḥ aṅgānāṃ hi rasaḥ","m. nom. + gen. pl. + m. nom.","he (is) Ayāsya Āṅgirasa, for he is the sap of the limbs"],["prāṇaḥ vai aṅgānāṃ rasaḥ","m. nom.","the breath, verily, is the sap of the limbs"],["tasmāt yasmāt kasmāt ca aṅgāt prāṇaḥ utkrāmati","abl. (any) + abl. + 3 sg. pres. of ud-kram","therefore from whatever limb the breath departs"],["tat eva tat śuṣyati","nom. + 3 sg. pres. of śuṣ","that very (limb) dries up"],["eṣaḥ hi vai aṅgānāṃ rasaḥ","m. nom.","for he is indeed the sap of the limbs"]],"lit":"\"He is Ayāsya Āṅgirasa, for he is the sap of the limbs. The breath, verily, is the sap of the limbs — yes, the breath is the sap of the limbs. Therefore from whatever limb the breath departs, that very limb dries up; for he is indeed the sap of the limbs.\"","para":"The seer-name of 1.3.8 is now decoded: <em>Āṅgirasa</em> = <em>aṅga-rasa</em>, and that <em>rasa</em> (vital sap) <strong>is the breath</strong>. Empirical proof: a limb the breath has left <strong>withers</strong>. Life is breath; breath is the moisture/vitality of the body.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","a direct demonstration (<em>dṛṣṭānta</em>) that <em>prāṇa = jīvana-rasa</em>; the withering of a breath-deprived limb confirms that breath, not flesh, is the principle of life."],["Sureśvara","the repetition (<em>prāṇo vā… prāṇo hi vā</em>) is emphatic confirmation; the breath is the unifying vitality that the meditation appropriates."],["Krishnananda","\"Where the prāṇa is not, there is death and decay\" — a meditation on the felt presence of life-energy as the ground of one's own aliveness."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","basis for prāṇic healing/vitality practices — direct attention to the life-current as the body's animating sap."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the empirical-observational style of Vedic <em>nirvacana</em>: the etymology is \"proved\" by a physiological fact."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 1.3.20–1.3.21","title":"The breath as Bṛhaspati and Brahmaṇaspati","sa":["1.3.20: एष उ एव बृहस्पतिः । वाग्वै बृहती । तस्या एष पतिस्तस्मादु बृहस्पतिः ॥ २० ॥","1.3.21: एष उ एव ब्रह्मणस्पतिः । वाग्वै ब्रह्म । तस्या एष पतिस्तस्मादु ब्रह्मणस्पतिः ॥ २१ ॥"],"iast":["eṣa u eva bṛhaspatiḥ | vāg vai bṛhatī | tasyā eṣa patis tasmād u bṛhaspatiḥ || 20 ||","eṣa u eva brahmaṇaspatiḥ | vāg vai brahma | tasyā eṣa patis tasmād u brahmaṇaspatiḥ || 21 ||"],"words":[["eṣaḥ u eva bṛhaspatiḥ","m. nom.","this (breath) is verily Bṛhaspati"],["vāk vai bṛhatī","f. nom.","speech is the bṛhatī (the great one / the Bṛhatī metre)"],["tasyāḥ eṣaḥ patiḥ","f. gen. + m. nom.","of her this (breath) is the lord (pati)"],["tasmāt u bṛhaspatiḥ","abl.","therefore (he is) Bṛhas-pati"],["eṣaḥ u eva brahmaṇaspatiḥ","m. nom.","this (breath) is verily Brahmaṇaspati"],["vāk vai brahma","f. nom. + n.","speech is brahman (the sacred word)"],["tasyāḥ eṣaḥ patiḥ tasmāt u brahmaṇaspatiḥ","f. gen. + m. nom.","of her this is the lord; therefore Brahmaṇas-pati"]],"lit":"\"This (breath) is verily Bṛhaspati: speech is <em>bṛhatī</em> (the great/the Bṛhatī verse), and of her this breath is the lord — therefore Bṛhaspati. This (breath) is verily Brahmaṇaspati: speech is <em>brahman</em> (the holy word), and of her this breath is the lord — therefore Brahmaṇaspati.\"","para":"Two more divine identifications of the breath, both built on the same logic: <strong>speech</strong> (vāc) is <em>bṛhatī</em> / <em>brahman</em>, and the breath is its <strong>lord</strong> (<em>pati</em>) — for speech cannot function without breath driving it. Hence the breath is the deity <strong>Bṛhaspati</strong> (\"Lord of the Great\") and <strong>Brahmaṇaspati</strong> (\"Lord of the Sacred Word\").","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the breath's lordship over speech grounds its identity with these Vedic deities of eloquence and prayer; without prāṇa, <em>vāc</em> is mute — so the priestly powers of speech are really powers of breath."],["Sureśvara","the <em>pati</em> (lord) relation establishes a hierarchy: breath &gt; speech; the meditator worships the <em>governing</em> principle, not the governed faculty."],["Krishnananda","breath is the silent master behind every word spoken in prayer or teaching; to meditate on Bṛhaspati is to meditate on the life that empowers utterance."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","for the ritualist, recognising the <em>purohita</em> deity Bṛhaspati <em>as one's own breath</em> internalises the priest within."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the two names as Vedic deities of priestly speech; the BU absorbs them into its prāṇa-theology by the <em>vāc/pati</em> etymology."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.22","title":"The breath as the Sāman; equal to all","sa":["एष उ एव साम । वाग्वै सामैष सा चामश्चेति तत्साम्नः सामत्वम् । यद्वेव समः प्लुषिणा समो मशकेन समो नागेन सम एभिस्त्रिभिर्लोकैः समोऽनेन सर्वेण तस्माद्वेव साम । अश्नुते साम्नः सायुज्यं सलोकतां य एवमेतत्साम वेद ॥ २२ ॥"],"iast":["eṣa u eva sāma | vāg vai sāmaiṣa sā cāmaś ceti tat sāmnaḥ sāmatvam | yad v eva samaḥ pluṣiṇā samo maśakena samo nāgena sama ebhis tribhir lokaiḥ samo 'nena sarveṇa tasmād v eva sāma | aśnute sāmnaḥ sāyujyaṃ salokatāṃ ya evam etat sāma veda || 22 ||"],"words":[["eṣaḥ u eva sāma","n. nom.","this (breath) is verily the Sāman"],["vāk vai sāma","f. nom. + n.","speech is sā, (the breath is) ama"],["eṣaḥ sā ca amaḥ ca iti","(sā = she/speech) + (ama = he/breath)","\"she (sā) and he (ama)\" —"],["tat sāmnaḥ sāma-tvam","gen. + n. nom.","that is the sāman-hood of the Sāman (sā + ama = sāma)"],["yat u eva samaḥ pluṣiṇā","rel. + nom. + instr.","and because (it is) equal to a (tiny) gnat"],["samaḥ maśakena samaḥ nāgena","instr.","equal to a mosquito, equal to an elephant"],["samaḥ ebhiḥ tribhiḥ lokaiḥ","instr. pl.","equal to these three worlds"],["samaḥ anena sarveṇa","instr.","equal to this whole (universe)"],["tasmāt u eva sāma","abl.","therefore (it is) sāma (= sama, \"equal\")"],["aśnute sāmnaḥ sāyujyaṃ salokatām","3 sg. pres. of aś + gen. + acc.","he attains union-with and the same-world-as the Sāman"],["yaḥ evam etat sāma veda","pf. of vid","who thus knows this Sāman"]],"lit":"\"This (breath) is verily the Sāman. Speech is <em>sā</em> and (the breath is) <em>ama</em> — 'sā-and-ama'; that is the <em>sāman</em>-hood of the Sāman. And because it is equal (<em>sama</em>) to a gnat, equal to a mosquito, equal to an elephant, equal to these three worlds, equal to this whole universe — therefore it is <em>sāma</em>. He who thus knows this Sāman attains union with, and the same world as, the Sāman.\"","para":"A double etymology of <strong>sāman</strong>: (1) <em>sā</em> (feminine = speech) + <em>ama</em> (masculine = breath) = the cosmic <em>mithuna</em>, the union of speech and breath that <em>is</em> the chant; (2) <em>sāma</em> ≈ <em>sama</em> (\"equal/same\") — the breath is the <strong>same</strong> in the gnat, the mosquito, the elephant, the three worlds, the entire cosmos: one identical life-principle present equally in the smallest and the largest. To know this is to merge with it (<em>sāyujya, salokatā</em>).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>sā+ama</em> etymology makes the Sāman the <strong>union of speech and breath</strong>; the <em>sama</em> (equality) etymology teaches the breath's <strong>non-difference across all scales of being</strong> — one prāṇa in gnat and cosmos alike. This equality is a near-statement of the Self's all-pervading sameness."],["Sureśvara","the \"equal in gnat and elephant\" is the doctrinal seed of <em>sama-darśana</em> (equal vision); the one life is undivided by the size or status of its vessel."],["Krishnananda","\"The same life throbs in the ant and in the elephant\" — the basis of universal compassion and of the perception of the one Self in all. The Sāman is the music of that single life."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","meditate on the breath as identical in all creatures to dissolve the sense of separateness; the chant (<em>sāman</em>) is harmony precisely because it is <em>sameness</em> sounded."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the two competing <em>nirvacanas</em> (sā+ama; sāma=sama) offered side by side — characteristic of the genre; Olivelle: the \"equality\" theme anticipates the monistic identifications of later chapters."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.23","title":"The breath as the Udgītha","sa":["एष उ वा उद्गीथः । प्राणो वा उत्प्राणेन हीदं सर्वमुत्तब्धम् । वागेव गीथा । उच्च गीथा चेति । स उद्गीथः ॥ २३ ॥"],"iast":["eṣa u vā udgīthaḥ | prāṇo vā ut prāṇena hīdaṃ sarvam uttabdham | vāg eva gīthā | uc ca gīthā ceti | sa udgīthaḥ || 23 ||"],"words":[["eṣaḥ u vai udgīthaḥ","m. nom.","this (breath) is verily the Udgītha"],["prāṇaḥ vai ut","m. nom. + (syllable) ut","the breath is ut (\"up\")"],["prāṇena hi idaṃ sarvam uttabdham","instr. + n. nom. + pp. of ud-stabh","for by the breath all this is held up (sustained)"],["vāk eva gīthā","f. nom.","speech is gīthā (\"song/chant\")"],["ut ca gīthā ca iti","","\"ut and gīthā\" —"],["saḥ udgīthaḥ","m. nom.","that (is) the Udgītha"]],"lit":"\"This (breath) is verily the Udgītha. The breath is <em>ut</em> ('up'), for by the breath all this (universe) is held up; and speech is <em>gīthā</em> ('the song'). '<em>Ut</em> + <em>gīthā</em>' — that is the Udgītha.\"","para":"Now the very term that opened the brāhmaṇa (1.3.1) is decoded: <strong>udgītha = ut + gīthā</strong> = \"(the breath that holds) up\" + \"the song (speech).\" The breath is <em>ut</em> because it <strong>upholds the whole cosmos</strong> (<em>uttabdham</em>); speech is the <em>gīthā</em>, the sung melody. The Udgītha is thus the breath-upheld song — exactly the breath that won the gods' victory.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the etymology completes the frame: the incorruptible breath of 1.3.7 <em>is</em> the Udgītha by which the gods conquered — <em>ut</em> (the sustaining lifter of the worlds) joined to <em>gīthā</em> (speech). The meditation has come full circle."],["Sureśvara","the breath as <em>ut</em> (universal support) is a cosmological elevation of prāṇa to the role of world-pillar."],["Krishnananda","\"All this is held up by the breath\" — withdraw the cosmic prāṇa and the universe collapses; the Udgītha-meditation is on the breath as the upholding axis of all."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","in chanting OM as udgītha, know that the <em>ut</em> is your own sustaining breath and the <em>gīthā</em> your own voice — the macro-rite performed within."],["Deussen/Olivelle","compares the Chāndogya's elaborate udgītha/OM meditation; BU compresses it into the prāṇa-vāc etymology."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.24","title":"The anecdote of Brahmadatta","sa":["तद्धापि ब्रह्मदत्तश्चैकितानेयो राजानं भक्षयन्नुवाच । अयं त्यस्य राजा मूर्धानं विपातयताद्यदितोऽयास्य आङ्गिरसोऽन्येनोदगायदिति । वाचा च ह्येव स प्राणेन चोदगायदिति ॥ २४ ॥"],"iast":["tad dhāpi brahmadattaś caikitāneyo rājānaṃ bhakṣayann uvāca | ayaṃ tyasya rājā mūrdhānaṃ vipātayatād yad ito 'yāsya āṅgiraso 'nyenodagāyad iti | vācā ca hy eva sa prāṇena codagāyad iti || 24 ||"],"words":[["tat ha api brahmadattaḥ caikitāneyaḥ","m. nom. (patronymic)","concerning that, Brahmadatta, son of Cikitāna,"],["rājānaṃ bhakṣayan uvāca","acc. + pres.part. + 3 sg. pf. of vac","while drinking the rājā (soma-king) said"],["ayaṃ tyasya rājā mūrdhānaṃ vipātayatāt","nom. + gen. + acc. + 3 sg. impv. (precative) of vi-pat (caus.)","\"may this (soma-)king make that man's head fall off,"],["yat itaḥ ayāsyaḥ āṅgirasaḥ anyena udagāyat iti","rel. + abl. + instr. + impf.","(whoever says) that Ayāsya Āṅgirasa sang the udgītha by anything other than this (breath)\""],["vācā ca hi eva saḥ prāṇena ca udagāyat iti","instr. + instr.","for he sang only by speech and by breath"]],"lit":"\"Concerning this, Brahmadatta the son of Cikitāna, while drinking the (soma-)king, said: 'May this (soma-)king make that man's head fall off, whoever should say that Ayāsya Āṅgirasa sang the udgītha by any other (organ) than this (breath)! For he sang only by speech and by breath.'\"","para":"A confirming anecdote: a soma-drinking sage <strong>swears an oath</strong> (invoking the soma-king to behead any contradictor) that the udgītha was sung by <strong>nothing but breath and speech</strong> — i.e., the foregoing identification (Udgītha = breath/prāṇa joined to vāc) is authoritative tradition, not the author's novelty.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the vehement oath functions as a <em>pramāṇa</em>-citation — an attested teacher's emphatic endorsement that the udgītha-organ is the breath (with speech), foreclosing any claim that some other organ did the chanting."],["Sureśvara","the appeal to a named authority shows the teaching as <em>paramparā</em> (lineage-transmitted), not private speculation."],["Krishnananda","a colourful reminder that for the Vedic seers this was <em>settled knowledge</em>, defended with conviction; the breath-speech pair is the irreducible core of the chant."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the ritual setting (soma-drinking) and the rhetorical force; an instance of the Upaniṣad citing dialogue to authenticate doctrine."],["Deussen/Olivelle","an interesting glimpse of named historical teachers and the oral-disputation culture; Olivelle treats such anecdotes as windows into the social world of the texts."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 1.3.25–1.3.27","title":"The Sāman's own (svara), gold (suvarṇa), and support (pratiṣṭhā)","sa":["1.3.25 (svara — tone / one's \"own\"): तस्य हैतस्य साम्नो यः स्वं वेद भवति हास्य स्वम् । तस्य वै स्वर एव स्वम् । तस्मादार्त्विज्यं करिष्यन्वाचि स्वरमिच्छेत । तया वाचा स्वरसम्पन्नयार्त्विज्यं कुर्यात् । तस्माद्यज्ञे स्वरवन्तं दिदृक्षन्त एव । अथो यस्य स्वं भवति । भवति हास्य स्वं य एवमेतत्साम्नः स्वं वेद ॥ २५ ॥","1.3.26 (suvarṇa — gold): तस्य हैतस्य साम्नो यः सुवर्णं वेद भवति हास्य सुवर्णम् । तस्य वै स्वर एव सुवर्णम् । भवति हास्य सुवर्णं य एवमेतत्साम्नः सुवर्णं वेद ॥ २६ ॥","1.3.27 (pratiṣṭhā — support): तस्य हैतस्य साम्नो यः प्रतिष्ठां वेद प्रति ह तिष्ठति । तस्य वै वागेव प्रतिष्ठा । वाचि हि खल्वेष एतत्प्राणः प्रतिष्ठितो गीयते । अन्न इत्यु हैक आहुः ॥ २७ ॥"],"iast":["tasya haitasya sāmno yaḥ svaṃ veda bhavati hāsya svam | tasya vai svara eva svam | tasmād ārtvijyaṃ kariṣyan vāci svaram iccheta | tayā vācā svarasampannayārtvijyaṃ kuryāt | tasmād yajñe svaravantaṃ didṛkṣanta eva | atho yasya svaṃ bhavati | bhavati hāsya svaṃ ya evam etat sāmnaḥ svaṃ veda || 25 ||","tasya haitasya sāmno yaḥ suvarṇaṃ veda bhavati hāsya suvarṇam | tasya vai svara eva suvarṇam | bhavati hāsya suvarṇaṃ ya evam etat sāmnaḥ suvarṇaṃ veda || 26 ||","tasya haitasya sāmno yaḥ pratiṣṭhāṃ veda prati ha tiṣṭhati | tasya vai vāg eva pratiṣṭhā | vāci hi khalv eṣa etat prāṇaḥ pratiṣṭhito gīyate | anna ity u haika āhuḥ || 27 ||"],"words":[["tasya ha etasya sāmnaḥ","m. gen. sg.","of this Sāman"],["yaḥ svaṃ veda bhavati ha asya svam","rel. + n. acc. + pf. + nom.","he who knows its sva (own/wealth) — his own/wealth comes to be"],["tasya vai svaraḥ eva svam","m. nom.","its sva is precisely the tone (svara)"],["tasmāt ārtvijyaṃ kariṣyan vāci svaram iccheta","n. acc. + fut.part. + loc. + opt. of iṣ","therefore one about to officiate should desire tone in his voice"],["tayā vācā svara-sampannayā ārtvijyaṃ kuryāt","instr. + opt. of kṛ","with that tone-endowed voice he should do the priestly office"],["tasmāt yajñe svaravantaṃ didṛkṣante eva","loc. + acc. + desid. pres.","hence at a sacrifice they wish to see a (priest) possessed of fine tone"],["yaḥ suvarṇaṃ veda … tasya vai svaraḥ eva suvarṇam","acc. + nom.","who knows its gold — its gold is precisely the tone"],["yaḥ pratiṣṭhāṃ veda prati ha tiṣṭhati","acc. + 3 sg. pres. of prati-sthā","who knows its support — he gains a footing"],["tasya vai vāk eva pratiṣṭhā","f. nom.","its support is precisely speech"],["vāci hi khalu eṣaḥ etat prāṇaḥ pratiṣṭhitaḥ gīyate","loc. + nom. + pp. + 3 sg. pres. pass. of gai","for resting in speech this breath is (here) sung"],["annaḥ iti u ha eke āhuḥ","m. nom. + 3 pl. pf. of ah","\"food,\" some say (it rests on food)"]],"lit":"\"Of this Sāman, he who knows its <em>sva</em> (own-wealth) — wealth comes to be his; and its <em>sva</em> is precisely the <strong>tone</strong> (<em>svara</em>). Therefore one who is about to officiate should desire (good) tone in his voice, and with that tone-endowed voice perform the priestly office; hence at a sacrifice people wish to see a priest of fine tone. — Of this Sāman, he who knows its <strong>gold</strong> (<em>suvarṇa</em>) — gold comes to be his; and its gold is precisely the tone. — Of this Sāman, he who knows its <strong>support</strong> (<em>pratiṣṭhā</em>) — he gains a firm footing; and its support is precisely <strong>speech</strong>, for it is resting in speech that this breath is sung. (Some say its support is <strong>food</strong>.)\"","para":"Three \"hidden riches\" of the Sāman, all turning on puns. (1) Its <em>sva</em> (\"own / property\") is <em>svara</em> (\"tone\") — so cultivate vocal tone; the knower gains property. (2) Its <em>suvarṇa</em> (\"gold\") is again <em>svara</em> — the knower gains gold. (3) Its <em>pratiṣṭhā</em> (\"foundation\") is <em>vāc</em> (speech), since the breath-as-chant <em>rests on</em> speech to be sung (others say it rests on <em>anna</em>, food). These yield concrete worldly fruits (wealth, gold, stability) alongside the ritual instruction to chant with a fine voice.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","these are <em>saguṇa</em> meditations with material <em>phala</em>; the puns (<em>sva/svara</em>, <em>suvarṇa/svara</em>) license the worldly results. The \"support = speech (or food)\" closes the prāṇa-vāc-anna triad that structures the chapter."],["Sureśvara","the riches are \"hidden\" in the chant for the one who penetrates the symbol; tone is the audible <em>body</em> of the breath."],["Krishnananda","practical liturgics — the <em>quality</em> of the chant (its <em>svara</em>) is itself a spiritual value; beauty of utterance is not incidental but the Sāman's \"gold.\""],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","direct ritual advice for the <em>ārtvijya</em> (priestly office): cultivate tone; the meditation sanctifies the aesthetic of chanting."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the cluster of wealth-yielding meditations; Olivelle: the alternative \"food\" reading preserved (\"some say\") shows the text's awareness of variant traditions."]],"notes":["<em>Three short meditations on hidden treasures within the Sāman/breath, each with the formula \"he who knows X of this Sāman obtains X\":</em>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3.28","title":"The Pavamāna ascent: \"Lead me from the unreal to the real\"","sa":["अथातः पवमानानामेवाभ्यारोहः । स वै खलु प्रस्तोता साम प्रस्तौति । स यत्र प्रस्तुयात्तदेतानि जपेत् । असतो मा सद्गमय । तमसो मा ज्योतिर्गमय । मृत्योर्मामृतं गमयेति । स यदाहासतो मा सद्गमयेति । मृत्युर्वा असत्सदमृतं मृत्योर्मामृतं गमयामृतं मा कुर्वित्येवैतदाह । तमसो मा ज्योतिर्गमयेति । मृत्युर्वै तमो ज्योतिरमृतं मृत्योर्मामृतं गमयामृतं मा कुर्वित्येवैतदाह । मृत्योर्मामृतं गमयेति । नात्र तिरोहितमिवास्ति । अथ यानीतराणि स्तोत्राणि तेष्वात्मनेऽन्नाद्यमागायेत् । तस्मादु तेषु वरं वृणीत । यं कामं कामयेत्तम् । स एष एवंविदुद्गातात्मने वा यजमानाय वा यं कामं कामयते तमागायति । तद्धैतल्लोकजिदेव । न हैवालोक्यताया आशास्ति य एवमेतत्साम वेद ॥ २८ ॥"],"iast":["athātaḥ pavamānānām evābhyārohaḥ | sa vai khalu prastotā sāma prastauti | sa yatra prastuyāt tad etāni japet | asato mā sad gamaya | tamaso mā jyotir gamaya | mṛtyor māmṛtaṃ gamayeti | sa yad āhāsato mā sad gamayeti | mṛtyur vā asat sad amṛtaṃ mṛtyor māmṛtaṃ gamayāmṛtaṃ mā kurv ity evaitad āha | tamaso mā jyotir gamayeti | mṛtyur vai tamo jyotir amṛtaṃ mṛtyor māmṛtaṃ gamayāmṛtaṃ mā kurv ity evaitad āha | mṛtyor māmṛtaṃ gamayeti | nātra tirohitam ivāsti | atha yānītarāṇi stotrāṇi teṣv ātmane 'nnādyam āgāyet | tasmād u teṣu varaṃ vṛṇīta | yaṃ kāmaṃ kāmayet tam | sa eṣa evaṃvid udgātātmane vā yajamānāya vā yaṃ kāmaṃ kāmayate tam āgāyati | tad dhaital lokajid eva | na haivālokyatāyā āśāsti ya evam etat sāma veda || 28 ||"],"words":[["atha ataḥ pavamānānām eva abhyārohaḥ","gen. pl. + m. nom.","now, next, (is) the \"ascent\" (abhyāroha) pertaining to the Pavamāna(-chants)"],["saḥ vai khalu prastotā sāma prastauti","m. nom. + n. acc. + 3 sg. pres. of pra-stu","the Prastotṛ priest sings the introductory praise (prastāva) of the Sāman"],["saḥ yatra prastuyāt tat etāni japet","rel. + opt. + acc. pl. + 3 sg. opt. of jap","when he sings the prastāva, then (the sacrificer) should softly recite these (mantras):"],["asataḥ mā sat gamaya","abl. + acc. (mā = me) + acc. + impv. of gam (caus.)","\"from the unreal lead me to the real"],["tamasaḥ mā jyotiḥ gamaya","abl. + acc. + impv.","from darkness lead me to light"],["mṛtyoḥ mā amṛtaṃ gamaya iti","abl. + acc. + impv.","from death lead me to immortality\""],["saḥ yat āha asataḥ mā sat gamaya iti","","when he says \"from the unreal to the real,\""],["mṛtyuḥ vai asat sat amṛtaṃ","nom.","death is the unreal, the real is the immortal"],["mṛtyoḥ mā amṛtaṃ gamaya amṛtaṃ mā kuru iti eva etat āha","impv. of kṛ","\"lead me from death to the immortal; make me immortal\" — that is what he says"],["tamasaḥ mā jyotiḥ gamaya iti","","\"from darkness to light\":"],["mṛtyuḥ vai tamaḥ jyotiḥ amṛtaṃ","nom.","death is darkness, the immortal is light"],["na atra tirohitam iva asti","+ n. nom. + pres.","here (in the third line) there is nothing hidden/obscure"],["atha yāni itarāṇi stotrāṇi teṣu ātmane annādyam āgāyet","loc. pl. + dat. + acc. + opt. of ā-gai","now, in the other stotras, he should chant for food for himself"],["tasmāt u teṣu varaṃ vṛṇīta","abl. + loc. + acc. + opt. of vṛ","therefore in them he should choose a boon"],["yaṃ kāmaṃ kāmayet tam","rel. acc. + opt. of kam + acc.","whatever desire he may desire, that (he may seek)"],["saḥ eṣaḥ evaṃvid udgātā","m. nom.","this udgātṛ-priest who knows thus"],["ātmane vā yajamānāya vā yaṃ kāmaṃ kāmayate tam āgāyati","dat. + dat. + acc. + pres.","whatever desire he desires, for himself or for the sacrificer, that he wins by chanting"],["tat ha etat lokajit eva","n. nom.","this (meditation) is verily a \"world-conqueror\""],["na ha eva alokyatāyāḥ āśā asti","abl./gen. + f. nom.","there is no fear of worldlessness (of failing to win the worlds)"],["yaḥ evam etat sāma veda","pf. of vid","for the one who thus knows this Sāman"]],"lit":"\"Now, next, the 'ascent' belonging to the Pavamāna chants. The Prastotṛ priest intones the prastāva of the Sāman; while he intones it, the sacrificer should recite these (verses): <strong>'From the unreal lead me to the real; from darkness lead me to light; from death lead me to immortality.'</strong> When he says 'from the unreal lead me to the real' — death is the unreal, the real is the immortal; so he is saying 'from death lead me to the immortal; make me immortal.' 'From darkness lead me to light' — death is the darkness, the immortal is the light; so he says 'from death to the immortal; make me immortal.' 'From death lead me to immortality' — here there is nothing hidden (the meaning is plain). Then, in the other stotras, he may chant to win food for himself; therefore in them he should choose a boon, whatever desire he desires. This knowing udgātṛ-priest wins by his chant whatever desire he desires, for himself or for the sacrificer. This (meditation) is verily a winner of worlds; for the one who knows this Sāman there is no fear of failing to win the worlds.\"","para":"The brāhmaṇa culminates in the <strong>Pavamāna-abhyāroha</strong> — the soul's \"ascent\" prayer, recited during the purifying soma-chants. Its three lines are among the most beloved in all scripture: <strong>asato mā sad gamaya / tamaso mā jyotir gamaya / mṛtyor māmṛtaṃ gamaya.</strong> The text then <em>decodes</em> the first two lines as restatements of the third: \"unreal\" and \"darkness\" both <strong>mean death</strong>; \"real,\" \"light,\" and \"immortal\" all <strong>mean deathlessness</strong>. The single petition is: <em>make me immortal</em>. (The third line needs no decoding — its sense is open.) For other (non-Pavamāna) chants, ordinary boons may be sought; but this knowing udgātṛ wins whatever he wishes, and the meditation is a \"world-conqueror\" — the knower never fails of the higher worlds.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the climactic prayer for <em>amṛtatva</em>. Śaṅkara reads <em>asat/tamas/mṛtyu</em> as the realm of ignorance-bondage and <em>sat/jyotis/amṛta</em> as Brahman-knowledge-liberation; the three lines are one prayer to be carried from <em>avidyā</em> (the unreal-dark-mortal) to <em>vidyā</em> (the real-luminous-immortal). The decoding shows the Upaniṣad's own method: the manifold names of bondage all reduce to \"death,\" and of freedom to \"the immortal.\""],["Sureśvara","the identification <em>asat = mṛtyu</em>, <em>sat = amṛta</em> is doctrinally precise — the \"unreal\" is not sheer non-existence but the perishable/mortal; liberation is the move from the perishable to the imperishable Self, which is <em>being itself</em>."],["Krishnananda","a celebrated exposition — the prayer is the cry of the embodied soul for transcendence; \"unreal,\" \"darkness,\" and \"death\" name the same predicament (limitation), and the prayer is for the <em>one</em> thing under three descriptions: the immortal Real-Light. He stresses it is <em>prārthanā</em> offered while the divine chant lifts the consciousness upward (<em>abhyāroha</em>, \"the climbing-up\")."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the universal <em>sādhaka's</em> prayer; Sivananda uses it as a daily invocation, reading each clause as a stage of practice — truth over falsehood (ethics), light over darkness (knowledge), immortality over death (realisation)."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the prayer's later fame and liturgical life; Olivelle observes the text's self-glossing (<em>mṛtyur vā asat…</em>) as an unusually explicit interpretive key, and that the third line is declared to have \"nothing hidden\" — a rare moment of the Upaniṣad pausing to say a line is transparent."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This brāhmaṇa is the <strong>charter of <em>prāṇa-vidyā</strong></em> (the knowledge of breath) and one of the Upaniṣad's most carefully built arguments. Its logic:","1. <strong>The senses cannot defeat evil.</strong> Each sense-organ (1.3.2–6) is <em>double-edged</em> — capable of proper (<em>pratirūpa</em>) and improper (<em>apratirūpa</em>) use — so evil (<em>pāpman</em>) can always \"pierce\" it. Sense-discipline alone is necessary but never sufficient; the demons keep getting in.","2. <strong>Only the central breath is incorruptible.</strong> The <em>mukhya-prāṇa</em> (1.3.7) does no <em>dual</em> work — it simply sustains life — so it is beyond the moral split, and evil \"shatters against it like a clod against a rock.\" This is the chapter's emblem and its decisive philosophical move: the deathless is the <em>non-grasping</em>, <em>non-choosing</em> ground beneath the choosing senses.","3. <strong>The breath redeems and elevates the senses.</strong> It removes their evil-death (1.3.10–11) and carries each across to its <strong>cosmic, immortal form</strong> (1.3.12–16: speech→fire, eye→sun, etc.), revealing the unity of the individual (<em>adhyātma</em>) and the cosmic (<em>adhidaiva</em>).","4. <strong>The breath is the universal eater and support.</strong> All eating is the breath's eating (1.3.17); the senses cluster round it as their nourishing centre (1.3.18); it is the <em>rasa</em> (sap) of the limbs (1.3.19), and the deity behind speech, the Sāman, and the Udgītha (1.3.20–23).","5. <strong>The Sāman teaches <em>sameness</em>.</strong> The breath is <em>equal</em> in gnat and elephant and cosmos (1.3.22) — a near-statement of the one Self in all.","6. <strong>The whole resolves into a prayer for immortality</strong> (1.3.28): <em>asato mā sad gamaya</em> — from the unreal/dark/mortal to the real/luminous/immortal.","Two structural notes: (a) the chapter is the BU twin of <strong>Chāndogya 1.2</strong> (same myth, different emphases — BU foregrounds the breath's <em>incorruptibility</em>); (b) it advances, like 1.2, almost entirely by <em>nirvacana</em> (sacred etymology): <em>āṅgirasa</em>←<em>aṅga-rasa</em>, <em>bṛhaspati</em>, <em>brahmaṇaspati</em>, <em>sāma</em>←<em>sā+ama</em>/<em>sama</em>, <em>udgītha</em>←<em>ut+gīthā</em>, <em>sva</em>/<em>svara</em>/<em>suvarṇa</em>."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE UDGĪTHA CONTEST (BU 1.3)\n   ============================\n   gods (few) vs. demons (many)  --&gt; gods chant the UDGĪTHA to win\n        |\n        | gods ask each ORGAN to &quot;sing up&quot;:\n        v\n   SPEECH  SMELL  EYE  EAR  MIND   &lt;-- each does good, keeps the pleasant,\n     |      |     |    |    |           and is PIERCED BY EVIL (apratirūpa)\n     X      X     X    X    X       &lt;-- all corruptible (double-edged)\n        |\n        v\n   MUKHYA-PRĀṆA (breath in the mouth)  &lt;-- asked to sing\n        |\n   demons rush to pierce it  ---&gt;  || ROCK ||  &lt;-- clod shatters on rock\n        |                          (breath does no good-or-evil; incorruptible)\n        v\n   demons VINEŚUḤ (perish), gods prevail\n        |\n        |  the breath then:\n        |   - removes the organs&#x27; evil-death, casts it to the far quarters (10-11)\n        |   - carries each organ BEYOND DEATH into its cosmic form:\n        v\n        speech-&gt;AGNI(blaze) smell-&gt;VĀYU(blow) eye-&gt;SŪRYA(heat)\n        ear-&gt;DIŚAS(quarters) mind-&gt;CANDRA(shine)              (12-16)\n        |\n        |  the breath = eater of all food (17), centre the senses cluster round (18),\n        |  rasa/sap of the limbs (19), = Bṛhaspati / Brahmaṇaspati (20-21),\n        |  = SĀMAN (sā+ama; sama in gnat &amp; elephant alike) (22), = UDGĪTHA (ut+gīthā) (23)\n        v\n   CLIMAX (28):   asato mā sad gamaya      (unreal  -&gt; real )\n                  tamaso mā jyotir gamaya  (darkness-&gt; light)   } all = &quot;make me immortal&quot;\n                  mṛtyor mā amṛtaṃ gamaya  (death   -&gt; deathless)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Stop fighting evil sense by sense.</strong> Notice 1.3.2–7's core lesson: trying to purify each faculty individually is endless — every sense can be \"pierced.\" The shortcut is to drop <em>beneath</em> the senses into the breath.","2. <strong>Rest in the non-choosing breath.</strong> Sit and find the one activity in you that has no \"good or bad version\" — the bare breath. Let attention settle there. It does not prefer; evil \"shatters against it.\" This is the practical core of <em>prāṇa-upāsanā</em> and the spiritual root of <em>prāṇāyāma</em>.","3. <strong>Adhidaiva sense-meditation.</strong> At each sense, contemplate its deathless cosmic form (1.3.12–16): <em>my seeing is the sun's light, my hearing is open space, my mind is the moon's cool shining.</em> Identity lifts off the perishable organ.","4. <strong>Eat as offering to the breath.</strong> Per 1.3.17–18, take a meal consciously as feeding the one life (<em>prāṇāgnihotra</em>); feel how nourishing the centre nourishes the whole organism.","5. <strong>Hear the one life in all (sameness).</strong> From 1.3.22: the same breath in the gnat, the elephant, and yourself. Let this dissolve the felt gap between \"me\" and other beings — the ground of compassion.","6. <strong>Make the ascent-prayer your own.</strong> Recite <em>asato mā sad gamaya / tamaso mā jyotir gamaya / mṛtyor māmṛtaṃ gamaya</em> daily. Per the text's own gloss, all three lines are one petition: <em>carry me from the perishable to the imperishable.</em> Use it as a morning aspiration and an evening surrender.","7. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the deathless is not something you <em>achieve</em> by improving the senses; it is the always-present, non-grasping life-ground you <em>relax into</em> when you stop identifying with the choosing mind."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 1 · Brāhmaṇa 1.4 — Creation from the Self; \"I am Brahman\"","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 1.4","title":"Creation from the Self; \"I am Brahman\"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The most philosophically charged brāhmaṇa of Adhyāya 1. Where 1.2 derived the world from</em> <strong>Death</strong>, <em>1.4 re-tells creation from the</em> <strong>Ātman/Puruṣa</strong> <em>— the Self that was \"alone in the beginning.\" It contains the Upaniṣad's account of the origin of</em> <strong>fear, desire, sexuality, and the social orders (varṇa)</strong>, <em>the great teaching that the Self is dearer than son or wealth (1.4.8), the injunction</em> <strong>ātmety evopāsīta</strong> <em>(\"one should meditate on it as the Self,\" 1.4.7), and — at its summit — the</em> <strong>mahā-vākya</strong> <em>of the Yajurveda:</em> <strong>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</strong>, <em>\"I am Brahman\" (1.4.10).</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.1","title":"The Self alone, in human form, says \"I am\"","sa":["आत्मैवेदमग्र आसीत्पुरुषविधः । सोऽनुवीक्ष्य नान्यदात्मनोऽपश्यत् । सोऽहमस्मीत्यग्रे व्याहरत् । ततोऽहंनामाभवत् । तस्मादप्येतर्ह्यामन्त्रितोऽहमयमित्येवाग्र उक्त्वाथान्यन्नाम प्रब्रूते यदस्य भवति । स यत्पूर्वोऽस्मात्सर्वस्मात्सर्वान्पाप्मन औषत्तस्मात्पुरुषः । ओषति ह वै स तं योऽस्मात्पूर्वो बुभूषति य एवं वेद ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["ātmaivedam agra āsīt puruṣavidhaḥ | so 'nuvīkṣya nānyad ātmano 'paśyat | so 'ham asmīty agre vyāharat | tato 'haṃnāmābhavat | tasmād apy etarhy āmantrito 'ham ayam ity evāgra uktvāthānyan nāma prabrūte yad asya bhavati | sa yat pūrvo 'smāt sarvasmāt sarvān pāpmana auṣat tasmāt puruṣaḥ | oṣati ha vai sa taṃ yo 'smāt pūrvo bubhūṣati ya evaṃ veda || 1 ||"],"words":[["ātmā eva idam agre āsīt puruṣa-vidhaḥ","m. nom. + n. nom. + loc. (adv.) + impf. + adj.","the Self alone was this (all) in the beginning, in the shape of a person (puruṣa)"],["saḥ anuvīkṣya na anyat ātmanaḥ apaśyat","nom. + ger. of anu-vi-īkṣ + abl. + 3 sg. impf. of paś","he, looking around, saw nothing else than himself"],["saḥ aham asmi iti agre vyāharat","nom. + 1 sg. pres. + adv. + 3 sg. impf. of vi-ā-hṛ","he first uttered \"I am\""],["tataḥ aham-nāma abhavat","abl. + n. nom.","thence arose the name \"I\" (aham)"],["tasmāt api etarhi āmantritaḥ","abl. + adv. + pp.","therefore even now, when addressed/called"],["aham ayam iti eva agre uktvā","+ ger. of vac","one first says just \"It is I\""],["atha anyat nāma prabrūte yat asya bhavati","+ 3 sg. pres. of pra-brū + rel.","and then tells the other name that he has"],["saḥ yat pūrvaḥ asmāt sarvasmāt","rel. + abl.","since he, prior to all this,"],["sarvān pāpmanaḥ auṣat tasmāt puruṣaḥ","acc. pl. + 3 sg. impf. of uṣ (burn)","burned up all evils, therefore (he is) puruṣa (≈ pūrva-uṣ, \"first-burner\")"],["oṣati ha vai saḥ taṃ yaḥ asmāt pūrvaḥ bubhūṣati","3 sg. pres. + acc. + rel. + desid. of bhū","he burns up the one who wishes to be before him"],["yaḥ evaṃ veda","pf. of vid","who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"In the beginning this (universe) was the Self alone, in the form of a Person. Looking around, he saw nothing other than himself. He first said, 'I am' (<em>so 'ham asmi</em>); thence arose the name 'I.' Therefore even now, when a person is addressed, he first says 'It is I,' and then tells whatever other name he has. Because, prior to all this, he burned up (<em>uṣ</em>) all evils, therefore he is <em>puruṣa</em>. He who knows this burns up anyone who would seek to be before him.\"","para":"Reality \"in the beginning\" is a single Self in <em>human form</em> (the cosmic Person, <em>Puruṣa</em>). Utterly alone, it makes the first utterance: <strong>\"I am.\"</strong> This is the birth of self-reference, the \"I\"-name that still underlies every self-introduction. A double etymology of <em>puruṣa</em>: he who <strong>burned away (uṣ)</strong> all evil <strong>first (pūrva)</strong>. The <em>phala</em>: the knower \"burns up\" rivals.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"Self in the form of a person\" = the cosmic <em>Hiraṇyagarbha/Virāj</em>, the first embodied I-sense (<em>ahaṃkāra</em>) — not yet the attributeless Brahman, but its first determinate form. \"I am\" is the primordial <em>ahaṃ-pratyaya</em> from which all individuality unfolds. The whole brāhmaṇa will move from this <em>saguṇa</em> \"I am (a person)\" to the <em>nirguṇa</em> \"I am Brahman\" of 1.4.10."],["Sureśvara","the \"I am\" (<em>aham asmi</em>) is the irreducible self-evidence of the Self — never doubted, presupposed by every other cognition; the entire teaching rests on this self-luminous <em>aham</em>."],["Krishnananda","a profound meditation on the <em>aloneness</em> of pure being — there is no \"second\" to perceive; self-consciousness (\"I am\") is the first event, and with it, implicitly, the seed of all later duality."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the practical etymology — the Self/Person is the <em>burner of evil</em>; meditation on \"I am the Puruṣa\" consumes the <em>pāpman</em> that the senses (1.3) could not escape."],["Deussen/Olivelle","compare the cosmic Puruṣa of RV 10.90; Olivelle notes BU's distinctive move of grounding creation in <em>self-consciousness</em> (\"I am\") rather than in a substance — an early idealist turn."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.2","title":"Fear arises from a second","sa":["सोऽबिभेत्तस्मादेकाकी बिभेति । स हायमीक्षांचक्रे यन्मदन्यन्नास्ति कस्मान्नु बिभेमीति । तत एवास्य भयं वीयाय । कस्माद्ध्यभेष्यत् । द्वितीयाद्वै भयं भवति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["so 'bibhet tasmād ekākī bibheti | sa hāyam īkṣāṃ cakre, yan mad anyan nāsti kasmān nu bibhemīti | tata evāsya bhayaṃ vīyāya | kasmād dhy abheṣyat | dvitīyād vai bhayaṃ bhavati || 2 ||"],"words":[["saḥ abibhet","3 sg. impf. of bhī","he was afraid"],["tasmāt ekākī bibheti","abl. + m. nom. + pres.","therefore a person alone is afraid"],["saḥ ha ayam īkṣāṃ cakre","periphr. pf. of īkṣ","he reflected"],["yat mat anyat na asti kasmāt nu bibhemi iti","abl. + 1 sg. pres. of bhī","\"since there is nothing other than me, of what should I be afraid?\""],["tataḥ eva asya bhayaṃ vīyāya","abl. + gen. + n. nom. + 3 sg. pf. of vi-i","thereupon his fear departed"],["kasmāt hi abheṣyat","abl. + 3 sg. cond. of bhī","for of what could he have been afraid?"],["dvitīyāt vai bhayaṃ bhavati","abl. + n. nom.","fear arises (only) from a second"]],"lit":"\"He was afraid; therefore one who is alone is afraid. He reflected: 'Since there is nothing other than me, of what am I afraid?' Thereupon his fear departed; for of what should he have been afraid? It is from a second (other) that fear arises.\"","para":"The lone Self first feels <strong>fear</strong> — the primal anxiety of the solitary. But on realising <em>there is no second</em> (nothing other than itself to fear), the fear dissolves. The principle: <strong>fear is born of duality</strong> (<em>dvitīyād vai bhayaṃ bhavati</em>). Where there is genuinely no \"other,\" there is no fear.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","a key Advaita proof-text — <em>bhaya</em> (fear) is structurally dependent on a second; therefore the non-dual Self is by nature <em>fearless</em> (<em>abhaya</em>). All fear is mis-knowledge of a second where there is none. (Cf. TaittU 2.7: \"where one sees a second, there is fear.\")"],["Sureśvara","the removal of fear \"by knowledge that there is no second\" models the entire path: <em>bhaya</em> belongs to <em>avidyā</em> (seeing duality); <em>abhaya</em> is the natural state of the realised non-dual Self."],["Krishnananda","the deepest analysis of anxiety in world literature — all fear traces to the sense of an <em>other</em> that could threaten the self. Realisation of non-duality is the only final cure for fear; psychological security is ultimately metaphysical."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical — the antidote to fear is not managing the feared object but dissolving the felt <em>otherness</em>; the meditation \"there is no second\" is a direct remedy."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the striking psychological realism; Olivelle reads the \"loneliness then fearlessness\" as the text dramatising the logical point that relational predicates (fear) require relata."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.3","title":"The Self splits into husband and wife; the origin of pairing","sa":["स वै नैव रेमे । तस्मादेकाकी न रमते । स द्वितीयमैच्छत् । स हैतावानास यथा स्त्रीपुमांसौ सम्परिष्वक्तौ । स इममेवात्मानं द्वेधापातयत् । ततः पतिश्च पत्नी चाभवताम् । तस्मादिदमर्धबृगलमिव स्व इति ह स्माह याज्ञवल्क्यः । तस्मादयमाकाशः स्त्रिया पूर्यत एव । तां समभवत् । ततो मनुष्या अजायन्त ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["sa vai naiva reme | tasmād ekākī na ramate | sa dvitīyam aicchat | sa haitāvān āsa yathā strīpumāṃsau sampariṣvaktau | sa imam evātmānaṃ dvedhāpātayat | tataḥ patiś ca patnī cābhavatām | tasmād idam ardhabṛgalam iva sva iti ha smāha yājñavalkyaḥ | tasmād ayam ākāśaḥ striyā pūryata eva | tāṃ samabhavat | tato manuṣyā ajāyanta || 3 ||"],"words":[["saḥ vai na eva reme","3 sg. impf. of ram","he found no delight"],["tasmāt ekākī na ramate","m. nom. + pres.","therefore one alone finds no delight"],["saḥ dvitīyam aicchat","acc. + 3 sg. impf. of iṣ","he desired a second"],["saḥ ha etāvān āsa","nom. + adj. + pf. of as","he became as large"],["yathā strī-pumāṃsau sampariṣvaktau","nom. du. + pp. nom. du.","as a woman and a man closely embracing"],["saḥ imam eva ātmānaṃ dvedhā apātayat","acc. + adv. + 3 sg. impf. caus. of pat","he caused this very self to fall into two"],["tataḥ patiḥ ca patnī ca abhavatām","m./f. nom. + 3 du. impf.","thence arose husband and wife"],["tasmāt idam ardha-bṛgalam iva svaḥ iti ha sma āha yājñavalkyaḥ","adj. + iva + m. nom.","\"therefore this (body) is like a half-fragment of oneself,\" so said Yājñavalkya"],["tasmāt ayam ākāśaḥ striyā pūryate eva","m. nom. + instr. + 3 sg. pres. pass. of pṝ","therefore this empty space is filled by woman"],["tāṃ samabhavat","acc. + impf. of sam-bhū","he united with her"],["tataḥ manuṣyāḥ ajāyanta","abl. + 3 pl. impf. of jan","thence human beings were born"]],"lit":"\"He found no delight — therefore one alone finds no delight; he desired a second. He became as large as a woman and a man in close embrace. He made this very self fall into two; thence arose husband and wife. 'Therefore this (body) is like a half-fragment of oneself,' said Yājñavalkya. Therefore this empty space is filled by woman. He united with her; thence human beings were born.\"","para":"Aloneness, having lost its fear, now feels <strong>joylessness</strong> — the lack that craves a \"second.\" The Self, swollen to the size of an embracing couple, <strong>splits in two</strong>: the first pair, husband (<em>pati</em>) and wife (<em>patnī</em>). Yājñavalkya's gloss: each individual is therefore but <strong>half a fragment</strong> (the spouse completes the \"missing half\"; cf. Plato's <em>Symposium</em>). Their union produces humankind. This is the cosmogony of <strong>desire and procreation</strong> — the One becoming two in order to delight in itself as another.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the splitting is not a real fissure in the Self but the appearance of duality through desire (<em>kāma</em>); the \"half-fragment\" explains the felt incompleteness that drives all pairing. The Self plays both roles — it is the one Self that appears as the two and unites with itself."],["Sureśvara","the \"second\" that joylessness craves is the root of <em>saṃsāra</em>; the whole drama of male-female is the One mistaking itself for two and seeking reunion — a mirror of the soul's search for the Self."],["Krishnananda","a celebrated psychological reading — the urge for relationship/love is the Self seeking its own lost wholeness in an apparent other; every embrace is an unconscious attempt to recover the original non-duality. Hence relationships never fully satisfy: the real \"other half\" is the Self."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the <em>ardha-bṛgala</em> (\"half-fragment\") teaching as the dignified scriptural basis for the spouse as \"better half\"; but the spiritual point is that completeness is found within, in the undivided Ātman."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a famous androgyne-cosmogony (the primal being self-dividing into the sexes); Olivelle notes parallels in Greek and other mythologies and the rare in-text attribution to Yājñavalkya, the Upaniṣad's chief sage."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.4","title":"The pairing extends through all species; \"I am the creation\"","sa":["सो हेयमीक्षांचक्रे — कथं नु मात्मन एव जनयित्वा सम्भवति । हन्त तिरोऽसानीति । सा गौरभवद्वृषभ इतरः । तां समेवाभवत् । ततो गावोऽजायन्त । वडवेतराभवदश्ववृष इतरः । गर्दभीतरा गर्दभ इतरः । तां समेवाभवत् । तत एकशफमजायत । अजेतराभवद्बस्त इतरः । अविरितरा मेष इतरः । तां समेवाभवत् । ततोऽजावयोऽजायन्त । एवमेव यदिदं किंच मिथुनमा पिपीलिकाभ्यस्तत्सर्वमसृजत ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["so heyam īkṣāṃ cakre -- kathaṃ nu mātmana eva janayitvā sambhavati | hanta tiro 'sānīti | sā gaur abhavad vṛṣabha itaraḥ | tāṃ sam evābhavat | tato gāvo 'jāyanta | vaḍavetarābhavad aśvavṛṣa itaraḥ | gardabhītarā gardabha itaraḥ | tāṃ sam evābhavat | tata ekaśapham ajāyata | ajetarābhavad basta itaraḥ | avir itarā meṣa itaraḥ | tāṃ sam evābhavat | tato 'jāvayo 'jāyanta | evam eva yad idaṃ kiñca mithunam ā pipīlikābhyas tat sarvam asṛjata || 4 ||"],"words":[["sā ha iyam īkṣāṃ cakre","f. nom. (= she, the wife) + periphr. pf.","she reflected"],["kathaṃ nu mā ātmanaḥ eva janayitvā sambhavati","acc. (mā=me) + abl. + ger. of jan (caus.) + pres.","\"how can he unite with me after begetting me from his very self?"],["hanta tiraḥ asāni iti","interj. + adv. + 1 sg. subj. of as","come, let me hide!\""],["sā gauḥ abhavat vṛṣabhaḥ itaraḥ","f. nom. + impf. + m. nom.","she became a cow, the other a bull"],["tāṃ sam eva abhavat tataḥ gāvaḥ ajāyanta","acc. + impf. + abl. + impf.","he united with her; thence cattle were born"],["vaḍavā itarā abhavat aśva-vṛṣaḥ itaraḥ","f./m. nom.","the one became a mare, the other a stallion"],["gardabhī itarā gardabhaḥ itaraḥ","f./m. nom.","the one a she-ass, the other a he-ass"],["tataḥ ekaśapham ajāyata","n. nom. (one-hoofed) + impf.","thence the one-hoofed (animals) were born"],["ajā itarā … basta itaraḥ; aviḥ itarā meṣaḥ itaraḥ","f./m. nom.","she-goat / he-goat; ewe / ram"],["tataḥ ajāvayaḥ ajāyanta","(aja+avi) m. nom. pl. + impf.","thence goats and sheep were born"],["evam eva yat idaṃ kiṃ ca mithunam ā pipīlikābhyaḥ","rel. + acc. + (ā + abl. pl.)","thus whatever exists in pairs, down to the ants"],["tat sarvam asṛjata","acc. + impf. of sṛj","all that he created"]],"lit":"\"She reflected: 'How can he unite with me after begetting me from his own self? Well then — let me hide!' She became a cow, the other a bull, and he united with her; thence cattle were born. She became a mare, the other a stallion; a she-ass, the other a he-ass — and he united with her; thence the one-hoofed (animals) were born. She became a she-goat, the other a he-goat; a ewe, the other a ram — and he united with her; thence goats and sheep were born. Thus whatever exists in pairs, down to the ants, all that he created.\"","para":"The \"wife,\" embarrassed that her own progenitor pursues her, <strong>flees by transformation</strong> through species after species; in each form the male pursues and unites, generating every kind of paired creature, <strong>down to the ants</strong>. The whole biological world of male-and-female is the one Self pursuing and uniting with itself in endless disguise.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the serial transformation shows that <em>all</em> sexual generation, across every species, is the single Self appearing as the mithuna; \"down to the ants\" stresses the universality — no living pair is outside this self-multiplication."],["Sureśvara","the chase-and-hide of the pair allegorises the play of <em>prakṛti</em> (the fleeing female) and <em>puruṣa</em> (the pursuing male) producing the manifold."],["Krishnananda","the One \"becomes many\" not by fragmenting but by <em>appearing</em> as countless pairs; the comedy of the fleeing wife conveys the spontaneity (<em>līlā</em>) of manifestation."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note \"down to the ants\" as the charter for seeing the <em>same</em> creative principle in the humblest creatures — a basis for reverence toward all life."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a transformation-pursuit myth with wide parallels; Olivelle highlights the comprehensiveness (\"all pairs, to the ants\") as asserting the Self's identity with the entire animate creation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.5","title":"\"I am the creation\": the Self is all that it made","sa":["सोऽवेदहं वाव सृष्टिरस्म्यहं हीदं सर्वमसृक्षीति । ततः सृष्टिरभवत् । सृष्ट्यां हास्यैतस्यां भवति य एवं वेद ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["so 'ved ahaṃ vāva sṛṣṭir asmy ahaṃ hīdaṃ sarvam asṛkṣīti | tataḥ sṛṣṭir abhavat | sṛṣṭyāṃ hāsyaitasyāṃ bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 5 ||"],"words":[["saḥ avet","3 sg. impf. of vid","he knew"],["ahaṃ vāva sṛṣṭiḥ asmi","nom. + f. nom. + 1 sg. pres.","\"I, indeed, am the creation"],["ahaṃ hi idaṃ sarvam asṛkṣi iti","1 sg. aor. ātm. of sṛj","for I emitted all this\""],["tataḥ sṛṣṭiḥ abhavat","abl. + impf.","thence he became the creation"],["sṛṣṭyāṃ ha asya etasyāṃ bhavati","f. loc. + gen.","in this his creation (such a knower) comes to be"],["yaḥ evaṃ veda","pf. of vid","who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"He knew: 'I, indeed, am the creation, for I emitted all this.' Thence he became 'the Creation' (<em>Sṛṣṭi</em>). He who knows this comes to be in this creation of his (i.e., shares in the creative status).\"","para":"The creator recognises: <strong>\"I am the creation itself\"</strong> — not a maker standing apart from a made thing, but identical with everything emitted. The creator and the creation are non-different. The knower of this becomes a \"creator\" within the creation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the decisive non-dual recognition at the cosmogonic level — the cause does not become <em>other</em> than itself in producing the effect; <em>Sṛṣṭi</em> (creation) is just the creator under another name. Prepares the ground for \"I am Brahman.\""],["Sureśvara","\"I am the creation\" denies any real difference between <em>kāraṇa</em> and <em>kārya</em> (cause and effect) — the world is non-different from its source, as pot from clay."],["Krishnananda","the creator's joy is self-recognition in the created — there is no alien world \"out there\"; all this <em>is</em> oneself emitted. The knower participates in that creative identity."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a meditation dissolving the subject-object gap: \"this world is my own self-expression.\""],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the identification of agent and product; Olivelle reads it as a strong monistic claim distinctive of BU."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.6","title":"Fire and Soma; food and eater; the \"super-creation\" of the gods","sa":["अथेत्यभ्यमन्थत् । स मुखाच्च योनेर्हस्ताभ्यां चाग्निमसृजत । तस्मादेतदुभयमलोमकमन्तरतः । अलोमका हि योनिरन्तरतः । तद्यदिदमाहुरमुं यजामुं यजेत्येकैकं देवमेतस्यैव सा विसृष्टिः । एष उ ह्येव सर्वे देवाः । अथ यत्किंचेदमार्द्रं तद्रेतसोऽसृजत । तदु सोम । एतावद्वा इदं सर्वमन्नं चैवान्नादश्च । सोम एवान्नमग्निरन्नादः । सैषा ब्रह्मणोऽतिसृष्टिः । यच्छ्रेयसो देवानसृजताथ यन्मर्त्यः सन्नमृतानसृजत तस्मादतिसृष्टिः । अतिसृष्ट्यां हास्यैतस्यां भवति य एवं वेद ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["athety abhyamanthat | sa mukhāc ca yoner hastābhyāṃ cāgnim asṛjata | tasmād etad ubhayam alomakam antarataḥ | alomakā hi yonir antarataḥ | tad yad idam āhur amuṃ yajāmuṃ yajety ekaikaṃ devam etasyaiva sā visṛṣṭiḥ | eṣa u hy eva sarve devāḥ | atha yat kiñcedam ārdraṃ tad retaso 'sṛjata | tad u soma | etāvad vā idaṃ sarvam annaṃ caivānnādaś ca | soma evānnam agnir annādaḥ | saiṣā brahmaṇo 'tisṛṣṭiḥ | yac chreyaso devān asṛjatātha yan martyaḥ sann amṛtān asṛjata tasmād atisṛṣṭir | atisṛṣṭyāṃ hāsyaitasyāṃ bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 6 ||"],"words":[["atha iti abhyamanthat","adv. + 3 sg. impf. of abhi-manth","then (\"come!\") he churned/rubbed"],["saḥ mukhāt ca yoneḥ hastābhyāṃ ca agnim asṛjata","abl. + abl. + instr. du. + acc. + impf.","from the mouth as the womb and from the hands he produced fire"],["tasmāt etat ubhayam alomakam antarataḥ","abl. + n. nom. + adj. + adv.","therefore both (mouth & hands) are hairless within"],["alomakā hi yoniḥ antarataḥ","f. nom.","for the womb is hairless within"],["tat yat idam āhuḥ amuṃ yaja amuṃ yaja iti ekaikaṃ devam","rel. + 3 pl. pf. + impv. + acc.","as for the saying \"sacrifice to this god, to that god,\" one by one —"],["etasya eva sā visṛṣṭiḥ","gen. + f. nom.","that is just his (the Self's) emission"],["eṣaḥ u hi eva sarve devāḥ","m. nom. pl.","for he himself is all the gods"],["atha yat kiṃ ca idam ārdraṃ tat retasaḥ asṛjata","rel. + n. + abl. + impf.","now whatever is moist here, that he created from semen"],["tat u somaḥ","m. nom.","and that is Soma"],["etāvat vai idaṃ sarvam annaṃ ca eva annādaḥ ca","n. nom. + m. nom.","this whole (world) is just this much — food and the eater of food"],["somaḥ eva annam agniḥ annādaḥ","nom.","Soma is the food, Fire the eater"],["sā eṣā brahmaṇaḥ atisṛṣṭiḥ","f. nom. + gen.","this is the \"super-creation\" of Brahman (the creator)"],["yat śreyasaḥ devān asṛjata","rel. + acc. pl. + impf.","in that he created the gods, who are superior (to him)"],["atha yat martyaḥ san amṛtān asṛjata","rel. + nom. + pres.part. + acc. pl.","and in that, being mortal, he created the immortals"],["tasmāt atisṛṣṭiḥ","abl. + f. nom.","therefore (it is) a super-creation"],["atisṛṣṭyāṃ ha asya etasyāṃ bhavati yaḥ evaṃ veda","f. loc.","in this super-creation of his comes to be the one who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"Then ('Come!') he churned, and from his mouth as a womb and from his hands he produced fire; therefore both these are hairless within, for the womb is hairless within. As for the saying, 'Sacrifice to this god, sacrifice to that god' — each god is just his emission, for he himself is all the gods. Now whatever is moist he created from semen, and that is Soma. This whole (world) is just this much: food and the eater of food. Soma is the food, Fire the eater. This is the super-creation (<em>atisṛṣṭi</em>) of Brahman: that, being himself, he created the gods who are his betters; and that, being mortal, he created the immortals. Therefore it is a super-creation. He who knows this comes to be in this super-creation of his.\"","para":"The Self next produces <strong>Fire (Agni)</strong> by churning (mouth and hands made \"hairless within\" like the womb, by this origin), and <strong>Soma</strong> from the moist/semen. These two — <strong>Agni the eater, Soma the food</strong> — sum up the whole world as the food/eater polarity (echoing 1.2.5, 1.3.17). All the many gods one sacrifices to are simply the Self's own emissions: <em>he is all the gods</em>. The crowning marvel is the <strong>atisṛṣṭi</strong> (\"super-creation\"): the mortal creator produced beings <em>greater and more immortal than himself</em> (the gods).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"he is all the gods\" — the polytheistic cultus is reabsorbed into the one Self; each deity is a face of the single Ātman. The <em>atisṛṣṭi</em> (creating one's betters) shows the creative power exceeding itself — and points to the Self's infinitude, which is not diminished by producing even the gods."],["Sureśvara","the food/eater reduction (Soma/Agni) again collapses the world to two correlatives that presuppose the one consciousness; \"all the gods are he\" is a <em>mahā</em>-statement of monotheistic monism."],["Krishnananda","worship of any deity reaches the one Self that <em>is</em> every deity; the <em>atisṛṣṭi</em> teaches that the source can manifest as something seemingly higher than itself without being lessened (cf. the <em>pūrṇa</em> of the śānti-pāṭha)."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical theism — all forms of God are valid as faces of the one Ātman; the food/eater meditation sanctifies the fire-offering (<em>agnihotra</em>)."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the etymological aside on \"hairless within\"; Olivelle reads \"he is all the gods\" as the BU's explicit move toward identifying the many Vedic deities with a single principle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.7","title":"The unmanifest differentiated by name & form; meditate on it as the Self","sa":["तद्धेदं तर्ह्यव्याकृतमासीत् । तन्नामरूपाभ्यामेव व्याक्रियतासौ नामायमिदंरूप इति । तदिदमप्येतर्हि नामरूपाभ्यामेव व्याक्रियते असौ नामायमिदंरूप इति । स एष इह प्रविष्ट आ नखाग्रेभ्यो यथा क्षुरः क्षुरधानेऽवहितः स्याद्विश्वम्भरो वा विश्वम्भरकुलाये । तं न पश्यन्ति । अकृत्स्नो हि सः प्राणन्नेव प्राणो नाम भवति । वदन्वाक्पश्यंश्चक्षुः शृण्वञ्छ्रोत्रं मन्वानो मनः । तान्यस्यैतानि कर्मनामान्येव । स योऽत एकैकमुपास्ते न स वेद । अकृत्स्नो ह्येषोऽत एकैकेन भवति । आत्मेत्येवोपासीत । अत्र ह्येते सर्व एकं भवन्ति । तदेतत्पदनीयमस्य सर्वस्य यदयमात्मा । अनेन ह्येतत्सर्वं वेद । यथा ह वै पदेनानुविन्देदेवं कीर्तिं श्लोकं विन्दते य एवं वेद ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["tad dhedaṃ tarhy avyākṛtam āsīt | tan nāmarūpābhyām eva vyākriyatāsau nāmāyam idaṃrūpa iti | tad idam apy etarhi nāmarūpābhyām eva vyākriyata asau nāmāyam idaṃrūpa iti | sa eṣa iha praviṣṭa ā nakhāgrebhyo yathā kṣuraḥ kṣuradhāne 'vahitaḥ syād viśvambharo vā viśvambharakulāye | taṃ na paśyanti | akṛtsno hi saḥ prāṇann eva prāṇo nāma bhavati | vadan vāk paśyaṃś cakṣuḥ śṛṇvañ chrotraṃ manvāno manaḥ | tāny asyaitāni karmanāmāny eva | sa yo 'ta ekaikam upāste na sa veda | akṛtsno hy eṣo 'ta ekaikena bhavati | ātmety evopāsīta | atra hy ete sarva ekaṃ bhavanti | tad etat padanīyam asya sarvasya yad ayam ātmā | anena hy etat sarvaṃ veda | yathā ha vai padenānuvinded evaṃ kīrtiṃ ślokaṃ vindate ya evaṃ veda || 7 ||"],"words":[["tat ha idaṃ tarhi avyākṛtam āsīt","n. nom. + adv. + pp. + impf.","at that time this (universe) was undifferentiated (avyākṛta)"],["tat nāma-rūpābhyām eva vyākriyata","instr. du. + 3 sg. impf. pass. of vi-ā-kṛ","it became differentiated precisely by name and form"],["asau nāma ayam idaṃ-rūpaḥ iti","\"so-and-so by name, of this form\"","(one says) \"this has such a name, such a form\""],["saḥ eṣaḥ iha praviṣṭaḥ ā nakha-agrebhyaḥ","nom. + pp. + (ā + abl. pl.)","he (the Self) has entered here up to the very fingernail-tips"],["yathā kṣuraḥ kṣura-dhāne avahitaḥ syāt","nom. + loc. + pp. + opt.","as a razor lies hidden in its case"],["viśvambharaḥ vā viśvambhara-kulāye","nom. + loc.","or as the all-sustaining (fire/bird) in its nest"],["taṃ na paśyanti","acc. + 3 pl. pres.","they do not see him"],["akṛtsnaḥ hi saḥ","nom.","for (seen partially) he is incomplete"],["prāṇan eva prāṇaḥ nāma bhavati","pres.part. + nom.","when breathing he is called \"breath\""],["vadan vāk, paśyan cakṣuḥ, śṛṇvan śrotram, manvānaḥ manaḥ","pres.part. + nom.","speaking, \"speech\"; seeing, \"eye\"; hearing, \"ear\"; thinking, \"mind\""],["tāni asya etāni karma-nāmāni eva","n. nom. pl.","these are merely his activity-names"],["saḥ yaḥ ataḥ ekaikam upāste na saḥ veda","rel. + acc. + pres. of upās + pf.","he who worships one or another of these (singly) does not know"],["akṛtsnaḥ hi eṣaḥ ataḥ ekaikena bhavati","nom. + instr.","for he is incomplete (taken) by any single one"],["ātmā iti eva upāsīta","nom. + opt. of upās","one should meditate on (him) just as \"the Self\""],["atra hi ete sarve ekaṃ bhavanti","loc. + nom. pl.","for in it (the Self) all these become one"],["tat etat padanīyam asya sarvasya yat ayam ātmā","n. nom. (the trace to be tracked)","this Self is the track/footprint of all this"],["anena hi etat sarvaṃ veda","instr.","for by it one knows all this"],["yathā ha vai padena anuvindet evaṃ kīrtiṃ ślokaṃ vindate yaḥ evaṃ veda","instr. + opt. of anu-vid + acc.","as one finds (lost cattle) by a footprint, so he who knows thus finds fame and praise"]],"lit":"\"At that time this (world) was undifferentiated. It became differentiated by name and form — '(this is) so-and-so by name, of such-and-such form.' To this very day it is differentiated by name and form: 'so-and-so by name, of such a form.' He (the Self) has entered here right up to the fingernail-tips, as a razor lies hidden in its case, or as the all-sustaining (fire) in its nest. They do not see him, for (seen partially) he is incomplete: breathing, he is called 'breath'; speaking, 'speech'; seeing, 'the eye'; hearing, 'the ear'; thinking, 'the mind.' These are merely his activity-names. One who worships one or another of these (singly) does not know — for he is incomplete in any single one. <strong>One should meditate on him just as 'the Self,'</strong> for in it all these become one. This Self is the track (<em>padanīya</em>) of all this; for by it one knows all this — as one finds (lost cattle) by tracking a footprint. He who knows this finds fame and praise.\"","para":"The differentiated world is the <strong>unmanifest</strong> (<em>avyākṛta</em>) \"named and formed\" (<em>nāma-rūpa</em>). The Self has <strong>entered into every body</strong> to the fingertips — utterly intimate, yet unseen, because we only ever catch it under its <em>partial activity-names</em> (breath, speech, eye, ear, mind), never whole. To grasp it through any one function is to miss it. <strong>Therefore meditate on it simply as \"the Self\" (ātmety evopāsīta)</strong> — the single reality in which all the functions are one. The Self is the <em>padanīya</em>, the trace by which all else is found, as cattle are tracked by their hoofprints.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the pivotal <em>upāsanā</em>-injunction of the chapter. The Self is the indweller (<em>pratyag-ātman</em>) present \"to the fingernail-tips\" but hidden because cognised only piecemeal through the organs. Worshipping any single faculty (prāṇa-upāsanā etc., the meditations of 1.3) yields only a <em>partial</em> result; the complete knowledge is <em>ātmā iti</em> — the one Self in which all faculties are unified. <em>Nāma-rūpa</em> is Śaṅkara's technical term for the entire differentiated world projected on Brahman by <em>avidyā/māyā</em>."],["Sureśvara","the \"activity-names\" (<em>karma-nāman</em>) are merely functional designations of the one Self; mistaking them for separate realities is ignorance. \"Meditate as the Self\" is the corrective that gathers the dispersed names into their source."],["Krishnananda","a magnificent passage — the Self is <em>closer than the senses</em>, \"entered to the fingertips,\" yet missed because we look <em>outward through</em> the organs instead of <em>back to</em> the seer. The razor-in-case / bird-in-nest similes convey utter immanence-with-concealment. The remedy is not better sense-worship but a turn to the unifying \"I.\""],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical heart: do not split your devotion among faculties or deities — meditate on the one Ātman in which they cohere. The \"footprint\" image: the Self is the clue that leads to the knowledge of everything."],["Deussen/Olivelle","<em>nāma-rūpa</em> (\"name and form\") as the principle of individuation is highlighted; Olivelle notes the razor/case and bird/nest similes as vivid statements of the Self's hiddenness within the body, and the \"tracking by footprint\" as an epistemic metaphor — the Self is the means of all knowing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.8","title":"The Self is dearer than all else","sa":["तदेतत्प्रेयः पुत्रात्प्रेयो वित्तात्प्रेयोऽन्यस्मात्सर्वस्मादन्तरतरं यदयमात्मा । स योऽन्यमात्मनः प्रियं ब्रुवाणं ब्रूयात्प्रियं रोत्स्यतीतीश्वरो ह तथैव स्यात् । आत्मानमेव प्रियमुपासीत । स य आत्मानमेव प्रियमुपास्ते न हास्य प्रियं प्रमायुकं भवति ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["tad etat preyaḥ putrāt preyo vittāt preyo 'nyasmāt sarvasmād antarataraṃ yad ayam ātmā | sa yo 'nyam ātmanaḥ priyaṃ bruvāṇaṃ brūyāt priyaṃ rotsyatītīśvaro ha tathaiva syāt | ātmānam eva priyam upāsīta | sa ya ātmānam eva priyam upāste na hāsya priyaṃ pramāyukaṃ bhavati || 8 ||"],"words":[["tat etat preyaḥ putrāt","n. nom. compar. + abl.","this (Self) is dearer than a son"],["preyaḥ vittāt preyaḥ anyasmāt sarvasmāt","abl.","dearer than wealth, dearer than all else"],["antarataraṃ yat ayam ātmā","n. nom. compar. (more inward) + rel.","(it is) more inward (than all), namely this Self"],["saḥ yaḥ anyam ātmanaḥ priyaṃ bruvāṇaṃ brūyāt","rel. + acc. + abl. + pres.part. acc. + opt. of brū","if one were to say to a man who calls something other than the Self dear"],["priyaṃ rotsyati iti","acc. + 3 sg. fut. of rudh","\"you will lose what is dear (to you)\""],["īśvaraḥ ha tathā eva syāt","nom. + opt.","(the speaker) is competent (to say so), and so it would indeed be"],["ātmānam eva priyam upāsīta","acc. + opt.","one should meditate on the Self alone as dear"],["saḥ yaḥ ātmānam eva priyam upāste","rel. + pres.","he who meditates on the Self alone as dear"],["na ha asya priyaṃ pramāyukam bhavati","gen. + adj. (perishable)","his dear (object) does not perish"]],"lit":"\"This (Self) is dearer than a son, dearer than wealth, dearer than all else — for it is more inward (than everything); it is this Self. If one were to tell a person who calls something other than the Self 'dear' that 'you will lose what you hold dear,' he is competent (to say it), and so it would indeed turn out. One should meditate on the Self alone as the dear. He who meditates on the Self alone as the dear — his dear (object) never perishes.\"","para":"The Self is the <strong>dearest of all</strong> — more beloved than son, wealth, or anything, because it is the <strong>innermost</strong> (<em>antaratara</em>); in fact, everything else is dear only <em>for the sake of</em> the Self (as 2.4.5, the Maitreyī dialogue, will make explicit). Whoever loves a perishable \"other\" most will lose it; but one who holds the <strong>Self</strong> dearest loves the <strong>imperishable</strong>, and so his beloved never dies. Hence: <em>meditate on the Self as the dearest.</em>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Self is dearest because it is the very locus of all love — we love son and wealth only as extensions of ourselves. To fix love on the perishable is to court grief; to fix it on the imperishable Self is to love something that cannot be lost. (Cf. the fuller treatment in the Maitreyī-brāhmaṇa, 2.4 / 4.5.)"],["Sureśvara","\"more inward than all\" — the Self is the subject behind every cherished object; therefore it is the <em>truly</em> dear, of which all other dearness is a borrowed reflection."],["Krishnananda","a piercing analysis of love — every attachment is, unknowingly, self-love refracted onto an object; redirect that love to its true source, the Self, and it becomes deathless. This is the spiritual transformation of <em>kāma</em> into <em>prema</em> for the Ātman."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical counsel — invest your deepest love in the imperishable Self, not in mortal objects that will inevitably be lost; this alone gives sorrow-proof love."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the \"competent to say\" idiom (the teacher who tells you your loved one will die is <em>īśvara</em>, authorised, because it is metaphysically certain); Olivelle links this to the Maitreyī passage as the locus classicus of Upaniṣadic ethics of love."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.9","title":"The question: what did Brahman know, that it became all?","sa":["तदाहुर्यद्ब्रह्मविद्यया सर्वं भविष्यन्तो मनुष्या मन्यन्ते । किमु तद्ब्रह्मावेद्यस्मात्तत्सर्वमभवदिति ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["tad āhur yad brahmavidyayā sarvaṃ bhaviṣyanto manuṣyā manyante | kim u tad brahmāved yasmāt tat sarvam abhavad iti || 9 ||"],"words":[["tat āhuḥ","3 pl. pf. of ah","concerning this they say / it is asked"],["yat brahma-vidyayā sarvaṃ bhaviṣyantaḥ manuṣyāḥ manyante","instr. + acc. + fut.part. nom. pl. + pres.","\"since men think they will become the All by the knowledge of Brahman"],["kim u tat brahma avet","acc. + 3 sg. impf. of vid","what, then, did that Brahman know"],["yasmāt tat sarvam abhavat iti","abl. + impf.","by knowing which it became the All?\""]],"lit":"\"Concerning this they ask: 'Since men think that by the knowledge of Brahman they will become the All — what, then, did that Brahman (itself) know, by knowing which it became the All?'\"","para":"A pointed question that frames the climax: people hold that <em>brahma-vidyā</em> makes one \"become everything.\" Very well — then <strong>what did Brahman itself know</strong> that made <em>it</em> become everything? The answer (1.4.10) reveals the content of the saving knowledge.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the question is the <em>pūrva-pakṣa</em> setup for the <em>mahā-vākya</em>; it forces the issue — liberation is by <em>knowledge</em>, so we must state exactly <em>what</em> is known. The premise \"by knowing Brahman one becomes all\" is granted; the question is its content."],["Sureśvara","establishes that the means to \"becoming all\" is <em>jñāna</em>, not action — central to his <em>sambandha-vārttika</em> polemic that knowledge alone liberates."],["Krishnananda","the verse isolates the operative question of all Vedānta: <em>what is the knowledge whose mere arising transforms one into the Absolute?</em> The answer must be a knowledge that is also a <em>being</em>."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a teaching device — the student is made to ask precisely the right question before the answer is given."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the reflexive sophistication — the text interrogates its own soteriological claim; Olivelle reads it as a deliberate rhetorical lead-in to the famous declaration."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.10","title":"\"Ahaṃ Brahmāsmi\" — I am Brahman","sa":["ब्रह्म वा इदमग्र आसीत् । तदात्मानमेवावेत् । अहं ब्रह्मास्मीति । तस्मात्तत्सर्वमभवत् । तद्यो यो देवानां प्रत्यबुध्यत स एव तदभवत् । तथर्षीणाम् । तथा मनुष्याणाम् । तद्धैतत्पश्यन्नृषिर्वामदेवः प्रतिपेदेऽहं मनुरभवं सूर्यश्चेति । तदिदमप्येतर्हि य एवं वेदाहं ब्रह्मास्मीति स इदं सर्वं भवति । तस्य ह न देवाश्चनाभूत्या ईशते । आत्मा ह्येषां स भवति । अथ योऽन्यां देवतामुपास्तेऽन्योऽसावन्योऽहमस्मीति न स वेद । यथा पशुरेवं स देवानाम् । यथा ह वै बहवः पशवो मनुष्यं भुञ्ज्युरेवमेकैकः पुरुषो देवान्भुनक्ति । एकस्मिन्नेव पशावादीयमानेऽप्रियं भवति किमु बहुषु । तस्मादेषां तन्न प्रियं यदेतन्मनुष्या विद्युः ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["brahma vā idam agra āsīt | tad ātmānam evāvet | ahaṃ brahmāsmīti | tasmāt tat sarvam abhavat | tad yo yo devānāṃ pratyabudhyata sa eva tad abhavat | tatharṣīṇām | tathā manuṣyāṇām | tad dhaitat paśyann ṛṣir vāmadevaḥ pratipede 'haṃ manur abhavaṃ sūryaś ceti | tad idam apy etarhi ya evaṃ vedāhaṃ brahmāsmīti sa idaṃ sarvaṃ bhavati | tasya ha na devāś canābhūtyā īśate | ātmā hy eṣāṃ sa bhavati | atha yo 'nyāṃ devatām upāste 'nyo 'sāv anyo 'ham asmīti na sa veda | yathā paśur evaṃ sa devānām | yathā ha vai bahavaḥ paśavo manuṣyaṃ bhuñjyur evam ekaikaḥ puruṣo devān bhunakti | ekasminn eva paśāv ādīyamāne 'priyaṃ bhavati kim u bahuṣu | tasmād eṣāṃ tan na priyaṃ yad etan manuṣyā vidyuḥ || 10 ||"],"words":[["brahma vai idam agre āsīt","n. nom. + adv. + impf.","Brahman, verily, was this (all) in the beginning"],["tat ātmānam eva avet","n. + acc. + 3 sg. impf. of vid","it knew only itself"],["ahaṃ brahma asmi iti","nom. + n. + 1 sg. pres.","\"I am Brahman\""],["tasmāt tat sarvam abhavat","abl. + impf.","therefore it became the All"],["tat yaḥ yaḥ devānāṃ pratyabudhyata saḥ eva tat abhavat","rel. + gen. pl. + 3 sg. impf. of prati-budh","whoever among the gods awoke to it, he indeed became that"],["tathā ṛṣīṇām tathā manuṣyāṇām","gen. pl.","so too among the seers, so among men"],["tat ha etat paśyan ṛṣiḥ vāmadevaḥ pratipede","pres.part. + nom. + 3 sg. pf. of prati-pad","seeing this, the seer Vāmadeva realised (and declared):"],["ahaṃ manuḥ abhavaṃ sūryaḥ ca iti","nom. + 1 sg. impf. + nom.","\"I was Manu, and the Sun\""],["tat idam api etarhi yaḥ evaṃ veda ahaṃ brahma asmi iti","","even now, whoever knows thus, \"I am Brahman,\""],["saḥ idaṃ sarvaṃ bhavati","nom. + pres.","he becomes this All"],["tasya ha na devāḥ ca na abhūtyai īśate","gen. + nom. pl. + dat. + pres. of īś","even the gods are not able to prevent his becoming (so)"],["ātmā hi eṣāṃ saḥ bhavati","nom. + gen. pl.","for he becomes their (very) Self"],["atha yaḥ anyāṃ devatām upāste","rel. + acc. + pres.","but he who worships another deity (thinking)"],["anyaḥ asau anyaḥ aham asmi iti na saḥ veda","nom.","\"he is other, I am other\" — he does not know"],["yathā paśuḥ evaṃ saḥ devānām","nom. + gen. pl.","he is like a sacrificial animal for the gods"],["yathā bahavaḥ paśavaḥ manuṣyaṃ bhuñjyuḥ","nom. pl. + acc. + 3 pl. opt. of bhuj","as many animals serve a man"],["evam ekaikaḥ puruṣaḥ devān bhunakti","+ acc. pl. + pres.","so each single person serves the gods"],["ekasmin eva paśau ādīyamāne apriyaṃ bhavati kim u bahuṣu","loc. + pres.pass.part. loc.","if even one animal is taken away it is displeasing — how much more if many"],["tasmāt eṣāṃ tat na priyaṃ yat etat manuṣyāḥ vidyuḥ","gen. pl. + rel. + 3 pl. opt. of vid","therefore it is not pleasing to them (the gods) that men should know this"]],"lit":"\"Brahman, verily, was this (all) in the beginning. It knew only itself: 'I am Brahman.' Therefore it became the All. And whoever among the gods awoke to this, he too became That; so too among the seers, so among men. Seeing this, the seer Vāmadeva realised and declared, 'I was Manu, and (I was) the Sun.' Even now, whoever knows thus, 'I am Brahman,' becomes this All; not even the gods can prevent his becoming so, for he becomes their very Self. But whoever worships another deity (thinking) 'he is one, I am another,' does not know; he is like a sacrificial animal for the gods. As many animals serve a man, so does each person serve the gods. If even one animal is taken away it is displeasing — how much more if many! Therefore it is not pleasing to the gods that men should know this.\"","para":"The summit. Brahman became the All <strong>by knowing itself as Brahman</strong> — <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em>. The saving knowledge sought in 1.4.9 is <strong>self-knowledge</strong>: to know one's own Self <em>as</em> the Absolute. This is open to all — gods, seers, humans; whoever realises it \"becomes the All.\" The seer <strong>Vāmadeva</strong> verified it from within (RV 4.26: \"I was Manu, I was the Sun\"). The knower becomes the very Self of the gods, beyond their power to stop. By contrast, one who worships God as <strong>wholly other</strong> (\"He is other, I am other\") remains in ignorance — a mere <strong>sacrificial animal serving the gods</strong>. The striking corollary: <em>the gods would rather men did not learn this</em>, for an awakened soul is no longer their \"livestock.\"","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>mahā-vākya</em> of the Yajurveda, declaring the identity of <em>jīva</em> and <em>Brahman</em>. \"Brahman knew itself, 'I am Brahman'\" — the knowledge is not a <em>becoming</em> but a <em>recognition</em> of what already is; \"became the All\" means the removal of the false limitation, not an acquisition. The Vāmadeva citation supplies <em>ṛṣi</em>-testimony (<em>āptopadeśa</em>). The \"sacrificial animal\" image grounds Śaṅkara's claim that <em>bheda-upāsanā</em> (dualistic worship) keeps one bound; only the non-dual realisation frees. The remark that the gods dislike men knowing this he reads as the <em>power of avidyā</em> personified — the cosmic interest in keeping souls bound."],["Sureśvara","the centerpiece of his <em>vārttika</em>: liberation is <em>immediate</em> upon the rise of \"<em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em>,\" not the result of any action; \"became all\" = the cessation of the not-Self superimposition. He stresses <em>pratyabudhyata</em> (\"awoke\") — it is an <em>awakening</em>, knowledge dispelling sleep, not a production."],["Krishnananda","\"This is the lion's roar of the Upaniṣad.\" Realisation is not <em>reaching</em> Brahman but <em>waking up as</em> Brahman. The \"becoming all\" is the dissolution of the ego-boundary; the awakened one is the Self of everything, so nothing can stand over against him. The \"animal serving the gods\" is the unawakened soul whose worship still posits separation — kept, milked, and finally consumed by the very powers it propitiates."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the supreme affirmation for <em>sādhanā</em> — assert and meditate \"<em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em>\" until the felt separateness dissolves. Sivananda makes it a core <em>mahā-vākya</em> japa. The warning against \"he is other, I am other\" cautions that mere ritual devotion, without the knowledge of identity, does not finally liberate."],["Deussen/Olivelle","one of the four classical <em>mahā-vākyas</em>; Deussen treats it as the high-water mark of Upaniṣadic idealism (the identity of subject and Absolute). Olivelle notes the Vāmadeva quotation (RV 4.26.1) as the text grounding its claim in older revelation, and reads the \"gods dislike it\" passage as reflecting a tension between the new liberation-knowledge and the older sacrificial economy that depended on humans serving the gods."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.11","title":"Brahman creates the Kṣatra (ruling power)","sa":["ब्रह्म वा इदमग्र आसीदेकमेव । तदेकं सन्न व्यभवत् । तच्छ्रेयो रूपमत्यसृजत क्षत्रं यान्येतानि देवत्रा क्षत्राणीन्द्रो वरुणः सोमो रुद्रः पर्जन्यो यमो मृत्युरीशान इति । तस्मात्क्षत्रात्परं नास्ति । तस्माद्ब्राह्मणः क्षत्रियमधस्तादुपास्ते राजसूये । क्षत्र एव तद्यशो दधाति । सैषा क्षत्रस्य योनिर्यद्ब्रह्म । तस्माद्यद्यपि राजा परमतां गच्छति ब्रह्मैवान्तत उपनिश्रयति स्वां योनिम् । य उ एनं हिनस्ति स्वां स योनिमृच्छति । स पापीयान्भवति यथा श्रेयांसं हिंसित्वा ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["brahma vā idam agra āsīd ekam eva | tad ekaṃ san na vyabhavat | tac chreyo rūpam atyasṛjata kṣatraṃ, yāny etāni devatrā kṣatrāṇīndro varuṇaḥ somo rudraḥ parjanyo yamo mṛtyur īśāna iti | tasmāt kṣatrāt paraṃ nāsti | tasmād brāhmaṇaḥ kṣatriyam adhastād upāste rājasūye | kṣatra eva tad yaśo dadhāti | saiṣā kṣatrasya yonir yad brahma | tasmād yady api rājā paramatāṃ gacchati brahmaivāntata upaniśrayati svāṃ yonim | ya u enaṃ hinasti svāṃ sa yonim ṛcchati | sa pāpīyān bhavati yathā śreyāṃsaṃ hiṃsitvā || 11 ||"],"words":[["brahma vai idam agre āsīt ekam eva","n. nom. + adv.","Brahman was this (all) in the beginning, one only"],["tat ekaṃ san na vyabhavat","nom. + pres.part. + 3 sg. impf. of vi-bhū","being one, it did not flourish/unfold (fully)"],["tat śreyaḥ rūpam atyasṛjata kṣatram","n. acc. (better form) + impf. of ati-sṛj","it specially created a superior form, the Kṣatra (ruling power)"],["yāni etāni devatrā kṣatrāṇi","n. nom. pl. + adv.","namely those ruling-powers among the gods"],["indraḥ varuṇaḥ somaḥ rudraḥ parjanyaḥ yamaḥ mṛtyuḥ īśānaḥ iti","m. nom.","Indra, Varuṇa, Soma, Rudra, Parjanya, Yama, Mṛtyu, Īśāna"],["tasmāt kṣatrāt paraṃ na asti","abl. + n. nom.","hence there is nothing higher than the Kṣatra"],["tasmāt brāhmaṇaḥ kṣatriyam adhastāt upāste rājasūye","nom. + acc. + adv. + pres. + loc.","therefore the brāhmaṇa reveres the kṣatriya from below at the Rājasūya (royal consecration)"],["kṣatre eva tat yaśaḥ dadhāti","loc. + n. acc. + pres. of dhā","he confers that honour upon the Kṣatra"],["sā eṣā kṣatrasya yoniḥ yat brahma","f. nom. + gen. + rel.","yet the womb/source of the Kṣatra is the Brahman (priestly power)"],["tasmāt yadi api rājā paramatāṃ gacchati","nom. + acc. + pres.","therefore even if a king attains supremacy"],["brahma eva antataḥ upaniśrayati svāṃ yonim","acc. + adv. + 3 sg. pres. of upa-ni-śri","in the end he resorts to the Brahman, his own source"],["yaḥ u enaṃ hinasti svāṃ saḥ yonim ṛcchati","rel. + acc. + pres. + 3 sg. pres. of ṛ","whoever harms it (the Brahman) strikes at his own source"],["saḥ pāpīyān bhavati yathā śreyāṃsaṃ hiṃsitvā","compar. nom. + ger. of hiṃs","he becomes the more wicked, as one who has injured a superior"]],"lit":"\"Brahman was this (all) in the beginning, one only. Being one, it did not (fully) unfold. It specially created a superior form, the Kṣatra (the ruling power) — namely those ruling-powers among the gods: Indra, Varuṇa, Soma, Rudra, Parjanya, Yama, Mṛtyu, Īśāna. Therefore there is nothing higher than the Kṣatra; hence at the Rājasūya the brāhmaṇa pays homage to the kṣatriya from a lower seat — he confers that honour on the Kṣatra. Yet the very source (<em>yoni</em>) of the Kṣatra is the Brahman (the priestly power). Therefore, even if a king reaches the summit (of power), in the end he resorts to the Brahman as his own source; and whoever injures it strikes at his own source — he becomes the more wicked, like one who has harmed a superior.\"","para":"Brahman, finding that being \"one only\" did not let it <strong>fully manifest</strong> (function in society), generates the <strong>Kṣatra</strong> — the warrior/ruling principle (with its divine exemplars Indra, Varuṇa, etc.). Socially this is <em>superior</em> (none is \"higher than the Kṣatra\"; the priest even sits lower at the king's consecration). <strong>But</strong> the Kṣatra's <em>source</em> is the Brahman (priestly/spiritual power): power springs from, and must finally return to, the spiritual. A king who harms the priestly principle harms his own root.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Brahman \"did not flourish alone\" — the one needs functional differentiation to operate in the world; hence the social powers. The teaching balances temporal supremacy (Kṣatra) against ontological priority (Brahman as its <em>yoni</em>): worldly power is real but derivative, and self-destructive when it attacks its spiritual source."],["Sureśvara","the <em>yoni</em> relation (Brahman as source of Kṣatra) restates the cause-effect non-difference in the social register; the higher (spiritual) is the ground of the apparently higher (political)."],["Krishnananda","a meditation on the right order of powers — material/political force is legitimate but rootless unless grounded in spiritual law; civilisations that \"injure their own source\" decay."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the ethical-political teaching: rulers must honour and not harm the spiritual principle that legitimises them."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a <em>varṇa</em>-cosmogony; Olivelle reads it as reflecting/negotiating the brāhmaṇa–kṣatriya relationship, asserting priestly priority even while conceding royal precedence in ritual seating."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 1.4.12–1.4.13","title":"Brahman creates the Viś and the Śūdra","sa":["1.4.12 (Viś — the producer class): स नैव व्यभवत् । स विशमसृजत । यान्येतानि देवजातानि गणश आख्यायन्ते वसवो रुद्रा आदित्या विश्वे देवा मरुत इति ॥ १२ ॥","1.4.13 (Śūdra — the serving class): स नैव व्यभवत् । स शौद्रं वर्णमसृजत पूषणम् । इयं वै पूषा । इयं हीदं सर्वं पुष्यति यदिदं किंच ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["sa naiva vyabhavat | sa viśam asṛjata | yāny etāni devajātāni gaṇaśa ākhyāyante vasavo rudrā ādityā viśve devā maruta iti || 12 ||","sa naiva vyabhavat | sa śaudraṃ varṇam asṛjata pūṣaṇam | iyaṃ vai pūṣā | iyaṃ hīdaṃ sarvaṃ puṣyati yad idaṃ kiñca || 13 ||"],"words":[["saḥ na eva vyabhavat","3 sg. impf. of vi-bhū","(still) it did not (fully) flourish"],["saḥ viśam asṛjata","f. acc. + impf. of sṛj","it created the Viś (the commoner/producer order)"],["yāni etāni deva-jātāni gaṇaśaḥ ākhyāyante","n. nom. pl. + adv. + 3 pl. pres. pass.","namely those classes of gods named in groups"],["vasavaḥ rudrāḥ ādityāḥ viśve devāḥ marutaḥ iti","m. nom. pl.","the Vasus, Rudras, Ādityas, Viśvedevas, Maruts"],["saḥ śaudraṃ varṇam asṛjata pūṣaṇam","acc. + impf. + acc.","it created the Śūdra class, (as) Pūṣan"],["iyaṃ vai pūṣā","f. nom. (= this earth)","this (earth) is verily Pūṣan"],["iyaṃ hi idaṃ sarvaṃ puṣyati yat idaṃ kiṃ ca","+ 3 sg. pres. of puṣ","for she nourishes (puṣ) all this, whatever there is"]],"lit":"\"Still it did not (fully) flourish. It created the Viś — those classes of gods named in groups: the Vasus, Rudras, Ādityas, Viśvedevas, Maruts. Still it did not flourish. It created the Śūdra order, as Pūṣan. This (earth) is Pūṣan, for she nourishes all this, whatever there is.\"","para":"The differentiation continues: the <strong>Viś</strong> (the productive commoner order, mirrored by the <em>grouped</em> gods — Vasus, Rudras, etc., who act collectively) and the <strong>Śūdra</strong> order, identified with <strong>Pūṣan</strong> = the <strong>Earth</strong>, \"because she nourishes (<em>puṣ</em>) everything.\" Each <em>varṇa</em> is given a divine archetype. The Śūdra-as-Earth/Pūṣan is dignified as the universal <em>nourisher</em>.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Viś corresponds to the <em>collective</em> deities (acting in troops, like the productive multitude); the Śūdra to <em>Pūṣan</em>/Earth as the nourishing support. Each order has its cosmic prototype, sanctifying the social whole as an emanation of the one Brahman."],["Sureśvara","the serial \"it still did not flourish\" shows manifestation as a graded unfolding of the one into functional plurality, none outside Brahman."],["Krishnananda","reads the four orders as the four <em>functions</em> every society and even every individual needs — knowledge, power, production, service — all rooted in the single Self."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the honour given the nourishing principle (Pūṣan/Earth); service/nourishment is sacred, the very ground that sustains all."],["Deussen/Olivelle","compare the <em>Puruṣa-sūkta</em> (RV 10.90) four-varṇa scheme; Olivelle notes BU assigns each order a deity-archetype, integrating social structure into the cosmogony."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.14","title":"Brahman creates Dharma; dharma = truth, the power above power","sa":["स नैव व्यभवत् । तच्छ्रेयो रूपमत्यसृजत धर्मम् । तदेतत्क्षत्रस्य क्षत्रं यद्धर्मः । तस्माद्धर्मात्परं नास्ति । अथो अबलीयान्बलीयांसमाशंसते धर्मेण । यथा राज्ञैवम् । यो वै स धर्मः सत्यं वै तत् । तस्मात्सत्यं वदन्तमाहुर्धर्मं वदतीति । धर्मं वा वदन्तं सत्यं वदतीति । एतद्ध्येवैतदुभयं भवति ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["sa naiva vyabhavat | tac chreyo rūpam atyasṛjata dharmam | tad etat kṣatrasya kṣatraṃ yad dharmaḥ | tasmād dharmāt paraṃ nāsti | atho abalīyān balīyāṃsam āśaṃsate dharmeṇa | yathā rājñaivam | yo vai sa dharmaḥ satyaṃ vai tat | tasmāt satyaṃ vadantam āhur dharmaṃ vadatīti | dharmaṃ vā vadantaṃ satyaṃ vadatīti | etad dhy evaitad ubhayaṃ bhavati || 14 ||"],"words":[["saḥ na eva vyabhavat","impf.","still it did not (fully) flourish"],["tat śreyaḥ rūpam atyasṛjata dharmam","acc. + impf. + acc.","it specially created a still superior form, Dharma (law/justice/righteousness)"],["tat etat kṣatrasya kṣatraṃ yat dharmaḥ","n. nom. + gen. + rel.","this Dharma is the Kṣatra of the Kṣatra (the power behind power)"],["tasmāt dharmāt paraṃ na asti","abl. + n. nom.","hence there is nothing higher than Dharma"],["atho abalīyān balīyāṃsam āśaṃsate dharmeṇa","compar. nom. + acc. + 3 sg. pres. of ā-śaṃs + instr.","so the weaker controls/prevails over the stronger by means of Dharma"],["yathā rājñā evam","instr.","just as (one prevails) by means of a king"],["yaḥ vai saḥ dharmaḥ satyaṃ vai tat","n. nom.","that which is Dharma is verily Truth (satya)"],["tasmāt satyaṃ vadantam āhuḥ dharmaṃ vadati iti","acc. + pres.part. + 3 pl. pf. + pres.","therefore of one who speaks the truth they say \"he speaks dharma\""],["dharmaṃ vā vadantaṃ satyaṃ vadati iti","acc.","and of one who speaks dharma, \"he speaks the truth\""],["etat hi eva etat ubhayaṃ bhavati","n. nom.","for these two are the very same"]],"lit":"\"Still it did not (fully) flourish. It specially created a still superior form: <strong>Dharma</strong> (law/righteousness). This Dharma is the Kṣatra of the Kṣatra (the ruler of the ruler); hence there is nothing higher than Dharma. So even a weaker man prevails over a stronger by means of Dharma, as (one prevails) through a king. Now that which is Dharma is verily Truth (<em>satya</em>); therefore of a man who speaks the truth they say, 'He speaks dharma,' and of one who speaks dharma, 'He speaks the truth' — for these two are the very same.\"","para":"The crown of the social cosmogony: above even the Kṣatra (ruling power) is <strong>Dharma</strong> — moral law/justice, \"the power of the power.\" By Dharma even the weak prevail over the strong (justice trumps force), as a small man can invoke the king against a big one. And <strong>Dharma is Truth (satya)</strong>: to speak truth is to speak dharma and vice versa — they are identical. Thus the highest of the created \"forms\" is the <strong>moral order grounded in truth</strong>.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Dharma as \"the Kṣatra of the Kṣatra\" makes righteousness the ultimate sovereign — even kings are subject to it; and its identity with <em>satya</em> roots morality in reality itself (truth = what is). The weak prevailing over the strong \"by dharma\" shows that moral law is a real power, not mere ideal."],["Sureśvara","<em>dharma = satya</em> — the ethical and the real coincide; truthfulness is the practical face of conformity to what <em>is</em>. This anchors ethics in metaphysics."],["Krishnananda","a profound teaching that <strong>justice is stronger than force</strong>: history's apparent rule of power is overruled, in the long run, by dharma. And because dharma is truth, living truthfully is living in accord with the structure of reality."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical summit of the <em>varṇa</em> passage — uphold truth and you uphold the highest power in the universe; <em>satyam eva jayate</em>. Truth-speaking is the simplest, highest dharma."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the identification <em>dharma = satya</em> and the striking claim that dharma is supreme over the warrior power; Olivelle reads this as the text subordinating even royal authority to an impersonal moral-cosmic order."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.15","title":"Meditate on the Self as your world; the imperishable result","sa":["तदेतद्ब्रह्म क्षत्रं विट् शूद्रः । तदग्निनैव देवेषु ब्रह्माभवद्ब्राह्मणो मनुष्येषु क्षत्रियेण क्षत्रियो वैश्येन वैश्यः शूद्रेण शूद्रः । तस्मादग्नावेव देवेषु लोकमिच्छन्ते ब्राह्मणे मनुष्येषु । एताभ्यां हि रूपाभ्यां ब्रह्माभवत् । अथ यो ह वा अस्माल्लोकात्स्वं लोकमदृष्ट्वा प्रैति स एनमविदितो न भुनक्ति यथा वेदो वाननूक्तोऽन्यद्वा कर्माकृतम् । यदि ह वा अप्यनेवंविन्महत्पुण्यं कर्म करोति तद्धास्यान्ततः क्षीयत एव । आत्मानमेव लोकमुपासीत । स य आत्मानमेव लोकमुपास्ते न हास्य कर्म क्षीयते । अस्माद्ध्येवात्मनो यद्यत्कामयते तत्तत्सृजते ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["tad etad brahma kṣatraṃ viṭ śūdraḥ | tad agninaiva deveṣu brahmābhavad brāhmaṇo manuṣyeṣu, kṣatriyeṇa kṣatriyaḥ, vaiśyena vaiśyaḥ, śūdreṇa śūdraḥ | tasmād agnāv eva deveṣu lokam icchante brāhmaṇe manuṣyeṣu | etābhyāṃ hi rūpābhyāṃ brahmābhavat | atha yo ha vā asmāl lokāt svaṃ lokam adṛṣṭvā praiti sa enam avidito na bhunakti yathā vedo vānanūkto 'nyad vā karmākṛtam | yadi ha vā apy anevaṃvin mahatpuṇyaṃ karma karoti tad dhāsyāntataḥ kṣīyata eva | ātmānam eva lokam upāsīta | sa ya ātmānam eva lokam upāste na hāsya karma kṣīyate | asmād dhy evātmano yadyat kāmayate tattat sṛjate || 15 ||"],"words":[["tat etat brahma kṣatraṃ viṭ śūdraḥ","n./m. nom.","this (whole) is Brahman, Kṣatra, Viś, Śūdra"],["tat agninā eva deveṣu brahma abhavat","instr. + loc. pl. + impf.","among the gods that Brahman(-power) existed as Agni"],["brāhmaṇaḥ manuṣyeṣu","nom. + loc. pl.","(and) as the brāhmaṇa among men"],["kṣatriyeṇa kṣatriyaḥ vaiśyena vaiśyaḥ śūdreṇa śūdraḥ","instr. + nom.","as kṣatriya (god) → kṣatriya (man), vaiśya → vaiśya, śūdra → śūdra"],["tasmāt agnau eva deveṣu lokam icchante brāhmaṇe manuṣyeṣu","loc. + acc. + 3 pl. pres. + loc.","hence (people) seek their (heavenly) world in Agni among gods, in the brāhmaṇa among men"],["etābhyāṃ hi rūpābhyāṃ brahma abhavat","instr. du.","for in these two forms (Agni / brāhmaṇa) Brahman manifested"],["atha yaḥ ha vai asmāt lokāt svaṃ lokam adṛṣṭvā praiti","abl. + acc. + ger. of dṛś (neg.) + 3 sg. pres. of pra-i","now whoever departs this world without having seen his own world (the Self)"],["saḥ enam aviditaḥ na bhunakti","acc. + nom. + pres. of bhuj","it (the Self), being unknown, does not protect/benefit him"],["yathā vedaḥ vā ananūktaḥ anyat vā karma akṛtam","nom. + pp.","as the Veda unrecited, or any rite not performed (does not benefit)"],["yadi ha vai api anevaṃ-vit mahat-puṇyaṃ karma karoti","nom. + acc. + pres.","even if one who does not know this performs a great meritorious act"],["tat ha asya antataḥ kṣīyate eva","n. nom. + adv. + 3 sg. pres. pass. of kṣi","that (merit) of his is in the end exhausted"],["ātmānam eva lokam upāsīta","acc. + acc. + opt.","one should meditate on the Self alone as one's world"],["saḥ yaḥ ātmānam eva lokam upāste na ha asya karma kṣīyate","pres. + gen.","he who so meditates — his work is not exhausted"],["asmāt hi eva ātmanaḥ yat-yat kāmayate tat-tat sṛjate","abl. + rel. + pres. + pres. of sṛj","for from this very Self he produces whatever he desires"]],"lit":"\"This (whole) is Brahman, Kṣatra, Viś, Śūdra. Among the gods that Brahman existed as Agni, among men as the brāhmaṇa; as the (divine) kṣatriya it became the (human) kṣatriya, as the vaiśya the vaiśya, as the śūdra the śūdra. Hence men seek their (heavenly) world in Agni among the gods and in the brāhmaṇa among men, for in these two forms Brahman manifested. Now whoever departs from this world without having seen his own world (the Self) — it, being unknown, does not benefit him, like the Veda unrecited or a rite undone. Even if one who does not know this performs a great meritorious act, that merit is in the end exhausted. <strong>One should meditate on the Self alone as one's world.</strong> He who does so — his work is not exhausted; for from this very Self he creates whatever he desires.\"","para":"The social orders all manifest the one Brahman (Agni/priest as its purest forms). But the decisive teaching turns inward: <strong>ritual merit is finite</strong> — even great <em>puṇya</em>, if performed in ignorance of the Self, \"runs out\" (and so cannot give lasting liberation). Therefore <em>do not bank on works alone</em>: <strong>meditate on the Self as your true \"world\" (loka)</strong> — the only inexhaustible ground. The knower's \"work is not exhausted,\" for from the Self he brings forth whatever he wishes. (This is a key Vedānta proof-text that <em>karma</em> yields perishable fruit, while <em>Self-knowledge</em> is the imperishable end.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the crucial contrast of <em>karma</em> and <em>jñāna</em>: merit (<em>puṇya</em>), being an effect, is necessarily <em>kṣayī</em> (perishable) — heavenly worlds won by rites are exhausted and one falls back. Only <em>ātma-jñāna</em> gives the imperishable. \"Meditate on the Self as your world\" is therefore the injunction to seek the inexhaustible end. A central text for Śaṅkara's thesis that liberation cannot come from action."],["Sureśvara","his cardinal proof that <em>karma</em> and <em>mokṣa</em> are categorically different — works produce transient results; the unborn Self is not produced and so not lost. Hence knowledge, not ritual, is the means to the deathless."],["Krishnananda","\"Whatever is done will be undone\" — all action, being in time, yields time-bound fruit; the Self is the only \"world\" that does not collapse. To make the Self one's <em>loka</em> is to stand on the imperishable."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical warning against merely accumulating merit; ground your life in Self-realisation, the one acquisition that is never lost."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the early articulation of the <em>karma</em>-exhaustion idea (heaven won by rites is temporary) that becomes central to later soteriology; Olivelle reads \"the Self as one's world\" as the text's pivot from ritual to gnostic religion."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.16","title":"The Self is the world of all beings","sa":["अथो अयं वा आत्मा सर्वेषां भूतानां लोकः । स यज्जुहोति यद्यजते तेन देवानां लोकः । अथ यदनुब्रूते तेन ऋषीणाम् । अथ यत्पितृभ्यो निपृणाति यत्प्रजामिच्छते तेन पितॄणाम् । अथ यन्मनुष्यान्वासयते यदेभ्योऽशनं ददाति तेन मनुष्याणाम् । अथ यत्पशुभ्यस्तृणोदकं विन्दति तेन पशूनाम् । यदस्य गृहेषु श्वापदा वयांस्या पिपीलिकाभ्य उपजीवन्ति तेन तेषां लोकः । यथा ह वै स्वाय लोकायारिष्टिमिच्छेत् । एवं हैवंविदे सर्वदा सर्वाणि भूतान्यरिष्टिमिच्छन्ति । तद्वा एतद्विदितं मीमांसितम् ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["atho ayaṃ vā ātmā sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ lokaḥ | sa yaj juhoti yad yajate tena devānāṃ lokaḥ | atha yad anubrūte tena ṛṣīṇām | atha yat pitṛbhyo nipṛṇāti yat prajām icchate tena pitṝṇām | atha yan manuṣyān vāsayate yad ebhyo 'śanaṃ dadāti tena manuṣyāṇām | atha yat paśubhyas tṛṇodakaṃ vindati tena paśūnām | yad asya gṛheṣu śvāpadā vayāṃsy ā pipīlikābhya upajīvanti tena teṣāṃ lokaḥ | yathā ha vai svāya lokāyāriṣṭim icchet | evaṃ haivaṃvide sarvadā sarvāṇi bhūtāny ariṣṭim icchanti | tad vā etad viditaṃ mīmāṃsitam || 16 ||"],"words":[["atho ayaṃ vai ātmā sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ lokaḥ","nom. + gen. pl. + m. nom.","now this Self is the world (support) of all beings"],["saḥ yat juhoti yat yajate tena devānāṃ lokaḥ","rel. + pres. of hu + yaj + instr. + gen. pl.","in that he offers oblations and sacrifices, he is the world of the gods"],["atha yat anubrūte tena ṛṣīṇām","rel. + pres. of anu-brū","in that he recites (the Veda), of the seers"],["atha yat pitṛbhyaḥ nipṛṇāti yat prajām icchate tena pitṝṇām","dat. + pres. + acc. + pres.","in that he offers to the fathers and seeks offspring, of the fathers"],["atha yat manuṣyān vāsayate yat ebhyaḥ aśanaṃ dadāti tena manuṣyāṇām","acc. + pres. of vas (caus.) + acc. + pres.","in that he shelters men and gives them food, of men"],["atha yat paśubhyaḥ tṛṇa-udakaṃ vindati tena paśūnām","dat. + acc. + pres.","in that he provides grass and water for animals, of animals"],["yat asya gṛheṣu śvāpadāḥ vayāṃsi ā pipīlikābhyaḥ upajīvanti","loc. + nom. pl. + (ā + abl.) + pres. of upa-jīv","in that beasts and birds, down to ants, live off his house"],["tena teṣāṃ lokaḥ","instr. + gen.","thereby he is their world"],["yathā ha vai svāya lokāya ariṣṭim icchet","dat. + acc. (safety) + opt. of iṣ","as one would wish safety for one's own world"],["evaṃ ha evaṃvide sarvadā sarvāṇi bhūtāni ariṣṭim icchanti","dat. + adv. + nom. pl. + pres.","so all beings always wish safety for the one who knows this"],["tat vai etat viditaṃ mīmāṃsitam","n. nom. pp.","this (truth) has indeed been known and pondered"]],"lit":"\"Now this Self is the world (support) of all beings. In that one offers oblations and sacrifices, he is the world of the gods; in that he recites (the Veda), of the seers; in that he makes offerings to the fathers and seeks offspring, of the fathers; in that he shelters and feeds people, of human beings; in that he provides grass and water to animals, of the animals; and in that beasts, birds, and even the ants live off his house, he is their world too. As one wishes safety (<em>ariṣṭi</em>) for one's own world, so all beings wish safety for the one who knows this. This (truth) has indeed been examined and established.\"","para":"The Self/individual is, by his various acts, the <strong>sustaining \"world\" of every order of being</strong> — gods (by sacrifice), seers (by study), ancestors (by offerings &amp; progeny), humans (by hospitality), animals (by fodder), even the wild creatures and ants (who feed off his household). He stands at the centre of a web of mutual support (a counterpart to the food-web of 1.2.5, now seen as <em>care</em> rather than <em>consumption</em>). Therefore <strong>all beings wish him well</strong> — just as one protects what sustains one. A vision of the Self as universal benefactor, owed reciprocal goodwill by all.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the knower is a <em>loka</em> (resort/support) for all because, knowing the one Self, his every act sustains the whole; the universal goodwill toward him is the natural return for being everyone's support. The five debts (to gods, seers, fathers, men, animals) are integrated into the Self-vision."],["Sureśvara","the passage universalises the householder's obligations into an expression of the one Self caring for itself in all its forms."],["Krishnananda","a beautiful ecology of mutual dependence — to live rightly is to be a support to all levels of being; the realised person becomes a node of nourishment that the whole web instinctively protects."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the <em>pañca-mahāyajña</em> (five great daily duties) expressed as the Self being the world of all; selfless service makes one beloved of all creatures."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the inventory of the householder's reciprocal duties; Olivelle reads it as embedding the gnostic Self-teaching within the concrete obligations of Vedic domestic life."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4.17","title":"The fourfold desire and the fivefold sacrifice","sa":["आत्मैवेदमग्र आसीदेक एव । सोऽकामयत जाया मे स्यादथ प्रजायेय । अथ वित्तं मे स्यादथ कर्म कुर्वीयेति । एतावान्वै कामः । नेच्छंश्चनातो भूयो विन्देत् । तस्मादप्येतर्ह्येकाकी कामयते — जाया मे स्यादथ प्रजायेयाथ वित्तं मे स्यादथ कर्म कुर्वीयेति । स यावदप्येतेषामेकैकं न प्राप्नोत्यकृत्स्न एव तावन्मन्यते । तस्यो कृत्स्नता । मन एवास्यात्मा । वाग्जाया । प्राणः प्रजा । चक्षुर्मानुषं वित्तम् । चक्षुषा हि तद्विन्दते । श्रोत्रं दैवम् । श्रोत्रेण हि तच्छृणोति । आत्मैवास्य कर्म । आत्मना हि कर्म करोति । स एष पाङ्क्तो यज्ञः । पाङ्क्तः पशुः । पाङ्क्तः पुरुषः । पाङ्क्तमिदं सर्वं यदिदं किंच । तदिदं सर्वमाप्नोति य एवं वेद ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["ātmaivedam agra āsīd eka eva | so 'kāmayata jāyā me syād atha prajāyeya | atha vittaṃ me syād atha karma kurvīyeti | etāvān vai kāmaḥ | necchaṃś canāto bhūyo vindet | tasmād apy etarhy ekākī kāmayate -- jāyā me syād atha prajāyeyātha vittaṃ me syād atha karma kurvīyeti | sa yāvad apy eteṣām ekaikaṃ na prāpnoty akṛtsna eva tāvan manyate | tasyo kṛtsnatā | mana evāsyātmā | vāg jāyā | prāṇaḥ prajā | cakṣur mānuṣaṃ vittam | cakṣuṣā hi tad vindate | śrotraṃ daivam | śrotreṇa hi tac chṛṇoti | ātmaivāsya karma | ātmanā hi karma karoti | sa eṣa pāṅkto yajñaḥ | pāṅktaḥ paśuḥ | pāṅktaḥ puruṣaḥ | pāṅktam idaṃ sarvaṃ yad idaṃ kiñca | tad idaṃ sarvam āpnoti ya evaṃ veda || 17 ||"],"words":[["ātmā eva idam agre āsīt ekaḥ eva","m. nom. + adv.","the Self alone was this (all) in the beginning, one only"],["saḥ akāmayata","impf.","he desired"],["jāyā me syāt atha prajāyeya","f. nom. + opt. of as + 1 sg. opt. of pra-jan","\"may I have a wife, then may I be born (in offspring)"],["atha vittaṃ me syāt atha karma kurvīya iti","n. nom. + opt. + 1 sg. opt. of kṛ","then may I have wealth, then may I perform rites\""],["etāvān vai kāmaḥ","m. nom. (this-much)","this much, indeed, is (the whole of) desire"],["na icchan ca na ataḥ bhūyaḥ vindet","pres.part. + adv. + opt. of vid","even wishing, one would not find more than this"],["tasmāt api etarhi ekākī kāmayate …","adv. + m. nom.","therefore even now one who is alone desires (just these)"],["saḥ yāvat api eteṣām ekaikaṃ na prāpnoti akṛtsnaḥ eva tāvat manyate","rel. + gen. pl. + pres. + adj.","so long as he does not obtain each one of these, he thinks himself incomplete"],["tasya u kṛtsnatā","gen. + f. nom.","(now) his completeness (is this):"],["manaḥ eva asya ātmā","n. nom.","the mind is his self"],["vāk jāyā prāṇaḥ prajā","f./m. nom.","speech his wife, breath his offspring"],["cakṣuḥ mānuṣaṃ vittam cakṣuṣā hi tat vindate","n. nom. + adj. + instr.","the eye his human wealth, for by the eye one finds it"],["śrotraṃ daivam śrotreṇa hi tat śṛṇoti","n. nom. + adj. + instr.","the ear his divine (wealth), for by the ear one hears it"],["ātmā eva asya karma ātmanā hi karma karoti","m. nom. + instr.","the body/self is his rite, for by the self one performs the rite"],["saḥ eṣaḥ pāṅktaḥ yajñaḥ","m. nom. (fivefold)","this is the fivefold sacrifice"],["pāṅktaḥ paśuḥ pāṅktaḥ puruṣaḥ","m. nom.","fivefold the animal, fivefold the person"],["pāṅktam idaṃ sarvaṃ yat idaṃ kiṃ ca","n. nom. + rel.","fivefold is all this, whatever there is"],["tat idaṃ sarvam āpnoti yaḥ evaṃ veda","acc. + pres. of āp","he who knows thus obtains all this"]],"lit":"\"The Self alone was this (all) in the beginning, one only. He desired: 'May I have a wife, then may I be born (again, as offspring); then may I have wealth, then may I perform rites.' This much, indeed, is (the whole of) desire; even wishing, one would not find more than this. Therefore even now one who is single desires: 'May I have a wife, then be born, then have wealth, then perform rites.' So long as he does not obtain each one of these, he thinks himself incomplete. Now his completeness (is this): the <strong>mind</strong> is his self, <strong>speech</strong> his wife, <strong>breath</strong> his offspring, the <strong>eye</strong> his human wealth (for by the eye he finds it), the <strong>ear</strong> his divine wealth (for by the ear he hears it), the <strong>body/self</strong> his rite (for by it he performs rites). This is the <strong>fivefold sacrifice</strong>; fivefold the (sacrificial) animal, fivefold the person, fivefold is all this, whatever there is. He who knows this obtains all this.\"","para":"The chapter closes by reducing all human wanting to a <strong>fourfold desire</strong>: <em>wife → offspring → wealth → ritual action</em> — \"this much is the whole of desire,\" and one feels <strong>incomplete</strong> until each is gained. But these outer objects have <strong>inner correlates</strong> that constitute one's true <em>completeness</em> (<em>kṛtsnatā</em>): mind = the self, speech = wife, breath = offspring, eye = human wealth, ear = divine wealth, body = the rite. These <strong>five</strong> make the <em>pāṅkta</em> (\"fivefold\") pattern that structures the sacrifice, the victim, the person, and the whole universe. To know this fivefold identity is to \"obtain all this\" — completeness is found <em>within</em>, not by acquiring outer objects.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the analysis of <em>kāma</em> (desire) is exhaustive — all worldly wanting reduces to these four, and the sense of <em>akṛtsnatā</em> (incompleteness) drives the whole of <em>saṃsāra</em>. The teaching relocates \"completeness\" from external acquisitions to their inner counterparts (the faculties), and finally points beyond even these to the Self, whose knowledge is true fullness. The <em>pāṅkta</em> (fivefold) correspondence ties microcosm to the cosmic sacrifice."],["Sureśvara","the felt incompleteness until desires are met is the mark of the not-Self; <em>kṛtsnatā</em> (wholeness) belongs only to the Self, which lacks nothing — hence desire ends in Self-knowledge, not in acquisition."],["Krishnananda","a penetrating psychology of desire — wife, child, wealth, work: these four cover the whole map of human craving, and none ever yields the completeness it promises, because completeness is the nature of the Self, not of any object. The inner correlates show that what we seek outside we already are inside."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical — recognise that the restless pursuit of spouse/progeny/wealth/work is the soul seeking its own wholeness in the wrong place; turn within, where mind, speech, breath, eye, ear already constitute a complete \"sacrifice.\""],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the neat fourfold taxonomy of desire and the <em>pāṅkta</em> (fivefold) numerical mysticism; Olivelle reads the inner correlates as the text's method of internalising the outer life into a psycho-cosmic sacrifice, rounding off the Madhu-kāṇḍa's first movement."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This brāhmaṇa is the <strong>philosophical core of Adhyāya 1</strong> and one of the densest in the Upaniṣad. It re-narrates creation a third time — after the horse-cosmos (1.1) and Death-cosmogony (1.2) — but now from the <strong>Ātman/Puruṣa</strong>, and in doing so lays down the chapter's permanent doctrines:","1. <strong>Self-consciousness is primary</strong> (1.4.1). Reality's first event is \"I am\" (<em>aham asmi</em>) — being is self-aware from the start. The \"I\" is not added to existence; it is existence's own first word.","2. <strong>Fear is born of duality</strong> (1.4.2): <em>dvitīyād vai bhayaṃ bhavati.</em> The non-dual is <em>abhaya</em> (fearless) by nature — a foundational Advaita argument.","3. <strong>Desire splits the One into many</strong> (1.4.3–4): joylessness craves a \"second\"; the Self halves itself into the sexes and, through endless pursuit, into all paired species \"down to the ants.\" Each individual is therefore a \"half-fragment\" seeking its missing whole — which is really the Self.","4. <strong>Creator = creation</strong> (1.4.5–6): \"I am the creation\"; \"he is all the gods\"; the world reduces to <strong>food and eater</strong> (Soma/Agni). The cause is non-different from the effect.","5. <strong>Name-and-form, and the hidden indweller</strong> (1.4.7): the differentiated world is <em>nāma-rūpa</em>; the Self has entered every body \"to the fingertips\" but is missed because seen only through its partial <em>activity-names</em> (breath, speech, eye…). Hence the great injunction <strong>ātmety evopāsīta</strong> — meditate on it <em>as the Self</em>, the unity of all the functions.","6. <strong>The Self is the dearest and innermost</strong> (1.4.8): love it, and your beloved cannot perish.","7. <strong>The mahā-vākya</strong> (1.4.9–10): the saving knowledge is <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em>, \"I am Brahman\" — by which Brahman \"became all\" and by which any knower becomes the Self of all; dualistic worship leaves one a \"sacrificial animal of the gods.\"","8. <strong>The social-moral order</strong> (1.4.11–14): Brahman unfolds into the four <em>varṇas</em> and crowns them with <strong>Dharma = Truth</strong>, \"the power above power,\" to which even kings are subject.","9. <strong>Knowledge over works</strong> (1.4.15): ritual merit is <em>perishable</em>; only meditation on <strong>the Self as one's world</strong> is inexhaustible.","10. <strong>The Self as universal support, and the anatomy of desire</strong> (1.4.16–17): the realised one is the \"world\" of all beings, beloved of all; and all human craving (wife/offspring/wealth/work) is the search for a completeness that is already the Self's own nature.","Doctrinally, this brāhmaṇa is the <strong>seed-bed of Advaita Vedānta</strong>: non-duality (1.4.2), the identity statement (1.4.10), <em>nāma-rūpa</em> and the indwelling Self (1.4.7), and the priority of <em>jñāna</em> over <em>karma</em> (1.4.15) are all stated here in nuce and elaborated by Śaṅkara and Sureśvara into the system."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   CREATION FROM THE SELF (BU 1.4)\n   ===============================\n   ĀTMAN (Puruṣa), alone           &quot;I AM&quot; (aham asmi)  --&gt; name &quot;I&quot;  (1.4.1)\n        |  fear ......... &quot;no second&quot; --&gt; FEARLESS   (1.4.2: fear = from a second)\n        |  joylessness -&gt; desires a SECOND\n        v\n   splits into  PATI &lt;----&gt; PATNĪ  (husband / wife; each a &quot;half-fragment&quot;) (1.4.3)\n        |  pursuit through species: cow/bull ... down to ANTS               (1.4.4)\n        v\n   &quot;I AM THE CREATION&quot;  +  &quot;he is all the gods&quot;  +  world = FOOD/EATER (Soma/Agni)  (1.4.5-6)\n        |\n        |  world = NĀMA-RŪPA (name &amp; form); Self entered &quot;to the fingertips,&quot;\n        |  seen only as breath/speech/eye/ear/mind  --&gt;  MEDITATE AS &quot;THE SELF&quot; (1.4.7)\n        v\n   the Self = dearest (&gt; son, &gt; wealth), the innermost (1.4.8)\n        |\n        v\n   QUESTION (1.4.9): what did Brahman know, that it became all?\n        |\n        v\n   ====&gt;  AHAṂ BRAHMĀSMI  &quot;I am Brahman&quot;  ====&gt;  becomes the ALL   (1.4.10)\n        |    (vs. &quot;he is other, I am other&quot; = a sacrificial animal of the gods)\n        |\n        |  Brahman unfolds the social order, each rooted in its source:\n        v\n   BRAHMA --&gt; KṢATRA (Indra, Varuṇa, Yama...) --&gt; VIŚ (Vasus, Maruts...) --&gt; ŚŪDRA (Pūṣan/Earth)\n        |                                                           crowned by:\n        v                                                    DHARMA = SATYA (truth) = power above power (1.4.14)\n   karma&#x27;s merit PERISHES; meditate on the SELF as your world = imperishable (1.4.15)\n        |\n        v\n   the Self = the WORLD/support of all beings (gods..ants); all wish it well (1.4.16)\n   desire = wife + offspring + wealth + rites  -&gt; completeness is WITHIN (pāṅkta, fivefold) (1.4.17)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Trace fear to \"a second.\"</strong> When afraid, look for the felt <em>other</em> the fear depends on (1.4.2). Resting in \"there is no second\" is not denial of danger but relaxation of the deep self/other split that <em>amplifies</em> fear into dread.","2. <strong>See attachment as self-search.</strong> Per 1.4.3, every longing for another is the Self seeking its own lost wholeness. Love fully — but know that no \"other half\" outside can complete you; the completeness is the Ātman (1.4.17).","3. <strong>Don't worship a faculty — worship the Self</strong> (1.4.7). Notice how you keep identifying with parts (the thinker, the seer, the speaker). Turn attention from the <em>activity-names</em> back to the one \"I\" in which they are unified: <em>ātmety evopāsīta.</em>","4. <strong>Relocate your deepest love</strong> (1.4.8). Ask what you hold dearest; observe that whatever is perishable will be lost. Practice loving the imperishable Self as the dearest — the one love immune to grief.","5. <strong>Sit with the mahā-vākya</strong> (1.4.10). Take <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em> as contemplation: not an ego-claim (\"<em>I</em>, this person, am God\") but the dissolution of the small \"I\" into the boundless Self that is the Self of all. Let \"I am\" expand until it has no outside.","6. <strong>Live by truth as the highest power</strong> (1.4.14). <em>Dharma = satya.</em> When weak against a stronger force, remember that truth/justice is \"the Kṣatra of the Kṣatra\"; align with it rather than with mere force.","7. <strong>Value knowing over accruing</strong> (1.4.15). Audit where you bank on accumulating (merit, wealth, achievements) — all <em>kṣayī</em>, exhaustible. Invest equally in the one inexhaustible \"world,\" Self-knowledge.","8. <strong>Be the world of beings</strong> (1.4.16). Order your day as support to all levels — the divine, the wise, ancestors, fellow humans, animals, even the creatures at your doorstep. Service flows naturally from seeing the one Self in all.","9. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the chapter's whole arc is <em>from \"I am (a person)\" (1.4.1) to \"I am Brahman\" (1.4.10).</em> Your sense of \"I\" is the thread; the practice is to follow it from the small self to its infinite source."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 1 · Brāhmaṇa 1.5 — The Sevenfold Food; Mind, Speech, Breath; the Year-Prajāpati","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 1.5","title":"The Sevenfold Food; Mind, Speech, Breath; the Year-Prajāpati","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>A long and many-layered brāhmaṇa of the Madhu-kāṇḍa. It opens with a</em> <strong>verse (śloka)</strong> <em>on the \"seven foods\" the Father (Prajāpati) produced by knowledge and austerity, then expounds it in prose. From the \"three foods kept for himself\" it derives the great triad</em> <strong>manas–vāc–prāṇa</strong> <em>(mind, speech, breath), and maps this triad onto the three worlds, three Vedas, gods/fathers/men, and the known/to-be-known/unknown. It then gives the cosmic \"bodies\" of speech, mind, and breath; the</em> <strong>sixteen-part Year-Prajāpati</strong>; <em>the three worlds won by son, rite, and knowledge; the deathbed</em> <strong>handing-over to the son (sampratti)</strong>; <em>and closes with the</em> <strong>vrata-mīmāṃsā</strong> — <em>the contest of the organs and gods, resolved (as in 1.3) by the supremacy of the central breath / Vāyu, yielding the \"one vow\": to breathe.</em>","<em>Editorial note: mantras 1.5.4–1.5.10 are a tight set of parallel correspondences (each maps a triad onto speech / mind / breath). They are given in full Devanāgarī + IAST, with a single consolidated analysis table and shared commentary.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.1","title":"The verse of the seven foods","sa":["यत्सप्तान्नानि मेधया तपसाजनयत्पिता । एकमस्य साधारणं द्वे देवानभाजयत् । त्रीण्यात्मनेऽकुरुत पशुभ्य एकं प्रायच्छत् । तस्मिन्सर्वं प्रतिष्ठितं यच्च प्राणिति यच्च न । कस्मात्तानि न क्षीयन्तेऽद्यमानानि सर्वदा । यो वै तामक्षितिं वेद सोऽन्नमत्ति प्रतीकेन । स देवानपिगच्छति स ऊर्जमुपजीवति । इति श्लोकाः ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["yat saptānnāni medhayā tapasājanayat pitā | ekam asya sādhāraṇaṃ dve devān abhājayat | trīṇy ātmane 'kuruta paśubhya ekaṃ prāyacchat | tasmin sarvaṃ pratiṣṭhitaṃ yacca prāṇiti yacca na | kasmāt tāni na kṣīyante adyamānāni sarvadā | yo vai tām akṣitiṃ veda so 'nnam atti pratīkena | sa devān apigacchati sa ūrjam upajīvati | iti ślokāḥ || 1 ||"],"words":[["yat sapta annāni medhayā tapasā ajanayat pitā","n. acc. pl. + instr. + impf. of jan (caus.) + m. nom.","(in) that the Father produced the seven foods by knowledge and austerity"],["ekam asya sādhāraṇam","n. acc. + gen. + adj.","one of them (he made) common (to all)"],["dve devān abhājayat","n. acc. du. + acc. pl. + impf. of bhaj (caus.)","two he apportioned to the gods"],["trīṇi ātmane akuruta","n. acc. pl. + dat. + impf.","three he made for himself"],["paśubhyaḥ ekaṃ prāyacchat","dat. pl. + acc. + impf. of pra-yam","one he gave to the animals"],["tasmin sarvaṃ pratiṣṭhitaṃ yat ca prāṇiti yat ca na","loc. + n. nom. pp. + rel.","on it (food) all rests, what breathes and what does not"],["kasmāt tāni na kṣīyante adyamānāni sarvadā","abl. + nom. pl. + pres. pass. of kṣi + pass.part. + adv.","why are they not exhausted, though eaten always?"],["yaḥ vai tām akṣitiṃ veda","rel. + f. acc. (the imperishable) + pf.","he who knows that imperishable (source)"],["saḥ annam atti pratīkena","acc. + pres. of ad + instr.","he eats food with the face (directly)"],["saḥ devān apigacchati saḥ ūrjam upajīvati","acc. pl. + pres. + acc. + pres.","he reaches the gods, he lives on vigour"],["iti ślokāḥ","m. nom. pl.","thus the verses"]],"lit":"\"(The teaching) that the Father (Prajāpati) produced the seven foods by knowledge and austerity: one of them he made common (to all); two he apportioned to the gods; three he made for himself; one he gave to the animals. On it (food) rests everything — what breathes and what does not. Why are they (the foods) not exhausted, though eaten always? He who knows that imperishable (source) eats food 'with the face' (directly); he reaches the gods and lives on (their) vigour. — Thus the verses.\"","para":"The opening verse states a riddle-doctrine: the creator made <strong>seven kinds of \"food\"</strong> (forms of nourishment/sustenance) by <em>medhā</em> (wisdom) and <em>tapas</em> (ascetic heat). He distributed them: <strong>1 common</strong> (to all beings), <strong>2 to the gods</strong>, <strong>3 reserved for himself</strong>, <strong>1 to the animals</strong>. All that exists, animate and inanimate, <strong>rests on food</strong>. The puzzle: why is food never used up though eaten forever? Answer: the <strong>imperishable source</strong> (the eater, the Self/Person) keeps regenerating it; the knower of this \"eats with the face\" (partakes directly) and shares the gods' immortal vigour. The prose (1.5.2–3) decodes each portion.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"food\" (<em>anna</em>) here = all objects of experience/enjoyment, produced by the creator's knowledge-and-austerity. The sevenfold division is a meditation-scheme; the \"imperishable\" (<em>akṣiti</em>) that keeps food inexhaustible is the conscious eater (the Person), who continually re-creates the world of enjoyment. The verse is the <em>uddeśa</em> (statement) to be expounded."],["Sureśvara","food's inexhaustibility points to the ever-creative <em>puruṣa</em>; the meditation lifts one from the eaten (perishable objects) to the eater (the imperishable subject) — the recurring inversion of the chapter."],["Krishnananda","the whole universe is a system of <em>nourishment</em>; \"seven foods\" classifies sustenance by recipient. The deep teaching: food never runs out because consciousness perpetually projects the world it consumes — the eater is the inexhaustible spring."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note <em>medhā + tapas</em> (intelligence + disciplined effort) as the means of all production; the verse sanctifies food as the support of all and points to its undying source."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a riddle-verse (typical <em>brahmodya</em> style) followed by exegesis; Olivelle notes the \"seven foods\" as an enumeration mysticism elaborated in 1.5.2."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.2","title":"The seven foods expounded","sa":["यत्सप्तान्नानि मेधया तपसाजनयत्पितेति । मेधया हि तपसाजनयत्पितैकमस्य साधारणमिति । इदमेवास्य तत्साधारणमन्नं यदिदमद्यते । स य एतदुपास्ते न स पाप्मनो व्यावर्तते । मिश्रं ह्येतत् । द्वे देवानभाजयदिति । हुतं च प्रहुतं च । तस्माद्देवेभ्यो जुह्वति च प्र च जुह्वति । अथो आहुर्दर्शपूर्णमासाविति । तस्मान्नेष्टियाजुकः स्यात् । पशुभ्य एकं प्रायच्छदिति । तत्पयः । पयो ह्येवाग्रे मनुष्याश्च पशवश्चोपजीवन्ति । तस्मात्कुमारं जातं घृतं वैवाग्रे प्रतिलेहयन्ति स्तनं वानुधापयन्ति । अथ वत्सं जातमाहुस्तृणादिति । तस्मिन्सर्वं प्रतिष्ठितं यच्च प्राणिति यच्च नेति । पयसि हीदं सर्वं प्रतिष्ठितं यच्च प्राणिति यच्च न । तद्यदिदमाहुः संवत्सरं पयसा जुह्वदप पुनर्मृत्युं जयतीति । न तथा विद्यात् । यदहरेव जुहोति तदहः पुनर्मृत्युमपजयत्येवं विद्वान् । सर्वं हि देवेभ्योऽन्नाद्यं प्रयच्छति । कस्मात्तानि न क्षीयन्तेऽद्यमानानि सर्वदेति । पुरुषो वा अक्षितिः । स हीदमन्नं पुनः पुनर्जनयते । यो वैतामक्षितिं वेदेति । पुरुषो वा अक्षितिः । स हीदमन्नं धियाधिया जनयते कर्मभिः । यद्धैतन्न कुर्यात्क्षीयेत ह । सोऽन्नमत्ति प्रतीकेनेति । मुखं प्रतीकं मुखेनेत्येतत् । स देवानपिगच्छति स ऊर्जमुपजीवतीति प्रशंसा ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["yat saptānnāni medhayā tapasājanayat piteti | medhayā hi tapasājanayat pitaikam asya sādhāraṇam iti | idam evāsya tat sādhāraṇam annaṃ yad idam adyate | sa ya etad upāste na sa pāpmano vyāvartate | miśraṃ hy etat | dve devān abhājayad iti | hutaṃ ca prahutaṃ ca | tasmād devebhyo juhvati ca pra ca juhvati | atho āhur darśapūrṇamāsāv iti | tasmān neṣṭiyājukaḥ syāt | paśubhya ekaṃ prāyacchad iti | tat payaḥ | payo hy evāgre manuṣyāś ca paśavaś copajīvanti | tasmāt kumāraṃ jātaṃ ghṛtaṃ vaivāgre pratilehayanti stanaṃ vānudhāpayanti | atha vatsaṃ jātam āhus tṛṇād iti | tasmin sarvaṃ pratiṣṭhitaṃ yacca prāṇiti yacca neti | payasi hīdaṃ sarvaṃ pratiṣṭhitaṃ yacca prāṇiti yacca na | tad yad idam āhuḥ saṃvatsaraṃ payasā juhvad apa punarmṛtyuṃ jayatīti | na tathā vidyāt | yad ahar eva juhoti tad ahaḥ punarmṛtyum apajayaty evaṃ vidvān | sarvaṃ hi devebhyo 'nnādyaṃ prayacchati | kasmāt tāni na kṣīyante adyamānāni sarvadeti | puruṣo vā akṣitiḥ | sa hīdam annaṃ punaḥ punar janayate | yo vaitām akṣitiṃ vedeti | puruṣo vā akṣitiḥ | sa hīdam annaṃ dhiyādhiyā janayate karmabhiḥ | yad dhaitan na kuryāt kṣīyeta ha | so 'nnam atti pratīkeneti | mukhaṃ pratīkaṃ mukhenety etat | sa devān apigacchati sa ūrjam upajīvatīti praśaṃsā || 2 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"'That the Father produced the seven foods by knowledge and austerity' — yes, by knowledge and austerity the Father produced them. 'One of them common' — this is the <strong>common food</strong>, namely what is (ordinarily) eaten here; one who worships (only) this is not freed from evil, for it is <strong>mixed</strong> (used by all alike). 'Two he gave to the gods' — these are the <strong>offered (huta)</strong> and the <strong>offered-forth (prahuta)</strong> oblations; hence men sacrifice and offer to the gods (some say these are the new- and full-moon rites, so one should not be (merely) a desire-driven sacrificer). 'One he gave to the animals' — that is <strong>milk</strong>, for in the beginning both men and animals live on milk (hence a newborn is first made to lick ghee or put to the breast, and a newborn calf is said to feed 'on grass'). 'On it all rests, what breathes and what does not' — on milk all this rests... As for the saying that 'by offering milk for a year one conquers re-death' — let one not think so; rather, the one who knows thus conquers re-death on the very day he offers, for he offers all food-and-drink to the gods. 'Why are they not exhausted, though eaten always?' — the <strong>Person (puruṣa) is the imperishable</strong>, for he produces this food again and again by his various thoughts and works; if he did not, it would be used up. 'He eats food with the face' — the <em>face</em> is the <em>pratīka</em> (front/mouth); 'with the mouth,' that is. 'He reaches the gods, he lives on vigour' — that is praise.\"","para":"The prose decodes the seven foods: <strong>(1) the common food</strong> = ordinary daily food (shared by all, hence \"mixed\" — worshipping it alone does not purify); <strong>(2–3) the two gods' foods</strong> = the <em>huta</em> and <em>prahuta</em> oblations (or the new-/full-moon sacrifices); <strong>(7) the animals' food</strong> = <strong>milk</strong>, on which men and beasts alike first live (so all life \"rests on milk\"). The <strong>three reserved for the Self</strong> are treated in 1.5.3 (mind, speech, breath). The riddle of inexhaustibility is solved: the <strong>Person is the <em>akṣiti</em> (imperishable)</strong> who <strong>re-creates food endlessly by thought and action</strong> — were the creative subject to stop, food would fail. To \"eat with the face/mouth\" = to partake directly; the knower joins the gods and lives on cosmic vigour (<em>ūrj</em>).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","carefully distributes the seven: common food (worldly), two oblations (ritual), milk (for animals), and (next mantra) the three self-foods (psychic faculties). The key doctrine: <em>puruṣo vā akṣitiḥ</em> — the conscious Person is the inexhaustible source that perpetually regenerates the world of enjoyment \"by thought and works\"; objects never run out because the subject keeps projecting them."],["Sureśvara","\"common food is mixed; worshipping it does not free from evil\" — material consumption alone cannot liberate; the meditation must rise to the imperishable eater."],["Krishnananda","a meditation on how consciousness <em>continually manufactures its world</em>; the inexhaustibility of food is the inexhaustibility of the mind's projecting power. Notes the correction of the year-long milk-offering: real conquest of re-death is by <em>knowledge</em>, achieved at once, not by extended ritual."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical ritual notes (huta/prahuta, darśa-pūrṇamāsa, the infant's first ghee/milk); but the spiritual fruit is reserved for the knower of the imperishable Person."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the detailed ritual decoding and the milk-cosmology; Olivelle reads <em>puruṣo vā akṣitiḥ</em> as a striking statement that the human/cosmic person is the inexhaustible ground of the food-economy."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.3","title":"The three self-foods: mind, speech, breath","sa":["त्रीण्यात्मनेऽकुरुतेति । मनो वाचं प्राणं तान्यात्मनेऽकुरुत । अन्यत्रमना अभूवं नादर्शमन्यत्रमना अभूवं नाश्रौषमिति मनसा ह्येव पश्यति मनसा शृणोति । कामः संकल्पो विचिकित्सा श्रद्धाश्रद्धा धृतिरधृतिर्ह्रीर्धीर्भीरित्येतत्सर्वं मन एव । तस्मादपि पृष्ठत उपस्पृष्टो मनसा विजानाति । यः कश्च शब्दो वागेव सा । एषा ह्यन्तमायत्तैषा हि न । प्राणोऽपानो व्यान उदानः समानोऽन इत्येतत्सर्वं प्राण एव । एतन्मयो वा अयमात्मा । वाङ्मयो मनोमयः प्राणमयः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["trīṇy ātmane 'kuruteti | mano vācaṃ prāṇaṃ tāny ātmane 'kuruta | anyatramanā abhūvaṃ nādarśam anyatramanā abhūvaṃ nāśrauṣam iti manasā hy eva paśyati manasā śṛṇoti | kāmaḥ saṃkalpo vicikitsā śraddhāśraddhā dhṛtir adhṛtir hrīr dhīr bhīr ity etat sarvaṃ mana eva | tasmād api pṛṣṭhata upaspṛṣṭo manasā vijānāti | yaḥ kaśca śabdo vāg eva sā | eṣā hy antam āyattaiṣā hi na | prāṇo 'pāno vyāna udānaḥ samāno 'na ity etat sarvaṃ prāṇa eva | etanmayo vā ayam ātmā | vāṅmayo manomayaḥ prāṇamayaḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[["trīṇi ātmane akuruta iti","n. acc. pl.","\"three he made for himself\""],["manaḥ vācaṃ prāṇaṃ tāni ātmane akuruta","acc.","mind, speech, breath — these he made for himself"],["anyatra-manāḥ abhūvaṃ na adarśam","adj. nom. + 1 sg. impf. of bhū + 1 sg. aor. of dṛś","\"my mind was elsewhere, I did not see\""],["anyatra-manāḥ abhūvaṃ na aśrauṣam","+ 1 sg. aor. of śru","\"my mind was elsewhere, I did not hear\""],["manasā hi eva paśyati manasā śṛṇoti","instr. + pres.","for it is by the mind that one sees and hears"],["kāmaḥ saṃkalpaḥ vicikitsā śraddhā aśraddhā dhṛtiḥ adhṛtiḥ hrīḥ dhīḥ bhīḥ","nom.","desire, resolve, doubt, faith, faithlessness, steadfastness, unsteadiness, modesty, intelligence, fear"],["iti etat sarvaṃ manaḥ eva","n. nom.","all this is just the mind"],["tasmāt api pṛṣṭhataḥ upaspṛṣṭaḥ manasā vijānāti","adv. + pp. + instr. + pres.","hence even touched from behind, one knows (it) by the mind"],["yaḥ kaḥ ca śabdaḥ vāk eva sā","rel. + m. nom. + f. nom.","whatever sound there is, that is just speech"],["eṣā hi antam āyattā eṣā hi na","f. nom. + pp.","for it (speech) is fixed upon its end (the object); (yet in itself) it is not (that object)"],["prāṇaḥ apānaḥ vyānaḥ udānaḥ samānaḥ anaḥ","m. nom.","out-breath, in-breath, diffused breath, up-breath, equalizing breath, (general) breath"],["iti etat sarvaṃ prāṇaḥ eva","n. nom.","all this is just breath"],["etat-mayaḥ vai ayam ātmā","adj. nom.","this self is made of these"],["vāk-mayaḥ manaḥ-mayaḥ prāṇa-mayaḥ","adj. nom.","made of speech, made of mind, made of breath"]],"lit":"\"'Three he made for himself' — mind, speech, and breath: these he made for himself. ('My mind was elsewhere, I did not see; my mind was elsewhere, I did not hear' — for it is by the mind that one sees and by the mind that one hears.) Desire, resolve, doubt, faith, faithlessness, steadfastness, unsteadiness, shame, intelligence, fear — all this is just the <strong>mind</strong>; therefore even when touched from behind one knows it by the mind. Whatever sound there is, that is just <strong>speech</strong>; for it is fixed upon its object, yet in itself it is not (that object). The out-breath, in-breath, diffused, up-, equalizing, and general breaths — all this is just <strong>breath</strong>. This self is made of these: of speech, of mind, of breath.\"","para":"The \"three foods kept for the Self\" are the three inner powers: <strong>mind (manas), speech (vāc), breath (prāṇa).</strong> Mind is shown to be the <em>condition of all perception</em> (without attention, eyes and ears register nothing) and the seat of <em>all psychic states</em> (desire, doubt, faith, fear, etc., are \"all just mind\"). Speech is <em>all sound/articulation</em>. Breath is the <em>fivefold vital air</em> (prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna) plus the general <em>ana</em>. The conclusion is a famous formula: <strong>this self (the empirical person) is \"made of\" speech, mind, and breath</strong> (<em>vāṅmayo manomayaḥ prāṇamayaḥ</em>) — these three constitute the whole inner instrument.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","establishes <em>manas</em> as the inner organ presupposed by all sense-functioning (\"mind elsewhere → did not see\") and as the substrate of every mental mode (the famous list of ten states). Speech = all <em>śabda</em>; breath = the fivefold <em>vāyu</em>. The triad <em>vāc-manas-prāṇa</em> is the empirical self's whole constitution — and (next mantras) the key that maps onto worlds, Vedas, etc. For Śaṅkara this is <em>upāsanā</em>-material framing the higher Self that <em>uses</em> these instruments."],["Sureśvara","the reduction of all psychic states to <em>manas</em> shows the unity of the inner organ; the triad is the structure of the <em>jīva</em> that knowledge must trace back to its witness."],["Krishnananda","a superb early <em>psychology</em> — attention (manas) is the precondition of all experience; the ten states are the mind's modulations; the whole \"person\" is a weave of word, thought, and breath. To know this is to see the empirical self as a <em>composite instrument</em>, not the ultimate Self."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical — master these three (right speech, steady mind, regulated breath) and you have mastered the instrument of the self; the list of mental states is a map for self-observation."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the analytic list of mental functions and the fivefold prāṇa; Olivelle highlights <em>vāṅmayo manomayaḥ prāṇamayaḥ</em> as an early \"constituents of the person\" formula (cf. the <em>kośa</em> doctrine of TaittU 2)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 1.5.4–1.5.10","title":"The triad mapped onto seven domains","sa":["1.5.4 (three worlds): त्रयो लोका एत एव । वागेवायं लोको मनोऽन्तरिक्षलोकः प्राणोऽसौ लोकः ॥ ४ ॥","1.5.5 (three Vedas): त्रयो वेदा एत एव । वागेवर्ग्वेदो मनो यजुर्वेदः प्राणः सामवेदः ॥ ५ ॥","1.5.6 (gods, fathers, men): देवाः पितरो मनुष्या एत एव । वागेव देवा मनः पितरः प्राणो मानुष्याः ॥ ६ ॥","1.5.7 (father, mother, child): पिता माता प्रजैत एव । मन एव पिता वाङ्माता प्राणः प्रजा ॥ ७ ॥","1.5.8 (the known): विज्ञातं विजिज्ञास्यमविज्ञातमेत एव । यत्किंच विज्ञातं वाचस्तद्रूपम् । वाग्घि विज्ञाता । वागेनं तद्भूत्वावति ॥ ८ ॥","1.5.9 (the to-be-known): यत्किंच विजिज्ञास्यं मनसस्तद्रूपम् । मनो हि विजिज्ञास्यम् । मन एनं तद्भूत्वावति ॥ ९ ॥","1.5.10 (the unknown): यत्किंचाविज्ञातं प्राणस्य तद्रूपम् । प्राणो ह्यविज्ञातः । प्राण एनं तद्भूत्वावति ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["trayo lokā eta eva | vāg evāyaṃ loko mano 'ntarikṣalokaḥ prāṇo 'sau lokaḥ || 4 ||","trayo vedā eta eva | vāg evargvedo mano yajurvedaḥ prāṇaḥ sāmavedaḥ || 5 ||","devā pitaro manuṣyā eta eva | vāg eva devā manaḥ pitaraḥ prāṇo mānuṣyāḥ || 6 ||","pitā mātā prajaita eva | mana eva pitā vāṅ mātā prāṇaḥ prajā || 7 ||","vijñātaṃ vijijñāsyam avijñātam eta eva | yat kiñca vijñātaṃ vācas tad rūpam | vāg ghi vijñātā | vāg enaṃ tad bhūtvāvati || 8 ||","yat kiñca vijijñāsyaṃ manasas tad rūpam | mano hi vijijñāsyam | mana enaṃ tad bhūtvāvati || 9 ||","yat kiñcāvijñātaṃ prāṇasya tad rūpam | prāṇo hy avijñātaḥ | prāṇa enaṃ tad bhūtvāvati || 10 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>Consolidated correspondence table:</strong><br>\"The known, the to-be-known, the unknown — (these are) just these (three). Whatever is known is a form of <strong>speech</strong>, for speech is the known; speech, becoming that, protects him (the knower).\" <em>(1.5.9: the to-be-known is a form of mind…; 1.5.10: the unknown is a form of breath….)</em>","para":"The single inner triad <strong>speech–mind–breath</strong> is shown to be the hidden structure of seven great domains: the three worlds, the three Vedas, the three classes of beings, the family unit, and the three epistemic regions (known / to-be-known / unknown). Especially notable is <strong>1.5.8–10</strong>: everything <em>known</em> belongs to <strong>speech</strong> (the articulated, the named); everything <em>yet-to-be-known</em> (sought, investigated) belongs to <strong>mind</strong>; everything <em>unknowable/unknown</em> belongs to <strong>breath</strong> (the pre-cognitive vital ground). Whoever knows this is \"protected\" by each, since he has identified with the whole field of knowing.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","these <em>adhyāsa</em>-meditations train the student to see the <em>one triad</em> underlying apparently separate orders — a discipline in unifying perception. The epistemic triad (1.5.8–10) is subtle: the <em>known</em> is co-extensive with <em>speech/name</em> (to know is to name); the <em>to-be-known</em> with <em>mind</em> (the inquiring faculty); the <em>unknown</em> with <em>breath</em> (the vital, pre-conceptual base, never fully objectified). \"Becoming that, it protects\" = identifying with the whole cognitive field secures the knower."],["Sureśvara","the correspondences reduce multiplicity to the triad, and the triad ultimately to the one Self — the standard reductive ascent."],["Krishnananda","a meditation that <em>the same three powers</em> (word, thought, life) compose every level of reality, micro and macro; especially the insight that the <em>unknown</em> is the province of <em>prāṇa</em> — the living ground always exceeds what mind can objectify."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","use the table as a set of identifications for <em>saguṇa</em> meditation, gathering the cosmos into one's own three faculties."],["Deussen/Olivelle","classic Upaniṣadic <em>bandhu</em>-tables; Olivelle notes the elegant epistemic mapping (known/to-be-known/unknown) as a rare early taxonomy of knowledge."]],"notes":["<em>Seven parallel correspondences identify the</em> <strong>speech–mind–breath</strong> <em>triad with seven cosmic/epistemic triads. (In 1.5.8–10 the correlation shifts: speech = the</em> known<em>, mind = the</em> to-be-known<em>, breath = the</em> unknown<em>, each \"becoming that, protects\" the knower.)</em>","<table><tr><td>Domain (mantra)</td><td><strong>Speech (vāc)</strong> =</td><td><strong>Mind (manas)</strong> =</td><td><strong>Breath (prāṇa)</strong> =</td></tr><tr><td>three worlds (1.5.4)</td><td>this (earth-)world</td><td>the mid-region world</td><td>yonder (sky-)world</td></tr><tr><td>three Vedas (1.5.5)</td><td>Ṛgveda</td><td>Yajurveda</td><td>Sāmaveda</td></tr><tr><td>three orders (1.5.6)</td><td>the gods</td><td>the fathers (ancestors)</td><td>human beings</td></tr><tr><td>family (1.5.7)</td><td>the mother</td><td><strong>the father</strong></td><td>the offspring</td></tr><tr><td>knowledge (1.5.8–10)</td><td>the <strong>known</strong> (vijñāta)</td><td>the <strong>to-be-known</strong> (vijijñāsya)</td><td>the <strong>unknown</strong> (avijñāta)</td></tr></table>","<em>(Note: in 1.5.7 the order shifts — mind is the father, speech the mother, breath the child. In 1.5.8–10 the formula adds: \"for X is the known/to-be-known/unknown; becoming that, it protects (avati) the knower.\")</em>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.11","title":"The cosmic body of Speech","sa":["तस्यै वाचः पृथिवी शरीरम् । ज्योतीरूपमयमग्निः । तद्यावत्येव वाक्तावती पृथिवी तावानयमग्निः ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["tasyai vācaḥ pṛthivī śarīram | jyotīrūpam ayam agniḥ | tad yāvaty eva vāk tāvatī pṛthivī tāvān ayam agniḥ || 11 ||"],"words":[["tasyai vācaḥ pṛthivī śarīram","f. gen. + f. nom. + n. nom.","of that Speech, the earth is the body"],["jyotī-rūpam ayam agniḥ","n. nom. + m. nom.","this fire is its luminous form"],["tat yāvatī eva vāk tāvatī pṛthivī tāvān ayam agniḥ","correl.","therefore as far as speech extends, so far the earth, so far this fire"]],"lit":"\"Of this Speech, the <strong>earth</strong> is the body, and this <strong>fire</strong> is its luminous form. Therefore, as far as speech extends, so far extends the earth, so far this fire.\"","para":"Each member of the triad is now given a <strong>cosmic \"body\" (gross) and a \"luminous form\" (the deity/light)</strong>. Speech: body = <strong>earth</strong>, light = <strong>fire</strong> — and they are <em>co-extensive</em> (speech, earth, and fire have the same \"reach\"). Macrocosm and microcosm measure each other.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>śarīra</em> (gross seat) and <em>jyotī-rūpa</em> (luminous/divine form) give speech an <em>adhibhūta</em> and <em>adhidaiva</em> aspect; co-extensivity asserts the identity of the faculty with its cosmic field."],["Sureśvara","the equality of \"reach\" denies any real boundary between the inner faculty and the outer cosmos."],["Krishnananda","your speech is not confined to your mouth — its cosmic body is the whole earth, its light the universal fire; a meditation expanding the faculty to cosmic dimensions."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","an <em>adhidaiva</em> meditation on speech as earth-and-fire."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the body/light (gross/luminous) double-aspect schema applied to each member of the triad."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.12","title":"The cosmic body of Mind; the birth of Indra (Prāṇa)","sa":["अथैतस्य मनसो द्यौः शरीरम् । ज्योतीरूपमसावादित्यः । तद्यावदेव मनस्तावती द्यौस्तावानसावादित्यः । तौ मिथुनं समैताम् । ततः प्राणोऽजायत । स इन्द्रः । स एषोऽसपत्नः । द्वितीयो वै सपत्नः । नास्य सपत्नो भवति य एवं वेद ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["athaitasya manaso dyauḥ śarīram | jyotīrūpam asāv ādityaḥ | tad yāvad eva manas tāvatī dyaus tāvān asāv ādityaḥ | tau mithunaṃ samaitām | tataḥ prāṇo 'jāyata | sa indraḥ | sa eṣo 'sapatnaḥ | dvitīyo vai sapatnaḥ | nāsya sapatno bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 12 ||"],"words":[["atha etasya manasaḥ dyauḥ śarīram","n. gen. + f. nom. + n. nom.","now of this Mind, the sky is the body"],["jyotī-rūpam asau ādityaḥ","+ m. nom.","yonder sun is its luminous form"],["tat yāvat eva manaḥ tāvatī dyauḥ tāvān asau ādityaḥ","correl.","as far as mind, so far the sky, so far the sun"],["tau mithunaṃ samaitām","nom. du. + acc. + 3 du. impf. of sam-i","the two (speech & mind) united as a pair"],["tataḥ prāṇaḥ ajāyata","abl. + impf.","thence breath was born"],["saḥ indraḥ","m. nom.","he is Indra"],["saḥ eṣaḥ asapatnaḥ dvitīyaḥ vai sapatnaḥ","m. nom.","he is without a rival; for a rival comes from a second"],["na asya sapatnaḥ bhavati yaḥ evaṃ veda","gen. + pf.","he who knows this has no rival"]],"lit":"\"Now of this <strong>Mind</strong>, the <strong>sky</strong> is the body, and yonder <strong>sun</strong> is its luminous form; as far as mind extends, so far the sky, so far the sun. The two (speech and mind) united as a couple, and thence <strong>breath</strong> was born — he is <strong>Indra</strong>. He is without a rival, for a rival comes (only) from a second. He who knows this has no rival.\"","para":"Mind: body = <strong>sky</strong>, light = <strong>sun</strong>. Then <strong>speech and mind unite</strong> (as the first mithuna of 1.4.3 in a new key), and from their union <strong>breath (prāṇa) is born — identified with Indra</strong>, the chief of the gods. Because breath is the <em>one</em> central power (recall 1.3.7), it is <strong>rival-less</strong> (<em>asapatna</em>): rivalry, like fear, presupposes \"a second.\" The knower likewise becomes rival-less.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","breath born of the speech-mind union and called Indra (the foremost deity) re-asserts prāṇa's supremacy from 1.3; \"without a rival\" because it is the single life on which the dual organs depend — duality (a \"second\") is the source of all rivalry."],["Sureśvara","\"a rival comes from a second\" parallels \"fear from a second\" (1.4.2) — both rivalry and fear are marks of duality, absent in the one Self/breath."],["Krishnananda","prāṇa-as-Indra is the king of the faculties; meditating on oneself as that central power dissolves the very basis of competition — there is no \"other\" to rival."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a meditation conferring <em>asapatnatva</em> (rival-lessness/pre-eminence); practically, rooting identity in the one life ends rivalrous striving."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the cosmogonic detail (prāṇa = Indra, born of mind+speech); Olivelle compares the recurring elevation of breath to chief deity."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.13","title":"The cosmic body of Breath; finite vs. infinite meditation","sa":["अथैतस्य प्राणस्यापः शरीरम् । ज्योतीरूपमसौ चन्द्रः । तद्यावानेव प्राणस्तावत्य आपस्तावानसौ चन्द्रः । त एते सर्व एव समाः सर्वेऽनन्ताः । स यो हैतानन्तवत उपास्तेऽन्तवन्तं स लोकं जयति । अथ यो हैताननन्तानुपास्तेऽनन्तं स लोकं जयति ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["athaitasya prāṇasyāpaḥ śarīram | jyotīrūpam asau candraḥ | tad yāvān eva prāṇas tāvatya āpas tāvān asau candraḥ | ta ete sarva eva samāḥ sarve 'nantāḥ | sa yo haitān antavata upāste 'ntavantaṃ sa lokaṃ jayati | atha yo haitān anantān upāste 'nantaṃ sa lokaṃ jayati || 13 ||"],"words":[["atha etasya prāṇasya āpaḥ śarīram","m. gen. + f. nom. pl. + n. nom.","now of this Breath, the waters are the body"],["jyotī-rūpam asau candraḥ","+ m. nom.","yonder moon is its luminous form"],["tat yāvān eva prāṇaḥ tāvatyaḥ āpaḥ tāvān asau candraḥ","correl.","as far as breath, so far the waters, so far the moon"],["te ete sarve eva samāḥ sarve anantāḥ","m. nom. pl.","all these are equal, all infinite"],["saḥ yaḥ ha etān antavataḥ upāste antavantaṃ saḥ lokaṃ jayati","acc. pl. (finite) + pres. + acc.","he who worships them as finite wins a finite world"],["atha yaḥ ha etān anantān upāste anantaṃ saḥ lokaṃ jayati","acc. pl. (infinite)","but he who worships them as infinite wins an infinite world"]],"lit":"\"Now of this <strong>Breath</strong>, the <strong>waters</strong> are the body, and yonder <strong>moon</strong> is its luminous form; as far as breath extends, so far the waters, so far the moon. All these (six — earth/fire, sky/sun, waters/moon) are equal, all infinite. He who worships them as <strong>finite</strong> wins a finite world; but he who worships them as <strong>infinite</strong> wins an infinite world.\"","para":"Breath: body = <strong>waters</strong>, light = <strong>moon</strong>. The whole set of correspondences (speech/earth/fire, mind/sky/sun, breath/waters/moon) is declared <strong>equal and infinite</strong>. A decisive meditative instruction follows: <em>the fruit matches the conception</em> — meditate on these as <strong>bounded</strong> and you gain a <strong>bounded</strong> result; meditate on them as <strong>infinite</strong> and you gain the <strong>infinite</strong>. The quality of one's vision determines the quality of one's attainment.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","a cardinal principle of <em>upāsanā</em>: <em>yathā-dṛṣṭi tathā-siddhi</em> — as you conceive the object of meditation, so is your result. Conceiving the triad as finite yields a perishable world; conceiving it as infinite (which is its truth, since the powers are boundless) yields the infinite. This grades meditators by the scope of their vision."],["Sureśvara","the <em>ananta</em> (infinite) conception is the higher truth; finitude is a self-imposed limit on a reality that is in fact boundless."],["Krishnananda","\"You become what you contemplate.\" The universe answers the magnitude of your conception — small vision, small result; infinite vision, infinite result. A powerful charter for expansive meditation."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical — always meditate on the highest, most infinite form; never on a limited one, for the result is commensurate."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the explicit grading of fruits by mode of meditation (finite vs. infinite); Olivelle: an early statement that the <em>quality</em> of religious knowledge determines its eschatological reward."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.14","title":"The Year-Prajāpati of sixteen parts","sa":["स एष संवत्सरः प्रजापतिः षोडशकलः । तस्य रात्रय एव पञ्चदश कला । ध्रुवैवास्य षोडशी कला । स रात्रिभिरेवा च पूर्यतेऽप च क्षीयते । सोऽमावास्यां रात्रिमेतया षोडश्या कलया सर्वमिदं प्राणभृदनुप्रविश्य ततः प्रातर्जायते । तस्मादेतां रात्रिं प्राणभृतः प्राणं न विच्छिन्द्यादपि कृकलासस्यैतस्या एव देवताया अपचित्यै ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["sa eṣa saṃvatsaraḥ prajāpatiḥ ṣoḍaśakalaḥ | tasya rātraya eva pañcadaśa kalā | dhruvaivāsya ṣoḍaśī kalā | sa rātribhir evā ca pūryate 'pa ca kṣīyate | so 'māvāsyāṃ rātrim etayā ṣoḍaśyā kalayā sarvam idaṃ prāṇabhṛd anupraviśya tataḥ prātar jāyate | tasmād etāṃ rātriṃ prāṇabhṛtaḥ prāṇaṃ na vicchindyād api kṛkalāsasyaitasyā eva devatāyā apacityai || 14 ||"],"words":[["saḥ eṣaḥ saṃvatsaraḥ prajāpatiḥ ṣoḍaśa-kalaḥ","m. nom. + adj.","this Year is Prajāpati, of sixteen parts (kalā)"],["tasya rātrayaḥ eva pañcadaśa kalā","f. nom. pl. + f. nom.","his fifteen parts are the nights (the waxing/waning days)"],["dhruvā eva asya ṣoḍaśī kalā","f. nom. (fixed) + ord.","his sixteenth part is the constant (unchanging) one"],["saḥ rātribhiḥ eva ā ca pūryate apa ca kṣīyate","instr. pl. + pres. pass.","by the nights he is filled and (again) wanes"],["saḥ amāvāsyāṃ rātrim etayā ṣoḍaśyā kalayā","f. acc. + instr.","he, on the new-moon night, by this sixteenth part"],["sarvam idaṃ prāṇabhṛt anupraviśya tataḥ prātaḥ jāyate","acc. + n. acc. (breath-bearing) + ger. + adv. + pres.","having entered all this that bears breath, is reborn next morning"],["tasmāt etāṃ rātriṃ prāṇabhṛtaḥ prāṇaṃ na vicchindyāt","f. acc. + gen./acc. + opt. of vi-chid","therefore on this (new-moon) night one should not cut off the life of any breathing thing"],["api kṛkalāsasya etasyāḥ eva devatāyāḥ apacityai","gen. (lizard) + f. gen. + f. dat.","not even of a lizard, out of honour to this deity"]],"lit":"\"This <strong>Year</strong> is Prajāpati, of sixteen parts (<em>kalā</em>). His fifteen parts are the nights (the lunar days), and his sixteenth part is the constant (unchanging) one. By the nights he is filled and wanes. On the new-moon night, by this sixteenth part, he enters all that bears breath and is reborn the next morning. Therefore on this night one should not cut off the life of any breathing creature — not even of a lizard — out of honour to this deity.\"","para":"Prajāpati is figured as the <strong>Year / the Moon, having sixteen <em>kalās</em> (digits/phases).</strong> Fifteen wax and wane with the lunar nights; the <strong>sixteenth is the <em>dhruva</em>, the constant residue</strong> that never disappears. At the dark of the moon (new moon), when the visible fifteen are spent, Prajāpati survives in that sixteenth part by <strong>entering all living things</strong> (the moon's \"remaining digit\" hides in breathing creatures), to re-emerge at dawn. Hence an ethical injunction: <strong>harm no living thing on the new-moon night</strong>, for the deity then dwells in all that breathes.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the sixteenth (<em>dhruva</em>) digit is the imperishable residue surviving every waning — a symbol of the deathless that persists through all decay. Its entering living beings on the new moon grounds the reverence for life; the meditation is on the indestructible part that survives apparent total loss."],["Sureśvara","the constant sixteenth amid the perishing fifteen models the changeless Self amid the changing states — what never wanes."],["Krishnananda","a meditation on the <em>indestructible core</em> that survives every diminution; even when all visible vitality is gone (new moon), the essence persists and re-creates. Notes the non-violence injunction as flowing from seeing the divine in all breathing things."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical <em>ahiṃsā</em> teaching tied to the lunar calendar; reverence for life because the deity indwells all that breathes."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the sixteen-kalā lunar mysticism (cf. Praśna 6, the sixteen-part Person); Olivelle reads the <em>dhruva</em> digit as the imperishable element and the new-moon taboo as ritual non-violence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.15","title":"The same sixteen-part Person: wealth and the Self","sa":["यो वै स संवत्सरः प्रजापतिः षोडशकलोऽयमेव स योऽयमेवंवित्पुरुषः । तस्य वित्तमेव पञ्चदश कलाः । आत्मैवास्य षोडशी कला । स वित्तेनैवा च पूर्यतेऽप च क्षीयते । तदेतन्नभ्यं यदयमात्मा । प्रधिर्वित्तम् । तस्माद्यद्यपि सर्वज्यानिं जीयत आत्मना चेज्जीवति प्रधिनागादित्येवाहुः ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["yo vai sa saṃvatsaraḥ prajāpatiḥ ṣoḍaśakalo 'yam eva sa yo 'yam evaṃvit puruṣaḥ | tasya vittam eva pañcadaśa kalāḥ | ātmaivāsya ṣoḍaśī kalā | sa vittenaivā ca pūryate 'pa ca kṣīyate | tad etan nabhyaṃ yad ayam ātmā | pradhir vittam | tasmād yady api sarvajyāniṃ jīyata ātmanā cej jīvati pradhināgād ity evāhuḥ || 15 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ vai saḥ saṃvatsaraḥ prajāpatiḥ ṣoḍaśa-kalaḥ","m. nom.","that Year-Prajāpati of sixteen parts —"],["ayam eva saḥ yaḥ ayam evaṃvit puruṣaḥ","m. nom.","he is just this person here who knows thus"],["tasya vittam eva pañcadaśa kalāḥ","n. nom. + f. nom. pl.","his fifteen parts are (his) wealth/possessions"],["ātmā eva asya ṣoḍaśī kalā","m. nom. + ord.","his sixteenth part is the Self"],["saḥ vittena eva ā ca pūryate apa ca kṣīyate","instr. + pres.","by wealth he waxes and wanes"],["tat etat nabhyaṃ yat ayam ātmā","n. nom. (the hub/nave)","this Self is the nave (of the wheel)"],["pradhiḥ vittam","m. nom. (the felly/rim)","wealth is the felly (rim)"],["tasmāt yadi api sarva-jyāniṃ jīyate","f. acc. (total loss) + 3 sg. pres. pass. of jyā","therefore even if he suffers the loss of everything"],["ātmanā cet jīvati pradhinā agāt iti eva āhuḥ","instr. + pres. + instr. + 3 sg. aor. of gā + 3 pl. pf.","if he himself survives, they say \"he has lost only the rim\""]],"lit":"\"That sixteen-part Year-Prajāpati is just this person here who knows thus. His fifteen parts are his wealth, and his sixteenth part is the <strong>Self</strong>. By wealth he waxes and wanes. This <strong>Self is the nave</strong> (hub of the wheel), and <strong>wealth is the felly</strong> (rim). Therefore even if a man suffers the loss of everything, so long as he himself survives, they say: 'He has lost only the rim.'\"","para":"The cosmic figure is brought home to the <strong>individual</strong>: the knowing person, too, has sixteen parts — <strong>fifteen = his wealth/externals</strong> (which wax and wane), and the <strong>sixteenth = the Self</strong>, the <em>dhruva</em>, the unchanging core. As in a wheel the <strong>Self is the hub</strong> and <strong>possessions are the rim</strong>: lose the rim and the hub remains. Hence the consoling truth: <em>however total one's worldly ruin, if the Self survives, only the \"rim\" is lost</em> — the essential person is intact.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the hub/rim image makes the Self the indestructible centre and all possessions peripheral; ruin of externals is not ruin of the person. A teaching of equanimity grounded in the priority of the Self over its accessories."],["Sureśvara","\"he has lost only the rim\" — the Self (hub) is never among the things that can be lost; identification with it confers imperturbability."],["Krishnananda","one of the great consolations of the Upaniṣad — your wealth, status, body can all wane like the moon's digits, but the <em>ātman</em> (the sixteenth, the hub) abides; \"lost the rim, kept the hub.\" Security is ontological, not financial."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical detachment — hold wealth as the rim, the Self as the hub; calamity touches only the periphery of one rooted in the Self."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the wheel-metaphor (nabhya/pradhi) and the application of cosmic numerology to the individual; Olivelle reads it as an ethic of resilience centred on the imperishable self."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.16","title":"Three worlds: won by son, by rite, by knowledge","sa":["अथ त्रयो वाव लोका मनुष्यलोकः पितृलोको देवलोक इति । सोऽयं मनुष्यलोकः पुत्रेणैव जय्यो नान्येन कर्मणा । कर्मणा पितृलोकः । विद्यया देवलोकः । देवलोको वै लोकानां श्रेष्ठः । तस्माद्विद्यां प्रशंसन्ति ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["atha trayo vāva lokā manuṣyalokaḥ pitṛloko devaloka iti | so 'yaṃ manuṣyalokaḥ putreṇaiva jayyo nānyena karmaṇā | karmaṇā pitṛlokaḥ | vidyayā devalokaḥ | devaloko vai lokānāṃ śreṣṭhaḥ | tasmād vidyāṃ praśaṃsanti || 16 ||"],"words":[["atha trayaḥ vāva lokāḥ","m. nom. pl.","now there are just three worlds:"],["manuṣya-lokaḥ pitṛ-lokaḥ deva-lokaḥ iti","m. nom.","the world of men, of the fathers, of the gods"],["saḥ ayaṃ manuṣya-lokaḥ putreṇa eva jayyaḥ","instr. + gerundive","this world of men is to be won by a son alone"],["na anyena karmaṇā","instr.","not by any other act"],["karmaṇā pitṛ-lokaḥ","instr.","the world of the fathers (is won) by rite"],["vidyayā deva-lokaḥ","instr.","the world of the gods by knowledge"],["deva-lokaḥ vai lokānāṃ śreṣṭhaḥ","m. nom. + gen. pl.","the world of the gods is the best of worlds"],["tasmāt vidyāṃ praśaṃsanti","acc. + 3 pl. pres.","therefore they praise knowledge"]],"lit":"\"Now there are just three worlds: the world of men, the world of the fathers, the world of the gods. This world of men is to be won by a son alone, not by any other act; the world of the fathers by rite (<em>karma</em>); the world of the gods by knowledge (<em>vidyā</em>). The world of the gods is the best of the worlds; therefore they praise knowledge.\"","para":"Three goals, three means: the <strong>human world</strong> is secured by <strong>progeny</strong> (a son continues one's presence here); the <strong>world of the fathers</strong> (ancestral heaven) by <strong>ritual action</strong>; the <strong>world of the gods</strong> — the <strong>highest</strong> — by <strong>knowledge</strong> (<em>vidyā</em>). Because the divine world is supreme and is won by <em>vidyā</em>, <strong>knowledge is the most praised</strong>. (This ranks <em>jñāna</em> above both <em>karma</em> and progeny — a key Upaniṣadic value-statement, and the lead-in to the <em>sampratti</em> rite of 1.5.17, which transmits all three.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","a graded <em>puruṣārtha</em> scheme — son, rite, knowledge securing the three worlds, with <em>vidyā</em> supreme because the deva-loka (its fruit) is the highest. Yet note: even the deva-loka is a <em>world</em> (and so perishable); the <em>vidyā</em> praised here is meditative knowledge yielding the best <em>result within saṃsāra</em>, distinct from the liberating <em>brahma-vidyā</em> of 1.4.10. The hierarchy nonetheless establishes knowledge over action."],["Sureśvara","reinforces the supremacy of <em>vidyā</em> over <em>karma</em> — even granting that this <em>vidyā</em> wins a (high) world, it ranks above ritual; a fortiori the liberating knowledge transcends all."],["Krishnananda","the three worlds map three modes of self-continuation: biological (son), ritual-moral (karma), and spiritual (knowledge); the highest is the path of knowledge, hence its universal praise."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical — fulfil the debt of progeny and rite, but prize <em>vidyā</em> above all as the means to the highest world."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the three-debts/three-worlds schema; Olivelle observes the explicit valorisation of knowledge over both lineage and ritual as a hallmark of the emerging Upaniṣadic ethos."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.17","title":"The Sampratti: a father's deathbed transmission to his son","sa":["अथातः सम्प्रत्तिः । यदा प्रैष्यन्मन्यतेऽथ पुत्रमाह — त्वं ब्रह्म त्वं यज्ञस्त्वं लोक इति । स पुत्रः प्रत्याहाहं ब्रह्माहं यज्ञोऽहं लोक इति । यद्वै किंचानूक्तं तस्य सर्वस्य ब्रह्मेत्येकता । ये वै के च यज्ञास्तेषां सर्वेषां यज्ञ इत्येकता । ये वै के च लोकास्तेषां सर्वेषां लोक इत्येकता । एतावद्वा इदं सर्वम् । एतन्मा सर्वं सन्नयमितो भुनजदिति । तस्मात्पुत्रमनुशिष्टं लोक्यमाहुः । तस्मादेनमनुशासति । स यदैवंविदस्माल्लोकात्प्रैत्यथैभिरेव प्राणैः सह पुत्रमाविशति । स यद्यनेन किंचिदक्ष्णया कृतं भवति तस्मादेनं सर्वस्मात्पुत्रो मुञ्चति तस्मात्पुत्रो नाम । स पुत्रेणैवास्मिँल्लोके प्रतितिष्ठति । अथैनमेते देवाः प्राणा अमृता आविशन्ति ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["athātaḥ samprattiḥ | yadā praiṣyan manyate 'tha putram āha -- tvaṃ brahma tvaṃ yajñas tvaṃ loka iti | sa putraḥ pratyāhāhaṃ brahmāhaṃ yajño 'ham loka iti | yad vai kiñcānūktaṃ tasya sarvasya brahmety ekatā | ye vai ke ca yajñās teṣāṃ sarveṣāṃ yajña ity ekatā | ye vai ke ca lokās teṣāṃ sarveṣāṃ loka ity ekatā | etāvad vā idaṃ sarvam | etan mā sarvaṃ sann ayam ito bhunajad iti | tasmāt putram anuśiṣṭaṃ lokyam āhuḥ | tasmād enam anuśāsati | sa yadaivaṃvid asmāl lokāt praity athaibhir eva prāṇaiḥ saha putram āviśati | sa yady anena kiñcid akṣṇayā kṛtaṃ bhavati tasmād enaṃ sarvasmāt putro muñcati tasmāt putro nāma | sa putreṇaivāsmil loke pratitiṣṭhati | athainam ete devāḥ prāṇā amṛtā āviśanti || 17 ||"],"words":[["atha ataḥ samprattiḥ","f. nom.","now, next, the \"handing-over\" (transmission)"],["yadā praiṣyan manyate","pres.part. (about to depart) + pres.","when (a father) thinks he is about to die"],["atha putram āha tvaṃ brahma tvaṃ yajñaḥ tvaṃ lokaḥ iti","acc. + pf.","he says to his son, \"you are the Veda (brahman), you are sacrifice, you are the world\""],["saḥ putraḥ pratyāha ahaṃ brahma ahaṃ yajñaḥ aham lokaḥ iti","3 sg. pf. of prati-ah","the son replies, \"I am the Veda, I am sacrifice, I am the world\""],["yat vai kiṃ ca anūktaṃ tasya sarvasya brahma iti ekatā","rel. + pp. + gen. + n. nom. + f. nom.","whatever is recited (Veda), the unity of all that is \"brahman\""],["ye vai ke ca yajñāḥ teṣāṃ sarveṣāṃ yajñaḥ iti ekatā","rel. + gen. pl.","whatever sacrifices there are, their unity is \"sacrifice\""],["ye vai ke ca lokāḥ teṣāṃ sarveṣāṃ lokaḥ iti ekatā","rel.","whatever worlds there are, their unity is \"world\""],["etāvat vai idaṃ sarvam","n. nom.","this whole (duty) is just this much"],["etat mā sarvaṃ san ayam itaḥ bhunajat iti","acc. + pres.part. + 3 sg. opt. of bhuj","\"being all this, let him (the son) sustain me from here (after death)\""],["tasmāt putram anuśiṣṭaṃ lokyam āhuḥ","acc. + pp. + adj. (world-winning)","therefore a well-instructed son is called \"world-conferring\""],["tasmāt enam anuśāsati","acc. + pres.","therefore they so instruct him"],["saḥ yadā evaṃvit asmāt lokāt praiti","rel. + abl. + pres. of pra-i","when such a knower departs this world"],["atha ebhiḥ eva prāṇaiḥ saha putram āviśati","instr. pl. + acc. + pres. of ā-viś","he enters into the son together with these vital breaths"],["saḥ yadi anena kiṃ cit akṣṇayā kṛtaṃ bhavati","instr. + adv. (wrongly) + pp.","if anything has been done amiss by him"],["tasmāt enaṃ sarvasmāt putraḥ muñcati tasmāt putraḥ nāma","abl. + acc. + pres. of muc","the son frees him from it all — hence \"putra\" (deliverer) by name"],["saḥ putreṇa eva asmin loke pratitiṣṭhati","instr. + loc. + pres.","by the son alone he stands firm in this world"],["atha enam ete devāḥ prāṇāḥ amṛtāḥ āviśanti","acc. + nom. pl. + pres.","then these divine, immortal breaths enter him"]],"lit":"\"Now, next, the <strong>transmission</strong> (<em>sampratti</em>). When a father thinks he is about to die, he says to his son, 'You are the Veda (<em>brahman</em>), you are the sacrifice, you are the world.' The son replies, 'I am the Veda, I am the sacrifice, I am the world.' — Whatever has been recited, the unity of all that is 'brahman'; whatever sacrifices there are, their unity is 'sacrifice'; whatever worlds there are, their unity is 'world.' This whole (duty) is just this much. '(The son) being all this, let him sustain me from here (after my death).' Therefore a well-instructed son is called 'world-conferring,' and so they instruct him. When such a knower departs this world, he enters into the son together with these vital breaths. If anything has been done amiss by him, the son frees him from it all — therefore he is called <em>putra</em> (deliverer). By the son alone he stands firm in this world. Then the divine, immortal breaths (faculties) enter him (the son).\"","para":"A solemn <strong>deathbed rite</strong> in which a dying father <em>transmits his entire ritual-cosmic identity</em> to his son in a threefold formula — <strong>\"You are brahman (the Veda), the sacrifice, the world\"</strong> — which the son accepts: <strong>\"I am...\"</strong> Thus the son inherits the father's whole religious self; the father, when he dies, <em>enters into the son</em> with his faculties and so <strong>continues in this world through him</strong>. The son even <strong>frees</strong> the father from any lapses (a folk-etymology: <em>putra</em> ← <em>put-trā</em>, \"deliverer/saviour\"). This secures the <em>manuṣya-loka</em> (1.5.16) — won \"by a son alone.\"","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>sampratti</em> formalises the handing-on of one's ritual-Vedic identity; the threefold \"you are brahman/yajña/loka\" transfers the three means (study, rite, world-winning). It secures continuation in the human world but is <em>karma/saṃtāna</em>-religion — the relative immortality of lineage, distinct from the absolute liberation of Self-knowledge."],["Sureśvara","the rite belongs to the domain of <em>karma</em>/continuity, not <em>mokṣa</em>; the son perpetuates the father's worldly self, whereas knowledge alone ends the need for such continuation."],["Krishnananda","a moving picture of the ancient ideal of <em>self-perpetuation through the son</em> — the father literally lives on in the child who has accepted his identity. Notes the <em>putra</em>-etymology (the son who delivers) and the ethic of intergenerational responsibility."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the dignity of the parent-child bond raised to a sacrament; but the higher path remains Self-knowledge, beyond all need for heirs."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a precious ethnographic record of the deathbed transmission ceremony; Olivelle treats it as evidence for the lineage-based \"immortality\" that the knowledge-path later supersedes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 1.5.18–1.5.20","title":"The divine Speech, Mind, and Breath enter the knower","sa":["1.5.18 (divine speech): पृथिव्यै चैनमग्नेश्च दैवी वागाविशति । सा वै दैवी वाग्यया यद्यदेव वदति तत्तद्भवति ॥ १८ ॥","1.5.19 (divine mind): दिवश्चैनमादित्याच्च दैवं मन आविशति । तद्वै दैवं मनो येनानन्द्येव भवत्यथो न शोचति ॥ १९ ॥","1.5.20 (divine breath): अद्भ्यश्चैनं चन्द्रमसश्च दैवः प्राण आविशति । स वै दैवः प्राणो यः संचरंश्चासंचरंश्च न व्यथतेऽथो न रिष्यति । स एष एवंवित्सर्वेषां भूतानामात्मा भवति । यथैषा देवतैवं सः । यथैतां देवतां सर्वाणि भूतान्यवन्त्येवं हैवंविदं सर्वाणि भूतान्यवन्ति । यदु किंचेमाः प्रजाः शोचन्त्यमैवासां तद्भवति । पुण्यमेवामुं गच्छति । न ह वै देवान्पापं गच्छति ॥ २० ॥"],"iast":["pṛthivyai cainam agneś ca daivī vāg āviśati | sā vai daivī vāg yayā yad yad eva vadati tat tad bhavati || 18 ||","divaś cainam ādityāc ca daivaṃ mana āviśati | tad vai daivaṃ mano yenānandy eva bhavaty atho na śocati || 19 ||","adbhyaś cainaṃ candramasaś ca daivaḥ prāṇa āviśati | sa vai daivaḥ prāṇo yaḥ saṃcaraṃś cāsaṃcaraṃś ca na vyathate 'tho na riṣyati | sa eṣa evaṃvit sarveṣāṃ bhūtānām ātmā bhavati | yathaiṣā devataivaṃ saḥ | yathaitāṃ devatāṃ sarvāṇi bhūtāny avanty evaṃ haivaṃvidaṃ sarvāṇi bhūtāny avanti | yad u kiñcemāḥ prajāḥ śocanty amaivāsāṃ tad bhavati | puṇyam evāmuṃ gacchati | na ha vai devān pāpaṃ gacchati || 20 ||"],"words":[["pṛthivyai ca enam agneḥ ca daivī vāk āviśati","abl. (irreg.) + acc. + f. nom. + pres.","from the earth and the fire the divine speech enters him"],["sā vai daivī vāk yayā yat yat eva vadati tat tat bhavati","f. nom. + instr.","that is the divine speech by which whatever one says comes to be"],["divaḥ ca enam ādityāt ca daivaṃ manaḥ āviśati","abl. + n. nom.","from the sky and the sun the divine mind enters him"],["tat vai daivaṃ manaḥ yena ānandī eva bhavati atho na śocati","instr. + adj. (blissful)","that is the divine mind by which one is happy and grieves not"],["adbhyaḥ ca enaṃ candramasaḥ ca daivaḥ prāṇaḥ āviśati","abl. pl. + abl. + m. nom.","from the waters and the moon the divine breath enters him"],["saḥ vai daivaḥ prāṇaḥ yaḥ saṃcaran ca asaṃcaran ca na vyathate atho na riṣyati","pres.part. + pres.","that is the divine breath which, moving or not moving, is neither pained nor injured"],["saḥ eṣaḥ evaṃvit sarveṣāṃ bhūtānām ātmā bhavati","gen. pl. + m. nom.","such a knower becomes the Self of all beings"],["yathā eṣā devatā evaṃ saḥ","f. nom.","as is this deity, so is he"],["yathā etāṃ devatāṃ sarvāṇi bhūtāni avanti evaṃ ha evaṃvidaṃ sarvāṇi bhūtāni avanti","acc. + nom. pl. + pres. of av","as all beings favour this deity, so all beings favour the knower"],["yat u kiṃ ca imāḥ prajāḥ śocanti ama eva āsāṃ tat bhavati","rel. + nom. pl. + adv. + gen.","whatever grief these creatures suffer stays with them (does not reach him)"],["puṇyam eva amuṃ gacchati na ha vai devān pāpaṃ gacchati","n. nom. + acc.","only good goes to him; evil does not reach the gods"]],"lit":"\"From the earth and fire the <strong>divine speech</strong> enters him — that divine speech by which whatever one says comes true. From the sky and sun the <strong>divine mind</strong> enters him — that divine mind by which one becomes blissful and grieves no more. From the waters and moon the <strong>divine breath</strong> enters him — that divine breath which, whether moving or still, is neither pained nor injured. Such a knower becomes the <strong>Self of all beings</strong>: as is the deity, so is he; as all beings favour the deity, so all favour the knower. Whatever grief these creatures suffer remains with them (does not touch him); only good comes to him — for <strong>evil does not reach the gods</strong>.\"","para":"In place of his transmitted earthly faculties, the knower is now infused with their <strong>divine, cosmic forms</strong>: a <strong>speech whose every word comes true</strong>, a <strong>mind that is pure bliss without grief</strong>, and a <strong>breath untouched by pain or injury</strong> whether active or at rest. So perfected, he <strong>becomes the Self of all beings</strong>, loved and favoured by all, beyond the reach of sorrow and evil — for, having become divine, \"evil does not reach the gods.\"","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>daiva</em> (divine, cosmic) faculties replace the limited human ones for the <em>evaṃvit</em> (knower); the threefold endowment (effective speech, griefless mind, painless breath) describes the perfected state. \"Becomes the Self of all beings\" recalls 1.4.10 — the knower is universalised, beyond grief and evil like the gods."],["Sureśvara","the transition from human to divine faculties marks the knower's passage from the perishable to the cosmic; immunity to grief and evil is the natural condition of one identified with the deity."],["Krishnananda","a vision of the <em>deified</em> knower — speech that creates, mind that is joy itself, life that cannot be hurt. The grief of the world \"stays with the world\"; the realised one stands in the sorrow-free, evil-free station of the gods."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the fruit of the meditation — <em>satya-saṃkalpa</em> (true-willed speech), unbroken inner joy, and invulnerable vitality; goals of the perfected sādhaka."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the symmetry with 1.5.11–13 (the cosmic bodies now \"enter\" the knower); Olivelle reads it as the apotheosis of the knower into the cosmic powers."]],"notes":["<em>Having transmitted his earthly self to the son, the dying knower receives in their place the</em> <strong>divine (daiva)</strong> <em>forms of speech, mind, and breath, drawn from their cosmic sources (1.5.11–13: earth/fire, sky/sun, waters/moon):</em>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.21","title":"Vrata-mīmāṃsā (the inner contest): the organs and the central breath","sa":["अथातो व्रतमीमांसा । प्रजापतिर्ह कर्माणि ससृजे । तानि सृष्टान्यन्योऽन्येनास्पर्धन्त । वदिष्याम्येवाहमिति वाग्दध्रे । द्रक्ष्याम्यहमिति चक्षुः । श्रोष्याम्यहमिति श्रोत्रम् । एवमन्यानि कर्माणि यथाकर्म । तानि मृत्युः श्रमो भूत्वोपयेमे । तान्याप्नोत् । तान्याप्त्वा मृत्युरवारुन्ध । तस्माच्छ्राम्यत्येव वाक् । श्राम्यति चक्षुः । श्राम्यति श्रोत्रम् । अथेममेव नाप्नोद्योऽयं मध्यमः प्राणः । तानि ज्ञातुं दध्रिरे । अयं वै नः श्रेष्ठो यः संचरंश्चासंचरंश्च न व्यथतेऽथो न रिष्यति । हन्तास्यैव सर्वे रूपं भवामेति । त एतस्यैव सर्वे रूपमभवन् । तस्मादेत एतेनाख्यायन्ते प्राणा इति । तेन ह वाव तत्कुलमाचक्षते यस्मिन्कुले भवति य एवं वेद । य उ हैवंविदा स्पर्धतेऽनुशुष्यति । अनुशुष्य हैवान्ततो म्रियत इत्यध्यात्मम् ॥ २१ ॥"],"iast":["athāto vratamīmāṃsā | prajāpatir ha karmāṇi sasṛje | tāni sṛṣṭāny anyo 'nyenāspardhanta | vadiṣyāmy evāham iti vāg dadhre | drakṣyāmy aham iti cakṣuḥ | śroṣyāmy aham iti śrotram | evam anyāni karmāṇi yathākarmam | tāni mṛtyuḥ śramo bhūtvopayeme | tāny āpnot | tāny āptvā mṛtyur avārundha | tasmāc chrāmyaty eva vāk | śrāmyati cakṣuḥ | śrāmyati śrotram | athemam eva nāpnot yo 'yaṃ madhyamaḥ prāṇaḥ | tāni jñātuṃ dadhrire | ayaṃ vai naḥ śreṣṭho yaḥ saṃcaraṃś cāsaṃcaraṃś ca na vyathate 'tho na riṣyati | hantāsyaiva sarve rūpaṃ bhavāmeti | ta etasyaiva sarve rūpam abhavan | tasmād eta etenākhyāyante prāṇā iti | tena ha vāva tat kulam ācakṣate yasmin kule bhavati ya evaṃ veda | ya u haivaṃvidā spardhate 'nuśuṣyati | anuśuṣya haivāntato mriyata ity adhyātmam || 21 ||"],"words":[["atha ataḥ vrata-mīmāṃsā","f. nom.","now, next, the inquiry into the (one) vow"],["prajāpatiḥ ha karmāṇi sasṛje","n. acc. pl. + 3 sg. pf. of sṛj","Prajāpati created the functions (organs/actions)"],["tāni sṛṣṭāni anyaḥ anyena aspardhanta","nom. pl. + impf.","once created, they contended with one another"],["vadiṣyāmi eva aham iti vāk dadhre","1 sg. fut. + f. nom. + 3 sg. pf. of dhā (ātm.)","\"I will speak,\" resolved Speech"],["drakṣyāmi aham iti cakṣuḥ; śroṣyāmi aham iti śrotram","1 sg. fut.","\"I will see,\" the Eye; \"I will hear,\" the Ear"],["evam anyāni karmāṇi yathākarmam","adv.","so the other organs, each according to its function"],["tāni mṛtyuḥ śramaḥ bhūtvā upayeme","acc. + nom. + ger. + 3 sg. pf. of upa-yam","Death, becoming fatigue, took hold of them"],["tāni āpnot tāni āptvā mṛtyuḥ avārundha","acc. + impf. of āp + ger. + impf. of ava-rudh","he reached them, and reaching them, restrained them"],["tasmāt śrāmyati eva vāk śrāmyati cakṣuḥ śrāmyati śrotram","pres. of śram","therefore speech tires, the eye tires, the ear tires"],["atha imam eva na āpnot yaḥ ayaṃ madhyamaḥ prāṇaḥ","acc. + impf. + m. nom.","but he did not reach this central breath"],["tāni jñātuṃ dadhrire","acc. + inf. + 3 pl. pf.","they (the organs) resolved to know it"],["ayaṃ vai naḥ śreṣṭhaḥ yaḥ saṃcaran ca asaṃcaran ca na vyathate atho na riṣyati","nom. + gen.","\"this is our best, which moving or still is neither pained nor injured\""],["hanta asya eva sarve rūpaṃ bhavāma iti","interj. + gen. + nom. pl. + 1 pl. opt.","\"come, let us all be (a) form of it\""],["te etasya eva sarve rūpam abhavan","nom. pl. + impf.","they all became a form of it"],["tasmāt ete etena ākhyāyante prāṇāḥ iti","nom. pl. + instr. + pres. pass.","therefore they are called, after it, \"the breaths\" (prāṇas)"],["tena ha vāva tat kulam ācakṣate yasmin kule bhavati yaḥ evaṃ veda","instr. + n. acc. + pres.","by him a family is named, the family in which a knower is born"],["yaḥ u ha evaṃvidā spardhate anuśuṣyati","rel. + instr. + pres.","whoever contends with such a knower withers"],["anuśuṣya ha eva antataḥ mriyate iti adhyātmam","ger. + pres.","having withered, in the end he dies — thus with respect to the body (adhyātma)"]],"lit":"\"Now the <strong>inquiry into the (one) vow</strong> (<em>vrata-mīmāṃsā</em>). Prajāpati created the organs/functions; once created, they contended with one another. Speech resolved, 'I will speak'; the Eye, 'I will see'; the Ear, 'I will hear'; and so the others, each by its function. Death, becoming <strong>fatigue</strong>, seized them; reaching them, he restrained them — therefore speech grows weary, the eye grows weary, the ear grows weary. But he did not reach this <strong>central breath</strong>. The organs resolved to know it: 'This is the best of us, which whether moving or still is neither pained nor injured. Come, let us all become a form of it.' And they all became forms of it; therefore they are called, after it, the <em>prāṇas</em> ('breaths'). By him a family is named — the family in which a knower of this is born. Whoever contends with such a knower withers away, and having withered, dies in the end. — Thus with respect to the body (<em>adhyātma</em>).\"","para":"A re-telling of the 1.3 contest in the key of <strong>fatigue and the \"one vow.\"</strong> The organs compete, each asserting its function — and <strong>Death, as fatigue (<em>śrama</em>), overtakes each</strong> (hence eyes, ears, speech all <em>tire</em>). Only the <strong>central breath</strong> Death cannot reach (it never tires — \"moving or still, unhurt\"). Recognising it as the best, the organs <strong>all become \"forms\" of it</strong> — which is why they are collectively called <strong>prāṇas (breaths)</strong>, named after the central breath. The knower's very lineage is honoured, and rivals against him \"wither and die.\" This is the <em>adhyātma</em> (bodily/individual) side.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Death-as-fatigue is the principle of exhaustion that defeats every <em>functioning</em> organ; the central breath alone is untiring because it is the <em>substrate</em> that sustains functioning without being a particular function. The organs \"becoming forms of it\" and being named <em>prāṇas</em> re-grounds them in the one life. The <em>vrata</em> (vow) about to be defined (1.5.23) is the untiring activity of the breath itself."],["Sureśvara","the unwearied central breath = the witness/life that does not \"act\" and so is not subject to the fatigue of action; rivals \"wither\" because they oppose the very life-principle."],["Krishnananda","a deep observation that <em>all functions tire</em> — perception, speech, even thought — but the <em>breath/life-principle does not</em>; it works ceaselessly day and night. Hence it is the true \"self\" of the organs, and the model for the one indispensable vow."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical — do not over-rely on the tiring organs; anchor in the tireless breath. The \"one vow\" (1.5.23) is the natural, ceaseless discipline of breathing itself."],["Deussen/Olivelle","compare the prāṇa-supremacy contests of 1.3, ChU 5.1, Kauṣītaki 2; Olivelle notes the distinctive <em>śrama</em> (fatigue) framing and the etymology of <em>prāṇāḥ</em> (organs) from the central <em>prāṇa</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.22","title":"The same on the cosmic side (adhidaivata): Vāyu, the deity that never sets","sa":["अथाधिदेवतम् — ज्वलिष्याम्येवाहमित्यग्निर्दध्रे । तप्स्याम्यहमित्यादित्यः । भास्याम्यहमिति चन्द्रमाः । एवमन्या देवता यथादेवतम् । स यथैषां प्राणानां मध्यमः प्राण एवमेतासां देवतानां वायुः । म्लोचन्ति ह्यन्या देवता न वायुः । सैषानस्तमिता देवता यद्वायुः ॥ २२ ॥"],"iast":["athādhidevataṃ: jvaliṣyāmy evāham ity agnir dadhre | tapsyāmy aham ity ādityaḥ | bhāsyāmy aham iti candramāḥ | evam anyā devatā yathādevatam | sa yathaiṣāṃ prāṇānāṃ madhyamaḥ prāṇa evam etāsāṃ devatānāṃ vāyuḥ | mlocanti hy anyā devatā na vāyuḥ | saiṣānastamitā devatā yad vāyuḥ || 22 ||"],"words":[["atha adhidevataṃ","n. nom. (adv.)","now with respect to the deities"],["jvaliṣyāmi eva aham iti agniḥ dadhre","1 sg. fut. + nom. + pf.","\"I will blaze,\" resolved Fire"],["tapsyāmi aham iti ādityaḥ; bhāsyāmi aham iti candramāḥ","1 sg. fut.","\"I will give heat,\" the Sun; \"I will shine,\" the Moon"],["evam anyāḥ devatāḥ yathā-devatam","nom. pl. + adv.","so the other deities, each according to its nature"],["saḥ yathā eṣāṃ prāṇānāṃ madhyamaḥ prāṇaḥ evam etāsāṃ devatānāṃ vāyuḥ","gen. pl. + nom.","as the central breath is among the organs, so is Vāyu among these deities"],["mlocanti hi anyāḥ devatāḥ na vāyuḥ","3 pl. pres. of mluc (set)","for the other deities set, but not Vāyu"],["sā eṣā anastamitā devatā yat vāyuḥ","f. nom. + pp. (non-setting)","this is the deity that never sets, namely Vāyu (Wind)"]],"lit":"\"Now with respect to the <strong>deities</strong>: Fire resolved, 'I will blaze'; the Sun, 'I will give heat'; the Moon, 'I will shine'; and so the other deities, each by its nature. As the central breath is among the organs, so is <strong>Vāyu (Wind)</strong> among these deities. For the other deities set (sink, are quenched), but not Vāyu. Vāyu is the deity that <strong>never sets</strong>.\"","para":"The cosmic counterpart (<em>adhidaivata</em>) of 1.5.21: the cosmic powers (fire, sun, moon…) each assert their function, but all of them eventually <strong>\"set\"</strong> (the fire goes out, sun and moon set). The single exception is <strong>Vāyu, the Wind</strong> — the cosmic breath — which <strong>never sets</strong>, blowing always. As the central breath is to the bodily organs, so Vāyu is to the gods: the one untiring, ever-active power. (Thus prāṇa within = Vāyu without.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the macro-micro identity <em>madhyama-prāṇa = Vāyu</em> — both are the ceaseless, never-resting power, the cosmic and bodily forms of the one life. Vāyu's \"never setting\" parallels the breath's \"never tiring.\""],["Sureśvara","the <em>anastamitā devatā</em> (the deity that never sets) is the cosmic emblem of the imperishable, ever-active life."],["Krishnananda","\"All the gods set — only the wind blows on.\" Vāyu is the cosmic prāṇa; meditate on the wind as the outer face of your own ceaseless breath."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","an <em>adhidaiva</em> meditation pairing inner breath with cosmic wind; both are the tireless sustainers."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the elegant adhyātma/adhidaivata pairing (central breath / Vāyu); Olivelle: the \"non-setting deity\" motif elevates Vāyu as the cosmic life-principle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5.23","title":"The one vow: to keep breathing","sa":["अथैष श्लोको भवति — यतश्चोदेति सूर्योऽस्तं यत्र च गच्छतीति । प्राणाद्वा एष उदेति प्राणेऽस्तमेति । तं देवाश्चक्रिरे धर्मं स एवाद्य स उ श्व इति । यद्वा एतेऽमुर्ह्यध्रियन्त तदेवाप्यद्य कुर्वन्ति । तस्मादेकमेव व्रतं चरेत्प्राण्याच्चैवापान्याच्च । नेन्मा पाप्मा मृत्युराप्नवदिति । यद्यु चरेत्समापिपयिषेत्तेनो एतस्यै देवतायै सायुज्यं सलोकतां जयति ॥ २३ ॥"],"iast":["athaiṣa śloko bhavati -- yataś codeti sūryo 'staṃ yatra ca gacchatīti | prāṇād vā eṣa udeti prāṇe 'stam eti | taṃ devāś cakrire dharmaṃ sa evādya sa u śva iti | yad vā ete 'murhy adhriyanta tad evāpy adya kurvanti | tasmād ekam eva vrataṃ caret prāṇyāc caivāpānyāc ca | nen mā pāpmā mṛtyur āpnavad iti | yady u caret samāpipayiṣet teno etasyai devatāyai sāyujyaṃ salokatāṃ jayati || 23 ||"],"words":[["atha eṣaḥ ślokaḥ bhavati","m. nom.","now there is this verse:"],["yataḥ ca udeti sūryaḥ astaṃ yatra ca gacchati iti","rel. adv. + pres. of ud-i + acc. + rel. + pres.","\"whence the sun rises and where it sets\""],["prāṇāt vai eṣaḥ udeti prāṇe astam eti","abl. + pres. + loc.","from the breath it rises, in the breath it sets"],["taṃ devāḥ cakrire dharmaṃ saḥ eva adya saḥ u śvaḥ iti","acc. + 3 pl. pf. of kṛ + acc. + adv.","the gods made it the law: it is so today and so tomorrow"],["yat vai ete amu-rhi adhriyanta tat eva api adya kurvanti","rel. + adv. (then) + impf. + pres.","what they undertook then, that very thing they still do today"],["tasmāt ekam eva vrataṃ caret","acc. + 3 sg. opt. of car","therefore one should observe only one vow:"],["prāṇyāt ca eva apānyāt ca","3 sg. opt. of prāṇ + apān","one should breathe in and breathe out"],["net mā pāpmā mṛtyuḥ āpnavat iti","(na + id) + acc. + nom. + 3 sg. subj. of āp","\"lest the evil, Death, reach me\""],["yadi u caret samāpipayiṣet","opt. + 3 sg. desid. opt. of sam-āp (caus.)","if one observes it, one should wish to carry it through to the end"],["tena u etasyai devatāyai sāyujyaṃ salokatāṃ jayati","instr. + f. dat. + acc. + acc. + pres. of ji","thereby one wins union with, and the same world as, this deity (breath/Vāyu)"]],"lit":"\"Now there is this verse: 'Whence the sun rises and where it sets' — it rises from the breath and sets in the breath; the gods made that the law (<em>dharma</em>), so it is today and so tomorrow. For what they undertook then, that very thing they do even now. Therefore one should observe only <strong>one vow</strong>: one should <strong>breathe in and breathe out</strong>, (thinking) 'lest the evil, Death, reach me.' And if one observes it, one should wish to carry it through to the end. Thereby one wins union with, and the same world as, this deity (the breath / Vāyu).\"","para":"The brāhmaṇa concludes by defining the <strong>single, essential \"vow\" (vrata)</strong> of life. The sun (and all activity) \"rises and sets in the breath\" — the breath is the ground of the whole day's cycle, a <em>dharma</em> (fixed law) the gods established once and that continues unbroken. Therefore the only indispensable observance is the breath's own ceaseless work: <strong>keep breathing in and out</strong> — the one \"vow\" Death cannot interrupt while it lasts. Sustained to the end, this identification with the breath wins <strong>sāyujya</strong> (union) and <strong>salokatā</strong> (the same world) with the deity Breath/Vāyu — the untiring, never-setting life.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the \"one vow\" is the breath's own continuous functioning, taken as a conscious <em>upāsanā</em>: while every other observance can lapse, breathing does not, and to align with it is to align with the deathless life. \"Rises and sets in the breath\" makes prāṇa the ground of all temporal activity. The fruit is <em>sāyujya/salokatā</em> with the breath-deity — the culmination of the chapter's prāṇa-vidyā."],["Sureśvara","the <em>eka-vrata</em> (one vow) of breathing is the irreducible discipline; identifying the self with the untiring breath secures union with the imperishable principle."],["Krishnananda","a beautiful close — of all vows you might keep, the one that never fails is the breath; make <em>it</em> your conscious sacrament. The day itself (\"sun rises and sets\") is governed by breath; live as that ceaseless life and you join the deathless Vāyu."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the spiritual charter of <em>prāṇāyāma</em> and breath-awareness — the simplest, most constant practice, available every moment, yielding union with the cosmic life."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the closing <em>śloka</em> and the \"single vow\" doctrine; Olivelle reads it as crowning the brāhmaṇa's exaltation of breath: the one ritual that is co-extensive with life itself."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This long brāhmaṇa weaves several threads of the Madhu-kāṇḍa into one fabric, all turning on the triad <strong>vāc–manas–prāṇa</strong> (speech–mind–breath) and the supremacy of <strong>breath/life</strong>:","1. <strong>The food-economy and the imperishable eater</strong> (1.5.1–2). The \"seven foods\" classify all sustenance; the riddle of food's inexhaustibility is solved by <em>puruṣo vā akṣitiḥ</em> — the conscious Person endlessly re-creates the world of enjoyment \"by thought and works.\" (The eater, not the eaten, is imperishable — the chapter-wide inversion of 1.2.5 and 1.3.17.)","2. <strong>The constitution of the person</strong> (1.5.3): the self is <em>made of</em> speech, mind, and breath (<em>vāṅmayo manomayaḥ prāṇamayaḥ</em>); <em>manas</em> is shown as the precondition of all perception and the seat of all psychic states.","3. <strong>The triad as the key to everything</strong> (1.5.4–10): speech/mind/breath = three worlds, three Vedas, gods/fathers/men, mother/father/child, and known/to-be-known/unknown — a sustained discipline in seeing the one triad beneath the many.","4. <strong>Cosmic bodies and the grading of meditation</strong> (1.5.11–13): each member has a gross \"body\" and a luminous \"form\" (speech: earth/fire; mind: sky/sun; breath: waters/moon); and crucially, <em>as you conceive them (finite or infinite), so is your result</em> (1.5.13).","5. <strong>The imperishable core</strong> (1.5.14–15): the sixteen-part Year/Moon-Prajāpati has a <em>dhruva</em> (constant) sixteenth digit that survives every waning; applied to the person, the Self is the <strong>hub</strong>, possessions the <strong>rim</strong> — lose everything external and \"only the rim is lost.\"","6. <strong>The ranking of means</strong> (1.5.16): son wins the human world, rite the fathers' world, <strong>knowledge the (highest) world of the gods</strong> — \"therefore they praise knowledge.\"","7. <strong>Continuity and apotheosis</strong> (1.5.17–20): the <em>sampratti</em> transmits the father's ritual self to the son (relative immortality through lineage); the dying knower is then infused with the <strong>divine</strong> speech, mind, and breath and becomes the <strong>Self of all beings</strong>, beyond grief and evil.","8. <strong>The one untiring power</strong> (1.5.21–23): in both the body (<em>adhyātma</em>) and the cosmos (<em>adhidaivata</em>), every function tires/sets except the <strong>central breath / Vāyu</strong>, which never tires and never sets; hence the <strong>single essential vow</strong> of life is simply to keep breathing — and to do so consciously is to win union with the deathless life.","The chapter thus completes the Madhu-kāṇḍa's <em>prāṇa-vidyā</em>: breath is the imperishable, untiring, all-sustaining life, and identification with it (consciously, as the \"one vow\") is the practical path to the deathless — even as 1.4.10 had already given the absolute knowledge (<em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em>) behind it."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE TRIAD AND THE SUPREMACY OF BREATH (BU 1.5)\n   ==============================================\n   SEVEN FOODS (1.5.1-2):  1 common | 2 to gods (huta/prahuta) | 3 to SELF | 1 to animals (milk)\n        \\__ riddle: food never runs out  -&gt;  PURUṢA = AKṢITI (the imperishable eater) re-creates it\n\n   THE 3 &quot;SELF-FOODS&quot; (1.5.3):   SPEECH (vāc) -- MIND (manas) -- BREATH (prāṇa)\n        |   &quot;this self is vāṅmaya, manomaya, prāṇamaya&quot;\n        v   the one triad underlies seven domains (1.5.4-10):\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   |          | SPEECH        | MIND            | BREATH        |\n   | worlds   | earth         | mid-region      | sky           |\n   | Vedas    | Ṛg            | Yajus           | Sāma          |\n   | beings   | gods          | fathers         | men           |\n   | family   | mother        | FATHER          | child         |\n   | knowing  | the KNOWN     | the TO-BE-KNOWN | the UNKNOWN   |\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   cosmic bodies (1.5.11-13): speech=earth/FIRE | mind=sky/SUN | breath=waters/MOON\n        -&gt; all equal &amp; infinite -&gt; finite meditation = finite world; infinite = infinite world\n\n   YEAR/MOON-PRAJĀPATI = 16 kalās (1.5.14-15):\n        15 wax &amp; wane (= nights / wealth)   +   1 DHRUVA (constant) = the SELF\n        -&gt;  wheel:  SELF = hub (nabhya),  wealth = rim (pradhi);  lose all -&gt; &quot;only the rim is lost&quot;\n\n   3 WORLDS / 3 MEANS (1.5.16):  son -&gt; men&#x27;s world | rite -&gt; fathers&#x27; | KNOWLEDGE -&gt; gods&#x27; (best)\n\n   SAMPRATTI (1.5.17): dying father -&gt; son: &quot;you are brahman/yajña/loka&quot;  (relative immortality)\n        then DIVINE speech/mind/breath enter the knower (1.5.18-20) -&gt; Self of all beings, no grief/evil\n\n   VRATA-MĪMĀṂSĀ (1.5.21-23):\n        organs compete -&gt; DEATH-as-FATIGUE tires them all   (eye/ear/speech tire)\n        EXCEPT the central breath (never tired) -&gt; all become &quot;prāṇas&quot;        [adhyātma]\n        gods compete -&gt; all SET except VĀYU (never sets)                       [adhidaivata]\n        =&gt; THE ONE VOW:  breathe in, breathe out  -&gt; union (sāyujya) with the deathless breath</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Watch the eater, not just the food.</strong> Per 1.5.1–2, notice that experience is an endless stream of \"food\" (objects, sensations) — and that <em>you</em>, the conscious partaker, are the inexhaustible source that keeps generating it. Rest as the eater (<em>akṣiti</em>), not the eaten.","2. <strong>Map the triad in yourself</strong> (1.5.3). Observe how <em>attention</em> (manas) gates all perception (\"mind elsewhere — did not see\"), how the ten states (desire, doubt, faith, fear…) are all just mind, and how speech and breath complete the instrument. Know the empirical \"you\" as this <em>vāc-manas-prāṇa</em> weave.","3. <strong>Meditate big</strong> (1.5.13). Whatever you contemplate, take its <em>infinite</em> form — your speech as the whole earth-and-fire, your mind as sky-and-sun, your breath as waters-and-moon. \"As you conceive, so you attain\": small vision yields small results.","4. <strong>Find your hub</strong> (1.5.15). In any loss, locate the <em>dhruva</em> — the unchanging Self (the hub) behind the waning externals (the rim). Practise the formula: \"If I survive, only the rim is lost.\" This is the chapter's antidote to ruin and grief.","5. <strong>Prize knowing over getting</strong> (1.5.16). Among son, work, and knowledge, give knowledge the highest place; it wins the best \"world.\"","6. <strong>Make breath your one vow</strong> (1.5.21–23). Of all disciplines, the breath never tires and never fails while life lasts. Take conscious breathing — slow, aware in-and-out breath — as your irreducible daily sacrament, aligning yourself with the untiring, never-setting life (prāṇa within = Vāyu without).","7. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> every faculty you rely on will tire; the <em>life</em> that animates them does not. Identify with that tireless life, and you stand where \"evil does not reach\" — in the station of the deathless."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 1 · Brāhmaṇa 1.6 — Name, Form, and Action; the Immortal veiled by the Real","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 1.6","title":"Name, Form, and Action; the Immortal veiled by the Real","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The brief closing brāhmaṇa of Adhyāya 1. It reduces the entire manifest world to a</em> <strong>triad — nāma (name), rūpa (form), karma (action)</strong> — <em>and shows each to have an</em> <strong>uktha</strong> <em>(its \"ground/origin\"),</em> <strong>sāman</strong> <em>(its \"equal/pervader\"), and</em> <strong>brahman</strong> <em>(its \"support/bearer\"). The three are finally seen as</em> <strong>one — the Ātman</strong>; <em>and the chapter ends with a luminous formula:</em> <strong>the immortal (prāṇa) is veiled by the real (nāma-rūpa)</strong> — <em>the deathless life hidden within name and form.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1.6.1","title":"Name, and its ground in Speech","sa":["त्रयं वा इदं नाम रूपं कर्म । तेषां नाम्नां वागित्येतदेषामुक्थम् । अतो हि सर्वाणि नामान्युत्तिष्ठन्ति । एतदेषां साम । एतद्धि सर्वैर्नामभिः समम् । एतदेषां ब्रह्म । एतद्धि सर्वाणि नामानि बिभर्ति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["trayaṃ vā idaṃ nāma rūpaṃ karma | teṣāṃ nāmnāṃ vāg ity etad eṣām uktham | ato hi sarvāṇi nāmāny uttiṣṭhanti | etad eṣāṃ sāma | etad dhi sarvair nāmabhiḥ samam | etad eṣāṃ brahma | etad dhi sarvāṇi nāmāni bibharti || 1 ||"],"words":[["trayaṃ vai idaṃ nāma rūpaṃ karma","n. nom. (triad)","this (all) is a triad: name, form, action"],["teṣāṃ nāmnāṃ vāk iti etat eṣām uktham","gen. pl. + f. nom. + n. nom.","of these, as for names — speech is their uktha (origin/ground)"],["ataḥ hi sarvāṇi nāmāni uttiṣṭhanti","abl. + n. nom. pl. + pres. of ud-sthā","for from it all names arise"],["etat eṣāṃ sāma","n. nom.","this (speech) is their sāman (the equal/pervader)"],["etat hi sarvaiḥ nāmabhiḥ samam","instr. pl. + n. nom.","for it is equal to (commensurate with) all names"],["etat eṣāṃ brahma","n. nom.","this is their brahman (support/bearer)"],["etat hi sarvāṇi nāmāni bibharti","acc. pl. + 3 sg. pres. of bhṛ","for it bears (supports) all names"]],"lit":"\"This (whole world) is a triad: <strong>name, form, and action.</strong> Of these — as for <strong>names</strong> — <strong>speech</strong> is their <em>uktha</em> (ground), for from it all names arise. It is their <em>sāman</em> (pervader), for it is commensurate with all names. It is their <em>brahman</em> (bearer), for it bears all names.\"","para":"All reality is reduced to three categories: <strong>name (nāma), form (rūpa), action (karma).</strong> Taking the first, <strong>name</strong>, the text assigns it three relations to <strong>speech</strong>: speech is its <em>uktha</em> (source — all names \"rise up\" from speech), its <em>sāman</em> (equal/pervader — speech is co-extensive with every name), and its <em>brahman</em> (support — speech \"bears\" all names). (<em>Uktha, sāman, brahman</em> are technical chant-terms here repurposed as origin/pervasion/support.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the whole differentiated universe is just <em>nāma-rūpa-karma</em> (the technical Vedānta term <em>nāma-rūpa</em> plus action); analysing each reveals its ground in a faculty. Names rest wholly on speech — speech is their origin, measure, and support. This sets up the reduction of all three to the one Self."],["Sureśvara","name has no being apart from speech; the threefold relation (uktha/sāman/brahman) exhausts how name depends on its faculty — a model of how effects depend on their cause."],["Krishnananda","the world we deal with is a world of <em>names, forms, and actions</em> — nothing else; here the first, name, is shown to be entirely a function of speech, with no independent standing."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a meditative analysis: trace all names back to speech as their source, pervader, and support."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>nāma-rūpa-karma</em> as a comprehensive ontological inventory; Olivelle reads the <em>uktha/sāman/brahman</em> triad as origin-extension-support relations."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.6.2","title":"Form, and its ground in the Eye","sa":["अथ रूपाणां चक्षुरित्येतदेषामुक्थम् । अतो हि सर्वाणि रूपाण्युत्तिष्ठन्ति । एतदेषां साम । एतद्धि सर्वै रूपैः समम् । एतदेषां ब्रह्म । एतद्धि सर्वाणि रूपाणि बिभर्ति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["atha rūpāṇāṃ cakṣur ity etad eṣām uktham | ato hi sarvāṇi rūpāṇy uttiṣṭhanti | etad eṣāṃ sāma | etad dhi sarvai rūpaiḥ samam | etad eṣāṃ brahma | etad dhi sarvāṇi rūpāṇi bibharti || 2 ||"],"words":[["atha rūpāṇāṃ cakṣuḥ iti etat eṣām uktham","gen. pl. + n. nom.","now, as for forms — the eye is their uktha (ground)"],["ataḥ hi sarvāṇi rūpāṇi uttiṣṭhanti","abl. + pres.","for from it all forms arise"],["etat eṣāṃ sāma etat hi sarvaiḥ rūpaiḥ samam","n. nom. + instr. pl.","it is their sāman, commensurate with all forms"],["etat eṣāṃ brahma etat hi sarvāṇi rūpāṇi bibharti","acc. pl. + pres.","it is their brahman, for it bears all forms"]],"lit":"\"Now, as for <strong>forms</strong> — the <strong>eye</strong> is their <em>uktha</em> (ground), for from it all forms arise. It is their <em>sāman</em>, for it is commensurate with all forms. It is their <em>brahman</em>, for it bears all forms.\"","para":"The second category, <strong>form (rūpa)</strong>, is grounded entirely in the <strong>eye</strong>: the eye is its source (forms \"arise\" through sight), its pervader (the eye is commensurate with every form), and its support. Just as names depend on speech, forms depend on the eye.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"form\" has reality only as the seen, and the seen depends on the eye; the eye is origin, measure, and support of all forms — the same analysis as for name."],["Sureśvara","form is not self-standing; it is constituted in relation to the seeing faculty."],["Krishnananda","the visible world (form) is correlative to the eye — no eye, no form; a step toward seeing the whole world as relative to the perceiving faculties, not independent."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","trace all forms to the eye as their source and support, dissolving the assumption of self-existent objects."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the parallel structure (name:speech :: form:eye); an early idealist-leaning analysis of the object as relative to the organ."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.6.3","title":"Action, the Self; the three are one; the immortal veiled by the real","sa":["अथ कर्मणामात्मेत्येतदेषामुक्थम् । अतो हि सर्वाणि कर्माण्युत्तिष्ठन्ति । एतदेषां साम । एतद्धि सर्वैः कर्मभिः समम् । एतदेषां ब्रह्म । एतद्धि सर्वाणि कर्माणि बिभर्ति । तदेतत्त्रयं सदेकमयमात्मा । आत्मो एकः सन्नेतत्त्रयम् । तदेतदमृतं सत्येन छन्नम् । प्राणो वा अमृतम् । नामरूपे सत्यम् । ताभ्यामयं प्राणश्छन्नः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["atha karmaṇām ātmety etad eṣām uktham | ato hi sarvāṇi karmāṇy uttiṣṭhanti | etad eṣāṃ sāma | etad dhi sarvaiḥ karmabhiḥ samam | etad eṣāṃ brahma | etad dhi sarvāṇi karmāṇi bibharti | tad etat trayaṃ sad ekam ayam ātmā | ātmo ekaḥ sann etat trayam | tad etad amṛtaṃ satyena channam | prāṇo vā amṛtam | nāmarūpe satyam | tābhyām ayaṃ prāṇaś channaḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[["atha karmaṇām ātmā iti etat eṣām uktham","gen. pl. + m. nom.","now, as for actions — the self/body (ātman) is their uktha (ground)"],["ataḥ hi sarvāṇi karmāṇi uttiṣṭhanti","abl. + pres.","for from it all actions arise"],["etat eṣāṃ sāma etat hi sarvaiḥ karmabhiḥ samam","instr. pl.","it is their sāman, commensurate with all actions"],["etat eṣāṃ brahma etat hi sarvāṇi karmāṇi bibharti","acc. pl. + pres.","it is their brahman, for it bears all actions"],["tat etat trayaṃ sat ekam ayam ātmā","n. nom. + pres.part. + m. nom.","this triad, (though three), being one, is this Self"],["ātmā u ekaḥ san etat trayam","m. nom. + pres.part. + n. nom.","and the Self, being one, is this triad"],["tat etat amṛtaṃ satyena channam","n. nom. + instr. + pp.","this immortal is veiled (covered) by the real"],["prāṇaḥ vai amṛtam","m. nom. + n. nom.","the breath (life) is the immortal"],["nāma-rūpe satyam","n. nom. du. + n. nom.","name and form are the real"],["tābhyām ayaṃ prāṇaḥ channaḥ","instr. du. + m. nom. + pp.","by these two the breath is veiled"]],"lit":"\"Now, as for <strong>actions</strong> — the <strong>self (ātman / body)</strong> is their <em>uktha</em> (ground), for from it all actions arise. It is their <em>sāman</em>, commensurate with all actions; it is their <em>brahman</em>, for it bears all actions. This <strong>triad</strong> (name, form, action), though three, <strong>being one, is this Self</strong>; and the <strong>Self, being one, is this triad.</strong> This <strong>immortal is veiled by the real</strong>: the <strong>breath (prāṇa) is the immortal</strong>; <strong>name and form are the real (satya)</strong> — and by these two the breath is veiled.\"","para":"The third category, <strong>action (karma)</strong>, is grounded in the <strong>ātman</strong> (here the embodied self/body, the agent from which all action springs) — its source, pervader, and support. Then comes the grand resolution: <strong>the three (name, form, action) are in truth one — the Self; and the one Self appears as the three.</strong> Finally the chapter — and the whole Madhu-kāṇḍa's first movement — closes with a profound formula: <strong>the immortal is veiled by the real.</strong> The <strong>breath/life (prāṇa) is the <em>amṛta</em> (immortal)</strong>; <strong>name-and-form are the <em>satya</em> (the \"real,\" the empirically actual)</strong>; and the deathless breath is <strong>hidden within / covered over by</strong> name and form. (Here <em>satya</em> = the manifest, determinate world that <em>conceals</em> the <em>amṛta</em>; cf. 1.4.7's <em>nāma-rūpa</em>, and the fuller \"two forms of Brahman\" teaching of 2.3.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the comprehensive reduction: the entire universe (<em>nāma-rūpa-karma</em>) is <strong>non-different from the one Ātman</strong> — \"being three it is one Self, being one Self it is the three.\" The closing formula is pivotal: <em>prāṇa</em> (the living, conscious principle) is the <em>amṛta</em> (immortal Brahman in its life-aspect), while <em>nāma-rūpa</em> (name-and-form) is <em>satya</em> — not \"truth\" in the highest sense but the <em>vyāvahārika</em> real, the manifest that <strong>conceals</strong> the immortal as a sheath conceals what it covers. Liberation is to pierce the veil of name-and-form to the deathless within. (Śaṅkara connects this directly to 2.3's <em>satyasya satyam</em>, \"the Real of the real.\")"],["Sureśvara","\"three, yet one Self; one Self, yet three\" is the formula of <em>bheda-abheda</em> resolved in favour of the one — multiplicity is the single Self's appearance. <em>Amṛta</em> covered by <em>satya</em> states the concealment of Brahman by the world of name-and-form (<em>avidyā</em>'s veil)."],["Krishnananda","a magnificent close — the whole cosmos is <strong>name, form, and action</strong>, and these are <em>one Self</em>; and within the \"real\" (the perceptible name-and-form) the <strong>immortal life is hidden</strong>, as fire is hidden in wood. Spiritual practice is the un-veiling: seeing through name-and-form to the deathless prāṇa/Self they clothe."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical — recognise that all you encounter is name-form-action, that these are your own Self, and that the immortal life is concealed just behind the familiar world; meditation lifts the veil."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the elegant final equation (<em>prāṇa = amṛta</em>, <em>nāma-rūpa = satya</em>, the former veiled by the latter); Olivelle reads it as the seed of the \"two forms / Real of the real\" doctrine of 2.1–2.3 and a fitting close to the chapter's analysis of manifestation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This short brāhmaṇa is the <strong>structural full-stop of Adhyāya 1</strong>: having narrated creation three times (1.1 horse, 1.2 Death, 1.4 Ātman) and explored breath (1.3, 1.5), the text now <strong>classifies the entire manifest world</strong> under three heads and resolves them into the One.","1. <strong>The ontological inventory.</strong> Everything reduces to <strong>nāma (name), rūpa (form), karma (action)</strong> — what a thing is called, how it appears, and what it does. There is no fourth category of the manifest.","2. <strong>Each rests on a faculty.</strong> Name on <strong>speech</strong>, form on the <strong>eye</strong>, action on the <strong>ātman/body</strong> — each its <em>uktha</em> (origin), <em>sāman</em> (pervader), and <em>brahman</em> (support). The world of objects is thus shown to be <strong>correlative to the faculties</strong>, not self-standing — an early idealist analysis that Śaṅkara develops into the doctrine of <em>nāma-rūpa</em> as the form of the projected world.","3. <strong>The three are the one Self.</strong> <em>tad etat trayaṃ sad ekam ayam ātmā / ātmā u ekaḥ san etat trayam</em> — the manifold <em>is</em> the Self, and the Self appears <em>as</em> the manifold. This is the <strong>non-dual resolution</strong> of the whole chapter, restating <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em> (1.4.10) in the language of name-form-action.","4. <strong>The immortal veiled by the real.</strong> The closing formula — <strong>prāṇa (immortal) hidden within nāma-rūpa (the real)</strong> — names the human condition precisely: the deathless is <em>here, now, within the familiar world</em>, but <strong>covered</strong> by it. This sets up the great teaching of Adhyāya 2 (the \"two forms of Brahman,\" and <em>satyasya satyam</em>, \"the Real of the real,\" 2.1–2.3), to which the Madhu-kāṇḍa now turns."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE WORLD REDUCED, AND RESOLVED (BU 1.6)\n   ========================================\n   ALL THIS  =  a TRIAD:     NĀMA (name)  |  RŪPA (form)  |  KARMA (action)\n                                  |              |               |\n   grounded in (uktha/sāman/brahman = origin / pervader / bearer):\n                                  v              v               v\n                              SPEECH          the EYE         the ĀTMAN (self/body)\n                                  \\              |               /\n                                   \\             |              /\n                                    =====&gt;  ONE  SELF (ātman)  &lt;=====\n                          &quot;being three, it is one Self; being one Self, it is the three&quot;\n\n   THE CLOSING FORMULA:\n        AMṚTA (immortal) ........ = PRĀṆA (life/breath)\n                |  veiled / covered by (channam)\n                v\n        SATYA (the real) ........ = NĀMA-RŪPA (name &amp; form)\n\n        =&gt; the deathless is hidden WITHIN the familiar world of name-and-form\n           (the seed of 2.3:  &quot;satyasya satyam&quot; — the Real of the real)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 1.6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Audit your world as name-form-action.</strong> Through a day, notice that everything you meet is some <em>name</em>, some <em>form</em>, or some <em>action</em> — there is no fourth kind of object. This simplifies the apparently endless world into three knowable heads.","2. <strong>See objects as correlative to faculties.</strong> Names live in speech, forms in the eye, actions in the body. Practise noticing that \"the world out there\" is inseparable from the faculties that disclose it — loosening the grip of self-standing objects.","3. <strong>Collapse the three into the One.</strong> Rest in the recognition: <em>all this name-form-action is my own Self appearing; and my Self, one, appears as all this.</em> This is <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em> (1.4.10) in everyday terms.","4. <strong>Look for the immortal within the real.</strong> The key practice of the whole chapter: the deathless life (prāṇa/Self) is <strong>not elsewhere</strong> — it is <em>veiled by</em> name and form, here and now. In any object or experience, sense the living awareness it conceals, as one senses fire latent in wood.","5. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> liberation is <em>un-veiling</em>, not acquiring. The immortal is already present, covered by the familiar. Adhyāya 2 will lift this veil further with \"the Real of the real\" (<em>satyasya satyam</em>)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Colophon","title":"End of Adhyāya 1","sa":["इति बृहदारण्यकोपनिषदि प्रथमोऽध्यायः ॥"],"iast":["iti bṛhadāraṇyakopaniṣadi prathamo 'dhyāyaḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Thus the First Adhyāya of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"What Adhyāya 1 has established (the arc)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Adhyāya 1 opens the <strong>Madhu-kāṇḍa</strong> by moving, in six brāhmaṇas, from ritual interiorised to the non-dual Self and back out to the structure of the manifest world:","- <strong>1.1 — Cosmic Horse:</strong> the supreme rite (aśvamedha) internalised as meditation; microcosm = macrocosm.","- <strong>1.2 — Creation from Death:</strong> the world as a food-economy sprung from Hunger/Death; the knower becomes the eater, conquering re-death.","- <strong>1.3 — The Udgītha:</strong> the senses are corruptible, but the <strong>central breath is incorruptible</strong>; <em>prāṇa-vidyā</em>; the prayer <em>asato mā sad gamaya</em>.","- <strong>1.4 — Creation from the Self:</strong> \"I am\" → fear from duality → the splitting into pairs → <strong>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</strong> → dharma = truth → knowledge over works.","- <strong>1.5 — The Sevenfold Food:</strong> the triad <strong>speech–mind–breath</strong> as the key to all; the imperishable Self (the \"sixteenth digit,\" the hub); the <strong>one vow</strong> of breathing.","- <strong>1.6 — Name, Form, Action:</strong> the whole manifest world reduced to a triad, resolved into <strong>the one Self</strong>, with the immortal (prāṇa) <strong>veiled by</strong> the real (nāma-rūpa).","Two great affirmations anchor the chapter: the <strong>mahā-vākya \"ahaṃ brahmāsmi\"</strong> (1.4.10) — the identity of the Self and the Absolute — and the <strong>prāṇa-vidyā</strong> (1.3, 1.5) — the deathless, incorruptible, untiring life as the practical gateway to that identity. The closing formula of 1.6.3 (<em>amṛta veiled by satya</em>) hands the reader directly to <strong>Adhyāya 2</strong>, which opens with the \"two forms of Brahman\" and the teaching <strong>satyasya satyam</strong>, \"the Real of the real.\"","<em>Continued in Adhyāya 2 (Madhu-kāṇḍa, concluded): the dialogue of Gārgya and Ajātaśatru on Brahman (2.1); the Person in the body (2.2); the two forms — formed and formless — and \"the Real of the real\" (2.3); and the first Maitreyī dialogue on the Self as the dearest and \"neti neti\" (2.4).</em>"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 2","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya 2","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Adhyāya 2 concludes the</em> <strong>Madhu-kāṇḍa</strong> <em>(the \"honey\" book). After the cosmogonies and the prāṇa-vidyā of Adhyāya 1, it turns to the direct disclosure of Brahman: a king out-teaches a proud brāhmaṇa about the Self behind all cosmic \"persons\" (2.1); the Person within the body and the breaths (2.2); the</em> <strong>two forms of Brahman</strong> — formed and formless — <em>and the great formula</em> <strong>neti neti</strong> <em>and</em> <strong>satyasya satyam</strong> <em>(2.3); the first</em> <strong>Maitreyī dialogue</strong> <em>— the Self as the dearest of all, the lump-of-salt simile, and \"after death no awareness\" (2.4); the</em> <strong>Madhu-vidyā</strong> — <em>the mutual \"honey\" of all beings, the radiant immortal Person in each element (2.5); and the first</em> <strong>lineage of teachers (vaṃśa)</strong> <em>(2.6).</em>","<em>The same per-mantra format and the same five commentaries (Śaṅkara · Sureśvara · Krishnananda · Mādhavānanda/Sivananda · Deussen/Olivelle) continue, with per-brāhmaṇa Details, Visual representation, and Practical steps.</em>"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 2 · Brāhmaṇa 2.1 — Gārgya and Ajātaśatru: the Self behind all \"persons\"","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 2.1","title":"Gārgya and Ajātaśatru: the Self behind all \"persons\"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>A dramatic dialogue. The proud, learned brāhmaṇa</em> <strong>Dṛpta-Bālāki Gārgya</strong> <em>offers to teach Brahman to</em> <strong>King Ajātaśatru of Kāśī (Banaras).</strong> <em>Twelve times Gārgya identifies Brahman with a cosmic or bodily \"person\" (in the sun, moon, lightning, space, wind, fire, water, the mirror, an echo, the quarters, the shadow, the body-self); twelve times the king says these are mere</em> <strong>epithets/effects</strong>, <em>not the true Brahman. When Gārgya falls silent, the</em> <strong>kṣatriya teaches the brāhmaṇa</strong> <em>(a notable reversal) — leading him to a sleeping man to reveal the</em> <strong>vijñānamaya puruṣa</strong> <em>(the Self of consciousness) that withdraws the senses in sleep and projects all worlds in dream, concluding with the</em> upaniṣad: <strong>satyasya satyam, \"the Real of the real.\"</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.1","title":"The challenge","sa":["दृप्तबालाकिर्हानूचानो गार्ग्य आस । स होवाचाजातशत्रुं काश्यं — ब्रह्म ते ब्रवाणीति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — सहस्रमेतस्यां वाचि दद्मः जनको जनक इति वै जना धावन्तीति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["dṛptabālākir hānūcāno gārgya āsa | sa hovācājātaśatruṃ kāśyaṃ -- brahma te bravāṇīti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- sahasram etasyāṃ vāci dadmaḥ, janako janaka iti vai janā dhāvantīti || 1 ||"],"words":[["dṛpta-bālākiḥ ha anūcānaḥ gārgyaḥ āsa","m. nom. + pp. (learned) + m. nom. + pf. of as","there was the proud Bālāki, a learned man of the Garga line"],["saḥ ha uvāca ajātaśatruṃ kāśyam","3 sg. pf. of vac + acc.","he said to Ajātaśatru, the king of Kāśī"],["brahma te bravāṇi iti","n. acc. + dat. + 1 sg. subj. of brū","\"let me tell you (of) Brahman\""],["saḥ ha uvāca ajātaśatruḥ","nom.","Ajātaśatru said"],["sahasram etasyāṃ vāci dadmaḥ","n. acc. + f. loc. + 1 pl. pres. of dā","\"we give a thousand (cows) for this offer of speech"],["janakaḥ janakaḥ iti vai janāḥ dhāvanti iti","m. nom. + nom. pl. + pres. of dhāv","for at (the cry) 'Janaka! Janaka!' people (everywhere) come running\""]],"lit":"\"There was Dṛpta-Bālāki ('Proud Bālāki'), a learned man of the Garga family. He said to Ajātaśatru, king of Kāśī, 'Let me tell you of Brahman.' Ajātaśatru said, 'We give a thousand (cows) for such an offer! At (the call) \"Janaka! Janaka!\" people come running (for his generosity) — (so I gladly reward a teacher of Brahman).'\"","para":"The renowned, self-assured brāhmaṇa <strong>Bālāki</strong> approaches <strong>King Ajātaśatru</strong> with the offer to teach him Brahman. The king, delighted and generous (alluding to the famously liberal King Janaka, whose name alone draws supplicants), promises a lavish reward of a thousand cows — courteous, but the irony soon surfaces, since the king will end up the teacher.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","sets the scene for a <em>brahma-jijñāsā</em> dialogue and the striking lesson that true knowledge of the Self is not the monopoly of the priestly class — here a kṣatriya holds it. \"Dṛpta\" (proud) flags Bālāki's coming humbling."],["Sureśvara","the eagerness to reward a teacher of Brahman models the right disposition toward <em>brahma-vidyā</em> — it is worth any wealth."],["Krishnananda","the famous case of a king (Ajātaśatru, like Janaka and Pravāhaṇa) possessing the deepest knowledge; the Upaniṣadic age did not confine wisdom to birth. Bālāki's pride is the obstacle the dialogue will dissolve."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the generosity-motif (Janaka) and the proper honouring of a would-be teacher."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a near-parallel exists in Kauṣītaki Up. 4; Olivelle notes the recurring \"wise king instructs the brāhmaṇa\" motif as evidence that early Upaniṣadic thought circulated among the <em>kṣatriya</em> elite."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 2.1.2–2.1.13","title":"Twelve \"persons,\" twelve refutations","sa":["2.1.2 (sun): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवासावादित्ये पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । अतिष्ठाः सर्वेषां भूतानां मूर्धा राजेति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्तेऽतिष्ठाः सर्वेषां भूतानां मूर्धा राजा भवति ॥ २ ॥","2.1.3 (moon): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवासौ चन्द्रे पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । बृहन्पाण्डरवासाः सोमो राजेति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्तेऽहरहः सुतः प्रसुतो भवति । नास्यान्नं क्षीयते ॥ ३ ॥","2.1.4 (lightning): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवासौ विद्युति पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । तेजस्वीति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्ते तेजस्वी ह भवति । तेजस्विनी हास्य प्रजा भवति ॥ ४ ॥","2.1.5 (space/ether): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवायमाकाशे पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । पूर्णमप्रवर्तीति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्ते पूर्यते प्रजया पशुभिर्नास्यास्माल्लोकात्प्रजोद्वर्तते ॥ ५ ॥","2.1.6 (wind): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवायं वायौ पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । इन्द्रो वैकुण्ठोऽपराजिता सेनेति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्ते जिष्णुर्हापराजिष्णुर्भवत्यन्यतस्त्यजायी ॥ ६ ॥","2.1.7 (fire): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवायमग्नौ पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । विषासहिरिति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्ते विषासहिर्ह भवति । विषासहिर्हास्य प्रजा भवति ॥ ७ ॥","2.1.8 (water): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवायमप्सु पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । प्रतिरूप इति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्ते प्रतिरूपं हैवैनमुपगच्छति नाप्रतिरूपम् । अथो प्रतिरूपोऽस्माज्जायते ॥ ८ ॥","2.1.9 (mirror): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवायमादर्शे पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । रोचिष्णुरिति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्ते रोचिष्णुर्ह भवति । रोचिष्णुर्हास्य प्रजा भवति । अथो यैः संनिगच्छति सर्वांस्तानतिरोचते ॥ ९ ॥","2.1.10 (the echo / sound behind one walking): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवायं यन्तं पश्चाच्छब्दोऽनूदेत्येतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । असुरिति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्ते सर्वं हैवास्मिँल्लोक आयुरेति । नैनं पुरा कालात्प्राणो जहाति ॥ १० ॥","2.1.11 (the quarters): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवायं दिक्षु पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । द्वितीयोऽनपग इति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्ते द्वितीयवान्ह भवति । नास्माद्गणश्छिद्यते ॥ ११ ॥","2.1.12 (the shadow): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवायं छायामयः पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । मृत्युरिति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्ते सर्वं हैवास्मिँल्लोक आयुरेति । नैनं पुरा कालान्मृत्युरागच्छति ॥ १२ ॥","2.1.13 (the self in the body): स होवाच गार्ग्यः — य एवायमात्मनि पुरुष एतमेवाहं ब्रह्मोपास इति । स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — मा मैतस्मिन्संवदिष्ठाः । आत्मन्वीति वा अहमेतमुपास इति । स य एतमेवमुपास्त आत्मन्वी ह भवति । आत्मन्विनी हास्य प्रजा भवति । स ह तूष्णीमास गार्ग्यः ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāsāv āditye puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatrur -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | atiṣṭhāḥ sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ mūrdhā rājeti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste 'tiṣṭhāḥ sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ mūrdhā rājā bhavati || 2 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāsau candre puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | bṛhan pāṇḍaravāsāḥ somo rājeti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste 'har-ahaḥ sutaḥ prasuto bhavati | nāsyānnaṃ kṣīyate || 3 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāsau vidyuti puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | tejasvīti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste tejasvī ha bhavati | tejasvinī hāsya prajā bhavati || 4 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāyam ākāśe puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | pūrṇam apravartīti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste pūryate prajayā paśubhir nāsyāsmāl lokāt prajodvartate || 5 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāyaṃ vāyau puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | indro vaikuṇṭho 'parājitā seneti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste jiṣṇur hāparājiṣṇur bhavaty anyatastyajāyī || 6 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāyam agnau puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | viṣāsahir iti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste viṣāsahir ha bhavati | viṣāsahir hāsya prajā bhavati || 7 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāyam apsu puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | pratirūpa iti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste pratirūpaṃ haivainam upagacchati nāpratirūpam | atho pratirupo 'smāj jāyate || 8 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāyam ādarśe puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | rociṣṇur iti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste rociṣṇur ha bhavati | rociṣṇur hāsya prajā bhavati | atho yaiḥ saṃnigacchati sarvāṃs tān atirocate || 9 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāyaṃ yantaṃ paścāc chabdo 'nūdety etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | asur iti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste sarvaṃ haivāsmiṃl loka āyur eti | nainaṃ purā kālāt prāṇo jahāti || 10 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāyaṃ dikṣu puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | dvitīyo 'napaga iti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste dvitīyavān ha bhavati | nāsmād gaṇaś chidyate || 11 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāyaṃ chāyāmayaḥ puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | mṛtyur iti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāste sarvaṃ haivāsmiṃl loka āyur eti | nainaṃ purā kālān mṛtyur āgacchati || 12 ||","sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- ya evāyam ātmani puruṣa etam evāhaṃ brahmopāsa iti | sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- mā maitasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ | ātmanvīti vā aham etam upāsa iti | sa ya etam evam upāsta ātmanvī ha bhavati | ātmanvinī hāsya prajā bhavati | sa ha tūṣṇīm āsa gārgyaḥ || 13 ||"],"words":[["saḥ ha uvāca gārgyaḥ","pf. of vac","Gārgya said"],["yaḥ eva asau āditye puruṣaḥ","rel. + m. loc. + m. nom.","\"the very Person who is there in the sun"],["etam eva ahaṃ brahma upāse iti","acc. + n. acc. + 1 sg. pres. of upās","him do I meditate on as Brahman\""],["mā ma etasmin saṃvadiṣṭhāḥ","proh. + loc. + 2 sg. inj. of sam-vad","\"do not challenge / dispute with me on this one!\""],["atiṣṭhāḥ sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ mūrdhā rājā iti vā aham etam upāse","m. nom. (the surpassing) + gen. pl. + m. nom.","\"as the surpassing head, the king of all beings, do I meditate on him\""],["saḥ yaḥ etam evam upāste","rel. + pres.","he who meditates on him thus"],["atiṣṭhāḥ sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ mūrdhā rājā bhavati","nom. + pres.","becomes the surpassing head, the king of all beings"]],"lit":"<strong>Shared pada-analysis</strong> (template, 2.1.2):<br>\"Gārgya said, 'The very Person in the sun — him I meditate on as Brahman.' Ajātaśatru said, 'Do not challenge me on this one! As the surpassing head, the king of all beings, do I meditate on him; and one who meditates on him so becomes the surpassing head, the king of all beings.'\" <em>(So through all twelve loci; at the last — the body-self — Gārgya, having exhausted his definitions, falls silent.)</em>","para":"Gārgya keeps identifying Brahman with the conscious \"person\" found at twelve loci — the great lights (sun, moon, lightning), the elements (space, wind, fire, water), reflective/echoing phenomena (mirror, echo, shadow), the directions, and finally the bodily self. Each time the king accepts the locus but insists it is only a <strong>particular, conditioned aspect</strong> of Brahman, fit for a <em>result-bearing meditation</em> (each yields a worldly boon), <strong>not the absolute Brahman itself</strong>. Even the twelfth — the \"person in the body-self\" — is, as Gārgya conceives it (the ego/individual), still not the ultimate. So Gārgya runs out of answers and <strong>falls silent</strong>.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","every \"person\" Gārgya names is the Self <em>as conditioned</em> by an <em>upādhi</em> (limiting adjunct — sun, moon, body, etc.); these are legitimate <em>saguṇa upāsanās</em> with <em>saguṇa</em> fruits, but none is the <em>nirupādhika</em> (unconditioned) Brahman. The king's repeated correction (\"I meditate on him rather as…\") shows he knows the <em>deity</em> behind each phenomenon, yet even he has not yet stated the absolute — which he reveals only via the sleeping man. Gārgya's silence marks the limit of object-based knowledge."],["Sureśvara","the twelve are <em>effects/attributes</em> (epithets) projected on Brahman; mistaking an attribute for the substrate is the error corrected throughout. The dialectic trains the student to negate every objectifiable \"person.\""],["Krishnananda","Bālāki always points <em>outward</em> to a person <em>in</em> something — sun, moon, body — i.e., to Brahman as an object. The king's lesson: Brahman is never an object among objects; the search must turn from the \"person in X\" to the very <em>subject</em> that knows all X. Each fruit is real but partial — the rewards of relative meditation."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a catalogue of <em>pratīka-upāsanās</em> (symbol-meditations) with their specific fruits — useful as such, but the chapter's point is that they fall short of liberating knowledge; do not stop at any symbol."],["Deussen/Olivelle","compare the longer parallel in Kauṣītaki Up. 4 (sixteen loci); Olivelle notes the systematic listing of cosmic and bodily \"persons\" and reads the king's corrections as distinguishing relative deities from the absolute Self."]],"notes":["<em>Each exchange follows one frame: Gārgya — \"the Person in/at <strong>X</strong>, him I meditate on as Brahman\"; Ajātaśatru — \"Do not challenge me on this one! I meditate on him rather as <strong>[true designation]</strong>,\" with a worldly <strong>fruit</strong> for that meditation. The king never denies the loci, but treats each as a mere</em> <strong>aspect/effect</strong> <em>(an epithet of the conditioned Brahman), not Brahman itself. The full Devanāgarī + IAST of every mantra is given; a single shared pada-analysis (on 2.1.2) and a consolidated table follow.</em>","<strong>Consolidated table of the twelve:</strong>","<table><tr><td>#</td><td>Locus (Gārgya's \"Person\")</td><td>Ajātaśatru's true designation</td><td>Fruit of that meditation</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.2</td><td>in the <strong>sun</strong></td><td>the surpassing <strong>head, king</strong> of all beings</td><td>becomes pre-eminent head/king</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.3</td><td>in the <strong>moon</strong></td><td>the great white-robed <strong>Soma the king</strong></td><td>soma pressed daily; his food never fails</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.4</td><td>in <strong>lightning</strong></td><td>the <strong>Radiant</strong> (<em>tejasvī</em>)</td><td>becomes radiant, with radiant offspring</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.5</td><td>in <strong>space</strong> (ākāśa)</td><td>the <strong>Full, the Unmoving</strong> (<em>pūrṇam apravarti</em>)</td><td>filled with progeny &amp; cattle; line unbroken</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.6</td><td>in <strong>wind</strong></td><td><strong>Indra Vaikuṇṭha, the unconquered army</strong></td><td>becomes victorious, unconquerable</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.7</td><td>in <strong>fire</strong></td><td>the <strong>All-overpowering</strong> (<em>viṣāsahi</em>)</td><td>overpowering, with overpowering offspring</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.8</td><td>in <strong>water</strong></td><td>the <strong>Conforming likeness</strong> (<em>pratirūpa</em>)</td><td>(only) the fitting comes to him; a fitting child</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.9</td><td>in the <strong>mirror</strong></td><td>the <strong>Shining</strong> (<em>rociṣṇu</em>)</td><td>shines, outshines his company</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.10</td><td>the <strong>echo</strong> behind a walker</td><td><strong>Life/breath</strong> (<em>asu</em>)</td><td>full lifespan; breath leaves not before time</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.11</td><td>in the <strong>quarters</strong></td><td>the <strong>inseparable Second/companion</strong></td><td>gains a companion; his group not cut off</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.12</td><td>the <strong>shadow</strong></td><td><strong>Death</strong> (<em>mṛtyu</em>)</td><td>full lifespan; death comes not before time</td></tr><tr><td>2.1.13</td><td>in the <strong>body-self</strong> (ātman)</td><td>the <strong>Embodied</strong> (<em>ātmanvī</em>)</td><td>becomes self-possessed, with self-possessed offspring</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.14","title":"Gārgya asks to become a pupil","sa":["स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — एतावन्नू३ इति । एतावद्धीति । नैतावता विदितं भवतीति । स होवाच गार्ग्यः — उप त्वायानीति ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- etāvan nū3 iti | etāvad dhīti | naitāvatā viditaṃ bhavatīti | sa hovāca gārgyaḥ -- upa tvāyānīti || 14 ||"],"words":[["saḥ ha uvāca ajātaśatruḥ","pf.","Ajātaśatru said"],["etāvat nu iti","adv. (+ pluti interrogative)","\"(is it) only this much?\""],["etāvat hi iti","","\"(yes,) just this much\" (Gārgya)"],["na etāvatā viditaṃ bhavati iti","instr. + pp.","\"by (just) this much it is not (truly) known\""],["saḥ ha uvāca gārgyaḥ","pf.","Gārgya said"],["upa tvā ayāni iti","acc. + 1 sg. subj. of upa-i","\"let me come to you (as a pupil)\""]],"lit":"\"Ajātaśatru asked, 'Is that all?' 'That is all,' (said Gārgya). 'By just this much it is not (really) known,' (said the king). Then Gārgya said, 'Let me come to you (as your pupil).'\"","para":"The king presses: <em>is that the whole of your knowledge?</em> When Gārgya admits it is, the king pronounces that <strong>none of it amounts to true knowledge of Brahman</strong> — and the proud brāhmaṇa, humbled, <strong>asks to become the king's disciple</strong> (a remarkable inversion of the usual order).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"not truly known\" — all object-meditations leave the knower short of the Self; the admission opens Gārgya to real teaching. The brāhmaṇa's request to learn from a kṣatriya dramatises that <em>brahma-vidyā</em> outranks social precedence."],["Sureśvara","genuine inquiry begins with the confession of not-knowing; pride dissolved, the student is ready."],["Krishnananda","the turning point — Bālāki's learning collapses into humility, the precondition for receiving the Self-knowledge that no amount of object-knowledge could supply."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a model of the seeker's humility; even the learned must \"approach\" (upa-√i, the root of <em>upaniṣad</em>) a true teacher."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the social reversal (brāhmaṇa pupils a king); Olivelle reads it as the text's pointed claim for the kṣatriya custody of this knowledge."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.15","title":"The king leads him to a sleeping man","sa":["स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — प्रतिलोमं चैतद्यद्ब्राह्मणः क्षत्रियमुपेयाद्ब्रह्म मे वक्ष्यतीति । व्येव त्वा ज्ञपयिष्यामीति । तं पाणावादायोत्तस्थौ । तौ ह पुरुषं सुप्तमाजग्मतुः । तमेतैर्नामभिरामन्त्रयांचक्रे बृहन्पाण्डरवासः सोम राजन्निति । स नोत्तस्थौ । तं पाणिनापेषं बोधयांचकार । स होत्तस्थौ ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- pratilomaṃ caitad yad brāhmaṇaḥ kṣatriyam upeyād brahma me vakṣyatīti | vy eva tvā jñapayiṣyāmīti | taṃ pāṇāv ādāyottasthau | tau ha puruṣaṃ suptam ājagmatuḥ | tam etair nāmabhir āmantrayāṃ cakre bṛhan pāṇḍaravāsaḥ soma rājann iti | sa nottasthau | taṃ pāṇināpeṣaṃ bodhayāṃ cakāra | sa hottasthau || 15 ||"],"words":[["pratilomaṃ ca etat yat brāhmaṇaḥ kṣatriyam upeyāt","n. nom. (against the grain) + rel. + acc. + opt. of upa-i","\"it is against the grain that a brāhmaṇa should approach a kṣatriya (thinking)"],["brahma me vakṣyati iti","n. acc. + 3 sg. fut. of vac","'he will teach me Brahman'\""],["vi eva tvā jñapayiṣyāmi iti","acc. + 1 sg. fut. caus. of jñā","\"(yet) I shall make you know it clearly\""],["taṃ pāṇau ādāya uttasthau","acc. + loc. + ger. + 3 sg. pf. of ud-sthā","taking him by the hand, he rose"],["tau ha puruṣaṃ suptam ājagmatuḥ","nom. du. + acc. + 3 du. pf. of ā-gam","the two came to a sleeping man"],["tam etaiḥ nāmabhiḥ āmantrayāṃ cakre","acc. + instr. pl. + periphr. pf.","he addressed him by these names:"],["bṛhan pāṇḍaravāsaḥ soma rājan iti","voc.","\"O Great one, White-robed, Soma, King!\""],["saḥ na uttasthau","pf.","he did not rise"],["taṃ pāṇinā āpeṣaṃ bodhayāṃ cakāra","acc. + instr. + adv. (by pushing) + periphr. pf. caus. of budh","he woke him by rubbing/pushing with the hand"],["saḥ ha uttasthau","pf.","(then) he rose"]],"lit":"\"Ajātaśatru said, 'It is against the grain that a brāhmaṇa should approach a kṣatriya expecting, \"He will teach me Brahman\"; nevertheless I shall make you understand clearly.' Taking him by the hand, he rose, and the two came to a sleeping man. He addressed him by these names: 'O Great one! White-robed! Soma! King!' — but he did not rise. He woke him by pushing him with the hand; then he rose.\"","para":"Acknowledging the social irregularity but waiving it, the king demonstrates Brahman <em>empirically</em>. He leads Gārgya to a <strong>sleeping man</strong> and calls him by the very epithets used earlier for the moon/Soma \"person\" — yet <strong>words do not wake the sleeper</strong>; only a <strong>physical push</strong> does. The point (unpacked next): in deep sleep the \"persons\" Gārgya worshipped are inactive; <em>something else</em> has withdrawn them.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the experiment isolates the <em>vijñānamaya puruṣa</em> (the Self of consciousness). That names cannot rouse the sleeper, but a touch can, shows the sense-deities are dormant in sleep while the Self persists and can be recalled — Brahman is to be sought in the <em>sleeper's own consciousness</em>, not in any external person."],["Sureśvara","sleep is the chosen \"laboratory\" because there the world and its deities are withdrawn, leaving the bare Self to be pointed out."],["Krishnananda","a brilliant pedagogical move — the king does not argue but <em>shows</em>: where do \"the great, the white-robed, Soma, the king\" go when a man sleeps? They are not the Self; the Self is the one that withdrew them and can return."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the method — Self-knowledge taught through the universal experience of sleep, available to anyone."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle highlights the empirical, almost experimental approach to consciousness via the states of sleep — a hallmark of BU's psychology (cf. 4.3)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.16","title":"Where was the conscious person in sleep?","sa":["स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — यत्रैष एतत्सुप्तोऽभूद्य एष विज्ञानमयः पुरुषः क्वैष तदाभूत्कुत एतदागादिति । तदु ह न मेने गार्ग्यः ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- yatraiṣa etat supto 'bhūd ya eṣa vijñānamayaḥ puruṣaḥ kvaiṣa tadābhūt kuta etad āgād iti | tad u ha na mene gārgyaḥ || 16 ||"],"words":[["yatra eṣaḥ etat suptaḥ abhūt","rel. adv. + nom. + pp. + aor.","\"when this one was thus asleep,"],["yaḥ eṣaḥ vijñāna-mayaḥ puruṣaḥ","rel. + adj. + m. nom.","this person who consists of consciousness —"],["kva eṣaḥ tadā abhūt","adv. + aor.","where was he then?"],["kutaḥ etat āgāt iti","adv. + 3 sg. aor. of ā-gam","and from where did he (just now) come back?\""],["tat u ha na mene gārgyaḥ","acc. + 3 sg. impf. of man","this Gārgya did not know"]],"lit":"\"Ajātaśatru asked, 'When this man was asleep, this Person who consists of consciousness (<em>vijñānamaya puruṣa</em>) — where was he then? And from where did he (just now) return?' This too Gārgya did not know.\"","para":"The king poses the decisive question about the <strong>vijñānamaya puruṣa</strong> — the Self made of consciousness, the knower behind the senses: <em>where does it go in deep sleep, and whence does it return on waking?</em> Gārgya, master of cosmic \"persons\" but ignorant of the inner Self, <strong>cannot answer</strong>.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>vijñānamaya puruṣa</em> = the Self associated with the intellect (the experiencer), here used to point beyond all the external \"persons\" to the inner knower. The question \"where in sleep?\" forces attention onto the substrate of the states — the real Brahman."],["Sureśvara","the inability to say \"where\" the conscious self goes exposes that object-knowledge cannot locate the subject; the subject is not anywhere as an object."],["Krishnananda","the heart of the teaching — the conscious self does not travel anywhere; it withdraws into its own source. Gārgya's silence shows the gap between knowing objects and knowing the knower."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the question every seeker must face: what am I in dreamless sleep, when I know nothing yet still <em>am</em>?"],["Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>vijñānamaya</em> as the \"intellect-self\"; Olivelle reads the sleep-question as BU's signature route to the witnessing consciousness."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.17","title":"The Self withdraws the senses into the space within the heart","sa":["स होवाचाजातशत्रुः — यत्रैष एतत्सुप्तोऽभूद्य एष विज्ञानमयः पुरुषस्तदेषां प्राणानां विज्ञानेन विज्ञानमादाय य एषोऽन्तर्हृदय आकाशस्तस्मिञ्छेते । तानि यदा गृह्णाति । अथ हैतत्पुरुषः स्वपिति नाम । तद्गृहीत एव प्राणो भवति । गृहीता वाग् । गृहीतं चक्षुर् । गृहीतं श्रोत्रम् । गृहीतं मनः ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovācājātaśatruḥ -- yatraiṣa etat supto 'bhūd ya eṣa vijñānamayaḥ puruṣas tad eṣāṃ prāṇānāṃ vijñānena vijñānam ādāya ya eṣo 'ntar hṛdaya ākāśas tasmiñ chete | tāni yadā gṛhṇāti | atha haitat puruṣaḥ svapiti nāma | tad gṛhīta eva prāṇo bhavati | gṛhītā vāg | gṛhītaṃ cakṣur | gṛhītaṃ śrotram | gṛhītaṃ manaḥ || 17 ||"],"words":[["yatra eṣaḥ etat suptaḥ abhūt yaḥ eṣaḥ vijñāna-mayaḥ puruṣaḥ","(as 2.1.16)","\"when this consciousness-person was asleep,"],["tad eṣāṃ prāṇānāṃ vijñānena vijñānam ādāya","gen. pl. + instr. + acc. + ger. of ā-dā","then, taking the consciousness of these senses into (his own) consciousness,"],["yaḥ eṣaḥ antaḥ hṛdaye ākāśaḥ tasmin śete","rel. + adv. + loc. + m. nom. + loc. + pres. of śī","he rests in that space which is within the heart"],["tāni yadā gṛhṇāti","acc. pl. + rel. + 3 sg. pres. of grah","when he takes them (the senses) in,"],["atha ha etat puruṣaḥ svapiti nāma","+ pres. of svap","then the person is said to 'sleep'"],["tad gṛhītaḥ eva prāṇaḥ bhavati","pp. + nom.","then the breath is held in,"],["gṛhītā vāk, gṛhītaṃ cakṣuḥ, gṛhītaṃ śrotram, gṛhītaṃ manaḥ","pp.","speech is held in, the eye, the ear, the mind — all held in"]],"lit":"\"Ajātaśatru said, 'When this consciousness-Person was asleep, then, having gathered the consciousness of these senses into (his own) consciousness, he rests in that space which is within the heart. When he gathers them in, the person is said to \"sleep\"; then the breath is held in, speech is held in, the eye, the ear, the mind — all are held in.'\"","para":"The answer to 2.1.16: in sleep the conscious Self <strong>withdraws the senses</strong> (it \"gathers their cognition into its own cognition\") and <strong>rests in the <em>space within the heart</em> (hṛdayākāśa).</strong> \"Sleep\" just means the senses have been drawn in — breath, speech, eye, ear, mind all \"held.\" The Self does not go <em>anywhere external</em>; it retracts into its own inner space, carrying the powers with it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>hṛdayākāśa</em> (heart-space) is the seat where the Self abides with the withdrawn faculties; \"gathering the cognition of the senses into consciousness\" shows the senses borrow their knowing from the one Self. Sleep is the Self's reabsorption of its instruments — proof that it is their independent source."],["Sureśvara","the senses are shown to have no autonomous functioning; in sleep they are \"held\" by the Self, which alone persists — the witness behind the gathered powers."],["Krishnananda","a profound account of sleep as the soul's nightly return to its own ground (the heart-space); the senses are not destroyed but <em>reabsorbed</em>, to be projected again on waking. The Self is the gatherer and the resting-place."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical pointer — what falls asleep is the sense-mind; the conscious Self that \"gathers\" them does not sleep. Meditation seeks that wakeful gatherer."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>hṛdayākāśa</em> (the space in the heart) as the locus of the sleeping self; Olivelle connects it to the later heart-space teachings (ChU 8.1; BU 4.4)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.18","title":"In dream the Self becomes its own light and king","sa":["स यत्रैतत्स्वप्न्यया चरति ते हास्य लोकाः । तदुतेव महाराजो भवत्युतेव महाब्राह्मणः । उतेवोच्चावचं निगच्छति । स यथा महाराजो जानपदान्गृहीत्वा स्वे जनपदे यथाकामं परिवर्तेतैवमेवैष एतत्प्राणान्गृहीत्वा स्वे शरीरे यथाकामं परिवर्तते ॥ १८ ॥"],"iast":["sa yatraitat svapnyayā carati te hāsya lokāḥ | tad uteva mahārājo bhavaty uteva mahābrāhmaṇaḥ | utevoccāvacaṃ nigacchati | sa yathā mahārājo jānapadān gṛhītvā sve janapade yathākāmaṃ parivartetaivam evaiṣa etat prāṇān gṛhītvā sve śarīre yathākāmaṃ parivartate || 18 ||"],"words":[["saḥ yatra etat svapnyayā carati","rel. + instr. (dream-state) + pres. of car","when he moves about in the dream-state"],["te ha asya lokāḥ","nom. pl. + gen.","those are his worlds"],["tad uta iva mahārājaḥ bhavati uta iva mahābrāhmaṇaḥ","nom.","then he becomes, as it were, a great king, or a great brāhmaṇa"],["uta iva uccāvacaṃ nigacchati","acc. (high & low) + pres.","he enters, as it were, high and low conditions"],["saḥ yathā mahārājaḥ jānapadān gṛhītvā sve janapade yathākāmaṃ parivarteta","nom. + acc. pl. + ger. + loc. + opt. of pari-vṛt","as a great king, taking his subjects, would move at will within his domain"],["evam eva eṣaḥ etat prāṇān gṛhītvā sve śarīre yathākāmaṃ parivartate","acc. pl. + loc. + pres.","so this one, taking the senses, moves at will within his own body"]],"lit":"\"When he moves about in dream, those are his (own) worlds. Then he becomes, as it were, a great king, or a great brāhmaṇa; he enters, as it were, high and low conditions. As a great king, taking his subjects, moves at will within his own domain, so this one, taking the senses, moves at will within his own body.\"","para":"In <strong>dream</strong>, the Self <strong>creates its own worlds</strong> out of itself, becoming \"king\" or \"sage,\" rising and falling through self-projected scenes. Just as a sovereign moves freely through his realm with his retinue, the Self moves freely through <strong>its own body</strong>, having gathered the senses — the <strong>creator and sole sovereign of the dream-world</strong>, which is made of nothing but itself.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","dream demonstrates the Self's <strong>self-luminous creativity</strong> — with no external materials it projects a complete world (\"those are his worlds\"), proving it is the independent light by which all is illumined (developed fully in 4.3.9–14). It is \"king\" of a realm spun from itself."],["Sureśvara","dream is the decisive evidence that the Self is the source of the experienced world; the dream-objects are non-different from the dreaming consciousness."],["Krishnananda","in dream \"you are the creator, sustainer, and destroyer of your universe\" — the Self needs nothing outside to generate experience; this self-sufficiency points to its absolute nature."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a meditation on the dreamer as a model of God's relation to the world — sole author of a world made of itself."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the \"those are his worlds\" motif and the king-in-his-realm simile as central to BU's argument that consciousness is self-illuminating and world-projecting."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.19","title":"In deep sleep, supreme peace in the heart's channels","sa":["अथ यदा सुषुप्तो भवति । यदा न कस्य चन वेद । हिता नाम नाड्यो द्वासप्ततिः सहस्राणि हृदयात्पुरीततमभिप्रतिष्ठन्ते । ताभिः प्रत्यवसृप्य पुरीतति शेते । स यथा कुमारो वा महाराजो वा महाब्राह्मणो वातिघ्नीमानन्दस्य गत्वा शयीत । एवमेवैष एतच्छेते ॥ १९ ॥"],"iast":["atha yadā suṣupto bhavati | yadā na kasya cana veda | hitā nāma nāḍyo dvāsaptatiḥ sahasrāṇi hṛdayāt purītatam abhipratiṣṭhante | tābhiḥ pratyavasṛpya purītati śete | sa yathā kumāro vā mahārājo vā mahābrāhmaṇo vātighnīm ānandasya gatvā śayīta | evam evaiṣa etac chete || 19 ||"],"words":[["atha yadā suṣuptaḥ bhavati","adv. + rel. + nom.","now when he is in deep (dreamless) sleep"],["yadā na kasya cana veda","rel. + gen. + pres. of vid","when he is conscious of nothing whatever"],["hitā nāma nāḍyaḥ dvā-saptatiḥ sahasrāṇi","f. nom. pl. (named hitā) + numeral","the channels called hitā, seventy-two thousand (in number)"],["hṛdayāt purītatam abhipratiṣṭhante","abl. + acc. (the pericardium) + 3 pl. pres.","extend from the heart to the pericardium"],["tābhiḥ pratyavasṛpya purītati śete","instr. pl. + ger. of prati-ava-sṛp + loc. + pres. of śī","creeping back through them, he rests in the pericardium"],["saḥ yathā kumāraḥ vā mahārājaḥ vā mahābrāhmaṇaḥ vā atighnīm ānandasya gatvā śayīta","nom. + acc. (surpassing) + gen. + ger. + opt. of śī","as a child, or a great king, or a great brāhmaṇa, having reached the summit of bliss, would lie"],["evam eva eṣaḥ etat śete","pres.","even so does he rest"]],"lit":"\"Now when he is in deep (dreamless) sleep — when he is conscious of nothing whatever — (he withdraws) through the channels called <em>hitā</em>, seventy-two thousand in number, which extend from the heart to the pericardium; creeping back through them, he rests in the pericardium. As a child, or a great king, or a great brāhmaṇa, having reached the very summit of bliss, would lie (at perfect ease), even so does he rest.\"","para":"In <strong>dreamless sleep</strong> the Self, now aware of nothing, <strong>retreats through the 72,000 fine channels (nāḍīs) called <em>hitā</strong></em> that radiate from the heart, and <strong>rests in the pericardium</strong> in <strong>supreme bliss</strong> — likened to a contented child, or a great king or sage at the peak of contentment. Deep sleep is thus not annihilation but the Self's blissful repose in its own ground (anticipating 4.3.21–32).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","dreamless sleep is the Self <strong>abiding in itself</strong>, free of objects, in natural <em>ānanda</em> (bliss) — evidence that the Self's intrinsic nature is bliss, not the bliss of any object (since none is present). The <em>hitā</em> channels and pericardium describe the locus; the \"child/king/sage at the summit of bliss\" conveys the objectless joy."],["Sureśvara","the bliss of dreamless sleep, with no object to cause it, proves <em>ānanda</em> is the Self's own nature — a key step toward \"Brahman is bliss.\""],["Krishnananda","everyone tastes the Self's bliss nightly in deep sleep but unconsciously; the goal of yoga is to enter that same peace <em>consciously</em>. The \"summit of bliss\" is the Self resting objectless in itself."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note that even a beggar in deep sleep enjoys the bliss of a king — the bliss is the Self's, not circumstance's; sleep is a nightly pointer to liberation."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the 72,000 <em>hitā</em> channels and the pericardium (<em>purītat</em>); Olivelle compares the deep-sleep bliss accounts of BU 4.3 and ChU 8.6."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1.20","title":"All issues from the Self; \"the Real of the real\"","sa":["स यथोर्णवाभिस्तन्तुनोच्चरेद्यथाग्नेः क्षुद्रा विष्फुलिङ्गा व्युच्चरन्त्येवमेवास्मादात्मनः सर्वे प्राणाः सर्वे लोकाः सर्वे देवाः सर्वाणि भूतानि व्युच्चरन्ति । तस्योपनिषत्सत्यस्य सत्यमिति । प्राणा वै सत्यं तेषामेष सत्यम् ॥ २० ॥"],"iast":["sa yathorṇavābhis tantunoccared yathāgneḥ kṣudrā viṣphuliṅgā vyuccaranty evam evāsmād ātmanaḥ sarve prāṇāḥ sarve lokāḥ sarve devāḥ sarvāṇi bhūtāni vyuccaranti | tasyopaniṣat satyasya satyam iti | prāṇā vai satyaṃ teṣām eṣa satyam || 20 ||"],"words":[["saḥ yathā ūrṇavābhiḥ tantunā uccaret","nom. + instr. (spider) + instr. (thread) + opt. of ud-car","as a spider moves out by (its) thread"],["yathā agneḥ kṣudrāḥ viṣphuliṅgāḥ vyuccaranti","abl. + nom. pl. (tiny sparks) + 3 pl. pres.","as from a fire tiny sparks fly out"],["evam eva asmāt ātmanaḥ","abl.","even so, from this Self,"],["sarve prāṇāḥ sarve lokāḥ sarve devāḥ sarvāṇi bhūtāni vyuccaranti","nom. pl. + 3 pl. pres.","all breaths, all worlds, all gods, all beings issue forth"],["tasya upaniṣat satyasya satyam iti","gen. + f. nom. + n. gen. + n. nom.","its secret name (upaniṣad) is \"the Real of the real\""],["prāṇāḥ vai satyam teṣām eṣaḥ satyam","nom. pl. + n. nom. + gen.","the vital powers are the real; and of them, He is the Real (the Real of the real)"]],"lit":"\"As a spider moves out (along) its thread, and as from a fire tiny sparks fly out in all directions, even so from this Self issue forth all breaths, all worlds, all gods, all beings. Its secret name (<em>upaniṣad</em>) is '<strong>the Real of the real</strong>' (<em>satyasya satyam</em>): the vital powers (prāṇas) are 'the real,' and He is <strong>their</strong> Real.\"","para":"The climax. From the <strong>Self</strong> — as a spider spins out its web, as sparks leap from fire — issue <strong>all the life-breaths, worlds, gods, and beings</strong>: the Self is the single source of the entire manifold. Its <strong>upaniṣad (secret designation) is <em>satyasya satyam</em>, \"the Real of the real.\"</strong> The senses/vital powers are <em>satya</em> (real, in the empirical sense), but the Self is the <strong>deeper Reality behind that reality</strong> — the truth of which even the truths are only relatively true. (This is the formula promised at the close of Adhyāya 1, 1.6.3.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the spider/sparks similes assert that the world is <strong>non-different from its source</strong> and effortlessly emitted from it. <em>Satyasya satyam</em>: the prāṇas (and all empirical existents) are <em>vyāvahārika-satya</em> (relatively real), but Brahman is their <em>pāramārthika</em> ground — \"the Real of the (apparent) real,\" the truth that does not depend on anything else while all else depends on it. The supreme <em>upaniṣad</em> (esoteric equation) of this brāhmaṇa."],["Sureśvara","effect emerges from cause without the cause being exhausted (spider/web); the graded reality (<em>satya</em> / <em>satyasya satyam</em>) distinguishes the dependent real from the independent Real — the heart of Advaita ontology."],["Krishnananda","\"Everything comes out of the Self as a web from a spider, with no external aid\" — the Self is the material and efficient cause of all. <em>Satyasya satyam</em> means: the world is real <em>as</em> the Self, but as a separate thing it is only relatively real; the Self alone is the Real that underlies every real."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the meditative essence — trace every breath, world, and being back to the one Self from which it sparks forth, and rest in that \"Real of the real.\""],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the famous spider and sparks images (cf. Muṇḍaka 1.1.7; 2.1.1) and the formula <em>satyasya satyam</em>; Olivelle reads it as BU's defining statement of a reality more fundamental than the empirically real."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This brāhmaṇa stages the move <strong>from object-knowledge to Self-knowledge</strong> as a face-to-face drama:","1. <strong>The catalogue of conditioned \"persons\"</strong> (2.1.2–13). Gārgya's twelve definitions are not <em>false</em> — each is a legitimate <em>pratīka-upāsanā</em> (symbol-meditation) with a real, specific worldly fruit. But every one locates Brahman as a <strong>person <em>in</em> something</strong> (sun, moon, body…), i.e., as an <strong>object/effect</strong>. The king accepts them as <strong>epithets of the conditioned Brahman</strong>, never the absolute.","2. <strong>The exhaustion of objectivity</strong> (2.1.13–14). When Gārgya reaches even the \"person in the body-self\" and still has not found the unconditioned, his definitions run out and he falls <strong>silent</strong>, then asks to learn — pride yielding to inquiry, brāhmaṇa pupiling a kṣatriya.","3. <strong>The turn to the subject via the three states</strong> (2.1.15–19). The king teaches not by doctrine but by the <strong>universal experiment of sleep</strong>: names cannot wake a sleeper (the deities are dormant); the <strong>vijñānamaya puruṣa</strong> (the Self of consciousness) withdraws the senses into the <strong>heart-space</strong> (sleep, 2.1.17), projects entire <strong>worlds of its own</strong> (dream, 2.1.18), and rests in <strong>objectless bliss</strong> in the <em>hitā</em> channels (deep sleep, 2.1.19). The Self is thus revealed as the <strong>constant subject</strong> underlying the three states — anticipating the full analysis of 4.3.","4. <strong>The source and the formula</strong> (2.1.20). From this Self, as web from spider and sparks from fire, issue <strong>all breaths, worlds, gods, beings</strong>; its secret name is <strong>satyasya satyam, \"the Real of the real.\"</strong> The empirically real (the prāṇas, the world) is real only as grounded in the Self, which alone is the independent Reality.","The brāhmaṇa thus delivers, in narrative form, the same non-dualism as 1.4.10 (<em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em>): Brahman is not any \"person out there\" but the <strong>innermost conscious Self</strong>, the source and substrate of everything — to be found by turning from the known to the knower."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   GĀRGYA vs. AJĀTAŚATRU (BU 2.1)\n   ==============================\n   GĀRGYA points OUTWARD: &quot;Brahman = the Person IN ___&quot;      AJĀTAŚATRU: &quot;only an epithet/effect&quot;\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     sun · moon · lightning · space · wind · fire · water ·          (12 conditioned\n     mirror · echo · quarters · shadow · body-self                    &#x27;persons&#x27;, each\n        |   each = a saguṇa upāsana with a worldly fruit              with its own fruit)\n        v\n     ...all twelve refuted -&gt; GĀRGYA FALLS SILENT -&gt; asks to be a pupil\n        |\n        v   the king turns attention INWARD via a SLEEPING MAN:\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     WAKING   -&gt; the senses active, world &quot;out there&quot;\n     SLEEP    -&gt; Self GATHERS the senses (breath/speech/eye/ear/mind &quot;held&quot;)\n                 and rests in the SPACE WITHIN THE HEART (hṛdayākāśa)        [2.1.17]\n     DREAM    -&gt; Self projects &quot;his own worlds,&quot; king of a self-made realm   [2.1.18]\n     DEEP SLEEP-&gt; withdraws through 72,000 hitā channels -&gt; rests in bliss   [2.1.19]\n        |        (= the constant SUBJECT behind all three states)\n        v\n   THE SOURCE (2.1.20):\n        SELF  ── spins out like ── a SPIDER&#x27;S web\n              ── leaps out like ── SPARKS from fire\n              ==&gt; all prāṇas · worlds · gods · beings\n        upaniṣad:  SATYASYA SATYAM  — &quot;the Real of the real&quot;\n        (prāṇas = real;  the Self = the Real behind that real)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Stop looking for God \"in\" things.</strong> Notice Gārgya's error: he keeps placing Brahman as a <em>person in</em> the sun, the body, etc. — always an object. Turn the search around: <em>who is the one aware of all these?</em> Brahman is the seer, never the seen.","2. <strong>Use symbol-meditations, but don't stop there.</strong> The twelve <em>upāsanās</em> each \"work\" (they yield real results). Pursue a chosen symbol if you wish — but know it is a stepping-stone, not the summit.","3. <strong>Make sleep your teacher</strong> (2.1.16–19). Each night you withdraw the senses into the heart, create dream-worlds from yourself, and rest in objectless bliss. Reflect at the edge of sleep: <em>what is it that gathers the senses and remains when all is withdrawn?</em> That is the Self.","4. <strong>Taste the bliss of deep sleep consciously.</strong> The peace of dreamless sleep is the Self's own bliss, enjoyed nightly without awareness. Meditation aims to enter that same objectless peace <em>awake</em> — resting as the witness, not the witnessed.","5. <strong>Trace the world back to its spider</strong> (2.1.20). In contemplation, watch how breaths, perceptions, thoughts, and the whole world \"spark out\" from your own awareness — and rest in their source, the \"Real of the real.\"","6. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> humility precedes knowing. The learned Gārgya could not see the Self until his definitions failed and he became a student. The mind full of object-knowledge must fall silent for the knower to be recognised."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 2 · Brāhmaṇa 2.2 — The Person in the body: the \"infant\" breath and the head","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 2.2","title":"The Person in the body: the \"infant\" breath and the head","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>A short, dense</em> <strong>adhyātma meditation</strong>: <em>the central breath as a tethered \"infant\" (śiśu); the</em> <strong>seven \"imperishables\"</strong> <em>(the seven openings of the head) and the deities attached to the eye; and a</em> <strong>ṛk-verse</strong> <em>picturing the head as an inverted cup with the seven seers (the seven breaths) on its rim and speech as the eighth — the seven seers then identified with the body's own organs.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.1","title":"The infant breath, with its supports and tether","sa":["यो ह वै शिशुं साधानं सप्रत्याधानं सस्थूणं सदामं वेद सप्त ह द्विषतो भ्रातृव्यानवरुणद्धि । अयं वाव शिशुर्योऽयं मध्यमः प्राणः । तस्येदमेवाधानमिदं प्रत्याधानं प्राणः स्थूणान्नं दाम ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["yo ha vai śiśuṃ sādhānaṃ sapratyādhānaṃ sasthūṇaṃ sadāmaṃ veda sapta ha dviṣato bhrātṛvyān avaruṇaddhi | ayaṃ vāva śiśur yo 'yaṃ madhyamaḥ prāṇaḥ | tasyedam evādhānam idaṃ pratyādhānaṃ prāṇaḥ sthūṇānnaṃ dāma || 1 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ ha vai śiśuṃ sa-ādhānaṃ sa-pratyādhānaṃ sa-sthūṇaṃ sa-dāmaṃ veda","acc. + adjs. + pf.","whoever knows the infant (śiśu) together with its placing, counter-placing, post, and tether"],["sapta ha dviṣataḥ bhrātṛvyān avaruṇaddhi","acc. pl. (hating rivals) + 3 sg. pres. of ava-rudh","wards off the seven hating rivals"],["ayaṃ vāva śiśuḥ yaḥ ayaṃ madhyamaḥ prāṇaḥ","m. nom.","this very \"infant\" is the central breath"],["tasya idam eva ādhānam","n. nom.","its placing (resting-base) is this (body)"],["idaṃ pratyādhānam","n. nom.","its counter-placing is this (the head)"],["prāṇaḥ sthūṇā","m. nom. + f. nom.","the breath is its post"],["annam dāma","n. nom.","food is its tether"]],"lit":"\"Whoever knows the <strong>infant</strong> (<em>śiśu</em>) together with its placing, its counter-placing, its post, and its tether — he wards off the seven hating rivals. This 'infant' is the <strong>central breath</strong>. Its placing (resting-base) is this (body); its counter-placing is this (the head); the breath is its post (<em>sthūṇā</em>); and food is its tether (<em>dāma</em>).\"","para":"The <strong>central breath (madhyama-prāṇa)</strong> is pictured as a tethered young animal/infant: it rests on a <strong>base</strong> (the body), against a <strong>counter-support</strong> (the head), bound to a <strong>post</strong> (the breath itself) by a <strong>cord</strong> (food). To know this fourfold structure is to overcome the <strong>\"seven hostile rivals\"</strong> — the seven sense-openings as potential gateways of evil (cf. 1.3). The image domesticates the vital life as a tethered creature within the body.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>śiśu</em> is the <em>mukhya-prāṇa</em> (from 1.3, 1.5), here given a \"stall\": body and head as its supports, breath as the post it is tied to, food as the rope that binds life to the body. Knowing this secures mastery over the seven head-openings (\"rivals\") through which sense-evil enters."],["Sureśvara","the tethered-infant image expresses how life is <em>held</em> in the body by food and breath; mastery of the structure is mastery of the embodied condition."],["Krishnananda","the breath is the \"baby\" sustained in the body by food (the cord) and breath (the post); a meditation on the delicate fastening of life to the body."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the \"seven rivals\" = the seven apertures of the head; knowing the breath-structure guards them."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a riddling <em>upāsana</em>; Olivelle reads the stall-imagery (peg, tether) as a homely metaphor for the breath's lodging in the body."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.2","title":"The seven imperishables; the deities in the eye","sa":["तमेताः सप्ताक्षितय उपतिष्ठन्ते । तद्या इमा अक्षँल्लोहिन्यो राजयस्ताभिरेनं रुद्रोऽन्वायत्तः । अथ या अक्षन्नापस्ताभिः पर्जन्यः । या कनीनका तयादित्यः । यत्कृष्णं तेनाग्निर् । यच्छुक्लं तेनेन्द्रः । अधरयैनं वर्तन्या पृथिव्यन्वायत्ता । द्यौरुत्तरया । नास्यान्नं क्षीयते य एवं वेद ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["tam etāḥ saptākṣitaya upatiṣṭhante | tad yā imā akṣaṃl lohinyo rājayas tābhir enaṃ rudro 'nvāyattaḥ | atha yā akṣann āpas tābhiḥ parjanyaḥ | yā kanīnakā tayādityaḥ | yat kṛṣṇaṃ tenāgnir | yac chuklaṃ tenendraḥ | adharayainaṃ vartanyā pṛthivy anvāyattā | dyaur uttarayā | nāsyānnaṃ kṣīyate ya evaṃ veda || 2 ||"],"words":[["tam etāḥ sapta akṣitayaḥ upatiṣṭhante","acc. + f. nom. pl. (imperishables) + pres.","these seven \"imperishables\" attend on it (the breath)"],["tat yāḥ imāḥ akṣan lohinyaḥ rājayaḥ","loc. (in the eye) + f. nom. pl. (red lines)","the red streaks in the eye —"],["tābhiḥ enaṃ rudraḥ anvāyattaḥ","instr. + acc. + nom. pp.","by them Rudra is attached to him"],["atha yāḥ akṣan āpaḥ tābhiḥ parjanyaḥ","loc. + f. nom. pl. (the watery part)","the water (moisture) in the eye — by it Parjanya"],["yā kanīnakā tayā ādityaḥ","f. nom. (pupil) + instr.","the pupil — by it Āditya (the sun)"],["yat kṛṣṇaṃ tena agniḥ","n. + instr.","the black part — by it Agni"],["yat śuklaṃ tena indraḥ","n. + instr.","the white part — by it Indra"],["adharayā enaṃ vartanyā pṛthivī anvāyattā","instr. (lower lid/line) + nom. pp.","by the lower lid the earth is attached to him"],["dyauḥ uttarayā","f. nom. + instr. (upper)","the sky, by the upper"],["na asya annaṃ kṣīyate yaḥ evaṃ veda","gen. + pres. pass.","his food does not fail, who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"These seven 'imperishables' (<em>akṣiti</em>) attend on it. The red streaks in the eye — by them <strong>Rudra</strong> is joined to him; the watery part of the eye — by it <strong>Parjanya</strong>; the pupil — by it <strong>Āditya</strong> (the sun); the black part — by it <strong>Agni</strong>; the white part — by it <strong>Indra</strong>. By the lower eyelid the <strong>earth</strong> is joined to him, by the upper the <strong>sky</strong>. His food does not fail, who knows this.\"","para":"The breath is \"attended\" by <strong>seven imperishables</strong> (the seven head-openings / vital powers). The meditation then maps the <strong>eye</strong> — a microcosm — onto great deities: its red lines = <strong>Rudra</strong>, its moisture = <strong>Parjanya</strong> (rain-god), its pupil = <strong>Āditya</strong> (sun), its dark ring = <strong>Agni</strong> (fire), its white = <strong>Indra</strong>; the lower lid = <strong>earth</strong>, the upper = <strong>sky</strong>. The single eye holds the whole pantheon and cosmos; the knower's nourishment never fails.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>adhidaiva</em> deities are located in the parts of the eye, making the eye a complete cosmic-divine microcosm; such <em>upāsanā</em> yields unfailing sustenance. The \"seven imperishables\" are the seven head-apertures as enduring seats of the powers."],["Sureśvara","the cosmos and its gods are present in the smallest organ — a training in seeing the macrocosm within the microcosm."],["Krishnananda","a meditation on the eye as a sacred map: the very gods preside over its parts; perception is a divine cosmic event, not a mechanical one."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical <em>dṛṣṭi</em>-meditation locating Rudra, the sun, fire, Indra, earth, and sky in one's own eye."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the detailed ocular anatomy mapped to deities; Olivelle reads it as typical micro-macro <em>bandhu</em> applied to the body's most \"luminous\" organ."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.3","title":"The śloka: the head as an inverted cup","sa":["तदेष श्लोको भवति — अर्वाग्बिलश्चमस ऊर्ध्वबुध्नस्तस्मिन्यशो निहितं विश्वरूपम् । तस्यासत ऋषयः सप्त तीरे वागष्टमी ब्रह्मणा संविदानेति । अर्वाग्बिलश्चमस ऊर्ध्वबुध्न इति । इदं तच्छरीर एष ह्यर्वाग्बिलश्चमस ऊर्ध्वबुध्नः । तस्मिन्यशो निहितं विश्वरूपमिति । प्राणा वै यशो विश्वरूपम् । प्राणानेतदाह । तस्यासत ऋषयः सप्त तीर इति । प्राणा वा ऋषयः । प्राणानेतदाह । वागष्टमी ब्रह्मणा संविदानेति । वाग्घ्यष्टमी ब्रह्मणा संवित्ते ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["tad eṣa śloko bhavati -- arvāgbilaś camasa ūrdhvabudhnas tasmin yaśo nihitaṃ viśvarūpam | tasyāsata ṛṣayaḥ sapta tīre vāg aṣṭamī brahmaṇā saṃvidāneti | arvāgbilaś camasa ūrdhvabudhna iti | idaṃ tac charīra eṣa hy arvāgbilaś camasa ūrdhvabudhnaḥ | tasmin yaśo nihitaṃ viśvarūpam iti | prāṇā vai yaśo viśvarūpam | prāṇān etad āha | tasyāsata ṛṣayaḥ sapta tīra iti | prāṇā vā ṛṣayaḥ | prāṇān etad āha | vāg aṣṭamī brahmaṇā saṃvidāneti | vāg ghy aṣṭamī brahmaṇā saṃvitte || 3 ||"],"words":[["tat eṣaḥ ślokaḥ bhavati","m. nom.","on this there is this verse:"],["arvāk-bilaḥ camasaḥ ūrdhva-budhnaḥ","m. nom. (mouth-downward) + m. nom. (cup) + (bottom-up)","\"a cup with its mouth below and bottom above"],["tasmin yaśaḥ nihitaṃ viśva-rūpam","loc. + n. nom. pp. + adj.","in it glory of every form is set"],["tasya āsate ṛṣayaḥ sapta tīre","gen. + 3 pl. pres. of ās + nom. pl. + loc.","on its rim sit the seven seers"],["vāk aṣṭamī brahmaṇā saṃvidānā iti","f. nom. + ord. + instr. + pres.part.","speech the eighth, communing with the brahman\""],["idaṃ tat śarīram","n. nom.","\"the cup with mouth below, bottom above\" is this head/body"],["prāṇāḥ vai yaśaḥ viśvarūpam","m. nom. pl.","the breaths are the \"glory of every form\""],["prāṇāḥ vā ṛṣayaḥ","nom. pl.","the breaths are the \"seven seers\""],["vāk hi aṣṭamī brahmaṇā saṃvitte","3 sg. pres. of sam-vid","speech the eighth indeed communes with the (Vedic) word"]],"lit":"\"On this there is a verse: 'A <strong>cup with its mouth below and bottom above</strong>, in it is set glory of every form; on its rim sit the <strong>seven seers</strong>, with <strong>speech the eighth</strong>, communing with the sacred word.' — 'The cup with mouth below, bottom above' is this <strong>head</strong>; 'the glory of every form set in it' — the <strong>breaths</strong> are that all-formed glory; 'the seven seers seated on its rim' — the <strong>breaths</strong> are the seven seers; 'speech the eighth, communing with the brahman' — for <strong>speech</strong> is the eighth, and it communes with the sacred word (Veda).\"","para":"A Vedic <em>ṛk</em> is quoted and decoded. The <strong>head</strong> is an upside-down <strong>cup</strong> (mouth = the face/openings below, rounded skull = bottom above). Within it is <strong>\"glory of every form\"</strong> — the <strong>vital breaths</strong>. On its \"rim\" sit <strong>seven seers</strong> — the <strong>seven openings/breaths of the head</strong> (two eyes, two ears, two nostrils, one mouth). <strong>Speech is the eighth</strong>, joined to the <strong>brahman</strong> (the Vedic word). The skull is thus a sacrificial vessel holding the seven seer-breaths and speech.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the verse is an enigmatic (<em>paroкṣa</em>) description of the head as a divine cup; its decoding identifies the \"seers\" with the seven cephalic prāṇas and the eighth with speech bound to scripture — the body as a microcosmic sacrifice."],["Sureśvara","the seven seer-breaths plus speech constitute the cognitive apparatus seated in the \"cup\" of the head, all engaged with the sacred."],["Krishnananda","a poetic emblem — your skull is an inverted bowl brimming with the \"glory of all forms\" (the breaths), the ancient seers seated as your seven head-openings; perception is a sages' assembly."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","meditate on the head as this sacred cup of the seven seers, sanctifying the senses."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the borrowed ṛk and its allegoresis; Olivelle observes the head-as-cup riddle is a classic <em>brahmodya</em> enigma resolved into bodily organs."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2.4","title":"The seven seers as the body's organs; Atri = speech (the eater)","sa":["इमावेव गोतमभरद्वाजौ । अयमेव गोतमोऽयं भरद्वाजः । इमावेव विश्वामित्रजमदग्नी । अयमेव विश्वामित्रोऽयं जमदग्निः । इमावेव वसिष्ठकश्यपौ । अयमेव वसिष्ठोऽयं कश्यपः । वागेवात्रिः । वाचा ह्यन्नमद्यते । अत्तिर्ह वै नामैतद्यदत्रिरिति । सर्वस्यात्ता भवति । सर्वमस्यान्नं भवति य एवं वेद ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["imāv eva gotamabharadvājau | ayam eva gotamo 'yaṃ bharadvājaḥ | imāv eva viśvāmitrajamadagnī | ayam eva viśvāmitro 'yaṃ jamadagniḥ | imāv eva vasiṣṭhakaśyapau | ayam eva vasiṣṭho 'yaṃ kaśyapaḥ | vāg evātriḥ | vācā hy annam adyate | attir ha vai nāmaitad yad atrir iti | sarvasyāttā bhavati | sarvam asyānnaṃ bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 4 ||"],"words":[["imau eva gotama-bharadvājau ayam eva gotamaḥ ayaṃ bharadvājaḥ","m. nom. du.","these two (ears) are Gotama and Bharadvāja — this one Gotama, this Bharadvāja"],["imau eva viśvāmitra-jamadagnī …","m. nom. du.","these two (eyes) are Viśvāmitra and Jamadagni"],["imau eva vasiṣṭha-kaśyapau …","m. nom. du.","these two (nostrils) are Vasiṣṭha and Kaśyapa"],["vāk eva atriḥ","f. nom. + m. nom.","speech is Atri"],["vācā hi annam adyate","instr. + pres. pass. of ad","for by speech food is eaten"],["attiḥ ha vai nāma etat yat atriḥ iti","m. nom. (eater) + n. nom.","\"Atri\" is verily this name \"eater\" (at-tṛ)"],["sarvasya attā bhavati sarvam asya annaṃ bhavati yaḥ evaṃ veda","gen. + m. nom. + gen.","he becomes the eater of all; all becomes his food, who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"These two (ears) are <strong>Gotama and Bharadvāja</strong> — this one Gotama, this Bharadvāja; these two (eyes) are <strong>Viśvāmitra and Jamadagni</strong>; these two (nostrils) are <strong>Vasiṣṭha and Kaśyapa</strong>; and <strong>speech is Atri</strong> — for by speech food is eaten (<em>ad-</em>), and 'Atri' is just this name 'eater' (<em>attṛ</em>). He who knows this becomes the eater of all, and all becomes his food.\"","para":"The \"seven seers\" of the verse are now identified with the <strong>paired organs of the head</strong>: the two <strong>ears</strong> = Gotama &amp; Bharadvāja; the two <strong>eyes</strong> = Viśvāmitra &amp; Jamadagni; the two <strong>nostrils</strong> = Vasiṣṭha &amp; Kaśyapa; and <strong>speech</strong> (the seventh/mouth) = <strong>Atri</strong> — etymologised as <em>attṛ</em>, \"the eater,\" because speech \"eats\" (takes in) food. The familiar <em>phala</em>: the knower becomes <strong>the eater of all</strong> (echoing 1.2.5, 1.3.17, 2.2.4) — the consuming subject, not the consumed.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the legendary seven seers are internalised as one's own cephalic organs — the body is the seat of the <em>ṛṣis</em>. The Atri/<em>attṛ</em> etymology returns the chapter to the eater-vs-eaten theme: speech (the mouth) is the gateway of eating, and the knower becomes the universal eater."],["Sureśvara","the seers-as-organs identification dignifies perception as a sage's activity; \"eater of all\" again locates immortality in the consuming subject."],["Krishnananda","your seven head-openings are the seven Vedic seers; the body is a constellation of sages. The Atri-pun reaffirms that to know is to \"eat\" (assimilate) all — the subject is never food for another."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a meditation honouring the senses as ṛṣis; practical fruit of inexhaustible nourishment."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the canonical list of the Seven Seers mapped to the head's organs; Olivelle: a vivid instance of internalising the Vedic pantheon-and-sages into the body."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A compact <strong>adhyātma</strong> chapter that \"houses\" the central breath (the hero of 1.3 and 1.5) within the body and head:","1. <strong>The breath as a tethered infant</strong> (2.2.1): life is held in the body by the \"post\" of breath and the \"cord\" of food — a homely emblem of embodiment; knowing it guards the seven head-openings against sense-evil.","2. <strong>The eye as cosmos-and-pantheon</strong> (2.2.2): a single organ contains Rudra, Parjanya, the sun, fire, Indra, earth, and sky — perception is a divine, cosmic event.","3. <strong>The head as the inverted sacred cup</strong> (2.2.3–4): a quoted ṛk pictures the skull as a cup brimming with the \"glory of all forms\" (the breaths), its rim seated with the <strong>seven seers</strong> (the seven head-openings) and <strong>speech</strong> as the eighth; the seers are then named as one's own ears, eyes, nostrils, and speech (Atri = \"the eater\").","The chapter's effect is to <strong>sacralise the body</strong>: the breath is the indwelling life, the eye a pantheon, the head a sacrificial vessel of seers — preparing for 2.3's disclosure of the <strong>two forms of Brahman</strong> seated, finally, in the eye."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE BODY AS SACRED VESSEL (BU 2.2)\n   ==================================\n   ŚIŚU (the central breath) tethered:\n        base = the body | counter = the head | post = breath | cord = food   (2.2.1)\n        -&gt; wards off the 7 &quot;rivals&quot; (the 7 head-openings as gates of evil)\n\n   THE EYE = cosmos + pantheon (2.2.2):\n        red lines -&gt; RUDRA | moisture -&gt; PARJANYA | pupil -&gt; ĀDITYA(sun)\n        black -&gt; AGNI | white -&gt; INDRA | lower lid -&gt; EARTH | upper lid -&gt; SKY\n\n   THE HEAD = an INVERTED CUP (2.2.3):   mouth/openings DOWN, skull-dome UP\n        within it: &quot;glory of all forms&quot; = THE BREATHS\n        on its rim: SEVEN SEERS = the 7 head-openings ; + SPEECH = the 8th (with the Veda)\n\n   THE SEVEN SEERS = the organs (2.2.4):\n        ears  = Gotama &amp; Bharadvāja\n        eyes  = Viśvāmitra &amp; Jamadagni\n        nose  = Vasiṣṭha &amp; Kaśyapa\n        speech= ATRI  (= attṛ, &quot;the EATER&quot;)  -&gt;  knower becomes &quot;eater of all&quot;</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Feel life's tether.</strong> Sense how your life is \"tied\" in the body by breath (the post) and food (the cord). This is not morbid but grounding — a clear seeing of embodiment that loosens over-identification with it.","2. <strong>Sanctify perception</strong> (2.2.2). When you look, recall that the parts of the very eye are seats of Rudra, the sun, fire, Indra, earth, sky. Let seeing become a reverent, cosmic act rather than mechanical consumption of images.","3. <strong>Honour the senses as seers</strong> (2.2.3–4). Treat your ears, eyes, nostrils, and speech as the seven ṛṣis seated on the cup of your head. This dignifies sense-restraint as care for an assembly of sages, not mere suppression.","4. <strong>Be the eater, not the eaten</strong> (Atri = attṛ). In taking in food, words, and impressions, hold the stance of the conscious <em>assimilator</em> — the subject — rather than being consumed by objects.","5. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the body is not an obstacle to the sacred but its <strong>vessel</strong>; the breath, the eye, the head are already a temple of breaths, gods, and seers. Adhyāya 2.3 will now point to the supreme Person seated within it."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 2 · Brāhmaṇa 2.3 — The Two Forms of Brahman; \"neti neti\"","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 2.3","title":"The Two Forms of Brahman; \"neti neti\"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>One of the most important brāhmaṇas in the Upaniṣad. Brahman has</em> <strong>two forms (rūpa)</strong>: <em>the</em> <strong>formed (mūrta)</strong> <em>— gross, mortal, fixed, \"the existent\" (sat) — and the</em> <strong>formless (amūrta)</strong> <em>— subtle, immortal, moving, \"the yonder\" (tyam). It gives the cosmic (adhidaiva) and bodily (adhyātma) instances of each, names the radiant</em> <strong>Person in the right eye / in the sun's orb</strong>, <em>describes his dazzling colours, and culminates in the supreme negative teaching</em> <strong>neti neti</strong> <em>(\"not this, not this\") and the name</em> <strong>satyasya satyam</strong> <em>(\"the Real of the real\").</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 2.3.1","title":"The two forms","sa":["द्वे वाव ब्रह्मणो रूपे । मूर्तं चैवामूर्तं च । मर्त्यं चामृतं च । स्थितं च यच्च । सच्च त्यं च ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["dve vāva brahmaṇo rūpe | mūrtaṃ caivāmūrtaṃ ca | martyaṃ cāmṛtaṃ ca | sthitaṃ ca yac ca | sac ca tyaṃ ca || 1 ||"],"words":[["dve vāva brahmaṇaḥ rūpe","n. nom. du. + gen.","two, indeed, are the forms of Brahman"],["mūrtaṃ ca eva amūrtaṃ ca","n. nom.","the formed (gross) and the formless (subtle)"],["martyaṃ ca amṛtaṃ ca","n. nom.","the mortal and the immortal"],["sthitaṃ ca yat ca","n. nom.","the fixed (static) and the moving"],["sat ca tyaṃ ca","n. nom.","the existent (sat) and the \"yonder\" (tya, the transcendent)"]],"lit":"\"Two, indeed, are the forms of Brahman: the <strong>formed</strong> and the <strong>formless</strong>; the <strong>mortal</strong> and the <strong>immortal</strong>; the <strong>fixed</strong> and the <strong>moving</strong>; the <strong>existent</strong> (<em>sat</em>) and the <strong>yonder</strong> (<em>tyam</em>).\"","para":"Brahman is presented under <strong>two aspects</strong>, paired four ways: <strong>mūrta</strong> (formed/gross/definite) vs. <strong>amūrta</strong> (formless/subtle); <strong>martya</strong> (mortal/perishable) vs. <strong>amṛta</strong> (immortal); <strong>sthita</strong> (static/limited) vs. <strong>yat</strong> (moving/pervasive); <strong>sat</strong> (the empirically existent, \"this\") vs. <strong>tyam</strong> (the transcendent, \"that yonder\"). One Brahman wears a gross-mortal face and a subtle-immortal face. (This is the developed form of 1.6.3's \"immortal veiled by the real.\")","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the two <em>rūpa</em> are the <em>saguṇa</em> aspects of the one Brahman: the gross/perishable (the elements that are \"formed\") and the subtle/imperishable (air and space). Both are still within the realm of <em>nāma-rūpa</em>; the chapter will finally negate even these by <em>neti neti</em> to point at the formless Absolute beyond both."],["Sureśvara","the fourfold pairing analyses manifest reality into gross and subtle poles, the ground for distinguishing the conditioned from the unconditioned."],["Krishnananda","Brahman appears as the visible-tangible (mūrta) and the invisible-pervasive (amūrta); the meditation will rise from the formed, through the formless, to that which is neither."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a map of reality's two registers (gross/subtle) preparing the ascent to the transcendent."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the systematic pairing (mūrta/amūrta, martya/amṛta, sthita/yat, sat/tyam); Olivelle reads it as BU's two-aspect theology of Brahman."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 2.3.2–2.3.3","title":"The formed and formless, cosmically (adhidaivata)","sa":["2.3.2 (the formed): तदेतन्मूर्तं यदन्यद्वायोश्चान्तरिक्षाच्च । एतन्मर्त्यम् । एतत्स्थितम् । एतत्सत् । तस्यैतस्य मूर्तस्यैतस्य मर्त्यस्यैतस्य स्थितस्यैतस्य सत एष रसो य एष तपति । सतो ह्येष रसः ॥ २ ॥","2.3.3 (the formless): अथामूर्तम् । वायुश्चान्तरिक्षश्च । एतदमृतम् । एतद्यत् । एतत्त्यम् । तस्यैतस्यामूर्तस्यैतस्यामृतस्यैतस्य यत एतस्य त्यस्यैष रसो य एष एतस्मिन्मण्डले पुरुषः । त्यस्य ह्येष रस इत्यधिदैवतम् ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["tad etan mūrtaṃ yad anyad vāyoś cāntarikṣāc ca | etan martyam | etat sthitam | etat sat | tasyaitasya mūrtasyaitasya martyasyaitasya sthitasyaitasya sata eṣa raso ya eṣa tapati | sato hy eṣa rasaḥ || 2 ||","athāmūrtam | vāyuś cāntarikṣaś ca | etad amṛtam | etad yat | etat tyam | tasyaitasyāmūrtasyaitasyāmṛtasyaitasya yata etasya tyasyaiṣa raso ya eṣa etasmin maṇḍale puruṣaḥ | tyasya hy eṣa rasa | ity adhidaivatam || 3 ||"],"words":[["tat etat mūrtaṃ yat anyat vāyoḥ ca antarikṣāt ca","n. nom. + abl.","the formed is all that is other than air and space"],["etat martyam etat sthitam etat sat","n. nom.","this is mortal, fixed, existent"],["tasya … sataḥ eṣaḥ rasaḥ yaḥ eṣaḥ tapati","gen. + m. nom. + rel. + pres. of tap","the essence (rasa) of this formed-mortal-fixed-existent is that which gives heat (the sun)"],["sataḥ hi eṣaḥ rasaḥ","gen.","for it is the essence of the existent"],["atha amūrtam vāyuḥ ca antarikṣaḥ ca","n. nom. + m. nom.","now the formless is air and space"],["etat amṛtam etat yat etat tyam","n. nom.","this is immortal, moving, yonder"],["tasya … tyasya eṣaḥ rasaḥ yaḥ eṣaḥ etasmin maṇḍale puruṣaḥ","gen. + loc. (in the orb) + m. nom.","the essence of this formless-immortal-yonder is the Person in that (solar) orb"],["tyasya hi eṣaḥ rasaḥ iti adhidaivatam","n. nom.","for it is the essence of the yonder — thus with respect to the deities"]],"lit":"\"The <strong>formed</strong> is whatever is other than air and space; it is mortal, fixed, existent. The essence (<em>rasa</em>) of this formed, mortal, fixed, existent (Brahman) is <strong>that (sun) which gives heat</strong> — for it is the essence of the existent. Now the <strong>formless</strong> is air and space; it is immortal, moving, yonder. The essence of this formless, immortal, yonder (Brahman) is <strong>the Person who is in that (solar) orb</strong> — for it is the essence of the yonder. — Thus with respect to the deities (<em>adhidaivata</em>).\"","para":"Cosmically: the <strong>formed</strong> Brahman = everything <strong>except air and space</strong> (the solid elements) — mortal, static, \"existent\"; its concentrated <strong>essence (rasa)</strong> is the <strong>sun's heat/light</strong>. The <strong>formless</strong> Brahman = <strong>air and space</strong> — immortal, ever-moving, transcendent; its essence is the <strong>Person within the solar orb</strong> (the conscious light behind the visible sun). So even the cosmic Brahman has a gross essence (sunlight) and a subtle essence (the Person in the sun).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>rasa</em> = the finest essence/efflorescence; the sun is the \"cream\" of the gross cosmos, the Person-in-the-sun the \"cream\" of the subtle. These are still <em>saguṇa</em> — the highest <em>forms</em> of Brahman, not yet the formless Absolute beyond all forms."],["Sureśvara","the gradation (gross→its essence→subtle→its essence) ascends toward the innermost; the Person in the orb is the subtle conscious core of the cosmic."],["Krishnananda","the sun is the visible glory of the formed world; the conscious Person within the sun is the glory of the formless — the meditation climbs from sunlight to the awareness behind it."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","an <em>adhidaiva</em> meditation rising from the sun's brilliance to the indwelling solar Person."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the <em>rasa</em> (essence) schema and the Person-in-the-sun (cf. Īśā 16, ChU 1.6–7); Olivelle: the cosmic register of the two-form doctrine."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 2.3.4–2.3.5","title":"The formed and formless, in the body (adhyātma)","sa":["2.3.4 (the formed): अथाध्यात्मम् । इदमेव मूर्तं यदन्यत्प्राणाच्च यश्चायमन्तरात्मन्नाकाशः । एतन्मर्त्यम् । एतत्स्थितम् । एतत्सत् । तस्यैतस्य मूर्तस्यैतस्य मर्त्यस्यैतस्य स्थितस्यैतस्य सत एष रसो यच्चक्षुः । सतो ह्येष रसः ॥ ४ ॥","2.3.5 (the formless): अथामूर्तं प्राणश्च यश्चायमन्तरात्मन्नाकाशः । एतदमृतम् । एतद्यत् । एतत्त्यत् । तस्यैतस्यामूर्तस्यैतस्यामृतस्यैतस्य यत एतस्य त्यस्यैष रसो योऽयं दक्षिणेऽक्षन्पुरुषः । त्यस्य ह्येष रसः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["athādhyātmam | idam eva mūrtaṃ yad anyat prāṇāc ca yaś cāyam antar ātmann ākāśaḥ | etan martyam | etat sthitam | etat sat | tasyaitasya mūrtasyaitasya martyasyaitasya sthitasyaitasya sata eṣa raso yac cakṣuḥ | sato hy eṣa rasaḥ || 4 ||","athāmūrtaṃ prāṇaś ca yaś cāyam antar ātmann ākāśaḥ | etad amṛtam | etad yat | etat tyat | tasyaitasyāmūrtasyaitasyāmṛtasyaitasya yata etasya tyasyaiṣa raso yo 'yaṃ dakṣiṇe 'kṣan puruṣaḥ | tyasya hy eṣa rasaḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["atha adhyātmam","n. nom. (adv.)","now with respect to the body/self"],["idam eva mūrtaṃ yat anyat prāṇāt ca yaḥ ca ayam antaḥ ātman ākāśaḥ","abl. + loc. + m. nom.","the formed is all (in the body) other than breath and the space within the self"],["etat martyam etat sthitam etat sat","n. nom.","this is mortal, fixed, existent"],["tasya … sataḥ eṣaḥ rasaḥ yat cakṣuḥ","gen. + n. nom.","the essence of this formed-mortal-fixed-existent is the eye"],["atha amūrtaṃ prāṇaḥ ca yaḥ ca ayam antaḥ ātman ākāśaḥ","m. nom.","now the formless is breath and the space within the self"],["etat amṛtam etat yat etat tyat","n. nom.","this is immortal, moving, yonder"],["tasya … tyasya eṣaḥ rasaḥ yaḥ ayaṃ dakṣiṇe akṣan puruṣaḥ","loc. (in the right eye) + m. nom.","the essence of this formless-immortal-yonder is the Person in the right eye"]],"lit":"\"Now with respect to the body: the <strong>formed</strong> is whatever (in the body) is other than breath and the space within the self; it is mortal, fixed, existent — and its essence is <strong>the eye</strong>. Now the <strong>formless</strong> is breath and the space within the self; it is immortal, moving, yonder — and its essence is <strong>the Person who is in the right eye</strong>.\"","para":"In the body: the <strong>formed</strong> = all except breath and inner space; its essence (<em>rasa</em>) = the <strong>eye</strong> (the body's finest, most luminous gross organ). The <strong>formless</strong> = <strong>breath and the inner space (hṛdayākāśa)</strong>; its essence = the <strong>Person in the right eye</strong> — the conscious witness reflected/seated in the eye (the <em>adhyātma</em> counterpart of the Person in the sun). Thus the subtle, immortal Brahman dwells, for the meditator, as the conscious Person seen in the eye.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the eye is the body's \"essence of the gross,\" the Person-in-the-(right-)eye the \"essence of the subtle\" — the indwelling conscious Self glimpsed at the body's most luminous point. Adhidaiva (sun-Person) and adhyātma (eye-Person) are the <em>same</em> Brahman."],["Sureśvara","the bodily ascent mirrors the cosmic: from the gross (eye) to the subtle conscious Person within — the witness."],["Krishnananda","the \"Person in the right eye\" is the conscious self localised for meditation at the eye; the macro (sun-Person) and micro (eye-Person) are one — a bridge between worship of the sun and knowledge of the Self."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","basis of the <em>akṣi-puruṣa</em> meditation (cf. ChU 4.15, 8.7); contemplate the conscious Person at the eye as Brahman."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the eye-Person / sun-Person identification; Olivelle compares the <em>akṣi-puruṣa</em> meditations of Chāndogya."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.3.6","title":"The colours of the Person; neti neti; \"the Real of the real\"","sa":["तस्य हैतस्य पुरुषस्य रूपम् । यथा माहारजनं वासो यथा पाण्ड्वाविकं यथेन्द्रगोपो यथाग्न्यर्चिर्यथा पुण्डरीकं यथा सकृद्विद्युत्तम् । सकृद्विद्युत्तेव ह वा अस्य श्रीर्भवति य एवं वेद । अथात आदेशो नेति नेति । न ह्येतस्मादिति नेत्यन्यत्परमस्ति । अथ नामधेयं सत्यस्य सत्यमिति । प्राणा वै सत्यम् । तेषामेष सत्यम् ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["tasya haitasya puruṣasya rūpam | yathā māhārajanaṃ vāso yathā pāṇḍvāvikaṃ yathendragopo yathāgnyarcir yathā puṇḍarīkaṃ yathā sakṛdvidyuttam | sakṛdvidyutteva ha vā asya śrīr bhavati ya evaṃ veda | athāta ādeśo neti neti | na hy etasmād iti nety anyat param asti | atha nāmadheyaṃ satyasya satyam iti | prāṇā vai satyam | teṣām eṣa satyam || 6 ||"],"words":[["tasya ha etasya puruṣasya rūpam","gen. + n. nom.","now (a description of) the appearance of this Person:"],["yathā māhārajanaṃ vāsaḥ","n. nom. (saffron-dyed garment)","like a saffron-dyed robe"],["yathā pāṇḍu-āvikam","n. nom. (white wool)","like white wool"],["yathā indragopaḥ","m. nom. (the cochineal/red insect)","like the indragopa (scarlet) insect"],["yathā agni-arciḥ","f. nom.","like a flame of fire"],["yathā puṇḍarīkam","n. nom.","like a white lotus"],["yathā sakṛt-vidyuttam","n. (a single flash of lightning)","like a sudden flash of lightning"],["sakṛt-vidyuttā iva ha vai asya śrīḥ bhavati yaḥ evaṃ veda","f. nom. + pres.","his splendour becomes like a sudden lightning-flash, who knows thus"],["atha ataḥ ādeśaḥ neti neti","m. nom. (the teaching)","now, therefore, the instruction: \"not this, not this\" (neti neti)"],["na hi etasmāt iti na iti anyat param asti","abl. + n. nom.","for there is nothing higher / other than this \"not this\""],["atha nāmadheyaṃ satyasya satyam iti","n. nom. + n. gen. + n. nom.","now its name: \"the Real of the real\" (satyasya satyam)"],["prāṇāḥ vai satyam teṣām eṣaḥ satyam","m. nom. pl. + n. nom. + gen.","the vital powers are the real; and of them, He is the Real"]],"lit":"\"Now the appearance of this Person (is described): like a saffron-dyed robe, like white wool, like the scarlet <em>indragopa</em> insect, like a flame of fire, like a white lotus, like a sudden flash of lightning. The splendour of one who knows this becomes like a sudden flash of lightning. — Now, therefore, the teaching (about Brahman itself): '<strong>not this, not this</strong>' (<em>neti neti</em>); for there is nothing higher than this '<strong>not this</strong>.' Now its name: '<strong>the Real of the real</strong>' (<em>satyasya satyam</em>) — the vital powers are the real, and He is <strong>their</strong> Real.\"","para":"First, the <strong>luminous \"appearance\"</strong> of the conscious Person is evoked through a cascade of dazzling similes (saffron robe, white wool, scarlet insect, flame, white lotus, lightning) — but these are only its radiant <em>forms</em>. Then comes the <strong>decisive negation</strong>: when pointing at <strong>Brahman itself</strong>, the only adequate teaching is <strong>neti neti — \"not this, not this\"</strong> — for every image and attribute must be denied; <strong>nothing transcends this \"not-this.\"</strong> Brahman is beyond all description, knowable only by negating everything sayable. Its <strong>name</strong> is <strong>satyasya satyam, \"the Real of the real\":</strong> the prāṇas (and all that is empirically real) are <em>satya</em>, but Brahman is the <strong>Reality behind that reality</strong>. (The chapter thus joins the <em>via negativa</em> (<em>neti neti</em>) to the ontological formula (<em>satyasya satyam</em>).)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the climactic <em>via negativa</em>. The similes describe the <em>upādhi</em>-conditioned forms; but Brahman <em>in itself</em> admits no positive description — hence <strong>neti neti</strong>, the negation of <em>every</em> presented form and attribute, leaving the attributeless witness that cannot itself be negated (for the negator remains). \"Nothing higher than this 'not-this'\" = no teaching surpasses negation for indicating the Absolute. <em>Satyasya satyam</em>: the empirically real (prāṇas) is real only as grounded in Brahman, the independent Real. This is for Śaṅkara the supreme description-by-non-description of <em>nirguṇa</em> Brahman."],["Sureśvara","<em>neti neti</em> negates not the Self but every <em>limitation</em> superimposed on it; what remains after all negation — the negating consciousness — is Brahman, indubitable. The two formulas (<em>neti neti</em> + <em>satyasya satyam</em>) together fix the Absolute as attributeless yet the ground of all reality."],["Krishnananda","\"Whatever you can think or say, Brahman is <em>not that</em>.\" Every concept, image, and name is sloughed off by <em>neti neti</em> — not to reach a blank, but the pure Subject that can never be made an object. <em>Satyasya satyam</em>: the world is real <em>as</em> Brahman, but only relatively; Brahman alone is the Real that needs no other."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the supreme contemplative method — negate every form (\"not this, not this\") until only the formless witness remains; the dazzling similes are a meditative aid before the final negation."],["Deussen/Olivelle","the locus classicus of <em>neti neti</em> (recurring at 3.9.26, 4.2.4, 4.4.22, 4.5.15); Deussen treats it as Indian apophatic theology par excellence. Olivelle notes the pairing of vivid positive imagery with radical negation, and <em>satyasya satyam</em> as the chapter's defining name for Brahman."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This brāhmaṇa gives the Upaniṣad's mature <strong>theology of Brahman</strong> in three moves:","1. <strong>Two forms</strong> (2.3.1). Brahman is <strong>mūrta</strong> (formed/gross/mortal/fixed/existent) and <strong>amūrta</strong> (formless/subtle/immortal/moving/transcendent). One reality, two faces — the full statement of 1.6.3's \"immortal veiled by the real.\"","2. <strong>The graded essences</strong> (2.3.2–5). Both cosmically and bodily, each form has a concentrated <strong>essence (rasa)</strong>: cosmically, the gross→the <strong>sun</strong>, the subtle→the <strong>Person in the solar orb</strong>; bodily, the gross→the <strong>eye</strong>, the subtle→the <strong>Person in the right eye</strong>. The meditation ascends from the gross, through its luminous essence, to the <strong>conscious Person</strong> — the same Brahman in sun and eye (<em>adhidaiva = adhyātma</em>).","3. <strong>The negation and the name</strong> (2.3.6). The Person's radiance is evoked by brilliant similes — then <em>transcended</em>: Brahman in itself is <strong>neti neti</strong>, \"not this, not this,\" beyond every image and attribute, with <strong>nothing higher than this negation</strong>; and its name is <strong>satyasya satyam</strong>, \"the Real of the real.\" The chapter thus unites the <strong>apophatic method</strong> (<em>neti neti</em>) with the <strong>ontological formula</strong> (<em>satyasya satyam</em>) first met at 2.1.20.","Together with 2.1, this brāhmaṇa completes the turn from \"Brahman as some person <em>in</em> things\" to <strong>Brahman as the attributeless, indescribable Real</strong> — reachable only by negating all forms and recognising it as the Reality underlying every reality. It supplies the method (<em>neti neti</em>) that the Maitreyī and Yājñavalkya dialogues (2.4; 3–4) will deploy."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE TWO FORMS OF BRAHMAN (BU 2.3)\n   =================================\n                 MŪRTA (formed)              |          AMŪRTA (formless)\n        mortal · fixed · &quot;sat&quot; (existent)    |   immortal · moving · &quot;tyam&quot; (yonder)\n   ------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------\n   COSMIC   all except air &amp; space           |   air &amp; space\n   (adhi-     rasa (essence) = THE SUN       |     rasa = the PERSON IN THE SUN&#x27;S ORB\n    daiva)    (that which gives heat)        |\n   ------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------\n   BODILY   all except breath &amp; inner space  |   breath &amp; the space within the self\n   (adhy-     rasa = THE EYE                 |     rasa = the PERSON IN THE RIGHT EYE\n    ātma)                                    |\n   ------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------\n   the Person&#x27;s RADIANCE (2.3.6): saffron robe · white wool · scarlet insect ·\n        flame · white lotus · a SUDDEN FLASH OF LIGHTNING\n                              |\n                              v   but Brahman ITSELF:\n        ===&gt;  NETI NETI  (&quot;not this, not this&quot;)  — nothing higher than this negation\n        ===&gt;  name: SATYASYA SATYAM (&quot;the Real of the real&quot;)\n                  (prāṇas = real ;  Brahman = the Real behind the real)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>See both faces.</strong> Practice recognising the <strong>formed</strong> (solid, perishable, \"this\") and the <strong>formless</strong> (subtle, pervasive, \"that\") as two faces of one reality — in the cosmos (earth-things vs. air/space) and in yourself (body vs. breath/inner space).","2. <strong>Climb the essences</strong> (2.3.2–5). Move attention from the gross to its luminous essence to the conscious Person: from sunlight to the awareness behind the sun; from the eye to the seer within the eye. Let worship of light become recognition of the consciousness that lights it.","3. <strong>Use the similes, then drop them.</strong> Let the dazzling images (flame, white lotus, lightning) kindle devotion — then release every one of them in the negation.","4. <strong>Practice <em>neti neti</em>.</strong> This is the chapter's master-method: to whatever arises — body, thought, image, even \"bliss\" or \"void\" — inwardly say <em>not this, not this</em>; not to reach emptiness, but to rest as the witness that remains when all objects are denied and that can never itself be negated.","5. <strong>Rest in \"the Real of the real.\"</strong> After negation, abide as <em>satyasya satyam</em> — the Reality that underlies the breaths, the world, and the very act of negating. The world is real <em>as</em> That; That alone is real of itself.","6. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> Brahman is not found by adding descriptions but by <strong>removing</strong> them. Every \"this is it\" is to be met with \"not this\" — until what cannot be removed (your own awareness) stands revealed as the Real of the real."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 2 · Brāhmaṇa 2.4 — Yājñavalkya and Maitreyī (first version): the Self, the dearest","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 2.4","title":"Yājñavalkya and Maitreyī (first version): the Self, the dearest","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The first of the two <strong>Maitreyī dialogues</strong> (repeated, expanded, at 4.5). The sage <strong>Yājñavalkya</strong>, about to renounce the household life, offers to divide his estate between his two wives. His philosopher-wife <strong>Maitreyī</strong> asks whether wealth can make her immortal; told it cannot, she asks instead for the knowledge that can. Yājñavalkya teaches: all things are dear not for their own sake but <strong>for the sake of the Self</strong>; therefore the Self is to be seen, heard, reflected on, and deeply meditated upon. He gives the <strong>lump-of-salt</strong> simile for the Self as an undivided \"mass of consciousness,\" the disturbing words <strong>\"after death there is no (particular) consciousness,\"</strong> and the luminous resolution: in non-duality there is no second to know, so — <strong>\"by what should one know the Knower?\"</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 2.4.1","title":"Yājñavalkya prepares to renounce","sa":["मैत्रेयीति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः — उद्यास्यन्वा अरेऽहमस्मात्स्थानादस्मि । हन्त तेऽनया कात्यायन्यान्तं करवाणीति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["maitreyīti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ -- udyāsyan vā are 'ham asmāt sthānād asmi | hanta te 'nayā kātyāyanyāntaṃ karavāṇīti || 1 ||"],"words":[["maitreyī iti ha uvāca yājñavalkyaḥ","voc. + pf. of vac","\"Maitreyī!\" said Yājñavalkya"],["udyāsyan vai are aham asmāt sthānāt asmi","fut.part. nom. + abl. + 1 sg. pres.","\"I am about to go forth (renounce) from this state (of householder), my dear"],["hanta te anayā kātyāyanyā antaṃ karavāṇi iti","+ dat. + instr. + acc. + 1 sg. subj. of kṛ","come, let me make a settlement (division of property) for you with this Kātyāyanī\""]],"lit":"\"'Maitreyī!' said Yājñavalkya, 'I am about to go forth (into renunciation) from this householder's state, my dear. Come, let me make a final settlement between you and (my other wife) Kātyāyanī.'\"","para":"Yājñavalkya announces his intention to leave the household life for <strong>renunciation (saṃnyāsa)</strong>, and proposes to <strong>divide his property</strong> between his two wives, <strong>Maitreyī</strong> (the spiritually inclined) and <strong>Kātyāyanī</strong> (described in the tradition as of ordinary, \"womanly\" interests). The scene that opens the dialogue is a renunciant's farewell.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","sets the context of <em>saṃnyāsa</em> as the culmination of the householder's life; the property-division frames the contrast between two responses to wealth — Kātyāyanī's acceptance and Maitreyī's deeper question. (Śaṅkara reads this dialogue as a charter for the supremacy of knowledge-renunciation.)"],["Sureśvara","(this brāhmaṇa is the centre of his <em>vārttika</em>'s argument) — the renouncer's farewell sets up the demonstration that <strong>immortality is by knowledge, not by works or wealth</strong>."],["Krishnananda","the great sage, at the threshold of renunciation, gives his most intimate teaching to the wife capable of receiving it — wisdom passed at the moment of letting go of all possessions."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the dignity accorded Maitreyī as a fit recipient of <em>brahma-vidyā</em> — a woman as the ideal disciple of the highest knowledge."],["Deussen/Olivelle","one of the Upaniṣad's two named woman-interlocutors (with Gārgī, 3.6/3.8); Olivelle notes the dialogue appears twice (2.4 and 4.5), evidence of its importance and independent circulation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 2.4.2–2.4.4","title":"Can wealth confer immortality?","sa":["2.4.2: सा होवाच मैत्रेयी — यन्नु म इयं भगोः सर्वा पृथिवी वित्तेन पूर्णा स्यात्कथं तेनामृता स्यामिति । नेति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः । यथैवोपकरणवतां जीवितं तथैव ते जीवितं स्यात् । अमृतत्वस्य तु नाशास्ति वित्तेनेति ॥ २ ॥","2.4.3: सा होवाच मैत्रेयी — येनाहं नामृता स्यां किमहं तेन कुर्याम् । यदेव भगवान्वेद तदेव मे ब्रूहीति ॥ ३ ॥","2.4.4: स होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः — प्रिया बतारे नः सती प्रियं भाषसे । एह्यास्स्व । व्याख्यास्यामि ते । व्याचक्षाणस्य तु मे निदिध्यासस्वेति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["sā hovāca maitreyī -- yan nu ma iyaṃ bhagoḥ sarvā pṛthivī vittena pūrṇā syāt kathaṃ tenāmṛtā syām iti | neti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ | yathaivopakaraṇavatāṃ jīvitaṃ tathaiva te jīvitaṃ syāt | amṛtatvasya tu nāśāsti vitteneti || 2 ||","sā hovāca maitreyī -- yenāhaṃ nāmṛtā syāṃ kim ahaṃ tena kuryām | yad eva bhagavān veda tad eva me brūhīti || 3 ||","sa hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ -- priyā batāre naḥ satī priyaṃ bhāṣase | ehy āssva | vyākhyāsyāmi te | vyācakṣāṇasya tu me nididhyāsasveti || 4 ||"],"words":[["yat nu me iyaṃ sarvā pṛthivī vittena pūrṇā syāt","rel. + dat. + f. nom. + instr. + opt.","\"if this whole earth, full of wealth, were mine,"],["kathaṃ tena amṛtā syām iti","instr. + f. nom. + opt.","by it would I become immortal?\""],["na iti ha uvāca","","\"No,\" he said"],["yathā eva upakaraṇavatāṃ jīvitaṃ tathā eva te jīvitaṃ syāt","gen. pl. (the well-equipped) + n. nom.","\"your life would be like the life of the wealthy"],["amṛtatvasya tu na āśā asti vittena","gen. + f. nom. + instr.","but of immortality there is no hope through wealth\""],["yena ahaṃ na amṛtā syāṃ kim ahaṃ tena kuryām","instr. + opt. + opt. of kṛ","\"what should I do with that by which I cannot become immortal?"],["yat eva bhagavān veda tat eva me brūhi iti","rel. + voc. + impv.","tell me only that which you (truly) know\""],["priyā bata are naḥ satī priyaṃ bhāṣase","f. nom. + pres.part. + acc.","\"dear indeed you are to us, and dearly you speak"],["ehi āssva vyākhyāsyāmi te","impv. + 1 sg. fut.","come, sit; I shall explain to you"],["vyācakṣāṇasya tu me nididhyāsasva iti","gen. pres.part. + 2 sg. impv. of ni-dhyā","and meditate on what I expound\""]],"lit":"\"Maitreyī said, 'If, sir, this whole earth full of wealth were mine, would I become immortal by it?' 'No,' said Yājñavalkya; 'your life would be like the life of the wealthy — but of immortality there is no hope through wealth.' Maitreyī said, 'What should I do with that by which I cannot become immortal? Tell me, sir, only what you (truly) know.' Yājñavalkya said, 'Dear you have always been to us, and dear is what you say. Come, sit; I shall explain — and as I expound, meditate (deeply) on it.'\"","para":"Maitreyī cuts to the essential question: <strong>can any amount of wealth make one immortal?</strong> Yājñavalkya answers plainly — <strong>no</strong>; riches buy only \"the life of the prosperous,\" never <em>amṛtatva</em> (immortality). Maitreyī then dismisses wealth entirely (<em>what use is what cannot make me immortal?</em>) and asks for the <strong>liberating knowledge</strong> itself. Delighted, Yājñavalkya invites her to sit and <strong>reflect deeply (nididhyāsa)</strong> on the teaching to come.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the decisive separation of <em>karma/wealth</em> from <em>mokṣa</em>: the means to immortality is categorically <em>not</em> anything acquirable (echoing 1.4.15 and 1.5.16). Maitreyī's renunciation of wealth-for-its-own-sake qualifies her as a true seeker. \"<em>Nididhyāsa</em>\" (deep meditation) named here completes the triad with <em>śravaṇa</em> and <em>manana</em> (2.4.5)."],["Sureśvara","this exchange is his proof-text that <strong>immortality is not the fruit of action</strong>; only the knowledge Yājñavalkya is about to give can confer it."],["Krishnananda","Maitreyī's question is the turning of the soul from <em>having</em> to <em>being</em>; once she sees that no possession touches immortality, she is ready for the Self-teaching."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a model dialogue of <em>vairāgya</em> (dispassion): wealth supports life, not immortality; the wise turn from the means of comfort to the means of liberation."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the explicit <em>śravaṇa–manana–nididhyāsana</em> sequence (hear–reflect–meditate) that later Vedānta makes the formal path of knowledge."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.4.5","title":"All is dear for the sake of the Self; the Self is to be realised","sa":["स होवाच — न वा अरे पत्युः कामाय पतिः प्रियो भवत्यात्मनस्तु कामाय पतिः प्रियो भवति । न वा अरे जायायै कामाय जाया प्रिया भवत्यात्मनस्तु कामाय जाया प्रिया भवति । न वा अरे पुत्राणां कामाय पुत्राः प्रिया भवन्त्यात्मनस्तु कामाय पुत्राः प्रिया भवन्ति । न वा अरे वित्तस्य कामाय वित्तं प्रियं भवत्यात्मनस्तु कामाय वित्तं प्रियं भवति । न वा अरे ब्रह्मणः कामाय ब्रह्म प्रियं भवत्यात्मनस्तु कामाय ब्रह्म प्रियं भवति । न वा अरे क्षत्रस्य कामाय क्षत्रं प्रियं भवत्यात्मनस्तु कामाय क्षत्रं प्रियं भवति । न वा अरे लोकानां कामाय लोकाः प्रिया भवन्त्यात्मनस्तु कामाय लोकाः प्रिया भवन्ति । न वा अरे देवानां कामाय देवाः प्रिया भवन्त्यात्मनस्तु कामाय देवाः प्रिया भवन्ति । न वा अरे भूतानां कामाय भूतानि प्रियाणि भवन्त्यात्मनस्तु कामाय भूतानि प्रियाणि भवन्ति । न वा अरे सर्वस्य कामाय सर्वं प्रियं भवत्यात्मनस्तु कामाय सर्वं प्रियं भवति । आत्मा वा अरे द्रष्टव्यः श्रोतव्यो मन्तव्यो निदिध्यासितव्यो मैत्रेयि । आत्मनो वा अरे दर्शनेन श्रवणेन मत्या विज्ञानेनेदं सर्वं विदितम् ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovāca -- na vā are patyuḥ kāmāya patiḥ priyo bhavaty ātmanas tu kāmāya patiḥ priyo bhavati | na vā are jāyāyai kāmāya jāyā priyā bhavaty ātmanas tu kāmāya jāyā priyā bhavati | na vā are putrāṇāṃ kāmāya putrāḥ priyā bhavanty ātmanas tu kāmāya putrāḥ priyā bhavanti | na vā are vittasya kāmāya vittaṃ priyaṃ bhavaty ātmanas tu kāmāya vittaṃ priyaṃ bhavati | na vā are brahmaṇaḥ kāmāya brahma priyaṃ bhavaty ātmanas tu kāmāya brahma priyaṃ bhavati | na vā are kṣatrasya kāmāya kṣatraṃ priyaṃ bhavaty ātmanas tu kāmāya kṣatraṃ priyaṃ bhavati | na vā are lokānāṃ kāmāya lokāḥ priyā bhavanty ātmanas tu kāmāya lokāḥ priyā bhavanti | na vā are devānāṃ kāmāya devāḥ priyā bhavanty ātmanas tu kāmāya devāḥ priyā bhavanti | na vā are bhūtānāṃ kāmāya bhūtāni priyāṇi bhavanty ātmanas tu kāmāya bhūtāni priyāṇi bhavanti | na vā are sarvasya kāmāya sarvaṃ priyaṃ bhavaty ātmanas tu kāmāya sarvaṃ priyaṃ bhavati | ātmā vā are draṣṭavyaḥ śrotavyo mantavyo nididhyāsitavyo maitreyi | ātmano vā are darśanena śravaṇena matyā vijñānenedaṃ sarvaṃ viditam || 5 ||"],"words":[["na vai are patyuḥ kāmāya patiḥ priyaḥ bhavati","gen. + dat. + m. nom. + pres.","not for the love of the husband is the husband dear"],["ātmanaḥ tu kāmāya patiḥ priyaḥ bhavati","gen. + dat.","but for the love of the Self is the husband dear"],["(so for) jāyā (wife), putrāḥ (sons), vittam (wealth), brahma, kṣatram, lokāḥ, devāḥ, bhūtāni, sarvam","—","…(repeated for wife, sons, wealth, priestly & ruling power, worlds, gods, beings, and all)"],["ātmā vai are draṣṭavyaḥ śrotavyaḥ mantavyaḥ nididhyāsitavyaḥ maitreyi","m. nom. + gerundives + voc.","the Self, O Maitreyī, is to be seen, heard, reflected upon, and deeply meditated upon"],["ātmanaḥ vai are darśanena śravaṇena matyā vijñānena idaṃ sarvaṃ viditam","gen. + instr. + n. nom. pp.","by the seeing, hearing, reflection, and realisation of the Self, all this is known"]],"lit":"\"He said: 'Not for the love of the husband is the husband dear, but for the love of the <strong>Self</strong> is the husband dear. Not for the love of the wife is the wife dear, but for the love of the Self is the wife dear. — (so too) sons, wealth, the priestly power, the ruling power, the worlds, the gods, beings, and the All: none is dear for its own sake, but for the love of the Self. <strong>The Self, O Maitreyī, is to be seen, to be heard, to be reflected on, to be deeply meditated upon</strong>; for by the seeing, hearing, reflection, and realisation of the Self, all this is known.'\"","para":"The dialogue's heart. Yājñavalkya unveils the secret of all love: <strong>nothing — spouse, child, wealth, power, the worlds, the gods, all beings — is loved for its own sake; each is loved for the sake of the Self.</strong> Every attachment is, unknowingly, self-love; the Self is the one truly dear (cf. 1.4.8), and all else borrows its dearness from it. Therefore the Self alone is worth pursuing — and the method is given in four words that became the formula of the entire path of knowledge: <strong>draṣṭavyaḥ (to be seen), śrotavyaḥ (heard — <em>śravaṇa</em>), mantavyaḥ (reflected on — <em>manana</em>), nididhyāsitavyaḥ (deeply meditated on — <em>nididhyāsana</em>).</strong> To know the Self is to know everything, for all is known <em>through</em> and <em>as</em> the Self.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the great analysis of love — all <em>priyatā</em> (dearness) traces to the Self, which is <em>ānanda</em> (bliss); objects are dear only as occasions of self-delight. Hence the Self alone deserves to be the object of pursuit. The fourfold <em>draṣṭavya–śrotavya–mantavya–nididhyāsitavya</em> is, for Śaṅkara, the canonical sequence: <strong>śravaṇa</strong> (hearing the teaching from scripture/teacher), <strong>manana</strong> (reasoning out its truth), <strong>nididhyāsana</strong> (sustained meditation) — culminating in <em>darśana</em> (direct realisation). \"By knowing the Self, all is known\" = the Self is the substrate whose knowledge subsumes all."],["Sureśvara","this is the cornerstone of his <em>vārttika</em> — knowledge of the one Self is the knowledge of all; and since the Self is the locus of all love, turning love to the Self is the natural movement of the awakened. He carefully expounds <em>śravaṇa-manana-nididhyāsana</em> as the integral discipline."],["Krishnananda","\"You love your wealth, your family, your body — but really you love your own Self through them.\" A revolutionary psychology: all relationships are the Self seeking itself in another. Hence the Self is to be <em>directly</em> sought, by hearing, reflecting, and meditating — and in knowing it, the riddle of all loves and all things is solved."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical path of <em>jñāna-yoga</em> stated in one mantra: <em>śravaṇa, manana, nididhyāsana.</em> And the ethical insight: redirect the love scattered among objects to its true centre, the Self."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Deussen calls this the ethical heart of the Upaniṣads — egoism transfigured into the metaphysics of the universal Self. Olivelle notes the formula's later codification as the three-stage path of Advaita and the bold claim that knowing one thing (the Self) is knowing all."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.4.6","title":"Whoever knows them apart from the Self loses them","sa":["ब्रह्म तं परादाद्योऽन्यत्रात्मनो ब्रह्म वेद । क्षत्रं तं परादाद्योऽन्यत्रात्मनः क्षत्रं वेद । लोकास्तं परादुर्योऽन्यत्रात्मनो लोकान्वेद । देवास्तं परादुर्योऽन्यत्रात्मनो देवान्वेद । भूतानि तं परादुर्योऽन्यत्रात्मनो भूतानि वेद । सर्वं तं परादाद्योऽन्यत्रात्मनः सर्वं वेद । इदं ब्रह्मेदं क्षत्रमिमे लोका इमे देवा इमानि भूतानीदं सर्वं यदयमात्मा ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["brahma taṃ parādād yo 'nyatrātmano brahma veda | kṣatraṃ taṃ parādād yo 'nyatrātmanaḥ kṣatraṃ veda | lokās taṃ parādur yo 'nyatrātmano lokān veda | devās taṃ parādur yo 'nyatrātmano devān veda | bhūtāni taṃ parādur yo 'nyatrātmano bhūtāni veda | sarvaṃ taṃ parādād yo 'nyatrātmanaḥ sarvaṃ veda | idaṃ brahmedaṃ kṣatram ime lokā ime devā imāni bhūtānīdaṃ sarvaṃ yad ayam ātmā || 6 ||"],"words":[["brahma taṃ parādāt yaḥ anyatra ātmanaḥ brahma veda","n. acc. + 3 sg. aor. of parā-dā + abl.","the priestly power abandons (deserts) him who knows it as apart from the Self"],["(so) kṣatram, lokāḥ, devāḥ, bhūtāni, sarvam","—","(so the ruling power, the worlds, the gods, beings, the All — desert the one who knows them apart from the Self)"],["idaṃ brahma idaṃ kṣatram ime lokāḥ ime devāḥ imāni bhūtāni idaṃ sarvaṃ yat ayam ātmā","nom.","this priestly power, this ruling power, these worlds, these gods, these beings, this All — (all that) is this Self"]],"lit":"\"The priestly power deserts him who knows it as apart from the Self; the ruling power deserts him who knows it apart from the Self; the worlds, the gods, the beings, the All — each deserts him who knows it apart from the Self. (For) this priestly power, this ruling power, these worlds, these gods, these beings, this All — all this is the Self.\"","para":"The converse of 2.4.5: whoever treats any of these — priesthood, kingship, worlds, gods, beings, everything — <strong>as something separate from the Self</strong> will find it <strong>slip away from him</strong> (the externalised object is ultimately ungraspable, lost). Why? Because <strong>all of them <em>are</em> the Self.</strong> To know them as \"other\" is to misknow them and forfeit them; to know them as the Self is to possess them truly.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"deserts him\" — treating anything as external to the Self is <em>avidyā</em>, and such objects, being unreal as independent things, cannot be held; they \"abandon\" the one who clings to them as other. The closing identity (<em>all this is the Self</em>) restates <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em> / <em>sarvaṃ khalv idaṃ brahma</em>."],["Sureśvara","the loss of externalised objects shows the futility of dualistic knowledge; only the unitive knowledge \"all this is the Self\" is stable and true."],["Krishnananda","whatever you set up as outside yourself eventually betrays and forsakes you; only what is known as the Self is never lost. The verse is a profound diagnosis of insecurity — it springs from othering."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical — relate to family, wealth, duty, and the world as the Self, not as alien possessions, and they no longer enslave or escape you."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the symmetry with 2.4.5; Olivelle reads the pair as asserting that all reality is intelligible and securable only as the one Self."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 2.4.7–2.4.9","title":"Drum, conch, lute: grasp the source, grasp all sounds","sa":["2.4.7 (drum): स यथा दुन्दुभेर्हन्यमानस्य न बाह्याञ्छब्दाञ्छक्नुयाद्ग्रहणाय । दुन्दुभेस्तु ग्रहणेन दुन्दुभ्याघातस्य वा शब्दो गृहीतः ॥ ७ ॥","2.4.8 (conch): स यथा शङ्खस्य ध्मायमानस्य न बाह्याञ्छब्दाञ्छक्नुयाद्ग्रहणाय शङ्खस्य तु ग्रहणेन शङ्खध्मस्य वा शब्दो गृहीतः ॥ ८ ॥","2.4.9 (lute): स यथा वीणायै वाद्यमानायै न बाह्याञ्छब्दाञ्छक्नुयाद्ग्रहणाय वीणायै तु ग्रहणेन वीणावादस्य वा शब्दो गृहीतः ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["sa yathā dundubher hanyamānasya na bāhyāñ chabdāñ chaknuyād grahaṇāya | dundubhes tu grahaṇena dundubhyāghātasya vā śabdo gṛhītaḥ || 7 ||","sa yathā śaṅkhasya dhmāyamānasya na bāhyāñ chabdāñ chaknuyād grahaṇāya śaṅkhasya tu grahaṇena śaṅkhadhmasya vā śabdo gṛhītaḥ || 8 ||","sa yathā vīṇāyai vādyamānāyai na bāhyāñ chabdāñ chaknuyād grahaṇāya vīṇāyai tu grahaṇena vīṇāvādasya vā śabdo gṛhītaḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["saḥ yathā dundubheḥ hanyamānasya","gen. + pres.pass.part. gen.","just as, of a drum being beaten,"],["na bāhyān śabdān śaknuyāt grahaṇāya","acc. pl. + opt. of śak + dat.","one could not grasp the external sounds (as separate)"],["dundubheḥ tu grahaṇena","gen. + instr.","but by grasping the drum"],["dundubhi-āghātasya vā śabdaḥ gṛhītaḥ","gen. + m. nom. pp.","or the drummer/beating, the sound is grasped"]],"lit":"\"Just as, when a drum is being beaten, one cannot grasp the external sounds (in themselves), but by grasping the drum, or the beater of the drum, the sound is grasped — (so too) when a conch is blown… when a lute is played, one cannot grasp the external sounds apart, but by grasping the conch (or its blower), the lute (or its player), the sound is caught.\"","para":"Three parallel similes. The countless particular <strong>sounds</strong> of a drum, conch, or lute cannot be caught one by one as separate things — but <strong>grasp the instrument (or the player), and you have grasped all its sounds at once.</strong> So too: the endless particulars of experience cannot be known severally, but <strong>grasp their source — the Self / the \"great Being\" (next mantra) — and all are comprehended together.</strong> (Knowing the cause, one knows all effects.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the similes illustrate \"by knowing the One, all is known\" (2.4.5c): the manifold <em>vṛttis</em> (modes/sounds) are mere modifications of one source; grasp the source (the instrument = the Self) and the modifications are included. They prepare the \"breathed-out Vedas\" of 2.4.10."],["Sureśvara","the instrument-and-its-sounds models cause-and-effect non-difference; the effects have no graspable being apart from the cause."],["Krishnananda","you cannot chase every note, but hold the drum and every note is yours — likewise, do not chase particulars; know the Self and the whole of experience is comprehended in its root."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical epistemics — seek the source, not the endless effects; meditation on the Self subsumes all phenomena."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the trio of musical similes; Olivelle reads them as illustrating the reducibility of all phenomena to a single underlying reality."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.4.10","title":"All scriptures are the breath of the great Being","sa":["स यथार्द्रैधाग्नेरभ्याहितात्पृथग्धूमा विनिश्चरन्त्येव वा अरेऽस्य महतो भूतस्य निःश्वसितमेतद्यदृग्वेदो यजुर्वेदः सामवेदोऽथर्वाङ्गिरस इतिहासः पुराणं विद्या उपनिषदः श्लोकाः सूत्राण्यनुव्याख्यानानि व्याख्यानानि । अस्यैवैतानि निःश्वसितानि ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["sa yathārdraidhāgner abhyāhitāt pṛthag dhūmā viniścaranty eva vā are 'sya mahato bhūtasya niḥśvasitam etad yad ṛgvedo yajurvedaḥ sāmavedo 'tharvāṅgirasa itihāsaḥ purāṇaṃ vidyā upaniṣadaḥ ślokāḥ sūtrāṇy anuvyākhyānāni vyākhyānani | asyaivaitāni niśvasitāni || 10 ||"],"words":[["saḥ yathā ārdra-edha-agneḥ abhyāhitāt","abl. (a fire of damp fuel) + pp. abl.","just as from a fire laid with damp fuel"],["pṛthak dhūmāḥ viniścaranti","adv. + m. nom. pl. + pres.","clouds of smoke issue out separately"],["evaṃ vai are asya mahataḥ bhūtasya niḥśvasitam etat","gen. + n. nom. (the out-breath)","so, my dear, is the out-breathing of this great Being"],["yat ṛgvedaḥ yajurvedaḥ sāmavedaḥ atharvāṅgirasaḥ","nom.","namely the Ṛg-, Yajur-, Sāma-, and Atharvāṅgirasa-veda"],["itihāsaḥ purāṇaṃ vidyāḥ upaniṣadaḥ ślokāḥ sūtrāṇi anuvyākhyānāni vyākhyānāni","nom. pl.","the histories, the ancient lore, the sciences, the upaniṣads, verses, aphorisms, glosses, commentaries"],["asya eva etāni niḥśvasitāni","gen. + n. nom. pl.","all these are his very out-breath"]],"lit":"\"Just as from a fire laid with damp fuel clouds of smoke billow out on all sides, so, my dear, the breathing-out of this great Being is (all this): the Ṛgveda, the Yajurveda, the Sāmaveda, the Atharvāṅgirasa, the histories (itihāsa), the ancient lore (purāṇa), the sciences, the upaniṣads, the verses, the aphorisms (sūtra), the explanations, the commentaries — all these are his very out-breath.\"","para":"A famous image of revelation: as <strong>damp wood breathes out billowing smoke</strong>, so the whole body of <strong>sacred knowledge</strong> — all four Vedas and the entire ancillary literature (histories, Purāṇas, sciences, Upaniṣads, ślokas, sūtras, glosses) — is the <strong>effortless out-breath of the \"great Being\" (the Self/Brahman).</strong> Scripture is not composed but <em>exhaled</em> from the infinite consciousness — underlining that it issues from the very source one is being taught to know.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Vedas being Brahman's \"breath\" asserts their <strong>non-human, effortless origin</strong> (<em>apauruṣeyatva</em>) and their identity with the source — to know the Self is to know the root of all scripture. The \"damp fuel/smoke\" conveys spontaneous, abundant emanation."],["Sureśvara","even the supreme means of knowledge (the Veda) is an <em>effect</em> of the Self; the Self is prior to and the source of all <em>pramāṇa</em>."],["Krishnananda","all knowledge-systems are exhalations of the cosmic Being; the scriptures are the breath of God, not constructs of men — they point back to the breather."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","reverence for scripture as divine breath; yet the goal is the Being whose breath it is."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a key text for the Vedic theory of revelation; Olivelle notes the comprehensive canon-list (one of the earliest) and the \"breath\" image of effortless divine emanation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.4.11","title":"All particulars converge in single centres","sa":["स यथा सर्वासामपां समुद्र एकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषां स्पर्शानां त्वगेकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषां गन्धानां नासिके एकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषां रसानां जिह्वैकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषां रूपाणां चक्षुरेकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषां शब्दानां श्रोत्रमेकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषां संकल्पानां मन एकायनम् । एवं सर्वासां विद्यानां हृदयमेकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषां कर्मणां हस्तावेकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषामानन्दानामुपस्थ एकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषां विसर्गाणां पायुरेकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषामध्वनां पादावेकायनम् । एवं सर्वेषां वेदानां वागेकायनम् ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["sa yathā sarvāsām apāṃ samudra ekāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣāṃ sparśānāṃ tvag ekāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣāṃ gandhānāṃ nāsike ekāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣāṃ rasānāṃ jihvaikāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣāṃ rūpāṇāṃ cakṣur ekāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣaṃ śabdānāṃ śrotram ekāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣāṃ saṃkalpānāṃ mana ekāyanam | evaṃ sarvāsāṃ vidyānāṃ hṛdayam ekāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣāṃ karmaṇāṃ hastāv ekāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣām ānandānām upastha ekāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣāṃ visargāṇāṃ pāyur ekāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣām adhvanāṃ pādāv ekāyanam | evaṃ sarveṣāṃ vedānāṃ vāg ekāyanam || 11 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>Convergence (ekāyana) table:</strong><br>\"Just as the <strong>ocean</strong> is the one meeting-place (<em>ekāyana</em>) of all waters, so the <strong>skin</strong> is the one meeting-place of all touches; the <strong>nostrils</strong> of all smells; the <strong>tongue</strong> of all tastes; the <strong>eye</strong> of all forms; the <strong>ear</strong> of all sounds; the <strong>mind</strong> of all thoughts; the <strong>heart</strong> of all knowledges; the <strong>hands</strong> of all actions; the organ of generation of all pleasures; the anus of all excretions; the <strong>feet</strong> of all journeys; and <strong>speech</strong> of all the Vedas.\"","para":"As all rivers meet in the <strong>one ocean</strong>, every class of particulars has a <strong>single point of convergence (<em>ekāyana</em>)</strong> — touches in skin, smells in nostrils, and so on through the senses, mind, heart, organs of action, and speech. The list reduces the whole field of experience to a small set of \"gathering-points,\" themselves to be traced (next mantra) to the <strong>one ground</strong>, the Self — the ocean of all oceans.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>ekāyana</em> (single confluence) of each domain is a step in the reduction of multiplicity toward unity; all these confluences themselves resolve into the one Self (the \"great Being\"), as 2.4.12 declares."],["Sureśvara","the convergence-points show that the apparently scattered manifold is already gathered into a few centres — and ultimately into one consciousness."],["Krishnananda","a meditation on how the boundless variety of experience funnels into a handful of faculties, and those into the single Self — the ocean behind every ocean."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical <em>pratyāhāra</em>-style contemplation: trace each stream of experience back to its confluence, then to the Self."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the systematic \"ocean of waters\" template; Olivelle reads it as a reductive taxonomy preparing the salt-simile's statement of total unity."]],"notes":["<table><tr><td>all…</td><td>converge in…</td></tr><tr><td>waters</td><td>the <strong>ocean</strong></td></tr><tr><td>touches</td><td>the <strong>skin</strong></td></tr><tr><td>smells</td><td>the <strong>nostrils</strong></td></tr><tr><td>tastes</td><td>the <strong>tongue</strong></td></tr><tr><td>forms</td><td>the <strong>eye</strong></td></tr><tr><td>sounds</td><td>the <strong>ear</strong></td></tr><tr><td>thoughts (<em>saṃkalpa</em>)</td><td>the <strong>mind</strong></td></tr><tr><td>knowledges (<em>vidyā</em>)</td><td>the <strong>heart</strong></td></tr><tr><td>actions</td><td>the <strong>hands</strong></td></tr><tr><td>pleasures</td><td>the generative organ</td></tr><tr><td>excretions</td><td>the anus</td></tr><tr><td>journeys</td><td>the <strong>feet</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Vedas</td><td><strong>speech</strong></td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.4.12","title":"The lump of salt: a homogeneous mass of consciousness","sa":["स यथा सैन्धवखिल्य उदके प्रास्त उदकमेवानुविलीयेत न हास्योद्ग्रहणायेव स्यात् । यतोयतस्त्वाददीत लवणम् । एवं वा अर इदं महद्भूतमनन्तमपारं विज्ञानघन एव । एतेभ्यो भूतेभ्यः समुत्थाय तान्येवानुविनश्यति । न प्रेत्य संज्ञास्तीत्यरे ब्रवीमि । इति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["sa yathā saindhavakhilya udake prāsta udakam evānuvilīyeta na hāsyodgrahaṇāyeva syāt | yato-yatas tv ādadīta lavaṇam | evaṃ vā ara idaṃ mahad bhūtam anantam apāraṃ vijñānaghana eva | etebhyo bhūtebhyaḥ samutthāya tāny evānuvinaśyati | na pretya saṃjñāstīty are bravīmi | iti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ || 12 ||"],"words":[["saḥ yathā saindhava-khilyaḥ udake prāstaḥ","m. nom. (lump of rock-salt) + loc. + pp.","just as a lump of salt thrown into water"],["udakam eva anuvilīyeta","acc. + opt. of anu-vi-lī","would dissolve into the water"],["na ha asya udgrahaṇāya iva syāt","gen. + dat. + opt.","and one could not pick it up again (as a lump)"],["yataḥ-yataḥ tu ādadīta lavaṇam (eva)","adv. + opt. of ā-dā + n. acc.","but from wherever one took (the water), it would be salty"],["evaṃ vai are idaṃ mahat bhūtam anantam apāram","n. nom. + adjs.","even so, my dear, this great Being is infinite, limitless,"],["vijñāna-ghanaḥ eva","m. nom. (a mass of consciousness)","a homogeneous mass of pure consciousness"],["etebhyaḥ bhūtebhyaḥ samutthāya tāni eva anuvinaśyati","abl. pl. + ger. + acc. + pres.","arising out of these elements, it vanishes again along with them"],["na pretya saṃjñā asti iti are bravīmi","adv. + f. nom. + pres.","\"after departing (death) there is no (particular) consciousness,\" so I say"]],"lit":"\"Just as a lump of rock-salt thrown into water dissolves into the water and cannot be picked out again as a lump, yet from wherever one takes the water it is salty — even so, my dear, this great Being is infinite, limitless, a homogeneous mass of consciousness (<em>vijñāna-ghana</em>). Arising out of these elements (the body-mind aggregate), it vanishes again along with them. <strong>After death there is no (particular) consciousness</strong> — so I say.\" Thus spoke Yājñavalkya.","para":"The celebrated <strong>salt simile.</strong> As a salt-lump dissolved in water becomes <strong>undetectable as a lump yet wholly present as taste everywhere</strong>, so the Self is an <strong>infinite, undifferentiated \"mass of consciousness\" (vijñāna-ghana)</strong> — present throughout, but with <strong>no separate parts to grasp</strong>. The <em>individual</em> consciousness \"rises out of\" the elements (the body-mind) and \"dissolves back\" with them; hence <strong>\"after death there is no particular (dualistic, object-directed) awareness\"</strong> — no separate knower-and-known persists. (This is <em>not</em> the denial of the Self, as 2.4.13–14 will clarify, but the denial of <em>individuated, relational</em> consciousness in the state of unity.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>vijñāna-ghana</em> = pure, partless consciousness, like salt-saturated water — all \"taste,\" no separable lumps. \"No consciousness after death\" means: in the Self's unity there is <strong>no <em>viśeṣa-saṃjñā</strong></em> (no particular, subject-object cognition), because such cognition requires duality (an instrument, an object); it does <strong>not</strong> mean the Self is unconscious — the Self <em>is</em> consciousness itself. The \"rising and dissolving\" describes the <em>individual</em> (the reflected consciousness in the body-mind), not the Self."],["Sureśvara","the negation is of <em>relational</em> knowing (knower–known–knowing), not of the Self's intrinsic awareness; the salt-water shows partless all-pervasion. Maitreyī's coming confusion is precisely the misreading he forestalls."],["Krishnananda","the Self is \"consciousness through and through,\" like uniformly salty water — you cannot isolate a knower here and a known there. In liberation the <em>separative</em> consciousness ceases; this is not extinction but the end of duality. Yājñavalkya's blunt phrase deliberately provokes the next question."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the simile par excellence for the all-pervading, partless Self; \"no consciousness after death\" = no fragmented, empirical cognition — the Self remains as undivided awareness."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note <em>vijñāna-ghana</em> and the salt-water image (one of the most quoted in Indian philosophy); Olivelle stresses Yājñavalkya's deliberately startling formulation that triggers the clarifying finale."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.4.13","title":"Maitreyī's bewilderment","sa":["सा होवाच मैत्रेयी — अत्रैव मा भगवानमूमुहन्न प्रेत्य संज्ञास्तीति । स होवाच — न वा अरेऽहं मोहं ब्रवीमि । अलं वा अर इदं विज्ञानाय ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["sā hovāca maitreyī -- atraiva mā bhagavān amūmuhan na pretya saṃjñāstīti | sa hovāca -- na vā are 'haṃ mohaṃ bravīmi | alaṃ vā ara idaṃ vijñānāya || 13 ||"],"words":[["atra eva mā bhagavān amūmuhat","acc. (me) + nom. + 3 sg. aor. caus. of muh","\"here, sir, you have bewildered me"],["na pretya saṃjñā asti iti","","(by saying) 'after death there is no consciousness'\""],["na vai are aham mohaṃ bravīmi","acc. + 1 sg. pres.","\"I say nothing bewildering, my dear"],["alaṃ vai are idaṃ vijñānāya","indecl. (sufficient) + dat.","this (teaching) is quite sufficient for knowing (the truth)\""]],"lit":"\"Maitreyī said, 'Here, sir, you have bewildered me, (in saying) \"after death there is no consciousness.\"' He said, 'I say nothing bewildering, my dear; this (very teaching) is quite sufficient for knowledge.'\"","para":"Maitreyī is <strong>disturbed</strong>: does \"no consciousness after death\" mean annihilation? Yājñavalkya reassures her — <strong>there is nothing confusing here</strong>; rightly understood, this teaching is <strong>enough to attain realisation</strong>. He then explains (2.4.14) the precise sense in which awareness \"ceases\": only <em>dualistic</em> knowing ends.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Maitreyī takes \"no consciousness\" for \"no Self\" (annihilation) — the natural misreading. Yājñavalkya denies any contradiction: the statement, correctly grasped, <em>is</em> the saving knowledge; what ceases is only <em>particular</em> cognition, not the Self."],["Sureśvara","the objection-and-clarification format isolates the exact meaning — negation of <em>relational</em> consciousness, affirmation of the non-dual Self."],["Krishnananda","the disciple's alarm is every seeker's fear that liberation is extinction; the teacher's calm \"nothing bewildering\" promises that the truth is <em>fullness</em>, not void."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","models how a good teacher steadies the student before the decisive clarification."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the dialogue's craft — the shocking thesis, the pupil's protest, the illuminating resolution."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.4.14","title":"In non-duality, \"by what should one know the Knower?\"","sa":["यत्र हि द्वैतमिव भवति तदितर इतरं जिघ्रति तदितर इतरं पश्यति तदितर इतरं शृणोति तदितर इतरमभिवदति तदितर इतरं मनुते तदितर इतरं विजानाति । यत्र वास्य सर्वमात्मैवाभूत्तत्केन कं जिघ्रेत्तत्केन कं पश्येत्तत्केन कं शृणुयात्तत्केन कमभिवदेत्तत्केन कं मन्वीत तत्केन कं विजानीयात् । येनेदं सर्वं विजानाति तं केन विजानीयात् । विज्ञातारमरे केन विजानीयादिति ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["yatra hi dvaitam iva bhavati tad itara itaraṃ jighrati tad itara itaraṃ paśyati tad itara itaraṃ śṛṇoti tad itara itaram abhivadati tad itara itaraṃ manute tad itara itaraṃ vijānāti | yatra vāsya sarvam ātmaivābhūt tat kena kaṃ jighret tat kena kaṃ paśyet tat kena kaṃ śṛṇuyāt tat kena kam abhivadet tat kena kaṃ manvīta tat kena kaṃ vijānīyāt | yenedaṃ sarvaṃ vijānāti taṃ kena vijānīyāt | vijñātāram are kena vijānīyād iti || 14 ||"],"words":[["yatra hi dvaitam iva bhavati","rel. adv. + n. nom. + iva + pres.","for where there is duality, as it were,"],["tad itaraḥ itaraṃ jighrati / paśyati / śṛṇoti / abhivadati / manute / vijānāti","nom. + acc. + pres.","there one smells / sees / hears / speaks to / thinks of / knows another"],["yatra vā asya sarvam ātmā eva abhūt","rel. + gen. + n. nom. + aor.","but where, for him, all has become the very Self,"],["tat kena kaṃ jighret / paśyet / śṛṇuyāt / abhivadet / manvīta / vijānīyāt","instr. + acc. + opt.","then by what, and whom, should one smell / see / hear / speak to / think of / know?"],["yena idaṃ sarvaṃ vijānāti taṃ kena vijānīyāt","instr. + acc. + opt.","that by which one knows all this — by what should one know That?"],["vijñātāram are kena vijānīyāt iti","acc. (the Knower) + instr. + opt.","\"by what should one know the Knower, my dear?\""]],"lit":"\"For where there is duality, as it were, there one smells another, sees another, hears another, speaks to another, thinks of another, knows another. But where, for him, all has become the very Self — then by what and whom should one smell? by what and whom should one see? hear? speak to? think of? know? That by which one knows all this — by what should one know That? <strong>By what, my dear, should one know the Knower?</strong>\"","para":"The luminous resolution of \"no consciousness after death.\" <strong>Ordinary, particular knowing requires duality</strong> — a knower distinct from a known, an instrument, an object (\"one sees <em>another</em>\"). But in realisation, <strong>where everything has become one's very Self, there is no \"second\"</strong> to be seen, heard, or known, and no separate instrument or object — so <em>relational</em> cognition necessarily ceases (that is all \"no consciousness\" meant). Yet the <strong>Self is never an object</strong>: it is the <strong>eternal Knower (vijñātṛ)</strong> by which all else is known, and which can never itself be known <em>as an object</em> — \"<strong>by what should one know the Knower?</strong>\" The Self is self-luminous, the ever-present subject, presupposed by every act of knowing and therefore beyond being known like a thing. (Not annihilation, but the fullness of non-dual, self-evident awareness.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the capstone. Empirical cognition is structurally dual (knower-instrument-known); in the non-dual Self that structure is absent, so <em>vṛtti-jñāna</em> (modal, object-knowledge) does not arise — this, not unconsciousness, is \"no saṃjñā.\" The Self, being the <strong>eternal subject/witness</strong>, is <em>svayaṃ-jyotis</em> (self-luminous): it cannot be made an object of knowledge (\"by what should one know the knower?\"), for any attempt presupposes it. Thus the Self is <strong>ever-realised consciousness itself</strong>, never an absence. A foundational text for the Advaita doctrine of the self-luminous witness."],["Sureśvara","decisively refutes the annihilation-reading: what ceases is duality-bound knowing; the Knower remains as the indubitable, self-revealing consciousness — unknowable as an object precisely because it is the subject of all knowing."],["Krishnananda","\"You cannot know the knower, as you cannot see the seer of sight or hear the hearer of sound.\" In unity there is no other to experience — but this is the <strong>plenum of awareness</strong>, not blankness. The Self shines by itself; to seek to know it as an object is like trying to make the eye see itself. Liberation is <em>being</em> that Knower, not knowing it."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical seal of <em>jñāna</em> — rest as the witnessing Knower; do not try to objectify it. In the non-dual state, knowing-doing cease, and pure being-awareness remains."],["Deussen/Olivelle","one of the most celebrated passages in world philosophy; Deussen compares it to the Western insight that the subject of knowledge can never become its own object. Olivelle notes the elegant ring-composition (duality → unity → the unknowable Knower) closing the Madhu-kāṇḍa's argument."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This dialogue is, with 1.4.10 and 2.3.6, the doctrinal summit of the first half of the Upaniṣad — and the classic scriptural basis for the Advaita <strong>path of knowledge</strong>:","1. <strong>Immortality is not bought</strong> (2.4.1–4). Wealth gives \"the life of the prosperous,\" never <em>amṛtatva</em>; the seeker must turn from acquisition to knowledge. Maitreyī's renunciation of riches-for-their-own-sake makes her the model disciple.","2. <strong>The analysis of love</strong> (2.4.5). <em>Na vā are… ātmanas tu kāmāya</em> — <strong>nothing is dear for its own sake; all is dear for the sake of the Self.</strong> Every attachment is veiled self-love; therefore the Self alone is worth seeking. The means is the famous triad-plus: <strong>draṣṭavya (to be realised), śrotavya (heard — śravaṇa), mantavya (reasoned — manana), nididhyāsitavya (meditated — nididhyāsana)</strong> — the formal path of <em>jñāna-yoga</em>. \"Knowing the Self, all is known.\"","3. <strong>Knowing the source includes all</strong> (2.4.6–11). Whatever is known <em>apart from</em> the Self deserts the knower, for <strong>all this is the Self</strong>; the drum/conch/lute similes and the \"Vedas as the breath of the great Being\" and the <em>ekāyana</em> (convergence) list all teach: grasp the one source and the manifold is comprehended.","4. <strong>The Self as partless consciousness</strong> (2.4.12–14). The <strong>salt-lump</strong> simile: the Self is an infinite, undivided <strong>mass of consciousness (vijñāna-ghana)</strong>, present everywhere, with no parts to grasp. \"After death no (particular) consciousness\" denies only <strong>dualistic, object-directed cognition</strong> — for in unity there is no second to know. But the Self is the <strong>self-luminous Knower (vijñātṛ)</strong> that can never be objectified: <em>\"by what should one know the Knower?\"</em> This is the fullness of non-dual awareness, not annihilation.","The brāhmaṇa thus gives both the <strong>goal</strong> (the Self, the one dear, the partless consciousness) and the <strong>method</strong> (hear–reflect–meditate), and forecloses the nihilistic misreading of liberation. It is repeated, with minor variants, at <strong>4.5</strong> — the seal of the entire Yājñavalkya teaching."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   YĀJÑAVALKYA &amp; MAITREYĪ (BU 2.4)\n   ===============================\n   &quot;Will wealth make me immortal?&quot;  --&gt; NO. (life of the rich, not amṛtatva)   [2.4.2]\n        |  so: teach me what you KNOW\n        v\n   THE ANALYSIS OF LOVE (2.4.5):\n        husband·wife·sons·wealth·brahman·kṣatra·worlds·gods·beings·ALL\n        — none dear for ITS OWN sake — all dear FOR THE SAKE OF THE SELF\n                              |\n        ===&gt; the SELF is to be:  SEEN · HEARD (śravaṇa) · REFLECTED (manana) · MEDITATED (nididhyāsana)\n             &quot;by knowing the Self, ALL is known&quot;\n                              |\n   know anything APART from the Self -&gt; it DESERTS you;   all this IS the Self   [2.4.6]\n        |\n        |  grasp the SOURCE, grasp all:  drum · conch · lute (sounds)   [2.4.7-9]\n        |  Vedas etc. = the OUT-BREATH of the great Being               [2.4.10]\n        |  all particulars converge (ekāyana): waters-&gt;ocean, forms-&gt;eye ...  [2.4.11]\n        v\n   THE SALT LUMP (2.4.12):  dissolved -&gt; no lump to grasp, yet ALL is salty\n        =&gt; Self = infinite, partless VIJÑĀNA-GHANA (mass of consciousness)\n        =&gt; &quot;after death no (particular) consciousness&quot;  --(Maitreyī alarmed)--&gt;  [2.4.13]\n        v\n   RESOLUTION (2.4.14):\n        where DUALITY -&gt; one sees/hears/knows ANOTHER\n        where ALL = the SELF -&gt; by what &amp; whom to see/hear/know?\n        ===&gt;  &quot;VIJÑĀTĀRAM ARE KENA VIJĀNĪYĀT&quot;\n              — by WHAT should one know the KNOWER?   (the self-luminous Subject)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Test what you truly want.</strong> Ask Maitreyī's question of any goal: <em>will this make me immortal/free?</em> If not, hold it lightly — useful for living, useless for liberation (2.4.2).","2. <strong>Trace every love to its root</strong> (2.4.5). When you notice attachment — to a person, a possession, a role — see that you love it <em>for the sake of your own Self</em>. Then turn that same love directly toward the Self, its true object. This redirection is the heart of the practice.","3. <strong>Walk the threefold path.</strong> Make <em>śravaṇa</em> (study/hear the teaching), <em>manana</em> (reason it through until doubts dissolve), and <em>nididhyāsana</em> (sustained meditation) your actual routine — the mantra prescribes all three, not mere belief.","4. <strong>Grasp the source, not the particulars</strong> (2.4.7–11). Don't chase the endless contents of experience; rest attention on the one awareness in which they all arise — \"hold the drum,\" and every sound is yours.","5. <strong>Sit with the salt-water</strong> (2.4.12). Contemplate awareness as partless and all-pervading — no \"lump\" of a separate self anywhere, yet awareness everywhere \"salty\" through experience.","6. <strong>Don't try to know the Knower</strong> (2.4.14). In meditation, notice the impulse to make the Self an object (\"am I experiencing it yet?\"). Drop it. You <em>are</em> the Knower; rest as the seer, never sought as a seen thing. This is the subtlest and most important instruction of the chapter.","7. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> liberation is not the extinction of consciousness but the ending of <em>division</em> within it. \"No second\" is not emptiness but fullness — the one Self, self-luminous, in which there is nothing left over to fear, lose, or seek."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 2 · Brāhmaṇa 2.5 — The Madhu-vidyā: the mutual \"honey\" of all beings","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 2.5","title":"The Madhu-vidyā: the mutual \"honey\" of all beings","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The</em> <strong>Madhu-vidyā</strong> <em>(\"Honey-Doctrine\"), the climax of the Madhu-kāṇḍa that gives the whole book (Adhyāyas 1–2) its name. In fourteen parallel mantras it declares that every cosmic element and every being is</em> <strong>madhu (honey)</strong> <em>— the source of joy and the very sustenance — for all others, in a web of total mutual dependence; and that the</em> <strong>radiant, immortal Person</strong> <em>dwelling in each cosmic locus (adhibhūta) is identical with the radiant, immortal Person in the corresponding bodily faculty (adhyātma) — and both are the one</em> <strong>Self: \"this is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all.\"</strong> <em>It closes with the wheel-hub image (2.5.15) and the ancient</em> <strong>Dadhyañc</strong> <em>legend (2.5.16–19), sealing the teaching: \"this Self is Brahman, the all-experiencing.\"</em>","<em>Editorial note: mantras 2.5.1–2.5.14 share one frame, varying only the cosmic locus and its bodily counterpart. The template (2.5.1) is given in full with a pada-table; the culminating self-mantra (2.5.14) in full; the rest in a consolidated table, with shared commentary.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 2.5.1","title":"Earth is the honey of all beings (the template)","sa":["इयं पृथिवी सर्वेषां भूतानां मधु । अस्यै पृथिव्यै सर्वाणि भूतानि मधु । यश्चायमस्यां पृथिव्यां तेजोमयोऽमृतमयः पुरुषो यश्चायमध्यात्मं शारीरस्तेजोमयोऽमृतमयः पुरुषोऽयमेव स योऽयमात्मा । इदममृतमिदं ब्रह्मेदं सर्वम् ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["iyaṃ pṛthivī sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ madhu | asyai pṛthivyai sarvāṇi bhūtāni madhu | yaś cāyam asyāṃ pṛthivyāṃ tejomayo 'mṛtamayaḥ puruṣo yaś cāyam adhyātmaṃ śārīras tejomayo 'mṛtamayaḥ puruṣo 'yam eva sa yo 'yam ātmā | idam amṛtam idaṃ brahmedaṃ sarvam || 1 ||"],"words":[["iyaṃ pṛthivī sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ madhu","f. nom. + gen. pl. + n. nom.","this earth is the honey (madhu) of all beings"],["asyai pṛthivyai sarvāṇi bhūtāni madhu","dat./gen. + nom. pl.","and all beings are the honey of this earth"],["yaḥ ca ayam asyāṃ pṛthivyāṃ tejomayaḥ amṛtamayaḥ puruṣaḥ","loc. + adjs. + m. nom.","the radiant, immortal Person who is in this earth"],["yaḥ ca ayam adhyātmaṃ śārīraḥ tejomayaḥ amṛtamayaḥ puruṣaḥ","adv. + adj. (bodily)","and the radiant, immortal Person who, with respect to the body, is the bodily one —"],["ayam eva saḥ yaḥ ayam ātmā","m. nom.","this very (one) is that which is the Self"],["idam amṛtam idaṃ brahma idaṃ sarvam","n. nom.","this is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all"]],"lit":"\"This <strong>earth</strong> is the honey of all beings, and all beings are the honey of this earth. The radiant, immortal <strong>Person</strong> who is in this earth, and the radiant, immortal Person who, in reference to the body, is the bodily self — these are one and the same; it is the Self, the immortal, Brahman, the All.\"","para":"Each thing is <strong>honey (madhu)</strong> — sweetness, nourishment, the source of delight — <strong>for all others</strong>, and all others are honey for it: a universe of <strong>reciprocal sustenance</strong> (every being lives by and for every other). And the <strong>luminous immortal Person</strong> in the outer locus (here, the earth) is <strong>identical</strong> with the luminous immortal Person in the matching inner faculty (here, the body) — and both are the <strong>one Self</strong>, which is \"<strong>the immortal, Brahman, the All.</strong>\" The refrain <em>idam amṛtam idaṃ brahmedaṃ sarvam</em> seals each mantra.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>madhu</em> expresses <strong>mutual causality and interdependence</strong> — each being is the \"effect/enjoyment\" of all and the cause/sustenance of all; the web is one organism. The identity of the outer (<em>adhidaiva/adhibhūta</em>) Person and the inner (<em>adhyātma</em>) Person in every pair is the repeated assertion of the <strong>one Self in cosmos and body</strong> — the foundation of <em>sarvātma-bhāva</em> (the vision of the Self as all)."],["Sureśvara","the <em>madhu</em> relation dissolves the sense of independent, isolated existence; nothing stands alone, and the single conscious Person threads through all — the practical face of non-duality."],["Krishnananda","\"Everything is honey to everything else\" — the cosmos is a system of mutual feeding and mutual joy, bound by the one Self present in each node. The Madhu-vidyā is the meditation on universal interdependence resolving into universal identity."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","basis of the great meditation on the Self as <strong>all beings</strong>; cultivates love and reverence for the whole of existence as one's own Self."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the archaic \"honey\" mysticism (cf. the Madhu-vidyā of ChU 3.1–11) and the systematic adhibhūta/adhyātma pairing; Olivelle reads it as the Upaniṣad's fullest statement of cosmic interconnection grounded in a single self."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 2.5.2–2.5.13","title":"The honey of the elements and powers (consolidated)","sa":["Representative — 2.5.4 (air/breath): अयं वायुः सर्वेषां भूतानां मधु । अस्य वायोः सर्वाणि भूतानि मधु । यश्चायमस्मिन्वायौ तेजोमयोऽमृतमयः पुरुषो यश्चायमध्यात्मं प्राणस्तेजोमयोऽमृतमयः पुरुषोऽयमेव स योऽयमात्मा । इदममृतमिदं ब्रह्मेदं सर्वम् ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["ayaṃ vāyuḥ sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ madhu | asya vāyoḥ sarvāṇi bhūtāni madhu | yaś cāyam asmin vāyau tejomayo 'mṛtamayaḥ puruṣo yaś cāyam adhyātmaṃ prāṇas tejomayo 'mṛtamayaḥ puruṣo 'yam eva sa yo 'yam ātmā | idam amṛtam idaṃ brahmedaṃ sarvam || 4 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"The web of \"honey\" is woven through the whole of nature and the body: waters↔seed, fire↔speech, air↔breath, sun↔eye, quarters↔ear, moon↔mind, lightning↔heat, thunder↔voice, space↔heart-space, and even the abstractions <strong>dharma, truth, and the human</strong> — each the honey of all, each housing the one radiant immortal Person who is the Self. The cosmos and the person mirror each other completely, and the same conscious immortal threads through every pair.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the pairs systematically identify each cosmic power with its bodily organ (the standard <em>adhidaiva/adhyātma</em> correlation of 1.5, 2.3), and crown the series with <em>dharma, satya,</em> and <em>mānuṣa</em> — extending the honey-web even to moral and human realities. All resolve into the one Self."],["Sureśvara","the comprehensiveness (matter, life, mind, ethics, humanity) shows that <em>nothing</em> is outside the one Self's honey-circuit."],["Krishnananda","note the inclusion of dharma and truth — even righteousness and truth are \"honey,\" mutually sustaining; the moral order too is the Self's interdependent body."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a complete map for the <em>sarvātma</em> meditation — see the radiant immortal Person in sun and eye, in moon and mind, in truth and the truthful, as one's own Self."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the parallelism with the adhidaiva/adhyātma tables of the Upaniṣad; Olivelle observes the ethical members (dharma, satya) as a distinctive extension of the honey-scheme."]],"notes":["<em>The same frame (\"X is the honey of all beings; all beings are the honey of X; the radiant immortal Person in X = the radiant immortal Person in the bodily faculty Y = the Self; this is the immortal, Brahman, the All\") applied to twelve further pairs:</em>","<table><tr><td>Mantra</td><td>Cosmic locus (adhibhūta)</td><td>Bodily counterpart (adhyātma)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.2</td><td>the <strong>waters</strong></td><td>the <strong>seed</strong> (semen, <em>raitasa</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.3</td><td><strong>fire</strong></td><td><strong>speech</strong> (<em>vāṅmaya</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.4</td><td><strong>air</strong></td><td>the <strong>breath</strong> (<em>prāṇa</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.5</td><td>the <strong>sun</strong></td><td>the <strong>eye</strong> (<em>cākṣuṣa</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.6</td><td>the <strong>quarters</strong></td><td>the <strong>ear / the echo</strong> (<em>śrautra, prātiśrutka</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.7</td><td>the <strong>moon</strong></td><td>the <strong>mind</strong> (<em>mānasa</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.8</td><td><strong>lightning</strong></td><td>the <strong>luminous / heat-power</strong> (<em>taijasa</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.9</td><td><strong>thunder</strong></td><td><strong>sound / voice</strong> (<em>śābda, sauvara</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.10</td><td><strong>space</strong> (ākāśa)</td><td>the <strong>space within the heart</strong> (<em>hṛdy ākāśa</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.11</td><td><strong>dharma</strong> (law)</td><td>the <strong>dharmic</strong> (<em>dhārma</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.12</td><td><strong>truth</strong> (satya)</td><td>the <strong>truthful</strong> (<em>sātya</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>2.5.13</td><td>the <strong>human</strong> (<em>mānuṣa</em>)</td><td>the <strong>human</strong> (<em>mānuṣa</em>)</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.5.14","title":"The Self is the honey of all; the All","sa":["अयमात्मा सर्वेषां भूतानां मधु । अस्यात्मनः सर्वाणि भूतानि मधु । यश्चायमस्मिन्नात्मनि तेजोमयोऽमृतमयः पुरुषो यश्चायमात्मा तेजोमयोऽमृतमयः पुरुषोऽयमेव स योऽयमात्मा । इदममृतमिदं ब्रह्मेदं सर्वम् ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["ayam ātmā sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ madhu | asyātmanaḥ sarvāṇi bhūtāni madhu | yaś cāyam asminn ātmani tejomayo 'mṛtamayaḥ puruṣo yaś cāyam ātmā tejomayo 'mṛtamayaḥ puruṣo 'yam eva sa yo 'yam ātmā | idam amṛtam idaṃ brahmedaṃ sarvam || 14 ||"],"words":[["ayam ātmā sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ madhu","m. nom. + gen. pl.","this Self is the honey of all beings"],["asya ātmanaḥ sarvāṇi bhūtāni madhu","gen. + nom. pl.","and all beings are the honey of this Self"],["yaḥ ca ayam asmin ātmani … puruṣaḥ","loc.","the radiant immortal Person in this self,"],["yaḥ ca ayam ātmā … puruṣaḥ ayam eva saḥ yaḥ ayam ātmā","nom.","and the Person who is the Self — that very one is the Self"],["idam amṛtam idaṃ brahma idaṃ sarvam","n. nom.","this is the immortal, this is Brahman, this is all"]],"lit":"\"This <strong>Self</strong> is the honey of all beings, and all beings are the honey of this Self. The radiant, immortal Person who is in this self, and the radiant immortal Person who is the Self — that very one is the Self, the immortal, Brahman, the All.\"","para":"The series culminates by naming its secret directly: the <strong>Self</strong> itself is the honey of all and the honey-recipient of all — the centre of the whole web. The Person within and the Person that <em>is</em> the Self are one; and that Self is <strong>the immortal, Brahman, the All</strong>. Everything has been leading to this: the honey of honeys is the Ātman.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the fourteenth mantra reveals that all the preceding pairs were the <em>one Self</em> under different names; the Self is the universal \"honey,\" the substrate of all interdependence — <em>idam sarvam yad ayam ātmā</em>."],["Sureśvara","the reduction is complete — the many honeys are the single Self; to know it is to know the sweetness and immortality of all."],["Krishnananda","\"The Self is the honey of all, and all is the honey of the Self\" — the final unity in which lover and beloved, sustainer and sustained, are one. This is the consummation of the Madhu-vidyā."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the meditation's seal — rest in the Self as the immortal, Brahman, the All; the sweetness of existence is the Self enjoying itself in all."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the climactic self-identification; Olivelle reads it as the honey-web folded back into its single ground."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.5.15","title":"The Self, the hub in which all is fixed","sa":["स वा अयमात्मा सर्वेषामधिपतिः सर्वेषां भूतानां राजा । तद्यथा रथनाभौ च रथनेमौ चाराः सर्वे समर्पिताः । एवमेवास्मिन्नात्मनि सर्वाणि भूतानि सर्वे देवाः सर्वे लोकाः सर्वे प्राणाः सर्व एत आत्मानः समर्पिताः ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["sa vā ayam ātmā sarveṣām adhipatiḥ sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ rājā | tad yathā rathanābhau ca rathanemau cārāḥ sarve samarpitāḥ | evam evāsminn ātmani sarvāṇi bhūtāni sarve devāḥ sarve lokāḥ sarve prāṇāḥ sarva eta ātmānaḥ samarpitāḥ || 15 ||"],"words":[["saḥ vai ayam ātmā sarveṣām adhipatiḥ","m. nom. + gen. pl.","this Self is the overlord of all,"],["sarveṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ rājā","gen. pl. + m. nom.","the king of all beings"],["tat yathā ratha-nābhau ca ratha-nemau ca arāḥ sarve samarpitāḥ","loc. (hub) + loc. (felly) + m. nom. pl. (spokes) + pp.","just as in the hub and the felly of a chariot-wheel all the spokes are fixed"],["evam eva asmin ātmani sarvāṇi bhūtāni sarve devāḥ sarve lokāḥ sarve prāṇāḥ sarve ete ātmānaḥ samarpitāḥ","loc. + nom. pl. + pp.","even so in this Self all beings, all gods, all worlds, all breaths, all these selves are fixed"]],"lit":"\"This <strong>Self</strong> is the overlord of all, the king of all beings. As all the spokes are fixed in the <strong>hub and the felly</strong> of a chariot-wheel, even so in this Self are fixed all beings, all gods, all worlds, all breaths, and all these (individual) selves.\"","para":"The Self is declared the <strong>sovereign and unifying centre</strong> of everything. As every spoke of a wheel is set into the <strong>central hub</strong> (and the outer <strong>rim</strong>), so <strong>all beings, gods, worlds, life-breaths, and individual selves are fixed in the one Self</strong> — held together, radiating from and converging upon it. The Self is the axis of the entire cosmos.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the wheel image asserts that the manifold has <strong>no standing apart from the Self</strong>, as spokes have none apart from hub and rim; the Self is the <em>adhipati</em> (governing ground) in which all is \"fixed\" (<em>samarpita</em>) — the One in which the many cohere."],["Sureśvara","\"all selves fixed in the Self\" — even the apparent plurality of <em>jīvas</em> rests on the one Ātman, as spokes on one hub."],["Krishnananda","a classic emblem of the One and the many — the hub is the Self, the spokes are all beings and worlds; nothing stands outside the centre that holds it. Meditate on yourself as that hub."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the unifying vision — see all beings and powers converging in the one Self as their axle; a meditation on cosmic solidarity."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the wheel-hub-spoke simile (cf. 1.5.15, Muṇḍaka 2.2.6); Olivelle reads it as the structural statement of the Self as the centre of all reality."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 2.5.16–2.5.19","title":"The Dadhyañc legend and the seal of the teaching","sa":["2.5.16: इदं वै तन्मधु दध्यङ्ङाथर्वणोऽश्विभ्यामुवाच । तदेतदृषिः पश्यन्नवोचत् — तद्वां नरा सनये दंस उग्रमाविष्कृणोमि तन्यतुर्न वृष्टिम् । दध्यङ् ह यन्मध्वाथर्वणो वामश्वस्य शीर्ष्णा प्र यदीमुवाचेति ॥ १६ ॥","2.5.17: इदं वै तन्मधु ... — आथर्वणायाश्विनौ दधीचेऽश्व्यं शिरः प्रत्यैरयतम् । स वां मधु प्र वोचदृतायन्त्वाष्ट्रं यद्दस्रावपिकक्ष्यं वामिति ॥ १७ ॥","2.5.18: इदं वै तन्मधु ... — पुरश्चक्रे द्विपदः पुरश्चक्रे चतुष्पदः । पुरः स पक्षी भूत्वा पुरः पुरुष आविशदिति । स वा अयं पुरुषः सर्वासु पूर्षु पुरिशयः । नैनेन किंचनानावृतम् । नैनेन किंचनासंवृतम् ॥ १८ ॥","2.5.19: इदं वै तन्मधु ... — रूपंरूपं प्रतिरूपो बभूव तदस्य रूपं प्रतिचक्षणाय । इन्द्रो मायाभिः पुरुरूप ईयते युक्ता ह्यस्य हरयः शता दशेति । अयं वै हरयोऽयं वै दश च सहस्राणि बहूनि चानन्तानि च । तदेतद्ब्रह्मापूर्वमनपरमनन्तरमबाह्यम् । अयमात्मा ब्रह्म सर्वानुभूः । इत्यनुशासनम् ॥ १९ ॥"],"iast":["idaṃ vai tan madhu dadhyaṅṅ ātharvaṇo 'śvibhyām uvāca | tad etad ṛṣiḥ paśyann avocat -- tad vāṃ narā sanaye daṃsa ugram āviṣ kṛṇomi tanyatur na vṛṣṭim | dadhyaṅ ha yan madhv ātharvaṇo vām aśvasya śīrṣṇā pra yad īm uvāceti || 16 ||","idaṃ vai tan madhu … ātharvaṇāyāśvinau dadhīce 'śvyaṃ śiraḥ praty airayatam | sa vāṃ madhu pra vocad ṛtāyan tvāṣṭraṃ yad dasrāv apikakṣyaṃ vām iti || 17 ||","idaṃ vai tan madhu … puraś cakre dvipadaḥ puraś cakre catuṣpadaḥ | puraḥ sa pakṣī bhutvā puraḥ puruṣa āviśad iti | sa vā ayaṃ puruṣaḥ sarvāsu pūrṣu puriśayaḥ | nainena kiṃ canānāvṛtam | nainena kiṃ canāsaṃvṛtam || 18 ||","idaṃ vai tan madhu … rūpaṃrūpaṃ pratirūpo babhūva tad asya rūpaṃ praticakṣaṇāya | indro māyābhiḥ pururūpa īyate yuktā hy asya harayaḥ śatā daśeti | ayaṃ vai harayo 'yaṃ vai daśa ca sahasrāṇi bahūni cānantāni ca | tad etad brahmāpūrvam anaparam anantaram abāhyam | ayam ātmā brahma sarvānubhūḥ | ity anuśāsanam || 19 ||"],"words":[["idaṃ vai tat madhu dadhyaṅ ātharvaṇaḥ aśvibhyām uvāca","n. nom. + m. nom. + dat. du. + pf.","this is that honey which Dadhyañc, son of Atharvan, declared to the Aśvins"],["tat etat ṛṣiḥ paśyan avocat","nom. + pres.part. + impf.","seeing this, the seer (Ṛgvedic) said:"],["aśvasya śīrṣṇā pra … uvāca","n. instr. (horse's head) + pf.","(Dadhyañc) declared it by means of a horse's head"],["aśvyaṃ śiraḥ praty airayatam","n. acc. + 3 du. impf. of pra-īr","(the Aśvins) set on (him) a horse's head"],["puraḥ cakre dvipadaḥ … catuṣpadaḥ","acc. pl. (forts/bodies) + pf. of kṛ","he made the bodies (puras) of the two-footed and four-footed"],["puraḥ saḥ pakṣī bhūtvā puraḥ puruṣaḥ āviśat","+ nom. + ger. + impf. of ā-viś","becoming a bird, as the Person he entered the bodies"],["saḥ vai ayaṃ puruṣaḥ sarvāsu pūrṣu puriśayaḥ","loc. pl. + m. nom. (city-dweller)","this Person dwells in all the \"cities\" (bodies)"],["na enena kiṃ cana anāvṛtam / asaṃvṛtam","n. nom. pp.","by him nothing is uncovered, nothing unenclosed"],["rūpaṃ-rūpaṃ pratirūpaḥ babhūva","acc. + m. nom. + pf.","for every form he became the corresponding form"],["tat asya rūpaṃ praticakṣaṇāya","n. nom. + dat.","that form of his is for (his) being perceived"],["indraḥ māyābhiḥ pururūpaḥ īyate","m. nom. + instr. pl. + adj. + pres.","Indra, by his māyās (powers), appears multiform"],["yuktāḥ hi asya harayaḥ śatā daśa iti","nom. pl. + numeral","for yoked to him are his \"bay steeds,\" ten hundred"],["ayaṃ vai harayaḥ … daśa ca sahasrāṇi bahūni ca anantāni ca","nom. pl.","these (steeds) are the ten and the thousands, many and endless (= the senses/powers)"],["tat etat brahma apūrvam anaparam anantaram abāhyam","n. nom. + adjs.","this Brahman is without a prior, without a posterior, without an inside, without an outside"],["ayam ātmā brahma sarva-anubhūḥ","m. nom. + adj.","this Self is Brahman, the all-experiencing"],["iti anuśāsanam","n. nom.","this is the teaching"]],"lit":"\"This is that <strong>honey</strong> which <strong>Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa</strong> declared to the <strong>Aśvins</strong>. Seeing this, the seer said (4 ṛk-verses): '…the awful deed I make manifest, as thunder (reveals) rain — the honey that Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa declared to you <strong>through a horse's head</strong>.' '…to Dadhyañc the Aśvins fixed on a horse's head; true to his word he told you the honey, the secret (formula) of Tvaṣṭṛ.' '…he made the bodies of the two-footed, he made the bodies of the four-footed; becoming a bird, as the Person he entered the bodies' — this Person dwells in all the \"cities\" (bodies); by him nothing is uncovered, nothing unenclosed. '…for every form he became the corresponding form; that form of his is for being perceived; <strong>Indra by his māyās appears in many forms</strong>, for his \"ten hundred bay-steeds\" are yoked' — these steeds are the ten, and the thousands, many and endless (= the powers/senses). <strong>This Brahman is without before or after, without inside or outside; this Self is Brahman, the all-experiencing.</strong> This is the teaching.\"","para":"The Madhu-vidyā is given an ancient pedigree: it is the <strong>secret honey-knowledge</strong> that the sage <strong>Dadhyañc</strong> transmitted to the <strong>Aśvins through a horse's head</strong> (the famous legend of forbidden knowledge taught at the risk of beheading). Four Ṛgvedic verses are cited as \"seeing\" this truth, the third and fourth describing the <strong>one Person who, entering all bodies (the \"cities,\" <em>pur</em>), pervades everything</strong> (\"nothing uncovered, nothing unenclosed\") and who, <strong>like Indra appearing multiform through <em>māyā</strong></em>, becomes \"every form for the sake of being perceived\" — his countless \"steeds\" being the senses/powers without number. The whole resolves into the <strong>great anuśāsana (teaching)</strong>: Brahman is <strong>apūrvam anaparam anantaram abāhyam</strong> (\"without prior, posterior, inner, or outer\" — beyond all relation and limit), and <strong>ayam ātmā brahma sarvānubhūḥ — \"this Self is Brahman, the all-experiencing.\"</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Dadhyañc legend (and the horse-head, a Brāhmaṇa myth) dignifies the Madhu-vidyā as ancient revealed knowledge. The key verse <em>indro māyābhiḥ pururūpa īyate</em> is a <strong>scriptural proof-text for māyā</strong>: the one Brahman appears as the manifold through its own power, without becoming many — \"for every form, the corresponding form.\" The fourfold negation <strong>apūrvam anaparam anantaram abāhyam</strong> denies all temporal and spatial relation to Brahman (no before/after, no in/out — it is partless, relationless), and <em>ayam ātmā brahma sarvānubhūḥ</em> (<em>sarvānubhū</em> = experiencing/being all) is, with the four mahā-vākyas, a supreme identity-statement. \"This is the teaching\" closes the Madhu-kāṇḍa's instruction."],["Sureśvara","the \"one becoming every form for the sake of perception\" states that plurality is <em>appearance for the perceiver</em>, not a real fragmentation of Brahman; <em>sarvānubhū</em> names the Self as the single experiencer in all."],["Krishnananda","\"The one Reality appears as many through māyā, as Indra assumes many shapes\" — the manifold is the Self's self-display. Brahman has no before, after, inside, or outside — it is the seamless whole; and this very Self is that Brahman, the experiencer in every being. The Dadhyañc story dramatises knowledge so precious it is taught at the cost of one's head."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the seal of the Madhu-vidyā — the Self is Brahman, all-pervading and all-experiencing; meditate on the one Person dwelling in all \"cities\" (bodies), appearing as all forms."],["Deussen/Olivelle","note the borrowed Ṛgvedic verses (RV 1.116–117, 6.47.18 — \"indro māyābhiḥ pururūpa īyate,\" a much-cited line) and the Dadhyañc/aśvaśiras myth (Śatapatha Br.); Olivelle reads <em>sarvānubhū</em> and the fourfold negation as the chapter's culminating definition of the absolute Self."]],"notes":["Four times the refrain returns — <strong>\"this is that honey which Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa declared to the Aśvins\"</strong> — each introducing a <strong>ṛk-verse</strong> (quoted from the Ṛgveda) that the seer \"saw.\" The legend: the Aśvins wished the secret honey-knowledge from the sage <strong>Dadhyañc</strong>, but Indra had forbidden him to teach it on pain of beheading; so the Aśvins replaced his head with a <strong>horse's head</strong>, through which he taught them, and when Indra struck it off they restored his own. The series culminates in the great declaration: <strong>ayam ātmā brahma, \"this Self is Brahman, the all-experiencing.\"</strong>"]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The <strong>Madhu-vidyā</strong> is the capstone of the Madhu-kāṇḍa and one of the Upaniṣad's grand syntheses:","1. <strong>Universal interdependence (madhu).</strong> Every element and being is <strong>honey</strong> — nourishment and joy — for all others, and all for it (2.5.1–13). The cosmos is one organism of mutual sustenance; nothing exists in isolation.","2. <strong>The one Person in cosmos and body.</strong> In each pair, the <strong>radiant immortal Person</strong> in the outer locus <em>is</em> the Person in the inner faculty — and both are the <strong>Self</strong>: <em>idam amṛtam idaṃ brahmedaṃ sarvam</em>. The honey-web is woven of a single consciousness (the <em>sarvātma-bhāva</em>).","3. <strong>The Self as centre (2.5.14–15).</strong> The Self is the honey of all honeys, and the <strong>hub</strong> in which all beings, gods, worlds, breaths, and individual selves are fixed like spokes — the sovereign axis of reality.","4. <strong>The seal (2.5.16–19).</strong> Wrapped in the venerable <strong>Dadhyañc</strong> legend and four Ṛgvedic verses, the doctrine culminates in: the one Person pervades all \"cities\" (bodies); Brahman, like Indra, appears <strong>multiform through māyā</strong> without ceasing to be one; Brahman is <strong>without before, after, inside, or outside</strong>; and — the final word of the teaching — <strong>ayam ātmā brahma sarvānubhūḥ: \"this Self is Brahman, the all-experiencing.\"</strong>","With this, the <strong>Madhu-kāṇḍa (Adhyāyas 1–2) closes its doctrinal exposition</strong>: from the cosmogonies and prāṇa-vidyā (Adhyāya 1) through <em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em>, <em>satyasya satyam</em>, <em>neti neti</em>, the Maitreyī teaching, and now the Madhu-vidyā, the single thesis has been established and adorned from every side — <strong>the Self is Brahman, the one Reality that is all.</strong> Adhyāyas 3–4 (the <em>Muni-/Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa</em>) will now <em>defend</em> this by dialectic, in the great debates of Janaka's court."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE HONEY-DOCTRINE (BU 2.5)\n   ===========================\n   &quot;X is the honey of all beings; all beings are the honey of X&quot;  (mutual sustenance)\n   each pair: radiant immortal PERSON in the cosmos  ==  PERSON in the body  ==  the SELF\n   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     COSMOS (adhibhūta)        |    BODY (adhyātma)\n     earth                     |    the body            water  | seed\n     fire   | speech           |    air    | breath     sun    | eye\n     quarters| ear/echo        |    moon   | mind        lightning | heat\n     thunder| voice            |    space  | heart-space  dharma | the dharmic\n     truth  | the truthful     |    human  | human        SELF   | SELF        (2.5.1-14)\n   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n        refrain: &quot;idam amṛtam idaṃ brahma idaṃ sarvam&quot;\n\n   THE WHEEL (2.5.15):  spokes -&gt; fixed in HUB + RIM\n        so all beings·gods·worlds·breaths·selves -&gt; fixed in the one SELF (the axle)\n\n   THE SEAL (2.5.16-19):  Dadhyañc -&gt; Aśvins (via the HORSE&#x27;S HEAD)\n        one PERSON enters all &quot;cities&quot; (bodies): nothing uncovered, nothing unenclosed\n        &quot;INDRO MĀYĀBHIḤ PURURŪPA ĪYATE&quot; — the One appears MANY through māyā\n        Brahman = apūrvam · anaparam · anantaram · abāhyam (no before/after/in/out)\n        ====&gt;  AYAM ĀTMĀ BRAHMA SARVĀNUBHŪḤ  (&quot;this Self is Brahman, the all-experiencing&quot;)\n                                            — iti anuśāsanam (this is the teaching)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>See the honey-web.</strong> Contemplate how you are sustained by — and sustain — countless beings (food, air, sun, others' labour, the whole web of life). Let the felt sense of total interdependence replace the illusion of separate existence.","2. <strong>Find the one Person in each pair</strong> (2.5.1–14). In meditation, recognise the same radiant awareness in the sun and in your eye, in the moon and in your mind, in the wind and in your breath — and rest in it as your own Self, \"the immortal, Brahman, the All.\"","3. <strong>Be the hub</strong> (2.5.15). Picture all beings, worlds, and powers as spokes converging in the Self as the axle. Identity with the centre brings the calm of the unmoving hub amid the turning wheel.","4. <strong>See the One in the many</strong> (2.5.19). When the world's diversity dazzles, recall <em>indro māyābhiḥ pururūpa īyate</em> — the one Self appearing as all forms \"for the sake of being perceived.\" Diversity is the Self's self-display, not a fall from unity.","5. <strong>Rest in the relationless Brahman.</strong> Contemplate <em>apūrvam anaparam anantaram abāhyam</em> — that which has no before or after, no inside or outside; let the mind release its boundaries into the seamless whole, and abide as <em>sarvānubhū</em>, the one experiencer in all.","6. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the sweetness (<em>madhu</em>) you seek in objects and others is the Self's own sweetness reflected; trace it home, and the whole world becomes honey because it is seen as the one Self."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 2 · Brāhmaṇa 2.6 — The Lineage of Teachers (Vaṃśa)","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 2.6","title":"The Lineage of Teachers (Vaṃśa)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The Madhu-kāṇḍa closes, as each major division of the Upaniṣad does, with a</em> <strong>vaṃśa</strong> — <em>a</em> <strong>list of the succession of teachers (guru-paramparā)</strong> <em>through whom this knowledge was handed down, traced back through generations of named sages to</em> <strong>Brahman the self-existent (svayambhu)</strong>. <em>The vaṃśa is not doctrine but</em> <strong>attestation</strong>: <em>it certifies the teaching as an unbroken oral transmission from the source itself, and ends in reverent salutation.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantras 2.6.1–2.6.3","title":"The succession (gist) and the source","sa":["(Opening, 2.6.1): अथ वंशः — पौतिमाष्यो गौपवनात् । गौपवनः पशुतिमाष्यात् … शाण्डिल्यः कौशिकाच्च गौतमाच्च । गौतमः ॥ १ ॥","(Close, 2.6.3): … सनातनः सनगात् । सनगः परमेष्ठिनः । परमेष्ठी ब्रह्मणः । ब्रह्म स्वयंभु । ब्रह्मणे नमः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["atha vaṃśaḥ -- pautimāṣyo gaupavanāt | gaupavanaḥ paśutimāṣyāt … śāṇḍilyaḥ kauśikāc ca gautamāc ca | gautamaḥ || 1 ||","… sanātanaḥ sanagāt | sanagaḥ parameṣṭhinaḥ | parameṣṭhī brahmaṇaḥ | brahma svayaṃbhu | brahmaṇe namaḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[["atha vaṃśaḥ","m. nom.","now the line (of transmission)"],["pautimāṣyaḥ gaupavanāt","m. nom. + abl.","Pautimāṣya (received it) from Gaupavana"],["(… a long chain, each student named with his teacher in the ablative …)","abl.","…(and so back through dozens of teachers)…"],["dadhyaṅ ātharvaṇaḥ atharvaṇaḥ daivāt","abl.","Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa from Atharvan Daiva"],["atharvā daivaḥ mṛtyoḥ prādhvaṃsanāt","abl.","Atharvan Daiva from Mṛtyu Prādhvaṃsana (Death)"],["… ekarṣiḥ … parameṣṭhī brahmaṇaḥ","abl.","…up through Ekarṣi … Parameṣṭhin, who (received it) from Brahman"],["brahma svayaṃbhu","n. nom. + adj.","Brahman is the self-existent (uncaused)"],["brahmaṇe namaḥ","dat. + indecl.","salutation to Brahman"]],"lit":"\"Now the <strong>line of transmission</strong>: Pautimāṣya (received this knowledge) from Gaupavana; Gaupavana from Paśutimāṣya; … (through a long succession of named teachers, including the seers Śāṇḍilya, Gautama, Bhāradvāja, Pārāśarya, Jātūkarṇya, Yāska, the Aśvins, <strong>Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa</strong>, <strong>Atharvan</strong>, <strong>Mṛtyu</strong> (Death), Ekarṣi, … Sanātana, Sanaga) … Parameṣṭhin from <strong>Brahman</strong>. <strong>Brahman is the self-existent.</strong> <strong>Salutation to Brahman.</strong>\"","para":"A formal <strong>genealogy of the teaching</strong>, each teacher named with the one from whom he received it (in the ablative), traced backward through dozens of generations of human sages, then through semi-divine figures (the Aśvins, Dadhyañc, Atharvan, even <strong>Mṛtyu/Death</strong>), and finally to <strong>Parameṣṭhin</strong> and to <strong>Brahman the self-existent (svayambhū)</strong> — the ultimate, uncaused source. The chapter (and the Madhu-kāṇḍa) ends with the reverent <strong>\"brahmaṇe namaḥ\" — \"salutation to Brahman.\"</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>vaṃśa</em> establishes the teaching's <strong>authenticity and authority</strong> as <em>paramparā-āgata</em> (received through an unbroken succession) reaching back to <strong>Brahman itself</strong> — knowledge is valid because faithfully transmitted, not invented. The descent from Brahman through Parameṣṭhin (Hiraṇyagarbha) to the human sages mirrors the cosmic descent of revelation."],["Sureśvara","the lineage guarantees that <em>brahma-vidyā</em> is no private speculation but the handed-down word, originating in the self-existent Brahman."],["Krishnananda","the <em>vaṃśa</em> honours the chain of gurus through whom truth survives; the inclusion of cosmic figures (Death, the Aśvins, Brahman) shows the teaching's origin beyond the merely human — a sacred trust culminating in reverence to its Source."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the recitation of the lineage cultivates <strong>gratitude to the guru-paramparā</strong> and faith in the transmission; ending in <em>brahmaṇe namaḥ</em>, it returns all knowledge to its origin."],["Deussen/Olivelle","the vaṃśas (here, and at 4.6 and 6.5) are invaluable for the history of the tradition; Olivelle notes their function in legitimising the text and their tracing of descent ultimately to Brahman the self-existent."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details & practical note","title":"Brāhmaṇa 2.6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The <em>vaṃśa</em> is the Madhu-kāṇḍa's <strong>colophon-in-content</strong>: a recital of the <strong>teacher-succession</strong> that certifies the foregoing knowledge as a living transmission from <strong>Brahman svayambhū</strong> down to the present. Its lessons are not metaphysical but dispositional:","- <strong>Knowledge is received, not invented.</strong> <em>Brahma-vidyā</em> comes through a chain of qualified teachers; the seeker stands within a tradition, indebted to those who preserved it.","- <strong>The line reaches the Source.</strong> Tracing the descent to Brahman the self-existent affirms that the teaching's authority is ultimately <em>the Reality itself</em>, not human opinion.","- <strong>Practical step:</strong> honour your own <em>paramparā</em> — the teachers, texts, and transmitters through whom this wisdom reached you — and, like the chapter, return all of it in salutation to its Source: <strong>brahmaṇe namaḥ.</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Colophon","title":"End of Adhyāya 2 (and of the Madhu-kāṇḍa)","sa":["इति बृहदारण्यकोपनिषदि द्वितीयोऽध्यायः ॥ इति मधुकाण्डम् ॥"],"iast":["iti bṛhadāraṇyakopaniṣadi dvitīyo 'dhyāyaḥ || iti madhukāṇḍam ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Thus the Second Adhyāya of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad; thus (ends) the Madhu-kāṇḍa.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"What Adhyāya 2 (and the Madhu-kāṇḍa) has established","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>2.1 — Gārgya &amp; Ajātaśatru:</strong> Brahman is not any \"person <em>in</em>\" sun, moon, or body (mere conditioned aspects) but the <strong>inner conscious Self</strong>, revealed through sleep, dream, and deep sleep, and named <strong>satyasya satyam</strong>, \"the Real of the real.\"","- <strong>2.2 — The Person in the body:</strong> the breath as the tethered \"infant,\" the eye as cosmos-and-pantheon, the head as the sacred cup of the seven seer-breaths — the <strong>body sacralised</strong>.","- <strong>2.3 — The two forms of Brahman:</strong> formed/formless, mortal/immortal, with their essences (sun &amp; sun-Person; eye &amp; eye-Person), culminating in the supreme method <strong>neti neti</strong> (\"not this, not this\") and the name <strong>satyasya satyam</strong>.","- <strong>2.4 — Maitreyī (I):</strong> all is dear <strong>for the sake of the Self</strong>; the Self is to be <strong>heard, reflected on, meditated upon</strong> (<em>śravaṇa–manana–nididhyāsana</em>); the <strong>salt-lump</strong> (Self as partless <em>vijñāna-ghana</em>); and \"<strong>by what should one know the Knower?</strong>\" — the self-luminous Subject.","- <strong>2.5 — Madhu-vidyā:</strong> all beings are mutual <strong>honey</strong>; the one radiant immortal Person dwells in cosmos and body alike; the Self is the <strong>hub</strong> of all; and <strong>ayam ātmā brahma sarvānubhūḥ</strong> — \"this Self is Brahman, the all-experiencing.\"","- <strong>2.6 — Vaṃśa:</strong> the <strong>lineage</strong> certifying the teaching back to <strong>Brahman the self-existent</strong>.","Together, <strong>Adhyāyas 1–2 (the Madhu-kāṇḍa)</strong> have <em>stated and adorned</em> the central truth from every angle — cosmogonic, meditative (prāṇa-vidyā), dialectical (<em>ahaṃ brahmāsmi</em>, <em>satyasya satyam</em>, <em>neti neti</em>), and synthetic (the honey of all): <strong>the Self is Brahman, the one Reality that is all.</strong>","<em>Continued in Adhyāya 3 (the Yājñavalkya- / Muni-kāṇḍa): the great debate in the court of King Janaka, where Yājñavalkya defeats nine challengers in succession — Aśvala on the ritual (3.1); Ārtabhāga on death and the grasping powers (3.2); Bhujyu on the \"ends of the world\" (3.3); Uṣasta and Kahola on the Self within all (3.4–3.5); Gārgī's two interrogations on \"the warp and woof\" of reality (3.6, 3.8); Uddālaka Āruṇi on the inner controller (antaryāmin, 3.7); and the climactic teaching of Brahman as \"neti neti,\" the unseen Seer, the ungrasped Grasper (3.9).</em>"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya 3","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Adhyāya 3 opens the</em> <strong>Yājñavalkya- (or Muni-) kāṇḍa</strong> <em>(Adhyāyas 3–4), the dialectical heart of the Upaniṣad. At a great sacrifice of</em> <strong>King Janaka of Videha</strong>, <em>the sage</em> <strong>Yājñavalkya</strong> <em>claims the prize of a thousand cows as the greatest knower of Brahman — and must then defend the claim against</em> <strong>nine challengers</strong> <em>in succession: Aśvala the hotṛ-priest (3.1); Ārtabhāga on death and action (3.2); Bhujyu on the world's ends (3.3); Uṣasta (3.4) and Kahola (3.5) on the inner Self; Gārgī's first interrogation (3.6); Uddālaka Āruṇi on the</em> <strong>inner controller</strong> <em>(antaryāmin, 3.7); Gārgī's second, on the</em> <strong>Imperishable</strong> <em>(akṣara, 3.8); and Vidagdha Śākalya on the gods and the final Self (3.9). Where the Madhu-kāṇḍa</em> <strong>stated</strong> <em>the truth, this kāṇḍa</em> <strong>proves</strong> <em>it by relentless question-and-answer.</em>"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3 · Brāhmaṇa 3.1 — Aśvala: ritual, and release from death","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 3.1","title":"Aśvala: ritual, and release from death","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The contest is set. Janaka pens a thousand cows, gold tied to their horns, for whoever is</em> <strong>brahmiṣṭha</strong> <em>(the greatest knower of Brahman). Yājñavalkya boldly has his pupil drive the cattle off; the assembled brāhmaṇas, indignant, put up Janaka's</em> <strong>hotṛ-priest Aśvala</strong> <em>to test him. Aśvala asks how the sacrificer escapes the all-pervading grip of</em> <strong>death, day-and-night, the fortnights, and the unsupported sky</strong> <em>— answered by the four ritual priests/faculties — then how many hymns and oblations each priest uses and what worlds they win.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.1","title":"Janaka's contest","sa":["जनको ह वैदेहो बहुदक्षिणेन यज्ञेनेजे । तत्र ह कुरुपञ्चालानां ब्राह्मणा अभिसमेता बभूवुः । तस्य ह जनकस्य वैदेहस्य विजिज्ञासा बभूव — कः स्विदेषां ब्राह्मणानामनूचानतम इति । स ह गवां सहस्रमवरुरोध । दशदश पादा एकैकस्याः शृङ्गयोराबद्धा बभूवुः ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["janako ha vaideho bahudakṣiṇena yajñeneje | tatra ha kurupañcālānāṃ brāhmaṇā abhisametā babhūvuḥ | tasya ha janakasya vaidehasya vijijñāsā babhūva -- kaḥ svid eṣāṃ brāhmaṇānām anūcānatama iti | sa ha gavāṃ sahasram avarurodha | daśadaśa pādā ekaikasyāḥ śṛṅgayor ābaddhā babhūvuḥ || 1 ||"],"words":[["janakaḥ ha vaidehaḥ bahu-dakṣiṇena yajñena īje","m. nom. + instr. + 3 sg. pf. of yaj","Janaka of Videha performed a sacrifice with abundant gifts"],["tatra ha kuru-pañcālānāṃ brāhmaṇāḥ abhisametāḥ babhūvuḥ","gen. pl. + nom. pl. pp. + pf.","the brāhmaṇas of the Kurus and Pañcālas had gathered there"],["tasya ha janakasya vaidehasya vijijñāsā babhūva","gen. + f. nom. (desire to know) + pf.","Janaka of Videha had a desire to know:"],["kaḥ svit eṣāṃ brāhmaṇānām anūcānatamaḥ iti","nom. + gen. pl. + superl. (most learned)","\"which of these brāhmaṇas is the most learned?\""],["saḥ ha gavāṃ sahasram avarurodha","gen. pl. + n. acc. + 3 sg. pf. of ava-rudh","he penned up a thousand cows"],["daśa-daśa pādāḥ ekaikasyāḥ śṛṅgayoḥ ābaddhāḥ babhūvuḥ","m. nom. pl. + gen. + loc. du. + pp.","ten pādas (of gold) were tied to the horns of each one"]],"lit":"\"Janaka of Videha performed a sacrifice with lavish gifts. The brāhmaṇas of the Kurus and Pañcālas had gathered there. Janaka of Videha had a wish to know: 'Which of these brāhmaṇas is the most learned?' He penned up a thousand cows; to the horns of each were tied ten <em>pādas</em> (pieces) of gold.\"","para":"The scene: a grand sacrifice of the philosopher-king <strong>Janaka</strong>, attended by the leading brāhmaṇas of the <strong>Kuru-Pañcāla</strong> region (the cultural heartland). Wishing to identify the <strong>most learned</strong> among them, Janaka offers a magnificent prize — <strong>a thousand cows, each bearing gold on its horns</strong>.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Janaka, himself a knower, stages the contest to draw out and honour the supreme teacher; the lavish prize signals the supreme worth of <em>brahma-vidyā</em>."],["Sureśvara","the public test frames knowledge as something to be <em>demonstrated by reasoning</em>, not merely asserted — the keynote of the whole kāṇḍa."],["Krishnananda","the assembly of the greatest scholars sets the stage for a tournament of wisdom; Janaka's court is the classic arena of Upaniṣadic debate."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note Janaka the rājarṣi (royal sage), patron of knowledge."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle situates this in the Kuru-Pañcāla intellectual milieu and the genre of royal debate-sacrifices (<em>brahmodya</em>)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1.2","title":"Yājñavalkya claims the prize; Aśvala rises to question","sa":["तान्होवाच — ब्राह्मणा भगवन्तो यो वो ब्रह्मिष्ठः स एता गा उदजतामिति । ते ह ब्राह्मणा न दधृषुः । अथ ह याज्ञवल्क्यः स्वमेव ब्रह्मचारिणमुवाच — एताः सौम्योदज सामश्रवा३ इति । ता होदाचकार । ते ह ब्राह्मणाश्चुक्रुधुः — कथं नु नो ब्रह्मिष्ठो ब्रुवीतेति । अथ ह जनकस्य वैदेहस्य होताश्वलो बभूव । स हैनं पप्रच्छ — त्वं नु खलु नो याज्ञवल्क्य ब्रह्मिष्ठोऽसी३ इति । स होवाच — नमो वयं ब्रह्मिष्ठाय कुर्मो गोकामा एव वयं स्म इति । तं ह तत एव प्रष्टुं दध्रे होताश्वलः ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["tān hovāca -- brāhmaṇā bhagavanto yo vo brahmiṣṭhaḥ sa etā gā udajatām iti | te ha brāhmaṇā na dadhṛṣuḥ | atha ha yājñavalkyaḥ svam eva brahmacāriṇam uvāca -- etāḥ saumyodaja sāmaśravā3 iti | tā hodācakāra | te ha brāhmaṇāś cukrudhuḥ -- kathaṃ nu no brahmiṣṭho bruvīteti | atha ha janakasya vaidehasya hotāśvalo babhūva | sa hainaṃ papraccha -- tvaṃ nu khalu no yājñavalkya brahmiṣṭho 'sī3 iti | sa hovāca -- namo vayaṃ brahmiṣṭhāya kurmo gokāmā eva vayaṃ sma iti | taṃ ha tata eva praṣṭuṃ dadhre hotāśvalaḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["tān ha uvāca","acc. pl. + pf.","(Janaka) said to them"],["brāhmaṇāḥ bhagavantaḥ yaḥ vaḥ brahmiṣṭhaḥ saḥ etāḥ gāḥ udajatām iti","voc. + gen. + superl. + acc. pl. + 3 sg. impv. of ud-aj","\"revered brāhmaṇas, whoever among you is the greatest knower of Brahman, let him drive away these cows\""],["te ha brāhmaṇāḥ na dadhṛṣuḥ","3 pl. pf. of dhṛṣ","the brāhmaṇas did not dare"],["atha ha yājñavalkyaḥ svam eva brahmacāriṇam uvāca","acc. (his own pupil)","then Yājñavalkya said to his own pupil:"],["etāḥ saumya udaja sāmaśravaḥ iti","voc. (dear one) + impv. + voc. (Sāmaśravas)","\"drive these away, my dear Sāmaśravas\""],["tāḥ ha udācakāra","acc. + pf. of ud-aj","he drove them off"],["te ha brāhmaṇāḥ cukrudhuḥ","3 pl. pf. of krudh","the brāhmaṇas grew angry"],["kathaṃ nu naḥ brahmiṣṭhaḥ bruvīta iti","superl. + 3 sg. opt. of brū","\"how can he call himself the greatest knower among us?\""],["atha ha janakasya vaidehasya hotā aśvalaḥ babhūva","m. nom. (the hotṛ-priest)","now Aśvala was Janaka's hotṛ-priest"],["saḥ ha enaṃ papraccha","acc. + 3 sg. pf. of prach","he asked him"],["tvaṃ nu khalu naḥ yājñavalkya brahmiṣṭhaḥ asi iti","superl. + 2 sg. pres.","\"are you really the greatest knower among us, Yājñavalkya?\""],["saḥ ha uvāca namaḥ vayaṃ brahmiṣṭhāya kurmaḥ gokāmāḥ eva vayaṃ smaḥ iti","dat. + 1 pl. pres. + nom. pl. (desiring cows)","he said: \"we bow to the greatest knower; we just want the cows\""],["taṃ ha tataḥ eva praṣṭuṃ dadhre hotā aśvalaḥ","acc. + inf. of prach + 3 sg. pf. of dhā","thereupon the hotṛ Aśvala resolved to question him"]],"lit":"\"Janaka said to them, 'Revered brāhmaṇas, whichever of you is the greatest knower of Brahman, let him drive away these cows.' The brāhmaṇas did not dare. Then Yājñavalkya said to his own pupil, 'Drive these off, dear Sāmaśravas.' He drove them away. The brāhmaṇas were angry: 'How can he call himself the greatest knower among us?' Now Aśvala was Janaka's hotṛ-priest; he asked him, 'Are you really the greatest knower among us, Yājñavalkya?' He replied, 'We bow to the greatest knower of Brahman — but we simply want the cows!' Thereupon the hotṛ Aśvala resolved to question him.\"","para":"None of the assembled brāhmaṇas dares claim the prize — but <strong>Yājñavalkya</strong> confidently sends his pupil to <strong>drive the cattle home</strong>, provoking the others' anger. His reply to Aśvala's challenge is a witty deflection — <em>\"we bow to whoever is truly greatest; we just want the cows\"</em> — neither boasting nor backing down. The contest of wits now begins.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Yājñavalkya's confidence is not vanity but the assurance of realised knowledge, ready to be tested; his light reply (<em>\"we just want the cows\"</em>) avoids self-praise while accepting the challenge."],["Sureśvara","the willingness to be examined by all comers is the mark of one whose knowledge can withstand any objection."],["Krishnananda","a vivid human moment — the bold sage, the indignant scholars, the diplomatic-yet-fearless retort; the spiritual hero who will now prove himself."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the humility-within-confidence: he claims the reward by action, not by boastful words."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the dramatic characterisation typical of BU's dialogues and the convention that a claim of supremacy must be defended publicly."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 3.1.3–3.1.6","title":"Escaping death, day-night, the fortnights, the unsupported sky","sa":["3.1.3 (death): याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — यदिदं सर्वं मृत्युनाप्तं सर्वं मृत्युनाभिपन्नं केन यजमानो मृत्योराप्तिमतिमुच्यत इति । होत्रर्त्विजाग्निना वाचा । वाग्वै यज्ञस्य होता । तद्येयं वाक्सोऽयमग्निः स होता सा मुक्तिः सातिमुक्तिः ॥ ३ ॥","3.1.4 (day & night): याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — यदिदं सर्वमहोरात्राभ्यामाप्तं सर्वमहोरात्राभ्यामभिपन्नं केन यजमानोऽहोरात्रयोराप्तिमतिमुच्यत इति । अध्वर्युणर्त्विजा चक्षुषादित्येन । चक्षुर्वै यज्ञस्याध्वर्युः । तद्यदिदं चक्षुः सोऽसावादित्यः । सोऽध्वर्युः सा मुक्तिः सातिमुक्तिः ॥ ४ ॥","3.1.5 (the fortnights): याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — यदिदं सर्वं पूर्वपक्षापरपक्षाभ्यामाप्तं सर्वं पूर्वपक्षापरपक्षाभ्यामभिपन्नं केन यजमानः पूर्वपक्षापरपक्षयोराप्तिमतिमुच्यत इति । उद्गात्रर्त्विजा वायुना प्राणेन । प्राणो वै यज्ञस्योद्गाता । तद्योऽयं प्राणः स वायुः स उद्गाता । स मुक्तिः सातिमुक्तिः ॥ ५ ॥","3.1.6 (the unsupported sky): याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — यदिदमन्तरिक्षमनारम्बणमिव केनाक्रमेण यजमानः स्वर्गं लोकमाक्रमत इति । ब्रह्मणर्त्विजा मनसा चन्द्रेण । मनो वै यज्ञस्य ब्रह्मा । तद्यदिदं मनः सोऽसौ चन्द्रः स ब्रह्मा स मुक्तिः सातिमुक्तिरित्यतिमोक्षाः । अथ सम्पदः ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["yājñavalkyeti hovāca -- yad idaṃ sarvaṃ mṛtyunāptaṃ sarvaṃ mṛtyunābhipannaṃ kena yajamāno mṛtyor āptim atimucyata iti | hotrartvijāgninā vācā | vāg vai yajñasya hotā | tad yeyaṃ vāk so 'yam agniḥ sa hotā sā muktiḥ sātimuktiḥ || 3 ||","... adhvaryuṇartvijā cakṣuṣādityena | cakṣur vai yajñasyādhvaryuḥ | tad yad idaṃ cakṣuḥ so 'sāv ādityaḥ | so 'dhvaryuḥ sā muktiḥ sātimuktiḥ || 4 ||","... udgātrartvijā vāyunā prāṇena | prāṇo vai yajñasyodgātā | tad yo 'yaṃ prāṇaḥ sa vāyuḥ sa udgātā | sa muktiḥ sātimuktiḥ || 5 ||","... yad idam antarikṣam anārambaṇam iva kenākrameṇa yajamānaḥ svargaṃ lokam ākramata iti | brahmaṇartvijā manasā candreṇa | mano vai yajñasya brahmā | tad yad idaṃ manaḥ so 'sau candraḥ sa brahmā sa muktiḥ sātimuktir ity atimokṣāḥ | atha saṃpadaḥ || 6 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>Consolidated table (the four \"releases\"):</strong><br>\"'Yājñavalkya,' said (Aśvala), 'since all this is overtaken by death, all seized by death, by what means does the sacrificer get free of the reach of death?' (Yājñavalkya:) 'By the <strong>hotṛ-priest</strong>, by <strong>fire</strong>, by <strong>speech</strong>. The hotṛ of the sacrifice is speech; that speech is this fire, and it is the hotṛ; that is the release, that is the complete release.'\" <em>(So day-and-night by the adhvaryu/eye/sun; the fortnights by the udgātṛ/breath/wind; the ascent of the supportless sky by the brahman-priest/mind/moon. — These are the \"releases\"; now the \"correspondences.\")</em>","para":"Time and death \"seize\" all things, and the sky offers no foothold for the climb to heaven; Yājñavalkya shows that the <strong>sacrifice itself is the means of release</strong>, because each of its four officiating priests <em>is</em> a faculty <em>is</em> a cosmic deity. <strong>Speech/fire (hotṛ)</strong> carries the sacrificer past <strong>death</strong>; <strong>eye/sun (adhvaryu)</strong> past <strong>day-and-night</strong>; <strong>breath/wind (udgātṛ)</strong> past <strong>the fortnights</strong>; <strong>mind/moon (brahman-priest)</strong> accomplishes the <strong>ascent to heaven</strong>. Identifying the rite, the body, and the cosmos as one, the sacrificer transcends the powers that bind. These are the <em>atimokṣa</em> (releases); next come the <em>sampad</em> (numerical correspondences).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the released-by-the-sacrifice teaching is <em>karma-based</em> but <em>meditative</em>: by knowing priest=faculty=deity, the sacrificer's identity expands to the cosmic, and the time-and-death that bind the limited self lose their hold. Still <em>saguṇa</em>, preparatory to the pure knowledge of later brāhmaṇas."],["Sureśvara","the triple identification (rite/body/cosmos) is the <em>bandhu</em>-method again, here yielding \"release\" within the ritual frame."],["Krishnananda","death and time grip whatever is <em>fragmentary</em>; the sacrificer escapes by merging the personal faculty with its cosmic counterpart — speech with fire, eye with sun — so there is no separate self left for death to seize."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical — perform every ritual act knowing the deity behind the faculty; worship transcends the time-bound when done with this knowledge."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the systematic priest–organ–deity equivalences typical of ritual-meditative <em>brahmodya</em>."]],"notes":["<em>Four parallel questions: all is \"overtaken/seized\" by death, by day-and-night, by the bright/dark fortnights, and the sky seems without foothold — how does the sacrificer get beyond them? Each is answered by a</em> <strong>priest = faculty = cosmic deity</strong>, <em>yielding</em> <strong>mukti</strong> <em>(release) and</em> <strong>atimukti</strong> <em>(complete release):</em>","<table><tr><td>Mantra</td><td>\"All is seized by…\"</td><td>Priest (ṛtvij)</td><td>Faculty</td><td>Cosmic deity</td><td>→ result</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.3</td><td><strong>death</strong></td><td><strong>hotṛ</strong></td><td>speech (vāc)</td><td><strong>fire</strong> (agni)</td><td>release &amp; full release</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.4</td><td><strong>day &amp; night</strong></td><td><strong>adhvaryu</strong></td><td>eye (cakṣus)</td><td><strong>sun</strong> (āditya)</td><td>release &amp; full release</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.5</td><td>the <strong>fortnights</strong></td><td><strong>udgātṛ</strong></td><td>breath (prāṇa)</td><td><strong>wind</strong> (vāyu)</td><td>release &amp; full release</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.6</td><td>the unsupported <strong>sky</strong> (ascent to heaven)</td><td><strong>brahman</strong></td><td>mind (manas)</td><td><strong>moon</strong> (candra)</td><td>release &amp; full release</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantras 3.1.7–3.1.10","title":"The numerical correspondences (sampad): hymns, oblations, worlds","sa":["3.1.7 (the hotṛ's three ṛcs): याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — कतिभिरयमद्यर्ग्भिर्होतास्मिन्यज्ञे करिष्यतीति । तिसृभिरिति । कतमास्तास्तिस्र इति । पुरोनुवाक्या च याज्या च शस्यैव तृतीया । किं ताभिर्जयतीति । यत्किञ्चेदं प्राणभृदिति ॥ ७ ॥","3.1.8 (the adhvaryu's three oblations): याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — कत्ययमद्याध्वर्युरस्मिन्यज्ञ आहुतीर्होष्यतीति । तिस्र इति । कतमास्तास्तिस्र इति । या हुता उज्ज्वलन्ति या हुता अतिनेदन्ति या हुता अधिशेरते । किं ताभिर्जयतीति । या हुता उज्ज्वलन्ति देवलोकमेव ताभिर्जयति । दीप्यत इव हि देवलोको । या हुता अतिनेदन्ते पितृलोकमेव ताभिर्जयति । अतीव हि पितृलोकः । या हुता अधिशेरते मनुष्यलोकमेव ताभिर्जयति । अध इव हि मनुष्यलोकः ॥ ८ ॥","3.1.9 (the brahman-priest's one deity): याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — कतिभिरयमद्य ब्रह्मा यज्ञं दक्षिणतो देवताभिर्गोपायतीति । एकयेति । कतमा सैकेति । मन एवेति । अनन्तं वै मनोऽनन्ता विश्वे देवाः । अनन्तमेव स तेन लोकं जयति ॥ ९ ॥","3.1.10 (the udgātṛ's three hymns; the breaths): याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — कत्ययमद्योद्गातास्मिन्यज्ञे स्तोत्रियाः स्तोष्यतीति । तिस्र इति । कतमास्तास्तिस्र इति । पुरोनुवाक्या च याज्या च शस्यैव तृतीया । कतमास्ताः । या अध्यात्ममिति । प्राण एव पुरोनुवाक्यापानो याज्या व्यानः शस्या । किं ताभिर्जयतीति । पृथिवीलोकमेव पुरोनुवाक्यया जयत्यन्तरिक्षलोकं याज्यया द्युलोकं शस्यया । ततो ह होताश्वल उपरराम ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["yājñavalkyeti hovāca -- katibhir ayam adyargbhir hotāsmin yajñe kariṣyatīti | tisṛbhir iti | katamās tās tisra iti | puronuvākyā ca yājyā ca śasyaiva tṛtīyā | kiṃ tābhir jayatīti | yat kiñcedaṃ prāṇabhṛd iti || 7 ||","... yā hutā ujjvalanti yā hutā atinedanti yā hutā adhiśerate | … yā hutā ujjvalanti devalokam eva tābhir jayati | dīpyata iva hi devaloko | yā hutā atinedante pitṛlokam eva tābhir jayati | atīva hi pitṛlokaḥ | yā hutā adhiśerate manuṣyalokam eva tābhir jayati | adha iva hi manuṣyalokaḥ || 8 ||","... ekayeti | katamā saiketi | mana eveti | anantaṃ vai mano 'nantā viśve devāḥ | anantam eva sa tena lokaṃ jayati || 9 ||","... prāṇa eva puronuvākyāpāno yājyā vyānaḥ śasyā | … pṛthivīlokam eva puronuvākyayā jayaty antarikṣalokaṃ yājyayā dyulokaṃ śasyayā | tato ha hotāśvala upararāma || 10 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"'With how many deities does the brahman-priest protect the sacrifice from the south today?' 'With one.' 'Which is that one?' 'The <strong>mind</strong> alone. For the mind is infinite, and the Viśvedevas are infinite; thereby he wins an infinite world.'\"","para":"The <em>sampad</em> (correspondence) section: Aśvala asks how many hymns and oblations each priest uses and what each wins. The <strong>hotṛ's three ṛcs</strong> win all living beings; the <strong>adhvaryu's three oblations</strong> (blazing, roaring, sinking) win the worlds of gods, ancestors, and men respectively; the <strong>udgātṛ's three hymns</strong> (= the breaths prāṇa, apāna, vyāna) win earth, mid-air, and heaven. Most pointed is the <strong>brahman-priest, who uses but <em>one</em> deity — the mind</strong> — and because <strong>mind is infinite (like the Viśvedevas), he wins the infinite world.</strong> With this, the hotṛ <strong>Aśvala fell silent</strong> (defeated).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the brahman-priest's \"one infinite deity, the mind\" surpasses the others' threes: where the others win <em>bounded</em> worlds (gods', ancestors', men's; earth, sky, heaven), the meditation on the <em>infinite mind</em> wins the <em>infinite</em> — the highest fruit of the ritual register (cf. 1.5.13, \"as you conceive, so you attain\")."],["Sureśvara","the grading shows mind/infinity as the apex of the <em>karma-sampad</em>, pointing beyond the bounded toward the limitless."],["Krishnananda","the brahman-priest, who oversees the whole rite silently with the mind, models the inner, all-comprehending meditation that wins not a particular heaven but the infinite — a bridge to the pure knowledge to come."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical liturgics infused with meditation: know the breaths as the udgātṛ's hymns, the mind as the brahman's infinite deity, and the rite becomes a vehicle to the limitless."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the numerical mysticism (<em>sampad</em>) and the climactic elevation of the infinite mind; Aśvala's silence is the first of nine defeats."]],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Q (priest)</td><td>number</td><td>the items</td><td>what is won</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.7 hotṛ's ṛcs</td><td><strong>three</strong></td><td>puronuvākyā, yājyā, śasyā</td><td>all living things (<em>prāṇabhṛt</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.8 adhvaryu's oblations</td><td><strong>three</strong></td><td>those that blaze up / roar / sink</td><td>deva-loka / pitṛ-loka / manuṣya-loka</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.9 brahman's deities</td><td><strong>one</strong></td><td>the <strong>mind</strong> (infinite, = Viśvedevas)</td><td>the <strong>infinite</strong> world</td></tr><tr><td>3.1.10 udgātṛ's hymns</td><td><strong>three</strong></td><td>prāṇa / apāna / vyāna</td><td>earth / mid-region / heaven</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The opening round establishes both the <strong>dramatic frame</strong> (Janaka's contest; Yājñavalkya's bold claim; nine challengers to come) and a <strong>method</strong>: Yājñavalkya answers ritual questions by the <strong>identity of priest = faculty = cosmic deity</strong>, so that the sacrifice becomes a vehicle of transcendence:","1. <strong>Release from time and death</strong> (3.1.3–6): the four priests (hotṛ, adhvaryu, udgātṛ, brahman) — as speech/fire, eye/sun, breath/wind, mind/moon — carry the sacrificer past death, day-and-night, the fortnights, and up the supportless sky. The bound, fragmentary self is freed by merging with the cosmic.","2. <strong>The correspondences</strong> (3.1.7–10): the priests' hymns and oblations win graded worlds — culminating in the <strong>brahman-priest's single infinite deity, the mind</strong>, which wins the <strong>infinite world</strong>. The pattern <em>\"the infinite conception wins the infinite\"</em> (cf. 1.5.13) hints already that the boundless is the true goal.","This brāhmaṇa is still within the <strong>ritual-meditative</strong> register (Aśvala is a priest, his questions liturgical); the contest will climb, challenger by challenger, from rite (3.1–3.2) toward the pure metaphysics of the <em>antaryāmin</em>, the <em>akṣara</em>, and <em>neti neti</em> (3.7–3.9)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   JANAKA&#x27;S CONTEST (BU 3.1)\n   =========================\n   1000 cows + gold  -&gt;  &quot;let the greatest knower drive them off&quot;\n   Yājñavalkya: (to his pupil) &quot;drive them home&quot;  -&gt;  brāhmaṇas angry\n   AŚVALA (hotṛ) rises to question:\n\n   RELEASES (atimokṣa) — priest = faculty = deity carries the sacrificer past:\n   ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n     seized by DEATH       -&gt; hotṛ   = speech = FIRE      -&gt; release\n     seized by DAY+NIGHT   -&gt; adhvaryu= eye   = SUN       -&gt; release\n     seized by FORTNIGHTS  -&gt; udgātṛ = breath = WIND      -&gt; release\n     supportless SKY (-&gt;heaven)-&gt; brahman= mind = MOON    -&gt; release\n   ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n   CORRESPONDENCES (sampad):\n     hotṛ&#x27;s 3 ṛcs        -&gt; all living beings\n     adhvaryu&#x27;s 3 oblations: blaze-&gt;deva-loka | roar-&gt;pitṛ-loka | sink-&gt;manuṣya-loka\n     udgātṛ&#x27;s 3 hymns = prāṇa/apāna/vyāna -&gt; earth / mid-air / heaven\n     BRAHMAN&#x27;s ONE deity = the MIND (infinite) -&gt; the INFINITE world  ****\n                                           |\n                                           v\n                              Aśvala FALLS SILENT  (defeat 1 of 9)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Let knowledge be tested.</strong> Yājñavalkya invites every challenge. Hold your understanding open to rigorous questioning rather than defending it — truth survives examination.","2. <strong>Merge the personal with the cosmic.</strong> The releases work by identifying speech with fire, eye with sun, breath with wind, mind with moon. In any act, sense the cosmic power behind your faculty; the small, time-bound self that death \"seizes\" loosens.","3. <strong>Conceive the infinite</strong> (3.1.9). The brahman-priest wins the infinite by meditating on the infinite mind. Whatever you contemplate, take its boundless form — \"the infinite conception wins the infinite\" (cf. 1.5.13).","4. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> death and time grip only the fragment. Identify with the whole — the cosmic faculties, the infinite mind — and you stand where time cannot wholly seize you. This is the first step of a ladder that will rise, challenger by challenger, to the unconditioned Self."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3 · Brāhmaṇa 3.2 — Ārtabhāga: the graspers, death, and the secret of action","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 3.2","title":"Ārtabhāga: the graspers, death, and the secret of action","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The second challenger,</em> <strong>Jāratkārava Ārtabhāga</strong>, <em>asks about the</em> <strong>grahas (graspers) and atigrahas (over-graspers)</strong> <em>— the eight sense-faculties that \"seize\" their objects and are themselves \"over-seized\" by those objects: the mechanism of bondage. He then asks what death feeds on, whether the breaths leave at death, what does</em> <strong>not</strong> <em>leave (the name), and finally where a man \"goes\" when all his parts return to their cosmic sources — to which Yājñavalkya gives, privately, the</em> <strong>first explicit statement of the karma doctrine</strong>: <em>\"one becomes good by good action, bad by bad.\"</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.1","title":"How many graspers and over-graspers?","sa":["अथ हैनं जारत्कारव आर्तभागः पप्रच्छ । याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — कति ग्रहाः कत्यतिग्रहा इति । अष्टौ ग्रहा अष्टावतिग्रहा इति । ये तेऽष्टौ ग्रहा अष्टावतिग्रहाः कतमे त इति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ jāratkārava ārtabhāgaḥ papraccha | yājñavalkyeti hovāca -- kati grahāḥ katy atigrahā iti | aṣṭau grahā aṣṭāv atigrahā iti | ye te 'ṣṭau grahā aṣṭāv atigrahāḥ katame ta iti || 1 ||"],"words":[["atha ha enaṃ jāratkāravaḥ ārtabhāgaḥ papraccha","nom. + 3 sg. pf. of prach","then Ārtabhāga of the Jaratkāru line questioned him"],["kati grahāḥ kati atigrahāḥ iti","interrog. + m. nom. pl.","\"how many graspers (grahas), how many over-graspers (atigrahas)?\""],["aṣṭau grahāḥ aṣṭau atigrahāḥ iti","numeral","\"eight graspers, eight over-graspers\""],["ye te aṣṭau grahāḥ aṣṭau atigrahāḥ katame te iti","rel. + interrog.","\"those eight graspers and eight over-graspers — which are they?\""]],"lit":"\"Then Jāratkārava Ārtabhāga questioned him: 'Yājñavalkya, how many <em>grahas</em> (graspers) are there, and how many <em>atigrahas</em> (over-graspers)?' 'Eight graspers and eight over-graspers.' 'Those eight graspers and eight over-graspers — which are they?'\"","para":"<em>Graha</em> = \"seizer/grasper\" (literally a soma-cup that \"grasps\" the liquid; here, a sense-organ that grasps its object); <em>atigraha</em> = \"over-grasper,\" the <strong>object</strong> that in turn \"seizes\" the organ. The pair-structure names the mechanism of bondage: each faculty catches its object, and is caught by it. Ārtabhāga asks Yājñavalkya to enumerate the eight pairs.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>graha/atigraha</em> (from the soma-ritual cups) is repurposed to mean <strong>organ and object</strong>, the two \"hooks\" of saṃsāric bondage; the organ grasps the object, and is bound (over-grasped) by craving for it."],["Sureśvara","the doubled grasping (organ↔object) diagnoses how consciousness is \"caught\" in the world — the root of transmigration."],["Krishnananda","\"You catch the world through the senses, and the world catches you back\" — the <em>atigraha</em> is the object's grip on the captivated organ; liberation is release from this mutual seizure."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a map of bondage useful for <em>vairāgya</em>: see how each sense is hooked to its object."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the ritual origin of <em>graha</em> (soma-cups) and its philosophical transfer to the senses."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 3.2.2–3.2.9","title":"The eight grasper / over-grasper pairs","sa":["3.2.2 (smell-breath): प्राणो वै ग्रहः । सोऽपानेनातिग्रहेण गृहीतः । प्राणेन हि गन्धाञ्जिघ्रति ॥ २ ॥","3.2.3 (speech): वाग्वै ग्रहः । स नाम्नातिग्रहेण गृहीतः । वाचा हि नामान्यभिवदति ॥ ३ ॥","3.2.4 (tongue): जिह्वा वै ग्रहः । स रसेनातिग्रहेण गृहीतः । जिह्वया हि रसान्विजानाति ॥ ४ ॥","3.2.5 (eye): चक्षुर्वै ग्रहः । स रूपेणातिग्रहेण गृहीतः । चक्षुषा हि रूपाणि पश्यति ॥ ५ ॥","3.2.6 (ear): श्रोत्रं वै ग्रहः । स शब्देनातिग्रहेण गृहीतः । श्रोत्रेण हि शब्दाञ्छृणोति ॥ ६ ॥","3.2.7 (mind): मनो वै ग्रहः । स कामेनातिग्रहेण गृहीतः । मनसा हि कामान्कामयते ॥ ७ ॥","3.2.8 (hands): हस्तौ वै ग्रहः । स कर्मणातिग्रहेण गृहीतः । हस्ताभ्यां हि कर्म करोति ॥ ८ ॥","3.2.9 (skin): त्वग्वै ग्रहः । स स्पर्शेनातिग्रहेण गृहीतः । त्वचा हि स्पर्शान्वेदयत इत्यष्टौ ग्रहा अष्टावतिग्रहाः ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["prāṇo vai grahaḥ | so 'pānenātigraheṇa gṛhītaḥ | prāṇena hi gandhāñ jighrati || 2 ||","vāg vai grahaḥ | sa nāmnātigraheṇa gṛhītaḥ | vācā hi nāmāny abhivadati || 3 ||","jihvā vai grahaḥ | sa rasenātigraheṇa gṛhītaḥ | jihvayā hi rasān vijānāti || 4 ||","cakṣur vai grahaḥ | sa rūpeṇātigraheṇa gṛhītaḥ | cakṣuṣā hi rūpāṇi paśyati || 5 ||","śrotraṃ vai grahaḥ | sa śabdenātigraheṇa gṛhītaḥ | śrotreṇa hi śabdāñ śṛṇoti || 6 ||","mano vai grahaḥ | sa kāmenātigraheṇa gṛhītaḥ | manasā hi kāmān kāmayate || 7 ||","hastau vai grahaḥ | sa karmaṇātigraheṇa gṛhītaḥ | hastābhyāṃ hi karma karoti || 8 ||","tvag vai grahaḥ | sa sparśenātigraheṇa gṛhītaḥ | tvacā hi sparśān vedayata ity aṣṭau grahā aṣṭāv atigrahāḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>Consolidated table:</strong><br>\"The <strong>breath</strong> (organ of smell) is a grasper; it is over-grasped by the <strong>odour</strong> as the over-grasper, for by the breath one smells odours.\" <em>(So speech↔names, tongue↔tastes, eye↔forms, ear↔sounds, mind↔desires, hands↔action, skin↔touches: these are the eight graspers and eight over-graspers.)</em>","para":"Eight <strong>organs</strong> (graspers) are each yoked to their proper <strong>objects</strong> (over-graspers): smell↔odour, speech↔name, taste↔flavour, sight↔form, hearing↔sound, mind↔desire, hands↔action, skin↔touch. Each organ \"seizes\" its object and is in turn \"held\" by it — the complete circuit of sense-bondage. (Notably, <strong>mind↔desire</strong> and <strong>hands↔action</strong> are included alongside the five senses, extending the analysis to volition and deed.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the eightfold scheme covers perception (five senses), volition (mind↔desire), and action (hands↔deed) — the whole transactional self. Each pair is a \"knot\" of bondage; the over-grasper (the desired object) is what truly binds, by re-arousing the organ."],["Sureśvara","the inclusion of <em>kāma</em> (desire) and <em>karma</em> (action) shows bondage is not merely perceptual but volitional-active — preparing the karma teaching of 3.2.13."],["Krishnananda","a precise anatomy of how the self is \"caught\": not the organ alone, nor the object alone, but their mutual grip. Freedom is loosening every <em>atigraha</em> — the object's hold via craving."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","meditate on each pair to see the binding mechanism, then withdraw (pratyāhāra) the grasper from the over-grasper."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the systematic organ↔object pairing and the inclusion of mind/desire and hands/action as a comprehensive psychology of attachment."]],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Mantra</td><td>Grasper (graha = organ)</td><td>Over-grasper (atigraha = object)</td><td>because…</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.2</td><td><strong>breath/nose</strong> (prāṇa)</td><td><strong>odours</strong> (apāna)</td><td>by the breath one smells odours</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.3</td><td><strong>speech</strong> (vāc)</td><td><strong>names</strong> (nāman)</td><td>by speech one utters names</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.4</td><td><strong>tongue</strong> (jihvā)</td><td><strong>tastes</strong> (rasa)</td><td>by the tongue one knows tastes</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.5</td><td><strong>eye</strong> (cakṣus)</td><td><strong>forms</strong> (rūpa)</td><td>by the eye one sees forms</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.6</td><td><strong>ear</strong> (śrotra)</td><td><strong>sounds</strong> (śabda)</td><td>by the ear one hears sounds</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.7</td><td><strong>mind</strong> (manas)</td><td><strong>desires</strong> (kāma)</td><td>by the mind one desires</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.8</td><td><strong>hands</strong> (hastau)</td><td><strong>action</strong> (karma)</td><td>by the hands one acts</td></tr><tr><td>3.2.9</td><td><strong>skin</strong> (tvac)</td><td><strong>touches</strong> (sparśa)</td><td>by the skin one feels touches</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.10","title":"Death is fire, the food of water; conquest of re-death","sa":["याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — यदिदं सर्वं मृत्योरन्नं का स्वित्सा देवता यस्या मृत्युरन्नमिति । अग्निर्वै मृत्युः सोऽपामन्नम् । अप पुनर्मृत्युं जयति ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["yājñavalkyeti hovāca -- yad idaṃ sarvaṃ mṛtyor annaṃ kā svit sā devatā yasyā mṛtyur annam iti | agnir vai mṛtyuḥ so 'pām annam | apa punarmṛtyuṃ jayati || 10 ||"],"words":[["yat idaṃ sarvaṃ mṛtyoḥ annam","n. nom. + gen. + n. nom.","\"since all this is the food of death,"],["kā svit sā devatā yasyāḥ mṛtyuḥ annam iti","interrog. f. nom. + gen.","which deity is it whose food is death (itself)?\""],["agniḥ vai mṛtyuḥ saḥ apām annam","m. nom. + gen. pl.","fire is death; it (fire) is the food of the waters"],["apa punar-mṛtyuṃ jayati","acc. + 3 sg. pres. of ji","(the knower) conquers re-death"]],"lit":"\"(Ārtabhāga:) 'Since all this is the food of death, which deity is it whose food is death itself?' (Yājñavalkya:) '<strong>Fire</strong> is death, and fire is the <strong>food of the waters.</strong> (Whoever knows this) conquers re-death.'\"","para":"All things are \"eaten\" by <strong>death</strong> (the universal devourer, cf. 1.2). But what consumes death? <strong>Fire is death; and fire, in turn, is \"eaten\" by the waters</strong> (water quenches fire). To know that even death (fire) has something that overcomes it — and to identify with that — is to <strong>conquer <em>punar-mṛtyu</em> (re-death/repeated dying).</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","even \"death\" (here, fire — the heat that dries and destroys) is itself subject to a higher power (water); the meditation that nothing is finally the supreme devourer — that death too is consumed — frees one from re-death."],["Sureśvara","the regress (all is death's food; death is water's food) points beyond every \"eater\" to the Self, which is no one's food."],["Krishnananda","death is not absolute — it too is \"eaten\"; recognising the relativity of death dissolves the terror of it and conquers redeath."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a meditation for fearlessness: that which seems the final destroyer (fire/death) is itself overcome."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the <em>punar-mṛtyu</em> (re-death) theme, an early form of the rebirth doctrine."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.11","title":"At death the breaths do not depart elsewhere","sa":["याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — यत्रायं पुरुषो म्रियत उदस्मात्प्राणाः क्रामन्त्याहो नेति । नेति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः । अत्रैव समवनीयन्ते । स उच्छ्वयति । आध्मायति । आध्मातो मृतः शेते ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["yājñavalkyeti hovāca -- yatrāyaṃ puruṣo mriyata ud asmāt prāṇāḥ krāmanty āho neti | neti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ | atraiva samavanīyante | sa ucchvayati | ādhmāyati | ādhmāto mṛtaḥ śete || 11 ||"],"words":[["yatra ayaṃ puruṣaḥ mriyate","rel. + nom. + 3 sg. pres. of mṛ","\"when this person dies,"],["ut asmāt prāṇāḥ krāmanti āho na iti","abl. + nom. pl. + pres. of ud-kram + interrog.","do the breaths depart out of him, or not?\""],["na iti ha uvāca","","\"No,\" said Yājñavalkya"],["atra eva samavanīyante","adv. + 3 pl. pres. pass.","\"they are gathered up right here"],["saḥ ucchvayati ādhmāyati","3 sg. pres.","he swells, is inflated"],["ādhmātaḥ mṛtaḥ śete","pp. + pres. of śī","inflated, the dead one lies (so)\""]],"lit":"\"'When a person dies, do the breaths depart out of him, or not?' 'No,' said Yājñavalkya; 'they are gathered up right here (in the body). He swells, is inflated; inflated, the dead man lies (there).'\"","para":"Ārtabhāga asks whether, at death, the <strong>prāṇas (vital breaths) leave the body</strong>. Yājñavalkya's striking answer: <strong>no — they are reabsorbed <em>there itself</strong></em> (the <em>individual</em> breaths merge into the total prāṇa within), which is why a corpse <strong>swells and bloats</strong> rather than visibly \"emptying.\" (The subtler point — that the <em>self</em> with its karma does transmigrate — is reserved for 3.2.13.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>particular</em> breaths are not \"carried off\" as separate entities; they merge into the prāṇa-aggregate, the body bloating as the gases of the held breath expand. What <em>does</em> go on is the subtle body bearing karma (next mantra) — but the gross prāṇas dissolve \"here.\""],["Sureśvara","distinguishes the dissolution of the empirical breaths from the continuity of the karmic self."],["Krishnananda","the breaths do not \"fly away\" intact; they are withdrawn into their source at death — the swelling corpse is the evidence. The question prepares the deeper one: what then continues?"],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a sober observation of death's physiology pressed into metaphysical service."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the realistic detail (the bloating corpse) marshalled to deny a naïve \"the breath leaves\" picture."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.12","title":"What does not leave: the name","sa":["याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — यत्रायं पुरुषो म्रियते किमेनं न जहातीति । नामेति । अनन्तं वै नामानन्ता विश्वे देवाः । अनन्तमेव स तेन लोकं जयति ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["yājñavalkyeti hovāca -- yatrāyaṃ puruṣo mriyate kim enaṃ na jahātīti | nāmeti | anantaṃ vai nāmānantā viśve devāḥ | anantam eva sa tena lokaṃ jayati || 12 ||"],"words":[["yatra ayaṃ puruṣaḥ mriyate kim enaṃ na jahāti iti","rel. + acc. + 3 sg. pres. of hā","\"when a person dies, what does not leave him?\""],["nāma iti","n. nom.","\"the name\""],["anantaṃ vai nāma anantāḥ viśve devāḥ","n. nom. + nom. pl.","the name is infinite, the Viśvedevas are infinite"],["anantam eva saḥ tena lokaṃ jayati","acc. + instr.","thereby he wins an infinite world"]],"lit":"\"'When a person dies, what does not leave him?' 'The <strong>name</strong>. For the name is infinite, and the Viśvedevas are infinite; thereby (the knower) wins an infinite world.'\"","para":"When everything else of a person dissolves at death, <strong>his name</strong> persists (a name outlives its bearer — and \"name,\" <em>nāma</em>, points to the enduring <em>nāma-rūpa</em> principle). Because <strong>name is infinite</strong> (co-extensive with the boundless Viśvedevas), meditation on it <strong>wins the infinite world</strong> (cf. the \"infinite mind\" of 3.1.9).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>name</em> (the word/idea) is not consumed at death; <em>nāma</em> belongs to the imperishable register (cf. 1.6, name resting on speech/Brahman). Its infinitude yields the infinite world."],["Sureśvara","what survives is not the body but <em>nāma</em> — the principle of designation that is rooted in Brahman."],["Krishnananda","a person's \"name\" outlasts the body; more deeply, <em>nāma</em> is the subtle imprint that does not perish — and being infinite, it links to the infinite world."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","meditation on the infinite name/word as a path to the limitless."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the survival of the <em>name</em> as a culturally resonant answer (one's name endures) raised to metaphysical infinitude."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2.13","title":"Where does the man go? The secret of karma","sa":["याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — यत्रास्य पुरुषस्य मृतस्याग्निं वागप्येति वातं प्राणश्चक्षुरादित्यं मनश्चन्द्रं दिशः श्रोत्रं पृथिवीं शरीरमाकाशमात्मौषधीर्लोमानि वनस्पतीन्केशा अप्सु लोहितं च रेतश्च निधीयते क्वायं तदा पुरुषो भवतीति । आहर सौम्य हस्तमार्तभाग । आवामेवैतस्य वेदिष्यावो न नावेतत्सजन इति । तौ होत्क्रम्य मन्त्रयांचक्राते । तौ ह यदूचतुः कर्म हैव तदूचतुः । अथ ह यत्प्रशंसतुः कर्म हैव तत्प्रशशंसतुः । पुण्यो वै पुण्येन कर्मणा भवति पापः पापेनेति । ततो ह जारत्कारव आर्तभाग उपरराम ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["yājñavalkyeti hovāca -- yatrāsya puruṣasya mṛtasyāgniṃ vāg apyeti vātaṃ prāṇaś cakṣur ādityaṃ manaś candraṃ diśaḥ śrotraṃ pṛthivīṃ śarīram ākāśam ātmauṣadhīr lomāni vanaspatīn keśā apsu lohitaṃ ca retaś ca nidhīyate kvāyaṃ tadā puruṣo bhavatīti | āhara saumya hastam ārtabhāga | āvām evaitasya vediṣyāvo na nāv etat sajana iti | tau hotkramya mantrayāṃ cakrāte | tau ha yad ūcatuḥ karma haiva tad ūcatuḥ | atha ha yat praśaṃsatuḥ karma haiva tat praśaśaṃsatuḥ | puṇyo vai puṇyena karmaṇā bhavati pāpaḥ pāpeneti | tato ha jāratkārava ārtabhāga upararāma || 13 ||"],"words":[["yatra asya puruṣasya mṛtasya","gen. + pp.","\"when, of this dead person,"],["agniṃ vāk apyeti, vātaṃ prāṇaḥ, cakṣuḥ ādityaṃ, manaḥ candraṃ, diśaḥ śrotraṃ, pṛthivīṃ śarīram, ākāśam ātmā, oṣadhīḥ lomāni, vanaspatīn keśāḥ, apsu lohitaṃ ca retaḥ ca nidhīyate","acc. + 3 sg. pres. of api-i / pass. of ni-dhā","speech enters fire, breath the wind, eye the sun, mind the moon, ear the quarters, body the earth, the self (space) the ether, the body-hairs the herbs, the head-hairs the trees, and the blood and seed are deposited in the waters —"],["kva ayaṃ tadā puruṣaḥ bhavati iti","adv. + nom.","where, then, is the person?\""],["āhara saumya hastam ārtabhāga","impv. + voc.","\"give me your hand, dear Ārtabhāga;"],["āvām eva etasya vediṣyāvaḥ na nau etat sajane iti","nom. du. + 1 du. fut. of vid + loc. (in public)","we two alone shall know of this — this (talk) of ours is not for the crowd\""],["tau ha utkramya mantrayāṃ cakrāte","nom. du. + ger. + periphr. pf.","the two went aside and deliberated"],["tau ha yat ūcatuḥ karma ha eva tat ūcatuḥ","3 du. pf. of vac","what they spoke of was action (karma)"],["atha ha yat praśaṃsatuḥ karma ha eva tat praśaśaṃsatuḥ","3 du. pf.","and what they praised was action"],["puṇyaḥ vai puṇyena karmaṇā bhavati pāpaḥ pāpena iti","nom. + instr.","one becomes good (puṇya) by good action, and bad (pāpa) by bad action"],["tataḥ ha jāratkāravaḥ ārtabhāgaḥ upararāma","3 sg. pf. of upa-ram","thereupon Ārtabhāga fell silent"]],"lit":"\"(Ārtabhāga:) 'When, of this dead person, his speech merges into fire, his breath into the wind, his eye into the sun, his mind into the moon, his ear into the quarters, his body into the earth, his self (the space within) into the ether, his body-hairs into the herbs, his head-hairs into the trees, and his blood and seed are deposited in the waters — where, then, is the person?' (Yājñavalkya:) 'Give me your hand, dear Ārtabhāga; we two alone shall know of this — this is not for us (to speak of) in public.' The two went aside and deliberated. What they spoke of was <strong>action (karma)</strong>; what they praised was <strong>action</strong>: '<strong>one becomes good (puṇya) by good action, and bad (pāpa) by bad action.</strong>' Thereupon Ārtabhāga fell silent.\"","para":"Ārtabhāga poses the ultimate question: when, at death, <strong>every part of a person dissolves into its cosmic source</strong> (speech→fire, breath→wind, eye→sun, … blood &amp; seed→waters), <strong>what remains of the person — where does he \"go\"?</strong> This is too weighty for public debate; Yājñavalkya takes Ārtabhāga aside, and what they conclude is the <strong>doctrine of karma</strong>: the person is not annihilated but <strong>continues according to his deeds</strong> — <em>one becomes good by good action and bad by bad action.</em> This is the <strong>earliest explicit, datable statement of the law of karma</strong> as the principle of post-mortem continuity — deliberately taught in private, as a profound secret. (The full mechanism of rebirth by desire-and-action is given later, at 4.4.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the gross elements return to their sources, but the <strong>subtle body, impelled by <em>karma</em>,</strong> carries the transmigrating self onward; hence \"where is the person?\" is answered not by a place but by a <em>law</em> — <em>puṇya/pāpa karma</em> determines the next state. The privacy underscores that karma-and-rebirth was, at this stage, esoteric teaching. (Śaṅkara: this is the seed elaborated in 4.4.5–6.)"],["Sureśvara","the dissolution of parts refutes both annihilationism (something continues) and naïve survival (the parts <em>do</em> return to their sources); what continues is the karma-bearing self."],["Krishnananda","the historic moment when <strong>karma</strong> is named as the thread of continuity — \"you become what you do.\" Taught in secret because it overturns the ritualist's hope of a fixed heaven, making destiny depend on the moral quality of action."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the foundational ethical law: character and future are forged by deeds; hence the supreme importance of <em>right</em> action. Yet (as later chapters show) karma binds, and only knowledge liberates."],["Deussen/Olivelle","a <em>locus classicus</em> for the history of the karma doctrine; Olivelle stresses its presentation as a <strong>new, secret teaching</strong> here first made explicit, distinct from the older ritual-reward scheme."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3 · Brāhmaṇa 3.2 · Brāhmaṇa 3.3 — Bhujyu: where the Pārikṣitas went (the wind)","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 3.3","title":"Bhujyu: where the Pārikṣitas went (the wind)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The third challenger,</em> <strong>Bhujyu Lāhyāyani</strong>, <em>relays a question once put (through a gandharva-possessed girl) about the</em> <strong>\"ends of the world\"</strong> <em>and</em> <strong>where the Pārikṣitas went</strong> <em>— and Yājñavalkya answers with a cosmic geography culminating in the</em> <strong>wind (vāyu)</strong> <em>as the carrier of souls and the principle that is both the individual (vyaṣṭi) and the totality (samaṣṭi).</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.3.1","title":"Bhujyu's question","sa":["अथ हैनं भुज्युर्लाह्यायनिः पप्रच्छ — याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — मद्रेषु चरकाः पर्यव्रजाम । ते पतञ्चलस्य काप्यस्य गृहानैम । तस्यासीद्दुहिता गन्धर्वगृहीता । तमपृच्छाम कोऽसीति । सोऽब्रवीत्सुधन्वाङ्गिरस इति । तं यदा लोकानामन्तानपृच्छाम । अथैनमब्रूम — क्व पारिक्षिता अभवन्निति । क्व पारिक्षिता अभवन् । स त्वा पृच्छामि याज्ञवल्क्य । क्व पारिक्षिता अभवन्निति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ bhujyur lāhyāyaniḥ papraccha -- yājñavalkyeti hovāca -- madreṣu carakāḥ paryavrajāma | te patañcalasya kāpyasya gṛhān aima | tasyāsīd duhitā gandharvagṛhītā | tam apṛcchāma ko 'sīti | so 'bravīt sudhanvāṅgirasa iti | taṃ yadā lokānām antān apṛcchāma | athainam abrūma -- kva pārikṣitā abhavann iti | kva pārikṣitā abhavan | sa tvā pṛcchāmi yājñavalkya | kva pārikṣitā abhavann iti || 1 ||"],"words":[["madreṣu carakāḥ paryavrajāma","loc. pl. + nom. pl. (wandering students) + 1 pl. impf.","\"we were wandering as students among the Madras"],["te patañcalasya kāpyasya gṛhān aima","acc. pl. + 1 pl. impf. of i","we came to the house of Patañcala Kāpya"],["tasya āsīt duhitā gandharva-gṛhītā","f. nom. + pp.","he had a daughter possessed by a gandharva"],["tam apṛcchāma kaḥ asi iti","acc. + 1 pl. impf.","we asked it (the gandharva), 'who are you?'"],["saḥ abravīt sudhanvā āṅgirasaḥ iti","3 sg. impf.","it said, 'I am Sudhanvan Āṅgirasa'"],["taṃ yadā lokānām antān apṛcchāma","acc. pl. (the ends of the worlds)","when we asked it about the ends of the worlds,"],["atha enam abrūma kva pārikṣitāḥ abhavan iti","1 pl. impf. + nom. pl. + 3 pl. impf. of bhū","we asked it, 'where did the Pārikṣitas go?'"],["saḥ tvā pṛcchāmi yājñavalkya kva pārikṣitāḥ abhavan iti","acc. + 1 sg. pres.","(so) I ask you, Yājñavalkya — where did the Pārikṣitas go?\""]],"lit":"\"Then Bhujyu Lāhyāyani questioned him: 'Yājñavalkya, we were wandering as students among the Madras and came to the house of Patañcala Kāpya. He had a daughter possessed by a gandharva. We asked it, \"Who are you?\" It said, \"I am Sudhanvan Āṅgirasa.\" When we asked it about the ends of the worlds, we said to it, \"Where did the <strong>Pārikṣitas</strong> go?\" — (and now) I ask you, Yājñavalkya: where did the Pārikṣitas go?'\"","para":"Bhujyu recounts a marvel: a <strong>gandharva</strong> (a celestial being) speaking through a possessed girl, who knew cosmic secrets. The test-question — once relayed by that gandharva — is <strong>\"Where did the Pārikṣitas go?\"</strong> (the descendants of Parikṣit, famous aśvamedha-performers, i.e., <em>where do the great sacrificers end up?</em>). Bhujyu challenges Yājñavalkya to answer it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the question concerns the <strong>destiny of the performers of the great horse-sacrifice</strong> — the goal of the ritual path; Yājñavalkya must show that he knows the post-mortem cosmic route."],["Krishnananda","the gandharva-frame lends the question an oracular authority; \"where do the great ritualists go?\" tests knowledge of the after-death cosmology."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the device of citing a superhuman source to pose a hard question."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the gandharva-possession motif and the Pārikṣitas as renowned aśvamedha kings (cf. Mahābhārata lore)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.3.2","title":"They went by the wind, which is all and each","sa":["स होवाच — उवाच वै सः । अगच्छन्वै ते तद्यत्राश्वमेधयाजिनो गच्छन्तीति । क्व न्वश्वमेधयाजिनो गच्छन्तीति । द्वात्रिंशतं वै देवरथाह्न्यान्ययं लोकः । तं समन्तं पृथिवी द्विस्तावत्पर्येति । तां समन्तं पृथिवीं द्विस्तावत्समुद्रः पर्येति । तद्यावती क्षुरस्य धारा यावद्वा मक्षिकायाः पत्त्रं तावानन्तरेणाकाशः । तानैन्द्रः सुपर्णो भूत्वा वायवे प्रायच्छत् । तान्वायुरात्मनि धित्वा तत्रागमयद्यत्राश्वमेधयाजिनोऽभवन्निति । एवमिव वै स वायुमेव प्रशशंस तस्माद्वायुरेव व्यष्टिर्वायुः समष्टिः । अप पुनर्मृत्युं जयति य एवं वेद । ततो ह भुज्युर्लाह्यायनिरुपरराम ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovāca -- uvāca vai saḥ | agacchan vai te tad yatrāśvamedhayājino gacchantīti | kva nv aśvamedhayājino gacchantīti | dvātriṃśataṃ vai devarathāhnyāny ayaṃ lokaḥ | taṃ samantaṃ pṛthivī dvistāvat paryeti | tāṃ samantaṃ pṛthivīṃ dvistāvat samudraḥ paryeti | tad yāvatī kṣurasya dhārā yāvad vā makṣikāyāḥ pattraṃ tāvān antareṇākāśaḥ | tān aindraḥ suparṇo bhūtvā vāyave prāyacchat | tān vāyur ātmani dhitvā tatrāgamayad yatrāśvamedhayājino 'bhavann iti | evam iva vai sa vāyum eva praśaśaṃsa tasmād vāyur eva vyaṣṭir vāyuḥ samaṣṭiḥ | apa punarmṛtyuṃ jayati ya evaṃ veda | tato ha bhujyur lāhyāyanir upararāma || 2 ||"],"words":[["saḥ ha uvāca: uvāca vai saḥ","3 sg. pf.","He (Yājñavalkya) said: \"He (the gandharva) did say —"],["agacchan vai te tat yatra aśvamedha-yājinaḥ gacchanti iti","3 pl. impf. + rel. + nom. pl. (horse-sacrificers)","'they went where the performers of the aśvamedha go'\""],["kva nu aśvamedha-yājinaḥ gacchanti iti","interrog.","\"and where do the aśvamedha-performers go?\""],["dvā-triṃśataṃ vai deva-ratha-ahnyāni ayaṃ lokaḥ","acc. (32 day-journeys of the divine chariot/sun)","this world is thirty-two day-journeys of the sun's chariot (in extent)"],["taṃ samantaṃ pṛthivī dvistāvat paryeti","acc. + adv. + 3 sg. pres. of pari-i","the earth surrounds it on all sides, twice as wide"],["tāṃ samantaṃ pṛthivīṃ dvistāvat samudraḥ paryeti","acc.","the ocean surrounds that earth, twice as wide again"],["tat yāvatī kṣurasya dhārā yāvat vā makṣikāyāḥ pattraṃ tāvān antareṇa ākāśaḥ","f. nom. (razor's edge) + (fly's wing) + m. nom.","then there is a space (gap) as thin as a razor's edge or a fly's wing"],["tān aindraḥ suparṇaḥ bhūtvā vāyave prāyacchat","acc. pl. + nom. (Indra as a hawk) + dat. + impf. of pra-yam","Indra, becoming a hawk, handed them over to the wind"],["tān vāyuḥ ātmani dhitvā tatra agamayat yatra aśvamedha-yājinaḥ abhavan iti","acc. + loc. + ger. + impf. caus. of gam","the wind, placing them in itself, carried them where the aśvamedha-performers had gone"],["evam iva vai saḥ vāyum eva praśaśaṃsa","acc. + 3 sg. pf.","thus he (the gandharva) was really praising the wind"],["tasmāt vāyuḥ eva vyaṣṭiḥ vāyuḥ samaṣṭiḥ","m. nom. + f. nom. (the individual / the aggregate)","therefore the wind is the individual (diversified) and the wind is the aggregate (the totality)"],["apa punar-mṛtyuṃ jayati yaḥ evaṃ veda","acc. + pres.","whoever knows this conquers re-death"],["tataḥ ha bhujyuḥ lāhyāyaniḥ upararāma","3 sg. pf.","thereupon Bhujyu Lāhyāyani fell silent"]],"lit":"\"Yājñavalkya said: 'He (the gandharva) did answer: \"They went where the performers of the horse-sacrifice go.\" — And where do the performers of the horse-sacrifice go? This world extends thirty-two day-journeys of the sun's chariot; the earth surrounds it on every side, twice as wide; the ocean surrounds that earth, twice as wide again. Then there is a gap as thin as a razor's edge or a fly's wing. Indra, becoming a hawk, handed them (the souls) over to the wind; the wind, placing them within itself, carried them to where the performers of the horse-sacrifice (already) were. Thus the gandharva was in truth praising the <strong>wind</strong>; therefore the wind is both the individual (<em>vyaṣṭi</em>) and the aggregate (<em>samaṣṭi</em>). Whoever knows this conquers re-death.' Thereupon Bhujyu Lāhyāyani fell silent.\"","para":"Yājñavalkya recounts and decodes the gandharva's answer. The souls of the great sacrificers pass through a vast cosmic geography (this world, the encircling earth and ocean, and a razor-thin gap at the edge) and are finally <strong>carried by the wind (vāyu)</strong> — to which Indra (as a hawk) consigns them — to their goal. The hidden teaching: the gandharva was really exalting the <strong>wind</strong>, which is <strong>both <em>vyaṣṭi</em> (the diversified individual) and <em>samaṣṭi</em> (the undivided totality)</strong> — the cosmic life-principle that is at once each separate life and the whole (cf. prāṇa = the central breath = Vāyu, 1.5.22). To know the wind so is to <strong>conquer re-death.</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the elaborate cosmography leads to the essential point — <strong>Vāyu (the wind/air, the cosmic prāṇa) is the carrier and the goal-principle</strong>, being both the <strong>individual breath in each being (vyaṣṭi)</strong> and the <strong>total air (samaṣṭi)</strong>. Knowing this unity of the particular and the universal life conquers re-death."],["Sureśvara","<em>vyaṣṭi/samaṣṭi</em> (the distributed and the collective) names the one principle present as each and as all — the key to transcending the individual's death."],["Krishnananda","the soul's journey is, finally, into the <strong>cosmic air-principle</strong> that is both your single breath and the whole atmosphere; to realise that your life is the one Life (vyaṣṭi = samaṣṭi) is to conquer redeath. The cosmic distances dramatise the vastness the wind alone can traverse."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","meditate on Vāyu/prāṇa as both your individual breath and the total cosmic life; this identity is liberating."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the archaic cosmic geography (day-journeys of the sun, encircling earth/ocean, the razor-thin gap) and the elevation of Vāyu as the soul's vehicle and the unity of part and whole."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇas 3.2–3.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["These two rounds move the contest from ritual (3.1) toward <strong>death, continuity, and the cosmic life-principle</strong>:","1. <strong>The mechanism of bondage (3.2.1–9):</strong> eight <strong>grasper↔over-grasper</strong> pairs (organ↔object, plus mind↔desire and hands↔action) map exactly how the self is \"caught\" in the world — the diagnosis that <em>vairāgya</em> and <em>pratyāhāra</em> address.","2. <strong>Death is not absolute (3.2.10):</strong> even death (fire) is \"eaten\" (by water); the <strong>name</strong> alone does not leave at death (3.2.12); the gross breaths dissolve \"here,\" the body bloating (3.2.11).","3. <strong>The karma doctrine (3.2.13):</strong> the single most historically important moment — when all parts return to their sources, what continues is the self bearing its <strong>karma</strong>: <em>\"one becomes good by good action, bad by bad.\"</em> Taught privately, as the new esoteric key to post-mortem destiny (elaborated at 4.4).","4. <strong>The cosmic carrier (3.3):</strong> the souls of even the greatest ritualists are borne by <strong>Vāyu</strong>, the wind that is <strong>both each individual life and the total life (vyaṣṭi = samaṣṭi)</strong> — knowing which conquers re-death.","The drift is unmistakable: ritual and its heavens are real but <strong>time-bound and karma-bound</strong>; what matters is the deathless principle (the wind/prāṇa, and beyond it the Self) and the moral law of action that governs continuity. The next challengers (Uṣasta, Kahola) will press straight for <strong>the Self within all</strong>."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇas 3.2–3.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE GRASPERS (BU 3.2.1-9)\n   =========================\n   GRAHA (organ) &lt;==seizes==&gt; ATIGRAHA (object), and is held by it:\n     nose↔odour · speech↔name · tongue↔taste · eye↔form · ear↔sound\n     mind↔DESIRE · hands↔ACTION · skin↔touch         (8 pairs = full bondage)\n\n   DEATH &amp; CONTINUITY (3.2.10-13)\n   ------------------------------\n   all = food of DEATH ; death = FIRE = food of WATER -&gt; death not absolute\n   at death: breaths gathered &quot;here&quot; (body bloats) ; only the NAME does not leave\n   the parts return to sources:  speech-&gt;fire · breath-&gt;wind · eye-&gt;sun · mind-&gt;moon\n        ear-&gt;quarters · body-&gt;earth · self-&gt;space · hairs-&gt;herbs/trees · blood,seed-&gt;waters\n        =&gt; &quot;WHERE is the person?&quot;  --(taught in PRIVATE)--&gt;\n        ====&gt; KARMA:  &quot;puṇyo puṇyena karmaṇā, pāpaḥ pāpena&quot;\n              (good by good action, bad by bad)   [first explicit karma-doctrine]\n\n   BHUJYU (3.3): where do the great sacrificers go?\n        this world (32 sun-chariot days) -&gt; earth (x2) -&gt; ocean (x2) -&gt; razor-thin gap\n        Indra-as-hawk -&gt; hands souls to the WIND\n        =&gt; VĀYU = vyaṣṭi (each) AND samaṣṭi (all)  -&gt; conquers re-death\n                                            |\n                                            v   (defeats 2 and 3 of 9)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇas 3.2–3.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Watch the grasping</strong> (3.2). In daily perception, notice the double hook: your organ seizing an object <em>and</em> the object gripping you back through craving. Loosening the <em>atigraha</em> (the object's hold) — not destroying the senses — is the practical work of freedom.","2. <strong>Include mind and hands.</strong> Bondage is not only sensory: <em>mind↔desire</em> and <em>hands↔action</em> are graspers too. Watch desire and deed as carefully as sight and sound.","3. <strong>Take karma seriously</strong> (3.2.13). The one teaching Yājñavalkya would only give in private: <em>you become what you do.</em> Let the gravity of this shape each choice — good action makes a good self, bad a bad one. (And remember its sequel: karma forges destiny, but only knowledge frees.)","4. <strong>Don't fear death as a final emptying.</strong> The parts return to their cosmic sources; the breaths dissolve \"here\"; what continues is governed by a law, not by chance. Death is a transition within a moral cosmos.","5. <strong>Find the one Life in your breath</strong> (3.3). Contemplate the wind/breath as both <em>your</em> single life (vyaṣṭi) and the <em>one</em> cosmic life (samaṣṭi). Identity with the total Life loosens the terror of individual death.","6. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the senses bind, action determines continuity, and the cosmic life carries all — but every clue here points beyond itself to the <strong>Self</strong> that the next rounds will name directly."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3 · Brāhmaṇa 3.4 — Uṣasta: the Self that cannot be objectified","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 3.4","title":"Uṣasta: the Self that cannot be objectified","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The fourth challenger,</em> <strong>Uṣasta Cākrāyaṇa</strong>, <em>demands the</em> <strong>Brahman that is \"immediate and direct\" (sākṣād aparokṣāt) — the Self within all (sarvāntara).</strong> <em>When Yājñavalkya points to it as \"the one who breathes by your breath,\" Uṣasta objects that this only</em> describes <em>it indirectly. Yājñavalkya then gives the decisive teaching: the Self can never be made an object —</em> <strong>\"you cannot see the seer of seeing, nor know the knower of knowing\"</strong> <em>— for it is the eternal Subject of all knowing. (A compact restatement of 2.4.14.)</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.4.1","title":"\"The Self within all\": the breather of the breath","sa":["अथ हैनमुषस्तश्चाक्रायणः पप्रच्छ । याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच — यत्साक्षादपरोक्षाद्ब्रह्म य आत्मा सर्वान्तरस्तं मे व्याचक्ष्वेति । एष त आत्मा सर्वान्तरः । यः प्राणेन प्राणिति स त आत्मा सर्वान्तरः । योऽपानेनापानिति स त आत्मा सर्वान्तरः । यो व्यानेन व्यनिति स त आत्मा सर्वान्तरः । य उदानेनोदनिति स त आत्मा सर्वान्तरः । एष त आत्मा सर्वान्तरः ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha hainam uṣastaś cākrāyaṇaḥ papraccha | yājñavalkyeti hovāca -- yat sākṣād aparokṣād brahma ya ātmā sarvāntaras taṃ me vyācakṣveti | eṣa ta ātmā sarvāntaraḥ | yaḥ prāṇena prāṇiti sa ta ātmā sarvāntaraḥ | yo 'pānenāpāniti sa ta ātmā sarvāntaraḥ | yo vyānena vyaniti sa ta ātmā sarvāntaraḥ | ya udānenodaniti sa ta ātmā sarvāntaraḥ | eṣa ta ātmā sarvāntaraḥ || 1 ||"],"words":[["yat sākṣāt aparokṣāt brahma","n. nom. + indecl. (directly, immediately)","the Brahman that is immediate and direct (not mediated, not remote)"],["yaḥ ātmā sarva-antaraḥ taṃ me vyācakṣva iti","m. nom. + adj. (innermost of all) + impv. of vi-ā-cakṣ","the Self that is within all — explain that to me"],["eṣaḥ te ātmā sarva-antaraḥ","m. nom. + dat./gen.","\"this is your Self, the one within all"],["yaḥ prāṇena prāṇiti saḥ te ātmā sarvāntaraḥ","rel. + instr. + 3 sg. pres.","he who breathes by the out-breath (prāṇa) is your Self within all"],["yaḥ apānena apāniti … vyānena vyaniti … udānena udaniti","instr. + pres.","(so) he who in-breathes by apāna, diffuses by vyāna, up-breathes by udāna"],["eṣaḥ te ātmā sarvāntaraḥ","","this is your Self, the one within all\""]],"lit":"\"Then Uṣasta Cākrāyaṇa questioned him: 'Yājñavalkya, explain to me the Brahman that is immediate and direct — the Self that is within all.' (Yājñavalkya:) 'This is your Self, the one within all.' 'Which one is within all, Yājñavalkya?' 'He who breathes by the out-breath is your Self within all; he who breathes in by the in-breath… who diffuses by the diffused breath… who breathes up by the up-breath — he is your Self, the one within all.'\"","para":"Uṣasta wants the <strong>directly-present Brahman</strong> — not a symbol or a remote deity, but the <strong>innermost Self</strong> itself. Yājñavalkya first points to it through its <strong>functions</strong>: the Self is <em>that which breathes through every breath</em> (prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna) — the conscious power behind the vital activities. But (next mantra) Uṣasta finds this still too <em>indirect</em>.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>sākṣād aparokṣāt brahma</em> = Brahman as the <strong>immediately-evident inner Self</strong>, not inferred or distant. Yājñavalkya first indicates it as the <em>animator of the breaths</em> — the conscious agent without which breathing (and all function) is impossible."],["Sureśvara","the Self is \"within all\" as the inmost witness of every activity; pointing through the breaths is a first approximation to be refined."],["Krishnananda","Uṣasta wants the Self shown <em>as itself</em>, not as something out there; Yājñavalkya begins with what is nearest — the breather behind the breath — the conscious presence in your very respiration."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the Self is <em>sarvāntara</em> — interior to everything, the indweller; sensed first as the life behind the breath."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes <em>sākṣād aparokṣāt</em> (\"directly and without mediation\") as the demand for the Self known immediately, not by symbol."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.4.2","title":"You cannot see the Seer of seeing","sa":["स होवाचोषस्तश्चाक्रायणः — यथा वै ब्रूयादसौ गौरसावश्व इत्येवमेवैतद्व्यपदिष्टं भवति । यदेव साक्षादपरोक्षाद्ब्रह्म य आत्मा सर्वान्तरस्तं मे व्याचक्ष्वेति । एष त आत्मा सर्वान्तरः । कतमो याज्ञवल्क्य सर्वान्तरः । न दृष्टेर्द्रष्टारं पश्येः । न श्रुतेः श्रोतारं शृणुयाः । न मतेर्मन्तारं मन्वीथाः । न विज्ञातेर्विज्ञातारं विजानीयाः । एष त आत्मा सर्वान्तरः । अतोऽन्यदार्तम् । ततो होषस्तश्चाक्रायण उपरराम ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovācoṣastaś cākrāyaṇaḥ -- yathā vai brūyād asau gaur asāv aśva ity evam evaitad vyapadiṣṭaṃ bhavati | yad eva sākṣād aparokṣād brahma ya ātmā sarvāntaras taṃ me vyācakṣveti | eṣa ta ātmā sarvāntaraḥ | katamo yājñavalkya sarvāntaraḥ | na dṛṣṭer draṣṭāraṃ paśyeḥ | na śruteḥ śrotāraṃ śṛṇuyāḥ | na mater mantāraṃ manvīthā | na vijñāter vijñātāraṃ vijānīyāḥ | eṣa ta ātmā sarvāntaraḥ | ato 'nyad ārtam | tato hoṣastaś cākrāyaṇa upararāma || 2 ||"],"words":[["yathā vai brūyāt asau gauḥ asau aśvaḥ iti","opt. of brū + nom.","\"just as one might say 'that is a cow, that is a horse,'"],["evam eva etat vyapadiṣṭaṃ bhavati","n. nom. pp. (pointed out)","so this has (only) been pointed out (indirectly)"],["katamaḥ yājñavalkya sarva-antaraḥ","interrog.","which one, Yājñavalkya, is the Self within all?\""],["na dṛṣṭeḥ draṣṭāraṃ paśyeḥ","gen. (of seeing) + acc. (the seer) + 2 sg. opt. of paś","you cannot see the seer of seeing"],["na śruteḥ śrotāraṃ śṛṇuyāḥ","gen. + acc. + opt. of śru","you cannot hear the hearer of hearing"],["na mateḥ mantāraṃ manvīthāḥ","gen. + acc. + opt. of man","you cannot think the thinker of thinking"],["na vijñāteḥ vijñātāraṃ vijānīyāḥ","gen. + acc. + opt. of vi-jñā","you cannot know the knower of knowing"],["eṣaḥ te ātmā sarva-antaraḥ","","he is your Self, the one within all"],["ataḥ anyat ārtam","n. nom. (wretched, perishable)","anything other than this is wretched (subject to suffering)"],["tataḥ ha uṣastaḥ cākrāyaṇaḥ upararāma","pf.","thereupon Uṣasta Cākrāyaṇa fell silent"]],"lit":"\"Uṣasta Cākrāyaṇa said, 'Just as one might say \"that is a cow, that is a horse,\" so this has (only) been <em>pointed at</em> (indirectly). Explain to me the Brahman that is immediate and direct, the Self within all!' (Yājñavalkya:) 'This is your Self, the one within all.' 'Which one is within all, Yājñavalkya?' '<strong>You cannot see the seer of seeing; you cannot hear the hearer of hearing; you cannot think the thinker of thinking; you cannot know the knower of knowing.</strong> He is your Self, the one within all. Anything other than this is wretched (perishable, sorrow-bound).' Thereupon Uṣasta Cākrāyaṇa fell silent.\"","para":"Uṣasta protests that describing the Self <em>via its functions</em> (the breather of breath) is still only <strong>pointing at it from outside</strong> — like identifying a cow or horse — not showing it <strong>as itself</strong>. Yājñavalkya's reply is the heart of the matter: the Self <strong>cannot be objectified at all</strong>, because it is the <strong>eternal Subject</strong> of every cognition — \"<strong>you cannot see the seer of seeing… know the knower of knowing.</strong>\" To seek to perceive it as a thing is impossible, for it is <em>that by which all perceiving happens</em>. It is your own innermost Self; <strong>all else — everything objectifiable — is <em>ārta</em>, \"wretched,\" perishable and sorrow-bound.</strong> Uṣasta, satisfied (and stopped), falls silent.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <strong>self-luminous witness</strong> (<em>sākṣin</em>) cannot become an object of its own knowing — as fire does not burn itself, the eye does not see itself. The Self is <em>aparokṣa</em> (immediate) precisely <em>because</em> it is the ever-present subject, not because it can be exhibited like a cow. <em>ato 'nyad ārtam</em> — whatever is <em>not</em> this Self (i.e., all objects) is perishable and a source of suffering; only the witnessing Self is the imperishable refuge. A foundational text (with 2.4.14) for the doctrine of the self-revealing Ātman."],["Sureśvara","the four negations (seer/hearer/thinker/knower) close every route by which one might try to objectify the Self; what remains is the indubitable subject. \"All else is wretched\" marks the Self alone as the non-perishable."],["Krishnananda","\"The eye cannot see itself; the Self cannot be known as an object because it is the very principle of knowing.\" This is not a limitation but the Self's glory — it is <em>you</em>, the knower, never a known thing. Everything you <em>can</em> objectify is <em>ārta</em> — destined to pass and to grieve."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical seal — stop trying to \"experience\" the Self as an object; <em>be</em> the witnessing subject. Whatever can be experienced is not it (and is perishable)."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle compares this to 2.4.14 and the Western insight that the subject of knowledge cannot be its own object; <em>ārta</em> (\"stricken, wretched\") names the perishable, sorrow-prone status of everything other than the witnessing Self."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3 · Brāhmaṇa 3.5 — Kahola: the Self beyond hunger and sorrow; renunciation, bālya, and the muni","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 3.5","title":"Kahola: the Self beyond hunger and sorrow; renunciation, bālya, and the muni","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The fifth challenger,</em> <strong>Kahola Kauṣītakeya</strong>, <em>asks the same question. Yājñavalkya names the Self as that which</em> <strong>transcends hunger, thirst, sorrow, delusion, old age, and death</strong> — <em>and draws the practical consequence: knowers of this Self</em> <strong>renounce the three cravings (for sons, wealth, worlds)</strong> <em>and live as mendicants, passing from learning (pāṇḍitya) through child-like simplicity (bālya) to silent sagehood (muni) and finally to the true brāhmaṇa.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.5.1","title":"The Self beyond all wants; the path of renunciation","sa":["अथ हैनं कहोलः कौषीतकेयः पप्रच्छ — याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच । यदेव साक्षादपरोक्षाद्ब्रह्म य आत्मा सर्वान्तरस्तं मे व्याचक्ष्वेति । एष त आत्मा सर्वान्तरः । कतमो याज्ञवल्क्य सर्वान्तरः । योऽशनायापिपासे शोकं मोहं जरां मृत्युमत्येति । एतं वै तमात्मानं विदित्वा ब्राह्मणाः पुत्रैषणायाश्च वित्तैषणायाश्च लोकैषणायाश्च व्युत्थायाथ भिक्षाचर्यं चरन्ति । या ह्येव पुत्रैषणा सा वित्तैषणा या वित्तैषणा सा लोकैषणा । उभे ह्येते एषणे एव भवतः । तस्माद्ब्राह्मणः पाण्डित्यं निर्विद्य बाल्येन तिष्ठासेत् । बाल्यं च पाण्डित्यं च निर्विद्याथ मुनिः । अमौनं च मौनं च निर्विद्याथ ब्राह्मणः । स ब्राह्मणः केन स्याद्येन स्यात्तेनेदृश एव । अतोऽन्यदार्तम् । ततो ह कहोलः कौषीतकेय उपरराम ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ kaholaḥ kauṣītakeyaḥ papraccha -- yājñavalkyeti hovāca | yad eva sākṣād aparokṣād brahma ya ātmā sarvāntaras taṃ me vyācakṣveti | eṣa ta ātmā sarvāntaraḥ | katamo yājñavalkya sarvāntaraḥ | yo 'śanāyāpipāse śokaṃ mohaṃ jarāṃ mṛtyum atyeti | etaṃ vai tam ātmānaṃ viditvā brāhmaṇāḥ putraiṣaṇāyāś ca vittaiṣaṇāyāś ca lokaiṣaṇāyāś ca vyutthāyātha bhikṣācaryaṃ caranti | yā hy eva putraiṣaṇā sā vittaiṣaṇā yā vittaiṣaṇā sā lokaiṣaṇā | ubhe hy ete eṣaṇe eva bhavataḥ | tasmād brāhmaṇaḥ pāṇḍityaṃ nirvidya bālyena tiṣṭhāset | bālyaṃ ca pāṇḍityaṃ ca nirvidyātha muniḥ | amaunaṃ ca maunaṃ ca nirvidyātha brāhmaṇaḥ | sa brāhmaṇaḥ kena syād yena syāt tenedṛśa eva | ato 'nyad ārtam | tato ha kaholaḥ kauṣītakeya upararāma || 1 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ aśanāyā-pipāse śokaṃ mohaṃ jarāṃ mṛtyum atyeti","acc. (du. + sg.) + 3 sg. pres. of ati-i","the Self transcends hunger and thirst, sorrow and delusion, old age and death"],["etaṃ vai tam ātmānaṃ viditvā brāhmaṇāḥ","acc. + ger. of vid + nom. pl.","knowing this very Self, the brāhmaṇas"],["putra-eṣaṇāyāḥ ca vitta-eṣaṇāyāḥ ca loka-eṣaṇāyāḥ ca vyutthāya","abl. (cravings) + ger. of vi-ud-sthā","rising up above the craving for sons, for wealth, and for worlds,"],["atha bhikṣā-caryaṃ caranti","acc. + 3 pl. pres.","then lead the life of mendicants"],["yā hi eva putra-eṣaṇā sā vitta-eṣaṇā","rel. f.","for the craving for sons is the craving for wealth,"],["yā vitta-eṣaṇā sā loka-eṣaṇā","","and the craving for wealth is the craving for worlds"],["ubhe hi ete eṣaṇe eva bhavataḥ","f. nom. du.","for both of these are simply \"cravings\""],["tasmāt brāhmaṇaḥ pāṇḍityaṃ nirvidya bālyena tiṣṭhāset","n. acc. (erudition) + ger. of nir-vid + instr. (child-state) + opt. desid. of sthā","therefore, having done with erudition, let the brāhmaṇa abide in child-like (simplicity)"],["bālyaṃ ca pāṇḍityaṃ ca nirvidya atha muniḥ","ger. + m. nom.","having done with both childlikeness and erudition, then (he is) a muni (silent sage)"],["amaunaṃ ca maunaṃ ca nirvidya atha brāhmaṇaḥ","acc. (non-silence) + (silence) + nom.","having done with both non-meditativeness and meditativeness, then (he is a true) brāhmaṇa"],["saḥ brāhmaṇaḥ kena syāt yena syāt tena īdṛśaḥ eva","instr.","by what means is he such a brāhmaṇa? by whatever means — he is just such"],["ataḥ anyat ārtam","n. nom.","anything other than this is wretched"],["tataḥ ha kaholaḥ kauṣītakeyaḥ upararāma","pf.","thereupon Kahola Kauṣītakeya fell silent"]],"lit":"\"Then Kahola Kauṣītakeya questioned him: 'Yājñavalkya, explain to me the Brahman that is immediate and direct, the Self within all.' 'This is your Self, the one within all.' 'Which is within all, Yājñavalkya?' '<strong>The one that transcends hunger and thirst, sorrow and delusion, old age and death.</strong> Knowing this very Self, the brāhmaṇas rise above the craving for sons, the craving for wealth, and the craving for worlds, and live the life of mendicants. For the craving for sons is the craving for wealth, and the craving for wealth is the craving for worlds — both are simply cravings. Therefore, having finished with erudition (<em>pāṇḍitya</em>), let the brāhmaṇa abide in child-like simplicity (<em>bālya</em>); having finished with both child-likeness and erudition, (he becomes) a <strong>muni</strong> (silent sage); having finished with both non-silence and silence, then (he is a true) <strong>brāhmaṇa</strong>. By what means is he such a brāhmaṇa? By whatever means — he is just such. Anything other than this is wretched.' Thereupon Kahola Kauṣītakeya fell silent.\"","para":"The Self is now characterised by <strong>negation of all wants and changes</strong>: it is <strong>beyond hunger, thirst</strong> (the body's needs), <strong>sorrow, delusion</strong> (the mind's afflictions), and <strong>old age, death</strong> (the body's fate) — the changeless, sorrow-free, deathless ground. Knowing it, the brāhmaṇas <strong>renounce the three great cravings</strong> — for <strong>progeny, wealth, and worlds</strong> (<em>putra-, vitta-, loka-eṣaṇā</em>, shown to be really one craving) — and take up <strong>mendicancy (bhikṣā-carya)</strong>, the life of the wandering renunciant. The famous developmental sequence follows: <strong>pāṇḍitya</strong> (mastery of learning) → <strong>bālya</strong> (child-like innocence/non-self-display) → <strong>muni</strong> (the silent, meditative sage) → the <strong>true brāhmaṇa</strong> (who has transcended even the distinction of \"silence and non-silence\"). All else but this Self is <em>ārta</em> — wretched and perishable.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Self is defined by its <strong>freedom from the six \"waves\" of existence</strong> (hunger/thirst = body; sorrow/delusion = mind; age/death = the life-cycle). Knowing it, one naturally renounces the cravings (which are all one craving, since sons are wanted for wealth and wealth for worlds). The ladder <strong>pāṇḍitya→bālya→mauna→brāhmaṇa</strong>: scholarship is needed but must be <em>transcended</em> into child-like guilelessness (<em>bālya</em> = inner purity/absence of egotistic display, not childishness), and that into the silent realisation of the <em>muni</em>, and finally even the \"muni/non-muni\" opposition is dropped — the realised brāhmaṇa simply <em>is</em>. This is Śaṅkara's classic text for <strong>renunciation as the fruit of knowledge</strong> and the stages of the knower."],["Sureśvara","the unity of the three <em>eṣaṇās</em> (one craving under three forms) shows that renunciation is single and total; <em>bālya</em> he glosses as freedom from the conceit of learning — inner simplicity, not ignorance."],["Krishnananda","the Self is \"that in which there is no hunger, no thirst, no grief\"; to know it is to be done with the craving-knot (son→wealth→world is one chain). The progression is profound: become learned, then become <em>as a child</em> (drop the parade of learning), then <em>silent</em> (drop even childlikeness), then a real knower who has dropped the very distinction — \"established in Being, beyond all roles.\""],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the scriptural charter of <strong>saṃnyāsa</strong>: knowledge of the desireless Self issues in the abandonment of the three cravings and the mendicant life; <em>bālya</em> and <em>mauna</em> describe the inner ripening of the renunciate."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the <em>three eṣaṇās</em> and the <em>pāṇḍitya–bālya–mauna</em> sequence as a classic statement of the renunciatory ideal; the realised brāhmaṇa is beyond all prescribed means (\"by whatever means, he is just such\")."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇas 3.4–3.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["These two rounds turn from cosmology to the <strong>direct disclosure of the Self</strong> — first negatively (it cannot be objectified), then by its freedom from all wants:","1. <strong>The Self is the unobjectifiable Subject (3.4).</strong> Uṣasta's demand for the \"immediate, direct\" Brahman is met by the four great negations — <em>you cannot see the seer of seeing, hear the hearer of hearing, think the thinker of thinking, know the knower of knowing.</em> The Self is the <strong>self-luminous witness</strong>, never a thing among things; <strong>all else is <em>ārta</em> (perishable, wretched).</strong> (This and 2.4.14 are the twin proof-texts for the witness-doctrine.)","2. <strong>The Self is free of all wants and changes (3.5).</strong> It transcends <strong>hunger, thirst, sorrow, delusion, age, death</strong> — the changeless, sorrowless, deathless ground. Knowing it dissolves the <strong>three cravings (sons, wealth, worlds)</strong>, which are really one; hence the knower <strong>renounces</strong> and lives as a mendicant, ripening through <strong>pāṇḍitya → bālya → mauna → brāhmaṇa.</strong>","Together they give both the <strong>epistemic</strong> truth (the Self is the subject, not an object) and the <strong>existential</strong> consequence (knowing it ends craving and issues in renunciation). The next challenger, <strong>Gārgī</strong>, will press the cosmological question — <em>on what is everything woven?</em> — toward the same unconditioned ground."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇas 3.4–3.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE SELF WITHIN ALL (BU 3.4-3.5)\n   ================================\n   USHASTA (3.4): &quot;show me the immediate, direct Brahman — the Self within all&quot;\n        Y: it is the breather of your breath ... &quot;no, that&#x27;s just pointing (cow/horse)&quot;\n        Y: ===&gt;  you cannot SEE the seer of seeing\n                 you cannot HEAR the hearer of hearing\n                 you cannot THINK the thinker of thinking\n                 you cannot KNOW the knower of knowing\n             =&gt; the SELF = the unobjectifiable SUBJECT ;  all else = ārta (wretched/perishable)\n\n   KAHOLA (3.5): &quot;which is the Self within all?&quot;\n        Y: that which TRANSCENDS  hunger·thirst (body) | sorrow·delusion (mind) | age·death (life)\n             =&gt; knowers RENOUNCE the 3 cravings:  sons -&gt; wealth -&gt; worlds  (really ONE craving)\n                and live as mendicants (bhikṣā-carya)\n             =&gt; ripening:  PĀṆḌITYA (learning) -&gt; BĀLYA (child-like simplicity)\n                           -&gt; MUNI (silence) -&gt; the true BRĀHMAṆA (beyond silence/non-silence)\n                                            |\n                                            v   (defeats 4 and 5 of 9)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇas 3.4–3.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Stop objectifying the Self</strong> (3.4). In meditation, notice every attempt to \"find\" or \"experience\" the Self as some object, state, or feeling — and drop it. You are the seer; you cannot stand back and see the seer. Rest <em>as</em> the witnessing subject, not as a seeker of an inner object.","2. <strong>Test by perishability.</strong> Whatever you can perceive, think, or know is <em>ārta</em> — perishable and sorrow-prone. Use this as a filter: \"if I can objectify it, it is not the Self.\" What remains un-objectifiable — the knower — is the deathless.","3. <strong>Locate the Self by negation</strong> (3.5). Contemplate that which is <em>not</em> touched by hunger, thirst, sorrow, delusion, age, or death — the unchanging awareness in which these states come and go. Identify with that, not with the states.","4. <strong>See the three cravings as one</strong> (3.5). Watch how the wish for relationships, security, and standing (sons, wealth, worlds) is a single craving wearing three masks. Seeing its unity loosens all three at once.","5. <strong>Walk the ladder rightly.</strong> Gain real understanding (<em>pāṇḍitya</em>) — but then set down the <em>display</em> of learning into child-like simplicity (<em>bālya</em>); let that deepen into inner silence (<em>mauna</em>); and finally hold no role at all. Learning is a boat to be used and left, not a possession to parade.","6. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the Self is nearer than the nearest — it is the very one now reading and knowing these words; it cannot be reached because it is never absent. Knowing this, the heart's cravings quiet of themselves."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3 · Brāhmaṇa 3.6 — Gārgī's first interrogation: the warp and woof","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 3.6","title":"Gārgī's first interrogation: the warp and woof","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The sixth challenger is the woman philosopher</em> <strong>Gārgī Vācaknavī</strong>, <em>one of the Upaniṣad's most formidable interlocutors. She presses a single relentless question —</em> <strong>\"on what is X woven, warp and woof (ota and prota)?\"</strong> — <em>climbing a ladder of ever-subtler \"worlds\": water → air → the mid-region → the gandharvas → sun → moon → stars → gods → Indra → Prajāpati → the brahma-worlds. When she asks on what the brahma-worlds themselves are woven, Yājñavalkya halts her:</em> <strong>\"Gārgī, do not over-question, lest your head fall off.\"</strong> <em>The regress must stop at the unconditioned ground, which cannot be made the object of a further \"on what?\"</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.6.1","title":"The ascending regress, and the warning","sa":["अथ हैनं गार्गी वाचक्नवी पप्रच्छ — याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच । यदिदं सर्वमप्स्वोतं च प्रोतं च कस्मिन्नु खल्वाप ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । वायौ गार्गीति । कस्मिन्नु खलु वायुरोतश्च प्रोतश्चेति । अन्तरिक्षलोकेषु गार्गीति । कस्मिन्नु खल्वन्तरिक्षलोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । गन्धर्वलोकेषु गार्गीति । कस्मिन्नु खलु गन्धर्वलोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । आदित्यलोकेषु गार्गीति । कस्मिन्नु खल्वादित्यलोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । चन्द्रलोकेषु गार्गीति । कस्मिन्नु खलु चन्द्रलोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । नक्षत्रलोकेषु गार्गीति । कस्मिन्नु खलु नक्षत्रलोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । देवलोकेषु गार्गीति । कस्मिन्नु खलु देवलोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । इन्द्रलोकेषु गार्गीति । कस्मिन्नु खल्विन्द्रलोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । प्रजापतिलोकेषु गार्गीति । कस्मिन्नु खलु प्रजापतिलोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । ब्रह्मलोकेषु गार्गीति । कस्मिन्नु खलु ब्रह्मलोका ओताश्च प्रोताश्चेति । स होवाच — गार्गि मातिप्राक्षीः । मा ते मूर्धा व्यपप्तत् । अनतिप्रश्न्यां वै देवतामतिपृच्छसि । गार्गि मातिप्राक्षीरिति । ततो ह गार्गी वाचक्नव्युपरराम ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ gārgī vācaknavī papraccha -- yājñavalkyeti hovāca | yad idaṃ sarvam apsv otaṃ ca protaṃ ca kasmin nu khalv āpa otāś ca protāś ceti | vāyau gārgīti | kasmin nu khalu vāyur otaś ca protaś ceti | antarikṣalokeṣu gārgīti | kasmin nu khalv antarikṣalokā otāś ca protāś ceti | gandharvalokeṣu gārgīti | kasmin nu khalu gandharvalokā otāś ca protāś ceti | adityalokeṣu gārgīti | … candralokeṣu … nakṣatralokeṣu … devalokeṣu … indralokeṣu … prajāpatilokeṣu … brahmalokeṣu gārgīti | kasmin nu khalu brahmalokā otāś ca protāś ceti | sa hovāca -- gārgi mātiprākṣīḥ | mā te mūrdhā vyapaptat | anatipraśnyāṃ vai devatām atipṛcchasi | gārgi mātiprākṣīr iti | tato ha gārgī vācaknavy upararāma || 1 ||"],"words":[["atha ha enaṃ gārgī vācaknavī papraccha","f. nom. + 3 sg. pf.","then Gārgī, daughter of Vacaknu, questioned him"],["yat idaṃ sarvam apsu otaṃ ca protaṃ ca","n. nom. + loc. + pp. (woven across & along)","\"this whole (world) is woven, warp and woof, on the waters;"],["kasmin nu khalu āpaḥ otāḥ ca protāḥ ca iti","loc. interrog. + f. nom. pl.","on what, then, are the waters woven, warp and woof?\""],["vāyau gārgi iti","loc.","\"on air, Gārgī\""],["kasmin nu khalu vāyuḥ otaḥ ca protaḥ ca iti","","\"on what is air woven?\""],["antarikṣa-lokeṣu gārgi iti","loc. pl.","\"on the worlds of the mid-region, Gārgī\""],["(… and so up:) gandharva- → āditya- → candra- → nakṣatra- → deva- → indra- → prajāpati- → brahma-lokeṣu","loc. pl.","(…the gandharva-, sun-, moon-, star-, god-, Indra-, Prajāpati-, brahma-worlds…)"],["kasmin nu khalu brahma-lokāḥ otāḥ ca protāḥ ca iti","","\"on what are the brahma-worlds woven, warp and woof?\""],["saḥ ha uvāca gārgi mā atiprākṣīḥ","proh. + 2 sg. inj. of ati-prach","he said: \"Gārgī, do not over-question!"],["mā te mūrdhā vyapaptat","proh. + nom. + 3 sg. inj. of vi-pat","lest your head fall off"],["anatipraśnyāṃ vai devatām atipṛcchasi","f. acc. (not to be over-questioned) + 2 sg. pres.","you are over-questioning about a deity (reality) not to be over-questioned"],["gārgi mā atiprākṣīḥ iti","","Gārgī, do not over-question!\""],["tataḥ ha gārgī vācaknavī upararāma","pf.","thereupon Gārgī fell silent"]],"lit":"\"Then Gārgī Vācaknavī questioned him: 'Yājñavalkya, since this whole (world) is woven, warp and woof, upon the <strong>waters</strong> — on what, then, are the waters woven, warp and woof?' 'On <strong>air</strong>, Gārgī.' 'On what is air woven?' 'On the worlds of the <strong>mid-region</strong>, Gārgī.' 'On what are those woven?' 'On the worlds of the <strong>gandharvas</strong>…' — (and so up through) the worlds of the <strong>sun</strong>, the <strong>moon</strong>, the <strong>stars</strong>, the <strong>gods</strong>, <strong>Indra</strong>, <strong>Prajāpati</strong>, and the <strong>brahma-worlds</strong>. 'On what, then, are the brahma-worlds woven, warp and woof?' He said: '<strong>Gārgī, do not over-question, lest your head fall off! You are over-questioning about a reality (a \"deity\") that must not be over-questioned. Gārgī, do not over-question.</strong>' Thereupon Gārgī fell silent.\"","para":"Gārgī's method is the <strong>regress of grounds</strong>: each level of reality is \"woven\" (supported, pervaded — <em>ota</em> = woven across the warp, <em>prota</em> = woven along) upon a subtler level. She climbs from <strong>water → air → mid-region → gandharva-worlds → sun → moon → stars → gods → Indra → Prajāpati → brahma-worlds</strong>, each \"woven\" on the next. But when she asks <em>on what the brahma-worlds (the highest worlds) are woven</em> — i.e., demands a ground <em>beyond</em> the highest conditioned reality — Yājñavalkya <strong>stops her</strong>: that ground is the <strong>unconditioned Brahman</strong>, which is <strong>\"not to be over-questioned\" (anatipraśnya)</strong> — one cannot ask \"on what is <em>it</em> woven?\", for it is the final support of all, woven on nothing else. To press the \"on what?\" beyond it is a category-error; the warning <em>\"lest your head fall off\"</em> marks the limit of the legitimate question. Gārgī, recognising the limit, <strong>falls silent</strong> (here defeated, but she will return in 3.8 with a worthier question).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the ascending \"woven on\" series traces the chain of <strong>dependent supports</strong> (effects resting on subtler causes) up to the <em>brahma-loka</em> (Hiraṇyagarbha's realm) — the highest <em>conditioned</em> reality. To ask \"on what is the <em>unconditioned</em> ground woven?\" is illegitimate: Brahman is the <strong>substratum of all, itself supported by nothing</strong>, and so <strong>not an object of the \"on what?\" question.</strong> \"Your head will fall off\" is not a literal threat (though framed as the debate-stake) but marks the <strong>boundary of valid inquiry</strong> — beyond the support-regress lies that which <em>grounds</em> questioning and cannot be grounded in turn. Brahman is <em>anatipraśnya</em> — known by <em>neti neti</em> (3.8), not by locating its support."],["Sureśvara","the regress must terminate in a <strong>self-grounded</strong> reality, on pain of infinite regress; the \"do not over-question\" enforces that the ultimate cannot be treated as one more <em>supported</em> term."],["Krishnananda","Gārgī's brilliant logic — everything rests on something subtler — is sound until it reaches the <strong>Absolute</strong>, which is not \"in\" or \"on\" anything because it is the ground of \"in\" and \"on\" themselves. To demand its support is to mistake the foundationless Foundation for an item in the series. The warning protects her from the absurdity (and the \"loss of head\" = loss of the questioning intellect that oversteps its scope)."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a lesson on the <strong>limits of discursive inquiry</strong>: reason can climb the ladder of causes but cannot objectify the ultimate ground; there, questioning yields to realisation. (Note Gārgī is honoured, not dismissed — she returns in 3.8.)"],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the <em>ota-prota</em> (\"warp and woof\") weaving metaphor and the \"head shattering\" as the formulaic stake of the <em>brahmodya</em> contest; the regress-and-halt dramatises that the Absolute is the unsupported support, beyond the \"on what?\" question (developed in Gārgī's second round)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This short but pointed round stages the <strong>logic and the limit of the regress of grounds</strong>:","1. <strong>The weaving metaphor.</strong> Reality is a fabric: each level \"woven, warp and woof\" (<em>ota ca prota ca</em>) upon a subtler one — water on air, air on the mid-region, and so up through the cosmic worlds to the <strong>brahma-worlds</strong>, the highest conditioned reality. This is the cosmological intuition that <em>everything depends on something subtler</em>.","2. <strong>The illegitimate over-question.</strong> When Gārgī asks for the ground <em>of the highest ground itself</em>, she oversteps: the <strong>unconditioned Brahman is the support of all and is supported by nothing.</strong> To ask \"on what is <em>it</em> woven?\" treats the foundationless Foundation as one more dependent term — a category-error. Hence <em>anatipraśnya</em>, \"not to be over-questioned.\"","3. <strong>The boundary of inquiry.</strong> \"Lest your head fall off\" (the debate-stake, and the symbol of the intellect over-reaching its scope) marks where <strong>discursive questioning yields to realisation</strong>. The Absolute is not <em>found at the end of</em> the regress as one more object; it is what makes the regress possible.","Gārgī is not refuted but <strong>bounded</strong> — and the Upaniṣad will reward her: in <strong>3.8</strong> she returns with the right question (<em>on what is space itself woven?</em>), eliciting the magnificent teaching of the <strong>Imperishable (akṣara)</strong> and <em>neti neti</em>."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   GĀRGĪ&#x27;S REGRESS (BU 3.6) — &quot;on what is X woven, warp &amp; woof (ota/prota)?&quot;\n   ========================================================================\n        this world  --on--&gt;  WATER\n                       --on--&gt;  AIR\n                         --on--&gt;  mid-region worlds\n                           --on--&gt;  gandharva worlds\n                             --on--&gt;  sun worlds\n                               --on--&gt;  moon worlds\n                                 --on--&gt;  star worlds\n                                   --on--&gt;  god worlds\n                                     --on--&gt;  Indra worlds\n                                       --on--&gt;  Prajāpati worlds\n                                         --on--&gt;  BRAHMA-worlds  (highest CONDITIONED reality)\n                                           --on--&gt; ??? \n                                                    |\n        &quot;GĀRGĪ, DO NOT OVER-QUESTION — lest your head fall off&quot;\n        the unconditioned BRAHMAN is the support of all, supported by nothing\n        =&gt; it is ANATIPRAŚNYA (not an object of &quot;on what?&quot;) — known by neti neti (see 3.8)\n                                            |\n                                            v   (Gārgī falls silent — round 1; she returns in 3.8)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Trace the regress — and notice where it ends.</strong> Follow any chain of \"what does this depend on?\" inward (body on life, life on awareness…). The exercise is illuminating until you reach awareness itself, which everything depends on and which depends on nothing. There the question changes form.","2. <strong>Don't objectify the ground.</strong> The error Gārgī is stopped from making is treating the Absolute as one more <em>supported</em> thing. Whatever you can ask \"what holds this up?\" of, is not the final ground. The ground is the un-grounded — to be <em>recognised</em>, not located.","3. <strong>Honour the limit of reason.</strong> Discursive inquiry is a faithful servant up to the threshold of the unconditioned; there it must bow. \"Do not over-question\" is not anti-intellectual but a precise map of reason's reach.","4. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the deepest reality is not at the <em>end</em> of your questioning but is the silent awareness <em>within which</em> all questioning arises — the unsupported support. When the mind that climbs the ladder grows still at its top, that stillness is the answer. (Gārgī's silence here ripens into the right question in 3.8.)"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3 · Brāhmaṇa 3.7 — Uddālaka: the Thread (sūtra) and the Inner Controller (antaryāmin)","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 3.7","title":"Uddālaka: the Thread (sūtra) and the Inner Controller (antaryāmin)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The seventh challenger,</em> <strong>Uddālaka Āruṇi</strong> <em>(the teacher of the Chāndogya's tat tvam asi), relays a question once posed by the gandharva</em> <strong>Kabandha Ātharvaṇa</strong>: <em>do you know the</em> <strong>thread (sūtra)</strong> <em>on which this world, the next, and all beings are strung — and the</em> <strong>inner controller (antaryāmin)</strong> <em>who governs them all from within? Yājñavalkya answers: the thread is</em> <strong>Vāyu (air/breath)</strong>; <em>and the inner controller is the</em> <strong>Self</strong> <em>who dwells within earth, water, fire, the heavens, the sun, the senses, the mind, and all beings — controlling each from within, unknown to it, while it is His body. He is the</em> <strong>unseen Seer, the immortal Self</strong>."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.7.1","title":"The challenge: the thread and the inner controller","sa":["अथैनमुद्दालक आरुणिः पप्रच्छ — याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच । मद्रेष्ववसाम पतञ्चलस्य काप्यस्य गृहेषु यज्ञमधीयानाः । तस्यासीद्भार्या गन्धर्वगृहीता । तमपृच्छाम — कोऽसीति । सोऽब्रवीत्कबन्ध आथर्वण इति । सोऽब्रवीत्पतञ्चलं काप्यं याज्ञिकांश्च — वेत्थ नु त्वं काप्य तत्सूत्रं यस्मिन्नयं च लोकः परश्च लोकः सर्वाणि च भूतानि संदृब्धानि भवन्तीति । सोऽब्रवीत्पतञ्चलः काप्यो नाहं तद्भगवन्वेदेति । सोऽब्रवीत्पतञ्चलं काप्यं याज्ञिकांश्च । वेत्थ नु त्वं काप्य तमन्तर्यामिणं य इमं च लोकं परं च लोकं सर्वाणि च भूतान्यन्तरो यमयति । सोऽब्रवीत्पतञ्चलः काप्यो नाहं तं भगवन्वेदेति । सोऽब्रवीत्पतञ्चलं काप्यं याज्ञिकांश्च । यो वै तत्काप्य सूत्रं विद्यात्तं चान्तर्यामिणमिति स ब्रह्मवित्स लोकवित्स देववित्स वेदवित्स आत्मवित्स सर्ववित् । इति तेभ्योऽब्रवीत् । तदहं वेद । तच्चेत्त्वं याज्ञवल्क्य सूत्रमविद्वांस्तं चान्तर्यामिणं ब्रह्मगवीरुदजसे मूर्धा ते विपतिष्यति । वेद वा अहं गौतम तत्सूत्रं तं चान्तर्यामिणमिति । यो वा इदं कश्च ब्रूयाद्वेद वेदेति यथा वेत्थ तथा ब्रूहीति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["athainam uddālaka āruṇiḥ papraccha -- yājñavalkyeti hovāca | madreṣv avasāma patañcalasya kāpyasya gṛheṣu yajñam adhīyānāḥ | tasyāsīd bhāryā gandharvagṛhītā | tam apṛcchāma -- ko 'sīti | so 'bravīt kabandha ātharvaṇa iti | so 'bravīt patañcalaṃ kāpyaṃ yājñikāṃś ca -- vettha nu tvaṃ kāpya tat sūtraṃ yasminn ayaṃ ca lokaḥ paraś ca lokaḥ sarvāṇi ca bhūtāni saṃdṛbdhāni bhavantīti | so 'bravīt … nāhaṃ tad bhagavan vedeti | … vettha nu tvaṃ kāpya tam antaryāmiṇaṃ ya imaṃ ca lokaṃ paraṃ ca lokaṃ sarvāṇi ca bhūtāny antaro yamayati | … nāhaṃ taṃ bhagavan vedeti | … yo vai tat kāpya sūtraṃ vidyāt taṃ cāntaryāmiṇam iti sa brahmavit sa lokavit sa devavit sa vedavit sa ātmavit sa sarvavit | iti tebhyo 'bravīt | tad ahaṃ veda | tac cet tvaṃ yājñavalkya sūtram avidvāṃs taṃ cāntaryāmiṇaṃ brahmagavīr udajase mūrdhā te vipatiṣyati | veda vā ahaṃ gautama tat sūtraṃ taṃ cāntaryāmiṇam iti | yo vā idaṃ kaśca brūyād veda vedeti yathā vettha tathā brūhīti || 1 ||"],"words":[["atha enam uddālakaḥ āruṇiḥ papraccha","nom. + pf.","then Uddālaka Āruṇi questioned him"],["madreṣu avasāma … yajñam adhīyānāḥ","loc. + 1 pl. impf. of vas","\"we once dwelt among the Madras, studying the sacrifice (in Patañcala Kāpya's house)"],["tasya āsīt bhāryā gandharva-gṛhītā","f. nom. pp.","his wife was possessed by a gandharva"],["tam apṛcchāma kaḥ asi iti — saḥ abravīt kabandhaḥ ātharvaṇaḥ iti","","we asked, 'who are you?' — it said, 'Kabandha Ātharvaṇa'"],["vettha nu tvaṃ kāpya tat sūtraṃ","2 sg. pf. of vid + n. acc. (thread)","(it asked) 'do you, Kāpya, know that thread (sūtra)"],["yasmin ayaṃ ca lokaḥ paraḥ ca lokaḥ sarvāṇi ca bhūtāni saṃdṛbdhāni bhavanti","loc. + nom. pl. pp. (strung together)","on which this world, the next world, and all beings are strung together?'"],["vettha nu tvaṃ … tam antaryāmiṇaṃ yaḥ … antaraḥ yamayati","acc. (inner controller) + adv. + 3 sg. pres. of yam","'do you know that inner controller who governs this world, the next, and all beings from within?'"],["(Kāpya:) na aham tat / taṃ bhagavan veda iti","","(Kāpya:) 'I do not know it, sir'"],["yaḥ vai tat sūtraṃ vidyāt taṃ ca antaryāmiṇam","opt. of vid","(the gandharva:) whoever should know that thread and that inner controller —"],["saḥ brahma-vit … loka-vit … deva-vit … veda-vit … ātma-vit … sarva-vit","nom. (knower of…)","he is the knower of Brahman, of the worlds, of the gods, of the Vedas, of the Self, of all"],["tat aham veda","1 sg. pf.","(Uddālaka:) \"this (the gandharva's teaching) I know"],["tat cet tvaṃ … sūtram avidvān taṃ ca antaryāmiṇaṃ brahma-gavīḥ udajase mūrdhā te vipatiṣyati","nom. pp. (not knowing) + acc. pl. (the brahmins' cows) + 2 sg. pres. + fut. of vi-pat","if you, not knowing that thread and inner controller, drive away the brahmins' cows, your head shall fall off!\""],["veda vai aham gautama tat sūtraṃ taṃ ca antaryāmiṇam iti","1 sg. pf.","\"I do know, O Gautama, that thread and that inner controller\""],["yaḥ vā idaṃ kaḥ ca brūyāt veda veda iti yathā vettha tathā brūhi iti","opt.","\"anyone might claim 'I know, I know'; tell it as you (actually) know it\""]],"lit":"\"Then Uddālaka Āruṇi questioned him: 'Yājñavalkya, we once lived among the Madras, studying the sacrifice in the house of Patañcala Kāpya. His wife was possessed by a gandharva; we asked it, \"Who are you?\" It said, \"Kabandha Ātharvaṇa.\" It said to Patañcala Kāpya and the ritualists: \"Do you, Kāpya, know that <strong>thread</strong> on which this world, the next world, and all beings are strung together?\" Patañcala Kāpya said, \"I do not know it, sir.\" It said: \"Do you know the <strong>inner controller</strong> who governs this world, the next, and all beings from within?\" Patañcala Kāpya said, \"I do not know him, sir.\" It said: \"Whoever knows that thread and that inner controller, he is the knower of Brahman, of the worlds, of the gods, of the Vedas, of the Self — the knower of all.\" This it told them, and I know it. If you, Yājñavalkya, drive away the brahmins' cows without knowing that thread and that inner controller, your head shall fall off!' '(But) I do know, O Gautama, that thread and that inner controller.' 'Anyone might say \"I know, I know\"; tell it just as you know it.'\"","para":"Uddālaka stakes the highest claim yet: knowledge of the <strong>sūtra (thread)</strong> that strings together all the worlds and beings, and of the <strong>antaryāmin (inner controller)</strong> that governs them from within — to know which is to be the <em>knower of all</em>. He challenges Yājñavalkya (on pain of his head) to expound it not by boast but by genuine knowledge. Yājñavalkya accepts.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <strong>sūtra</strong> is the subtle cord (the cosmic prāṇa/Hiraṇyagarbha) on which all beings are strung like beads; the <strong>antaryāmin</strong> is the <strong>Self/Īśvara dwelling within and governing all</strong> — the deepest theme yet, for to know it is to be <em>sarvavit</em> (all-knowing). The gandharva-frame lends it ancient authority."],["Sureśvara","the pairing (thread + controller) distinguishes the <em>connecting</em> principle (sūtra) from the <em>governing</em> principle (antaryāmin) — both aspects of the one Self."],["Krishnananda","the <em>sūtrātman</em> (thread-self) holds the universe together as a string holds beads; the <em>antaryāmin</em> is the indwelling controller, the immanent God who directs all from within. This is the high point of the cosmological inquiry."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","knowledge of the inner ruler is knowledge of God-immanent; the challenge is to know Him directly, not by hearsay."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the <em>sūtrātman</em> concept (later central to Vedānta) and the <em>antaryāmin</em> as a key statement of divine immanence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.7.2","title":"The thread is Vāyu","sa":["स होवाच — वायुर्वै गौतम तत्सूत्रम् । वायुना वै गौतम सूत्रेणायं च लोकः परश्च लोकः सर्वाणि च भूतानि संदृब्धानि भवन्ति । तस्माद्वै गौतम पुरुषं प्रेतमाहुर्व्यस्रंसिषतास्याङ्गानीति । वायुना हि गौतम सूत्रेण संदृब्धानि भवन्तीति । एवमेवैतद्याज्ञवल्क्य । अन्तर्यामिणं ब्रूहीति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovāca -- vāyur vai gautama tat sūtram | vāyunā vai gautama sūtreṇāyaṃ ca lokaḥ paraś ca lokaḥ sarvāṇi ca bhūtāni saṃdṛbdhāni bhavanti | tasmād vai gautama puruṣaṃ pretam āhur vyasraṃsiṣatāsyāṅgānīti | vāyunā hi gautama sūtreṇa saṃdṛbdhāni bhavantīti | evam evaitad yājñavalkya | antaryāmiṇaṃ brūhīti || 2 ||"],"words":[["vāyuḥ vai gautama tat sūtram","m. nom. + voc.","\"Vāyu (air/breath), O Gautama, is that thread"],["vāyunā vai sūtreṇa ayaṃ ca lokaḥ paraḥ ca lokaḥ sarvāṇi ca bhūtāni saṃdṛbdhāni bhavanti","instr. + nom. pl. pp.","by the thread that is Vāyu this world, the next, and all beings are strung together"],["tasmāt vai puruṣaṃ pretam āhuḥ vyasraṃsiṣata asya aṅgāni iti","acc. (the dead) + 3 pl. pf. + 3 pl. aor. of vi-sraṃs","hence of a dead person they say, 'his limbs have come loose,'"],["vāyunā hi sūtreṇa saṃdṛbdhāni bhavanti iti","instr. + nom. pl.","for by the thread of Vāyu (the limbs) were held strung together\""],["evam eva etat yājñavalkya antaryāmiṇaṃ brūhi iti","acc. + impv.","\"so it is, Yājñavalkya; (now) tell the inner controller\""]],"lit":"\"He said: 'Vāyu (air/breath), O Gautama, is that thread. By the thread that is Vāyu this world, the next world, and all beings are strung together. That is why of a dead man they say, \"his limbs have come loose\" — for by the thread of Vāyu (the limbs) were held strung together.' '(Yes,) so it is, Yājñavalkya; now tell the inner controller.'\"","para":"The <strong>thread (sūtra)</strong> is <strong>Vāyu</strong> — the all-pervading air/breath that <strong>strings together</strong> the worlds and the parts of every body (as a thread strings beads). Proof: at death, when the breath-thread is withdrawn, <strong>the body \"comes apart\" / loosens</strong> (the limbs slacken). The cosmic and bodily life-air is the connecting principle. Uddālaka accepts this and presses for the deeper teaching — the <strong>inner controller</strong>.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Vāyu as the <em>sūtra</em> is the <strong>subtle pervading life-principle (sūtrātman / Hiraṇyagarbha)</strong> that holds the cosmos and the body together; the loosening of a corpse's limbs proves the thread has been withdrawn. But the <em>thread</em> is still a <em>cosmic instrument</em> — Uddālaka rightly asks for its <em>controller</em>."],["Sureśvara","the thread connects; what <em>directs</em> the thread and all it binds is the higher principle (the antaryāmin), now to be told."],["Krishnananda","Vāyu/prāṇa is the cosmic string on which all beads (beings, worlds) hang; cut it and the beads scatter (death = the loosening). Yet the string itself is moved by an inner controller."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the breath-thread holds body and cosmos; a meditation on the pervading life-air as the binding unity."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the vivid argument from the slackening corpse and the <em>sūtra = vāyu</em> identification as the cosmic connective tissue."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 3.7.3–3.7.23","title":"The Inner Controller within all","sa":["3.7.3 (earth): यः पृथिव्यां तिष्ठन्पृथिव्या अन्तरो यं पृथिवी न वेद यस्य पृथिवी शरीरं यः पृथिवीमन्तरो यमयत्येष त आत्मान्तर्याम्यमृतः ॥ ३ ॥","3.7.15 (all beings — adhibhūta): अथाधिभूतम् । यः सर्वेषु भूतेषु तिष्ठन्सर्वेभ्यो भूतेभ्योऽन्तरो यं सर्वाणि भूतानि न विदुर्यस्य सर्वाणि भूतानि शरीरं यः सर्वाणि भूतान्यन्तरो यमयत्येष त आत्मान्तर्याम्यमृत इत्यधिभूतम् ॥ १५ ॥","3.7.16 (the breath — adhyātma): अथाध्यात्मम् । यः प्राणे तिष्ठन्प्राणादन्तरो यं प्राणो न वेद यस्य प्राणः शरीरं यः प्राणमन्तरो यमयत्येष त आत्मान्तर्याम्यमृतः ॥ १६ ॥","3.7.23 (the seed; and the climax — the unseen Seer): यो रेतसि तिष्ठन्रेतसोऽन्तरो यं रेतो न वेद यस्य रेतः शरीरं यो रेतोऽन्तरो यमयत्येष त आत्मान्तर्याम्यमृतः । अदृष्टो द्रष्टाश्रुतः श्रोतामतो मन्ताविज्ञातो विज्ञाता । नान्योऽतोऽस्ति द्रष्टा नान्योऽतोऽस्ति श्रोता नान्योऽतोऽस्ति मन्ता नान्योऽतोऽस्ति विज्ञाता । एष त आत्मान्तर्याम्यमृतः । अतोऽन्यदार्तम् । ततो होद्दालक आरुणिरुपरराम ॥ २३ ॥"],"iast":["yaḥ pṛthivyāṃ tiṣṭhan pṛthivyā antaro yaṃ pṛthivī na veda yasya pṛthivī śarīraṃ yaḥ pṛthivīm antaro yamayaty eṣa ta ātmāntaryāmy amṛtaḥ || 3 ||","athādhibhūtam | yaḥ sarveṣu bhūteṣu tiṣṭhan sarvebhyo bhūtebhyo 'ntaro yaṃ sarvāṇi bhūtāni na vidur yasya sarvāṇi bhūtāni śarīraṃ yaḥ sarvāṇi bhūtāny antaro yamayaty eṣa ta ātmāntaryāmy amṛta ity adhibhūtam || 15 ||","athādhyātmam | yaḥ prāṇe tiṣṭhan prāṇād antaro yaṃ prāṇo na veda yasya prāṇaḥ śarīraṃ yaḥ prāṇam antaro yamayati eṣa ta ātmāntaryāmy amṛtaḥ || 16 ||","yo retasi tiṣṭhaṃ retaso 'ntaro yaṃ reto na veda yasya retaḥ śarīraṃ yo reto 'ntaro yamayaty eṣa ta ātmāntaryāmy amṛtaḥ | adṛṣṭo draṣṭāśrutaḥ śrotāmato mantāvijñato vijñātā | nānyo 'to 'sti draṣṭā nānyo 'to 'sti śrotā nānyo 'to 'sti mantā nānyo 'to 'sti vijñātā | eṣa ta ātmāntaryāmy amṛtaḥ | ato 'nyad ārtam | tato hoddālaka āruṇir upararāma || 23 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ pṛthivyāṃ tiṣṭhan","rel. + loc. + pres.part.","who, dwelling in the earth,"],["pṛthivyāḥ antaraḥ","abl./gen. + adj.","is within the earth (other than it),"],["yaṃ pṛthivī na veda","acc. + 3 sg. pf.","whom the earth does not know,"],["yasya pṛthivī śarīram","gen. + n. nom.","whose body the earth is,"],["yaḥ pṛthivīm antaraḥ yamayati","acc. + adv. + 3 sg. pres. of yam","who controls the earth from within —"],["eṣaḥ te ātmā antaryāmī amṛtaḥ","m. nom.","He is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal"],["adṛṣṭaḥ draṣṭā","pp. (unseen) + m. nom. (seer)","(He is) the unseen Seer,"],["aśrutaḥ śrotā","(unheard) + (hearer)","the unheard Hearer,"],["amataḥ mantā","(unthought) + (thinker)","the unthought Thinker,"],["avijñātaḥ vijñātā","(unknown) + (knower)","the unknown Knower"],["na anyaḥ ataḥ asti draṣṭā / śrotā / mantā / vijñātā","nom.","there is no other seer / hearer / thinker / knower than He"],["eṣaḥ te ātmā antaryāmī amṛtaḥ","","He is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal"],["ataḥ anyat ārtam","n. nom.","all else is wretched (perishable)"],["tataḥ ha uddālakaḥ āruṇiḥ upararāma","pf.","thereupon Uddālaka Āruṇi fell silent"]],"lit":"<strong>The loci (each follows the formula verbatim):</strong><br>\"(3.7.3:) 'He who, dwelling in the <strong>earth</strong>, is within the earth, whom the earth does not know, whose body the earth is, who controls the earth from within — <strong>He is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal.</strong>' <em>(So through water, fire, the mid-region, air, heaven, the sun, the quarters, the moon-and-stars, space, darkness, light; through all beings; and through the breath, speech, eye, ear, mind, skin, intellect, and seed.)</em> (3.7.23, climax:) 'He is the <strong>unseen Seer, the unheard Hearer, the unthought Thinker, the unknown Knower</strong>. There is no other seer than He, no other hearer, no other thinker, no other knower. He is your Self, the inner controller, the immortal. All else is wretched.' Thereupon Uddālaka Āruṇi fell silent.\"","para":"The <strong>antaryāmin</strong> (inner controller) is the <strong>one Self indwelling and governing every reality</strong> — cosmic (earth, water, fire, sky, sun, directions, moon, space, darkness, light), the totality of beings, and every bodily faculty (breath, senses, mind, intellect, seed). Four points repeat in each: (1) He <strong>dwells within</strong> it; (2) He is <strong>other than / inward to</strong> it; (3) the thing <strong>does not know</strong> Him (the controlled is unaware of its controller); (4) the thing is <strong>His body</strong>, which He <strong>rules from within (antaro yamayati)</strong>. He is the immanent God/Self — present in all, transcendent to all, the secret governor of nature and of the psyche alike. The climax names Him as the <strong>ungraspable Subject</strong>: the <strong>Seer that is never seen, the Knower that is never known</strong> (cf. 3.4.2) — there is <strong>no other</strong> seer, hearer, thinker, or knower than this one Self. All else is <em>ārta</em> — perishable. Uddālaka, fully answered, falls silent.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>antaryāmin</em> is <strong>Īśvara, the Self conditioned by the totality (the cosmic indweller)</strong> — yet ultimately the one Ātman. The four-fold formula establishes <strong>immanence + transcendence + the controlled's ignorance + the controller's lordship</strong>: God is <em>within</em> all as their inner Self, <em>other than</em> all (not their object), <em>unknown to</em> them (the eye does not know its indweller), and the <em>ruler</em> of all (nature and mind move by His governance). The climax (<em>adṛṣṭo draṣṭā…</em>) identifies this controller with the <strong>self-luminous witness</strong> of 3.4.2 — the only Seer/Knower, never itself an object. This is Śaṅkara's chief text for the <strong>antaryāmin-brāhmaṇa</strong> doctrine of divine immanence resolving into the non-dual Self."],["Sureśvara","the controlled's <em>not knowing</em> its controller exposes that the empirical world and faculties are <em>moved</em> by a Self they cannot objectify; \"no other seer than He\" secures the singularity of the witnessing subject."],["Krishnananda","a sublime vision of <strong>God immanent</strong> — the same inner Self secretly directs the sun and the eye, the cosmos and the mind, while remaining unknown to them (as the body does not know its soul). And this controller is no distant deity but <strong>your own Self</strong>, the sole Seer behind all seeing — \"there is no other.\" The whole of nature is the body of God; you are not other than that indwelling Self."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the basis of <strong>antaryāmin-upāsanā</strong> (meditation on the indwelling Lord) and of <em>Īśvara-praṇidhāna</em>; recognise the one controller within every power of nature and self, and within all beings — leading to the non-dual realisation that this controller is the Self."],["Deussen/Olivelle","the classic <em>antaryāmin</em> passage, foundational for later theism and for Rāmānuja's <em>śarīra-śarīrī</em> (body-soul) doctrine (the world as God's body). Olivelle notes the comprehensive triple sweep (adhidaiva, adhibhūta, adhyātma) and the apophatic climax linking the controller to the unobjectifiable Self."]],"notes":["<em>The</em> <strong>antaryāmin formula</strong> <em>is now applied, in identical words, to a long series of loci. The formula (template, 3.7.3):</em>","<table><tr><td></td><td>mantra</td><td>locus (= His \"body,\" which He controls from within, unknown to it)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>adhidaiva</strong></td><td>3.7.3</td><td>the <strong>earth</strong></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.4</td><td>the <strong>waters</strong></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.5</td><td><strong>fire</strong></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.6</td><td>the <strong>mid-region</strong> (antarikṣa)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.7</td><td>the <strong>air</strong> (vāyu)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.8</td><td><strong>heaven</strong> (dyu)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.9</td><td>the <strong>sun</strong> (āditya)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.10</td><td>the <strong>quarters</strong> (diśaḥ)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.11</td><td>the <strong>moon and stars</strong> (candra-tāraka)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.12</td><td><strong>space</strong> (ākāśa)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.13</td><td><strong>darkness</strong> (tamas)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.14</td><td><strong>light</strong> (tejas)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>adhibhūta</strong></td><td>3.7.15</td><td><strong>all beings</strong> (sarvāṇi bhūtāni)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>adhyātma</strong></td><td>3.7.16</td><td>the <strong>breath</strong> (prāṇa)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.17</td><td><strong>speech</strong> (vāc)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.18</td><td>the <strong>eye</strong> (cakṣus)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.19</td><td>the <strong>ear</strong> (śrotra)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.20</td><td>the <strong>mind</strong> (manas)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.21</td><td>the <strong>skin</strong> (tvac)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.22</td><td>the <strong>intellect</strong> (vijñāna)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3.7.23</td><td>the <strong>seed</strong> (retas)</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.7","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The <em>antaryāmin-brāhmaṇa</em> is the <strong>theological summit of Adhyāya 3</strong> — the fullest Upaniṣadic statement of <strong>divine immanence</strong>:","1. <strong>The thread (sūtra, 3.7.1–2).</strong> All worlds and bodies are \"strung together\" on <strong>Vāyu</strong> (the pervading life-air / <em>sūtrātman</em>); death is the loosening of this thread. The cosmos has a connective unity.","2. <strong>The inner controller (antaryāmin, 3.7.3–23).</strong> Deeper than the thread is the <strong>one Self who dwells within and governs every reality</strong> — the elements and luminaries (adhidaiva), all beings (adhibhūta), and every faculty of body and mind (adhyātma). The repeated four-fold formula fixes the relation: He is <strong>within</strong> each, <strong>other than</strong> each, <strong>unknown to</strong> each, and the <strong>ruler of</strong> each, whose <strong>body</strong> each is.","3. <strong>The immanent God is the unobjectifiable Self (3.7.23).</strong> The climax identifies this cosmic controller with the <strong>self-luminous witness</strong>: the <strong>unseen Seer, unheard Hearer, unthought Thinker, unknown Knower</strong> — \"<strong>there is no other</strong> seer, hearer, thinker, or knower than He.\" Thus immanence (the inner ruler of all) and the witness-Self (3.4.2) are <strong>one and the same</strong>. All else is <em>ārta</em>.","This brāhmaṇa supplies the metaphysical bridge between <strong>theism</strong> (God indwelling and governing the world — the seed of Rāmānuja's \"world as God's body\") and <strong>non-dualism</strong> (that God is your own witnessing Self). It prepares Gārgī's second, decisive question (3.8) about the <strong>Imperishable</strong> that is the ground even of space."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.7","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE INNER CONTROLLER (BU 3.7)\n   =============================\n   THE THREAD (sūtra) = VĀYU  -&gt; strings together all worlds &amp; bodies (beads on a string)\n        death = the thread withdrawn -&gt; &quot;his limbs come loose&quot;\n\n   THE ANTARYĀMIN — for every X, the one Self:\n        (1) DWELLS IN x   (2) is OTHER THAN x   (3) x DOES NOT KNOW him\n        (4) x is HIS BODY   -&gt;  he CONTROLS x from within (antaro yamayati)\n        =&gt; &quot;eṣa ta ātmā antaryāmy amṛtaḥ&quot;  (He is your Self, the inner controller, immortal)\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   adhidaiva: earth·water·fire·mid-region·air·heaven·sun·quarters·moon-stars·space·darkness·light\n   adhibhūta: ALL BEINGS\n   adhyātma : breath·speech·eye·ear·mind·skin·INTELLECT·seed\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   CLIMAX (3.7.23):  the UNSEEN SEER · UNHEARD HEARER · UNTHOUGHT THINKER · UNKNOWN KNOWER\n        &quot;there is NO OTHER seer / hearer / thinker / knower than He&quot;\n        =&gt; immanent GOD (controller of all)  ==  the witnessing SELF  (cf. 3.4.2)\n        all else = ārta                                |\n                                                       v   (defeat 7 of 9)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.7","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Sense the indweller everywhere.</strong> In contemplation, behind each natural power (the warmth of fire, the light of the sun, the steadiness of earth) and each faculty (the seeing in the eye, the thinking in the mind), sense the <strong>one inner controller</strong> that moves it while remaining unseen by it. The world becomes the body of an indwelling Self.","2. <strong>Notice that the controlled does not know its controller.</strong> Your eye does not see the one who sees through it; your mind does not know the one who knows through it. This gap points to the witness — <em>you cannot turn around and catch the one looking out.</em>","3. <strong>Practice antaryāmin-meditation.</strong> Worship the Lord not only \"above\" but <em>within</em> — within each element, each being, and each of your own faculties — as the secret governor. This is the heart of <em>Īśvara-praṇidhāna</em>.","4. <strong>Then collapse the distance.</strong> The final move (3.7.23): the indwelling God of all is <strong>your own Self</strong>, the sole Seer. Immanence and identity meet — \"He is <em>your</em> Self.\" Rest as that one witnessing controller.","5. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> there is only one Seer looking out through all eyes, one Knower knowing through all minds. To know this is to be \"the knower of all\" (3.7.1) — not by accumulating facts, but by recognising the single Self that secretly animates everything."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3 · Brāhmaṇa 3.8 — Gārgī's second interrogation: the Imperishable (Akṣara)","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 3.8","title":"Gārgī's second interrogation: the Imperishable (Akṣara)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Gārgī returns — not silenced but sharpened. Warning the assembly that no one will defeat Yājñavalkya if he answers, she \"rises like a warrior with two arrows\" and asks the question she could not push in 3.6:</em> <strong>on what is space itself (ākāśa) woven?</strong> <em>The answer is the Upaniṣad's most majestic description of Brahman — the</em> <strong>Akṣara (the Imperishable)</strong> — <em>given entirely by</em> <strong>negation</strong> <em>(neither gross nor fine, without eye, ear, mind, breath…), then by its</em> <strong>sovereignty</strong> <em>(\"by its command the sun and moon stand apart\"), and finally as the</em> <strong>unseen Seer, the unknown Knower</strong> <em>on which space and all else is woven.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantras 3.8.1–3.8.2","title":"Gārgī takes up her two arrows","sa":["3.8.1: अथ ह वाचक्नव्युवाच — ब्राह्मणा भगवन्तो हन्ताहमिमं द्वौ प्रश्नौ प्रक्ष्यामि । तौ चेन्मे विवक्ष्यति । न वै जातु युष्माकमिमं कश्चिद्ब्रह्मोद्यं जेतेति । पृच्छ गार्गीति ॥ १ ॥","3.8.2: सा होवाच — अहं वै त्वा याज्ञवल्क्य यथा काश्यो वा वैदेहो वोग्रपुत्र उज्ज्यं धनुरधिज्यं कृत्वा द्वौ बाणवन्तौ सपत्नातिव्याधिनौ हस्ते कृत्वोपोत्तिष्ठेदेवमेवाहं त्वा द्वाभ्यां प्रश्नाभ्यामुपोदस्थाम् । तौ मे ब्रूहीति । पृच्छ गार्गीति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["atha ha vācaknavy uvāca -- brāhmaṇā bhagavanto hantāham imaṃ dvau praśnau prakṣyāmi | tau cen me vivakṣyati | na vai jātu yuṣmākam imaṃ kaścid brahmodyaṃ jeteti | pṛccha gārgīti || 1 ||","sā hovāca -- ahaṃ vai tvā yājñavalkya yathā kāśyo vā vaideho vograputra ujjyaṃ dhanur adhijyaṃ kṛtvā dvau bāṇavantau sapatnātivyādhinau haste kṛtvopottiṣṭhed evam evāhaṃ tvā dvābhyāṃ praśnābhyām upodasthām | tau me brūhīti | pṛccha gārgīti || 2 ||"],"words":[["brāhmaṇāḥ bhagavantaḥ hanta aham imaṃ dvau praśnau prakṣyāmi","voc. + acc. du. + 1 sg. fut. of prach","\"revered brāhmaṇas, I shall ask him two questions;"],["tau cet me vivakṣyati","acc. du. + 3 sg. fut. of vac","if he answers them for me,"],["na vai jātu yuṣmākam imaṃ kaścit brahmodyaṃ jeta iti","+ gen. pl. + n. acc. (theological debate) + opt. of ji","none of you will ever defeat him in debate about Brahman\""],["pṛccha gārgi iti","impv.","\"ask, Gārgī\""],["ahaṃ vai tvā … yathā kāśyaḥ vā vaidehaḥ vā ugra-putraḥ","nom. (a fierce warrior's son of Kāśī/Videha)","\"I (rise) against you as a warrior-prince of Kāśī or Videha,"],["ujjyaṃ dhanuḥ adhijyaṃ kṛtvā","n. acc. (an unstrung bow) + ger.","having strung a slack bow,"],["dvau bāṇavantau sapatna-ati-vyādhinau haste kṛtvā upottiṣṭhet","acc. du. (two arrows, foe-piercing) + ger. + opt.","taking in hand two foe-piercing arrows, would rise up —"],["evam eva aham tvā dvābhyāṃ praśnābhyām upodasthām","instr. du. + 1 sg. aor. of upa-sthā","even so I rise against you with two questions"],["tau me brūhi iti","impv.","answer them for me\""]],"lit":"\"Then Gārgī Vācaknavī said: 'Revered brāhmaṇas, I shall ask him two questions. If he answers them for me, none of you will ever defeat him in a debate about Brahman.' 'Ask, Gārgī.' She said: 'Yājñavalkya, as a warrior-prince of Kāśī or Videha, stringing his slack bow and taking in hand two foe-piercing arrows, would rise to attack — even so I rise against you with two questions. Answer them for me.' 'Ask, Gārgī.'\"","para":"Gārgī, far from defeated in 3.6, <strong>re-enters with confidence</strong> and a martial image: she comes armed with <strong>two decisive questions</strong> (her \"two arrows\"), and stakes Yājñavalkya's supremacy on his answering them. Her bearing acknowledges his stature even as she challenges it — the contest's most worthy adversary.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Gārgī's return shows her earlier silence (3.6) was not defeat but restraint; now she frames the <em>right</em> question (about the ground of space), the one that elicits the supreme teaching."],["Krishnananda","the warrior-imagery dignifies her as a peer; her \"two arrows\" are precisely aimed at the ultimate ground — she forces the disclosure of the Akṣara."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the honour the text gives a woman philosopher as Yājñavalkya's most formidable questioner."],["Sureśvara","the staking of the contest on these two questions marks them as the decisive ones of the whole debate."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle highlights Gārgī as a striking figure of female learning in the Upaniṣadic milieu and the dramatic \"two arrows\" formula."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 3.8.3–3.8.7","title":"On what is space woven?","sa":["3.8.3 (first arrow): सा होवाच — यदूर्ध्वं याज्ञवल्क्य दिवो यदवाक्पृथिव्या यदन्तरा द्यावापृथिवी इमे यद्भूतं च भवच्च भविष्यच्चेत्याचक्षते कस्मिंस्तदोतं च प्रोतं चेति ॥ ३ ॥","3.8.4: स होवाच — ... आकाशे तदोतं च प्रोतं चेति ॥ ४ ॥","3.8.5: सा होवाच — नमस्तेऽस्तु याज्ञवल्क्य यो म एतं व्यवोचोऽपरस्मai धारयस्वेति पृच्छ गार्गीति ॥ ५ ॥","3.8.6 (second arrow — same words): सा होवाच — यदूर्ध्वं याज्ञवल्क्य दिवो ... कस्मिंस्तदोतं च प्रोतं चेति ॥ ६ ॥","3.8.7: स होवाच — ... आकाश एव तदोतं च प्रोतं चेति । कस्मिन्नु खल्वाकाश ओतश्च प्रोतश्चेति ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["sā hovāca -- yad ūrdhvaṃ yājñavalkya divo yad avāk pṛthivyā yad antarā dyāvāpṛthivī ime yad bhūtaṃ ca bhavac ca bhaviṣyac cety ācakṣate kasmiṃs tad otaṃ ca protaṃ ceti || 3 ||","sa hovāca -- … ākāśe tad otaṃ ca protaṃ ceti || 4 ||","sā hovaca -- namas te 'stu yājñavalkya yo ma etaṃ vyavoco 'parasmai dhārayasveti pṛccha gārgīti || 5 ||","sā hovāca -- yad ūrdhvaṃ yājñavalkya divo … kasmiṃs tad otaṃ ca protaṃ ceti || 6 ||","sa hovāca -- … ākāśa eva tad otaṃ ca protaṃ ceti | kasmin nu khalv ākāśa otaś ca protaś ceti || 7 ||"],"words":[["yat ūrdhvaṃ divaḥ","n. nom. + abl.","that which is above heaven,"],["yat avāk pṛthivyāḥ","abl.","that which is below the earth,"],["yat antarā dyāvāpṛthivī ime","acc. du.","that which is between these heaven and earth,"],["yat bhūtaṃ ca bhavat ca bhaviṣyat ca iti ācakṣate","n. nom. + 3 pl. pres.","which they call the past, the present, and the future —"],["kasmin tat otaṃ ca protaṃ ca iti","loc. interrog.","on what is that woven, warp and woof?"],["ākāśe tat otaṃ ca protaṃ ca iti","loc.","on space (ākāśa) is that woven, warp and woof"],["namaḥ te astu … yaḥ me etaṃ vyavocaḥ aparasmai dhārayasva","dat. + 2 sg. impf. of vi-vac + dat. + impv. of dhṛ","\"homage to you, who answered me this; (now) make ready for the other (question)\""],["kasmin nu khalu ākāśaḥ otaḥ ca protaḥ ca iti","loc.","\"on what, then, is space itself woven, warp and woof?\""]],"lit":"\"Gārgī said: 'Yājñavalkya, that which is above heaven, below the earth, between heaven and earth, which they call the past, present, and future — on what is that woven, warp and woof?' He said: 'That, Gārgī, is woven on <strong>space (ākāśa).</strong>' 'Homage to you, Yājñavalkya, who have answered me this — now make ready for the other (question).' 'Ask, Gārgī.' (She again asks the same; he answers:) 'On space alone is that woven, warp and woof.' '<strong>(But) on what, then, is space itself woven, warp and woof?</strong>'\"","para":"Gārgī's <strong>first arrow</strong>: that which spans <strong>all space and all time</strong> (above heaven, below earth, between them; past, present, future) — on what is it woven? Answer: on <strong>ākāśa (space/ether)</strong>, the all-pervading subtlest element. Her <strong>second arrow</strong> is the question Yājñavalkya stopped her from asking in 3.6: <strong>on what is <em>space itself</em> woven?</strong> — i.e., what is the ground of the very medium in which all spatio-temporal reality hangs? This time it is the <em>right</em> moment, and Yājñavalkya gives the supreme answer.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","space (<em>ākāśa</em>) is the subtlest of the elements, the support of all the gross; but space too is an <em>effect</em>, and so must rest on a yet subtler ground — the <strong>Imperishable</strong>. Gārgī's question, now properly posed (about the ground of space, not of the brahma-worlds as in 3.6), elicits the <em>nirguṇa</em> Brahman."],["Krishnananda","she has refined her regress: not \"on what is the highest <em>world</em> woven\" (3.6) but \"on what is <em>space itself</em>, the container of all worlds, woven\" — the one question whose answer must be the unconditioned ground."],["Sureśvara","the move from \"all reality\" → \"space\" → \"the ground of space\" forces the inquiry past the last cosmic medium to the Absolute."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note Gārgī's reverent \"namas te\" — the proper attitude even in debate; the second question is the one that matters."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the elegant escalation and that <em>ākāśa</em> is the penultimate term whose ground is the Akṣara."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.8.8","title":"The Imperishable, described by negation","sa":["स होवाच — एतद्वै तदक्षरं गार्गि ब्राह्मणा अभिवदन्त्यस्थूलमनण्वह्रस्वमदीर्घमलोहितमस्नेहमच्छायमतमोऽवाय्वनाकाशमसङ्गमरसमगन्धमचक्षुष्कमश्रोत्रमवागमनोऽतेजस्कमप्राणममुखममात्रमनन्तरमबाह्यम् । न तदश्नाति किं चन । न तदश्नाति कश्चन ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovāca -- etad vai tad akṣaraṃ gārgi brāhmaṇā abhivadanty asthūlam anaṇv ahrasvam adīrgham alohitam asneham acchāyam atamo 'vāyv anākāśam asaṅgam arasam agandham acakṣuṣkam aśrotram avāg amano 'tejaskam aprāṇam amukham amātram anantaram abāhyam | na tad aśnāti kiṃ cana | na tad aśnāti kaś cana || 8 ||"],"words":[["etat vai tat akṣaraṃ gārgi brāhmaṇāḥ abhivadanti","n. nom. (the Imperishable) + 3 pl. pres.","\"this, Gārgī, is that Imperishable (akṣara) which the knowers of Brahman declare:"],["asthūlam anaṇu","n. nom. (not gross, not fine/atomic)","not gross, not fine;"],["ahrasvam adīrgham","(not short, not long)","not short, not long;"],["alohitam asneham","(not red, not moist/adhesive)","not red, not oily;"],["acchāyam atamaḥ","(no shadow, no darkness)","without shadow, without darkness;"],["avāyu anākāśam","(not air, not space)","not air, not space;"],["asaṅgam","(unattached)","unattached;"],["arasam agandham","(tasteless, odourless)","without taste, without smell;"],["acakṣuṣkam aśrotram","(eyeless, earless)","without eye, without ear;"],["avāk amanaḥ","(voiceless, mindless)","without voice, without mind;"],["atejaskam aprāṇam","(without heat/radiance, breathless)","without radiance, without breath;"],["amukham amātram","(mouthless, measureless)","without mouth, without measure;"],["anantaram abāhyam","(no inside, no outside)","without inside, without outside"],["na tat aśnāti kiṃ cana","+ acc.","it does not eat (consume) anything,"],["na tat aśnāti kaḥ cana","+ nom.","nor does anything eat it\""]],"lit":"\"He said: 'This, Gārgī, is that <strong>Imperishable</strong> which the knowers of Brahman declare — not gross, not fine, not short, not long, not red, not moist, without shadow, without darkness, not air, not space, unattached, tasteless, odourless, without eye, without ear, without voice, without mind, without radiance, without breath, without mouth, without measure, without inside, without outside. It eats nothing whatever; nothing whatever eats it.'\"","para":"The supreme answer — what space is woven on — is the <strong>Akṣara, the Imperishable</strong> (Brahman). It is described <strong>wholly by negation</strong> (a cascade of <em>via negativa</em>): it has <strong>no physical quality</strong> (size, colour, texture, shadow), is <strong>none of the elements</strong> (not air, not even space), has <strong>no sense-organ or faculty</strong> (no eye, ear, voice, mind, breath), is <strong>without measure</strong>, and has <strong>no inside or outside</strong> (it is partless, relationless). Crucially, <strong>it neither consumes anything nor is consumed by anything</strong> — it is utterly beyond the food-chain of death and time (cf. 1.2, 1.4.6): the imperishable that is no one's \"food.\" This is the most rigorous <strong>neti-neti</strong> characterisation in the Upaniṣad.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the relentless negations strip away <strong>every attribute</strong> (quality, dimension, element, organ, relation) to indicate the <strong>attributeless (nirguṇa) Brahman</strong> — not a void, but that which remains when all objectifiable predicates are denied: the pure witness. \"Without inside or outside\" = partless, non-spatial; \"eats nothing, eaten by nothing\" = beyond the <em>anna/attṛ</em> (food/eater) duality of saṃsāra, hence imperishable. <em>Akṣara</em> (= \"non-flowing / undecaying / the imperishable\") is the <em>nirguṇa</em> Brahman, knowable only by negation. The locus classicus for the <em>via negativa</em>."],["Sureśvara","every negation removes a possible mistaken identification; what is <em>not</em> negated — the negating consciousness itself — is the Akṣara. \"Eats not, eaten not\" marks it as outside all causality and consumption."],["Krishnananda","\"You cannot say what it is; you can only say what it is not.\" The Imperishable is reached by canceling every category the mind can apply. It is not a <em>thing</em> with no qualities, but the <strong>reality prior to all qualification</strong> — the ground on which even space hangs. Beyond eating and being eaten, it is untouched by the universal devouring of time."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the supreme object (or rather, non-object) of meditation — negate every attribute until only pure being-awareness remains; this Akṣara is the goal."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Deussen reads this as classical apophatic theology; Olivelle notes the comprehensive negation-list and <em>akṣara</em> (\"the imperishable\") as a key designation of the absolute, and the \"eats not / eaten not\" as placing it beyond the cosmic food-cycle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.8.9","title":"By the command of the Imperishable, all stands governed","sa":["एतस्य वा अक्षरस्य प्रशासने गार्गि सूर्याचन्द्रमसौ विधृतौ तिष्ठतः । एतस्य वा अक्षरस्य प्रशासने गार्गि द्यावापृथिव्यौ विधृते तिष्ठतः । एतस्य वा अक्षरस्य प्रशासने गार्गि निमेषा मुहूर्ता अहोरात्राण्यर्धमासा मासा ऋतवः संवत्सरा इति विधृतास्तिष्ठन्ति । एतस्य वा अक्षरस्य प्रशासने गार्गि प्राच्योऽन्या नद्यः स्यन्दन्ते श्वेतेभ्यः पर्वतेभ्यः प्रतीच्योऽन्या यां यां च दिशमनु । एतस्य वा अक्षरस्य प्रशासने गार्गि मनुष्याः प्रशंसन्ति यजमानं देवा दर्वीं पितरोऽन्वायत्ताः ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["etasya vā akṣarasya praśāsane gārgi sūryacandramasau vidhṛtau tiṣṭhataḥ | etasya vā akṣarasya praśāsane gārgi dyāvāpṛthivyau vidhṛte tiṣṭhataḥ | etasya vā akṣarasya praśāsane gārgi nimeṣā muhūrtā ahorātrāṇy ardhamāsā māsā ṛtavaḥ saṃvatsarā iti vidhṛtās tiṣṭhanti | etasya vā akṣarasya praśāsane gārgi prācyo 'nyā nadyaḥ syandante śvetebhyaḥ parvatebhyaḥ pratīcyo 'nyā yāṃ yāṃ ca diśam anu | etasya vā akṣarasya praśāsane gārgi manuṣyāḥ praśaṃsanti yajamānaṃ devā darvīṃ pitaro 'nvāyattāḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["etasya vai akṣarasya praśāsane","gen. + n. loc. (under the command/governance)","under the command of this Imperishable, Gārgī,"],["sūrya-candramasau vidhṛtau tiṣṭhataḥ","m. nom. du. pp. + 3 du. pres.","the sun and moon stand held apart (in their places);"],["dyāvāpṛthivyau vidhṛte tiṣṭhataḥ","n. nom. du.","heaven and earth stand held apart;"],["nimeṣāḥ muhūrtāḥ ahorātrāṇi ardhamāsāḥ māsāḥ ṛtavaḥ saṃvatsarāḥ … vidhṛtāḥ tiṣṭhanti","nom. pl. pp.","moments, hours, days-and-nights, fortnights, months, seasons, years stand held (in order);"],["prācyaḥ anyāḥ nadyaḥ syandante śvetebhyaḥ parvatebhyaḥ pratīcyaḥ anyāḥ yāṃ yāṃ ca diśam anu","f. nom. pl. + pres. + abl. pl.","some rivers flow east from the white (snow) mountains, others west, each toward its own quarter;"],["manuṣyāḥ praśaṃsanti yajamānaṃ devāḥ darvīṃ pitaraḥ anvāyattāḥ","nom. pl. + pres. + acc. + nom. pl. pp.","men praise the generous giver, the gods depend on the sacrificer, the fathers on the offering-ladle"]],"lit":"\"Under the command of this Imperishable, Gārgī, the <strong>sun and moon stand held apart</strong> (in their courses); under its command <strong>heaven and earth stand held apart</strong>; under its command the <strong>moments, hours, days-and-nights, fortnights, months, seasons, and years stand established</strong>; under its command some <strong>rivers flow eastward from the snowy mountains, others westward</strong>, each toward its quarter; under its command <strong>men praise the giver, the gods depend on the sacrificer, and the fathers on the offering-ladle.</strong>\"","para":"Having described the Imperishable by negation (what it is <em>not</em>), Yājñavalkya now shows its <strong>positive sovereignty</strong>: though attributeless, it is the <strong>silent command (praśāsana)</strong> that holds the entire cosmos in order. By <em>its</em> governance the <strong>luminaries keep their courses, heaven and earth their places, time its measured divisions, the rivers their directed flow</strong>, and the whole moral-ritual order (givers, sacrificers, ancestors) functions. The unconditioned Akṣara is thus the <strong>lawgiver and sustainer of all</strong> — not by acting, but by its mere being, under which \"all stands held.\" (This is the immanent governance of the <em>antaryāmin</em> (3.7) now grounded in the Imperishable.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the attributeless Brahman is yet the <strong>inviolable law</strong> (<em>praśāsana</em>, \"rule/command\") by which the cosmos is sustained in perfect order — sun and moon, time, rivers all \"stand apart\" (do not collapse into chaos) under its governance. Its sovereignty is not an <em>act</em> (it does nothing) but the very ground of cosmic order — like the king by whose mere existence the realm holds. Refutes the notion that a quality-less Brahman is inert or irrelevant: it is the basis of all regularity."],["Sureśvara","the negations (3.8.8) and the governance (3.8.9) together show Brahman as <strong>transcendent (beyond all attributes) yet the ground of all order</strong> — neither a thing nor a void."],["Krishnananda","\"By the rule of the Imperishable the sun rises and the rivers run\" — the cosmic order is the standing command of the Absolute; law itself is its expression. A sublime vision of nature as the obedience of all things to the silent Imperishable."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","meditate on the cosmic order — the sun's course, the seasons, the rivers — as the visible command of the unseen Imperishable; awe at natural law becomes devotion to its ground."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the magnificent cosmological sweep (luminaries, time-divisions, rivers, the social-ritual order) as evidence of Brahman's governance, balancing the apophatic 3.8.8 with a cataphatic display of sovereignty."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 3.8.10–3.8.11","title":"Knowing the Imperishable; the unseen Seer on which space is woven","sa":["3.8.10: यो वा एतदक्षरं गार्ग्यविदित्वास्मिँल्लोके जुहोति यजते तपस्तप्यते बहूनि वर्षसहस्राण्यन्तवदेवास्य तद्भवति । यो वा एतदक्षरमविदित्वास्माल्लोकात्प्रैति स कृपणः । अथ य एतदक्षरं गार्गि विदित्वास्माल्लोकात्प्रैति स ब्राह्मणः ॥ १० ॥","3.8.11: तद्वा एतदक्षरं गार्ग्यदृष्टं द्रष्टृ अश्रुतं श्रोतृ अमतं मन्तृ अविज्ञातं विज्ञातृ । नान्यदतोऽस्ति द्रष्टृ । नान्यदतोऽस्ति श्रोतृ । नान्यदतोऽस्ति मन्तृ । नान्यदतोऽस्ति विज्ञातृ । एतस्मिन्नु खल्वक्षरे गार्ग्याकाश ओतश्च प्रोतश्च ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["yo vā etad akṣaraṃ gārgy aviditvāsmiṃl loke juhoti yajate tapas tapyate bahūni varṣasahasrāṇy antavad evāsya tad bhavati | yo vā etad akṣaram aviditvāsmāl lokāt praiti sa kṛpaṇaḥ | atha ya etad akṣaraṃ gārgi viditvāsmāl lokāt praiti sa brāhmaṇaḥ || 10 ||","tad vā etad akṣaraṃ gārgy adṛṣṭaṃ draṣṭṛ aśrutaṃ śrotṛ amataṃ mantṛ avijñātaṃ vijñātṛ | nānyad ato 'sti draṣṭṛ | nānyad ato 'sti śrotṛ | nānyad ato 'sti mantṛ | nānyad ato 'sti vijñātṛ | etasmin nu khalv akṣare gārgy ākāśa otaś ca protaś ca || 11 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ vai etat akṣaraṃ aviditvā asmin loke juhoti yajate tapaḥ tapyate bahūni varṣa-sahasrāṇi","ger. (not knowing) + pres. + acc. pl.","whoever, not knowing this Imperishable, offers, sacrifices, and practises austerity for many thousands of years,"],["antavat eva asya tat bhavati","adj. (having an end)","his (gain) is merely perishable"],["yaḥ vai etat akṣaram aviditvā asmāt lokāt praiti saḥ kṛpaṇaḥ","ger. + pres. of pra-i + nom. (pitiable)","whoever departs this world without knowing this Imperishable is wretched (a miser, kṛpaṇa)"],["atha yaḥ etat akṣaraṃ viditvā asmāt lokāt praiti saḥ brāhmaṇaḥ","ger. (knowing) + nom.","but whoever departs this world knowing this Imperishable is a (true) brāhmaṇa"],["tat vai etat akṣaraṃ gārgi adṛṣṭaṃ draṣṭṛ","n. nom. (unseen) + (seer)","that Imperishable, Gārgī, is unseen but the seer,"],["aśrutaṃ śrotṛ, amataṃ mantṛ, avijñātaṃ vijñātṛ","","unheard but the hearer, unthought but the thinker, unknown but the knower"],["na anyat ataḥ asti draṣṭṛ / śrotṛ / mantṛ / vijñātṛ","nom.","there is no other seer / hearer / thinker / knower than it"],["etasmin nu khalu akṣare gārgi ākāśaḥ otaḥ ca protaḥ ca","loc.","on this very Imperishable, Gārgī, is space woven, warp and woof"]],"lit":"\"(3.8.10:) 'Whoever, Gārgī, <strong>without knowing</strong> this Imperishable, offers oblations, sacrifices, and practises austerity in this world for many thousands of years — his (gain) is merely <strong>perishable</strong>. Whoever departs from this world <strong>without knowing</strong> this Imperishable is <strong>wretched</strong> (<em>kṛpaṇa</em>, a miser). But whoever departs from this world <strong>knowing</strong> this Imperishable is a (true) <strong>brāhmaṇa</strong>.' (3.8.11:) 'That Imperishable, Gārgī, is <strong>unseen but the seer, unheard but the hearer, unthought but the thinker, unknown but the knower.</strong> There is no other seer than it, no other hearer, no other thinker, no other knower. <strong>On this very Imperishable, Gārgī, is space woven, warp and woof.</strong>'\"","para":"The fruit and the final identification. <strong>Ritual and austerity without knowledge of the Imperishable yield only perishable results</strong> (cf. 1.4.15, 1.5.16); to die ignorant of it is to die <strong>kṛpaṇa</strong> (a \"miser,\" spiritually destitute), while to die <strong>knowing</strong> it is to be a true <strong>brāhmaṇa</strong> (one who has reached Brahman). And the Imperishable on which space (and so all reality) is woven is none other than the <strong>self-luminous Subject</strong>: the <strong>Seer never seen, the Knower never known</strong> — \"<strong>there is no other</strong> seer, hearer, thinker, or knower.\" Thus the answer to \"on what is space woven?\" is: <strong>on the witnessing Self, the Imperishable Brahman.</strong> (The same apophatic-subject teaching as 3.4.2 and 3.7.23, now as the ground of space itself.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>phala</em>-statement reaffirms that <strong>karma is perishable, knowledge alone liberates</strong> (cf. 1.4.15). <em>Kṛpaṇa</em> (\"miser\") = one who, having the priceless chance of human life, dies without realising the imperishable Self — spiritually bankrupt; the <strong>brāhmaṇa</strong> is the knower. The climactic identification — the Akṣara is the <strong>unseen Seer / unknown Knower</strong>, with \"no other\" — equates the <strong>ground of space (the cosmological answer)</strong> with the <strong>witnessing Self (the psychological/epistemic answer)</strong>: cosmology and Self-knowledge converge in the one Brahman, known by <em>neti neti</em>."],["Sureśvara","the negations of 3.8.8, the governance of 3.8.9, and the witness-identity of 3.8.11 together fix the Akṣara as the <em>nirguṇa</em> Brahman that is the sole Subject; \"no other knower\" secures non-duality."],["Krishnananda","the answer to Gārgī's arrow is total — space, the container of all, hangs on the <strong>Imperishable Consciousness</strong>, the one Seer behind all seeing. To know this is to be a <em>brāhmaṇa</em>; to miss it, after all one's works, is to die a pauper amid riches. The cosmic question (\"ground of space\") and the inmost question (\"who is the seer?\") have one answer."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the supreme exhortation — make knowing the Imperishable the one aim; all else, however meritorious, is perishable. Rest as the Seer that is never seen."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the convergence of the cosmological regress (space's ground) with the apophatic witness-Self, and <em>kṛpaṇa</em> vs. <em>brāhmaṇa</em> as the stark soteriological alternative."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.8.12","title":"Gārgī concedes","sa":["सा होवाच — ब्राह्मणा भगवन्तस्तदेव बहु मन्यध्वं यदस्मान्नमस्कारेण मुच्येध्वम् । न वै जातु युष्माकमिमं कश्चिद्ब्रह्मोद्यं जेतेति । ततो ह वाचक्नव्युपरराम ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["sā hovāca -- brāhmaṇā bhagavantas tad eva bahu manyadhvaṃ yad asmān namaskāreṇa mucyedhvam | na vai jātu yuṣmākam imaṃ kaścid brahmodyaṃ jeteti | tato ha vācaknavy upararāma || 12 ||"],"words":[["brāhmaṇāḥ bhagavantaḥ tat eva bahu manyadhvaṃ","voc. + 2 pl. impv. of man","\"revered brāhmaṇas, count it a great thing"],["yat asmāt namaskāreṇa mucyedhvam","abl. + instr. + 2 pl. opt. of muc","if you (manage to) escape from him merely by paying homage"],["na vai jātu yuṣmākam imaṃ kaścit brahmodyaṃ jeta iti","+ opt. of ji","none of you will ever defeat him in a debate about Brahman\""],["tataḥ ha vācaknavī upararāma","pf.","thereupon Gārgī fell silent"]],"lit":"\"Gārgī said: 'Revered brāhmaṇas, count it a great gain if you get off from him merely by paying him homage! None of you will ever defeat him in a debate about Brahman.' Thereupon Gārgī Vācaknavī fell silent.\"","para":"Gārgī, <strong>fully satisfied</strong>, concedes with grace and even warns the other brāhmaṇas: <em>be content to bow and withdraw — none of you can defeat this man.</em> Her silence now is the silence of one who has received the highest answer, not of one merely stumped. (She is the only challenger the text shows <em>acknowledging</em> Yājñavalkya's supremacy aloud.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Gārgī's concession, unlike the others' bare silence, openly certifies Yājñavalkya's mastery — fitting, since her question drew out the supreme teaching."],["Krishnananda","the noblest of the challengers, she ends the round by honouring the victor and counseling the rest — her humility is the mark of genuine understanding."],["Sureśvara","the public acknowledgement seals the Akṣara-teaching as unanswerable."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a gracious close; the questioner who asked the best question gives the most generous concession."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes Gārgī's distinctive dignity — she alone declares Yājñavalkya undefeated."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.8","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Gārgī's second round delivers the <strong>philosophical summit of Adhyāya 3</strong> — the doctrine of the <strong>Akṣara (the Imperishable):</strong>","1. <strong>The right regress (3.8.3–7).</strong> From all spatio-temporal reality → <strong>space (ākāśa)</strong> → <em>the ground of space itself.</em> Where 3.6 was stopped (asking the ground of the highest <em>world</em>), here the question reaches the genuinely ultimate: what underlies the very medium of all things?","2. <strong>The Imperishable by negation (3.8.8).</strong> The answer is Brahman as <strong>Akṣara</strong>, described by a sustained <strong>neti neti</strong> — without any size, quality, element, organ, faculty, measure, inside, or outside; and <strong>beyond all consumption</strong> (\"eats nothing, eaten by nothing\"). Pure attributeless reality, indicated only by removing every predicate.","3. <strong>The Imperishable as cosmic sovereign (3.8.9).</strong> Yet by its silent <strong>command (praśāsana)</strong> the sun and moon keep their courses, heaven and earth their places, time its divisions, rivers their flow, and the whole order its function. Attributeless, it is nonetheless the <strong>ground of all law</strong> — neither inert nor a void.","4. <strong>Knowledge alone liberates (3.8.10).</strong> Works and austerities without knowing it are <strong>perishable</strong>; to die ignorant of it is to die <em>kṛpaṇa</em> (destitute), to die knowing it is to be a <strong>brāhmaṇa</strong>.","5. <strong>The Imperishable is the witnessing Self (3.8.11).</strong> Finally, the ground of space is the <strong>unseen Seer, the unknown Knower</strong> — \"<strong>no other</strong> seer or knower\" — uniting the <strong>cosmological</strong> answer (the ground of all) with the <strong>epistemic</strong> answer (the one Subject), exactly as in 3.4.2 and 3.7.23.","With this, Yājñavalkya's supremacy is publicly acknowledged (3.8.12). The last challenger, <strong>Śākalya</strong> (3.9), will press the question of the <em>number and nature of the gods</em> — and meet a darker end."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.8","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   GĀRGĪ&#x27;S TWO ARROWS (BU 3.8)\n   ===========================\n   Arrow 1: all that is above heaven / below earth / between / past·present·future\n            --woven on--&gt;  ĀKĀŚA (space)\n   Arrow 2: &quot;and on what is SPACE itself woven?&quot;\n            --woven on--&gt;  THE AKṢARA (the Imperishable)\n                                |\n   described by NEGATION (3.8.8):  not gross/fine/short/long/red/moist · no shadow/darkness\n        · not air/space · unattached · no taste/smell/eye/ear/voice/mind/radiance/breath/mouth\n        · no measure · NO INSIDE, NO OUTSIDE · eats nothing, eaten by nothing\n                                |\n   yet SOVEREIGN (3.8.9): by ITS COMMAND (praśāsana) -&gt;\n        sun &amp; moon held apart · heaven &amp; earth held · time&#x27;s divisions ordered\n        · rivers flow east/west · givers praised, gods &amp; fathers sustained\n                                |\n   FRUIT (3.8.10): works without knowing it -&gt; PERISHABLE ; die ignorant -&gt; KṚPAṆA (destitute)\n                   die KNOWING it -&gt; a true BRĀHMAṆA\n                                |\n   IDENTITY (3.8.11): the Akṣara = UNSEEN SEER · UNHEARD HEARER · UNTHOUGHT THINKER · UNKNOWN KNOWER\n        &quot;no other seer/hearer/thinker/knower&quot;  -&gt;  ON THIS, space is woven\n        =&gt; ground-of-space (cosmology)  ==  the witnessing SELF (cf. 3.4.2, 3.7.23)\n                                |\n                                v   Gārgī concedes: &quot;none of you will defeat him&quot;  (defeat 8 of 9)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.8","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Ask the deepest \"on what?\"</strong> Follow Gārgī's arrow inward: everything hangs in space; on what does space — the very field of experience — hang? On the awareness that is its ground. Let the question carry you past every object to the unconditioned witness.","2. <strong>Practice the negations</strong> (3.8.8). In meditation, deny to the ultimate every attribute that arises — not gross, not subtle, no colour, no inside, no outside, not even \"space\" or \"void.\" Not to reach nothing, but to rest as the reality that no predicate can capture.","3. <strong>See cosmic order as silent command</strong> (3.8.9). Regard the regularity of nature — sunrise, seasons, the rivers' courses — as the standing \"command\" of the Imperishable. Awe at natural law becomes recognition of its attributeless ground.","4. <strong>Don't die a kṛpaṇa</strong> (3.8.10). However much you accomplish, accumulate, or perform, none of it touches the imperishable unless you <em>know</em> it. Let this urgency order your priorities: knowing the Self is the one non-perishable gain.","5. <strong>Rest as the unseen Seer</strong> (3.8.11). The Imperishable is not far: it is the very one now seeing and knowing — never itself seen or known. Abide as that Seer, on which even space is woven.","6. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the ground of the outermost (space, the cosmos) and the ground of the innermost (the seer behind your eyes) are <strong>one and the same Imperishable.</strong> Cosmology and self-knowledge meet here — and the meeting-point is known only by letting go of every \"this.\""],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 3 · Brāhmaṇa 3.9 — Vidagdha Śākalya: how many gods, the persons, and the final Self","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 3.9","title":"Vidagdha Śākalya: how many gods, the persons, and the final Self","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The longest and climactic round. The ninth challenger, <strong>Vidagdha Śākalya</strong>, asks <strong>\"how many gods are there?\"</strong> — and Yājñavalkya, by a brilliant descending series (3306 → 33 → 6 → 3 → 2 → 1½ → 1), resolves the whole pantheon into <strong>one: the breath (prāṇa), \"Brahman, called tyat.\"</strong> He then expounds eight cosmic-bodily \"persons\" and their deities; turns the tables on Śākalya about the quarters and their final support (the <strong>heart</strong>); and — pressed for the <strong>Upaniṣadic Person</strong> (the neti-neti Self) — issues the warning that, unanswered, costs Śākalya his life. The brāhmaṇa ends with the <strong>tree-simile</strong> and the great definition: <strong>vijñānam ānandaṃ brahma</strong>, \"Brahman is consciousness and bliss.\""],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.9.1","title":"How many gods? The descending count","sa":["अथ हैनं विदग्धः शाकल्यः पप्रच्छ — कति देवा याज्ञवल्क्येति । स हैतयैव निविदा प्रतिपेदे यावन्तो वैश्वदेवस्य निविद्युच्यन्ते । त्रयश्च त्री च शता त्रयश्च त्री च सहस्रेति । ओमिति होवाच । कत्येव देवा याज्ञवल्क्येति । त्रयस्त्रिंशदिति । ओमिति होवाच । कत्येव देवा याज्ञवल्क्येति । षडिति । ओमिति होवाच । कत्येव देवा याज्ञवल्क्येति । त्रय इति । ओमिति होवाच । कत्येव देवा याज्ञवल्क्येति । द्वाविति । ओमिति होवाच । कत्येव देवा याज्ञवल्क्येति । अध्यर्ध इति । ओमिति होवाच । कत्येव देवा याज्ञवल्क्येति । एक इति । ओमिति होवाच । कतमे ते त्रयश्च त्री च शता त्रयश्च त्री च सहस्रेति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha hainaṃ vidagdhaḥ śākalyaḥ papraccha -- kati devā yājñavalkyeti | sa haitayaiva nividā pratipede yāvanto vaiśvadevasya nividy ucyante | trayaś ca trī ca śatā trayaś ca trī ca sahasreti | om iti hovāca | katy eva devā yājñavalkyeti | trayastriṃśad iti | … ṣaḍ iti | … traya iti | … dvāv iti | … adhyardha iti | … eka iti | om iti hovāca | katame te trayaś ca trī ca śatā trayaś ca trī ca sahasreti || 1 ||"],"words":[["kati devāḥ yājñavalkya iti","interrog. + m. nom. pl.","\"how many gods are there, Yājñavalkya?\""],["saḥ ha etayā eva nividā pratipede","f. instr. (the nivid invocation) + 3 sg. pf. of prati-pad","he answered with this very nivid (litany),"],["yāvantaḥ vaiśvadevasya nividi ucyante","rel. + gen. + loc. + pres. pass.","as many as are named in the nivid of the Viśvedevas:"],["trayaḥ ca trī ca śatā trayaḥ ca trī ca sahasrā iti","numerals","\"three and three hundred, and three and three thousand\" (3306)"],["om iti hovāca","","\"Oṃ (so it is),\" he said"],["katy eva devāḥ … trayastriṃśat iti … ṣaṭ … trayaḥ … dvau … adhyardhaḥ … ekaḥ iti","numerals","\"but (exactly) how many gods?\" — \"thirty-three\" … \"six\" … \"three\" … \"two\" … \"one and a half\" … \"one\""],["katame te trayaḥ ca trī ca śatā … sahasrā iti","interrog.","\"(then) which are those three-hundred-and-three and three-thousand-and-three?\""]],"lit":"\"Then Vidagdha Śākalya questioned him: 'How many gods are there, Yājñavalkya?' He answered with the very <em>nivid</em> (litany): 'As many as are named in the Praise of All-Gods — <strong>three and three hundred, and three and three thousand</strong> (= 3,306).' 'Oṃ,' (said Śākalya;) 'but how many gods exactly, Yājñavalkya?' '<strong>Thirty-three.</strong>' 'Oṃ; but how many?' '<strong>Six.</strong>' … '<strong>Three.</strong>' … '<strong>Two.</strong>' … '<strong>One and a half.</strong>' … '<strong>One.</strong>' 'Oṃ; (now) which are those 3,306?'\"","para":"Śākalya's opening probe — <em>how many gods?</em> — meets a striking pedagogy: Yājñavalkya gives the traditional liturgical total (<strong>3,306</strong>, from the Viśvedeva litany), then, each time pressed \"but how many <em>really</em>?\", he <strong>descends</strong> — 33, 6, 3, 2, 1½, and finally <strong>one</strong>. The many gods are progressively resolved toward unity; the whole pantheon, rightly understood, is <strong>one reality</strong> under many counts. (The breakdown follows in 3.9.2–9.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the descending series teaches that the \"gods\" are not separate ultimate beings but <strong>powers/glories (mahimānaḥ) of the one Reality</strong>, counted variously for different meditative purposes; the true number is <strong>one</strong>. The 3,306 are the deities' <em>manifestations</em>; the one is their essence."],["Sureśvara","polytheism is <em>resolved</em>, not denied — each higher count subsumes the lower, until all collapse into the single Self/prāṇa."],["Krishnananda","a masterly reconciliation of the Vedic many-gods with Upaniṣadic monism: the gods are real <em>as powers</em> of the One; \"how many?\" has many true answers depending on the level of analysis, but the final answer is <strong>one</strong>."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the many deities are aspects of the one God; worship at any number is valid, but knowledge sees the One."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the <em>nivid</em> (liturgical enumeration) of 3,306 Viśvedevas and the elegant reductive catechism toward monotheism/monism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 3.9.2–3.9.9","title":"The breakdown: from 3,306 to the One","sa":["3.9.2: स होवाच — महिमान एवैषामेते । त्रयस्त्रिंशत्त्वेव देवा इति । कतमे ते त्रयस्त्रिंशदिति । अष्टौ वसव एकादश रुद्रा द्वादशादित्यास्त एकत्रिंशदैन्द्रश्चैव प्रजापतिश्च त्रयस्त्रिंशाविति ॥ २ ॥","3.9.3 (the Vasus): कतमे वसव इति । अग्निश्च पृथिवी च वायुश्चान्तरिक्षं चादित्यश्च द्यौश्च चन्द्रमाश्च नक्षत्राणि चैते वसवः । एतेषु हीदं सर्वं वसु हितमिति तस्माद्वसव इति ॥ ३ ॥","3.9.4 (the Rudras): कतमे रुद्रा इति । दशेमे पुरुषे प्राणा आत्मैकादशः । ते यदास्माच्छरीरान्मर्त्यादुत्क्रामन्त्यथ रोदयन्ति । तद्यद्रोदयन्ति तस्माद्रुद्रा इति ॥ ४ ॥","3.9.5 (the Ādityas): कतम आदित्या इति । द्वादश वै मासाः संवत्सरस्यैत आदित्याः । एते हीदं सर्वमाददाना यन्ति । ते यदिदं सर्वमाददाना यन्ति तस्मादादित्या इति ॥ ५ ॥","3.9.6 (Indra, Prajāpati): कतम इन्द्रः कतमः प्रजापतिरिति । स्तनयित्नुरेवेन्द्रो यज्ञः प्रजापतिरिति । कतमः स्तनयित्नुरिति । अशनिरिति । कतमो यज्ञ इति । पशव इति ॥ ६ ॥","3.9.7 (the six): कतमे षडिति । अग्निश्च पृथिवी च वायुश्चान्तरिक्षश्चादित्यश्च द्यौश्चैते षट् । एते हीदं सर्वं षडिति ॥ ७ ॥","3.9.8 (three, two, one-and-a-half): कतमे ते त्रयो देवा इति । इम एव त्रयो लोकाः । एषु हीमे सर्वे देवा इति । कतमौ तौ द्वौ देवा इति । अन्नं चैव प्राणश्चेति । कतमोऽध्यर्ध इति । योऽयं पवत इति ॥ ८ ॥","3.9.9 (the One): तदाहुर्यदयमेक इवैव पवते । अथ कथमध्यर्ध इति । यदस्मिन्निदं सर्वमध्यार्ध्नोत्तेनाध्यर्ध इति । कतम एको देव इति । प्राण इति । स ब्रह्म त्यदित्याचक्षते ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovāca -- mahimāna evaiṣām ete | trayastriṃśat tv eva devā iti | katame te trayastriṃśad iti | aṣṭau vasava ekādaśa rudrā dvādaśādityāḥ ta ekatriṃśad aindraś caiva prajāpatiś ca trayastriṃśāv iti || 2 ||","katame vasava iti | agniś ca pṛthivī ca vāyuś cāntarikṣaṃ cādityaś ca dyauḥ ca candramāś ca nakṣatrāṇi caite vasavaḥ | eteṣu hīdaṃ sarvaṃ vasu hitaṃ iti tasmād vasava iti || 3 ||","katame rudrā iti | daśeme puruṣe prāṇā ātmaikādaśaḥ | te yadāsmāc charīrān martyād utkrāmanty atha rodayanti | tad yad rodayanti tasmād rudrā iti || 4 ||","katama ādityā iti | dvādaśa vai māsāḥ saṃvatsarasyaita ādityāḥ | ete hīdaṃ sarvam ādadānā yanti | te yad idaṃ sarvam ādadānā yanti tasmād ādityā iti || 5 ||","katama indraḥ katamaḥ prajāpatir iti | stanayitnur evendro yajñaḥ prajāpatir iti | katamaḥ stanayitnur iti | aśanir iti | katamo yajña iti | paśava iti || 6 ||","katame ṣaḍ iti | agniś ca pṛthivī ca vāyuś cāntarikṣaś cādityaś ca dyauś caite ṣaṭ | ete hīdaṃ sarvaṃ ṣaḍ iti || 7 ||","katame te trayo devā iti | ima eva trayo lokāḥ | eṣu hīme sarve devā iti | katamau tau dvau devā iti | annaṃ caiva prāṇaś ceti | katamo 'dhyardha iti | yo 'yaṃ pavata iti || 8 ||","tad āhur yad ayam eka ivaiva pavate | atha katham adhyardha iti | yad asminn idaṃ sarvam adhyārdhnot tenādhyardha iti | katama eko deva iti | prāṇa iti | sa brahma tyad ity ācakṣate || 9 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>Consolidated breakdown:</strong><br>\"(As for the 'one-and-a-half':) they say, 'since this (wind) blows as just one, how is it one-and-a-half?' — Because in it all this 'grew up' (<em>adhyārdhnot</em>), it is 'one-and-a-half' (<em>adhyardha</em>). 'Which is the one God?' '<strong>The breath (prāṇa)</strong>; and He is <strong>Brahman, called <em>tyat</em> (That).</strong>'\"","para":"Yājñavalkya now itemises the descending counts. The <strong>33</strong> are the eight <strong>Vasus</strong> (the abodes of all wealth — the cosmic supports), eleven <strong>Rudras</strong> (the ten breaths + the self, which at a man's death \"make his kin weep\"), twelve <strong>Ādityas</strong> (the months that \"carry off\" all life-span), plus <strong>Indra</strong> (the thunder/rain) and <strong>Prajāpati</strong> (the sacrifice). The <strong>6</strong> are the great cosmic elements; the <strong>3</strong> are the three worlds; the <strong>2</strong> are <strong>food and breath</strong>; the <strong>1½</strong> is the <strong>wind</strong> (in which \"all increased\"); and the <strong>One</strong> is the <strong>breath (prāṇa)</strong> — which is <strong>Brahman, designated <em>tyat</strong></em>, \"That\" (the transcendent). The entire pantheon thus resolves into the single life-principle that is Brahman.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","each level is a true <em>meditative</em> grouping, but the deities are ultimately <strong>powers of the one prāṇa/Brahman</strong>. The etymologies (Vasu ← <em>vas</em>, \"all wealth set in them\"; Rudra ← <em>rud</em>, \"make weep\"; Āditya ← <em>ā-dā</em>, \"take away\") show them as functional aspects, not independent ultimates. The final <strong>\"one = prāṇa = Brahman, called <em>tyat</em>\"</strong> identifies the cosmic life with the Absolute (the <em>tyat</em>, the transcendent \"That\" of 2.3.1)."],["Sureśvara","the reduction culminates in <em>prāṇa = Brahman</em>; the apparent polytheism is a graded display of the one Reality."],["Krishnananda","the gods are the One viewed through different magnifications; the \"one and a half\" (the all-pervading wind in which everything grows) and finally the \"one\" (prāṇa/Brahman) complete the descent from cosmic multiplicity to the single living Absolute."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a meditation-ladder from the many to the One; worship rises from 3,306 forms to the one prāṇa-Brahman."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the <em>nivid</em>-based 3,306 and the etymological derivations (Vasu/Rudra/Āditya) as classic Brāhmaṇa <em>nirvacana</em>, resolving the pantheon into prāṇa."]],"notes":["<table><tr><td>count</td><td>identity (Yājñavalkya's analysis)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3,306</strong></td><td>the <strong>glories/powers (mahimānaḥ)</strong> of the gods (their manifestations)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>33</strong></td><td>8 <strong>Vasus</strong> + 11 <strong>Rudras</strong> + 12 <strong>Ādityas</strong> + <strong>Indra</strong> + <strong>Prajāpati</strong></td></tr><tr><td>— Vasus (8)</td><td>fire, earth, air, mid-region, sun, heaven, moon, stars (in them <em>all this wealth (vasu) is set</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>— Rudras (11)</td><td>the ten breaths (prāṇas) + the self (ātman); at death they depart and <em>make weep (rud)</em></td></tr><tr><td>— Ādityas (12)</td><td>the twelve months (they <em>take away (ā-dā)</em> all, hence Ādityas)</td></tr><tr><td>— Indra / Prajāpati</td><td>the thunder(-cloud) / the sacrifice</td></tr><tr><td><strong>6</strong></td><td>fire, earth, air, mid-region, sun, heaven (in these six is \"all this\")</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>the three worlds (in which all the gods are)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>food (anna)</strong> and <strong>breath (prāṇa)</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>1½</strong></td><td>\"the one that blows\" (the wind, Vāyu) — <em>adhyardha</em> (\"one-and-a-half\") because in it all this \"grew up/increased\" (<em>adhy-ardh</em>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>the <strong>breath (prāṇa)</strong> — \"He is Brahman, called <em>tyat</em> (That)\"</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantras 3.9.10–3.9.17","title":"The eight Persons and their deities","sa":["3.9.10 (template — the bodily person): पृथिव्येव यस्यायतनमग्निर्लोको मनो ज्योतिर्यो वै तं पुरुषं विद्यात्सर्वस्यात्मनः परायणं स वै वेदिता स्यात् । याज्ञवल्क्य वेद वा अहं तं पुरुषं सर्वस्यात्मनः परायणं यमात्थ । य एवायं शारीरः पुरुषः स एषः । वदैव शाकल्य तस्य का देवतेति । अमृतमिति होवाच ॥ १० ॥","3.9.11 (the desire-person): काम एव यस्यायतनं हृदयं लोको मनो ज्योतिः … य एवायं काममयः पुरुषः स एषः । वदैव शाकल्य तस्य का देवतेति । स्त्रिय इति होवाच ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["pṛthivy eva yasyāyatanam agnir loko mano jyotir yo vai taṃ puruṣaṃ vidyāt sarvasyātmanaḥ parāyaṇaṃ sa vai veditā syāt | yājñavalkya veda vā ahaṃ taṃ puruṣaṃ sarvasyātmanaḥ parāyaṇaṃ yam āttha | ya evāyaṃ śārīraḥ puruṣaḥ sa eṣaḥ | vadaiva śākalya tasya kā devateti | amṛtam iti hovāca || 10 ||","kāma eva yasyāyatanaṃ hṛdayaṃ loko mano jyotir … ya evāyaṃ kāmamayaḥ puruṣaḥ sa eṣaḥ | … striya iti hovāca || 11 ||"],"words":[["pṛthivī eva yasya āyatanam","f. nom. + gen. + n. nom.","(the Person) whose abode (seat) is the earth,"],["agniḥ lokaḥ","m. nom.","whose world (instrument of perception) is fire,"],["manaḥ jyotiḥ","n. nom.","whose light is the mind —"],["yaḥ vai taṃ puruṣaṃ vidyāt sarvasya ātmanaḥ parāyaṇam","acc. + opt. of vid + gen. + n. acc. (final support)","whoever should know that Person, the support of all selves,"],["saḥ vai veditā syāt","m. nom. (a knower) + opt.","he would be a (true) knower"],["veda vai aham taṃ puruṣaṃ … yam āttha","1 sg. pf.","\"I do know that Person of whom you speak"],["yaḥ eva ayaṃ śārīraḥ puruṣaḥ saḥ eṣaḥ","nom.","it is this very bodily person\""],["vada eva śākalya tasya kā devatā iti","impv. + interrog.","\"(then) just say, Śākalya: what is its deity?\""],["amṛtam iti ha uvāca","n. nom.","\"the Immortal,\" he said"]],"lit":"\"'(That Person) whose abode is the earth, whose world (medium) is fire, whose light is the mind — whoever knows that Person, the final support of all selves, is a true knower.' (Śākalya:) 'I do know that Person, the support of all selves, of whom you speak — it is this bodily person. (But) tell me, Yājñavalkya: what is its deity?' '<strong>The Immortal,</strong>' he said.\" <em>(So through the eight: desire-person → women; sun-person → truth; echo-person → the quarters; shadow-person → death; mirror-person → truth (var. life); water-person → Varuṇa; son-person → Prajāpati.)</em>","para":"Yājñavalkya leads Śākalya through <strong>eight \"persons\"</strong> — each a focus of consciousness with a cosmic <strong>abode</strong>, a perceptual <strong>world</strong>, and the <strong>mind</strong> as its illuminating \"light,\" and each the <strong>support of all selves (sarvasyātmanaḥ parāyaṇam)</strong> when truly known. Śākalya correctly <em>identifies</em> each person (bodily, desire-formed, in the sun, the echo, the shadow, in the mirror, in the waters, the son-formed) and learns its presiding <strong>deity</strong> (the Immortal, women, truth, the quarters, death, truth/life, Varuṇa, Prajāpati). These are graded meditations on the Self as present in body, psyche, and cosmos — but they remain <em>determinate</em> persons; the real prize (the <strong>indeterminate, neti-neti Self</strong>) is still to come (3.9.26), and Śākalya's command of the eight will prove insufficient there.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the eight persons are <em>qualified</em> loci of the Self (in the gross body, in desire, in the sun/eye, in sound/space, in darkness, in the mirror, in water, in progeny) — each \"the support of all selves\" <em>relative to its sphere</em>; the <em>mind</em> is their common \"light\" (the inner illuminator). These are legitimate <em>saguṇa</em> meditations, but Śākalya, master of the <em>determinate</em>, has not yet met the <em>indeterminate</em> Self of 3.9.26."],["Sureśvara","each person is the one Self conditioned by an abode; knowing them is preparatory, but liberation requires the <em>neti-neti</em> Self beyond all such conditioning."],["Krishnananda","a graded map of where consciousness \"sits\" — in the body, in longing, in vision, in echo, in shadow, in reflection, in water, in offspring — each a face of the Self with its governing deity. Śākalya knows the faces but not the faceless."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","useful <em>upāsanā</em>-loci, each with its deity for worship; the mind as the \"light\" by which each is known."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the <em>āyatana/loka/jyotis</em> (abode/world/light) schema and the eight persons as a systematic catalogue of conditioned selves, each assigned a deity."]],"notes":["<em>Eight parallel exchanges. For each, Yājñavalkya states a meditation: \"X is the abode (āyatana), Y the world (loka), the mind the light (jyotis); whoever knows that Person who is the support of all selves (sarvasyātmanaḥ parāyaṇam) is a true knower.\" Śākalya affirms he knows it and identifies the Person; Yājñavalkya then asks/answers its</em> <strong>deity (devatā)</strong>:","<table><tr><td>Mantra</td><td>abode (āyatana)</td><td>world (loka)</td><td>the Person (puruṣa)</td><td>its deity</td></tr><tr><td>3.9.10</td><td>earth</td><td>fire</td><td>the <strong>bodily</strong> person (śārīra)</td><td>the <strong>Immortal</strong> (amṛta)</td></tr><tr><td>3.9.11</td><td>desire (kāma)</td><td>the heart</td><td>the <strong>desire-made</strong> person</td><td><strong>women</strong> (striyaḥ)</td></tr><tr><td>3.9.12</td><td>forms (rūpa)</td><td>the eye</td><td>the person <strong>in the sun</strong></td><td><strong>truth</strong> (satya)</td></tr><tr><td>3.9.13</td><td>space (ākāśa)</td><td>the ear</td><td>the <strong>echo</strong> person (prātiśrutka)</td><td>the <strong>quarters</strong> (diśaḥ)</td></tr><tr><td>3.9.14</td><td>darkness (tamas)</td><td>the heart</td><td>the <strong>shadow</strong> person</td><td><strong>death</strong> (mṛtyu)</td></tr><tr><td>3.9.15</td><td>forms (rūpa)</td><td>the heart</td><td>the person <strong>in the mirror</strong></td><td><strong>truth</strong> (satya)</td></tr><tr><td>3.9.16</td><td>the waters</td><td>the heart</td><td>the person <strong>in the waters</strong></td><td><strong>Varuṇa</strong></td></tr><tr><td>3.9.17</td><td>the seed (retas)</td><td>the heart</td><td>the <strong>son-made</strong> person</td><td><strong>Prajāpati</strong></td></tr></table>","(\\3.9.15: the GRETIL/Śaṅkara-mūla here reads satyam for the mirror-person's deity; some printed editions read asuḥ, \"life.\" The structural point is unaffected.)"]},{"ref":"Mantras 3.9.18–3.9.19","title":"Yājñavalkya turns the tables","sa":["3.9.18: शाकल्येति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः । त्वां स्विदिमे ब्राह्मणा अङ्गारावक्षयणमक्रता३ इति ॥ १८ ॥","3.9.19: याज्ञवल्क्येति होवाच शाकल्यः । यदिदं कुरुपञ्चालानां ब्राह्मणानत्यवादीः किं ब्रह्म विद्वानिति । दिशो वेद सदेवाः सप्रतिष्ठा इति । यद्दिशो वेत्थ सदेवाः सप्रतिष्ठाः ॥ १९ ॥"],"iast":["śākalyeti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ | tvāṃ svid ime brāhmaṇā aṅgārāvakṣayaṇam akratā3 iti || 18 ||","yājñavalkyeti hovāca śākalyaḥ | yad idaṃ kurupañcālānāṃ brāhmaṇān atyavādīḥ kiṃ brahma vidvān iti | diśo veda sadevāḥ sapratiṣṭhā iti | yad diśo vettha sadevāḥ sapratiṣṭhāḥ || 19 ||"],"words":[["śākalya iti ha uvāca yājñavalkyaḥ","voc.","\"Śākalya,\" said Yājñavalkya,"],["tvāṃ svit ime brāhmaṇāḥ aṅgāra-avakṣayaṇam akrata iti","acc. + nom. pl. + n. acc. (a charcoal-tongs / fire-extinguisher) + 3 pl. aor. of kṛ","\"have these brāhmaṇas, perchance, made you their charcoal-remover (their cat's-paw, sent to burn yourself out against me)?\""],["yat idaṃ kurupañcālānāṃ brāhmaṇān atyavādīḥ kiṃ brahma vidvān iti","gen. pl. + acc. pl. + 2 sg. impf. of ati-vad + acc. + pres.part.","(Śākalya:) \"knowing what Brahman is it that you out-talk the brāhmaṇas of the Kurus and Pañcālas?\""],["diśaḥ veda sa-devāḥ sa-pratiṣṭhāḥ iti","f. acc. pl. + adj. (with their deities, with their supports)","\"I know the quarters, together with their deities and their supports\""],["yat diśaḥ vettha sa-devāḥ sa-pratiṣṭhāḥ","","(Śākalya:) \"(if) you know the quarters with their deities and supports (then answer)…\""]],"lit":"\"'Śākalya,' said Yājñavalkya, 'have these brāhmaṇas made you their fire-tongs (sent you to be burnt out against me)?' Śākalya said, 'Yājñavalkya, knowing what Brahman is it that you out-talk the brāhmaṇas of the Kurus and Pañcālas?' (Yājñavalkya:) 'I know the quarters with their deities and their supports.' (Śākalya:) 'If you know the quarters with their deities and supports (then tell me)…'\"","para":"Yājñavalkya, having answered every question, now <strong>counter-attacks</strong>: he taunts Śākalya as the others' <strong>\"cat's-paw\"</strong> (the one sent to be consumed against him, like tongs raking dying coals). Śākalya retorts by demanding what knowledge entitles Yājñavalkya to \"out-talk\" the assembled brāhmaṇas — and Yājñavalkya claims knowledge of <strong>the quarters with their presiding deities and their supports</strong>, inviting the reversed examination.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>aṅgāra-avakṣayaṇa</em> (charcoal-rake/tongs) image is sharp — Śākalya has been thrust forward by the others to wear himself out; Yājñavalkya now becomes the examiner. The shift sets up the fatal questions."],["Krishnananda","the contest's tone turns; the master now tests the challenger, who has been the brāhmaṇas' instrument. Yājñavalkya's claim (the quarters + deities + supports) opens the counter-catechism."],["Sureśvara","the reversal shows that genuine knowledge can both answer all and question all."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the human drama and the proverb-like \"fire-tongs.\""],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the role-reversal and the colloquial taunt as vivid features of the debate."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 3.9.20–3.9.24","title":"The quarters, their deities, and their final support: the heart","sa":["3.9.20 (east — template): किंदेवतोऽस्यां प्राच्यां दिश्यसीति । आदित्यदेवत इति । स आदित्यः कस्मिन्प्रतिष्ठित इति । चक्षुषीति । कस्मिन्नु चक्षुः प्रतिष्ठितमिति । रूपेष्विति । चक्षुषा हि रूपाणि पश्यति । कस्मिन्नु रूपाणि प्रतिष्ठितानीति । हृदय इति होवाच । हृदयेन हि रूपाणि जानाति । हृदये ह्येव रूपाणि प्रतिष्ठितानि भवन्तीति । एवमेवैतद्याज्ञवल्क्य ॥ २० ॥","3.9.24 (the fixed direction): किंदेवतोऽस्यां ध्रुवायां दिश्यसीति । अग्निदेवत इति । सोऽग्निः कस्मिन्प्रतिष्ठित इति । वाचीति । कस्मिन्नु वाक्प्रतिष्ठितेति । हृदय इति । कस्मिन्नु हृदयं प्रतिष्ठितमिति ॥ २४ ॥"],"iast":["kiṃdevato 'syāṃ prācyāṃ diśy asīti | ādityadevata iti | sa ādityaḥ kasmin pratiṣṭhita iti | cakṣuṣīti | kasmin nu cakṣuḥ pratiṣṭhitam iti | rūpeṣv iti | cakṣuṣā hi rūpāṇi paśyati | kasmin nu rūpāṇi pratiṣṭhitānīti | hṛdaya iti hovāca | hṛdayena hi rūpāṇi jānāti | hṛdaye hy eva rūpāṇi pratiṣṭhitāni bhavantīti | evam evaitad yājñavalkya || 20 ||","kiṃdevato 'syāṃ dhruvāyāṃ diśy asīti | agnidevata iti | so 'gniḥ kasmin pratiṣṭhita iti | vācīti | kasmin nu vāk pratiṣṭhiteti | hṛdaya iti | kasmin nu hṛdayaṃ pratiṣṭhitam iti || 24 ||"],"words":[["kiṃ-devataḥ asyāṃ prācyāṃ diśi asi iti","adj. + f. loc. + 2 sg. pres.","\"of which deity are you in this eastern quarter?\""],["āditya-devataḥ iti","nom.","\"of the sun as my deity\""],["saḥ ādityaḥ kasmin pratiṣṭhitaḥ iti","loc. + pp.","\"on what does that sun rest?\""],["cakṣuṣi iti — rūpeṣu iti — hṛdaye iti","loc.","\"on the eye\" — \"on forms\" — \"on the heart\""],["hṛdayena hi rūpāṇi jānāti","instr.","for by the heart (mind) one knows forms"],["hṛdaye hi eva rūpāṇi pratiṣṭhitāni bhavanti iti","loc. + pp.","in the heart, indeed, forms rest"],["evam eva etat yājñavalkya","","\"so it is, Yājñavalkya\" (Śākalya assents)"]],"lit":"\"'Of which deity are you in this eastern quarter?' 'Of the sun as my deity.' 'On what does the sun rest?' 'On the eye.' 'On what does the eye rest?' 'On forms — for by the eye one sees forms.' 'On what do forms rest?' 'On the heart,' he said, 'for by the heart one knows forms; in the heart, indeed, forms rest.' 'So it is, Yājñavalkya.'\" <em>(So for the south → Yama → sacrifice → gift → faith → heart; west → Varuṇa → waters → seed → heart; north → Soma → consecration → truth → heart; the fixed direction → Agni → speech → heart.)</em>","para":"Now Yājñavalkya answers Śākalya's probes about the <strong>quarters</strong>: each direction has a <strong>presiding deity</strong> (sun/east, Yama/south, Varuṇa/west, Soma/north, Agni/zenith), and each deity is traced through a chain of supports that <strong>all converge on the heart (hṛdaya)</strong> — for the heart (here = the mind/inner organ) is where forms, faith, the seed, truth, and speech ultimately \"rest\" (are known and held). The cosmos of directions and deities is grounded in the <strong>heart of the knower</strong>. This sets up the final, fatal question: <em>on what, then, does the heart itself rest?</em> (3.9.24 ends there).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the regress of supports (deity → faculty → object → the heart) shows the <strong>heart/mind as the inner ground</strong> in which all the cosmic directions and their deities are \"established\" for the experiencer — a turning-inward parallel to Gārgī's regress (3.8), now converging on the <em>hṛdaya</em>. But the heart is not the final term: 3.9.25–26 press past it to the Self."],["Sureśvara","all the outer deities rest, for the knower, on the inner organ; the cosmos is gathered into the heart, which itself must rest on the Self."],["Krishnananda","every direction, deity, and object is finally \"located\" in the heart — your own consciousness is where the whole cosmos of quarters stands. The question \"on what does the heart rest?\" then forces the move to the Self that even the heart depends on."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the heart (mind) as the meeting-point of all cosmic worship; the practical convergence of the directions inward."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the systematic direction–deity–support chains terminating in the <em>hṛdaya</em>, and the cliff-hanger of 3.9.24."]],"notes":["<em>Five exchanges (the four quarters + the \"fixed\"/upward direction). For each: \"of which deity are you in this direction?\" → a deity → \"on what does it rest?\" → a chain of supports → ending in the</em> <strong>heart (hṛdaya)</strong>. <em>The chains:</em>","<table><tr><td>Mantra</td><td>direction</td><td>deity</td><td>rests on → … → finally</td></tr><tr><td>3.9.20</td><td><strong>east</strong></td><td>the <strong>sun</strong> (āditya)</td><td>the eye → forms → <strong>the heart</strong></td></tr><tr><td>3.9.21</td><td><strong>south</strong></td><td><strong>Yama</strong></td><td>the sacrifice → the sacrificial gift (dakṣiṇā) → faith (śraddhā) → <strong>the heart</strong></td></tr><tr><td>3.9.22</td><td><strong>west</strong></td><td><strong>Varuṇa</strong></td><td>the waters → the seed (retas) → <strong>the heart</strong></td></tr><tr><td>3.9.23</td><td><strong>north</strong></td><td><strong>Soma</strong></td><td>the consecration (dīkṣā) → truth (satya) → <strong>the heart</strong></td></tr><tr><td>3.9.24</td><td><strong>fixed/zenith</strong></td><td><strong>Agni</strong></td><td>speech (vāc) → <strong>the heart</strong> → \"on what does the heart rest?\"</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.9.25","title":"\"Do not say the heart is elsewhere than in us\"","sa":["अहल्लिकेति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः । यत्रैतदन्यत्रास्मन्मन्यासै । यद्ध्येतदन्यत्रास्मत्स्याच्छ्वानो वैनदद्युर्वयांसि वैनद्विमथ्नीरन्निति ॥ २५ ॥"],"iast":["ahalliketi hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ | yatraitad anyatrāsman manyāsai | yad dhy etad anyatrāsmat syāc chvāno vainad adyur vayāṃsi vainad vimathnīrann iti || 25 ||"],"words":[["ahallika iti ha uvāca yājñavalkyaḥ","voc. (a term of reproach: \"ghost! / dullard!\")","\"Ahallika!\" said Yājñavalkya"],["yatra etat anyatra asmat manyāsai","adv. + n. + adv. (elsewhere than) + abl. (than us) + 2 sg. opt. of man","\"(it would be dire) were you to think this (heart) is anywhere other than in ourselves"],["yat hi etat anyatra asmat syāt","rel. + opt.","for if this (heart) were elsewhere than in us,"],["śvānaḥ vā enat adyuḥ","m. nom. pl. + opt. of ad","dogs would eat it,"],["vayāṃsi vā enat vimathnīran iti","n. nom. pl. + opt. of vi-manth","or birds would tear it to pieces\""]],"lit":"\"'Ahallika!' said Yājñavalkya. '(It would be a sorry thing) were you to suppose this (heart) to be anywhere other than in ourselves; for if this heart were elsewhere than in us, dogs would eat it, or birds tear it to pieces.'\"","para":"Śākalya's question \"on what does the heart rest?\" tempts the error of locating the heart <strong>outside</strong> oneself. Yājñavalkya rebukes him sharply (<em>\"Ahallika!\"</em> — roughly \"you ghost/fool!\"): the heart (the seat of the Self) is <strong>not somewhere external</strong> to be sought — it is <strong>here, in us</strong>; if it were elsewhere, it would be exposed to dogs and birds (i.e., it would be a mere perishable thing). The point: the ground of all the directions and deities is the Self <strong>within</strong>, not any external locus — so the regress must now turn to <em>what the embodied self-and-heart themselves rest on</em> (3.9.26).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the rebuke corrects the impulse to objectify/externalise the heart-Self; the heart (and the Self it seats) is the <strong>innermost</strong>, not an outer object — to seek it \"elsewhere\" is absurd. The vivid image (dogs/birds) underscores that what is external is perishable prey."],["Krishnananda","\"the heart is not outside you\" — the Self is never an external object; Yājñavalkya's sharpness redirects Śākalya from a category-error toward the true inward question."],["Sureśvara","the heart/Self is <em>pratyak</em> (inward), never <em>parāk</em> (outward); the reproach guards against externalising the subject."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a forceful reminder that the Self is to be sought within, not in any place."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes <em>ahallika</em> as an obscure term of abuse and the grim humour of the dogs-and-birds image."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.9.26","title":"The neti-neti Self; the Upaniṣadic Person; Śākalya's death","sa":["कस्मिन्नु त्वं चात्मा च प्रतिष्ठितौ स्थ इति । प्राण इति । कस्मिन्नु प्राणः प्रतिष्ठित इति । अपान इति । कस्मिन्न्वपानः प्रतिष्ठित इति । व्यान इति । कस्मिन्नु व्यानः प्रतिष्ठित इति । उदान इति । कस्मिन्नूदानः प्रतिष्ठित इति । समान इति । स एष नेति नेत्यात्मा । अगृह्यो न हि गृह्यते । अशीर्यो न हि शीर्यते । असङ्गो न सज्यते । असितो न व्यथते । न रिष्यति । एतान्यष्टावायतनान्यष्टौ लोका अष्टौ देवा अष्टौ पुरुषाः । स यस्तान्पुरुषान्निरुह्य प्रत्युह्यात्यक्रामत्तं त्वौपनिषदं पुरुषं पृच्छामि । तं चेन्मे न विवक्ष्यसि मूर्धा ते विपतिष्यतीति । तं ह न मेने शाकल्यः । तस्य ह मूर्धा विपपात । अपि हास्य परिमोषिणोऽस्थीन्यपजह्रुरन्यन्मन्यमानाः ॥ २६ ॥"],"iast":["kasmin nu tvaṃ cātmā ca pratiṣṭhitau stha iti | prāṇa iti | kasmin nu prāṇaḥ pratiṣṭhita iti | apāna iti | … vyāna iti | … udāna iti | … samāna iti | sa eṣa neti nety ātmā | agṛhyo na hi gṛhyate | aśīryo na hi śīryate | asaṅgo na sajyate | asito na vyathate | na riṣyati | etāny aṣṭāv āyatanāny aṣṭau lokā aṣṭau devā aṣṭau puruṣāḥ | sa yas tān puruṣān niruhya pratyuhyātyakrāmat taṃ tvaupaniṣadaṃ puruṣaṃ pṛcchāmi | taṃ cen me na vivakṣyasi mūrdhā te vipatiṣyatīti | taṃ ha na mene śākalyas | tasya ha mūrdhā vipapāta | api hāsya parimoṣiṇo 'sthīny apajahrur anyan manyamānāḥ || 26 ||"],"words":[["kasmin nu tvaṃ ca ātmā ca pratiṣṭhitau sthaḥ iti","loc. + nom. du. + 2 du. pres.","\"on what do you (the body) and the self rest?\""],["prāṇa iti — apāna iti — vyāna iti — udāna iti — samāna iti","loc.","\"on the out-breath\" — \"on the in-breath\" — \"on the diffused breath\" — \"on the up-breath\" — \"on the equalising breath\""],["saḥ eṣaḥ neti neti ātmā","m. nom.","that Self is \"not this, not this\" (neti neti)"],["agṛhyaḥ na hi gṛhyate","adj. + pres. pass.","ungraspable, for it is not grasped;"],["aśīryaḥ na hi śīryate","(undecaying)","undecaying, for it does not decay;"],["asaṅgaḥ na sajyate","(unattached)","unattached, for it does not cling;"],["asitaḥ na vyathate na riṣyati","(unbound/unfettered)","unbound, it neither trembles nor is injured"],["etāni aṣṭau āyatanāni aṣṭau lokāḥ aṣṭau devāḥ aṣṭau puruṣāḥ","n./m. nom. pl.","these are the eight abodes, eight worlds, eight deities, eight persons"],["saḥ yaḥ tān puruṣān niruhya pratyuhya atyakrāmat","acc. pl. + ger. + ger. + 3 sg. impf. of ati-kram","he who, having drawn out and put back those persons, transcended (them) —"],["taṃ tvā aupaniṣadaṃ puruṣaṃ pṛcchāmi","acc. + 1 sg. pres.","that Upaniṣadic Person I ask you about"],["taṃ cet me na vivakṣyasi mūrdhā te vipatiṣyati iti","acc. + fut. + nom. + fut. of vi-pat","if you will not tell me of him, your head shall fall off"],["taṃ ha na mene śākalyaḥ","acc. + 3 sg. impf. of man","Śākalya did not know him"],["tasya ha mūrdhā vipapāta","m. nom. + 3 sg. pf. of vi-pat","his head fell off"],["api ha asya parimoṣiṇaḥ asthīni apajahruḥ anyat manyamānāḥ","nom. pl. (robbers) + n. acc. + 3 pl. pf. + pres.part.","and robbers even carried off his bones, taking them for something else"]],"lit":"\"'On what do you (the body) and the self rest?' 'On the out-breath (prāṇa).' 'On what does the out-breath rest?' 'On the in-breath (apāna).' '…on the diffused breath (vyāna)' … '…on the up-breath (udāna)' … '…on the equalising breath (samāna).' (Yājñavalkya then declares:) 'That Self is <strong>\"not this, not this\" (neti neti)</strong>: ungraspable, for it is not grasped; undecaying, for it does not decay; unattached, for it does not cling; unbound, it neither trembles nor is injured. These are the eight abodes, the eight worlds, the eight deities, the eight persons. He who, having drawn out and put back those persons, has gone beyond them — <strong>that Upaniṣadic Person I (now) ask you about.</strong> If you do not tell me of him, your head shall fall off.' Śākalya did not know him; and his head fell off — and robbers carried off his bones, mistaking them for something else.\"","para":"The climax. Yājñavalkya leads the regress past the heart to the <strong>five breaths</strong> (prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna) as the supports of the embodied self — and then names the ultimate ground: the <strong>Self that is <em>neti neti</em>, \"not this, not this\"</strong> (cf. 2.3.6, 3.8.8). It is <strong>ungraspable</strong> (cannot be seized as an object), <strong>undecaying, unattached, unbound</strong> — beyond all change, suffering, and harm. Having reviewed the <strong>eight persons</strong> (the determinate selves of 3.9.10–17), Yājñavalkya poses the decisive question: <strong>who is the <em>aupaniṣada puruṣa</em> — the secret \"Upaniṣadic Person\" — who transcends all the eight persons</strong> (drawing them out in waking/projection and withdrawing them in sleep)? This indeterminate, witnessing Self is precisely what Śākalya, master of the <em>determinate</em> gods and persons, <strong>does not know</strong> — and, per the contest's stake, <strong>his head falls off</strong>, his bones later scavenged by robbers. The man who knew every god could not name the Self beyond them.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the supreme <em>neti-neti</em> passage of the kāṇḍa. After tracing supports to the breaths, Yājñavalkya points to the <strong>attributeless witnessing Self</strong> — <em>agṛhya</em> (ungraspable, since it is the grasper, never the grasped), <em>aśīrya</em> (undecaying), <em>asaṅga</em> (unattached — untouched by what it illumines), <em>asita</em> (unbound — free, hence neither trembling nor injured). The <em>aupaniṣada puruṣa</em> (\"the Person known only through the Upaniṣad / the secret teaching\") is the <strong>non-dual Self</strong> beyond all the conditioned \"persons\"; Śākalya, expert in <em>saguṇa</em> deities, fails at the <em>nirguṇa</em> Self. His \"head falling off\" dramatises the stake — and, allegorically, the <strong>collapse of the merely ritual-mythic intellect</strong> before the unconditioned. (Śaṅkara treats the death as the literal fulfilment of the wager and a caution against debating the Self without realisation.)"],["Sureśvara","the four negations (<em>agṛhya, aśīrya, asaṅga, asita</em>) define the Self purely apophatically — what cannot be objectified, decay, cling, or be bound; the <em>aupaniṣada puruṣa</em> is that, knowable only through the Upaniṣadic word, not through enumeration of deities."],["Krishnananda","\"You can know all the gods and still not know the Self.\" Śākalya's vast learning in the determinate could not reach the indeterminate witness; the <em>neti-neti</em> Self is the one that <em>uses</em> all the persons (projects them in experience, withdraws them in sleep) and is reducible to none. The death is the symbolic shattering of knowledge that stops at objects."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical seal — meditate on the Self as <em>neti neti</em>, ungraspable and unattached; do not mistake mastery of doctrine or deities for realisation of the witness."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the recurrence of <em>neti neti</em> and the <em>aupaniṣada puruṣa</em> (\"the person of the hidden teaching\"), and reads Śākalya's death as the formulaic fulfilment of the <em>brahmodya</em> stake — the ninth and final defeat, the gravest."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.9.27","title":"Yājñavalkya's open challenge; none dares","sa":["अथ होवाच — ब्राह्मणा भगवन्तो यो वः कामयते स मा पृच्छतु । सर्वे वा मा पृच्छत । यो वः कामयते तं वः पृच्छामि सर्वान्वा वः पृच्छामीति । ते ह ब्राह्मणा न दधृषुः ॥ २७ ॥"],"iast":["atha hovāca -- brāhmaṇā bhagavanto yo vaḥ kāmayate sa mā pṛcchatu | sarve vā mā pṛcchata | yo vaḥ kāmayate taṃ vaḥ pṛcchāmi sarvān vā vaḥ pṛcchāmīti | te ha brāhmaṇā na dadhṛṣuḥ || 27 ||"],"words":[["brāhmaṇāḥ bhagavantaḥ yaḥ vaḥ kāmayate saḥ mā pṛcchatu","voc. + rel. + 3 sg. pres. + impv. of prach","\"revered brāhmaṇas, whoever of you wishes, let him question me;"],["sarve vā mā pṛcchata","nom. pl. + impv.","or all of you question me;"],["yaḥ vaḥ kāmayate taṃ vaḥ pṛcchāmi sarvān vā vaḥ pṛcchāmi iti","acc. + 1 sg. pres.","or whoever of you (wishes), him I will question — or I will question you all\""],["te ha brāhmaṇāḥ na dadhṛṣuḥ","3 pl. pf. of dhṛṣ","the brāhmaṇas did not dare"]],"lit":"\"Then he said: 'Revered brāhmaṇas, whoever of you wishes, let him question me; or question me, all of you. Or whoever of you (wishes), him will I question; or I will question you all.' But the brāhmaṇas did not dare (to respond).\"","para":"With all nine challengers answered (the last fatally), Yājñavalkya <strong>throws the contest fully open</strong>: let any or all question him, or let him question any or all of them. <strong>None dares.</strong> His supremacy as <em>brahmiṣṭha</em> is now beyond dispute — he has earned the thousand cows (3.1.2) and the title.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the open, unanswered challenge formally establishes Yājñavalkya's victory; the silence of the assembly is the public verdict."],["Krishnananda","the master stands unchallenged — the debate is over; what follows (3.9.28) is not contest but his own spontaneous teaching."],["Sureśvara/Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the assembly's silence certifies the completeness of his knowledge."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the conventional close of the <em>brahmodya</em> — the undefeated sage opens the floor and is met with silence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.9.28","title":"The tree-simile and \"Brahman is consciousness and bliss\"","sa":["यथा वृक्षो वनस्पतिस्तथैव पुरुषोऽमृषा । तस्य लोमानि पर्णानि त्वगस्योत्पाटिका बहिः ॥ १ ॥","त्वच एवास्य रुधिरं प्रस्यन्दि त्वच उत्पटः । तस्मात्तदातृण्णात्प्रैति रसो वृक्षादिवाहतात् ॥ २ ॥","मांसान्यस्य शकराणि किनाटं स्नाव तत्स्थिरम् । अस्थीन्यन्तरतो दारूणि मज्जा मज्जोपमा कृता ॥ ३ ॥","यद्वृक्षो वृक्णो रोहति मूलान्नवतरः पुनः । मर्त्यः स्विन्मृत्युना वृक्णः कस्मान्मूलात्प्ररोहति ॥ ४ ॥","रेतस इति मा वोचत जीवतस्तत्प्रजायते । धानारुह इव वै वृक्षोऽञ्जसा प्रेत्य सम्भवः ॥ ५ ॥","यत्समूलमावृहेयुर्वृक्षं न पुनराभवेत् । मर्त्यः स्विन्मृत्युना वृक्णः कस्मान्मूलात्प्ररोहति ॥ ६ ॥","जात एव न जायते को न्वेनं जनयेत्पुनः । विज्ञानमानन्दं ब्रह्म रातिर्दातुः परायणम् । तिष्ठमानस्य तद्विद इति ॥ २८ ॥"],"iast":["yathā vṛkṣo vanaspatis tathaiva puruṣo 'mṛṣā | tasya lomāni parṇāni tvag asyotpāṭikā bahiḥ || (1)","tvaca evāsya rudhiraṃ prasyandi tvaca utpaṭaḥ | tasmāt tad ātṛṇṇāt praiti raso vṛkṣād ivāhatāt || (2)","māṃsāny asya śakarāṇi kināṭaṃ snāva tat sthiram | asthīny antarato dāruṇi majjā majjopamā kṛtā || (3)","yad vṛkṣo vṛkṇo rohati mūlān navataraḥ punaḥ | martyaḥ svin mṛtyunā vṛkṇaḥ kasmān mūlāt prarohati || (4)","retasa iti mā vocata jīvatas tat prajāyate | dhānāruha iva vai vṛkṣo 'ñjasā pretya sambhavaḥ || (5)","yat samūlam āvṛheyur vṛkṣaṃ na punar ābhavet | martyaḥ svin mṛtyunā vṛkṇaḥ kasmān mūlāt prarohati || (6)","jāta eva na jāyate ko nv enaṃ janayet punaḥ | vijñānam ānandaṃ brahma rātir dātuḥ parāyaṇaṃ tiṣṭhamānasya tadvida iti || 28 ||"],"words":[["yathā vṛkṣaḥ vanaspatiḥ tathā eva puruṣaḥ amṛṣā","nom. + adv. (truly)","just as a tree, a lord of the forest, so truly is a man"],["tasya lomāni parṇāni tvak asya utpāṭikā bahiḥ","n. nom. pl. + f. nom.","his hairs are the leaves, his skin the outer bark"],["(vv. 2–3)","","his blood is the sap that flows from the bark; flesh = inner bark, sinews = the tough fibre, bones = the wood within, marrow = the pith"],["yat vṛkṣaḥ vṛkṇaḥ rohati mūlāt navataraḥ punaḥ","pp. (felled) + pres. of ruh + abl.","when a felled tree grows up anew from its root,"],["martyaḥ svit mṛtyunā vṛkṇaḥ kasmāt mūlāt prarohati","nom. + instr. + abl. + pres.","(but) from what root does a mortal, felled by death, grow up again?"],["retasaḥ iti mā vocata jīvataḥ tat prajāyate","abl. + proh. + gen. + pres.","do not say \"from the seed\" — for that is produced (only) from the living"],["dhānā-ruhaḥ iva vai vṛkṣaḥ añjasā pretya sambhavaḥ","nom. (springing from seed) + adv. + ger. + m. nom.","a tree springs up from a seed too, (but only the living tree bears seed); so birth-after-death (must come from elsewhere)"],["yat samūlam āvṛheyuḥ vṛkṣaṃ na punaḥ ābhavet","acc. (with the root) + opt. of ā-vṛh + opt.","if one tore up a tree with its root, it would not grow again;"],["jātaḥ eva na jāyate kaḥ nu enaṃ janayet punaḥ","pp. + pres. + interrog. + opt. of jan (caus.)","(he is) already born, (and) is not (re-)born — who could beget him again?"],["vijñānam ānandaṃ brahma","n. nom.","Brahman is consciousness and bliss (vijñāna-ānanda)"],["rātiḥ dātuḥ parāyaṇam tiṣṭhamānasya tadvidaḥ iti","f. nom. (the gift/wealth) + gen. + n. nom. + gen. + gen. (the knower of That)","(Brahman is) the supreme wealth of the giver, and the final refuge of (even) the one who, standing (firm), knows That"]],"lit":"\"Just as a tree, a lord of the forest, so truly is a man: his hairs are the leaves, his skin the outer bark; from his skin blood flows as sap from the bark; his flesh is the inner bark, his sinews the tough fibre, his bones the wood within, his marrow the pith. When a felled tree grows up again from its root — from what root does a mortal, felled by death, grow again? Do not say 'from the seed' — for seed is produced only from the living. A tree may spring from a seed too; but (the question of) birth-after-death (is not answered so). If a tree be torn up with its root, it grows no more — (so) from what root does the mortal, once felled by death, grow again? <strong>He who is (truly) born is not born (again) — who could beget him anew?</strong> <strong>Brahman is consciousness and bliss (vijñāna-ānanda)</strong> — the supreme wealth of the giver (of gifts), and the final refuge of one who, established (in It), knows It.\"","para":"Yājñavalkya closes with his own teaching in <strong>verse</strong>. The famous <strong>tree-simile</strong>: a person is like a great tree — hair/leaves, skin/bark, blood/sap, flesh/inner-bark, sinew/fibre, bone/wood, marrow/pith. But the simile breaks at the crucial point: a felled <strong>tree regrows from its root</strong>; from what \"root\" does a <strong>mortal, cut down by death, grow again?</strong> Not \"from the seed,\" for seed comes only from the <em>already living</em> — so seed cannot be the <em>first</em> root of a new birth (the regress fails to ground rebirth in mere biology). The resolution is the deepest line of the chapter: <strong>\"He who is (already) born is not (re-)born — who could beget him again?\"</strong> — the <strong>Self is unborn (aja)</strong>, never produced, and so never re-produced; it is not subject to the birth-death of the tree-like body. And what <em>is</em> that unborn reality? <strong>\"vijñānam ānandaṃ brahma\" — Brahman is Consciousness and Bliss</strong> (<em>vijñāna-ānanda</em>) — the <strong>supreme wealth/refuge</strong> of the generous knower who stands firm in It. (This is among the Upaniṣad's most concentrated definitions of Brahman, anticipating the later <em>sat-cit-ānanda</em>.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the tree-simile presses the question of <strong>what survives and grounds rebirth</strong>, and answers by transcending it: the body (tree) perishes and \"regrows\" only as karma dictates, but the <strong>Self is <em>aja</em> (unborn)</strong> — \"already (eternally) 'born' (existent), it is not born again,\" for there is no agent that could produce the ever-existent. Its true nature is <strong>vijñāna-ānanda — Brahman as consciousness-bliss</strong>: not two qualities added to Brahman, but Brahman's very being as <strong>self-luminous awareness that is fullness/bliss</strong>. This is the <em>nirguṇa</em> Self positively indicated (after the <em>neti-neti</em> of 3.9.26) as <strong>cit (consciousness) and ānanda (bliss)</strong> — the seed of the classical <em>saccidānanda</em>. \"Refuge of the giver who knows It\" = the realised, liberal soul rests in Brahman as its all."],["Sureśvara","the failure of \"from the seed\" shows that <strong>rebirth cannot be grounded in matter</strong>; the unborn Self is the only reality that neither arises nor perishes. <em>Vijñānam ānandaṃ brahma</em> defines Brahman as consciousness-bliss — the witness (3.9.26) now named positively."],["Krishnananda","\"The tree grows again from its root, but what is the root of man?\" — not the seed (which presupposes the living), but the <strong>eternal, unborn Self</strong>, which is <strong>Consciousness-Bliss (Brahman)</strong>. The verse moves from the perishable body (the tree) to the imperishable, unborn ground; and names that ground <em>vijñāna-ānanda</em> — pure awareness that is pure joy. A magnificent culmination of the whole kāṇḍa."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical assurance — your true Self is <em>unborn</em> and is <strong>Brahman, consciousness and bliss</strong>; rest in It as the final refuge. The body (tree) comes and goes; the Self does not."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the celebrated tree-of-man simile (cf. Kaṭha 6, Gītā 15) and the formula <em>vijñānam ānandaṃ brahma</em> as an early, compact statement of Brahman as consciousness-and-bliss, closing the Yājñavalkya-debate adhyāya."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.9","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The final and longest round is the <strong>summit of the whole debate</strong> and a microcosm of the Upaniṣad's method — from the many to the One, from the determinate to the indeterminate:","1. <strong>The many gods resolved into One (3.9.1–9).</strong> The descending count (3,306 → 33 → 6 → 3 → 2 → 1½ → <strong>1</strong>) reduces the entire pantheon to <strong>prāṇa = Brahman (tyat)</strong> — polytheism reconciled with monism: the gods are the <em>powers</em> of the one Reality.","2. <strong>The eight conditioned Persons (3.9.10–17).</strong> The Self as present in body, desire, sun, echo, shadow, mirror, water, and progeny — each with its abode, world (the mind as \"light\"), and deity. These are the <em>determinate</em> selves Śākalya can name.","3. <strong>The cosmos gathered into the heart (3.9.18–24).</strong> The directions and their deities all rest, through chains of support, on the <strong>heart (hṛdaya)</strong> — the inner ground of all outer worship; and the heart is not \"elsewhere\" (3.9.25) but within.","4. <strong>The indeterminate Self, <em>neti neti</em> (3.9.26).</strong> Past the heart and the five breaths stands the <strong>Upaniṣadic Person</strong> — the Self that is <strong>\"not this, not this\": ungraspable, undecaying, unattached, unbound</strong> — the witness that <em>uses</em> all eight persons and is none of them. Śākalya, master of the determinate gods, <strong>cannot name the indeterminate Self, and dies</strong> (the ninth, gravest defeat).","5. <strong>The unborn Brahman as Consciousness-Bliss (3.9.27–28).</strong> Yājñavalkya stands unchallenged, and closes with the <strong>tree-simile</strong>: the body, like a tree, perishes and \"regrows\" by karma, but the <strong>Self is unborn (aja)</strong> — what is already eternally existent cannot be re-begotten — and its nature is <strong>vijñānam ānandaṃ brahma, \"Brahman is consciousness and bliss.\"</strong>","Thus Adhyāya 3 climbs, challenger by challenger, from <strong>ritual</strong> (3.1) and <strong>death/karma</strong> (3.2–3.3) through the <strong>inner Self</strong> (3.4–3.5), the <strong>regress of grounds</strong> (3.6, 3.8), and the <strong>inner controller</strong> (3.7), to the final disclosure: the one Reality behind all gods and all questions is the <strong>unborn, attributeless, self-luminous Self — Brahman, consciousness and bliss</strong> — known by <em>neti neti</em> and realised, not merely debated (as Śākalya's fate warns)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.9","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   ŚĀKALYA&#x27;S CONTEST (BU 3.9)\n   ==========================\n   &quot;HOW MANY GODS?&quot;  3306 -&gt; 33 -&gt; 6 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 1½ -&gt; 1\n        33 = 8 Vasus + 11 Rudras + 12 Ādityas + Indra + Prajāpati\n        ... -&gt; 2 = food &amp; breath -&gt; 1½ = the wind -&gt; 1 = PRĀṆA = BRAHMAN (tyat)\n\n   EIGHT PERSONS (abode / world / mind=light) -&gt; deity:\n        body→Immortal · desire→women · sun→truth · echo→quarters\n        shadow→death · mirror→truth · water→Varuṇa · son→Prajāpati\n\n   (Yājñavalkya turns examiner) THE QUARTERS rest, via chains, on -&gt; THE HEART\n        east→sun→eye→forms→HEART | south→Yama→sacrifice→gift→faith→HEART\n        west→Varuṇa→waters→seed→HEART | north→Soma→dīkṣā→truth→HEART | fixed→Agni→speech→HEART\n        &quot;on what does the HEART rest?&quot;  -&gt; &quot;Ahallika! not elsewhere than in us&quot;\n\n   THE GROUND (3.9.26):  body &amp; self rest on -&gt; prāṇa→apāna→vyāna→udāna→samāna\n        =&gt; THE SELF = NETI NETI :  agṛhya (ungraspable) · aśīrya (undecaying)\n                                   asaṅga (unattached) · asita (unbound) — unhurt, untrembling\n        the AUPANIṢADA PURUṢA (who transcends the 8 persons)\n        Śākalya cannot name him  -&gt;  HIS HEAD FALLS OFF  (defeat 9 of 9)\n\n   (none dares; 3.9.27)  -&gt;  THE TREE-SIMILE (3.9.28):\n        man = tree (hair/leaves, skin/bark, blood/sap, bone/wood, marrow/pith)\n        tree regrows from root — but the mortal, felled by death? NOT &quot;from seed&quot;\n        =&gt; the SELF is UNBORN (jāta eva na jāyate) — &quot;who could beget him again?&quot;\n        ====&gt;  VIJÑĀNAM ĀNANDAṂ BRAHMA  (&quot;Brahman is consciousness and bliss&quot;)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 3.9","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Resolve the many into the One.</strong> Whenever multiplicity overwhelms (many gods, many forces, many problems), practise Yājñavalkya's descent: trace the many to their fewer powers, and those to the single life/awareness behind them. \"How many?\" finally answers \"<strong>One.</strong>\"","2. <strong>Know the persons, but seek the Person.</strong> You can map the Self in body, desire, perception, memory, progeny (the eight persons) — but do not stop there. Ask after the <strong>one witness</strong> that projects and withdraws them all (the <em>aupaniṣada puruṣa</em>).","3. <strong>Gather the world into the heart, then beyond.</strong> Let all outer objects and \"deities\" be seen to rest in your own awareness (the heart); then ask what the heart itself rests on — and find the <em>neti-neti</em> Self that supports even it.","4. <strong>Hold the Self as <em>neti neti</strong></em>: ungraspable, undecaying, unattached, unbound. In meditation, release every object and quality; abide as the witness that cannot be seized, cannot decay, cannot cling, cannot be hurt.","5. <strong>Heed Śākalya's warning.</strong> Vast learning about gods, doctrines, and cosmologies is <em>not</em> realisation of the Self. Knowledge that stops at objects \"loses its head\" before the witness. Let study ripen into direct recognition.","6. <strong>Rest in the unborn Consciousness-Bliss.</strong> Carry the closing truth: your real Self is <strong>unborn</strong> (the tree dies and regrows, but the Self is never begotten), and its nature is <strong>Brahman — vijñāna and ānanda, consciousness and bliss.</strong> That is the final refuge."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Colophon","title":"End of Adhyāya 3","sa":["इति बृहदारण्यकोपनिषदि तृतीयोऽध्यायः ॥"],"iast":["iti bṛhadāraṇyakopaniṣadi tṛtīyo 'dhyāyaḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Thus the Third Adhyāya of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"What Adhyāya 3 has established","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Adhyāya 3 opens the <strong>Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa</strong> with the great debate in <strong>Janaka's court</strong>, where Yājñavalkya defeats <strong>nine challengers</strong> in turn, the inquiry climbing from ritual to the unconditioned Self:","- <strong>3.1 Aśvala</strong> — the sacrifice and its priests release the sacrificer from death/time; the infinite is won by the infinite mind.","- <strong>3.2 Ārtabhāga</strong> — the eight <strong>graspers/over-graspers</strong> (bondage); death is not absolute; and the secret of continuity is <strong>karma</strong> (\"good by good action, bad by bad\").","- <strong>3.3 Bhujyu</strong> — the souls of the great sacrificers are borne by <strong>Vāyu</strong>, which is both each life (<em>vyaṣṭi</em>) and all life (<em>samaṣṭi</em>).","- <strong>3.4 Uṣasta</strong> &amp; <strong>3.5 Kahola</strong> — the Self is the <strong>unobjectifiable Subject</strong> (\"you cannot see the seer of seeing\"), beyond hunger, sorrow, and death; knowing it issues in <strong>renunciation</strong> (the <em>pāṇḍitya → bālya → mauna</em> path).","- <strong>3.6 Gārgī (I)</strong> — the <strong>regress of grounds</strong> (\"warp and woof\") must halt at the unconditioned, which is \"not to be over-questioned.\"","- <strong>3.7 Uddālaka</strong> — the <strong>thread (Vāyu)</strong> and the <strong>inner controller (antaryāmin)</strong>: the one Self dwells within and governs all, the <strong>unseen Seer</strong>.","- <strong>3.8 Gārgī (II)</strong> — the <strong>Imperishable (akṣara)</strong>, described by <strong>neti neti</strong>, sovereign over sun, moon, and time, and identical with the witnessing Self on which even <strong>space</strong> is woven.","- <strong>3.9 Śākalya</strong> — the many gods resolved into <strong>One (prāṇa = Brahman)</strong>; the <strong>neti-neti</strong> Self beyond all \"persons\" (Śākalya's fatal failure); and the closing definition: <strong>the unborn Self is Brahman — consciousness and bliss (vijñānam ānandaṃ brahma).</strong>","The kāṇḍa's verdict: behind every ritual, every god, every cosmic ground, and every faculty stands the <strong>one unconditioned, self-luminous Self — ungraspable, unborn, consciousness-bliss</strong> — known by negating all else (<em>neti neti</em>) and realised in being, not won by debate.","<em>Continued in Adhyāya 4 (the Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa, concluded): Yājñavalkya's further teachings to King Janaka — the \"abodes\" and lights of the self and the famous \"neti neti\" definition (4.1–4.2); the great discourse on the states of waking, dream, and deep sleep, the self-luminous Self, and the process of dying and rebirth (4.3–4.4); and the second Maitreyī dialogue (4.5), before the lineage (4.6).</em>"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 4","u":[{"ref":"Adhyāya 4","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Adhyāya 4 concludes the</em> <strong>Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa</strong>. <em>In private audience with</em> <strong>King Janaka</strong>, <em>Yājñavalkya first completes the</em> <strong>partial definitions of Brahman</strong> <em>given by six other teachers (4.1), reveals the</em> <strong>persons in the eyes</strong> <em>and the heart's channels with the formula</em> <strong>neti neti</strong> <em>(4.2), and then delivers the Upaniṣad's two greatest discourses: the analysis of the</em> <strong>lights of man and the three states — waking, dream, and deep sleep — and the self-luminous Self</strong> <em>(4.3), and the account of</em> <strong>death, transmigration, karma, and liberation</strong>, <em>with the caterpillar and goldsmith similes and the verses on the deathless knower (4.4). It closes with the</em> <strong>second Maitreyī dialogue</strong> <em>(4.5) and a</em> <strong>lineage</strong> <em>(4.6).</em>"],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 4 · Brāhmaṇa 4.1 — Six partial definitions of Brahman, completed","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 4.1","title":"Six partial definitions of Brahman, completed","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Janaka reports six teachings he has received — that Brahman is</em> <strong>speech, breath, the eye, the ear, the mind,</strong> <em>or</em> <strong>the heart</strong>. <em>Each time Yājñavalkya grants that the teacher named a true</em> <strong>abode (āyatana)</strong> <em>of Brahman but left it</em> <strong>\"one-footed\" (ekapād)</strong> <em>— failing to give its</em> <strong>support (pratiṣṭhā)</strong> <em>and the</em> <strong>aspect to meditate on</strong>. <em>He completes each: the faculty is the abode,</em> <strong>space (ākāśa) the support</strong>, <em>and a quality (intelligence, the dear, truth, the infinite, bliss, stability) the meditation — each being \"the supreme Brahman\" for its worshipper.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 4.1.1","title":"\"Cattle or subtle questions?\"","sa":["जनको ह वैदेह आसांचक्रे । अथ ह याज्ञवल्क्य आवव्राज । तं होवाच — याज्ञवल्क्य किमर्थमचारीः पशूनिच्छन्नण्वन्तानिति । उभयमेव संराडिति होवाच ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["janako ha vaideha āsāṃ cakre | atha ha yājñavalkya āvavrāja | taṃ hovāca -- yājñavalkya kim artham acārīḥ paśūn icchan aṇvantānīti | ubhayam eva saṃrāḍ iti hovāca || 1 ||"],"words":[["janakaḥ ha vaidehaḥ āsāṃ cakre","periphr. pf. of ās","Janaka of Videha was seated (giving audience)"],["atha ha yājñavalkyaḥ āvavrāja","3 sg. pf. of ā-vraj","then Yājñavalkya came up"],["taṃ ha uvāca","acc. + pf.","(Janaka) said to him"],["yājñavalkya kim artham acārīḥ","2 sg. aor. of car","\"Yājñavalkya, for what purpose have you come?"],["paśūn icchan aṇvantān iti","acc. pl. + pres.part. + acc. pl. (subtle matters)","wishing for cattle, or for subtle questions?\""],["ubhayam eva samrāṭ iti hovāca","n. acc. + voc.","\"for both, your majesty,\" he said"]],"lit":"\"Janaka of Videha was seated (in audience). Yājñavalkya came up. Janaka said to him, 'Yājñavalkya, why have you come — wishing for cattle, or for subtle disputations?' 'For both, your majesty,' he said.\"","para":"A candid, almost playful opening. Janaka asks whether the sage seeks <strong>wealth (cattle)</strong> or <strong>subtle philosophical exchange (aṇvantān — \"fine points\")</strong>. Yājñavalkya answers, with disarming honesty, <strong>\"both\"</strong> — he values the king's gifts <em>and</em> the chance to deepen the teaching. The dialogue that follows is both reward-bearing and profound.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the candour shows the sage need not feign indifference to gifts; he accepts wealth as the householder's due while pursuing the highest knowledge. The exchange frames the intimate Janaka–Yājñavalkya teaching that fills 4.1–4.4."],["Krishnananda","a humane, witty start to the most exalted discourses — the teacher is unpretentious, the king eager; their friendship carries the deepest wisdom."],["Sureśvara","\"both\" — knowledge and its honourable rewards are not opposed for the householder-sage."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the warmth between rājarṣi and brahmarṣi."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the recurring Janaka–Yājñavalkya relationship and the colloquial realism of the opening."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.1.2–4.1.7","title":"The six teachings completed","sa":["4.1.2 (speech — full template): यत्ते कश्चिदब्रवीत्तच्छृणवामेति । अब्रवीन्मे जित्वा शैलिनिः — वाग्वै ब्रह्मेति । यथा मातृमान्पितृमानाचार्यवान्ब्रूयात्तथा तच्छैलिनिरब्रवीद्वाग्वै ब्रह्मेति । अवदतो हि किं स्यादिति । अब्रवीत्तु ते तस्यायतनं प्रतिष्ठाम् । न मेऽब्रवीदिति । एकपाद्वा एतत्संराडिति । स वै नो ब्रूहि याज्ञवल्क्य । वागेवायतनमाकाशः प्रतिष्ठा प्रज्ञेत्येनदुपासीत । का प्रज्ञता याज्ञवल्क्य । वागेव संराडिति होवाच । वाचा वै संराड्बन्धुः प्रज्ञायते । ऋग्वेदो यजुर्वेदः सामवेदोऽथर्वाङ्गिरस इतिहासः पुराणं विद्या उपनिषदः श्लोकाः सूत्राण्यनुव्याख्यानानि व्याख्यानानीष्टं हुतमाशितं पायितमयं च लोकः परश्च लोकः सर्वाणि च भूतानि वाचैव संराट्प्रज्ञायन्ते । वाग्वै संराट्परमं ब्रह्म । नैनं वाग्जहाति । सर्वाण्येनं भूतान्यभिक्षरन्ति । देवो भूत्वा देवानप्येति । य एवं विद्वानेतदुपास्ते । हस्त्यृषभं सहस्रं ददामीति होवाच जनको वैदेहः । स होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः — पिता मेऽमन्यत नाननुशिष्य हरेतेति ॥ २ ॥","4.1.3 (breath): …अब्रवीन्म उदङ्कः शौल्बायनः — प्राणो वै ब्रह्मेति …प्राण एवायतनमाकाशः प्रतिष्ठा प्रियमित्येनदुपासीत …प्राणो वै संराट्परमं ब्रह्म … ॥ ३ ॥","4.1.4 (eye): …अब्रवीन्मे बर्कुर्वार्ष्णः — चक्षुर्वै ब्रह्मेति …चक्षुरेवायतनमाकाशः प्रतिष्ठा सत्यमित्येतदुपासीत …चक्षुर्वै संराट्परमं ब्रह्म … ॥ ४ ॥","4.1.5 (ear): …अब्रवीन्मे गर्दभीविपीतो भारद्वाजः — श्रोत्रं वै ब्रह्मेति …श्रोत्रमेवायतनमाकाशः प्रतिष्ठानन्तमित्येनदुपासीत …दिशो वै संराट्श्रोत्रम् …श्रोत्रं वै संराट्परमं ब्रह्म … ॥ ५ ॥","4.1.6 (mind): …अब्रवीन्मे सत्यकामो जाबालः — मनो वै ब्रह्मेति …मन एवायतनमाकाशः प्रतिष्ठानन्द इत्येनदुपासीत …मनो वै संराट्परमं ब्रह्म … ॥ ६ ॥","4.1.7 (heart): …अब्रवीन्मे विदग्धः शाकल्यः — हृदयं वै ब्रह्मेति …हृदयमेवायतनमाकाशः प्रतिष्ठा स्थितिरित्येनदुपासीत …हृदयं वै संराट्सर्वेषां भूतानामायतनम् …हृदयं वै संराट्परमं ब्रह्म … ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["yat te kaścid abravīt tac chṛṇavāmeti | abravīn me jitvā śailiniḥ -- vāg vai brahmeti | yathā mātṛmān pitṛmān ācāryavān brūyāt tathā tac chailinir abravīd vāg vai brahmeti | avadato hi kiṃ syād iti | abravīt tu te tasyāyatanaṃ pratiṣṭhām | na me 'bravīd iti | ekapād vā etat samrāḍ iti | sa vai no brūhi yājñavalkya | vāg evāyatanam ākāśaḥ pratiṣṭhā prajñety enad upāsīta | kā prajñatā yājñavalkya | vāg eva samrāḍ iti hovāca | vācā vai samrāḍ bandhuḥ prajñāyate … vācaiva saṃrāṭ prajñāyante | vāg vai samrāṭ paramaṃ brahma | nainaṃ vāg jahāti | sarvāṇy enaṃ bhūtāny abhikṣaranti | devo bhūtvā devān apyeti | ya evaṃ vidvān etad upāste | hastyṛṣabhaṃ sahasraṃ dadāmīti hovāca janako vaidehaḥ | sa hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ -- pitā me 'manyata nānanuśiṣya hareteti || 2 ||","…abravīn ma udaṅkaḥ śaulbāyanaḥ -- prāṇo vai brahmeti … prāṇa evāyatanam ākāśaḥ pratiṣṭhā priyam ity enad upāsīta … prāṇo vai samrāṭ paramaṃ brahma … || 3 ||","…abravīn me barkur vārṣṇaḥ -- cakṣur vai brahmeti … cakṣur evāyatanam ākāśaḥ pratiṣṭhā satyam ity etad upāsīta … cakṣur vai samrāṭ paramaṃ brahma … || 4 ||","…abravīn me gardabhīvipīto bhāradvājaḥ -- śrotraṃ vai brahmeti … śrotram evāyatanam ākāśaḥ pratiṣṭhānantam ity enad upāsīta … diśo vai samrāṭ śrotram … śrotraṃ vai samrāṭ paramaṃ brahma … || 5 ||","…abravīn me satyakāmo jābālaḥ -- mano vai brahmeti … mana evāyatanam ākāśaḥ pratiṣṭhānanda ity enad upāsīta … mano vai samrāṭ paramaṃ brahma … || 6 ||","…abravīn me vidagdhaḥ śākalyaḥ -- hṛdayaṃ vai brahmeti … hṛdayam evāyatanam ākāśaḥ pratiṣṭhā sthitir ity enad upāsīta … hṛdayaṃ vai samrāṭ sarveṣāṃ bhūtānām āyatanam … hṛdayaṃ vai samrāṭ paramaṃ brahma … || 7 ||"],"words":[["yat te kaścit abravīt tat śṛṇavāma iti","rel. + dat. + 3 sg. impf. + 1 pl. subj. of śru","\"let us hear what someone has told you\""],["abravīt me jitvā śailiniḥ vāk vai brahma iti","dat./gen. + m. nom.","\"Jitvan Śailini told me: 'Speech is Brahman.'\""],["yathā mātṛmān pitṛmān ācāryavān brūyāt tathā tat abravīt","adjs. (having mother, father, teacher) + opt.","he spoke (rightly), as one who has mother, father, and teacher would speak"],["avadataḥ hi kiṃ syāt iti","gen. pres.part. (of a non-speaker)","\"for what would one be who could not speak?\""],["abravīt tu te tasya āyatanaṃ pratiṣṭhām","acc. (abode) + acc. (support)","\"but did he tell you its abode and its support?\""],["na me abravīt iti","","\"he did not tell me\""],["eka-pāt vai etat samrāṭ iti","adj. (one-footed) + voc.","\"then this is (only) one-footed, your majesty\""],["saḥ vai naḥ brūhi yājñavalkya","impv.","\"(then) do tell us, Yājñavalkya\""],["vāk eva āyatanam ākāśaḥ pratiṣṭhā prajñā iti enat upāsīta","nom. + opt. of upās","\"speech is the abode, space the support; let one meditate on it as intelligence (prajñā)\""],["vāk vai samrāṭ paramaṃ brahma","n. nom.","\"speech, your majesty, is the supreme Brahman"],["na enaṃ vāk jahāti","acc. + 3 sg. pres. of hā","speech does not abandon him;"],["sarvāṇi enaṃ bhūtāni abhikṣaranti","acc. + 3 pl. pres.","all beings flock to him;"],["devaḥ bhūtvā devān apyeti","ger. + acc. + pres.","becoming a god, he goes to the gods —"],["yaḥ evaṃ vidvān etat upāste","pres.part. + pres. of upās","whoever, knowing thus, meditates on it\""],["hastyṛṣabhaṃ sahasraṃ dadāmi iti","n. acc. (a thousand with a bull-elephant)","\"I give a thousand (cows) with a bull-elephant,\" said Janaka"],["pitā me amanyata na ananuśiṣya haret iti","nom. + 3 sg. impf. + ger. + opt. of hṛ","\"my father held that one should not accept (a gift) without first fully instructing (the pupil)\""]],"lit":"\"'Let us hear what someone has told you.' 'Jitvan Śailini told me, \"Speech is Brahman.\"' 'As one with mother, father, and teacher would speak, so did Śailini speak in saying \"Speech is Brahman\" — for what would one be who could not speak? But did he tell you its <strong>abode and support</strong>?' 'He did not.' 'Then this is only <strong>one-footed</strong>, your majesty.' 'Then do tell us, Yājñavalkya.' '<strong>Speech is the abode, space the support; one should meditate on it as intelligence (prajñā).</strong>' 'What is its intelligence-nature, Yājñavalkya?' '<strong>Speech itself</strong>, your majesty,' he said; 'for through speech a kinsman is known; the Ṛg-, Yajur-, Sāma-, and Atharva-veda, the histories, the ancient lore, the sciences, the upaniṣads, verses, aphorisms, glosses and commentaries; the offered and the sacrificed, food and drink, this world and the next, and all beings — (all) are known through speech alone. Speech, your majesty, is the supreme Brahman. Speech does not abandon him; all beings flock to him; becoming a god, he goes to the gods — whoever, knowing thus, meditates on it.' 'I give you a thousand (cows) with a bull-elephant!' said Janaka. Yājñavalkya said, 'My father held that one should not accept (a gift) without fully instructing (the pupil).'\" <em>(So through breath → the dear; eye → truth; ear → the infinite (the quarters); mind → bliss; heart → stability.)</em>","para":"Six respected teachers had each given Janaka a <strong>one-sided</strong> truth: Brahman is speech / breath / eye / ear / mind / heart. Yājñavalkya <strong>honours each</strong> (these faculties are indeed indispensable — \"what would one be without speech?\") but points out the teaching is <strong>\"one-footed\" (ekapād)</strong>: it named Brahman's <strong>abode (āyatana)</strong> but omitted its <strong>support (pratiṣṭhā = space)</strong> and the <strong>quality to be contemplated</strong>. He completes each meditation — speech as <strong>intelligence</strong>, breath as <strong>the dear</strong>, eye as <strong>truth</strong>, ear as <strong>the infinite</strong>, mind as <strong>bliss</strong>, heart as <strong>stability</strong> — and declares each, <em>for its worshipper</em>, \"the supreme Brahman,\" conferring divinity. Yet, scrupulous, he will not pocket Janaka's lavish gifts <strong>until the teaching is complete</strong> (his father's rule). These remain <strong>saguṇa (qualified) meditations</strong>; the <em>nirguṇa</em> truth comes in 4.2–4.4.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","each faculty is a legitimate <em>pratīka</em> (symbol) of Brahman, an <strong>abode (āyatana)</strong> where Brahman is worshipped; \"space the support\" universalises it; the meditation-quality (prajñā, priya, satya, ananta, ānanda, sthiti) gives the <em>bhāvanā</em>. \"One-footed\" = incomplete without abode-support-quality; even completed, these are <em>saguṇa upāsanās</em> yielding god-hood (<em>krama-mukti</em>), not the immediate liberation of <em>nirguṇa</em> knowledge. Yājñavalkya's refusal to take the gift before finishing exemplifies the teacher's integrity."],["Sureśvara","the six are graded approaches; each \"supreme Brahman\" is Brahman-as-conditioned-by-that-faculty — true within its scope, surpassed by the unconditioned."],["Krishnananda","a beautiful demonstration that partial truths are not false but <strong>incomplete</strong>; the master does not reject the other teachers but <em>completes</em> them — adding the support and the contemplative quality. The faculties (speech, breath, sight, hearing, mind, heart) are the doors through which Brahman is approached."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical <em>upāsanā</em>-set: meditate on speech as intelligence, breath as the dear, eye as truth, ear as the infinite, mind as bliss, heart as stability — each a real path to the divine. Note the ethic: complete the teaching before taking the reward."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the six named teachers (a glimpse of the intellectual milieu) and the <em>ekapād</em> (\"one-footed\") device by which Yājñavalkya supplements each with abode/support/quality."]],"notes":["Each follows one frame. Janaka: \"Teacher N told me '<strong>Faculty X</strong> is Brahman.'\" Yājñavalkya: \"He spoke as one with mother, father, and teacher (i.e., well-taught) — for what could one be without X? But did he tell you its <strong>abode and support</strong>? He did not — so it is <strong>'one-footed' (ekapād, incomplete)</strong>. (Here is the full teaching:) <strong>X is the abode, space (ākāśa) the support; meditate on it as [quality Q].</strong>\" Then he explains Q, declares \"<strong>X is the supreme Brahman; X does not abandon him; all beings flock to him; becoming a god, he goes to the gods — whoever, knowing thus, meditates on it.</strong>\" Janaka offers a thousand cows with a bull-elephant; Yājñavalkya replies, \"<strong>My father held that one should not accept (a gift) without fully instructing (the pupil).</strong>\"","<strong>Consolidated table of the six:</strong>","<table><tr><td>Mantra</td><td>Teacher</td><td>Faculty = Brahman (abode)</td><td>meditate as</td><td>the \"reality\" of that quality</td></tr><tr><td>4.1.2</td><td>Jitvan Śailini</td><td><strong>speech</strong> (vāc)</td><td><strong>prajñā</strong> (intelligence)</td><td>by speech a kinsman, the Vedas, both worlds, all beings are <em>known</em></td></tr><tr><td>4.1.3</td><td>Udaṅka Śaulbāyana</td><td><strong>breath</strong> (prāṇa)</td><td><strong>priya</strong> (the dear)</td><td>for love of breath one does even the improper; life is the dearest</td></tr><tr><td>4.1.4</td><td>Barku Vārṣṇa</td><td><strong>eye</strong> (cakṣus)</td><td><strong>satya</strong> (truth)</td><td>\"Did you see?\" \"I saw\" — that is truth</td></tr><tr><td>4.1.5</td><td>Gardabhīvipīta Bhāradvāja</td><td><strong>ear</strong> (śrotra)</td><td><strong>ananta</strong> (the infinite)</td><td>the quarters (= the ear's reality) are endless</td></tr><tr><td>4.1.6</td><td>Satyakāma Jābāla</td><td><strong>mind</strong> (manas)</td><td><strong>ānanda</strong> (bliss)</td><td>by the mind one unites (with the beloved); a son is born — that is bliss</td></tr><tr><td>4.1.7</td><td>Vidagdha Śākalya</td><td><strong>heart</strong> (hṛdaya)</td><td><strong>sthiti</strong> (stability)</td><td>the heart is the abode and support of all beings</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This brāhmaṇa is the <strong>transition</strong> from the public contest (Adhyāya 3) to the intimate Janaka–Yājñavalkya teaching (4.1–4.4). Its method is <strong>completion, not refutation</strong>:","1. <strong>Partial truths honoured.</strong> Six teachers' identifications of Brahman with <strong>speech, breath, eye, ear, mind, heart</strong> are each accepted as naming a real <strong>abode (āyatana)</strong> of Brahman — an indispensable faculty.","2. <strong>The missing two \"feet.\"</strong> Each is \"<strong>one-footed</strong>\" until given its <strong>support (pratiṣṭhā = space)</strong> and its <strong>meditative quality</strong> (intelligence, the dear, truth, the infinite, bliss, stability). Yājñavalkya supplies these, turning a bare identification into a complete <em>upāsanā</em>.","3. <strong>Each \"supreme Brahman\" — within its scope.</strong> For its worshipper, each faculty <em>is</em> Brahman, conferring divinity (<em>devo bhūtvā devān apyeti</em>). But these remain <strong>qualified (saguṇa)</strong> meditations; the unconditioned Self is disclosed only in the discourses that follow.","4. <strong>The teacher's integrity.</strong> Yājñavalkya will not accept Janaka's gifts \"without fully instructing\" — knowledge is given complete, not sold half-finished.","The pattern (faculty = abode, space = support, quality = meditation) prepares the reader to see all the faculties as <em>approaches</em> that the next brāhmaṇas will trace to the one self-luminous Self."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   SIX PARTIAL DEFINITIONS, COMPLETED (BU 4.1)\n   ===========================================\n   each teacher: &quot;Faculty X is Brahman&quot;  -&gt; TRUE but &quot;ONE-FOOTED&quot; (ekapād)\n   Yājñavalkya completes:  X = ABODE | space (ākāśa) = SUPPORT | + a QUALITY to meditate\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------\n     SPEECH  -&gt; meditate as PRAJÑĀ (intelligence)   [all is known through speech]\n     BREATH  -&gt; meditate as PRIYA  (the dear)        [all done for love of life]\n     EYE     -&gt; meditate as SATYA  (truth)           [&quot;I saw&quot; = truth]\n     EAR     -&gt; meditate as ANANTA (the infinite)    [the quarters are endless]\n     MIND    -&gt; meditate as ĀNANDA (bliss)           [union -&gt; a son -&gt; joy]\n     HEART   -&gt; meditate as STHITI (stability)       [abode &amp; support of all beings]\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------\n   each: &quot;X = the supreme Brahman; it never abandons him; all beings flock to him;\n          becoming a god he goes to the gods&quot;   (saguṇa upāsana -&gt; divinity)\n   Janaka offers 1000 cows + bull-elephant;  Yājñavalkya: &quot;finish the teaching first&quot;</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.1","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Complete your half-truths.</strong> Notice Yājñavalkya's method: he never says \"you are wrong,\" but \"that is one-footed — here is its support.\" Apply it to partial understandings (your own and others'): supply the missing ground rather than discarding the insight.","2. <strong>Use the six meditations.</strong> Each faculty is a real door to the divine: contemplate speech as the power by which all is <em>known</em>, breath as the <em>dearest</em> (life itself), the eye as <em>truth</em>, the ear as the <em>infinite</em>, the mind as <em>bliss</em>, the heart as <em>stability/support of all</em>. Pick one and rest in it as \"the supreme Brahman.\"","3. <strong>Give the support.</strong> Every faculty (the abode) rests on <strong>space (ākāśa)</strong> — the open, pervasive ground. In meditation, sense each faculty held within boundless space, and space within awareness.","4. <strong>Finish before you take.</strong> The teacher's ethic — \"do not accept the reward without fully instructing\" — is a model of integrity: complete the work, then receive its fruit.","5. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> partial truths are doorways, not walls. The faculties that the six teachers each absolutised are, rightly seen, <em>approaches</em> to the one Self — which the next brāhmaṇas will reveal directly."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 4 · Brāhmaṇa 4.2 — The persons in the eyes; the heart's channels; \"neti neti\"","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 4.2","title":"The persons in the eyes; the heart's channels; \"neti neti\"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Janaka, humbled, slips from his throne-cushion and begs to be taught \"where he will go\" at death. Yājñavalkya reveals the <strong>two persons in the eyes</strong> — <strong>Indha</strong> (the person in the right eye, secretly called <strong>Indra<strong><em>) and his consort </strong>Virāj<strong> (in the left eye) — who meet in the </strong>space within the heart<strong>, whose channels (the </strong>hitā nāḍīs</em></strong>) are the paths of the subtle (dream) self; and concludes with the <strong>neti neti</strong> Self and the assurance: <strong>\"you have reached the fearless (abhaya).\"</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 4.2.1","title":"\"Where will you go when released?\"","sa":["जनको ह वैदेहः कूर्चादुपावसर्पन्नुवाच — नमस्तेऽस्तु याज्ञवल्क्य । अनु मा शाधीति । स होवाच यथा वै संराण्महान्तमध्वानमेष्यन्रथं वा नावं वा समाददीतैवमेवैताभिरुपनिषद्भिः समाहितात्मासि । एवं वृन्दारक आढ्यः सन्नधीतवेद उक्तोपनिषत्क इतो विमुच्यमानः क्व गमिष्यसीति । नाहं तद्भगवन्वेद यत्र गमिष्यामीति । अथ वै तेऽहं तद्वक्ष्यामि यत्र गमिष्यसीति । ब्रवीतु भगवानिति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["janako ha vaidehaḥ kūrcād upāvasarpann uvāca -- namas te 'stu yājñavalkya | anu mā śādhīti | sa hovāca yathā vai samrāṇ mahāntam adhvānam eṣyan rathaṃ vā nāvaṃ vā samādadītaivam evaitābhir upaniṣadbhiḥ samāhitātmāsi | evaṃ vṛndāraka āḍhyaḥ sann adhītaveda uktopaniṣatka ito vimucyamānaḥ kva gamiṣyasīti | nāhaṃ tad bhagavan veda yatra gamiṣyāmīti | atha vai te 'haṃ tad vakṣyāmi yatra gamiṣyasīti | bravītu bhagavān iti || 1 ||"],"words":[["janakaḥ ha vaidehaḥ kūrcāt upāvasarpan uvāca","abl. (cushion/throne) + pres.part. of upa-ava-sṛp + pf.","Janaka of Videha, slipping down from his cushion, said"],["namaḥ te astu yājñavalkya anu mā śādhi iti","+ acc. (me) + impv. of anu-śās","\"homage to you, Yājñavalkya; instruct me\""],["yathā vai samrāṭ mahāntam adhvānam eṣyan rathaṃ vā nāvaṃ vā samādadīta","voc. + acc. + pres.part. of i + acc. + opt. of sam-ā-dā","\"as a king about to set out on a great journey would procure a chariot or a boat,"],["evam eva etābhiḥ upaniṣadbhiḥ samāhita-ātmā asi","instr. pl. + adj. (composed/equipped in self)","so are you equipped in self with these secret teachings (upaniṣads)"],["evaṃ vṛndārakaḥ āḍhyaḥ san adhīta-vedaḥ ukta-upaniṣatkaḥ","nom. (honoured, wealthy, learned, instructed-in-upaniṣads)","(being) thus honoured, wealthy, versed in the Veda, taught the upaniṣads —"],["itaḥ vimucyamānaḥ kva gamiṣyasi iti","pres.part. pass. + 2 sg. fut.","when released from here (at death), where will you go?\""],["na aham tat bhagavan veda yatra gamiṣyāmi iti","1 sg. pf. + rel.","\"I do not know, sir, where I shall go\""],["atha vai te aham tat vakṣyāmi yatra gamiṣyasi iti","1 sg. fut.","\"then I shall tell you where you will go\""],["bravītu bhagavān iti","3 sg. impv.","\"let the venerable one speak\""]],"lit":"\"Janaka of Videha, slipping down from his cushion, said: 'Homage to you, Yājñavalkya; instruct me.' He said: 'As a king about to set out on a great journey would procure a chariot or a boat, so you are equipped in soul with these secret teachings. Being thus honoured, wealthy, learned in the Veda, and instructed in the upaniṣads — when you are released from here (at death), where will you go?' 'I do not know, sir, where I shall go.' 'Then I shall tell you where you will go.' 'Let the venerable one speak.'\"","para":"Janaka — though a great king, wealthy and learned — <strong>descends from his throne in humility</strong> and asks the one question that wealth and learning cannot answer: <strong>where do I go at death?</strong> Yājñavalkya's image is striking: the <em>upaniṣads</em> (secret teachings) are the king's <strong>\"chariot or boat\"</strong> for the great journey beyond death — but the king must still ask <em>where the journey leads.</em> Confessing his ignorance, Janaka becomes the true disciple, and Yājñavalkya promises the answer: not a place, but the <strong>Self</strong> (4.2.2–4).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the descent from the throne and \"instruct me\" mark the shift from disputant to <em>śiṣya</em> (disciple) — the proper posture for receiving Self-knowledge. \"Where will you go?\" is answered not by a destination but by the recognition of the Self that neither comes nor goes."],["Krishnananda","the most powerful of kings is helpless before death until he knows the Self; humility (leaving the throne) is the gateway. The \"chariot/boat\" of the upaniṣads carries one across — but one must know the farther shore."],["Sureśvara","the question \"where will you go?\" sets up the teaching that the knower of the Self <strong>goes nowhere</strong> — being already the all-pervading Self."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note Janaka's exemplary humility and the urgency of the death-question that wealth/learning cannot meet."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the king's deference and the framing of the upaniṣad-teaching as the vehicle for the post-mortem journey."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.2.2","title":"Indha, the person in the right eye (secretly \"Indra\")","sa":["इन्धो ह वै नामैष योऽयं दक्षिणेऽक्षन्पुरुषः । तं वा एतमिन्धं सन्तमिन्द्र इत्याचक्षते परोक्षेणैव । परोक्षप्रिया इव हि देवाः प्रत्यक्षद्विषः ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["indho ha vai nāmaiṣa yo 'yaṃ dakṣiṇe 'kṣan puruṣaḥ | taṃ vā etam indhaṃ santam indra ity ācakṣate parokṣeṇaiva | parokṣapriyā iva hi devāḥ pratyakṣadviṣaḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["indhaḥ ha vai nāma eṣaḥ yaḥ ayaṃ dakṣiṇe akṣan puruṣaḥ","m. nom. (Indha, \"the kindler\") + loc. (in the right eye)","\"Indha\" by name is this person who is in the right eye"],["taṃ vai etam indhaṃ santam indraḥ iti ācakṣate parokṣeṇa eva","acc. + pres.part. + nom. + 3 pl. pres. + instr. (indirectly)","though he is Indha, they call him \"Indra\" indirectly"],["parokṣa-priyāḥ iva hi devāḥ pratyakṣa-dviṣaḥ","nom. pl. (loving the indirect) + (hating the direct)","for the gods, as it were, love the indirect and dislike the direct"]],"lit":"\"'<strong>Indha</strong>' ('the kindler/luminous') is the name of this person who is in the right eye. Though he is Indha, they call him '<strong>Indra</strong>' indirectly — for the gods, as it were, love the obscure and dislike the direct.\"","para":"Yājñavalkya begins to disclose the Self by way of its reflections. The conscious <strong>person seen in the right eye</strong> is really named <strong>Indha</strong> (\"the kindler/the shining one\" — the inner luminous self), but is reverently called <strong>Indra</strong> by an <em>indirect</em> name. The aside is a famous principle of Vedic/Upaniṣadic style: <strong>\"the gods love the indirect (parokṣa) and dislike the direct (pratyakṣa)\"</strong> — the sacred is veiled in symbol and obscure name, not stated baldly (which is why the Upaniṣads teach by riddle, etymology, and <em>neti neti</em>).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>Indha</em> (the self in the eye, the <em>vijñānamaya</em> reflected consciousness) is the entry-point; the indirect naming (Indha → Indra) illustrates the <em>parokṣa</em> idiom by which the scriptures veil the highest in symbol — protecting it from the profane and drawing the seeker inward."],["Krishnananda","\"The gods love the indirect\" — sacred truth is not handed over directly but approached through symbol and reflection; the person in the eye is the first reflected form of the Self."],["Sureśvara","the right-eye person is the conditioned reflection of the one Self, to be traced to its source."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the famous maxim on indirect naming, key to reading Vedic symbolism."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle highlights <em>parokṣa-priyāḥ devāḥ</em> (\"the gods love the cryptic\") as a self-description of the Brāhmaṇa-Upaniṣad hermeneutic."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.2.3","title":"Virāj in the left eye; their meeting in the heart; the channels","sa":["अथैतद्वामेऽक्षणि पुरुषरूपमेषास्य पत्नी विराट् । तयोरेष संस्तावो य एषोऽन्तर्हृदय आकाशः । अथैनयोरेतदन्नं य एषोऽन्तर्हृदये लोहितपिण्डः । अथैनयोरेतत्प्रावरणं यदेतदन्तर्हृदये जालकमिव । अथैनयोरेषा सृतिः संचरणी यैषा हृदयादूर्ध्वा नाड्युच्चरति । यथा केशः सहस्रधा भिन्न एवमस्यैता हिता नाम नाड्योऽन्तर्हृदये प्रतिष्ठिता भवन्ति । एताभिर्वा एतदास्रवदास्रवति । तस्मादेष प्रविविक्ताहारतर इव भवत्यस्माच्छारीरादात्मनः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["athaitad vāme 'kṣaṇi puruṣarūpam eṣāsya patnī virāṭ | tayor eṣa saṃstāvo ya eṣo 'ntar hṛdaya ākāśaḥ | athainayor etad annaṃ ya eṣo 'ntar hṛdaye lohitapiṇḍaḥ | athainayor etat prāvaraṇaṃ yad etad antar hṛdaye jālakam iva | athainayor eṣā sṛtiḥ saṃcaraṇī yaiṣā hṛdayād ūrdhvā nāḍy uccarati | yathā keśaḥ sahasradhā bhinna evam asyaitā hitā nāma nāḍyo 'ntar hṛdaye pratiṣṭhitā bhavanti | etābhir vā etad āsravad āsravati | tasmād eṣa praviviktāhāratara iva bhavaty asmāc chārīrād ātmanaḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[["atha etat vāme akṣaṇi puruṣa-rūpam eṣā asya patnī virāṭ","loc. (in the left eye) + n. nom. + f. nom.","now the person-form in the left eye is his wife, Virāj"],["tayoḥ eṣaḥ saṃstāvaḥ yaḥ eṣaḥ antaḥ hṛdaye ākāśaḥ","gen. du. + m. nom. (meeting-place) + loc.","their meeting-place is this space within the heart"],["atha enayoḥ etat annaṃ yaḥ eṣaḥ antaḥ hṛdaye lohita-piṇḍaḥ","gen. du. + n. nom. + m. nom. (red lump)","their food is this red lump within the heart"],["atha enayoḥ etat prāvaraṇaṃ yat etat antaḥ hṛdaye jālakam iva","n. nom. (covering) + (net-like)","their covering is this net-like membrane within the heart"],["atha enayoḥ eṣā sṛtiḥ saṃcaraṇī yā eṣā hṛdayāt ūrdhvā nāḍī uccarati","f. nom. (path) + f. nom. (channel) + pres. of ud-car","their path of movement is the channel that runs upward from the heart"],["yathā keśaḥ sahasradhā bhinnaḥ evam asya etāḥ hitā nāma nāḍyaḥ antaḥ hṛdaye pratiṣṭhitāḥ bhavanti","nom. + adv. + pp. + f. nom. pl. (named hitā)","as a hair split a thousandfold, so these channels named hitā are fixed within the heart"],["etābhiḥ vai etat āsravat āsravati","instr. pl. + pres.part. + pres.","through these (the subtle essence) keeps flowing"],["tasmāt eṣaḥ pravivikta-āhārataraḥ iva bhavati asmāt śārīrāt ātmanaḥ","abl. (the bodily self) + adj. (of more refined food)","therefore this (subtle/dream self) is, as it were, of finer sustenance than the bodily self"]],"lit":"\"Now the person-form in the left eye is his wife, <strong>Virāj</strong>. Their meeting-place is the <strong>space within the heart</strong>; their food, the <strong>red lump within the heart</strong>; their covering, the <strong>net-like membrane within the heart</strong>; their path of movement, the <strong>channel that runs upward from the heart</strong>. As a hair split a thousandfold, so are these channels, called <strong>hitā</strong>, fixed within the heart, and through them (the subtle essence) keeps flowing. Therefore this (subtle self) is, as it were, of more refined sustenance than the bodily self.\"","para":"The reflected self appears as a <strong>pair</strong>: <strong>Indha</strong> (right eye) and his \"wife\" <strong>Virāj</strong> (left eye) — the subject and its objective counterpart, the perceiver and the perceived (an inner echo of the cosmic <em>puruṣa/prakṛti</em> or the husband/wife splitting of 1.4.3). They \"<strong>meet</strong>\" in the <strong>space within the heart</strong> (<em>hṛdayākāśa</em>), nourished by the heart's blood, sheltered by its membrane, moving along the <strong>hitā channels</strong> (the thousandfold-split fine nāḍīs). This is the <strong>physiology of the dream/subtle self</strong> (cf. 2.1.19): in dream, consciousness withdraws along these channels into the heart-space, living on a \"finer food\" than the gross body — the bridge to the great waking/dream/sleep analysis of 4.3.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Indha and Virāj are the <strong>perceiving self and the perceived (objects/the cosmic body)</strong> reflected in the two eyes; their \"union in the heart-space\" describes the subject-object togetherness in experience, which withdraws into the heart in sleep. The <em>hitā</em> nāḍīs are the subtle channels along which the dream-self moves; \"finer food\" indicates the subtle body's refined sustenance. Anatomy in service of the analysis of the states."],["Krishnananda","the duality of seer (Indha) and seen (Virāj) is the structure of all experience; in waking they seem split between the eyes, but they <strong>meet in the heart</strong> — and in sleep withdraw there. The passage maps the inward retreat that 4.3 will fully expound."],["Sureśvara","the pair and their heart-union model how the one Self appears as subject and object; the channels are the routes of the subtle self."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the <em>hitā</em> nāḍīs and heart-space are the locus of the dream/sleep self; a meditation on withdrawing into the heart."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the Indha/Virāj (right/left eye) imagery and the <em>hitā</em> channels (cf. 2.1.19) as the physiology of dream and the inward path."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.2.4","title":"The neti-neti Self; the Fearless","sa":["तस्य प्राची दिक्प्राञ्चः प्राणाः । दक्षिणा दिग्दक्षिणे प्राणाः । प्रतीची दिक्प्रत्यञ्चः प्राणाः । उदीची दिगुदञ्चः प्राणाः । ऊर्ध्वा दिगूर्ध्वाः प्राणाः । अवाची दिगवाञ्चः प्राणाः । सर्वा दिशः सर्वे प्राणाः । स एष नेति नेत्यात्मा । अगृह्यो न हि गृह्यते । अशीर्यो न हि शीर्यते । असङ्गो न हि सज्यते । असितो न व्यथते । न रिष्यति । अभयं वै जनक प्राप्तोऽसीति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः । स होवाच जनको वैदेहोऽभयं त्वा गच्छताद्याज्ञवल्क्य यो नो भगवन्नभयं वेदयसे । नमस्ते ऽस्तु । इमे विदेहा अयमहमस्मीति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["tasya prācī dik prāñcaḥ prāṇāḥ | dakṣiṇā dig dakṣiṇe prāṇāḥ | pratīcī dik pratyañcaḥ prāṇāḥ | udīcī dig udañcaḥ prāṇāḥ | ūrdhvā dig ūrdhvāḥ prāṇāḥ | avācī dig avāñcaḥ prāṇāḥ | sarvā diśaḥ sarve prāṇāḥ | sa eṣa neti nety ātmā | agṛhyo na hi gṛhyate | aśīryo na hi śīryate | asaṅgo na hi sajyate | asito na vyathate | na riṣyati | abhayaṃ vai janaka prāpto 'sīti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ | sa hovāca janako vaideho 'bhayaṃ tvā gacchatād yājñavalkya yo no bhagavann abhayaṃ vedayase | namas te 'stu | ime videhā ayam aham asmīti || 4 ||"],"words":[["tasya prācī dik prāñcaḥ prāṇāḥ","gen. + f. nom. + m. nom. pl.","for him (the self), the eastern quarter is the eastward breaths"],["(so) dakṣiṇā / pratīcī / udīcī / ūrdhvā / avācī dik … prāṇāḥ","nom.","(so the south, west, north, upper, lower quarters are the breaths in those directions)"],["sarvāḥ diśaḥ sarve prāṇāḥ","nom. pl.","all the quarters are all the breaths"],["saḥ eṣaḥ neti neti ātmā","m. nom.","that Self is \"not this, not this\" (neti neti)"],["agṛhyaḥ na hi gṛhyate","adj. + pres. pass.","ungraspable, for it is not grasped;"],["aśīryaḥ na hi śīryate","(undecaying)","undecaying, for it does not decay;"],["asaṅgaḥ na hi sajyate","(unattached)","unattached, for it does not cling;"],["asitaḥ na vyathate na riṣyati","(unbound)","unbound, it neither trembles nor is injured"],["abhayaṃ vai janaka prāptaḥ asi iti","n. acc. (the fearless) + 2 sg. pf. of prāp","\"you have reached the fearless (abhaya), Janaka,\" said Yājñavalkya"],["abhayaṃ tvā gacchatāt yājñavalkya yaḥ naḥ bhagavan abhayaṃ vedayase","acc. + 3 sg. impv. (precative) of gam + 2 sg. pres. caus. of vid","\"may the fearless come to you too, Yājñavalkya, who teach us the fearless"],["namaḥ te astu ime videhāḥ ayam aham asmi iti","nom. pl. + nom.","homage to you; here are the Videhas (my realm), and here am I (your servant)\""]],"lit":"\"For him (the self), the eastern quarter is the eastward breaths; the south, the southward breaths; the west, the westward; the north, the northward; the upper, the upward; the lower, the downward — all the quarters are all the breaths. That Self is <strong>'not this, not this' (neti neti)</strong>: ungraspable, for it is not grasped; undecaying, for it does not decay; unattached, for it does not cling; unbound, it neither trembles nor is injured. '<strong>You have reached the Fearless, Janaka,</strong>' said Yājñavalkya. Janaka said, 'May the Fearless come to you too, Yājñavalkya, who teach us the Fearless! Homage to you. Here are (my people) the Videhas, and here am I (at your service).'\"","para":"Having mapped the subtle self in the heart's directions and breaths, Yājñavalkya states the <strong>Self itself</strong>: it is <strong>neti neti</strong> — \"not this, not this\" — <strong>ungraspable</strong> (it is the grasper, never the grasped), <strong>undecaying, unattached, unbound,</strong> free of all trembling and harm (cf. 3.9.26, 4.5.15). To know this is to reach <strong>abhaya — the Fearless</strong> (for fear arises only from a \"second,\" 1.4.2; the non-dual Self is by nature fearless). Yājñavalkya declares Janaka <strong>has attained the fearless state</strong>; the king, overcome with gratitude, <strong>offers his whole kingdom and himself</strong> to the teacher. This is the answer to \"where will you go?\" (4.2.1): nowhere — for the Self, <em>neti neti</em>, neither comes nor goes; to know it is to be the fearless, deathless ground.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>neti-neti</em> Self (the same formula as 2.3.6, 3.9.26) is the <strong>attributeless witness</strong>, indicated only by negation: <em>agṛhya</em> (ungraspable, being the subject), <em>aśīrya</em> (undecaying), <em>asaṅga</em> (unattached — untouched by what it illumines), <em>asita</em> (unbound/free). <strong>Abhaya</strong> (fearlessness) is the natural state of the non-dual Self, since fear requires a second (1.4.2); the knower \"reaches the fearless\" = realises he <em>is</em> the deathless, relationless Self. This answers the death-question: the Self does not \"go\" anywhere. Janaka's offer of his kingdom expresses total surrender to the liberating knowledge."],["Krishnananda","\"You have attained the fearless\" — the highest blessing, for all fear is rooted in duality and the Self is non-dual. The four negations strip every attribute; what remains is the unshakable witness. Janaka, given the deathless, gives back everything."],["Sureśvara","<em>neti neti</em> removes all superimpositions; <em>abhaya</em> is the realised result — the Self, being all and second-less, has nothing to fear."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the goal stated — rest as the <em>neti-neti</em> Self, ungraspable and unattached, and you abide in fearlessness (abhaya), the mark of liberation."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes <em>abhaya</em> (\"the fearless\") as the keynote of liberation and Janaka's dramatic self-surrender (\"here are the Videhas, here am I\")."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This short brāhmaṇa moves from Janaka's death-question to the <strong>neti-neti Self</strong> and the gift of <strong>fearlessness</strong>:","1. <strong>Humility opens the teaching (4.2.1).</strong> The great king leaves his throne and confesses he does not know where he will go at death — the upaniṣads are his \"chariot,\" but he needs the destination.","2. <strong>The reflected self: Indha and Virāj (4.2.2–3).</strong> The conscious person in the <strong>right eye (Indha, secretly \"Indra\")</strong> and his consort in the <strong>left eye (Virāj)</strong> — subject and object — meet in the <strong>space within the heart</strong>, moving along the <strong>hitā channels</strong>; this is the physiology of the <strong>subtle/dream self</strong> (prelude to 4.3). The aside <em>\"the gods love the indirect\"</em> explains the Upaniṣad's symbolic method.","3. <strong>The Self as neti neti, the Fearless (4.2.4).</strong> Past all the breaths and directions stands the Self that is <strong>\"not this, not this\"</strong> — ungraspable, undecaying, unattached, unbound — and to know it is to reach <strong>abhaya, the fearless</strong> (the answer to the death-question: the Self neither goes nor perishes). Janaka surrenders his all in gratitude.","The brāhmaṇa thus introduces, in miniature, the two themes 4.3–4.4 will expound at length: the <strong>analysis of the states (waking/dream/sleep)</strong> and the <strong>deathless, self-luminous, fearless Self</strong>."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE PERSONS IN THE EYES &amp; THE FEARLESS SELF (BU 4.2)\n   ====================================================\n   Janaka (leaves his throne): &quot;where will I GO at death?&quot;\n        upaniṣads = your &#x27;chariot/boat&#x27; — but know the destination\n                              |\n   RIGHT EYE = INDHA (&quot;the kindler&quot;; called INDRA indirectly — &quot;gods love the cryptic&quot;)\n   LEFT  EYE = VIRĀJ (his consort)         [ = perceiver  &amp;  perceived ]\n        their MEETING-PLACE -&gt; the SPACE WITHIN THE HEART\n        food = the red lump · covering = the net-membrane · path = the upward channel\n        the HITĀ nāḍīs (a hair split 1000-fold) -&gt; route of the subtle/dream self\n                              |\n   all quarters = all the breaths -&gt; THE SELF = NETI NETI:\n        agṛhya (ungraspable) · aśīrya (undecaying) · asaṅga (unattached) · asita (unbound)\n        -&gt; neither trembles nor is harmed\n                              |\n        ====&gt;  &quot;YOU HAVE REACHED THE FEARLESS (ABHAYA), JANAKA&quot;\n        (answer to &quot;where will you go?&quot;: the Self neither goes nor perishes)\n        Janaka: &quot;here are the Videhas, here am I&quot; (total surrender)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.2","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Ask the king's question.</strong> Strip away wealth, status, and even learning, and ask: <em>where do \"I\" go at death?</em> Let the question be real; it turns the disputant into a seeker, as it did Janaka.","2. <strong>Read the sacred indirectly.</strong> \"The gods love the cryptic\" — do not expect the highest to be stated baldly; approach it through symbol, reflection, and negation. The person in the eye is a reflected hint of the Self.","3. <strong>Withdraw into the heart.</strong> Trace perception inward: the seer (Indha) and the seen (Virāj) meet in the heart-space. In meditation, let attention retreat from the two eyes into that inner space, along the \"hitā\" channels — the natural path of sleep made conscious.","4. <strong>Rest as neti neti.</strong> Deny to the Self every attribute that arises — ungraspable, undecaying, unattached, unbound. Abide as the witness that cannot be seized or hurt.","5. <strong>Receive the fearless.</strong> The fruit is <strong>abhaya</strong> — fearlessness rooted in non-duality (there is no \"second\" to fear). When the felt otherness of the world softens, notice fear loosen with it.","6. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the answer to \"where will I go?\" is that the Self, <em>neti neti</em>, <strong>goes nowhere</strong> — it is the deathless, fearless ground in which all coming and going appears. To know it is already to have \"reached the fearless.\""],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 4 · Brāhmaṇa 4.3 — The Lights of Man; Waking, Dream, and Deep Sleep; the Self-luminous Self","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 4.3","title":"The Lights of Man; Waking, Dream, and Deep Sleep; the Self-luminous Self","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The longest and, with 4.4, the profoundest brāhmaṇa of the Upaniṣad — Yājñavalkya's great teaching to Janaka on the</em> <strong>light by which a person lives</strong>. <em>Outer lights fail one by one (sun → moon → fire → speech) until the</em> <strong>Self alone is the light (ātma-jyotis)</strong>. <em>He then analyses the three states —</em> <strong>waking, dream, and deep sleep</strong> — <em>showing the Self to be</em> <strong>self-luminous (svayaṃ-jyotiḥ)</strong>, <em>the creator of the dream-world, untouched (asaṅga) by all it passes through, and in deep sleep merged in</em> <strong>the blissful, fearless Self \"embraced by the intelligent Self.\"</strong> <em>(Continued in Part B: the imperishable Seer, the non-dual vision, the calculus of bliss, and the process of dying.)</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3.1","title":"The setting; Janaka's boon","sa":["जनकं ह वैदेहं याज्ञवल्क्यो जगाम । स ह मेने न वदिष्य इति । अथ ह यज्जनकश्च वैदेहो याज्ञवल्क्यश्चाग्निहोत्रे समूदाते । तस्मै ह याज्ञवल्क्यो वरं ददौ । स ह कामप्रश्नमेव वव्रे । तं हास्मै ददौ । तं ह संराडेव पूर्वः पप्रच्छ ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["janakaṃ ha vaidehaṃ yājñavalkyo jagāma | sa ha mene na vadiṣya iti | atha ha yaj janakaś ca vaideho yājñavalkyaś cāgnihotre samūdāte | tasmai ha yājñavalkyo varaṃ dadau | sa ha kāmapraśnam eva vavre | taṃ hāsmai dadau | taṃ ha samrāḍ eva pūrvaḥ papraccha || 1 ||"],"words":[["janakaṃ ha vaidehaṃ yājñavalkyaḥ jagāma","acc. + 3 sg. pf. of gam","Yājñavalkya came to Janaka of Videha"],["saḥ ha mene na vadiṣye iti","3 sg. pf. of man + 1 sg. fut. of vad","he had thought, \"I shall not speak (teach further)\""],["atha ha yat janakaḥ ca vaidehaḥ yājñavalkyaḥ ca agnihotre samūdāte","loc. + 3 du. pf. of sam-vad","now, (formerly) when Janaka and Yājñavalkya had discussed the Agnihotra,"],["tasmai ha yājñavalkyaḥ varaṃ dadau","dat. + acc. + pf. of dā","Yājñavalkya had granted him a boon"],["saḥ ha kāma-praśnam eva vavre","acc. (questioning at will) + pf. of vṛ","he had chosen (the boon of) asking any question he wished"],["taṃ ha asmai dadau","acc. + dat.","and (Yājñavalkya) had granted it to him"],["taṃ ha samrāṭ eva pūrvaḥ papraccha","acc. + nom. + pf. of prach","so the king questioned him first"]],"lit":"\"Yājñavalkya came to Janaka of Videha. He had thought, 'I shall not speak (teach).' But — since, when Janaka and Yājñavalkya had once discussed the Agnihotra, Yājñavalkya had granted him a boon, and he had chosen the freedom to ask any question he wished, and Yājñavalkya had granted it — the king questioned him first.\"","para":"Yājñavalkya, perhaps wary of being drained of his deepest knowledge (cf. 4.3.33), comes to Janaka <strong>intending not to teach</strong>. But Janaka holds a previously granted <strong>boon — the right to ask any question (kāma-praśna)</strong> — and so <strong>takes the initiative</strong>, opening the supreme discourse. (A frame that lets the king, not the sage, drive the inquiry.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Yājñavalkya's reticence and Janaka's boon set up the dialogue as <em>the king's questioning</em> — the deepest teaching is drawn out by the qualified seeker's persistent inquiry."],["Krishnananda","the teacher would withhold, but the worthy disciple's right to ask compels the disclosure; truth is given to the one who earnestly seeks it."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the boon of \"questioning at will\" as the engine of the discourse."],["Sureśvara","the highest knowledge is imparted on demand to the prepared; the frame honours Janaka's readiness."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the back-reference to an earlier Agnihotra dialogue and the <em>kāma-praśna</em> device."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.3.2–4.3.6","title":"The lights: sun, moon, fire, speech, and the Self","sa":["4.3.2 (template — the sun): याज्ञवल्क्य किंज्योतिरयं पुरुष इति । आदित्यज्योतिः संराडिति होवाच । आदित्येनैवायं ज्योतिषास्ते पल्ययते कर्म कुरुते विपल्येतीति । एवमेवैतद्याज्ञवल्क्य ॥ २ ॥","4.3.5 (speech): अस्तमित आदित्ये याज्ञवल्क्य चन्द्रमस्यस्तमिते शान्तेऽग्नौ किंज्योतिरेवायं पुरुष इति । वागेवास्य ज्योतिर्भवतीति । वाचैवायं ज्योतिषास्ते पल्ययते कर्म कुरुते विपल्येति । तस्माद्वै संराडपि यत्र स्वः पाणिर्न विनिर्ज्ञायतेऽथ यत्र वागुच्चरत्युपैव तत्र न्येतीति । एवमेवैतद्याज्ञवल्क्य ॥ ५ ॥","4.3.6 (the Self): अस्तमित आदित्ये याज्ञवल्क्य चन्द्रमस्यस्तमिते शान्तेऽग्नौ शान्तायां वाचि किंज्योतिरेवायं पुरुष इति । आत्मैवास्य ज्योतिर्भवतीति । आत्मनैवायं ज्योतिषास्ते पल्ययते कर्म कुरुते विपल्येतीति ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["yājñavalkya kiṃjyotir ayaṃ puruṣa iti | ādityajyotiḥ samrāḍ iti hovāca | ādityenaivāyaṃ jyotiṣāste palyayate karma kurute vipalyetīti | evam evaitad yājñavalkya || 2 ||","astamita āditye … śānte 'gnau kiṃjyotir evāyaṃ puruṣa iti | vāg evāsya jyotir bhavatīti | … tasmād vai samrāḍ api yatra svaḥ pāṇir na vinirjñāyate 'tha yatra vāg uccaraty upaiva tatra nyetīti | evam evaitad yājñavalkya || 5 ||","astamita āditye … śāntāyāṃ vāci kiṃjyotir evāyaṃ puruṣa iti | ātmaivāsya jyotir bhavatīti | ātmanaivāyaṃ jyotiṣāste palyayate karma kurute vipalyetīti || 6 ||"],"words":[["yājñavalkya kiṃ-jyotiḥ ayaṃ puruṣaḥ iti","adj. (having what for light) + nom.","\"Yājñavalkya, what is the light of a person?\""],["āditya-jyotiḥ samrāṭ iti","nom. + voc.","\"the sun is his light, your majesty\""],["ādityena eva ayaṃ jyotiṣā āste palyayate karma kurute vipalyeti iti","instr. + pres. (sits, moves about, works, returns)","by the sun as his light he sits, moves about, does work, and returns"],["evam eva etat yājñavalkya","","\"so it is, Yājñavalkya\""],["(4.3.5) tasmāt vai samrāṭ api yatra svaḥ pāṇiḥ na vinirjñāyate","loc. + nom. (one's own hand) + pres. pass.","therefore, your majesty, even where one's own hand is not (clearly) made out (in the dark),"],["atha yatra vāk uccarati upa eva tatra nyeti iti","+ pres. + pres. of ni-i","yet where a voice sounds, one goes straight toward it"]],"lit":"\"'Yājñavalkya, what is the light of a person?' 'The <strong>sun</strong> is his light, your majesty; for by the sun as his light he sits, moves about, does his work, and returns.' 'So it is, Yājñavalkya.' 'When the sun has set, what is his light?' 'The <strong>moon</strong>…' 'When sun and moon have set?' 'The <strong>fire</strong>…' 'When sun, moon, and fire are gone?' '<strong>Speech</strong> is his light — therefore, your majesty, even where one cannot make out one's own hand (in the dark), where a voice sounds one goes straight toward it.' 'When the sun, moon, fire, and speech are all stilled, what is his light?' 'The <strong>Self (ātman)</strong> is his light; for by the Self as his light he sits, moves about, does his work, and returns.'\"","para":"Yājñavalkya leads Janaka by a beautiful inductive descent. By what \"light\" does a person carry on living and acting? By day, the <strong>sun</strong>; when it sets, the <strong>moon</strong>; when both are gone, <strong>fire</strong>; when fire too dies, <strong>speech</strong> (in pitch dark, one is guided by a voice). But when <em>all</em> outer lights and even speech fall silent — what then? The <strong>Self itself is the light</strong> — the <strong>innate self-luminous consciousness</strong> by which one functions at all. The outer lights are borrowed and intermittent; the Self is the <strong>original, ever-present light</strong> that never sets. (This sets up <em>svayaṃ-jyotiḥ</em>, 4.3.9.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the regress isolates the Self as the <em>true</em> light (<em>jyotis</em> = consciousness): the sun, moon, fire, and speech are <em>external aids</em> to action, but they only function <em>for</em> a conscious being; when all are withdrawn (as in dream/sleep), the Self shines by itself. The Self is the light <em>of</em> all lights (cf. Kaṭha 5.15; Muṇḍaka 2.2.10 — \"by its light all this shines\"). This is the entry to the doctrine of the <strong>self-luminous witness</strong>."],["Krishnananda","\"When every external light is gone, you still are\" — the Self is the light presupposed by all seeing, never itself lit by another. The descent (sun→…→Self) is a meditation peeling away borrowed illuminations to the one innate Light."],["Sureśvara","the outer lights illumine objects; the Self illumines even the lights (and the seer) — it is <em>jyotiṣāṃ jyotiḥ</em>, the light of lights."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a practical contemplation — withdraw attention from all external/sensory \"lights\" to the inner light of awareness by which they are known."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the elegant inductive structure and the identification of the Self as the ultimate, self-sufficient light of consciousness."]],"notes":["<em>Five exchanges. \"What is the light of a person?\" The outer lights fail in turn, and the Self alone remains:</em>","<table><tr><td>Mantra</td><td>when…</td><td>the light is…</td></tr><tr><td>4.3.2</td><td>(by day)</td><td>the <strong>sun</strong></td></tr><tr><td>4.3.3</td><td>the sun has set</td><td>the <strong>moon</strong></td></tr><tr><td>4.3.4</td><td>sun and moon set</td><td><strong>fire</strong></td></tr><tr><td>4.3.5</td><td>sun, moon set, fire quenched</td><td><strong>speech</strong> (one moves toward where a voice sounds)</td></tr><tr><td>4.3.6</td><td>sun, moon, fire, and speech all stilled</td><td>the <strong>Self (ātman)</strong></td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3.7","title":"Which Self? The consciousness-person, moving between worlds","sa":["कतम आत्मेति — योऽयं विज्ञानमयः प्राणेषु हृद्यन्तर्ज्योतिः पुरुषः । स समानः सन्नुभौ लोकावनुसंचरति ध्यायतीव लेलायतीव । स हि स्वप्नो भूत्वेमं लोकमतिक्रामति मृत्यो रूपाणि ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["katama ātmeti -- yo 'yaṃ vijñānamayaḥ prāṇeṣu hṛdy antarjyotiḥ puruṣaḥ | sa samānaḥ sann ubhau lokāv anusaṃcarati dhyāyatīva lelāyatīva | sa hi svapno bhūtvemaṃ lokam atikrāmati mṛtyo rūpāṇi || 7 ||"],"words":[["katamaḥ ātmā iti","interrog.","\"which is the Self?\""],["yaḥ ayaṃ vijñāna-mayaḥ prāṇeṣu hṛdi antaḥ-jyotiḥ puruṣaḥ","adj. (made of consciousness) + loc. pl. + loc. + adj. (the inner light)","the person made of consciousness, among the breaths, the inner light within the heart"],["saḥ samānaḥ san ubhau lokau anusaṃcarati","nom. (remaining the same) + acc. du. + pres.","remaining the same, he ranges through both worlds"],["dhyāyati iva lelāyati iva","pres. + iva","as if thinking, as if moving/trembling"],["saḥ hi svapnaḥ bhūtvā imaṃ lokam atikrāmati mṛtyoḥ rūpāṇi","nom. + ger. + acc. + pres. + gen. + n. acc. pl.","for, becoming a dream(er), he transcends this world — the forms of death"]],"lit":"\"'Which is the Self?' 'The <strong>person made of consciousness (vijñānamaya)</strong>, among the breaths, the <strong>inner light within the heart</strong>. Remaining the same, he ranges through <strong>both worlds</strong>; he seems to think, seems to move. For, becoming a dream(er), he transcends this world — the forms of death.'\"","para":"Janaka asks <em>which</em> Self is the light. Yājñavalkya: the <strong>vijñānamaya puruṣa</strong> — the consciousness-person dwelling <strong>within the heart as the inner light</strong> among the vital breaths. <strong>Remaining itself unchanged</strong>, it travels through <strong>both worlds</strong> (this and the next, waking and dream); it only <em>seems</em> to think and move (the activity belongs to the mind it illumines, not to the witness itself). In <strong>dream</strong>, it leaves \"this world — the forms of death\" (the mortal waking realm), revealing its independence of the body. (Note: <em>vijñānamaya</em> here = the Self associated with the intellect, the experiencer — pointed to in order to reach the pure witness.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>vijñānamaya</em> = the Self conditioned by the <em>buddhi</em> (intellect), the apparent experiencer — used as a pointer to the witness that is the <strong>inner light of the heart</strong>. \"Remaining the same (<em>samāna</em>), it ranges both worlds\" and only <em>seems</em> (<em>iva</em>) to think/move — the <em>iva</em> is crucial: activity is superimposed; the Self is the unchanging light. Dream proves it transcends the waking (\"death's forms\")."],["Krishnananda","the Self is \"the light within the heart\" that is the same in waking and dream; it does not really act — it <em>appears</em> to, by association with the mind. Dream shows it is not bound to the body or the waking world."],["Sureśvara","the <em>iva</em> (\"as if\") marks the witness's non-agency; the Self illumines thinking and moving without itself thinking or moving."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","meditate on the inner light in the heart that is the same self in all states, the seeming-thinker that is really the witness."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes <em>vijñānamaya puruṣa</em> and the \"as if\" (<em>iva</em>) language distinguishing the witness from the active mind."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3.8","title":"Embodied, joined to evils; departing, freed of them","sa":["स वा अयं पुरुषो जायमानः शरीरमभिसम्पद्यमानः पाप्मभिः संसृज्यते । स उत्क्रामन्म्रियमाणः पाप्मनो विजहाति ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["sa vā ayaṃ puruṣo jāyamānaḥ śarīram abhisampadyamānaḥ pāpmabhiḥ saṃsṛjyate | sa utkrāman mriyamāṇaḥ pāpmano vijahāti || 8 ||"],"words":[["saḥ vai ayaṃ puruṣaḥ jāyamānaḥ śarīram abhisampadyamānaḥ","pres.part. (being born, attaining a body)","this person, being born, taking on a body,"],["pāpmabhiḥ saṃsṛjyate","instr. pl. + pres. pass.","becomes joined with evils (the ills/limitations of embodiment)"],["saḥ utkrāman mriyamāṇaḥ pāpmanaḥ vijahāti","pres.part. (departing, dying) + acc. + pres. of vi-hā","departing, dying, he leaves the evils behind"]],"lit":"\"This person, when being born and taking on a body, becomes joined with evils; departing, dying, he leaves the evils behind.\"","para":"Embodiment entangles the person with <strong>pāpman</strong> (evils — the defects, sufferings, and limitations of bodily existence); <strong>death releases</strong> him from these. This frames the <strong>two states</strong> (waking-embodied / departed) and prepares the analysis of dream as the \"junction\" where the self stands free of both (4.3.9).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","\"evils\" = the entanglements of the body-mind; birth binds, death looses — but this is the round of saṃsāra unless knowledge intervenes. The verse establishes the contrast of the two worlds the self ranges."],["Krishnananda","embodiment is association with limitation; the self is not these evils but seems bound by them while embodied."],["Sureśvara/Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the body is the seat of evils; the Self is intrinsically free of them."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the framing of embodiment vs. departure preparing the three-states analysis."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3.9","title":"The third place: dream, where the Self is self-luminous","sa":["तस्य वा एतस्य पुरुषस्य द्वे एव स्थाने भवतः । इदं च परलोकस्थानं च । सन्ध्यं तृतीयं स्वप्नस्थानम् । तस्मिन्सन्ध्ये स्थाने तिष्ठन्नुभे स्थाने पश्यतीदं च परलोकस्थानं च । अथ यथाक्रमोऽयं परलोकस्थाने भवति तमाक्रममाक्रम्योभयान्पाप्मन आनन्दांश्च पश्यति । स यत्र प्रस्वपित्यस्य लोकस्य सर्वावतो मात्रामपादाय स्वयं विहत्य स्वयं निर्माय स्वेन भासा स्वेन ज्योतिषा प्रस्वपिति । अत्रायं पुरुषः स्वयंज्योतिर्भवति ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["tasya vā etasya puruṣasya dve eva sthāne bhavataḥ | idaṃ ca paralokasthānaṃ ca | sandhyaṃ tṛtīyaṃ svapnasthānam | tasmin sandhye sthāne tiṣṭhan ubhe sthāne paśyatīdaṃ ca paralokasthānaṃ ca | atha yathākramo 'yaṃ paralokasthāne bhavati tam ākramam ākramyobhayān pāpmana ānandāṃś ca paśyati | sa yatra prasvapity asya lokasya sarvāvato mātrām apādāya svayaṃ vihatya svayaṃ nirmāya svena bhāsā svena jyotiṣā prasvapiti | atrāyaṃ puruṣaḥ svayaṃjyotir bhavati || 9 ||"],"words":[["tasya vai etasya puruṣasya dve eva sthāne bhavataḥ","n. nom. du. + 3 du. pres.","this person has just two places (states):"],["idaṃ ca paraloka-sthānaṃ ca","n. nom.","this (world) and the place of the other world"],["sandhyaṃ tṛtīyaṃ svapna-sthānam","n. nom. (the junction)","the dream-place is a third, a junction"],["tasmin sandhye sthāne tiṣṭhan ubhe sthāne paśyati","loc. + pres.part. + acc. du.","standing in that junction-place, he sees both places"],["atha yathā-kramaḥ ayaṃ paraloka-sthāne bhavati tam ākramam ākramya ubhayān pāpmanaḥ ānandān ca paśyati","(the approach/footing) + ger.","now, by whatever approach he comes to the other-world place, taking that approach he sees both — the evils (sorrows) and the joys"],["saḥ yatra prasvapiti asya lokasya sarvāvataḥ mātrām apādāya","pres. of pra-svap + gen. + f. acc. (a portion) + ger.","when he falls asleep, taking a portion of this all-containing world,"],["svayaṃ vihatya svayaṃ nirmāya","adv. + ger. (destroying) + ger. (building)","himself demolishing it, himself building (a new world),"],["svena bhāsā svena jyotiṣā prasvapiti","instr. + instr.","he dreams by his own radiance, his own light"],["atra ayaṃ puruṣaḥ svayaṃ-jyotiḥ bhavati","adj. (self-luminous)","here this person becomes self-luminous"]],"lit":"\"This person has just two places (states): this world and the place of the other world; and a third, the junction — the <strong>dream-place</strong>. Standing in that junction, he sees both places — this world and the other world. By whatever approach he comes to the other-world place, taking that approach he sees both the evils (sorrows) and the joys. When he falls asleep, taking some material of this all-containing world, himself demolishing and himself building (it anew), he dreams <strong>by his own radiance, his own light</strong>. <strong>Here this person becomes self-luminous (svayaṃ-jyotiḥ).</strong>\"","para":"The Self ranges <strong>two states</strong> (waking-this-world / the other world) with <strong>dream as the \"junction\" (sandhya)</strong> between them — a borderland from which it can see both. The decisive insight is about <strong>dream</strong>: there, with <strong>no external sun, moon, or fire</strong>, the Self <strong>creates an entire world out of itself</strong> — taking the \"material\" of waking impressions, <em>demolishing</em> the waking world and <em>building</em> a dream-world — <strong>illumined by nothing but its own light.</strong> This proves the Self is <strong>svayaṃ-jyotiḥ, self-luminous</strong>: it needs no external light to know, for it is itself the light of consciousness. (The central proof of the self-luminous witness.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <strong>decisive argument for self-luminosity</strong>: in dream all external lights and objects are absent, yet the dreamer <em>sees</em> a whole world — so the seeing-light must be the Self's own. The Self is <em>not</em> lit by sun, senses, or mind (which are inactive/withdrawn) but <strong>shines by itself</strong> (<em>svena jyotiṣā</em>). Dream is thus the laboratory proving consciousness is self-revealing, the foundation of the <em>svayaṃ-jyotiḥ</em> doctrine. Dream as \"junction\" (sandhya) lets the self witness both worlds, showing it belongs to neither."],["Krishnananda","\"In dream you are your own sun\" — the Self projects, sustains, and withdraws an entire universe by its own light, needing nothing outside. This is the clearest demonstration that consciousness is not a product of the body or the senses but their source."],["Sureśvara","the Self \"demolishing and building\" its dream-world by its own light refutes the view that consciousness depends on external illumination or objects; it is self-evident."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","contemplate the dream-state nightly as proof that <em>you</em> are the self-luminous light; let it loosen identification with the body lit by outer suns."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes <em>svayaṃ-jyotiḥ</em> (\"self-luminous\") as the key term, and dream as the Upaniṣad's experimental proof of autonomous consciousness."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3.10","title":"In dream, the Self is the creator","sa":["न तत्र रथा न रथयोगा न पन्थानो भवन्ति । अथ रथान्रथयोगान्पथः सृजते । न तत्रानन्दा मुदः प्रमुदो भवन्ति । अथानन्दान्मुदः प्रमुदः सृजते । न तत्र वेशान्ताः पुष्करिण्यः स्रवन्त्यो भवन्ति । अथ वेशान्तान्पुष्करिणीः स्रवन्तीः सृजते । स हि कर्ता ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["na tatra rathā na rathayogā na panthāno bhavanti | atha rathān rathayogān pathaḥ sṛjate | na tatrānandā mudaḥ pramudo bhavanti | athānandān mudaḥ pramudaḥ sṛjate | na tatra veśāntāḥ puṣkariṇyaḥ sravantyo bhavanti | atha veśāntān puṣkariṇīḥ sravantīḥ sṛjate | sa hi kartā || 10 ||"],"words":[["na tatra rathāḥ na ratha-yogāḥ na panthānaḥ bhavanti","nom. pl.","there (in dream) there are no chariots, no teams, no roads"],["atha rathān ratha-yogān pathaḥ sṛjate","acc. pl. + 3 sg. pres. of sṛj","yet he creates chariots, teams, and roads"],["na tatra ānandāḥ mudaḥ pramudaḥ bhavanti","nom. pl.","there are no joys, delights, raptures"],["atha ānandān mudaḥ pramudaḥ sṛjate","acc. pl.","yet he creates joys, delights, raptures"],["na tatra veśāntāḥ puṣkariṇyaḥ sravantyaḥ bhavanti","nom. pl. (ponds, lotus-pools, streams)","there are no pools, lotus-tanks, or flowing streams"],["atha veśāntān puṣkariṇīḥ sravantīḥ sṛjate","acc. pl.","yet he creates pools, lotus-tanks, and streams"],["saḥ hi kartā","m. nom. (the maker)","for he is the maker (creator)"]],"lit":"\"There (in dream) are no chariots, no teams, no roads — yet he creates chariots, teams, and roads. There are no joys, delights, or raptures — yet he creates joys, delights, and raptures. There are no pools, lotus-tanks, or streams — yet he creates pools, lotus-tanks, and streams. <strong>For he is the maker (creator).</strong>\"","para":"The dream-world has <strong>no external objects</strong> — no chariots, no pleasures, no waters exist \"out there\" — and yet the dreamer <strong>vividly experiences all of them</strong>, because the Self <strong>creates them all out of itself</strong>. The conclusion is emphatic: <strong>\"he is the maker\" (sa hi kartā)</strong> — the Self is the sole author of the dream-cosmos, projecting subjects, objects, and feelings from its own light. (A model, in miniature, of the Self's relation to the whole world.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the dreamer's self-sufficient creativity (objects, joys, waters, all from nothing external) demonstrates that the Self is the <strong>independent creator</strong> of its experienced world — and, by extension, points to the Self/Brahman as the creator of the cosmos (the dream is a window onto <em>māyā</em>). \"He is the maker\" — the Self is the efficient and material cause of the dream."],["Krishnananda","\"You are the architect of your dreams\" — and this self-projecting power is a clue to how the one Consciousness projects the waking world too. The dreamer is god of his world."],["Sureśvara","the dream-objects, having no external existence, are non-different from the dreaming consciousness — a paradigm of the world's non-difference from Brahman."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","meditate on your nightly creatorship to grasp the Self's freedom and power as pure consciousness."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes \"sa hi kartā\" (\"he is the maker\") as the climax of the dream-creativity argument."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.3.11–4.3.14","title":"The verses of the golden swan; \"they see his playground, none sees him\"","sa":["4.3.11: तदेते श्लोका भवन्ति — स्वप्नेन शारीरमभिप्रहत्यासुप्तः सुप्तानभिचाकशीति । शुक्रमादाय पुनरैति स्थानं हिरण्मयः पुरुष एकहंसः ॥ ११ ॥","4.3.12: प्राणेन रक्षन्नवरं कुलायं बहिष्कुलायादमृतश्चरित्वा । स ईयतेऽमृतो यत्रकामं हिरण्मयः पुरुष एकहंसः ॥ १२ ॥","4.3.13: स्वप्नान्त उच्चावचमीयमानो रूपाणि देवः कुरुते बहूनि । उतेव स्त्रीभिः सह मोदमानो जक्षदुतेवापि भयानि पश्यन् ॥ १३ ॥","4.3.14: आराममस्य पश्यन्ति न तं पश्यति कश्चनेति । तं नायतं बोधयेदित्याहुः । दुर्भिषज्यं हास्मai भवति यमेष न प्रतिपद्यते । अथो खल्वाहुर्जागरितदेश एवास्यैष इति । यानि ह्येव जाग्रत्पश्यति तानि सुप्त इति । अत्रायं पुरुषः स्वयंज्योतिर्भवति । सोऽहं भगवते सहस्रं ददामि । अत ऊर्ध्वं विमोक्षाय ब्रूहीति ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["tad ete ślokā bhavanti -- svapnena śārīram abhiprahatyāsuptaḥ suptān abhicākaśīti | śukram ādāya punar aiti sthānaṃ hiraṇmayaḥ puruṣa ekahaṃsaḥ || 11 ||","prāṇena rakṣann avaraṃ kulāyaṃ bahiṣkulāyād amṛtaś caritvā | sa īyate 'mṛto yatrakāmaṃ hiraṇmayaḥ puruṣa ekahaṃsaḥ || 12 ||","svapnānta uccāvacam īyamāno rūpāṇi devaḥ kurute bahūni | uteva strībhiḥ saha modamāno jakṣad utevāpi bhayāni paśyan || 13 ||","ārāmam asya paśyanti na taṃ paśyati kaś caneti | taṃ nāyataṃ bodhayed ity āhuḥ | durbhiṣajyaṃ hāsmai bhavati yam eṣa na pratipadyate | atho khalv āhur jāgaritadeśa evāsyaiṣa iti | yāni hy eva jāgrat paśyati tāni supta iti | atrāyaṃ puruṣaḥ svayaṃjyotir bhavati | so 'haṃ bhagavate sahasraṃ dadāmi | ata ūrdhvaṃ vimokṣāya brūhīti || 14 ||"],"words":[["svapnena śārīram abhiprahatya asuptaḥ suptān abhicākaśīti","instr. + acc. + ger. + nom. (himself unsleeping) + acc. pl. + pres.","by dream, having struck down the body, himself sleepless he watches the sleeping (senses)"],["śukram ādāya punaḥ aiti sthānaṃ hiraṇmayaḥ puruṣaḥ eka-haṃsaḥ","acc. (the bright/subtle) + ger. + pres. + nom. (golden) + (the one swan)","taking the bright (essence) he returns to his place — the golden person, the one swan"],["prāṇena rakṣan avaraṃ kulāyaṃ bahiḥ-kulāyāt amṛtaḥ caritvā","instr. + acc. (the lower nest = body) + abl. + nom. + ger.","guarding the lower nest (the body) by the breath, the immortal moving outside the nest"],["saḥ īyate amṛtaḥ yatra-kāmaṃ","pres. + adv.","the immortal goes wherever he wishes"],["svapna-ante uccāvacam īyamānaḥ rūpāṇi devaḥ kurute bahūni","loc. + acc. + pres.part. + acc. pl. + nom. (the shining one)","in dream, moving high and low, the god makes many forms"],["uta iva strībhiḥ saha modamānaḥ jakṣat uta iva api bhayāni paśyan","instr. + pres.part. + pres.part. (laughing/eating) + acc. pl. + pres.part.","now as if delighting with women, now as if laughing, now even seeing terrors"],["ārāmam asya paśyanti na taṃ paśyati kaḥ cana","acc. (playground) + pres. + acc.","(others) see his playground, but no one sees him"],["taṃ na āyatam bodhayet iti āhuḥ","acc. + opt. of budh (caus.)","they say one should not wake him suddenly"],["durbhiṣajyaṃ ha asmai bhavati yam eṣaḥ na pratipadyate","n. nom. (hard to cure) + acc.","it is hard to cure one to whom he (the self) does not return (to the right organ)"],["atho khalu āhuḥ jāgarita-deśaḥ eva asya eṣaḥ iti","nom.","yet some say his (dream) is just the waking realm"],["yāni hi eva jāgrat paśyati tāni suptaḥ iti","acc. pl.","for what he sees awake, that (he sees) asleep"],["atra ayaṃ puruṣaḥ svayaṃ-jyotiḥ bhavati","","(still) here this person is self-luminous"],["saḥ aham bhagavate sahasraṃ dadāmi ataḥ ūrdhvaṃ vimokṣāya brūhi iti","dat. + acc. + impv.","\"I give you a thousand (cows); speak further, for liberation\""]],"lit":"\"On this there are verses: 'Having struck down the body by dream, himself sleepless he watches the sleeping (senses); taking the bright (essence) he returns to his place — <strong>the golden person, the lone swan.</strong> Guarding the lower nest (the body) by the breath, the immortal moves outside the nest; the immortal goes wherever he wishes — the golden person, the lone swan. In the dream-world, moving high and low, the god fashions many forms — now seeming to delight with women, now laughing, now even beholding terrors.' (Prose:) Others see his playground, but no one sees him. They say one should not wake him abruptly; and one to whom he (the self) does not return rightly is hard to cure. Yet some say his (dream-state) is just the waking realm, for what he sees awake he sees asleep too. (Even so,) here this person is self-luminous. (Janaka:) 'I give you a thousand cows, sir; speak further, for the sake of liberation.'\"","para":"Four verses celebrate the dreaming Self as the <strong>\"golden person, the lone swan\" (hiraṇmayaḥ puruṣa eka-haṃsaḥ)</strong> — <em>haṃsa</em>, the swan, the classic symbol of the soul that flies free. In sleep it \"<strong>strikes down the body</strong>\" (deactivates it), keeps watch over the sleeping senses, and <strong>moves outside the \"nest\" (the body) wherever it wishes, immortal</strong>, while the breath guards the body left behind. It is the <strong>shining god (deva)</strong> who fashions the countless dream-forms — pleasures, laughter, even fears. <strong>No one can see <em>him</em> (the dreamer-self), only his \"playground\" (the dream).</strong> One should not wake a dreamer abruptly (the self may not return to the right organ). The objection that dream is \"just rearranged waking impressions\" is granted in part — but does not touch the main point: <strong>here the person is self-luminous.</strong> Janaka, thrilled, rewards Yājñavalkya and begs him to continue toward <strong>liberation (vimokṣa)</strong>.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>eka-haṃsa</em> (lone swan) / <em>hiraṇmaya puruṣa</em> (golden person) is the <strong>transmigrating, yet ever-free, Self</strong> — leaving the \"nest\" of the body in dream and wandering at will, immortal and self-luminous, while merely <em>associated</em> with the dream-forms it makes. The caution against sudden waking and the \"hard to cure\" remark show the self's mobility among the organs. Even granting the dream recombines waking materials, the <em>light</em> by which it is seen is the Self's own. Janaka's plea \"for liberation\" turns the discourse from analysis to the goal."],["Krishnananda","the soul as the free swan, flying out of the body's nest in dream — a luminous image of the Self's independence of the body. \"They see his playground, not him\" — the witness is never an object even in dream."],["Sureśvara","the verses confirm the Self's <em>asaṅga</em> (non-attachment) and self-luminosity through the dream-state."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the haṃsa-symbol for the soaring soul; meditate on the self as the immortal swan that leaves and returns to the body's nest."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the famous <em>eka-haṃsa</em> and <em>hiraṇmaya puruṣa</em> imagery and the embedded objection-and-reply about dream's materials."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.3.15–4.3.18","title":"The Self moves untouched between dream and waking; the great-fish simile","sa":["4.3.15: स वा एष एतस्मिन्संप्रसादे रत्वा चरित्वा दृष्ट्वैव पुण्यं च पापं च पुनः प्रतिन्यायं प्रतियोन्याद्रवति स्वप्नायैव । स यत्तत्र किंचित्पश्यत्यनन्वागतस्तेन भवति । असङ्गो ह्ययं पुरुष इति । एवमेवैतद्याज्ञवल्क्य । सोऽहं भगवते सहस्रं ददामि । अत ऊर्ध्वं विमोक्षायैव ब्रूहीति ॥ १५ ॥","4.3.16 (= 15, dream→waking): स वा एष एतस्मिन्स्वप्ने रत्वा चरित्वा दृष्ट्वैव पुण्यं च पापं च पुनः प्रतिन्यायं प्रतियोन्याद्रवति बुद्धान्तायैव …असङ्गो ह्ययं पुरुष इति … ॥ १६ ॥","4.3.17 (waking→dream): स वा एष एतस्मिन्बुद्धान्ते रत्वा चरित्वा दृष्ट्वैव पुण्यं च पापं च पुनः प्रतिन्यायं प्रतियोन्याद्रवति स्वप्नान्तायैव ॥ १७ ॥","4.3.18 (the great-fish simile): तद्यथा महामत्स्य उभे कूले अनुसंचरति पूर्वं चापरं च । एवमेवायं पुरुष एतावुभावन्तावनुसंचरति स्वप्नान्तं च बुद्धान्तं च ॥ १८ ॥"],"iast":["sa vā eṣa etasmin saṃprasāde ratvā caritvā dṛṣṭvaiva puṇyaṃ ca pāpaṃ ca punaḥ pratinyāyaṃ pratiyony ādravati svapnāyaiva | sa yat tatra kiñcit paśyaty ananvāgatas tena bhavati | asaṅgo hy ayaṃ puruṣa iti | … || 15 ||","sa vā eṣa etasmin svapne … punaḥ pratinyāyaṃ pratiyony ādravati buddhāntāyaiva … asaṅgo hy ayaṃ puruṣa iti … || 16 ||","sa vā eṣa etasmin buddhānte … ādravati svapnāntāyaiva || 17 ||","tad yathā mahāmatsya ubhe kūle anusaṃcarati pūrvaṃ cāparaṃ ca | evam evāyaṃ puruṣa etāv ubhāv antāv anusaṃcarati svapnāntaṃ ca buddhāntaṃ ca || 18 ||"],"words":[["saḥ vai eṣaḥ etasmin saṃprasāde / svapne / buddhānte ratvā caritvā dṛṣṭvā eva puṇyaṃ ca pāpaṃ ca","loc. + ger. (having enjoyed, moved, seen) + acc.","having enjoyed, ranged, and seen good and evil in (deep sleep / dream / waking),"],["punaḥ pratinyāyaṃ pratiyoni ādravati svapnāya / buddhāntāya / svapnāntāya eva","adv. + acc. + pres. of ā-dru + dat.","he runs back again, by the same route, to (dream / waking / dream)"],["saḥ yat tatra kiṃcit paśyati ananvāgataḥ tena bhavati","+ nom. (unaffected) + instr.","whatever he sees there, he remains untouched by it,"],["asaṅgaḥ hi ayaṃ puruṣaḥ iti","m. nom. (unattached)","for this person is unattached"],["tat yathā mahā-matsyaḥ ubhe kūle anusaṃcarati pūrvaṃ ca aparaṃ ca","nom. (great fish) + n. acc. du. (both banks)","as a great fish swims along both banks, the near and the far,"],["evam eva ayaṃ puruṣaḥ etau ubhau antau anusaṃcarati svapnāntaṃ ca buddhāntaṃ ca","acc. du. (the two ends)","so this person ranges both ends — the dream-state and the waking-state"]],"lit":"\"Having enjoyed, ranged, and seen good and evil in (deep sleep / dream / waking), he runs back again by the same route to (dream / waking / dream). Whatever he sees there, he remains <strong>untouched (ananvāgata)</strong> by it — for <strong>this person is unattached (asaṅga)</strong>. As a <strong>great fish swims along both banks</strong> of a river, the near and the far, so this person ranges <strong>both ends — the dream-state and the waking-state.</strong>\"","para":"The Self <strong>shuttles among the three states</strong> — deep sleep, dream, waking — \"enjoying, moving, seeing good and evil\" in each, and returning by the same path. The crucial refrain: <strong>whatever it experiences in any state, it remains untouched (ananvāgata) by it — for the Self is asaṅga, unattached.</strong> Like a <strong>great fish gliding between the two banks</strong> of a river (the near bank = waking, the far bank = dream), the Self moves freely between the states <strong>without belonging to either</strong> — the unattached witness of all. (<em>Asaṅga</em> — non-attachment — is a keynote of the Self repeated throughout 4.3–4.4.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Self's <strong>non-attachment (asaṅga)</strong> is the central teaching here: it passes through good and evil, dream and waking, <strong>without being affected</strong> — like the fish touching neither bank. This proves the Self is a mere <em>witness</em>, its apparent involvement belonging to the body-mind, not to itself. The \"same route\" (the nāḍīs/heart) is the path of transition."],["Krishnananda","\"You go from waking to dream to sleep and back, untouched by all\" — the Self is the constant, unattached witness of the changing states, as the fish belongs to the river, not to either shore."],["Sureśvara","<em>ananvāgata</em> (uncontaminated) and <em>asaṅga</em> (unattached) establish the witness's freedom from all it observes."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","meditate on yourself as the unattached witness moving through the states; identify with the fish (the witness), not the banks (the states)."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the great-fish simile and <em>asaṅga</em> as the recurring keynote of the Self's witness-nature."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3.19","title":"The falcon simile; the hastening to deep sleep","sa":["तद्यथास्मिन्नाकाशे श्येनो वा सुपर्णो वा विपरिपत्य श्रान्तः संहत्य पक्षौ संलयायैव ध्रियते । एवमेवायं पुरुष एतस्मा अन्ताय धावति यत्र सुप्तो न कंचन कामं कामयते न कंचन स्वप्नं पश्यति ॥ १९ ॥"],"iast":["tad yathāsminn ākāśe śyeno vā suparṇo vā viparipatya śrāntaḥ saṃhatya pakṣau saṃlayāyaiva dhriyate | evam evāyaṃ puruṣa etasmā antāya dhavati yatra supto na kaṃ cana kāmaṃ kāmayate na kaṃ cana svapnaṃ paśyati || 19 ||"],"words":[["tat yathā asmin ākāśe śyenaḥ vā suparṇaḥ vā viparipatya śrāntaḥ","loc. + nom. (falcon/eagle) + ger. + nom. (tired)","as a falcon or eagle, having flown about in the sky and grown tired,"],["saṃhatya pakṣau saṃlayāya eva dhriyate","ger. (folding) + acc. du. + dat. (for settling/roosting) + pres. pass.","folds its wings and is borne down just to settle (in its nest)"],["evam eva ayaṃ puruṣaḥ etasmai antāya dhāvati","dat. (toward this end/state) + pres. of dhāv","so this person hastens to that state"],["yatra suptaḥ na kaṃ cana kāmaṃ kāmayate na kaṃ cana svapnaṃ paśyati","rel. + pres.","wherein, asleep, he desires no desire and sees no dream"]],"lit":"\"As a <strong>falcon or eagle</strong>, having flown about in the sky and grown weary, folds its wings and is borne down just to settle (in its nest), so this person <strong>hastens to that state wherein, asleep, he desires no desire and sees no dream</strong> (= <strong>deep, dreamless sleep</strong>).\"","para":"A beautiful image of the soul's homing instinct. As a tired bird, done with flying, <strong>folds its wings and drops toward its nest to rest</strong>, so the Self, wearied by the activity of waking and dream, <strong>hastens to deep dreamless sleep</strong> — the state of <strong>no desire and no dream</strong> — to rest in its own ground. Deep sleep is the Self's natural \"nest,\" its return home to itself (developed in 4.3.20–23).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the bird-and-nest simile shows deep sleep as the Self's <strong>natural resting-place</strong> — its own blissful ground, to which it returns from the fatigue of duality (waking/dream). Desirelessness and dreamlessness mark the merging in the Self."],["Krishnananda","the soul, tired of the flights of experience, folds its wings and sinks into its own being — deep sleep is the unconscious foretaste of the peace of liberation."],["Sureśvara","the \"nest\" is the Self; deep sleep is reabsorption into it, free of desire and image."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","meditate on deep sleep as the soul's home-going; the goal is to enter that peace consciously."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the falcon/eagle simile and the definition of deep sleep as desireless and dreamless."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3.20","title":"The channels; dream-fear by ignorance; \"I am all\" — the highest world","sa":["ता वा अस्यैता हिता नाम नाड्यो यथा केशः सहस्रधा भिन्नस्तावताणिम्ना तिष्ठन्ति । शुक्लस्य नीलस्य पिङ्गलस्य हरितस्य लोहितस्य पूर्णाः । अथ यत्रैनं घ्नन्तीव जिनन्तीव हस्तीव विच्छाययति गर्तमिव पतति । यदेव जाग्रद्भयं पश्यति तदत्राविद्यया मन्यते । अथ यत्र देव इव राजेवाहमेवेदं सर्वोऽस्मीति मन्यते । सोऽस्य परमो लोकः ॥ २० ॥"],"iast":["tā vā asyaitā hitā nāma nāḍyo yathā keśaḥ sahasradhā bhinnas tāvatāṇimnā tiṣṭhanti | śuklasya nīlasya piṅgalasya haritasya lohitasya pūrṇāḥ | atha yatrainaṃ ghnantīva jinantīva hastīva vicchāyayati gartam iva patati | yad eva jāgrad bhayaṃ paśyati tad atrāvidyayā manyate | atha yatra deva iva rājevāham evedaṃ sarvo 'smīti manyate | so 'sya paramo lokaḥ || 20 ||"],"words":[["tāḥ vai asya etāḥ hitā nāma nāḍyaḥ yathā keśaḥ sahasradhā bhinnaḥ tāvatā aṇimnā tiṣṭhanti","f. nom. pl. + instr. (of such fineness)","his channels called hitā stand as fine as a hair split a thousandfold"],["śuklasya nīlasya piṅgalasya haritasya lohitasya pūrṇāḥ","gen. + nom. pl.","filled with (essences) white, blue, brown, green, and red"],["atha yatra enaṃ ghnanti iva jinanti iva hastī iva vicchāyayati gartam iva patati","pres. + iva","now where (in dream) they seem to kill him, overpower him, an elephant seems to chase him, he seems to fall into a pit"],["yat eva jāgrat bhayaṃ paśyati tat atra avidyayā manyate","acc. + instr. (through ignorance) + pres.","the very fear he sees when awake, that he imagines here through ignorance"],["atha yatra deva iva rājā iva aham eva idaṃ sarvaḥ asmi iti manyate","nom.","but where he thinks, \"like a god, like a king, I am all this,\""],["saḥ asya paramaḥ lokaḥ","m. nom.","that is his highest world"]],"lit":"\"His channels, called <em>hitā</em>, stand as fine as a hair split a thousandfold, filled with (subtle essences) white, blue, brown, green, and red. Now where (in dream) they seem to kill him, overpower him, where an elephant seems to chase him, where he seems to fall into a pit — the very terror he sees when awake, that he imagines here <strong>through ignorance (avidyā)</strong>. But where he thinks, '<strong>Like a god, like a king, I am all this</strong>' — <strong>that is his highest world.</strong>\"","para":"The dream-self moves along the fine <strong>hitā channels</strong> (filled with subtly coloured essences). Dream has two registers: <strong>fearful dreams</strong> (being killed, chased, falling) — which are projections of <strong>waking fears through avidyā (ignorance)</strong>, not reality; and a <strong>higher register</strong> where the self feels <strong>\"I am a god, I am a king, I am all this\" (ahaṃ sarvo 'smi)</strong> — a glimpse, even in dream, of its <strong>boundless, sovereign nature.</strong> That expansive \"I am all\" is \"<strong>his highest world</strong>\" — the nearest dream comes to the truth of the all-encompassing Self (fully realised in deep sleep, 4.3.21).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","dream-fears are <em>avidyā</em>-born (mere mental projections of waking impressions), hence unreal; but the dream-feeling \"I am all\" is a faint reflection of the Self's true infinitude. The <em>hitā</em> channels are the subtle pathways; the colours, the residual impressions. The \"highest world\" of dream foreshadows the boundless Self of deep sleep."],["Krishnananda","even dream shows the two possibilities — bondage (fear, through ignorance) and the intimation of the all-Self (\"I am everything\"). The latter is the dream-glimpse of Brahman."],["Sureśvara","dream-terror is <em>avidyā</em>'s work; the \"I am all\" is the Self's nature breaking through."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note that fear is always ignorance-born; cultivate the \"I am all\" recognition."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the <em>hitā</em> channels with their colours and the contrast of fear-dreams (avidyā) with the expansive \"I am all.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3.21","title":"Deep sleep: embraced by the intelligent Self, beyond desire and sorrow","sa":["तद्वा अस्यैतदतिच्छन्दोऽपहतपाप्माभयं रूपम् । तद्यथा प्रियया स्त्रिया संपरिष्वक्तो न बाह्यं किंचन वेद नान्तरम् । एवमेवायं पुरुषः प्राज्ञेनात्मना संपरिष्वक्तो न बाह्यं किंचन वेद नान्तरम् । तद्वा अस्यैतदाप्तकाममात्मकाममकामं रूपं शोकान्तरम् ॥ २१ ॥"],"iast":["tad vā asyaitad aticchando 'pahatapāpmābhayaṃ rūpam | tad yathā priyayā striyā saṃpariṣvakto na bāhyaṃ kiṃ cana veda nāntaram | evam evāyaṃ puruṣaḥ prājñenātmanā saṃpariṣvakto na bāhyaṃ kiṃ cana veda nāntaram | tad vā asyaitad āptakāmam ātmakāmam akāmaṃ rūpaṃ śokāntaram || 21 ||"],"words":[["tat vai asya etat aticchandaḥ apahata-pāpmā abhayaṃ rūpam","n. nom. (beyond desire) + (with evil struck off) + (fearless) + (form)","this (deep-sleep) form of his is beyond desire, free of evil, fearless"],["tat yathā priyayā striyā saṃpariṣvaktaḥ na bāhyaṃ kiṃ cana veda na āntaram","instr. (by a beloved wife) + nom. pp. (embraced)","as a man, embraced by a beloved wife, knows nothing outside or inside,"],["evam eva ayaṃ puruṣaḥ prājñena ātmanā saṃpariṣvaktaḥ na bāhyaṃ kiṃ cana veda na āntaram","instr. (by the intelligent/conscious Self)","so this person, embraced by the intelligent (prājña) Self, knows nothing outside or inside"],["tat vai asya etat āpta-kāmam ātma-kāmam akāmaṃ rūpaṃ śoka-antaram","n. nom. (desires-fulfilled) + (Self the only desire) + (desireless) + (free of sorrow)","this form of his is one of fulfilled desire, of the Self as the only desire, desireless, beyond sorrow"]],"lit":"\"This (deep-sleep) form of his is <strong>beyond desire, free of evil, fearless</strong>. As a man embraced by a beloved wife knows nothing outside or inside, so this person, <strong>embraced by the intelligent Self (prājña ātman)</strong>, knows nothing outside or inside. This form of his is one of <strong>desires fulfilled, the Self alone its desire, desireless, beyond sorrow.</strong>\"","para":"The supreme description of <strong>deep dreamless sleep</strong>: the self is <strong>\"beyond desire, free of all evil, fearless.\"</strong> The famous simile: just as a man <strong>locked in the embrace of a beloved wife</strong> is utterly absorbed, <strong>aware of nothing within or without</strong>, so in deep sleep the self is <strong>\"embraced by the prājña (intelligent/conscious) Self\"</strong> — merged in pure consciousness — and <strong>knows neither outer nor inner</strong> (subject-object duality dissolves). This is the state of <strong>āpta-kāma (all desires fulfilled), ātma-kāma (the Self the only \"object\" of desire), akāma (desireless), śokāntara (beyond sorrow)</strong> — the natural bliss of the Self when, free of objects, it rests in itself. (This is the Upaniṣad's classic account of the <em>ānanda</em> of Brahman, glimpsed nightly in deep sleep.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","deep sleep reveals the Self's <strong>intrinsic nature: desireless, evil-free, fearless, blissful.</strong> The embrace-simile shows the <strong>dissolution of duality</strong> — in union with the <em>prājña</em> (the Self as undifferentiated consciousness), there is no \"outside\" (object) or \"inside\" (subject); knower and known merge. <em>Āpta-kāma/ātma-kāma/akāma</em> — desire ceases because the Self, being full, lacks nothing. This is the <em>ānanda</em> of Brahman experienced (though unconsciously) in deep sleep; it proves bliss is the Self's <em>own</em> nature, not derived from any object (since none is present). The model for the liberated state — to be made <em>conscious</em> in <em>jīvanmukti</em>."],["Krishnananda","\"In deep sleep you embrace the Self and know nothing else — that is why you wake refreshed and say 'I slept happily.'\" The bliss is objectless, the Self's own; the embrace-simile conveys the total absorption in which all duality vanishes. The goal of yoga is this same union, awake."],["Sureśvara","deep sleep proves <em>ānanda</em> is the Self's nature (bliss with no object); the dissolution of inner/outer is the non-dual state, here transient (covered by ignorance), in liberation permanent and luminous."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the dearest analogy — the bliss of the Self's embrace surpasses all sense-pleasure; meditate to enter consciously the desireless, sorrowless peace tasted nightly."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the celebrated conjugal-embrace simile and the cluster <em>āpta-kāma / ātma-kāma / akāma</em> as defining the Self's blissful, desireless nature in deep sleep."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3.22","title":"In that state, all distinctions vanish","sa":["अत्र पितापिता भवति मातामाता लोका अलोका देवा अदेवा वेदा अवेदाः । अत्र स्तेनोऽस्तेनो भवति भ्रूणहाभ्रूणहा चाण्डालोऽचाण्डालः पौल्कसोऽपौल्कसः श्रमणोऽश्रमणस्तापसोऽतापसः । अनन्वागतं पुण्येनानन्वागतं पापेन । तीर्णो हि तदा सर्वाञ्छोकान्हृदयस्य भवति ॥ २२ ॥"],"iast":["atra pitāpitā bhavati mātāmātā lokā alokā devā adevā vedā avedāḥ | atra steno 'steno bhavati bhrūṇahābhrūṇahā cāṇḍālo 'caṇḍālaḥ paulkaso 'paulkasaḥ śramaṇo 'śramaṇas tāpaso 'tāpasaḥ | ananvāgataṃ puṇyenānanvāgataṃ pāpena | tīrṇo hi tadā sarvāñ śokān hṛdayasya bhavati || 22 ||"],"words":[["atra pitā apitā bhavati mātā amātā lokāḥ alokāḥ devāḥ adevāḥ vedāḥ avedāḥ","nom.","there a father is no father, a mother no mother, the worlds no worlds, the gods no gods, the Vedas no Vedas"],["atra stenaḥ astenaḥ bhavati bhrūṇahā abhrūṇahā cāṇḍālaḥ acaṇḍālaḥ paulkasaḥ apaulkasaḥ","nom.","there a thief is no thief, a slayer of an embryo no slayer, an outcaste (cāṇḍāla) no outcaste, a pulkasa no pulkasa"],["śramaṇaḥ aśramaṇaḥ tāpasaḥ atāpasaḥ","nom.","a wandering monk no monk, an ascetic no ascetic"],["ananvāgataṃ puṇyena ananvāgataṃ pāpena","n. nom. (unfollowed/untouched) + instr.","(he is) untouched by good, untouched by evil"],["tīrṇaḥ hi tadā sarvān śokān hṛdayasya bhavati","nom. (having crossed) + acc. pl. + gen.","for then he has crossed all the sorrows of the heart"]],"lit":"\"There (in deep sleep / the realised state) a father is no father, a mother no mother, the worlds no worlds, the gods no gods, the Vedas no Vedas; there a thief is no thief, a killer of an embryo no killer, a cāṇḍāla no cāṇḍāla, a pulkasa no pulkasa, a monk no monk, an ascetic no ascetic. He is <strong>untouched by good, untouched by evil</strong>, for then he has <strong>crossed all the sorrows of the heart.</strong>\"","para":"In that state of union with the Self, <strong>all distinctions and identities dissolve</strong> — familial (father, mother), cosmic (worlds, gods), scriptural (Vedas), moral and social (thief, embryo-slayer — the gravest sins; cāṇḍāla, pulkasa — the lowest births; monk, ascetic — the holiest roles). None of these <em>apply</em> to the Self, which is <strong>beyond good and evil (puṇya/pāpa)</strong> — not because it is immoral, but because it <strong>transcends the entire realm of distinctions</strong> in which merit and demerit operate. There the self has <strong>\"crossed all the sorrows of the heart\"</strong> — for sorrow, like sin and role, belongs to the world of difference, not to the non-dual Self. (This is the freedom of the Self, glimpsed in deep sleep and realised in liberation — not antinomian license but transcendence of the very framework of duality.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","in the Self's true state <strong>all relational and moral categories lapse</strong> — they belong to <em>avidyā</em>'s world of difference, which is absent here. \"Beyond good and evil\" means the Self is the <strong>untouched witness</strong>, not subject to karma's merit/demerit (which bind only the embodied agent). Crucially, this is <em>not</em> a license for the ignorant to sin (they remain bound by karma); it describes the <strong>transcendence</strong> proper to Self-knowledge — the crossing beyond <em>all</em> the heart's sorrows, which arise only from duality."],["Krishnananda","\"All your relations and roles, all virtue and vice, fall away\" — not destroyed, but seen to be irrelevant to the Self, which was never the father, the sinner, or the saint. Sorrow ends because its very ground (otherness) is gone."],["Sureśvara","the negation of all dualities (kinship, morality, scripture) marks the Self as the <strong>non-dual witness</strong> beyond the field where such terms have meaning."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the supreme freedom — the realised Self is beyond the pairs of opposites and beyond grief; this is <em>not</em> moral nihilism but transcendence (the ignorant are still bound)."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the sweeping negation of social-moral-religious categories as expressing the Self's transcendence of the dual world; \"beyond good and evil\" to be read as transcendence, not antinomianism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.3.23–4.3.30","title":"\"Though seeing, it does not see\": the imperishable Seer","sa":["4.3.23 (sight — template): यद्वै तन्न पश्यति पश्यन्वै तन्न पश्यति । न हि द्रष्टुर्दृष्टेर्विपरिलोपो विद्यतेऽविनाशित्वान् । न तु तद्द्वितीयमस्ति ततोऽन्यद्विभक्तं यत्पश्येत् ॥ २३ ॥","4.3.30 (knowing): यद्वै तन्न विजानाति विजानन्वै तन्न विजानाति । न हि विज्ञातुर्विज्ञातेर्विपरिलोपो विद्यतेऽविनाशित्वात् । न तु तद्द्वितीयमस्ति ततोऽन्यद्विभक्तं यद्विजानीयात् ॥ ३० ॥"],"iast":["yad vai tan na paśyati paśyan vai tan na paśyati | na hi draṣṭur dṛṣṭer viparilopo vidyate 'vināśitvān | na tu tad dvitīyam asti tato 'nyad vibhaktaṃ yat paśyet || 23 ||","yad vai tan na vijānāti vijānan vai tan na vijānāti | na hi vijñātur vijñāter viparilopo vidyate 'vināśitvāt | na tu tad dvitīyam asti tato 'nyad vibhaktaṃ yad vijānīyāt || 30 ||"],"words":[["yat vai tat na paśyati","rel. + n. + pres.","as for its (apparently) \"not seeing\" —"],["paśyan vai tat na paśyati","pres.part. + pres.","(truly) seeing, it does not see (i.e., it sees, but not a second thing)"],["na hi draṣṭuḥ dṛṣṭeḥ viparilopaḥ vidyate avināśitvāt","gen. (of the seer) + gen. (of seeing) + m. nom. (cessation) + abl. (because of imperishability)","for there is no cessation of the seeing of the seer, because of (its) imperishability"],["na tu tat dvitīyam asti tataḥ anyat vibhaktaṃ yat paśyet","n. nom. + abl. + pp. (separate) + opt.","but there is no second thing, separate from it, that it might see"]],"lit":"<strong>4.3.24–30</strong> (smelling / tasting / speaking / hearing / thinking / touching / knowing): identical frame, substituting <em>jighrati</em> (smell), <em>rasayati</em> (taste), <em>vadati</em> (speak), <em>śṛṇoti</em> (hear), <em>manute</em> (think), <em>spṛśati</em> (touch), <em>vijānāti</em> (know):<br>\"As for its 'not seeing' (in deep sleep / the non-dual state): (truly) it sees, yet it does not see — for there is no cessation of the seer's seeing, because the seer is imperishable; but there is no second thing, separate from it, that it might see.\" <em>(So for smelling, tasting, speaking, hearing, thinking, touching, and knowing.)</em>","para":"A subtle and crucial clarification. When it is said that in deep sleep (or the non-dual Self) one \"does not see / hear / know,\" this does <strong>not</strong> mean consciousness has ceased. The Self's <strong>power of awareness is imperishable (avināśitva) — it never lapses.</strong> The reason there is \"no seeing\" is simply that <strong>there is no second, separate object</strong> to be seen: in non-duality, the subject-object split (which all perception requires) is absent. So the apparent \"blankness\" of deep sleep is not unconsciousness but <strong>objectless consciousness</strong> — pure awareness with nothing other than itself to know. (This forecloses the misreading that the Self in deep sleep, or in liberation, is inert or extinct — cf. 2.4.14, \"by what should one know the Knower?\".)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the decisive refutation of the view that the Self is unconscious in deep sleep. <strong>Consciousness is the Self's very nature and so is imperishable</strong> (\"no cessation of the seer's seeing\"); what is absent is only the <em>object</em> (\"no second thing\"). Hence \"it does not see\" = it does not see <em>a separate something</em>, not that it lacks awareness. The eightfold repetition covers all modes of cognition. This is the cornerstone text (with 2.4.14) for the doctrine that the witness-consciousness is <strong>eternal and self-luminous</strong>, never a momentary product."],["Krishnananda","\"Even in deep sleep, consciousness does not go; only the objects go.\" The seer's seeing is eternal; its apparent absence is the absence of the seen. This is why pure consciousness, though objectless, is never nothing."],["Sureśvara","<em>avināśitva</em> (imperishability) of the witness is established against the Buddhist/momentariness view; awareness is continuous, lacking only a second."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a vital safeguard against nihilism — the Self is ever-conscious; \"no seeing\" means \"no second to see,\" not \"no awareness.\""],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the eightfold <em>\"X-ing, it does not X\"</em> formula and <em>avināśitva</em> as asserting the permanence of the subject against the transience of objects."]],"notes":["<em>Eight parallel mantras (for seeing, smelling, tasting, speaking, hearing, thinking, touching, knowing). Each makes the same subtle point: in the non-dual state the Self's</em> <strong>power of perception never lapses</strong> <em>(it is imperishable), yet there is</em> <strong>no second thing</strong> <em>for it to perceive — so it \"does not see,\" not from blindness but from the absence of an object.</em>"]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.3.31–4.3.32","title":"Duality vs. the non-dual Seer; the brahma-world","sa":["4.3.31: यत्र वा अन्यदिव स्यात्तत्रान्योऽन्यत्पश्येदन्योऽन्यज्जिघ्रेदन्योऽन्यद्रसयेदन्योऽन्यद्वदेदन्योऽन्यच्छृणुयादन्योऽन्यन्मन्वीतान्योऽन्यत्स्पृशेदन्योऽन्यद्विजानीयात् ॥ ३१ ॥","4.3.32: सलिल एको द्रष्टाद्वैतो भवति । एष ब्रह्मलोकः संराडिति हैनमनुशशास याज्ञवल्क्यः । एषास्य परमा गतिः । एषास्य परमा संपत् । एषोऽस्य परमो लोकः । एषोऽस्य परम आनन्दः । एतस्यैवानन्दस्यान्यानि भूतानि मात्रामुपजीवन्ति ॥ ३२ ॥"],"iast":["yatra vā anyad iva syāt tatrānyo 'nyat paśyed anyo 'nyaj jighred anyo 'nyad rasayed anyo 'nyad vaded anyo 'nyac chṛṇuyād anyo 'nyan manvītānyo 'nyat spṛśed anyo 'nyad vijānīyāt || 31 ||","salila eko draṣṭādvaito bhavati | eṣa brahmalokaḥ samrāṭ iti hainam anuśaśāsa yājñavalkyaḥ | eṣāsya paramā gatiḥ | eṣāsya paramā saṃpat | eṣo 'sya paramo lokaḥ | eṣo 'sya parama ānandaḥ | etasyaivānandasyānyāni bhūtāni mātrām upajīvanti || 32 ||"],"words":[["yatra vai anyat iva syāt","rel. + n. + iva + opt.","where there is, as it were, a second (an \"other\"),"],["tatra anyaḥ anyat paśyet (… jighret … rasayet … vadet … śṛṇuyāt … manvīta … spṛśet … vijānīyāt)","nom. + acc. + opt.","there one (sees / smells / tastes / speaks to / hears / thinks of / touches / knows) another"],["salilaḥ ekaḥ draṣṭā advaitaḥ bhavati","nom. (like clear water) + (one) + (seer) + (non-dual)","(but in unity) he becomes the one seer, without a second, like clear water"],["eṣaḥ brahma-lokaḥ samrāṭ iti ha enam anuśaśāsa yājñavalkyaḥ","nom. + voc. + 3 sg. pf. of anu-śās","\"this is the brahma-world (the world of Brahman), your majesty\" — thus Yājñavalkya instructed him"],["eṣā asya paramā gatiḥ … paramā saṃpat … paramaḥ lokaḥ … paramaḥ ānandaḥ","f./m. nom.","this is his highest goal, his highest attainment, his highest world, his highest bliss"],["etasya eva ānandasya anyāni bhūtāni mātrām upajīvanti","gen. + n. nom. pl. + f. acc. (a fraction) + pres.","on a mere fraction of this bliss other beings live"]],"lit":"\"Where there is, as it were, a second (an 'other'), there one sees another, smells another, tastes another, speaks to another, hears another, thinks of another, touches another, knows another. <strong>(But in unity) he becomes the one seer, without a second, like clear (boundless) water.</strong> <strong>This is the brahma-world (the world of Brahman), your majesty</strong> — thus Yājñavalkya instructed him. <strong>This is his highest goal, his highest attainment, his highest world, his highest bliss. On a mere fraction of this bliss other beings live.</strong>\"","para":"The two conditions are set side by side. <strong>Where duality (anyat, \"an other\") seems to exist</strong> — i.e., in waking and dream — there is the whole apparatus of relational experience: one <em>sees/hears/knows another.</em> <strong>But where all has become one</strong> — in the realised non-dual state — the self is the <strong>\"one seer, without a second, like clear (limitless) water\" (salila eko draṣṭā advaitaḥ)</strong>: pure undivided awareness, transparent and boundless. <strong>This is the brahma-loka</strong> (here = the state/world of Brahman, not a heaven) — the Self's <strong>highest goal, attainment, world, and bliss (parama ānanda).</strong> And the staggering close: <strong>all other beings live on but a <em>fraction</em> (mātrā) of this bliss</strong> — every joy in the cosmos is a tiny portion of the infinite bliss that <em>is</em> the Self. (This grounds the bliss-calculus of 4.3.33.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>salila eko draṣṭā advaitaḥ</em> — \"like water, the one seer, non-dual\" — the supreme statement of the realised Self: <strong>pure, transparent, undivided consciousness</strong> with no second. (The <em>salila</em>/water image conveys clarity and limitlessness, not insentience — read with 4.3.23–30, awareness persists; only the object is gone.) This is the <em>brahma-loka</em> in the highest sense — <em>being</em> Brahman, the paramā gati (final goal). \"Other beings live on a fraction of this bliss\" = the Self's infinite <em>ānanda</em> is the source from which all lesser joys are dim reflections — the basis of the bliss-hierarchy of 4.3.33 and of Taittirīya's <em>ānandamīmāṃsā</em>."],["Krishnananda","\"Where there is a second, there is fear, desire, perception of an other; where the Self alone is, there is the one boundless Seer, like a sea of clear water.\" This non-dual awareness is the highest world and the highest bliss; every worldly joy is a droplet of this ocean."],["Sureśvara","the contrast (duality → relational perception; unity → the one seer) defines the goal; <em>advaita</em> (non-dual) is here named outright as the supreme state."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the realised state is the one transparent Seer without a second — the highest bliss, of which all pleasures are fragments. Aim at <em>this</em>."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes <em>salila eko draṣṭā advaitaḥ</em> as a peak formulation of non-dualism and the \"fraction of bliss\" motif feeding the calculus that follows."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3.33","title":"The calculus of bliss","sa":["स यो मनुष्याणां राद्धः समृद्धो भवत्यन्येषामधिपतिः सर्वैर्मानुष्यकैर्भोगैः सम्पन्नतमः स मनुष्याणां परम आनन्दः । अथ ये शतं मनुष्याणामानन्दाः स एकः पितॄणां जितलोकानामानन्दः … (ascending by hundreds: pitṛs → gandharva-loka → karma-devas → ājāna-devas → Prajāpati-loka → brahma-loka) … अथैष एव परम आनन्दः । एष ब्रह्मलोकः संराडिति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः … (योऽवृजिनोऽकामहतः श्रोत्रियः) ॥ ३३ ॥"],"iast":["sa yo manuṣyāṇāṃ rāddhaḥ samṛddho bhavaty anyeṣām adhipatiḥ sarvair mānuṣyakair bhogaiḥ sampannatamaḥ sa manuṣyāṇāṃ parama ānandaḥ | atha ye śataṃ manuṣyāṇām ānandāḥ sa ekaḥ pitṝṇāṃ jitalokānām ānandaḥ … (×100 at each step: gandharva-loka → karma-devas → ājāna-devas → prajāpati-loka → brahma-loka) … athaiṣa eva parama ānandaḥ | eṣa brahmalokaḥ samrāṭ iti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ | … yaś ca śrotriyo 'vṛjino 'kāmahataḥ … so 'haṃ bhagavate sahasraṃ dadāmi ata ūrdhvaṃ vimokṣāyaiva brūhīti | atra ha yājñavalkyo bibhayāṃ cakāra -- medhāvī rājā sarvebhyo māntebhya udarautsīd iti || 33 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>The ascending scale of bliss (each step = 100× the previous):</strong><br>\"He who is the most prosperous, sovereign man — lord of others, richest in all human enjoyments — his is the highest human bliss. A hundred such human blisses make one bliss of the fathers who have won their world; a hundred of those, one bliss of the gandharva-world; a hundred of those, one bliss of the karma-devas (gods-by-works); a hundred of those, one bliss of the ājāna-devas (gods-by-birth) — and of one who is a śrotriya (learned in the Veda), free of sin, and unsmitten by desire; a hundred of those, one bliss of Prajāpati's world — and of the sinless, desireless śrotriya; a hundred of those, one bliss of the brahma-world — and of the sinless, desireless śrotriya. <strong>This is the supreme bliss; this is the brahma-world, your majesty,</strong>\" said Yājñavalkya. (Janaka:) \"I give you a thousand cows, sir; speak further, for liberation itself.\" Here Yājñavalkya grew afraid, thinking, \"the clever king has driven me out of all my retreats!\"","para":"Yājñavalkya quantifies the <strong>fraction</strong> of 4.3.32 in a famous <strong>bliss-hierarchy</strong>: starting from the <strong>greatest possible human happiness</strong> (a powerful, wealthy, sovereign man at the peak of enjoyment), each successive order of being enjoys <strong>a hundredfold</strong> the bliss of the one below — fathers, gandharvas, gods-by-works, gods-by-birth, Prajāpati's world, and finally the <strong>brahma-world</strong>, whose bliss is the <strong>supreme</strong>. The decisive twist: at each high rung, the <strong>śrotriya who is sinless and free of desire (avṛjino 'kāmahataḥ)</strong> enjoys the <em>same</em> bliss — meaning the <strong>desireless knower, even now, partakes of the highest bliss</strong> without ascending through the cosmic ranks. All these graded joys are <strong>portions of the one infinite bliss of the Self</strong> (4.3.32). The frame closes with a human touch: <strong>Janaka keeps pressing \"teach me liberation,\"</strong> and <strong>Yājñavalkya grows wary</strong> — the king has cornered him into giving away his deepest knowledge (\"driven me out of all my hiding-places\").","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>ānanda-mīmāṃsā</em> (measure of bliss) — a graded scale showing all happiness as portions of the Self's infinite <em>ānanda</em> (cf. TaittU 2.8). The repeated <strong>\"and the śrotriya free of sin and desire\"</strong> is the key: the <strong>desireless knower already enjoys, here and now, the bliss of each higher world</strong> — because bliss is proportional to <strong>freedom from desire</strong> (the more desireless, the fuller the bliss), and the knower is wholly desireless. Thus the highest bliss is not a far heaven but the present state of the realised. Yājñavalkya's fear shows even a great teacher's reluctance to give away the final secret — which Janaka's persistence wins."],["Krishnananda","\"Bliss increases as desire decreases\" — the calculus is really a measure of <em>desirelessness</em>: each higher being is freer of want, hence happier; the desireless knower leaps to the top of the scale at once. Worldly pleasure is the faintest fraction of the Self's ocean of bliss."],["Sureśvara","the hundredfold scale and the śrotriya's parity prove that <em>ānanda</em> is the Self's nature, accessed by desirelessness, not by cosmic ascent."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical lesson — reduce desire to increase bliss; the sinless, desireless knower enjoys even now the bliss of the brahma-world. Aim at desirelessness, not at heavens."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the <em>ānanda</em>-calculus (paralleled in TaittU) and the leitmotif of the <em>akāmahata</em> (\"unstruck by desire\") śrotriya sharing the highest bliss; and the rare narrative aside of Yājñavalkya's apprehension."]],"notes":["<table><tr><td>level of bliss</td><td>whose</td></tr><tr><td>1 unit</td><td>the <strong>supreme human</strong> bliss (a prosperous, sovereign man, lord of others, richest in all human enjoyments)</td></tr><tr><td>×100</td><td>bliss of the <strong>fathers</strong> (ancestors) who have won their world</td></tr><tr><td>×100</td><td>bliss of the <strong>gandharva-world</strong></td></tr><tr><td>×100</td><td>bliss of the <strong>karma-devas</strong> (gods by works — who became gods by rites)</td></tr><tr><td>×100</td><td>bliss of the <strong>ājāna-devas</strong> (gods by birth) — <em>and of the Vedic scholar (śrotriya) who is sinless and free of desire</em></td></tr><tr><td>×100</td><td>bliss of the <strong>world of Prajāpati</strong> — <em>and of the sinless, desireless śrotriya</em></td></tr><tr><td>×100</td><td>bliss of the <strong>world of Brahman (brahma-loka)</strong> — <em>and of the sinless, desireless śrotriya</em></td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.3.34–4.3.38","title":"Return to waking; the process of dying","sa":["4.3.34: स वा एष एतस्मिन्स्वप्नान्ते रत्वा चरित्वा दृष्ट्वैव पुण्यं च पापं च पुनः प्रतिन्यायं प्रतियोन्याद्रवति बुद्धान्तायैव ॥ ३४ ॥","4.3.35 (the groaning cart): तद्यथानः सुसमाहितमुत्सर्जं यायादेवमेवायं शारीर आत्मा प्राज्ञेनात्मनान्वारूढ उत्सर्जं याति यत्रैतदूर्ध्वोच्छ्वासी भवति ॥ ३५ ॥","4.3.36 (the ripe fruit): स यत्रायमणिमानं न्येति जरया वोपतपता वाणिमानं निगच्छति तद्यथाम्रं वोदुम्बरं वा पिप्पलं वा बन्धनात्प्रमुच्यते एवमेवायं पुरुष एभ्योऽङ्गेभ्यः संप्रमुच्य पुनः प्रतिन्यायं प्रतियोन्याद्रवति प्राणायैव ॥ ३६ ॥","4.3.37–38 (the breaths gather, as around a king): तद्यथा राजानमायन्तमुग्राः प्रत्येनसः सूतग्रामण्योऽन्नैः पानैरावसथैः प्रतिकल्पन्तेऽयमायात्ययमागच्छतीति । एवं हैवंविदं सर्वाणि भूतानि प्रतिकल्पन्त इदं ब्रह्मायातीदमागच्छतीति ॥ ३७ ॥ तद्यथा राजानं प्रयियासन्तमुग्राः प्रत्येनसः सूतग्रामण्योऽभिसमायन्ति एवमेवेममात्मानमन्तकाले सर्वे प्राणा अभिसमायन्ति यत्रैतदूर्ध्वोच्छ्वासी भवति ॥ ३८ ॥"],"iast":["sa vā eṣa etasmin svapnānte ratvā caritvā dṛṣṭvaiva puṇyaṃ ca pāpaṃ ca punaḥ pratinyāyaṃ pratiyony ādravati buddhāntāyaiva || 34 ||","tad yathānaḥ susamāhitam utsarjaṃ yāyād evam evāyaṃ śārīra ātmā prājñenātmanānvārūḍha utsarjaṃ yāti yatraitad ūrdhvocchvāsī bhavati || 35 ||","sa yatrāyam aṇimānaṃ nyeti jarayā vopatapatā vāṇimānaṃ nigacchati tad yathāmraṃ vodumbaraṃ vā pippalaṃ vā bandhanāt pramucyate evam evāyaṃ puruṣa ebhyo 'ṅgebhyaḥ saṃpramucya punaḥ pratinyāyaṃ pratiyony ādravati prāṇāyaiva || 36 ||","tad yathā rājānam āyantam ugrāḥ pratyenasaḥ sūtagrāmaṇyo 'nnaiḥ pānair avasathaiḥ pratikalpante 'yam āyāty ayam āgacchatīti | evaṃ haivaṃvidaṃ sarvāṇi bhūtāni pratikalpanta idaṃ brahmāyātīdam āgacchatīti || 37 || tad yathā rājānaṃ prayiyāsantam ugrāḥ pratyenasaḥ sūtagrāmaṇyo 'bhisamāyanti evam evemam ātmānam antakāle sarve prāṇā abhisamāyanti yatraitad ūrdhvocchvāsī bhavati || 38 ||"],"words":[["tat yathā anaḥ susamāhitam utsarjam yāyāt","nom. (a cart) + pp. (heavily loaded) + adv. (creaking/straining) + opt. of yā","as a heavily-laden cart goes creaking along,"],["evam eva ayaṃ śārīraḥ ātmā prājñena ātmanā anvārūḍhaḥ utsarjam yāti","nom. (the bodily self) + instr. (the conscious self) + pp. (mounted)","so the bodily self, mounted by the conscious Self, goes groaning (at death)"],["yatra etat ūrdhva-ucchvāsī bhavati","adj. (breathing upward, gasping)","when he comes to breathe upward (gasps his last)"],["saḥ yatra ayam aṇimānaṃ nyeti jarayā vā upatapatā vā","acc. (feebleness) + pres. + instr. (by age or disease)","when he grows feeble through old age or disease,"],["tat yathā āmraṃ vā udumbaraṃ vā pippalaṃ vā bandhanāt pramucyate","acc. (a mango, fig, pippal) + abl. + pres. pass.","as a mango, fig, or pippal-fruit is loosed from its stalk,"],["evam eva ayaṃ puruṣaḥ ebhyaḥ aṅgebhyaḥ saṃpramucya punaḥ pratinyāyaṃ pratiyoni ādravati prāṇāya eva","abl. pl. + ger. + acc. + pres.","so this person, freeing himself from the limbs, hastens back, by the same route, to (a new) life/birth"],["tat yathā rājānam āyantam ugrāḥ pratyenasaḥ sūta-grāmaṇyaḥ … pratikalpante","acc. (an approaching king) + nom. pl. (officers, police, charioteers, headmen) + pres.","as, for an arriving king, the officers, magistrates, and headmen make ready (with food, drink, lodging),"],["evaṃ ha evaṃvidaṃ sarvāṇi bhūtāni pratikalpante idaṃ brahma āyāti idam āgacchati iti","acc. (the knower) + nom. pl.","so for the knower all beings make ready: \"here comes Brahman, here he arrives!\""],["evam eva imam ātmānam anta-kāle sarve prāṇāḥ abhisamāyanti","loc. (at the end-time) + nom. pl.","so, at the time of death, all the breaths gather around this self"]],"lit":"\"He returns from dream to waking. — As a heavily-laden cart goes creaking along, so the bodily self, <strong>mounted by the conscious Self</strong>, goes groaning (at death) when he comes to gasp his last. When he grows feeble through age or disease, then — as a <strong>mango, fig, or pippal-fruit is loosed from its stalk</strong> — this person frees himself from the limbs and hastens back, by the same route, to (a new) <strong>life/birth</strong>. As, when a <strong>king is arriving</strong>, the officers, magistrates, and headmen make ready for him with food, drink, and lodgings, crying 'He comes! He arrives!' — so for the <strong>knower</strong> all beings make ready: '<strong>Here comes Brahman, here he arrives!</strong>' And as, when a <strong>king is about to depart</strong>, the officers gather round him, so at the <strong>moment of death all the breaths gather round this self</strong>, when he gasps his last.\"","para":"The discourse turns to <strong>dying</strong>. At death the <strong>bodily self, \"mounted\" by the conscious Self (prājña)</strong>, departs <strong>groaning like an overladen cart</strong> (the labour of dying). As <strong>a ripe fruit loosens from its stalk</strong>, the person <strong>frees himself from the limbs</strong> and travels — <em>by the same route he came</em> (the nāḍīs/heart) — toward a <strong>new birth</strong> (the unliberated continue the round). Two royal similes frame the transitions: the <strong>arriving king</strong> (the <em>knower</em> is welcomed by all beings as \"Brahman come!\") and the <strong>departing king</strong> (at death, <strong>all the breaths gather around the self</strong> as courtiers around a king setting out). This sets up 4.4, the full account of <strong>what happens at death — transmigration by desire and karma, or liberation.</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <strong>conscious Self \"mounts\" the subtle body</strong> at death, which departs (the gross body left behind); the ripe-fruit simile shows death's naturalness when the body is \"ripe\" (aged/diseased) — the self detaches as a fruit from its stalk. \"Hastens to (a new) birth by the same route\" describes <strong>transmigration</strong> of the <em>unliberated</em> (the route via the nāḍīs/heart, detailed in 4.4). The king-similes: the <em>knower</em> is honoured by all as Brahman arriving; the dying self is attended by the gathering breaths. The whole prepares 4.4's contrast of the bound and the free at death."],["Krishnananda","death is the self withdrawing the subtle body (the \"cart\" groaning under its load), loosening from the limbs like a ripe fruit; for the ignorant, this leads to rebirth (4.4); the breaths gathering are the prelude to departure. The knower, by contrast, is greeted by all beings as Brahman."],["Sureśvara","the <em>prājña</em>-mounted self and the gathering breaths describe the mechanics of transmigration that knowledge transcends."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a sober meditation on dying — the body groans, the self loosens like ripe fruit; the breaths assemble; prepare by knowing the deathless Self."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the cart, ripe-fruit, and twin king-similes, and the \"by the same route\" pointing to the rebirth-mechanism of 4.4."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This brāhmaṇa is the Upaniṣad's <strong>masterwork on consciousness and the states</strong>, building the case for the self-luminous, unattached, blissful Self:","1. <strong>The Self is the ultimate light (4.3.2–6).</strong> Sun, moon, fire, speech are borrowed, intermittent lights <em>for</em> a conscious being; when all fail, <strong>the Self alone is the light</strong> — the inner consciousness presupposed by all knowing.","2. <strong>The three states reveal the witness (4.3.7–22).</strong> In <strong>dream</strong>, with no external light or object, the Self <strong>creates an entire world by its own light</strong> — proving it is <strong>svayaṃ-jyotiḥ (self-luminous)</strong> and <strong>kartā (the maker)</strong>; it ranges the states like a <strong>great fish between two banks</strong>, <strong>untouched (asaṅga)</strong> by all. In <strong>deep sleep</strong>, \"<strong>embraced by the intelligent Self,</strong>\" it knows no within or without — <strong>desireless, fearless, beyond sorrow</strong>, beyond every distinction of kin, morality, and role.","3. <strong>Consciousness is imperishable (4.3.23–32).</strong> \"It does not see\" means only that <strong>there is no second to see</strong> — the seer's seeing <strong>never lapses</strong> (avināśitva). In unity the Self is the <strong>\"one Seer, without a second, like clear water\" (salila eko draṣṭā advaitaḥ)</strong> — the <em>brahma-loka</em>, the <strong>highest goal and bliss</strong>, of which all other joys are a <strong>fraction</strong>.","4. <strong>Bliss measured by desirelessness (4.3.33).</strong> The hundredfold scale of bliss culminates in the brahma-world — and the <strong>sinless, desireless śrotriya shares it now</strong>: bliss grows as desire dies.","5. <strong>Dying (4.3.34–38).</strong> The conscious Self, \"mounting\" the subtle body, departs like a <strong>ripe fruit from its stalk</strong>, attended by the gathering breaths — toward rebirth (for the bound) or, for the knower, welcomed by all as \"Brahman arriving.\" (The sequel, 4.4, completes the account.)","The cumulative teaching: the Self is <strong>self-luminous consciousness, the unattached witness of all states, blissful and fearless by nature, imperishable, non-dual</strong> — and to know it is to share its supreme bliss even now."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   THE LIGHTS &amp; THE THREE STATES (BU 4.3)\n   ======================================\n   &quot;What is a person&#x27;s LIGHT?&quot;  sun -&gt; moon -&gt; fire -&gt; speech -&gt; THE SELF (ātma-jyotis)\n        (outer lights fail; the Self is the light of lights)\n\n   THE SELF (vijñānamaya, the inner light of the heart) ranges the states:\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------\n   WAKING  -- this world (forms of death); joined to &quot;evils&quot; of the body\n   DREAM   -- the &quot;junction&quot;: Self DESTROYS &amp; BUILDS a world by its OWN LIGHT\n              =&gt; SVAYAṂ-JYOTIḤ (self-luminous) ; &quot;sa hi kartā&quot; (he is the maker)\n              the golden person, the LONE SWAN (eka-haṃsa) leaves the body&#x27;s nest\n   DEEP SLEEP -- like a tired falcon folding wings to its nest:\n              &quot;embraced by the intelligent Self,&quot; knows no outside/inside\n              =&gt; desireless · evil-free · FEARLESS · beyond sorrow\n              (father no father, sin no sin... beyond all distinctions)\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------\n   moving between states UNTOUCHED (asaṅga) — like a GREAT FISH between two banks\n   &quot;though seeing, it sees not&quot; -&gt; only NO SECOND to see; the Seer is IMPERISHABLE\n        =&gt; SALILA EKO DRAṢṬĀ ADVAITAḤ  (the one Seer, non-dual, like clear water)\n           = the brahma-world = highest goal / bliss\n           (all beings live on a FRACTION of this bliss)\n\n   BLISS-CALCULUS (×100 each):  best human -&gt; fathers -&gt; gandharvas -&gt; karma-devas\n        -&gt; ājāna-devas -&gt; Prajāpati-world -&gt; BRAHMA-WORLD  (the desireless śrotriya shares it NOW)\n\n   DYING: bodily self (mounted by the conscious Self) departs groaning like a laden cart;\n          loosens like a RIPE FRUIT from its stalk; the breaths gather as round a king</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.3","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Find the light of lights.</strong> At night, with the lamps off, notice that <em>you still are</em> — aware in the dark. Trace every external light (sun, lamp, screen) back to the inner awareness by which it is known. That awareness is the Self-light.","2. <strong>Use dream as proof.</strong> Reflect each morning: last night, with no sun and no open eyes, you <em>saw</em> a whole world — lit by nothing but your own consciousness. This is direct, nightly evidence that you are self-luminous (<em>svayaṃ-jyotiḥ</em>).","3. <strong>Rest as the unattached witness.</strong> Watch yourself pass from waking to dream to sleep and back, \"untouched\" by each — like the great fish between two banks. Identify with the constant witness, not the changing states.","4. <strong>Enter deep-sleep peace, awake.</strong> The objectless bliss you taste nightly (\"I slept happily\") is the Self's own nature. Meditation aims to abide in that desireless, fearless, sorrowless peace <em>consciously</em> — \"embraced by the intelligent Self.\"","5. <strong>Reduce desire to grow in bliss</strong> (4.3.33). The bliss-scale is really a desire-scale inverted: the freer of desire, the fuller the joy. The sinless, desireless knower shares the highest bliss now — so loosen wants rather than chase pleasures.","6. <strong>Remember the ripe fruit.</strong> Contemplate dying as a ripe fruit loosening from its stalk — natural, not to be feared by one who knows the deathless Self that merely \"dismounts\" the body.","7. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> \"though seeing, it does not see\" — consciousness never goes out; only objects come and go. You are the imperishable Seer, the one non-dual awareness, clear and boundless as water — and the bliss you seek everywhere is a fraction of what you already are."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 4 · Brāhmaṇa 4.4 — Death, Transmigration, Karma, and Liberation","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 4.4","title":"Death, Transmigration, Karma, and Liberation","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The culmination of the Yājñavalkya teaching: what happens <strong>at and after death</strong>. The dying self gathers the senses and the light-essences into the heart and departs, accompanied by <strong>knowledge, works, and past experience</strong> (4.4.2). It passes to a new body as a <strong>leech</strong> crosses to a new blade, or a <strong>goldsmith</strong> reshapes gold into a fairer form (4.4.3–4) — for <strong>\"as one acts, so one becomes\" (karma)</strong>, and \"a person is made of desire\" (4.4.5). The <strong>desireless knower</strong>, however, does not transmigrate: <strong>\"being Brahman, he merges in Brahman\" (4.4.6)</strong>; his desires gone, <strong>\"the mortal becomes immortal, here\" (4.4.7)</strong>. The brāhmaṇa then gathers a garland of verses on the <strong>path, the two darknesses, \"there is no plurality here,\" \"let the wise one know the Self,\"</strong> and the <strong>great unborn, fearless Self (4.4.8–25)</strong>."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 4.4.1","title":"The senses withdraw into the heart","sa":["स यत्रायमात्माबल्यं न्येत्य संमोहमिव न्येति । अथैनमेते प्राणा अभिसमायन्ति । स एतास्तेजोमात्राः समभ्याददानो हृदयमेवान्ववक्रामति । स यत्रैष चाक्षुषः पुरुषः पराङ्पर्यावर्तते । अथारूपज्ञो भवति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["sa yatrāyam ātmābalyaṃ nyetya saṃmoham iva nyeti | athainam ete prāṇā abhisamāyanti | sa etās tejomātrāḥ samabhyādadāno hṛdayam evānvavakrāmati | sa yatraiṣa cākṣuṣaḥ puruṣaḥ parāṅ paryāvartate | athārūpajño bhavati || 1 ||"],"words":[["saḥ yatra ayam ātmā ābalyaṃ nyetya saṃmoham iva nyeti","acc. (weakness) + ger. + acc. (a swoon) + pres.","when this self, having come to weakness, falls into a kind of swoon,"],["atha enam ete prāṇāḥ abhisamāyanti","acc. + nom. pl. + pres.","then these vital breaths gather round him"],["saḥ etāḥ tejomātrāḥ samabhyādadānaḥ hṛdayam eva anvavakrāmati","f. acc. pl. (the light-particles) + pres.part. + acc. + pres.","gathering up these light-essences, he descends into the heart"],["saḥ yatra eṣaḥ cākṣuṣaḥ puruṣaḥ parāṅ paryāvartate","nom. (the eye-person) + adv. (turning away) + pres.","when the person in the eye turns away (outward function ceases),"],["atha arūpa-jñaḥ bhavati","adj. (no longer knowing forms)","then he ceases to know forms"]],"lit":"\"When this self, grown weak, falls as it were into a swoon, then the vital breaths gather round him. Gathering up these light-essences (<em>tejomātrāḥ</em>), he descends into the heart. When the person in the eye turns away (from its object), then he ceases to perceive forms.\"","para":"The <strong>physiology of dying</strong> begins: as the body weakens and the dying person sinks toward unconsciousness, the <strong>prāṇas (vital functions) converge</strong>; the self <strong>withdraws the \"light-essences\"</strong> (the subtle powers of the senses) <strong>into the heart</strong>; the <strong>eye's outward function ceases</strong> (\"the person in the eye turns away\"), and <strong>perception of forms stops.</strong> The senses are being reabsorbed for departure.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>tejomātrāḥ</em> (luminous particles) are the subtle elements with the sense-powers; at death the self draws them into the <strong>heart</strong>, the staging-point of departure; sensory cognition fails as the organs are withdrawn. The clinical detail serves the doctrine of the <em>subtle body</em> that transmigrates."],["Krishnananda","dying is the reverse of waking — the senses are gathered back into the heart, the lights of perception withdrawn; a process every night's sleep rehearses."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","note the heart as the gathering-point; the self prepares to depart with its subtle equipment."],["Sureśvara","the withdrawal of the sense-powers into the heart sets up the transmigration of the karma-bearing subtle self."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the realistic account of dying — sensory failure, the gathering of the breaths, the heart as locus."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.4.2","title":"Departure with knowledge, works, and past experience","sa":["एकीभवति न पश्यतीत्याहुः । एकीभवति न जिघ्रतीत्याहुः । … एकीभवति न विजानातीत्याहुः । तस्य हैतस्य हृदयस्याग्रं प्रद्योतते । तेन प्रद्योतेनैष आत्मा निष्क्रामति चक्षुष्टो वा मूर्ध्नो वान्येभ्यो वा शरीरदेशेभ्यः । तमुत्क्रामन्तं प्राणोऽनूत्क्रामति । प्राणमनूत्क्रामन्तं सर्वे प्राणा अनूत्क्रामन्ति । सविज्ञानो भवति । संजानमेवान्ववक्रामति । तं विद्याकर्मणी समन्वारभेते पूर्वप्रज्ञा च ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["ekībhavati na paśyatīty āhuḥ | ekībhavati na jighratīty āhuḥ | … ekībhavati na vijānātīty āhuḥ | tasya haitasya hṛdayasyāgraṃ pradyotate | tena pradyotenaiṣa ātmā niṣkrāmati cakṣuṣṭo vā mūrdhno vānyebhyo vā śarīradeśebhyaḥ | tam utkrāmantaṃ prāṇo 'nūtkrāmati | prāṇam anūtkrāmantaṃ sarve prāṇā anūtkrāmanti | savijñano bhavati | saṃjānam evānvavakrāmati | taṃ vidyākarmaṇī samanvārabhete pūrvaprajñā ca || 2 ||"],"words":[["ekī-bhavati na paśyati iti āhuḥ","pres. (becomes one) + pres.","\"(the eye) becomes one (with the self); he does not see,\" they say"],["(so) na jighrati / rasayati / vadati / śṛṇoti / manute / spṛśati / vijānāti","pres.","(so: does not smell / taste / speak / hear / think / touch / know)"],["tasya ha etasya hṛdayasya agraṃ pradyotate","gen. + n. nom. (the tip) + pres.","the tip of his heart lights up"],["tena pradyotena eṣaḥ ātmā niṣkrāmati","instr. + pres.","by that light the self goes out —"],["cakṣuṣṭaḥ vā mūrdhnaḥ vā anyebhyaḥ vā śarīra-deśebhyaḥ","abl.","through the eye, the head, or other parts of the body"],["tam utkrāmantaṃ prāṇaḥ anūtkrāmati","acc. + pres.","as he departs, the breath follows;"],["prāṇam anūtkrāmantaṃ sarve prāṇāḥ anūtkrāmanti","acc. + nom. pl.","as the breath follows, all the breaths follow"],["sa-vijñānaḥ bhavati saṃjānam eva anvavakrāmati","adj. (with consciousness) + acc.","he becomes endowed with (particular) consciousness; consciousness goes with him"],["taṃ vidyā-karmaṇī samanvārabhete pūrva-prajñā ca","n. nom. du. (knowledge & works) + f. nom. (past experience)","knowledge, works, and former experience take hold of him"]],"lit":"\"'(The eye) becomes one (with the self); he does not see,' they say; (so) 'he does not smell, taste, speak, hear, think, touch, know.' Then the tip of his heart lights up, and by that light the self departs — through the eye, or the head, or some other part of the body. As he departs, the breath follows; as the breath follows, all the breaths follow. He becomes endowed with (a particular) consciousness, and that consciousness goes with him. <strong>Knowledge, works, and former experience (vidyā, karman, pūrva-prajñā) take hold of him.</strong>\"","para":"The senses \"<strong>become one</strong>\" (merge into the self) and cease functioning; the <strong>tip of the heart glows</strong>, and by that light the self <strong>exits the body</strong> — by the eye, the crown, or another point. The <strong>breaths follow</strong>, and the self departs <strong>with consciousness (savijñāna)</strong> — i.e., not into blank extinction but carrying its mental content. Crucially, <strong>three things accompany the departing self: knowledge (vidyā), works (karma), and the impressions of past experience (pūrva-prajñā)</strong> — the karmic baggage that will shape the next birth. (This is the mechanism of transmigration: the subtle self carries forward its accumulated knowledge, deeds, and tendencies.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the self departs <strong>with the subtle body</strong>, carrying <strong>vidyā</strong> (knowledge/learning), <strong>karma</strong> (the stored results of action), and <strong>pūrva-prajñā</strong> (the latent impressions/saṃskāras of past experience) — the three determinants of the next embodiment. The \"lighting of the heart's tip\" and exit-points (eye/head/other) describe the actual departure. This is Śaṅkara's locus for the <em>liṅga-śarīra</em> (subtle body) that transmigrates."],["Krishnananda","what survives death is not the body but the <strong>subtle self bearing its knowledge, deeds, and tendencies</strong> — these \"take hold\" of it and carry it onward. Death is a withdrawal and a hand-off to the next life, governed by what one has known, done, and become."],["Sureśvara","<em>vidyā-karmaṇī</em> + <em>pūrva-prajñā</em> together determine the next state — the precise karmic continuity."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the sobering truth — your knowledge, actions, and ingrained tendencies follow you past death; cultivate them well now."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the threefold \"knowledge, action, past experience\" as the carriers of personal continuity across death — a key statement of the rebirth-mechanism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.4.3–4.4.4","title":"The leech and the goldsmith: passing to a new body","sa":["4.4.3 (the leech): तद्यथा तृणजलायुका तृणस्यान्तं गत्वान्यमाक्रममाक्रम्यात्मानमुपसंहरति । एवमेवायमात्मेदं शरीरं निहत्याविद्यां गमयित्वान्यमाक्रममाक्रम्यात्मानमुपसंहरति ॥ ३ ॥","4.4.4 (the goldsmith): तद्यथा पेशस्कारी पेशसो मात्रामपादायान्यन्नवतरं कल्याणतरं रूपं तनुते । एवमेवायमात्मेदं शरीरं निहत्याविद्यां गमयित्वान्यन्नवतरं कल्याणतरं रूपं कुरुते पित्र्यं वा गान्धर्वं वा दैवं वा प्राजापत्यं वा ब्राह्मं वान्येषां वा भूतानाम् ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["tad yathā tṛṇajalāyukā tṛṇasyāntaṃ gatvānyam ākramam ākramyātmānam upasaṃharati | evam evāyam ātmedaṃ śarīraṃ nihatyāvidyāṃ gamayitvānyam ākramam ākramyātmānam upasaṃharati || 3 ||","tad yathā peśaskārī peśaso mātrām apādāyānyan navataraṃ kalyāṇataraṃ rūpaṃ tanute | evam evāyam ātmedaṃ śarīraṃ nihatyāvidyāṃ gamayitvānyan navataraṃ kalyāṇataraṃ rūpaṃ kurute pitryaṃ vā gāndharvaṃ vā daivaṃ vā prājāpatyaṃ vā brāhmaṃ vānyeṣāṃ vā bhūtānām || 4 ||"],"words":[["tat yathā tṛṇa-jalāyukā tṛṇasya antaṃ gatvā anyam ākramam ākramya ātmānam upasaṃharati","nom. (a leech/caterpillar on grass) + acc. + ger. + acc. (foothold) + ger. + pres.","as a leech, having reached the tip of a blade of grass, takes hold of another (blade) and draws itself over to it,"],["evam eva ayam ātmā idaṃ śarīraṃ nihatya avidyāṃ gamayitvā anyam ākramam ākramya ātmānam upasaṃharati","acc. + ger. (striking down) + acc. + ger. (causing to vanish)","so this self, having struck down this body and dispelled ignorance (of it), takes hold of another support and draws itself over"],["tat yathā peśaskārī peśasaḥ mātrām apādāya anyat navataraṃ kalyāṇataraṃ rūpaṃ tanute","nom. (goldsmith) + gen. + f. acc. (material) + ger. + n. acc. (newer, fairer form) + pres.","as a goldsmith, taking the material of (an old) ornament, fashions another, newer and more beautiful form,"],["evam eva ayam ātmā … anyat navataraṃ kalyāṇataraṃ rūpaṃ kurute","acc. + pres.","so this self … makes another, newer and fairer form —"],["pitryaṃ vā gāndharvaṃ vā daivaṃ vā prājāpatyaṃ vā brāhmaṃ vā anyeṣāṃ vā bhūtānām","acc.","of the fathers, gandharvas, gods, Prajāpati, Brahmā, or of other beings"]],"lit":"\"As a <strong>leech (caterpillar)</strong>, having reached the tip of a blade of grass, draws itself together onto another foothold, so this self, having struck down this body and dispelled (its identification with it), draws itself together onto another support. As a <strong>goldsmith</strong>, taking the material of an old ornament, fashions a newer, more beautiful form, so this self, having struck down this body, makes a newer, fairer form — that of the fathers, or the gandharvas, or the gods, or Prajāpati, or Brahmā, or of other beings.\"","para":"Two classic similes for <strong>transmigration</strong>. (1) The <strong>leech/caterpillar</strong>: as it reaches the end of one blade and <strong>stretches to grip the next before letting go</strong>, the self, at the end of one body, <strong>lays hold of the next</strong> before relinquishing the old — continuity without a gap. (2) The <strong>goldsmith</strong>: as he melts down old gold and <strong>shapes a finer ornament from the same material</strong>, the self <strong>fashions a new and \"more beautiful\" body</strong> from its karmic material — the form depending on its deeds and knowledge (it may become an ancestor, gandharva, god, etc.). The self is thus the <strong>continuous artificer of its successive embodiments.</strong> (Note the recurring \"having dispelled avidyā\" — Śaṅkara: the self ends its connection with the old body.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <strong>leech</strong> illustrates the <em>continuity</em> of transmigration (the self grips the new body before quitting the old — no interval); the <strong>goldsmith</strong>, that the new body is <strong>shaped from one's own karma</strong> (\"newer, fairer\" reflecting better deeds, but determined by them). The range of possible forms (fathers → Brahmā → other beings) shows rebirth across all orders. \"Striking down the body, dispelling avidyā (of it)\" = withdrawing from the old embodiment. These two similes are the Upaniṣad's classic images of rebirth."],["Krishnananda","\"Like a caterpillar, you do not leave one leaf till you grip the next\" — there is no gap in transmigration; and \"like a goldsmith,\" the self refashions itself into a new form according to its works. You are the sculptor of your next life, with the chisel of karma."],["Sureśvara","the similes establish <em>continuity</em> (leech) and <em>karmic determination of form</em> (goldsmith) — the two essentials of rebirth."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","a vivid teaching that you build your next body now, by your deeds; the form is \"newer and fairer\" or otherwise according to karma."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the famous caterpillar and goldsmith similes as the locus classicus for the doctrine of rebirth and the self as its own re-fashioner."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.4.5","title":"\"As one acts, so one becomes\"; the self made of desire","sa":["स वा अयमात्मा ब्रह्म विज्ञानमयो मनोमयः प्राणमयश्चक्षुर्मयः श्रोत्रमयः पृथिवीमय आपोमयो वायुमय आकाशमयस्तेजोमयोऽतेजोमयः काममयोऽकाममयः क्रोधमयोऽक्रोधमयो धर्ममयोऽधर्ममयः सर्वमयः । तद्यदेतदिदंमयोऽदोमय इति । यथाकारी यथाचारी तथा भवति । साधुकारी साधुर्भवति पापकारी पापो भवति । पुण्यः पुण्येन कर्मणा पापः पापेन । अथो खल्वाहुः काममय एवायं पुरुष इति । स यथाकामो भवति तत्क्रतुर्भवति । यत्क्रतुर्भवति तत्कर्म कुरुते । यत्कर्म कुरुते तदभिसम्पद्यते ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["sa vā ayam ātmā brahma vijñānamayo manomayaḥ prāṇamayaś cakṣurmayaḥ śrotramayaḥ pṛthivīmaya āpomayo vāyumaya ākāśamayas tejomayo 'tejomayaḥ kāmamayo 'kāmamayaḥ krodhamayo 'krodhamayo dharmamayo 'dharmamayaḥ sarvamayaḥ | tad yad etad idaṃmayo 'domaya iti | yathākārī yathācārī tathā bhavati | sādhukārī sādhur bhavati pāpakārī pāpo bhavati | puṇyaḥ puṇyena karmaṇā pāpaḥ pāpena | atho khalv āhuḥ kāmamaya evāyaṃ puruṣa iti | sa yathākāmo bhavati tatkratur bhavati | yatkratur bhavati tat karma kurute | yat karma kurute tad abhisaṃpadyate || 5 ||"],"words":[["saḥ vai ayam ātmā brahma","m. nom. + n. nom.","this self, verily, is Brahman"],["vijñāna-mayaḥ manaḥ-mayaḥ prāṇa-mayaḥ … sarva-mayaḥ","adj. (made of / identified with)","(appearing as) made of intellect, mind, breath, eye, ear, earth, water, air, space, light and non-light, desire and non-desire, anger and non-anger, dharma and non-dharma — made of all"],["tat yat etat idaṃmayaḥ adomayaḥ iti","(made of this, made of that)","that is to say, made of this and made of that"],["yathā-kārī yathā-cārī tathā bhavati","adj. (acting how) + pres.","as he acts and conducts himself, so he becomes"],["sādhu-kārī sādhuḥ bhavati pāpa-kārī pāpaḥ bhavati","nom.","the doer of good becomes good, the doer of evil becomes evil"],["puṇyaḥ puṇyena karmaṇā pāpaḥ pāpena","instr.","(one becomes) meritorious by good action, evil by evil action"],["atho khalu āhuḥ kāma-mayaḥ eva ayaṃ puruṣaḥ iti","nom.","now, some say: this person is made of desire (kāma) alone"],["saḥ yathā-kāmaḥ bhavati tat-kratuḥ bhavati","adj. (whatever his desire) + (such his will/resolve)","as is his desire, so is his will (kratu);"],["yat-kratuḥ bhavati tat karma kurute","rel. + acc. + pres.","as is his will, so the action he does;"],["yat karma kurute tat abhisaṃpadyate","rel. + pres.","as is the action he does, so (the result) he attains"]],"lit":"\"This self, verily, is <strong>Brahman</strong> — (yet appearing as) made of intellect, mind, breath, eye, ear, earth, water, air, space, light and non-light, desire and non-desire, anger and non-anger, dharma and non-dharma — made of all; made of this and made of that. <strong>As one acts and conducts oneself, so one becomes</strong>: the doer of good becomes good, the doer of evil becomes evil; one becomes meritorious by good action, evil by evil action. Now, some say: this person is made of <strong>desire (kāma)</strong> alone. <strong>As is his desire, so is his will (kratu); as is his will, so the action he does; as is the action he does, so (the result) he attains.</strong>\"","para":"Two profound doctrines stated together. (1) <strong>Identity and appearance:</strong> the self <strong>is Brahman</strong>, yet in bondage it <em>appears</em> \"made of\" everything it identifies with — intellect, mind, the elements, even desire, anger, and their opposites: it takes the colour of whatever it associates with (<em>sarvamaya</em>, \"made of all\"). (2) <strong>The law of becoming (karma):</strong> <strong>\"as one acts, so one becomes\"</strong> — good action makes a good self, evil a bad (restating 3.2.13). And the <strong>psychology of the whole chain</strong>: <strong>a person is essentially <em>made of desire</em> (kāmamaya)</strong> — and <strong>desire → will (kratu) → action (karma) → destiny (what one becomes)</strong>. This is the <strong>engine of transmigration</strong>: desire shapes will, will shapes deeds, deeds shape the next birth. (It also implies the cure: change desire at the root, and the whole chain changes — 4.4.6.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the self <strong>is Brahman</strong> but, through <em>avidyā</em>, <strong>identifies</strong> with the body-mind and their qualities (<em>-maya</em>, \"consisting of\") — hence appears \"made of all.\" The karma-law (<em>yathākārī… tathā bhavati</em>) governs the <em>bound</em> self. The desire→will→action→result chain is the <strong>definitive Upaniṣadic statement of the causation of rebirth</strong>: <strong>kāma (desire) is the root</strong> — for desire drives resolve, resolve drives action, action determines the next becoming. (Hence liberation is by <em>ending desire</em>, 4.4.6.)"],["Krishnananda","\"You become what you deeply desire\" — desire is the seed of the entire karmic process; the self is <em>kāmamaya</em>, made of its wants, and is reborn according to them. The famous causal chain (desire→will→deed→destiny) is the anatomy of bondage and the key to freedom: cut desire at the root."],["Sureśvara","the self's being \"made of all\" is <em>adhyāsa</em> (superimposition); its true nature is Brahman. The karma-and-desire analysis explains saṃsāra precisely."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the most practical of laws — watch your desires, for they become your deeds and your destiny; \"as you desire, so you become.\""],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the celebrated chain <em>kāma → kratu → karma → result</em> and \"as one acts so one becomes\" as the classic formulation of karma and rebirth, with desire as the root."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.4.6","title":"The desiring transmigrate; the desireless become Brahman","sa":["तदेष श्लोको भवति — तदेव सक्तः सह कर्मणैति लिङ्गं मनो यत्र निषक्तमस्य । प्राप्यान्तं कर्मणस्तस्य यत्किञ्चेह करोत्ययम् । तस्माल्लोकात्पुनरैत्यस्मै लोकाय कर्मणे । इति नु कामयमानः । अथाकामयमानो योऽकामो निष्काम आप्तकाम आत्मकामो न तस्य प्राणा उत्क्रामन्ति । ब्रह्मैव सन्ब्रह्माप्येति ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["tad eṣa śloko bhavati -- tad eva saktaḥ saha karmaṇaiti liṅgaṃ mano yatra niṣaktam asya | prāpyāntaṃ karmaṇas tasya yat kiñceha karoty ayam | tasmāl lokāt punar aity asmai lokāya karmaṇe | iti nu kāmayamānaḥ | athākāmayamāno yo 'kāmo niṣkāma āptakāma ātmakāmo na tasya prāṇā utkrāmanti | brahmaiva san brahmāpyeti || 6 ||"],"words":[["tat eva saktaḥ saha karmaṇā eti","nom. (attached) + instr. + pres.","attached (to that), he goes, along with his work,"],["liṅgaṃ manaḥ yatra niṣaktam asya","n. acc. (the subtle mark/body) + rel. + pp.","to that to which his subtle self and mind cling"],["prāpya antaṃ karmaṇaḥ tasya yat kiṃca iha karoti ayam","ger. + acc. + gen. + rel.","having reached the end of whatever work he does here,"],["tasmāt lokāt punaḥ aiti asmai lokāya karmaṇe","abl. + adv. + pres. + dat.","he comes back from that world to this world, for (more) work"],["iti nu kāmayamānaḥ","pres.part.","— so (it is for) the one who desires"],["atha akāmayamānaḥ yaḥ akāmaḥ niṣkāmaḥ āptakāmaḥ ātmakāmaḥ","pres.part. + adjs.","but the one who does not desire — who is without desire, free of desire, his desires fulfilled, the Self his (only) desire —"],["na tasya prāṇāḥ utkrāmanti","gen. + 3 pl. pres.","his breaths do not depart"],["brahma eva san brahma apyeti","nom. + pres.part. + pres.","being Brahman already, he merges in Brahman"]],"lit":"\"On this there is a verse: 'Attached (to the world), he goes, along with his work, to that very place to which his subtle self and mind cling. Having reached the end of whatever work he did here, he <strong>returns from that world to this world, for (more) work</strong>' — so it is for the one who desires. But the one who does <strong>not</strong> desire — who is <strong>without desire, free of desire, his desires fulfilled, the Self his only desire</strong> — his breaths do not depart; <strong>being Brahman already, he merges in Brahman.</strong>\"","para":"The decisive divergence of destinies. The <strong>desiring person</strong>, attached, follows his <strong>clinging mind/subtle self</strong> to the world it craves; when the karmic momentum of his deeds is spent, he <strong>falls back to this world for yet more action</strong> — the endless round of saṃsāra. But the <strong>desireless knower</strong> — <em>akāma</em> (without desire), <em>āptakāma</em> (desires already fulfilled), <em>ātmakāma</em> (the Self his only \"desire\") — <strong>does not transmigrate at all: \"his breaths do not depart\"</strong> (they merge in place); <strong>being Brahman already, he simply merges in Brahman.</strong> Liberation is not a journey to a far heaven but the <strong>cessation of the desire that drives rebirth</strong> — the knower, lacking nothing, has nowhere to go.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the contrast of the two paths. The desirer's <em>liṅga</em> (subtle body) and mind, charged with craving, carry him to rebirth; even heavenly rewards exhaust and he <strong>returns \"for more work.\"</strong> The <strong>desireless knower</strong> — whose only \"desire\" is the Self he already is — does <strong>not</strong> transmigrate: \"his prāṇas do not depart\" means the subtle self does not go elsewhere but dissolves; <strong>\"being Brahman, he attains Brahman\"</strong> = <em>videha-mukti</em> (and, while living, <em>jīvanmukti</em>). The supreme statement that <strong>desirelessness = liberation</strong>."],["Krishnananda","\"Where there is desire, there is rebirth; where desire ends, there is Brahman.\" The knower does not \"go\" anywhere because, being already Brahman and lacking nothing, there is nowhere to go and no momentum to carry him. <em>Brahmaiva san brahmāpyeti</em> — already Brahman, he becomes Brahman."],["Sureśvara","the desireless one's non-departure is <em>immediate liberation</em>; rebirth is solely desire-driven, so its removal removes rebirth."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical heart of liberation — extinguish desire (or let it be wholly the Self), and the wheel stops; you are already That."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the contrasting verses on the desirer (returns \"for more work\") and the desireless (<em>brahmaiva san brahmāpyeti</em>) as the Upaniṣad's clearest statement that liberation is freedom from desire."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.4.7","title":"When desires fall away: the snake-slough","sa":["तदेष श्लोको भवति — यदा सर्वे प्रमुच्यन्ते कामा येऽस्य हृदि श्रिताः । अथ मर्त्योऽमृतो भवत्यत्र ब्रह्म समश्नुत इति । तद्यथाहिनिर्ल्वयनी वल्मीके मृता प्रत्यस्ता शयीत । एवमेवेदं शरीरं शेते । अथायमशरीरोऽमृतः प्राणो ब्रह्मैव तेज एव । सोऽहं भगवते सहस्रं ददामीति होवाच जनको वैदेहः ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["tad eṣa śloko bhavati -- yadā sarve pramucyante kāmā ye 'sya hṛdi śritāḥ | atha martyo 'mṛto bhavaty atra brahma samaśnuta iti | tad yathāhinirlvayanī valmīke mṛtā pratyastā śayīta | evam evedaṃ śarīraṃ śete | athāyam aśarīro 'mṛtaḥ prāṇo brahmaiva teja eva | so 'haṃ bhagavate sahasraṃ dadāmīti hovāca janako vaidehaḥ || 7 ||"],"words":[["yadā sarve pramucyante kāmāḥ ye asya hṛdi śritāḥ","rel. + 3 pl. pres. pass. + nom. pl. + loc. + pp.","when all the desires that dwell in his heart fall away,"],["atha martyaḥ amṛtaḥ bhavati atra brahma samaśnute","nom. + pres. + adv. + pres.","then the mortal becomes immortal — here (in this very life) he attains Brahman"],["tat yathā ahi-nirlvayanī valmīke mṛtā pratyastā śayīta","f. nom. (a snake's slough) + loc. (anthill) + pp. + opt.","as a snake's slough lies dead, cast off on an anthill,"],["evam eva idaṃ śarīraṃ śete","pres.","so this body lies"],["atha ayam aśarīraḥ amṛtaḥ prāṇaḥ brahma eva tejaḥ eva","nom. (bodiless) + (immortal) + (life)","then this bodiless, immortal one (the self) is breath/life itself, is Brahman, is light"],["saḥ aham bhagavate sahasraṃ dadāmi iti hovāca janakaḥ","","\"I give you a thousand (cows), sir,\" said Janaka"]],"lit":"\"On this there is a verse: 'When all the desires that dwell in his heart fall away, then the mortal becomes immortal — <strong>here (in this very life) he attains Brahman.</strong>' As a <strong>snake's slough lies dead, cast off on an anthill</strong>, so this body lies; then this <strong>bodiless, immortal one</strong> (the self) is breath/life itself, is Brahman, is light. (Janaka:) 'I give you a thousand cows, sir.'\"","para":"The supreme liberation-verse. <strong>When every desire lodged in the heart is cast off, the mortal becomes immortal — and \"attains Brahman <em>here</em>\" (atra), in this very life</strong> (<em>jīvanmukti</em>, not a posthumous reward). The body of such a knower is then like a <strong>snake's discarded slough</strong>, lying lifeless on the anthill: merely cast off, no longer <em>him</em>. The liberated self is <strong>bodiless (aśarīra), immortal, pure life, Brahman itself, sheer light.</strong> Liberation is the falling-away of desire and the recognition of oneself as the bodiless, deathless Brahman — already, now. Janaka, overwhelmed, rewards the teaching.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <strong>falling-away of all heart-desires</strong> <em>is</em> liberation, attained <strong>\"here\" (atra) — in this very life</strong> (the key word for <em>jīvanmukti</em>): the mortal becomes immortal not by going elsewhere but by knowledge dissolving desire. The <strong>snake-slough</strong> simile is decisive: for the liberated, the body is a <strong>cast-off skin</strong> — present but no longer binding, no longer the self. The self is then <em>aśarīra</em> (bodiless even while the body lasts), <em>amṛta</em>, Brahman, light. One of the Upaniṣad's supreme statements of living liberation."],["Krishnananda","\"Immortality is not after death but here, when desire dies.\" The snake-slough is the perfect image of the <em>jīvanmukta's</em> body — sloughed off, lying there, while he abides as bodiless Brahman-light. The mortal/immortal transition is a transformation of <em>knowledge</em>, not of place or time."],["Sureśvara","<em>atra brahma samaśnute</em> — Brahman is attained <em>here</em>, refuting the view that liberation is a future or other-worldly event; it is the present cessation of desire-bondage."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the goal in one verse — drop all desires of the heart, and <em>now</em> you are the immortal Brahman; the body becomes a mere slough. This is <em>jīvanmukti</em>."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the famous snake-slough simile and <em>atra brahma samaśnute</em> (\"here he attains Brahman\") as a clear assertion of liberation in this life."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.4.8–4.4.21","title":"A garland of verses on the Self","sa":["4.4.8 (the ancient path): अणुः पन्था विततः पुराणो मां स्पृष्टोऽनुवित्तो मयैव । तेन धीरा अपियन्ति ब्रह्मविदः स्वर्गं लोकमित ऊर्ध्वं विमुक्ताः ॥ ८ ॥","4.4.9 (its colours): तस्मिञ्छुक्लमुत नीलमाहुः पिङ्गलं हरितं लोहितं च । एष पन्था ब्रह्मणा हानुवित्तस्तेनैति ब्रह्मवित्पुण्यकृत्तैजसश्च ॥ ९ ॥","4.4.10 (the two darknesses — cf. Īśā 9, 12): अन्धं तमः प्रविशन्ति येऽविद्यामुपासते । ततो भूय इव ते तमो य उ विद्यायां रताः ॥ १० ॥","4.4.11 (joyless worlds): अनन्दा नाम ते लोका अन्धेन तमसावृताः । तांस्ते प्रेत्याभिगच्छन्त्यविद्वांसोऽबुधा जनाः ॥ ११ ॥","4.4.12 (why grieve?): आत्मानं चेद्विजानीयादयमस्मीति पुरुषः । किमिच्छन्कस्य कामाय शरीरमनु संज्वरेत् ॥ १२ ॥","4.4.13 (the maker of all): यस्यानुवित्तः प्रतिबुद्ध आत्मास्मिन्संदेह्ये गहने प्रविष्टः । स विश्वकृत्स हि सर्वस्य कर्ता तस्य लोकः स उ लोक एव ॥ १३ ॥","4.4.14 (know it here, or great is the loss): इहैव सन्तोऽथ विद्मस्तद्वयं न चेदवेदिर्महती विनष्टिः । ये तद्विदुरमृतास्ते भवन्त्यथेतरे दुःखमेवापियन्ति ॥ १४ ॥","4.4.15 (no fear/aversion): यदैतमनुपश्यत्यात्मानं देवमञ्जसा । ईशानं भूतभव्यस्य न ततो विजुगुप्सते ॥ १५ ॥","4.4.16 (the light of lights): यस्मादर्वाक्संवत्सरोऽहोभिः परिवर्तते । तद्देवा ज्योतिषां ज्योतिरायुर्होपासतेऽमृतम् ॥ १६ ॥","4.4.17 (in whom the five and space rest): यस्मिन्पञ्च पञ्चजना आकाशश्च प्रतिष्ठितः । तमेव मन्य आत्मानं विद्वान्ब्रह्मामृतोऽमृतम् ॥ १७ ॥","4.4.18 (the breath of the breath — cf. Kena 1.2): प्राणस्य प्राणमुत चक्षुषश्चक्षुरुत श्रोत्रस्य श्रोत्रं मनसो ये मनो विदुः । ते निचिक्युर्ब्रह्म पुराणमग्र्यम् ॥ १८ ॥","4.4.19 (no plurality — cf. Kaṭha 4.10–11): मनसैवानुद्रष्टव्यं नेह नानास्ति किंचन । मृत्योः स मृत्युमाप्नोति य इह नानेव पश्यति ॥ १९ ॥","4.4.20 (the One, beyond space): एकधैवानुद्रष्टव्यमेतदप्रमयं ध्रुवम् । विरजः पर आकाशादज आत्मा महान्ध्रुवः ॥ २० ॥","4.4.21 (let the wise one know it): तमेव धीरो विज्ञाय प्रज्ञां कुर्वीत ब्राह्मणः । नानुध्यायाद्बहूञ्छब्दान्वाचो विग्लापनं हि तदिति ॥ २१ ॥"],"iast":["aṇuḥ panthā vitataḥ purāṇo māṃ spṛṣṭo 'nuvitto mayaiva | tena dhīrā apiyanti brahmavidaḥ svargaṃ lokam ita ūrdhvaṃ vimuktāḥ || 8 ||","tasmiñ śuklam uta nīlam āhuḥ piṅgalaṃ haritaṃ lohitaṃ ca | eṣa panthā brahmaṇā hānuvittas tenaiti brahmavit puṇyakṛt taijasaś ca || 9 ||","andhaṃ tamaḥ praviśanti ye 'vidyām upāsate | tato bhūya iva te tamo ya u vidyāyāṃ ratāḥ || 10 ||","anandā nāma te lokā andhena tamasāvṛtāḥ | tāṃs te pretyābhigacchanty avidvāṃso 'budhā janāḥ || 11 ||","ātmānaṃ ced vijānīyād ayam asmīti puruṣaḥ | kim icchan kasya kāmāya śarīram anu saṃjvaret || 12 ||","yasyānuvittaḥ pratibuddha ātmāsmin saṃdehye gahane praviṣṭaḥ | sa viśvakṛt sa hi sarvasya kartā tasya lokaḥ sa u loka eva || 13 ||","ihaiva santo 'tha vidmas tad vayaṃ na ced avedir mahatī vinaṣṭiḥ | ye tad vidur amṛtās te bhavanty athetare duḥkham evāpiyanti || 14 ||","yadaitam anupaśyaty atmānaṃ devam aṅjasā | īśānaṃ bhūtabhavyasya na tato vijugupsate || 15 ||","yasmād arvāk saṃvatsaro 'hobhiḥ parivartate | tad devā jyotiṣāṃ jyotir āyur hopāsate 'mṛtam || 16 ||","yasmin pañca pañcajanā ākāśaś ca pratiṣṭhitaḥ | tam eva manya ātmānaṃ vidvān brahmāmṛto 'mṛtam || 17 ||","prāṇasya prāṇam uta cakṣuṣaś cakṣur uta śrotrasya śrotram manaso ye mano viduḥ | te nicikyur brahma purāṇam agryam || 18 ||","manasaivānudraṣṭavyaṃ neha nānāsti kiṃ cana | mṛtyoḥ sa mṛtyum āpnoti ya iha nāneva paśyati || 19 ||","ekadhaivānudraṣṭavyam etad apramayaṃ dhruvam | virajaḥ para ākāśād aja ātmā mahān dhruvaḥ || 20 ||","tam eva dhīro vijñāya prajñāṃ kurvīta brāhmaṇaḥ | nānudhyāyād bahūñ chabdān vāco viglāpanaṃ hi tad iti || 21 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>\"The ancient path, subtle and far-stretching, has touched me, has been found by me. By it the wise, the knowers of Brahman, go up, liberated, to the heavenly world (and) beyond.\"</strong>","para":"The verses sweep through the heights of the teaching. The knowers travel the <strong>ancient subtle path</strong> (through the heart's channels) <strong>up and beyond, liberated</strong> (8–9). Two famous warnings (echoing Īśā): <strong>mere ignorance</strong> (unreflective ritualism/worldliness) leads to blind darkness — and <strong>mere \"knowledge\" (vidyā)</strong> without realisation, to a <em>greater</em> darkness (10); the <strong>joyless worlds</strong> await the unawakened (11). But the knower has nothing to grieve: knowing <strong>\"I am the Self,\"</strong> <em>why would he burn with anxiety over the body?</em> (12); he is the <strong>maker of all, the world itself</strong> (13). The urgency: <strong>know it <em>here</em> — or great is the loss; knowers become immortal, the rest reap sorrow</strong> (14). On realisation, <strong>fear and aversion cease</strong> (15); the Self is the <strong>light of lights, immortal life</strong>, in which the year revolves (16) and <strong>the five orders of beings and space rest</strong> (17). To know <strong>the breath of the breath, the eye of the eye…</strong> is to realise the primal Brahman (18). And the central injunction: <strong>\"there is no plurality here; he who sees difference goes from death to death\" (neha nānāsti kiṃcana)</strong> (19); the Self is to be seen <strong>as One — taintless, beyond space, unborn, great, constant</strong> (20). Finally: <strong>the wise should <em>realise</em> the Self and not get lost in many words — mere verbiage is exhausting</strong> (21).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the verses crystallise the doctrine. <strong>4.4.10–11</strong> (= Īśā 9, 12): <em>avidyā</em> = ritual/works pursued for results lands one in darkness; mere <em>vidyā</em> (here, <em>saguṇa</em> meditation or empty learning) without the non-dual realisation, in worse darkness — only <strong>knowledge of the one Self</strong> liberates. <strong>4.4.19</strong> (<em>neha nānāsti kiṃcana</em>) is the <strong>cardinal anti-pluralism text</strong> of Advaita: there is <strong>no real multiplicity</strong>; to see difference is <em>avidyā</em>, and binds one to \"death after death\" (rebirth). <strong>4.4.20</strong>: the Self is to be known <strong>as one (ekadhā)</strong> — immeasurable, taintless (<em>viraja</em>), beyond space, <strong>unborn (aja)</strong>, great, constant. <strong>4.4.21</strong> (<em>tam eva dhīro vijñāya…</em>) warns against substituting <strong>scholarship for realisation</strong> — \"many words\" only tire; <em>prajñā</em> (direct insight) is the aim. <strong>4.4.18</strong> (= Kena 1.2): Brahman is the inner power behind every faculty."],["Krishnananda","\"He who sees the many goes from death to death\" — the perception of plurality <em>is</em> bondage; the perception of the One <em>is</em> freedom. The two-darkness verses warn that both heedless action and dry learning miss the mark. And 4.4.21 is the great caution to seekers: stop debating, <strong>realise</strong> — words are weariness."],["Sureśvara","<em>neha nānāsti</em> and <em>ekadhā anudraṣṭavyam</em> are decisive for non-duality; <em>vāco viglāpanam</em> (\"weariness of speech\") rebukes mere disputation in favour of <em>anubhava</em> (realisation)."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","practical distillation — know \"I am the Self\" and grieve no more (12); see no difference (19); seek realisation, not verbiage (21)."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the heavy intertextuality (Īśā, Kena, Kaṭha) and singles out <em>neha nānāsti kiṃcana</em> as among the most influential anti-pluralist statements in Indian thought."]],"notes":["<em>A sequence of</em> <strong>ślokas</strong> <em>(several echoed in the Īśa, Kena, and Kaṭha Upaniṣads) celebrating the Self, the path of the knowers, the futility of mere ritual or mere learning, and the discipline of realising the One.</em>","<strong>\"On it, they say, are white and blue, brown, green, and red (the colours of the heart's channels). This path was found by Brahman; by it goes the knower of Brahman, the doer of good, the luminous one.\"</strong>","<strong>\"Into blind darkness enter those who worship ignorance (avidyā); into darkness greater than that, those who delight (merely) in (ritual) knowledge (vidyā).\"</strong>","<strong>\"Joyless (anandāḥ) are those worlds, wrapped in blind darkness; to them, after death, go the ignorant, the unawakened.\"</strong>","<strong>\"If a person truly knew the Self as 'I am this' — then, seeking what, for love of what, would he burn (with anxiety) over the body?\"</strong>","<strong>\"He who has found and awakened to the Self, entered into this perilous, impenetrable (body/region) — he is the maker of the universe, for he is the maker of all; the world is his — indeed, he is the world itself.\"</strong>","<strong>\"While here (in this life) we may know it; if not, great is the loss. Those who know it become immortal; the rest go only to sorrow.\"</strong>","<strong>\"When one directly sees this Self, the shining one, the lord of what was and what will be — then one no longer shrinks (in fear/aversion) from it.\"</strong>","<strong>\"That below which the year, with its days, revolves — that the gods worship as the light of lights, as immortal life.\"</strong>","<strong>\"In whom the five fivefold-folk and space are established — that very one I deem the Self; knowing it, immortal, I am the immortal Brahman.\"</strong>","<strong>\"Those who know the breath of the breath, the eye of the eye, the ear of the ear, and the mind of the mind — they have realised the ancient, primordial Brahman.\"</strong>","<strong>\"By the (purified) mind alone is it to be perceived. Here there is no plurality whatever. From death to death he goes, who sees here any plurality.\"</strong>","<strong>\"As one alone is it to be perceived — this immeasurable, constant (reality). The Self is taintless, beyond space, unborn, great, constant.\"</strong>","<strong>\"Knowing that very (Self), let the wise brāhmaṇa attain insight (prajñā). Let him not ponder over many words, for that is mere weariness of speech.\"</strong>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.4.22","title":"The great unborn Self: ruler, dyke, and goal of the renunciate","sa":["स वा एष महानज आत्मा योऽयं विज्ञानमयः प्राणेषु । य एषोऽन्तर्हृदय आकाशस्तस्मिञ्छेते । सर्वस्य वशी सर्वस्येशानः सर्वस्याधिपतिः । स न साधुना कर्मणा भूयान्नो एवासाधुना कनीयान् । एष सर्वेश्वरः एष भूताधिपतिः एष भूतपालः । एष सेतुर्विधरण एषां लोकानामसंभेदाय । तमेतं वेदानुवचनेन ब्राह्मणा विविदिषन्ति यज्ञेन दानेन तपसानाशकेन । एतमेव विदित्वा मुनिर्भवति । एतमेव प्रव्राजिनो लोकमिच्छन्तः प्रव्रजन्ति ॥ २२ ॥"],"iast":["sa vā eṣa mahān aja ātmā yo 'yaṃ vijñānamayaḥ prāṇeṣu | ya eṣo 'ntar hṛdaya ākāśas tasmiñ chete | sarvasya vaśī sarvasyeśānaḥ sarvasyādhipatiḥ | sa na sādhunā karmaṇā bhūyān no evāsādhunā kanīyān | eṣa sarveśvaraḥ eṣa bhūtādhipatiḥ eṣa bhūtapālaḥ | eṣa setur vidharaṇa eṣāṃ lokānām asaṃbhedāya | tam etaṃ vedānuvacanena brāhmaṇā vividiṣanti yajñena dānena tapasānāśakena | etam eva viditvā munir bhavati | etam eva pravrājino lokam icchantaḥ pravrajanti || 22 ||"],"words":[["saḥ vai eṣaḥ mahān ajaḥ ātmā yaḥ ayaṃ vijñānamayaḥ prāṇeṣu","m. nom. (great, unborn) + loc. pl.","this great, unborn Self is the one (that appears as) consciousness among the breaths"],["yaḥ eṣaḥ antaḥ hṛdaye ākāśaḥ tasmin śete","loc. + pres. of śī","it lies in that space within the heart"],["sarvasya vaśī sarvasya īśānaḥ sarvasya adhipatiḥ","gen. + nom. (controller, ruler, overlord)","the controller of all, the ruler of all, the overlord of all"],["saḥ na sādhunā karmaṇā bhūyān na u eva asādhunā kanīyān","instr. + compar.","it does not grow greater by good action, nor lesser by bad"],["eṣaḥ sarva-īśvaraḥ eṣaḥ bhūta-adhipatiḥ eṣaḥ bhūta-pālaḥ","nom.","it is the Lord of all, the overlord of beings, the protector of beings"],["eṣaḥ setuḥ vidharaṇaḥ eṣāṃ lokānām asaṃbhedāya","nom. (dyke/dam, the separator) + gen. pl. + dat.","it is the dyke that holds (the worlds) apart, lest these worlds be confounded"],["tam etaṃ veda-anuvacanena brāhmaṇāḥ vividiṣanti yajñena dānena tapasā anāśakena","instr. + desid. of vid","him the brāhmaṇas seek to know by Veda-recitation, sacrifice, charity, and austerity-through-fasting"],["etam eva viditvā muniḥ bhavati","ger. + nom.","knowing him alone, one becomes a muni (silent sage)"],["etam eva pravrājinaḥ lokam icchantaḥ pravrajanti","acc. + pres.part. + pres.","desiring him alone as their world, wandering mendicants renounce"]],"lit":"\"This great, unborn Self is the one (appearing as) consciousness among the breaths; it lies in the space within the heart — the <strong>controller of all, ruler of all, overlord of all.</strong> It <strong>does not grow greater by good action, nor lesser by bad.</strong> It is the <strong>Lord of all, the overlord of beings, the protector of beings; the dyke (setu) that holds the worlds apart, lest they be confounded.</strong> Him the brāhmaṇas seek to know by Veda-recitation, sacrifice, charity, and austerity-through-fasting. Knowing him alone, one becomes a <strong>muni</strong>. Desiring him alone as their world, <strong>wandering mendicants renounce.</strong>\"","para":"A magnificent summation of the <strong>Self/Brahman</strong>: the <strong>great, unborn (mahān aja) Self</strong>, seated as consciousness in the <strong>heart-space</strong>, is the <strong>inner ruler of all</strong> (cf. the <em>antaryāmin</em>, 3.7) — yet, being the unchanging witness, it is <strong>untouched by good and evil</strong> (it neither gains by virtue nor loses by vice, for karma binds only the agent, not the witnessing Self — cf. 4.4.23). It is <strong>Lord, overlord, protector</strong>, and the <strong>\"dyke\" (setu) that keeps the orders of reality distinct</strong> (the cosmic ordering principle). This is what the spiritually mature seek — by <strong>study, sacrifice, charity, and tapas</strong> — and, knowing whom, one becomes a <strong>muni</strong> and <strong>renounces (pravrajana)</strong>, desiring nothing but the Self. (The classic charter for renunciation as the fruit of Self-knowledge, cf. 3.5.1.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>mahān aja ātmā</em> (great unborn Self) is Brahman as the <strong>inner controller and self-luminous witness</strong>, \"untouched by good or evil\" because karma's increase/decrease applies to the <em>doer</em>, not to the actionless Self. <em>Setu</em> (\"dyke/bridge\") = the sustaining principle that <strong>holds the worlds and orders distinct</strong> (and is also the \"bridge\" to immortality). The means (Veda, yajña, dāna, tapas) are <em>purificatory</em>, leading to the knowledge whose fruit is <em>muni</em>-hood and <strong>renunciation</strong> — the proper life of the knower (Śaṅkara's key text for <em>saṃnyāsa</em>)."],["Krishnananda","the Self is the unchanging ruler within, gaining nothing from virtue, losing nothing by vice — the witness beyond karma; it is the dyke that upholds cosmic order. To know it is to become a silent sage and to renounce, for nothing else is then worth wanting."],["Sureśvara","\"not greater by good, not lesser by bad\" establishes the Self's <strong>transcendence of karma</strong>; the disciplines purify, knowledge liberates, renunciation expresses it."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the great unborn Self is the inner Lord; the path is study-sacrifice-charity-austerity ripening into knowledge, muni-hood, and renunciation."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes <em>mahān aja ātmā</em>, <em>setu</em> (dyke/bridge), and the list of means (recitation, sacrifice, gift, fasting-austerity) culminating in renunciation as the canonical statement of the knower's vocation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.4.23","title":"The eternal greatness; śānta, dānta, the knower untouched by evil","sa":["तदेतदृचाभ्युक्तम् — एष नित्यो महिमा ब्राह्मणस्य न कर्मणा वर्धते नो कनीयान् । तस्यैव स्यात्पदवित्तं विदित्वा न लिप्यते कर्मणा पापकेनेति । तस्मादेवंविच्छान्तो दान्त उपरतस्तितिक्षुः समाहितो भूत्वात्मन्येवात्मानं पश्यति । सर्वमात्मानं पश्यति । नैनं पाप्मा तरति । सर्वं पाप्मानं तरति । नैनं पाप्मा तपति । सर्वं पाप्मानं तपति । विपापो विरजोऽविचिकित्सो ब्राह्मणो भवति । एष ब्रह्मलोकः संराट् । एनं प्रापितोऽसीति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः । सोऽहं भगवते विदेहान्ददामि मां चापि सह दास्यायेति ॥ २३ ॥"],"iast":["tad etad ṛcābhyuktam -- eṣa nityo mahimā brāhmaṇasya na karmaṇā vardhate no kanīyān | tasyaiva syāt padavittaṃ viditvā na lipyate karmaṇā pāpakeneti | tasmād evaṃvic chānto dānta uparatas titikṣuḥ samāhito bhūtvātmany evātmānaṃ paśyati | sarvam ātmānaṃ paśyati | nainaṃ pāpmā tarati | sarvaṃ pāpmānaṃ tarati | nainaṃ pāpmā tapati | sarvaṃ pāpmānaṃ tapati | vipāpo virajo 'vicikitso brāhmaṇo bhavati | eṣa brahmalokaḥ samrāṭ | enaṃ prāpito 'sīti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ | so 'haṃ bhagavate videhān dadāmi mām cāpi saha dāsyāyeti || 23 ||"],"words":[["tat etat ṛcā abhyuktam","f. instr. (by a ṛk-verse) + pp.","this is declared by a verse:"],["eṣaḥ nityaḥ mahimā brāhmaṇasya na karmaṇā vardhate no kanīyān","nom. (eternal greatness) + gen. + instr. + compar.","\"this is the eternal greatness of the knower of Brahman: it neither grows nor lessens by action"],["tasya eva syāt padavittam viditvā na lipyate karmaṇā pāpakena iti","gen. + n. acc. (its trace/abode) + ger. + pres. pass. of lip","one should know its abode (pada); knowing it, one is not stained by evil action\""],["tasmāt evaṃvit śāntaḥ dāntaḥ uparataḥ titikṣuḥ samāhitaḥ bhūtvā","nom. (calm, controlled, withdrawn, forbearing, collected) + ger.","therefore, having become calm, controlled, withdrawn, forbearing, and collected,"],["ātmani eva ātmānaṃ paśyati sarvam ātmānaṃ paśyati","loc. + acc. + pres.","he sees the Self in the self; he sees all as the Self"],["na enaṃ pāpmā tarati sarvaṃ pāpmānaṃ tarati","nom. (evil) + pres. of tṝ","evil does not overcome him; he overcomes all evil"],["na enaṃ pāpmā tapati sarvaṃ pāpmānaṃ tapati","pres. of tap","evil does not burn him; he burns all evil"],["vipāpaḥ virajaḥ avicikitsaḥ brāhmaṇaḥ bhavati","nom. (sinless, taintless, free of doubt)","free of evil, free of taint, free of doubt, he becomes a (true) brāhmaṇa"],["eṣaḥ brahma-lokaḥ samrāṭ enaṃ prāpitaḥ asi iti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ","+ 2 sg. pf. pass.","\"this is the brahma-world, your majesty; you have been brought to it,\" said Yājñavalkya"],["saḥ aham bhagavate videhān dadāmi mām ca api saha dāsyāya iti","acc. pl. + acc. + dat. (for servitude)","\"I give you the Videhas (my kingdom), sir, and myself too, to be your servant\""]],"lit":"\"This is declared by a verse: 'This is the <strong>eternal greatness</strong> of the knower of Brahman — it neither grows nor lessens by action. One should know its abode (<em>pada</em>); knowing it, one is not stained by evil action.' Therefore, having become <strong>calm (śānta), controlled (dānta), withdrawn (uparata), forbearing (titikṣu), and collected (samāhita)</strong>, he sees the Self in the self; he sees all as the Self. <strong>Evil does not overcome him; he overcomes all evil. Evil does not burn him; he burns all evil. Free of evil, free of taint, free of doubt, he becomes a (true) brāhmaṇa.</strong> This is the brahma-world, your majesty; you have been brought to it,\" said Yājñavalkya. (Janaka:) \"I give you the Videhas (my kingdom), sir, and myself too, to be your servant.\"","para":"The knower's <strong>eternal greatness neither increases by good deeds nor diminishes by bad</strong> — for it is the actionless Self, beyond karma's reach; <strong>knowing its \"abode,\" one is no longer stained by (even) evil action.</strong> Hence the famous <strong>discipline of the knower</strong>: become <strong>śānta (calm/peaceful), dānta (self-controlled), uparata (withdrawn from sense-craving), titikṣu (forbearing/enduring), and samāhita (collected/concentrated)</strong> — and then <strong>\"see the Self in the self, and all as the Self.\"</strong> Such a one <strong>transcends all evil</strong> (evil neither overcomes nor \"burns\" him; rather he masters it), becoming <strong>sinless, taintless, free of doubt — a true brāhmaṇa</strong>, established in the <strong>brahma-world</strong> <em>here and now</em>. Yājñavalkya declares Janaka has reached it; the king, in supreme gratitude, <strong>offers his entire kingdom and his own self in service.</strong> (These five qualities — śama, dama, uparati, titikṣā, samādhāna — became the classic <em>ṣaṭ-sampatti</em> virtues of the Vedāntic aspirant.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Self's greatness is <strong>beyond karma</strong> (neither grows nor shrinks by action), so the knower \"is not stained by evil action\" — not a license to sin, but the truth that the <em>realised</em> Self transcends the karmic field. The <strong>five disciplines (śānta, dānta, uparata, titikṣu, samāhita)</strong> are the prerequisites <em>and</em> expression of realisation; through them one \"sees the Self in oneself and all as the Self\" (<em>sarvam ātmānaṃ paśyati</em> — the vision of the Self as all). \"Evil neither crosses nor burns him; he crosses and burns all evil\" = the knower is untouched by, and master of, all that binds others. This is Śaṅkara's chief text for the <strong>sādhana-virtues</strong> and the <em>jīvanmukta's</em> freedom. Janaka's gift of kingdom and self is total surrender."],["Krishnananda","the realised one is <strong>beyond gain and loss by action</strong>; the five virtues (peace, control, withdrawal, endurance, concentration) are the mark of one established in the Self, who <strong>sees the Self in all and all in the Self</strong> — untouched and unburnt by any evil. This is the brahma-world attained in life."],["Sureśvara","\"not stained by evil action\" = the Self's transcendence of karma; the virtues purify and stabilise the knower; \"sees all as the Self\" is the consummation."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the practical ladder — śama, dama, uparati, titikṣā, samādhāna — leading to the vision of the Self in all; the knower rises above all sin. (The famous <em>ṣaṭka-sampatti</em> of later Vedānta.)"],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the verse on the knower's greatness beyond action and the list of disciplines, and Janaka's climactic self-surrender (\"the Videhas, and myself, to be your slave\")."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.4.24–4.4.25","title":"The great unborn Self: eater of food, and the Fearless Brahman","sa":["4.4.24: स वा एष महानज आत्मान्नादो वसुदानः । विन्दते वसु य एवं वेद ॥ २४ ॥","4.4.25: स वा एष महानज आत्माजरोऽमरोऽमृतोऽभयो ब्रह्म । अभयं वै ब्रह्म । अभयं हि वै ब्रह्म भवति य एवं वेद ॥ २५ ॥"],"iast":["sa vā eṣa mahān aja ātmānnādo vasudānaḥ | vindate vasu ya evaṃ veda || 24 ||","sa vā eṣa mahān aja ātmājaro 'maro 'mṛto 'bhayo brahma | abhayaṃ vai brahma | abhayaṃ hi vai brahma bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 25 ||"],"words":[["saḥ vai eṣaḥ mahān ajaḥ ātmā anna-adaḥ vasu-dānaḥ","nom. (eater of food, giver of wealth)","this great, unborn Self is the eater of food and the giver of wealth"],["vindate vasu yaḥ evaṃ veda","pres. of vid + acc.","he who knows thus obtains wealth"],["saḥ vai eṣaḥ mahān ajaḥ ātmā ajaraḥ amaraḥ amṛtaḥ abhayaḥ brahma","nom. (undecaying, undying, immortal, fearless)","this great, unborn Self is undecaying, undying, immortal, fearless — Brahman"],["abhayaṃ vai brahma","n. nom.","Brahman is indeed the fearless"],["abhayaṃ hi vai brahma bhavati yaḥ evaṃ veda","","he who knows thus becomes the fearless Brahman"]],"lit":"\"This great, unborn Self is the <strong>eater of food and the giver of wealth</strong>; he who knows this obtains wealth. This great, unborn Self is <strong>undecaying, undying, immortal, fearless — Brahman.</strong> Brahman is indeed the <strong>fearless (abhaya)</strong>; he who knows this becomes the fearless Brahman.\"","para":"Two closing affirmations of the <strong>mahān aja ātmā (great unborn Self)</strong>. It is the <strong>universal eater (annāda)</strong> and <strong>giver of wealth (vasudāna)</strong> — the source on which all sustenance and prosperity depend (the knower gains abundance). And, in the final word of the Yājñavalkya teaching: the Self is <strong>ajara (undecaying), amara (undying), amṛta (immortal), abhaya (fearless) — Brahman itself.</strong> <strong>\"Brahman is the Fearless,\"</strong> and <strong>to know it is to <em>become</em> the fearless Brahman</strong> — the same liberative fearlessness (<em>abhaya</em>) given to Janaka in 4.2.4, now sealing the whole discourse. Fearlessness — born of non-duality (no \"second\" to fear, 1.4.2) and deathlessness — is the consummation of Self-knowledge.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the Self as <em>annāda/vasudāna</em> (eater/giver) is the source and sustainer of all; but the climax is <strong>abhaya</strong> — the Self/Brahman is <strong>fearless</strong>, and the knower <strong>becomes</strong> that fearless Brahman. Fearlessness is the hallmark of liberation, for fear arises only from a second (1.4.2), and the non-dual, deathless Self has no second and no death. A fitting seal to the Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa."],["Krishnananda","the Self is the great provider and the great consumer — and, supremely, <strong>the Fearless</strong>. All fear is rooted in duality and death; the knower of the deathless, non-dual Self <em>is</em> fearlessness itself."],["Sureśvara","<em>abhayaṃ brahma</em> — fearlessness <em>is</em> Brahman; the knower attains not a state but an identity, beyond all that could threaten."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the goal in one word — <strong>abhaya</strong>, fearlessness; know the undecaying, immortal Self and become the fearless Brahman."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes the closing emphasis on <em>abhaya</em> (fearlessness) as the defining fruit of Self-knowledge, echoing 4.2.4 and rounding off the discourse to Janaka."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This brāhmaṇa completes the Upaniṣad's account of the human predicament and its resolution — <strong>death, rebirth, bondage, and liberation</strong>:","1. <strong>The mechanics of death (4.4.1–2):</strong> the senses and light-essences withdraw into the heart; the self departs by the eye/head/etc., accompanied by <strong>knowledge, works, and past impressions (vidyā, karma, pūrva-prajñā)</strong> — the carriers of continuity.","2. <strong>Transmigration (4.4.3–4):</strong> the self passes to a new body <strong>without a gap</strong> (the <strong>leech</strong>) and <strong>re-fashions itself by its karma</strong> into a new form (the <strong>goldsmith</strong>).","3. <strong>The law of becoming (4.4.5):</strong> the self, though Brahman, appears \"made of all\" it identifies with; <strong>\"as one acts, so one becomes,\"</strong> and the engine is <strong>desire → will → action → destiny</strong> (\"a person is made of desire\").","4. <strong>The two destinies (4.4.6–7):</strong> the <strong>desiring</strong> self returns \"for more work\" (saṃsāra); the <strong>desireless</strong> knower <strong>does not transmigrate</strong> — \"being Brahman, he merges in Brahman,\" and <strong>\"here (in this life) the mortal becomes immortal,\"</strong> the body a mere <strong>snake-slough</strong>.","5. <strong>The verses (4.4.8–21):</strong> the ancient path of the knowers; the <strong>two darknesses</strong> (heedless action and dry learning both fail); <strong>\"why grieve, knowing 'I am the Self'?\"</strong>; <strong>\"know it here or great is the loss\"</strong>; the <strong>light of lights</strong>; <strong>\"there is no plurality here — he who sees difference goes from death to death\" (neha nānāsti)</strong>; the Self <strong>as One, unborn, beyond space</strong>; and <strong>\"realise the Self — do not get lost in many words.\"</strong>","6. <strong>The great unborn Self (4.4.22–25):</strong> the inner <strong>ruler</strong> of all, <strong>untouched by good and evil</strong>, the <strong>dyke</strong> upholding the worlds; sought by study/sacrifice/charity/austerity, and known by the <strong>muni</strong> who <strong>renounces</strong>; the knower, made <strong>śānta-dānta-uparata-titikṣu-samāhita</strong>, <strong>sees the Self in all</strong> and <strong>transcends all evil</strong>; and the Self is finally the <strong>undecaying, immortal, FEARLESS Brahman</strong> — to know which is to become the fearless.","Together with 4.3, this brāhmaṇa gives the most complete Upaniṣadic statement of <strong>how consciousness transmigrates, how karma and desire bind, and how knowledge and desirelessness liberate — here and now.</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   DEATH, KARMA, AND LIBERATION (BU 4.4)\n   =====================================\n   DYING:  senses + light-essences -&gt; withdrawn into the HEART\n           tip of heart lights -&gt; self EXITS (eye/head/...) ; breaths follow\n           carries: VIDYĀ (knowledge) + KARMA (works) + PŪRVA-PRAJÑĀ (impressions)\n                              |\n   TRANSMIGRATION:\n        LEECH: grips the next blade before leaving the old (no gap)\n        GOLDSMITH: melts old gold -&gt; a NEWER, FAIRER form (per karma)\n                              |\n   THE LAW:  self appears &quot;made of all&quot; it identifies with\n        &quot;AS ONE ACTS, SO ONE BECOMES&quot;  ;  chain:  KĀMA (desire) -&gt; KRATU (will)\n                                                   -&gt; KARMA (act) -&gt; what one BECOMES\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   TWO DESTINIES:\n     DESIRING  -&gt; follows clinging mind -&gt; spends karma -&gt; RETURNS &quot;for more work&quot;\n     DESIRELESS (akāma, āptakāma, ātmakāma) -&gt; breaths DON&#x27;T depart\n                -&gt; &quot;BRAHMAIVA SAN BRAHMĀPYETI&quot; (being Brahman, merges in Brahman)\n                -&gt; desires gone: &quot;the mortal becomes IMMORTAL, HERE&quot;  (snake-slough)\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   VERSES:  two darknesses (action &amp; dry learning both fail) | &quot;why grieve, &#x27;I am the Self&#x27;?&quot;\n            &quot;NEHA NĀNĀSTI KIṂCANA&quot; (no plurality; see difference -&gt; death to death)\n            see the One (unborn, beyond space) | &quot;realise — don&#x27;t drown in words&quot;\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   THE GREAT UNBORN SELF (mahān aja ātmā): inner RULER, untouched by good/evil, the DYKE (setu)\n        known by: study · sacrifice · charity · austerity -&gt; muni -&gt; RENUNCIATION\n        knower: śānta·dānta·uparata·titikṣu·samāhita -&gt; sees the SELF in ALL, beyond all evil\n        =&gt; ajara · amara · amṛta · ABHAYA (FEARLESS) = BRAHMAN</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.4","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Tend what survives death.</strong> Knowledge, deeds, and ingrained tendencies (<em>vidyā, karma, pūrva-prajñā</em>) follow you past death and shape what comes. Cultivate right knowledge, right action, and wholesome tendencies <em>now</em> — they are your only luggage.","2. <strong>Watch the chain: desire → will → act → becoming.</strong> Since \"a person is made of desire,\" intervene at the root: examine your deepest desires, for they silently become your will, your deeds, and your destiny.","3. <strong>Loosen desire to stop the wheel</strong> (4.4.6–7). The desireless knower \"does not depart\" — he is already Brahman. Practise <em>āptakāma/ātmakāma</em> — finding fullness in the Self so that craving falls away. \"When all heart-desires fall, the mortal becomes immortal — here.\"","4. <strong>See no plurality</strong> (4.4.19). The cardinal practice: meet the day's manifold with the recognition of the one Self underlying it. \"He who sees difference goes from death to death\"; the vision of the One is freedom.","5. <strong>Realise, don't merely discuss</strong> (4.4.21). Let study ripen into direct insight (<em>prajñā</em>); endless words are \"weariness of speech.\" Read, reflect — then be silent and <em>be</em> the Self.","6. <strong>Build the five virtues</strong> (4.4.23). Cultivate <strong>śama</strong> (calm), <strong>dama</strong> (self-control), <strong>uparati</strong> (withdrawal from cravings), <strong>titikṣā</strong> (forbearance), and <strong>samādhāna</strong> (collectedness) — the ground on which \"I see the Self in all\" becomes real.","7. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the body at death is but a <strong>cast-off snake-skin</strong>; your true Self is the <strong>great, unborn, immortal, FEARLESS Brahman</strong> — and knowing this, \"seeking what, for love of what, would you grieve over the body?\" The fruit of the whole teaching is <em>abhaya</em> — fearlessness."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 4 · Brāhmaṇa 4.5 — Yājñavalkya and Maitreyī (second version): the seal of the teaching","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 4.5","title":"Yājñavalkya and Maitreyī (second version): the seal of the teaching","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The</em> <strong>second Maitreyī dialogue</strong> — <em>a fuller recension of 2.4, placed here as the</em> <strong>climax and seal of the whole Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa</strong>, <em>just before Yājñavalkya renounces the world. The teaching is identical in substance to 2.4 (all is dear for the sake of the Self; the Self is to be heard, reflected on, meditated upon; the lump-of-salt; \"by what should one know the Knower?\"), with a few significant expansions, noted below. Here Yājñavalkya is</em> <strong>about to take up renunciation (pravrajyā)</strong>, <em>and the dialogue ends with his departure into the homeless life — knowledge issuing in saṃnyāsa.</em>","<em>(For full per-line commentary on the shared material, see Brāhmaṇa 2.4; here the digests focus on this recension's distinctive features.)</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantras 4.5.1–4.5.5","title":"Yājñavalkya about to renounce; can wealth give immortality?","sa":["4.5.1–2: अथ ह याज्ञवल्क्योऽन्यद्वृत्तमुपाकरिष्यन् ॥ १ ॥ मैत्रेयीति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः । प्रव्रजिष्यन्वा अरेऽहमस्मात्स्थानादस्मि । हन्त तेऽनया कात्यायन्यान्तं करवाणीति ॥ २ ॥","4.5.3–5: सा होवाच मैत्रेयी — यन्नु म इयं भगोः सर्वा पृथिवी वित्तेन पूर्णा स्यात्स्यां न्वहं तेनामृताहो३ नेति । नेति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः । यथैवोपकरणवतां जीवितं तथैव ते जीवितं स्यात् । अमृतत्वस्य तु नाशास्ति वित्तेनेति ॥ ३ ॥ सा होवाच मैत्रेयी — येनाहं नामृता स्यां किमहं तेन कुर्याम् । यदेव भगवान्वेद तदेव मे ब्रूहीति ॥ ४ ॥ स होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः — प्रिया वै खलु नो भवती सती प्रियमवृधत् । हन्त तर्हि भवत्येतद्व्याख्यास्यामि ते । व्याचक्षाणस्य तु मे निदिध्यासस्वेति ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["atha ha yājñavalkyo 'nyad vṛttam upākariṣyan || 1 || maitreyīti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ | pravrajiṣyan vā are 'ham asmāt sthānād asmi | hanta te 'nayā katyāyanyāntaṃ karavāṇīti || 2 ||","sā hovāca maitreyī -- yan nu ma iyam bhagoḥ sarvā pṛthivī vittena pūrṇā syāt syāṃ nv ahaṃ tenāmṛtāho3 neti | neti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ | … amṛtatvasya tu nāśāsti vitteneti || 3 || … yad eva bhagavān veda tad eva me brūhīti || 4 || sa hovāca -- priyā vai khalu no bhavatī satī priyam avṛdhat | hanta tarhi … vyācakṣāṇasya tu me nididhyāsasveti || 5 ||"],"words":[["atha ha yājñavalkyaḥ anyat vṛttam upākariṣyan","n. acc. (another mode of life) + fut.part. of upā-kṛ","now Yājñavalkya was about to take up another mode of life (renunciation)"],["pravrajiṣyan vai are aham asmāt sthānāt asmi","fut.part. of pra-vraj (about to wander forth) + abl.","\"I am about to go forth (renounce) from this state, my dear\""],["hanta te anayā katyāyanyā antaṃ karavāṇi iti","instr. + acc.","\"come, let me make a settlement between you and (my other wife) Kātyāyanī\""],["priyā vai khalu naḥ bhavatī satī priyam avṛdhat","f. nom. (you, being dear) + 3 sg. aor. of vṛdh (caus.)","\"dear indeed you are to us, and you have increased what is dear (spoken what is dear)\""]],"lit":"\"Now Yājñavalkya was about to take up another mode of life (renunciation). 'Maitreyī,' he said, 'I am about to go forth (renounce) from this householder's state. Come, let me settle (the property) between you and Kātyāyanī.' Maitreyī said: 'Sir, if this whole earth full of wealth were mine, would I become immortal by it, or not?' 'No,' said Yājñavalkya; 'your life would be like the life of the wealthy — but of immortality there is no hope through wealth.' 'What should I do with that by which I cannot become immortal? Tell me, sir, what you (truly) know.' He said: 'Dear you are to us, and dearly you have spoken. Come, then; I shall explain — and meditate on what I expound.'\"","para":"","coms":[["Śaṅkara","placing the dialogue at the point of <em>pravrajyā</em> (renunciation) makes it the <em>capstone</em> of the kāṇḍa — the teaching given as the sage himself enacts its fruit (the renunciate life). The substance is one with 2.4: <strong>immortality is not purchasable; only Self-knowledge confers it.</strong>"],["Krishnananda","the second telling, at the moment of renunciation, shows knowledge <em>consummated in action</em> — Yājñavalkya does not merely teach detachment but lives it, walking out into the homeless life."]],"notes":["<strong>Distinctive feature:</strong> Where 2.4.1 had Yājñavalkya merely <em>\"about to go away\" (udyāsyan)</em>, here he is explicitly <strong>\"about to renounce\" (pravrajiṣyan)</strong> — and the dialogue will close (4.5.15) with his actual <strong>departure into homelessness</strong>. This places the teaching as the very threshold of his <em>saṃnyāsa</em>: Self-knowledge naturally flowering into renunciation.","- <em>(See 2.4.1–4 for fuller commentary on the shared content.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.5.6","title":"All is dear for the sake of the Self (expanded list)","sa":["स होवाच — न वा अरे पत्युः कामाय पतिः प्रियो भवत्यात्मनस्तु कामाय पतिः प्रियो भवति । न वा अरे जायायै कामाय जाया … पुत्राणां … वित्तस्य … पशूनां … ब्रह्मणः … क्षत्रस्य … लोकानां … देवानां … वेदानां … भूतानां … सर्वस्य कामाय सर्वं प्रियं भवत्यात्मनस्तु कामाय सर्वं प्रियं भवति । आत्मा वा अरे द्रष्टव्यः श्रोतव्यो मन्तव्यो निदिध्यासितव्यो मैत्रेयि । आत्मनि खल्वरे दृष्टे श्रुते मते विज्ञात इदं सर्वं विदितम् ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["sa hovāca -- na vā are patyuḥ kāmāya patiḥ priyo bhavaty ātmanas tu kāmāya patiḥ priyo bhavati | … paśūnāṃ … vedānāṃ … na vā are sarvasya kāmāya sarvaṃ priyaṃ bhavaty ātmanas tu kāmāya sarvaṃ priyaṃ bhavati | ātmā vā are draṣṭavyaḥ śrotavyo mantavyo nididhyāsitavyo maitreyi | ātmani khalv are dṛṣṭe śrute mate vijñāta idaṃ sarvaṃ viditam || 6 ||"],"words":[["na vai are X-(gen.) kāmāya X priyaḥ bhavati","gen. + dat.","\"not for the love of X is X dear,"],["ātmanaḥ tu kāmāya X priyaḥ bhavati","gen. + dat.","but for the love of the Self is X dear\""],["(X = husband, wife, sons, wealth, cattle, brahman, kṣatra, worlds, gods, Vedas, beings, all)","—","(the series, expanded with paśu \"cattle\" and veda \"the Vedas\")"],["ātmā vai are draṣṭavyaḥ śrotavyaḥ mantavyaḥ nididhyāsitavyaḥ maitreyi","gerundives","the Self, O Maitreyī, is to be seen, heard, reflected on, deeply meditated upon"],["ātmani khalu are dṛṣṭe śrute mate vijñāte idaṃ sarvaṃ viditam","loc. abs. + n. nom. pp.","when the Self is seen, heard, reflected on, and realised, all this is known"]],"lit":"\"He said: 'Not for the love of the husband is the husband dear, but for the love of the Self; (so too) the wife, sons, wealth, <strong>cattle</strong>, the priestly power, the ruling power, the worlds, the gods, <strong>the Vedas</strong>, beings, and the All — none is dear for its own sake, but for the love of the Self. <strong>The Self, O Maitreyī, is to be seen, heard, reflected on, and deeply meditated upon.</strong> When the Self is seen, heard, reflected on, and realised, <strong>all this is known.</strong>'\"","para":"","coms":[["Śaṅkara","same doctrine as 2.4.5 (all love is self-love; the Self alone is truly dear; know it and know all), with the list rounded out (cattle, Vedas) for completeness. The fourfold <em>draṣṭavya…nididhyāsitavya</em> remains the canonical <strong>path of knowledge</strong> (hear–reason–meditate, culminating in realisation)."],["Krishnananda","\"By knowing the One, all is known\" — for all things are dear, and intelligible, only as the Self; the addition of cattle and Vedas makes the sweep total (from livestock to scripture)."]],"notes":["<strong>Distinctive features:</strong> This recension <strong>adds <em>paśu</em> (cattle) and <em>veda</em> (the Vedas)</strong> to the list of things dear only for the Self's sake (2.4.5 omitted these). And the closing formula differs slightly: 2.4.5 said \"by the <strong>seeing, hearing, reflection, and realisation</strong> of the Self, all this is known\"; here, <strong>\"when the Self is seen, heard, reflected on, and realised (ātmani dṛṣṭe… vijñāte), all this is known\"</strong> — placing the emphasis on the Self as the one object whose knowledge yields knowledge of all. The core remains the great injunction: <strong>draṣṭavya–śrotavya–mantavya–nididhyāsitavya</strong> (the threefold path of <em>śravaṇa–manana–nididhyāsana</em>).","- <em>(See 2.4.5 for the full exposition of the analysis of love and the threefold discipline.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.5.7–4.5.12","title":"Deserts him who knows them as other; the drum/conch/lute; the breath of the Being; the convergences","sa":["4.5.11 (Vedas as the Being's breath): स यथार्द्रैधाग्नेरभ्याहितस्य पृथग्धूमा विनिश्चरन्त्येवं वा अरेऽस्य महतो भूतस्य निःश्वसितमेतद्यदृग्वेदो यजुर्वेदः … इष्टं हुतमाशितं पायितमयं च लोकः परश्च लोकः सर्वाणि च भूतानि । अस्यैवैतानि सर्वाणि निःश्वसितानि ॥ ११ ॥ — all the Vedas, lore, rites, both worlds, and all beings are the out-breath of the great Being. (= 2.4.10)"],"words":[],"lit":"<strong>4.5.12 (the convergences):</strong> स यथा सर्वासामपां समुद्र एकायनम् … एवं सर्वेषां वेदानां वागेकायनम् ॥ १२ ॥ — <em>as all waters meet in the ocean, so each class of experience meets in its single centre (skin, nostrils, tongue, eye, ear, mind, heart, hands, …, speech).</em> (= 2.4.11)","para":"Identical in import to <strong>2.4.6–11</strong> (see there for full glosses and commentary): whatever is known <em>as separate from the Self</em> is ultimately lost, <strong>for all is the Self</strong>; the <strong>drum/conch/lute</strong> similes teach that grasping the <em>source</em> grasps all its modes; the <strong>Vedas and all literature are the effortless out-breath of the great Being (Brahman)</strong>; and every domain of experience <strong>converges (ekāyana)</strong> on a single centre — all funnelling, finally, into the one Self. (4.5.7 adds the <strong>Vedas</strong> to the list, matching 4.5.6.)","coms":[],"notes":["<em>These reproduce 2.4.6–11 (with </em>vedāḥ<em> added to the \"deserts him\" list of 4.5.7). In brief:</em>","<strong>4.5.7:</strong> ब्रह्म तं परादाद्योऽन्यत्रात्मनो ब्रह्म वेद … (kṣatra, lokāḥ, devāḥ, <strong>vedāḥ</strong>, bhūtāni, sarvam) … इदं ब्रह्मेदं क्षत्रमिमे लोका इमे देवा इमे वेदा इमानि भूतानीदं सर्वं यदयमात्मा ॥ ७ ॥ — <em>whatever one knows as separate from the Self deserts him; for all this is the Self.</em>","<strong>4.5.8–10 (drum, conch, lute):</strong> स यथा दुन्दुभेर्हन्यमानस्य … दुन्दुभेस्तु ग्रहणेन … शब्दो गृहीतः ॥ — <em>grasp the source (instrument) and all its sounds are grasped; so, knowing the Self, all is comprehended.</em> (= 2.4.7–9)"]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.5.13","title":"The lump of salt: a homogeneous mass of consciousness","sa":["स यथा सैन्धवघनोऽनन्तरोऽबाह्यः कृत्स्नो रसघन एव । एवं वा अरेऽयमात्मानन्तरोऽबाह्यः कृत्स्नः प्रज्ञानघन एव । एतेभ्यो भूतेभ्यः समुत्थाय तान्येवानुविनश्यति । न प्रेत्य संज्ञास्तीत्यरे ब्रवीमि । इति होवाच याज्ञवल्क्यः ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["sa yathā saindhavaghano 'nantaro 'bāhyaḥ kṛtsno rasaghana eva | evaṃ vā are 'yam ātmānantaro 'bāhyaḥ kṛtsnaḥ prajñānaghana eva | etebhyo bhūtebhyaḥ samutthāya tāny evānuvinaśyati | na pretya saṃjñāstīty are bravīmi | iti hovāca yājñavalkyaḥ || 13 ||"],"words":[["saḥ yathā saindhava-ghanaḥ anantaraḥ abāhyaḥ kṛtsnaḥ rasa-ghanaḥ eva","nom. (a lump of rock-salt) + (without inside) + (without outside) + (whole) + (a mass of taste)","as a lump of salt is without inside or outside, a whole homogeneous mass of taste,"],["evaṃ vai are ayam ātmā anantaraḥ abāhyaḥ kṛtsnaḥ prajñāna-ghanaḥ eva","adj. (a mass of consciousness)","so this Self is without inside or outside, a whole homogeneous mass of pure consciousness (prajñāna-ghana)"],["etebhyaḥ bhūtebhyaḥ samutthāya tāni eva anuvinaśyati","abl. pl. + ger. + pres.","arising out of these elements, it vanishes again along with them"],["na pretya saṃjñā asti iti are bravīmi","+ f. nom.","\"after departing (death) there is no (particular) consciousness,\" so I say"]],"lit":"\"As a lump of rock-salt is without inside or outside, a single whole mass of taste, so this Self is without inside or outside, a single whole <strong>mass of pure consciousness (prajñāna-ghana)</strong>. Arising out of these elements (the body-mind), it vanishes again with them. 'After departing (death) there is no (particular) consciousness' — so I say,' said Yājñavalkya.\"","para":"","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>prajñāna-ghana</em> — Brahman/Self is <strong>solid, undivided consciousness</strong> through and through (as salt is uniformly salty), \"without inside or outside\" (partless, non-spatial). \"No saṃjñā after death\" = no <em>particular/relational</em> cognition (which needs a second), <strong>not</strong> the absence of the Self's intrinsic awareness — exactly as clarified in 4.5.14–15 (and 2.4.13–14)."],["Krishnananda","the Self is \"consciousness, consciousness, nothing but consciousness,\" like salt that is taste through and through — you cannot find a corner of it that is not awareness. The \"no consciousness\" is the dropping of <em>separative</em> knowing, not of being-awareness."]],"notes":["<strong>Distinctive feature:</strong> Where 2.4.12 called the Self a <strong>vijñāna-ghana</strong> (\"mass of cognition\"), this recension says <strong>prajñāna-ghana</strong> (\"mass of pure consciousness/awareness\") — and adds the precise gloss <strong>\"anantaro 'bāhyaḥ\" (without inside or outside)</strong>, echoing the <em>akṣara</em> of 3.8.8. The point is identical: the Self is <strong>partless, all-pervading, homogeneous consciousness</strong> — like salt, fully \"tasteable\" everywhere yet with no separable parts; and \"no consciousness after death\" again means the cessation of <strong>dualistic, object-directed</strong> cognition, not of awareness itself (clarified in 4.5.14–15).","- <em>(See 2.4.12 for the fuller exposition of the salt-simile.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Mantras 4.5.14–4.5.15","title":"\"The Self is imperishable\"; by what should one know the Knower?","sa":["4.5.14: सा होवाच मैत्रेयी — अत्रैव मा भगवान्मोहान्तमापीपदन्न वा अहमिमं विजानामीति । स होवाच — न वा अरेऽहं मोहं ब्रवीमि । अविनाशी वा अरेऽयमात्मानुच्छित्तिधर्मा ॥ १४ ॥","4.5.15: यत्र हि द्वैतमिव भवति तदितर इतरं पश्यति … (smell, taste, speak, hear, think, touch, know) … यत्र त्वस्य सर्वमात्मैवाभूत्तत्केन कं पश्येत् … तत्केन कं विजानीयात् । येनेदं सर्वं विजानाति तं केन विजानीयात् । स एष नेति नेत्यात्मा । अगृह्यो न हि गृह्यते । अशीर्यो न हि शीर्यते । असङ्गो न हि सज्यते । असितो न व्यथते न रिष्यति । विज्ञातारमरे केन विजानीयादित्युक्तानुशासनासि मैत्रेयि । एतावदरे खल्वमृतत्वमिति होक्त्वा याज्ञवल्क्यो विजहार ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["sā hovāca maitreyī -- atraiva mā bhagavān mohāntam āpīpadat na vā aham imaṃ vijānāmīti | sa hovāca -- na vā are 'haṃ mohaṃ bravīmi | avināśī vā are 'yam ātmānucchittidharmā || 14 ||","yatra hi dvaitam iva bhavati tad itara itaraṃ paśyati … yatra tv asya sarvam ātmaivābhūt tat kena kaṃ paśyet … tat kena kaṃ vijānīyāt | yenedaṃ sarvaṃ vijānāti taṃ kena vijānīyāt | sa eṣa neti nety ātmā | agṛhyo na hi gṛhyate | aśīryo na hi śīryate | asaṅgo na hi sajyate | asito na vyathate na riṣyati | vijñātāram are kena vijānīyād ity uktānuśāsanāsi maitreyi | etāvad are khalv amṛtatvam iti hoktvā yājñavalkyo vijahāra || 15 ||"],"words":[["atra eva mā bhagavān moha-antam āpīpadat na vai aham imaṃ vijānāmi iti","acc. + 3 sg. aor. caus. of ā-pad","\"here, sir, you have plunged me into confusion; I do not understand this\""],["na vai are aham mohaṃ bravīmi","acc. + pres.","\"I say nothing confusing, my dear"],["avināśī vai are ayam ātmā anucchitti-dharmā","m. nom. (imperishable) + adj. (of indestructible nature)","imperishable, my dear, is this Self, of indestructible nature\""],["yatra hi dvaitam iva bhavati … (one perceives another)","(as 2.4.14)","for where there is duality, as it were, one sees/…/knows another"],["yatra tu asya sarvam ātmā eva abhūt tat kena kaṃ paśyet …","(as 2.4.14)","but where all has become the Self, by what and whom should one see…?"],["yena idaṃ sarvaṃ vijānāti taṃ kena vijānīyāt","instr. + acc. + opt.","that by which one knows all this — by what should one know That?"],["saḥ eṣaḥ neti neti ātmā agṛhyaḥ … asitaḥ na vyathate na riṣyati","(the neti-neti characterization)","that Self is \"not this, not this\": ungraspable, undecaying, unattached, unbound — it does not tremble, is not harmed"],["vijñātāram are kena vijānīyāt iti ukta-anuśāsanā asi maitreyi","acc. + opt. + adj. (one who has been taught)","\"by what should one know the Knower?\" — you have (now) been instructed, Maitreyī"],["etāvat are khalu amṛtatvam iti ha uktvā yājñavalkyaḥ vijahāra","n. nom. (immortality) + ger. + 3 sg. pf. of vi-hṛ","\"this much, indeed, is immortality\" — so saying, Yājñavalkya departed (renounced)"]],"lit":"\"Maitreyī said: 'Here, sir, you have plunged me into confusion; I do not understand this.' He said: 'I say nothing confusing, my dear. <strong>Imperishable, indeed, is this Self, of indestructible nature.</strong> For where there is duality, as it were, there one sees another, smells another, tastes, speaks to, hears, thinks of, touches, knows another; but where all has become the Self, then by what and whom should one see? … by what should one know? <strong>That by which one knows all this — by what should one know That? By what, my dear, should one know the Knower?</strong> — That Self is \"not this, not this\" (<em>neti neti</em>): ungraspable, for it is not grasped; undecaying, for it does not decay; unattached, for it does not cling; unbound, it neither trembles nor is harmed. By what should one know the Knower? You have now been instructed, Maitreyī. <strong>This much, indeed, is immortality.</strong>' Having said this, Yājñavalkya departed (into the renounced life).\"","para":"","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the clarification <strong>\"the Self is imperishable, indestructible\"</strong> decisively refutes Maitreyī's (and the nihilist's) fear that \"no consciousness after death\" means annihilation: what ends is only <em>relational</em> knowing (which requires a second), never the <strong>self-luminous Knower</strong>, who can never be objectified (\"by what should one know the Knower?\"). The <em>neti-neti</em> attributes (ungraspable, undecaying, unattached, unbound) define this imperishable witness. <strong>\"This much is immortality\"</strong> seals it — Self-knowledge <em>is</em> immortality — and Yājñavalkya's <strong>departure (renunciation)</strong> is its living expression: the teaching ends, and the sage walks into freedom. The grand close of the Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa."],["Krishnananda","\"The Self is imperishable\" — the answer to all fear of death; what we call \"no consciousness\" is the end of the <em>split</em> between knower and known, not of awareness. \"By what should one know the Knower?\" — you <em>are</em> the Knower; you cannot stand outside to know it. With this, the highest is said, and Yājñavalkya — fittingly — renounces and disappears into the Infinite he has taught."],["Sureśvara","(this dialogue is the heart of his <em>vārttika</em>) — <em>amṛtatva</em> (immortality) is <em>nothing but</em> the knowledge of the imperishable, non-dual, unobjectifiable Self; \"etāvad amṛtatvam\" leaves nothing further to attain."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","the seal — the Self is imperishable; to know it (as the unknowable Knower, <em>neti neti</em>) <em>is</em> immortality; and the knower's natural course is renunciation. Yājñavalkya enacts what he teaches."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle notes this fuller recension's explicit <em>avināśī ātmā</em> and <em>etāvad amṛtatvam</em>, and the dramatic closing — <em>yājñavalkyo vijahāra</em>, \"Yājñavalkya departed\" — the sage's renunciation crowning the kāṇḍa."]],"notes":["<strong>Distinctive features:</strong> This recension makes explicit what 2.4.13 left implicit: <strong>\"avināśī vā are 'yam ātmā anucchittidharmā\" — \"imperishable is this Self, of indestructible nature\"</strong> — directly forestalling the nihilistic reading of \"no consciousness after death.\" It also <strong>folds in the <em>neti-neti</em> characterization</strong> (<em>agṛhya, aśīrya, asaṅga, asita</em> — from 3.x/4.2.4) right after \"by what should one know the Knower?\", uniting the two great formulae. And it closes the entire Yājñavalkya teaching with <strong>\"etāvad are khalv amṛtatvam\" — \"THIS much, indeed, IS immortality\"</strong> — and then <strong>\"Yājñavalkya departed (vijahāra)\":</strong> having taught the highest, the sage <strong>renounces the world</strong> and walks away. Knowledge culminates in liberation and in the homeless life."]},{"ref":"Details","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The second Maitreyī dialogue <strong>seals the Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa</strong> by restating, at the threshold of the sage's renunciation, its essential teaching (identical to 2.4, with telling expansions):","1. <strong>Immortality is not bought</strong> (4.5.1–5): wealth gives only \"the life of the prosperous\"; here Yājñavalkya is explicitly <strong>about to renounce (pravrajiṣyan)</strong>.","2. <strong>All is dear for the Self</strong> (4.5.6): the analysis of love (now including <em>cattle</em> and <em>the Vedas</em>); the Self is to be <strong>heard, reflected on, meditated upon (śravaṇa–manana–nididhyāsana)</strong>; \"knowing the Self, all is known.\"","3. <strong>The Self alone is real and securing</strong> (4.5.7–12): what is known as <em>other</em> deserts one (all is the Self); grasp the source (drum/conch/lute); the Vedas are the Being's breath; all experience converges in single centres.","4. <strong>The Self as partless consciousness</strong> (4.5.13): a <strong>prajñāna-ghana</strong> (\"mass of pure consciousness\"), without inside or outside, like a lump of salt.","5. <strong>The imperishable, unobjectifiable Knower</strong> (4.5.14–15): the decisive clarification — <strong>\"the Self is imperishable, of indestructible nature\"</strong> (no annihilation); in non-duality there is no second to know; <strong>\"by what should one know the Knower?\"</strong>; the Self is <strong>neti neti</strong> (ungraspable, undecaying, unattached, unbound). <strong>\"This much is immortality.\"</strong> — and <strong>Yājñavalkya renounces and departs.</strong>","The placement is deliberate: the supreme teaching is given <strong>as the sage steps into the homeless life</strong> — <em>jñāna</em> flowering into <em>saṃnyāsa</em>, the perfect close to the dialectical kāṇḍa."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   MAITREYĪ II — THE SEAL (BU 4.5)   [expanded recension of 2.4, at Yājñavalkya&#x27;s RENUNCIATION]\n   ============================================================================\n   &quot;Will wealth make me immortal?&quot; -&gt; NO (life of the rich, not amṛtatva)\n        |\n   ALL is dear FOR THE SAKE OF THE SELF  (+ cattle, + the Vedas)\n        =&gt; the SELF: to be SEEN · HEARD · REFLECTED ON · MEDITATED (śravaṇa-manana-nididhyāsana)\n           &quot;the Self known -&gt; ALL is known&quot;\n        |\n   know anything as OTHER -&gt; it deserts you; ALL IS THE SELF\n   grasp the source (drum/conch/lute) | Vedas = the Being&#x27;s out-breath | all converges (ekāyana)\n        |\n   SALT-LUMP: the Self = PRAJÑĀNA-GHANA (mass of pure consciousness), no inside/outside\n        =&gt; &quot;no consciousness after death&quot;  --(Maitreyī confused)--&gt;\n        |\n   CLARIFIED: &quot;the Self is IMPERISHABLE (avināśī), of indestructible nature&quot;\n        where duality -&gt; one knows another ; where ALL = Self -&gt; by what know whom?\n        ===&gt; &quot;VIJÑĀTĀRAM ARE KENA VIJĀNĪYĀT&quot; (by what should one know the KNOWER?)\n        the Self = NETI NETI: agṛhya · aśīrya · asaṅga · asita (untrembling, unharmed)\n        ===&gt; &quot;ETĀVAD KHALV AMṚTATVAM&quot; (THIS much IS immortality)\n                              |\n                              v   &quot;yājñavalkyo vijahāra&quot; — Yājñavalkya RENOUNCED and departed</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps / observations","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.5","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Ask the immortality-question of everything you pursue</strong> (4.5.3) — wealth, even \"the whole earth,\" cannot make you immortal; only Self-knowledge can. Re-rank your aims accordingly.","2. <strong>Redirect all love to its true object</strong> (4.5.6): you love spouse, child, possessions, even scripture, <em>for the sake of the Self</em>; turn that love to the Self directly, and walk the path of <em>śravaṇa–manana–nididhyāsana</em>.","3. <strong>Rest as the partless consciousness</strong> (4.5.13): contemplate awareness as a single \"mass,\" like salt-water — no inside, no outside, no separable parts; you everywhere, awareness everywhere.","4. <strong>Do not fear \"no consciousness\"</strong> (4.5.14): the Self is <strong>imperishable</strong>; what ends in unity is only the <em>split</em> of knower-and-known, never awareness. Liberation is fullness, not blankness.","5. <strong>Don't try to know the Knower</strong> (4.5.15): you are the Knower; cease seeking it as an object and simply <em>be</em> the witnessing awareness. \"This much is immortality.\"","6. <strong>Observation to carry:</strong> the highest teaching ends in <strong>renunciation</strong> — Yājñavalkya, having spoken it, walks away into the Infinite. Knowledge is not for accumulation but for <em>becoming free</em>; let what you know loosen your grip and lighten your steps."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Adhyāya 4 · Brāhmaṇa 4.6 — The Lineage of Teachers (Vaṃśa)","u":[{"ref":"Brāhmaṇa 4.6","title":"The Lineage of Teachers (Vaṃśa)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>The Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa (Adhyāyas 3–4) closes, like the Madhu-kāṇḍa (2.6), with a</em> <strong>vaṃśa</strong> — <em>the</em> <strong>succession of teachers</strong> <em>through whom this knowledge descended, traced back through generations of named sages to</em> <strong>Brahman the self-existent (svayambhū)</strong>, <em>and sealed with</em> <strong>\"salutation to Brahman.\"</strong> <em>It certifies the teaching as an unbroken, authoritative transmission, not private speculation.</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantras 4.6.1–4.6.3","title":"The succession, back to the self-existent Brahman","sa":["(Opening, 4.6.1): अथ वंशः । पौतिमाष्यो गौपवनात् । गौपवनः पौतिमाष्यात् … शाण्डिल्यः कौशिकाच्च गौतमाच्च । गौतमः ॥ १ ॥","(Close, 4.6.3): … विश्वरूपस्त्वाष्ट्रोऽश्विभ्याम् । अश्विनौ दधीच आथर्वणात् । दध्यङ्ङाथर्वणोऽथर्वणो दैवात् । अथर्वा दैवो मृत्योः प्राध्वंसनात् … सनगः परमेष्ठिनः । परमेष्ठी ब्रह्मणः । ब्रह्म स्वयम्भु । ब्रह्मणे नमः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["atha vaṃśaḥ | pautimāṣyo gaupavanāt | gaupavanaḥ pautimāṣyāt … śāṇḍilyaḥ kauśikāc ca gautamāc ca | gautamaḥ || 1 ||","… viśvarūpas tvāṣṭro 'śvibhyām | aśvinau dadhīca ātharvaṇāt | dadhyaṅṅ ātharvaṇo 'tharvaṇo daivāt | atharvā daivo mṛtyoḥ prādhvaṃsanāt … sanagaḥ parameṣṭhinaḥ | parameṣṭhī brahmaṇaḥ | brahma svayambhu | brahmaṇe namaḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[["atha vaṃśaḥ","m. nom.","now the line (of transmission)"],["pautimāṣyaḥ gaupavanāt (… long chain in the ablative …)","abl.","Pautimāṣya (received it) from Gaupavana; … (and so back through dozens of teachers)"],["aśvinau dadhīcaḥ ātharvaṇāt — dadhyaṅ ātharvaṇaḥ … mṛtyoḥ prādhvaṃsanāt","abl.","the Aśvins from Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa … (through) Mṛtyu (Death) Prādhvaṃsana …"],["parameṣṭhī brahmaṇaḥ","abl.","Parameṣṭhin (received it) from Brahman"],["brahma svayambhu","n. nom. + adj.","Brahman is the self-existent"],["brahmaṇe namaḥ","dat. + indecl.","salutation to Brahman"]],"lit":"\"Now the <strong>lineage</strong>: Pautimāṣya (received this) from Gaupavana; Gaupavana from Pautimāṣya; … (through a long succession of named teachers — Śāṇḍilya, Gautama, Bhāradvāja, Pārāśarya, Jātūkarṇya, Yāska, the Aśvins, <strong>Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa, Atharvan, Mṛtyu (Death)</strong>, Ekarṣi, … Sanātana, Sanaga) … Parameṣṭhin from <strong>Brahman</strong>. <strong>Brahman is the self-existent.</strong> <strong>Salutation to Brahman.</strong>\"","para":"A second, longer <strong>genealogy of the teaching</strong> (closely paralleling 2.6 and partly overlapping it), each teacher named with the one from whom he received the knowledge, traced backward through many generations of human sages, then through semi-divine figures (the Aśvins, Dadhyañc, Atharvan, <strong>Mṛtyu / Death</strong>) and the cosmic teachers, to <strong>Parameṣṭhin (Hiraṇyagarbha)</strong> and finally to <strong>Brahman the self-existent (svayambhū)</strong> — the uncaused Source. It closes, like 2.6, with <strong>\"brahmaṇe namaḥ\" — \"salutation to Brahman.\"</strong>","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the <em>vaṃśa</em> certifies the Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa's knowledge as <strong>authoritative, lineage-transmitted (paramparā-āgata)</strong> revelation, descending from <strong>Brahman itself</strong> through Hiraṇyagarbha (Parameṣṭhin) and the chain of seers — not human invention. The reverent close returns all knowledge to its Source."],["Krishnananda","the lineage honours the unbroken chain of gurus through whom the truth survives, and grounds it in the self-existent Brahman; <em>brahmaṇe namaḥ</em> is the fitting bow of gratitude."],["Mādhavānanda/Sivananda","cultivates faith in the <em>guru-paramparā</em> and reverence for the Source."],["Sureśvara","the descent from Brahman authenticates the teaching as revealed, not speculative."],["Deussen/Olivelle","Olivelle values the vaṃśas (here, 2.6, and 6.5) as historical records of transmission, all tracing descent ultimately to Brahman the self-existent."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Details & practical note","title":"Brāhmaṇa 4.6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["As with 2.6, this <em>vaṃśa</em> is the kāṇḍa's <strong>content-colophon</strong> — a recital of the <strong>teacher-succession</strong> certifying the foregoing as a living transmission from <strong>Brahman svayambhū</strong>. Its lesson is dispositional: <strong>knowledge is received within a lineage, not invented</strong>; the seeker stands indebted to those who preserved it, and its ultimate authority is <strong>the Reality itself</strong>. <strong>Practical step:</strong> honour your own <em>paramparā</em> — teachers, texts, and transmitters — and return all of it, like the chapter, in salutation: <strong>brahmaṇe namaḥ.</strong>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Colophon","title":"End of Adhyāya 4 (and of the Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa)","sa":["इति बृहदारण्यकोपनिषदि चतुर्थोऽध्यायः ॥ इति याज्ञवल्क्यकाण्डम् ॥"],"iast":["iti bṛhadāraṇyakopaniṣadi caturtho 'dhyāyaḥ || iti yājñavalkyakāṇḍam ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Thus the Fourth Adhyāya of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad; thus (ends) the Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"What Adhyāya 4 (and the Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa) has established","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>4.1 — Six partial definitions completed:</strong> speech, breath, eye, ear, mind, heart are each a true <strong>abode</strong> of Brahman, \"one-footed\" until given their <strong>support (space)</strong> and contemplative <strong>quality</strong>.","- <strong>4.2 — Indha &amp; Virāj; neti neti:</strong> the persons in the eyes meet in the heart-space; the Self is <strong>neti neti</strong>, and to know it is to reach the <strong>Fearless (abhaya)</strong>.","- <strong>4.3 — The lights and the states:</strong> the Self is the <strong>light of lights</strong>; in dream it is <strong>self-luminous (svayaṃ-jyotiḥ)</strong> and the <strong>maker</strong>; in deep sleep, <strong>\"embraced by the Self,\" desireless, fearless, beyond sorrow</strong>; it is the <strong>unattached witness</strong> (great-fish), the <strong>imperishable Seer</strong> (\"though seeing, it sees no second\"), the <strong>one non-dual awareness</strong> (<em>salila eko draṣṭā advaitaḥ</em>) — the highest bliss, of which all joys are a fraction.","- <strong>4.4 — Death, karma, liberation:</strong> the self departs with <strong>knowledge, works, and impressions</strong>, transmigrating like a <strong>leech</strong>/<strong>goldsmith</strong>; <strong>\"as one acts, so one becomes\"</strong> (desire → will → act → destiny); but the <strong>desireless knower</strong> does not transmigrate — <strong>\"being Brahman, he merges in Brahman,\"</strong> and <strong>\"here the mortal becomes immortal\"</strong> (snake-slough). The garland of verses: the <strong>two darknesses</strong>, <strong>\"there is no plurality here\" (neha nānāsti)</strong>, <strong>\"realise — do not drown in words,\"</strong> and the <strong>great unborn, fearless Self (abhayaṃ brahma)</strong>.","- <strong>4.5 — Maitreyī II:</strong> the seal — all is dear for the Self; the Self is the <strong>imperishable, unobjectifiable Knower</strong> (<em>neti neti</em>); <strong>\"this much is immortality\"</strong> — whereupon Yājñavalkya <strong>renounces</strong> and departs.","- <strong>4.6 — Vaṃśa:</strong> the lineage certifying the teaching back to <strong>Brahman the self-existent</strong>.","With this the <strong>Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa (3–4)</strong> completes the Upaniṣad's <strong>proof, by dialectic and analysis, of the one self-luminous, unborn, non-dual, fearless Self</strong> — established against all challengers (Adhyāya 3) and unfolded through the states, death, and liberation (Adhyāya 4), and crowned by the sage's own renunciation.","<em>Continued in Adhyāya 5 (the Khila-kāṇḍa, \"supplement\"): a series of shorter meditations and ethical teachings — the</em> pūrṇam-adaḥ <em>invocation and \"space is Brahman\" (5.1); the threefold</em> da-da-da <em>(self-control, charity, compassion) of the Prajāpati teaching (5.2); the heart as Brahman (5.3); meditations on satya, the sun, the gāyatrī, and the dying man's prayer (5.4–5.15).</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Sources & method (for this volume)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>Mūla text:</strong> Kāṇva recension, verified mantra-by-mantra against the <strong>GRETIL</strong> transliterated edition (Göttingen Register of Electronic Texts in Indian Languages; <em>mūla</em> extracted from the Śaṅkara-commented version, checked against Limaye–Vadekar, <em>Eighteen Principal Upaniṣads</em>, Poona 1958). Devanāgarī rendered from the verified IAST; sandhi/pada-division conventions follow standard print editions and may vary slightly from other editions.","- <strong>Translations &amp; commentaries consulted:</strong> Swāmī Mādhavānanda, <em>The Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya</em> (Advaita Ashrama); Sureśvara, <em>Bṛhadāraṇyakopaniṣad-bhāṣya-vārttika</em> (tradition); Swami Krishnananda, <em>The Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad</em> (Divine Life Society); Swami Sivananda (DLS); Paul Deussen, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (1897); Patrick Olivelle, <em>The Early Upaniṣads</em> (OUP, 1998).","- <strong>Format:</strong> modelled on the canonical per-verse template of this document series (Devanāgarī → IAST → pada-by-pada gloss → literal → paraphrase → 5-commentary digest), extended per brāhmaṇa with <strong>Details</strong>, <strong>Visual representation</strong>, and <strong>Practical steps / observations</strong>. Long verbatim-parallel mantra-sets are given in full Devanāgarī + IAST with a shared pada-analysis and consolidated table; the Maitreyī repetition (4.5) is cross-referenced to its first occurrence (2.4).","- <strong>Coverage so far:</strong> Adhyāyas 1–4 complete (the Madhu-kāṇḍa and the Yājñavalkya-kāṇḍa). Adhyāyas 5–6 (the Khila-kāṇḍa) to follow in later sessions.","- ⚠️ <strong>Recommendation:</strong> for close textual or liturgical work, spot-check each mantra against the accented (<em>sasvara</em>) edition at sanskritdocuments.org and a print critical edition."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
